window.INTERVIEW_DB = {"version":2,"updatedAt":1792177335558,"categories":[{"id":"ade","name":"Azure Data Engineer","description":"Azure Data Engineering interview preparation","topics":[{"id":"adf","name":"Azure Data Factory (ADF)","icon":"🏭","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Azure Data Factory and is it an ETL or ELT tool?","type":"concept","a":"Azure Data Factory (ADF) is a cloud-based, serverless data integration/orchestration service used to move and transform data at scale.\n• Supports BOTH ETL and ELT: it can transform before load (Data Flows / Databricks) or load then transform in the target (e.g., Synapse).\n• Key building blocks: Pipelines, Activities, Datasets, Linked Services, Integration Runtimes, Triggers.\n• 90+ built-in connectors (on-prem, cloud, SaaS) via copy activity and mapping data flows.\n• Primarily an orchestrator — heavy transformations are usually pushed to Databricks/Synapse."},{"q":"Explain Linked Services, Datasets, and Pipelines. How do they differ?","type":"concept","a":"• Linked Service = connection info (like a connection string): endpoint + credentials/auth (e.g., Azure SQL, ADLS, Blob). Reusable across datasets.\n• Dataset = a pointer to the data within a linked service (table name, file path, folder). Defines structure + location, not the connection itself.\n• Pipeline = logical container of activities that performs the work (copy, transform, control flow). Triggered by a trigger or on demand.\nAnalogy: Linked Service = connection, Dataset = which table/file, Pipeline = the workflow."},{"q":"What are the types of Integration Runtime (IR) in ADF and when would you use each?","type":"concept","a":"• Azure IR — default, fully managed cloud compute for cloud-to-cloud data movement and Data Flow transformations. Auto-resolves region.\n• Self-hosted IR (SHIR) — installed on an on-prem/VM machine for accessing private/on-prem networks (on-prem SQL, file shares). Establishes outbound secure connection, so no inbound firewall ports needed.\n• Azure-SSIS IR — managed cluster to lift-and-shift/run existing SSIS packages (ETL) in Azure.\nRule: on-prem/private network → SHIR; cloud → Azure IR; SSIS packages → Azure-SSIS IR."},{"q":"How does a Self-Hosted Integration Runtime connect to ADF without opening inbound ports?","type":"concept","a":"• Install the IR software on an on-prem/VM machine.\n• Register it in ADF using the authentication key generated by ADF.\n• The SHIR opens an OUTBOUND HTTPS connection to the ADF service (no inbound ports/firewall changes needed).\n• ADF sends activity requests down that channel; SHIR reads/writes data between on-prem and Azure.\n• Once registered it shows as 'Online' in ADF Monitor."},{"q":"What are the different types of triggers available in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Schedule trigger — wall-clock recurrence (e.g., daily 10 PM), periodic jobs.\n• Tumbling Window trigger — time-sliced windows with state/backfill, supports dependencies between windows; ideal for incremental/scheduled loads with retries.\n• Event trigger — fires when an event occurs (e.g., a blob/file arrives in ADLS / Blob created).\n• Manual (on-demand) — run immediately for testing/ad-hoc.\nChoose: periodic → Schedule/Tumbling; file-landing → Event."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Copy Activity copies data from SQL source to ADLS. A new column was added at the source. How do you handle this?","type":"scenario","a":"• If schema mapping is Auto Mapping, extra columns are ignored/mapped automatically — copy still succeeds.\n• Enable 'Allow schema drift' in the sink/data-flow settings so the new column flows through.\n• In Mapping Data Flows, schema drift lets new/changed columns be handled by name at runtime (use 'Allow schema drift').\n• If using explicit mapping, add the new column to the mapping (or parameterize mapping).\n• Best practice: column-driven, parameterized pipelines + schema drift enabled, and land raw data so downstream (Databricks) handles evolution with mergeSchema."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The Copy Activity keeps failing because of a very large file/data size. How do you fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Increase the DIU (Data Integration Units) / parallelism for Copy to speed up and split the load.\n• For files, use binary copy for a straight file-to-file move (no parsing) — fastest.\n• Increase timeout & retry; enable 'Enable staging' or partitioned copy for large sinks.\n• Split the source read using partition options (physical/virtual partitions, dynamic range) to parallelize.\n• Increase the Self-hosted IR nodes/cores if on-prem, or use Azure IR for cloud.\n• Consider ADF only for movement and push heavy transforms to Databricks; land as Parquet/Delta to reduce size."},{"q":"SCENARIO: How do you design a pipeline that loads many tables with different schemas into ADLS? (metadata-driven ingestion)","type":"scenario","a":"• Store a control/config table (or JSON) listing each source table + its parameters (schema, table, target path, watermark column, load type).\n• Use Lookup/Get Metadata to read the list.\n• Wrap a ForEach over the list; inside, use a parameterized dataset (schema/table/path as parameters) and a Copy Activity.\n• Optionally use a Mapping Data Flow with 'Allow schema drift' to handle differing schemas generically.\n• Write raw to a Bronze landing zone (one folder per table) — no per-table code changes needed for new tables."},{"q":"SCENARIO: How would you copy a list of tables from on-prem SQL Server to ADLS?","type":"scenario","a":"• Create a Self-hosted IR (for on-prem access) and link to the on-prem SQL Server.\n• Use a Lookup/Get Metadata activity to fetch the list of tables (from INFORMATION_SCHEMA or a control table).\n• ForEach over the table list → Copy Activity with a parameterized source dataset (schema+table) and parameterized sink path.\n• Set ForEach concurrency + DIUs for throughput; add retry policy.\n• Write to ADLS Gen2 (Bronze) as Parquet/Delta, optionally partitioned by date/table."},{"q":"What is meant by 'degree of parallelism' and 'batch count' (DIU) in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Degree of parallelism — how many activities run in parallel inside a ForEach (max typically 50) or how Data Flows partition work. Higher = faster but more compute/cost.\n• Data Integration Units (DIU) — unit of compute for the Copy Activity (4–256); more DIUs = faster copy for large data.\n• Batch count — used by Data Flows to control the number of parallel processing batches/partitions. Higher batch count = more parallelism (needs more cores).\nTune both to balance throughput vs cost; over-parallelizing can overwhelm the source or IR."},{"q":"When would you use Mapping Data Flows vs a Databricks notebook for transformation in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Mapping Data Flows — code-free, visual Spark-based transforms (filter/join/aggregate/pivot/surrogate key). Good for moderate complexity, no-code teams, small-medium data.\n• Databricks notebook — full-code PySpark/Scala/SQL for complex logic, ML, large-scale processing, custom libraries. More control + performance tuning.\nRule: simple/medium transforms → Data Flows; complex/large/advanced → Databricks. Many shops use ADF only for orchestration and Databricks for all transformation."},{"q":"How do you implement incremental loading in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Maintain a watermark (last loaded max timestamp/ID) in a control table.\n• Use Lookup to read the last watermark; pass it into the source query as a parameter (WHERE modified_date > watermark).\n• Copy only the delta to the target.\n• After successful load, run a Stored Procedure activity to update the watermark with the new max value.\n• Add retry policy + failure handling so the watermark is only advanced on success (idempotency)."},{"q":"How do you optimize an ADF pipeline?","type":"scenario","a":"• Use incremental loads (watermark/CDC) instead of full loads.\n• Parameterize pipelines/datasets (metadata-driven) to reuse and avoid duplication.\n• Tune degree of parallelism, ForEach concurrency, DIUs, and batch/partition settings.\n• Use the right IR (Azure IR for cloud, SHIR for on-prem) and scale SHIR nodes.\n• Avoid unnecessary copies; use binary copy for raw file moves; use Tumbling Window triggers.\n• Enable staging (Sink staging) and partitioned copy for large loads; choose Parquet/ORC/Delta over CSV.\n• Push transforms to Databricks; monitor with Azure Monitor and set alerts."},{"q":"How do you debug or troubleshoot a failing ADF pipeline?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the Monitor tab → pipeline runs → identify the failed activity and read the error message + activity output (input/output).\n• Check the Linked Service (credentials/firewall) and Dataset (path/schema).\n• Verify parameters/variables and the Integration Runtime status.\n• Use Data Flow Debug mode to step through transforms and inspect data shape.\n• Common causes: auth failure, timeout, file not found, schema mismatch, network/permission issues.\n• Enable Azure Monitor / Log Analytics (KQL) for detailed logs and configure failure alerts; retry the activity."},{"q":"Can a Copy Activity fail on a column mismatch?","type":"concept","a":"Depends:\n• Auto mapping — extra/missing columns are ignored; matching columns are copied, so it usually succeeds.\n• Explicit mapping — if required mapped columns are missing, or the sink datatype is incompatible, it fails.\n• Schema mismatch in a strongly-typed sink (e.g., SQL table) can also fail.\nFix: enable schema drift, add/parameterize mapping, or map to a flexible sink."},{"q":"How is Azure Key Vault integrated with ADF for secrets management?","type":"concept","a":"• Create a Key Vault and store secrets (connection strings, passwords, SPN keys).\n• Create an ADF Managed Identity (or service principal) and grant it 'Get/List' secret permissions on the Key Vault.\n• In Linked Services, choose 'Azure Key Vault' as the secret store and reference secrets dynamically (no hard-coded secrets).\n• In Databricks, mount/read secrets via dbutils.secrets backed by Key Vault secret scope.\nBenefit: secrets never appear in pipeline JSON/notebooks; rotate centrally."},{"q":"How does ADF store pipeline logs and how do you monitor at scale?","type":"concept","a":"• Native monitoring: ADF Monitor tab (runs, activity I/O, durations, errors).\n• Diagnostic settings → send logs/metrics to Azure Monitor / Log Analytics workspace; query with KQL.\n• Create alerts on failed runs / long durations → email, SMS, action groups.\n• ADF stores run history ~45 days; export to storage/Log Analytics for long-term retention.\n• Azure Monitor = metrics + alerting; Log Analytics = queryable log store (KQL)."},{"q":"What is a tumbling window trigger and how does it differ from a schedule trigger?","type":"concept","a":"• Schedule trigger — simple recurrence; no built-in state/history; can miss/fire independently of data windows.\n• Tumbling window trigger — fires for non-overlapping, fixed-size time windows (each with a start/end), maintains state per window, supports retry of just the failed window and backfill of historical windows.\n• Supports dependencies on other tumbling windows and 'delay' to allow late data.\nUse tumbling windows for reliable incremental/scheduled loads with reprocessing needs."},{"q":"How do you pass values between pipelines/activities and parameterize environments?","type":"concept","a":"• Pipeline parameters (runtime inputs) and variables (mutable state).\n• Set Variable / Append Variable activities, and @pipeline().parameters / @variables() expressions.\n• Execute Pipeline activity passes parameters to a child pipeline; child returns via @activity('x').output or a stored proc.\n• Global parameters for cross-pipeline constants (e.g., environment, root paths).\n• Use ARM templates / CI-CD to override parameters per environment (dev/qa/prod)."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move data from an on-prem SQL Server to Azure SQL Database daily. How do you set this up?","type":"scenario","a":"• Install and register a Self-hosted IR on-prem for source access.\n• Create Linked Services: on-prem SQL (SHIR) and Azure SQL (Azure IR).\n• Create datasets for source table and target table; add a watermark column for incremental loads.\n• Build a pipeline: Lookup last watermark → parameterized Copy with WHERE modified > watermark → Stored Procedure to update watermark.\n• Add a Schedule or Tumbling Window trigger for daily runs + retry + alerts."},{"q":"What is schema drift vs schema evolution, and how does ADF handle each?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema drift = the source's columns change at runtime (new/renamed/reordered/removed columns) unexpectedly.\n• Schema evolution = intentionally allowing the target schema to change (add/modify columns) over time.\n• ADF: enable 'Allow schema drift' in Mapping Data Flows to handle incoming columns by name dynamically; enable mergeSchema in COPY/sink for evolution.\n• Downstream Deltabricks: use mergeSchema / autoMerge in Delta for evolution."},{"q":"How will you orchestrate Databricks notebooks using ADF?","type":"concept","a":"Flow: ADF Pipeline ↓ Lookup Ac(cid:415)vity ↓ ForEach ↓ Databricks Notebook Ac(cid:415)vity ↓ Stored Procedure / Email Steps:","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Create Databricks Linked Service","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Schedule using Trigger","type":"concept","a":"Notebook parameters: dbu(cid:415)ls.widgets.text(\"LoadDate\",\"\") loadDate = dbu(cid:415)ls.widgets.get(\"LoadDate\") ADF passes parameter into notebook.","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Have you worked on a GenAI project? What AI tools do you use day to day?","type":"concept","a":"• If you have experience: Yes\n• I have worked on GenAI-assisted development, mainly to improve produc(cid:415)vity rather than building large language model applica(cid:415)ons from scratch\n• I use AI tools for genera(cid:415)ng PySpark code, SQL queries, debugging, documenta(cid:415)on, code op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on, unit test crea(cid:415)on, and explaining complex logic\n• Common AI tools:  ChatGPT  GitHub Copilot  Microso(cid:332) Copilot  Azure AI Foundry  Azure OpenAI Service  Databricks Assistant  Claude (if permi(cid:425)ed by the organiza(cid:415)on) Typical day-to-day uses:  Generate PySpark transforma(cid:415)ons.  Op(cid:415)mize SQL queries.  Explain execu(cid:415)on plans.  Create documenta(cid:415)on.  Generate unit test cases.  Debug pipeline failures.  Convert SQL logic to PySpark.  Create sample datasets and mock data\n• If you have not worked on a GenAI project, a good response is: I have not yet worked on a dedicated GenAI project, but I regularly use tools like ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot to accelerate development, generate PySpark and SQL code, troubleshoot issues, and improve documenta(cid:415)on\n• I am also learning Azure OpenAI and Retrieval-Augmented Genera(cid:415)on (RAG) concepts to prepare for future AI-enabled data engineering projects","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"In Produc(cid:415)on, do we run notebooks manually?","type":"concept","a":"• Normally, No\n• Produc(cid:415)on pipelines are automated\n• Common op(cid:415)ons:  Databricks Workflows (Jobs) (most common)  Scheduled jobs (daily/hourly)  Trigger a(cid:332)er another job finishes  Trigger by file arrival  External orchestrators like: o Apache Airflow o Azure Data Factory o AWS Step Func(cid:415)ons o Control-M Typical produc(cid:415)on flow: Notebook A (Bronze) ↓ Notebook B (Silver) ↓ Notebook C (Gold) Databricks Workflows manage:  Scheduling  Dependencies  Retries  No(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons  Monitoring","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Azure Data Factory Scheduling","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create Linked Service In ADF → Manage → Linked Services → New → Azure Databricks.","type":"concept","a":"Provide workspace URL + access token (from Databricks → User Settings → Access Tokens).","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create Pipeline In ADF → Author → Pipelines → New Pipeline. Name it e.g.","type":"concept","a":"'ETL_Daily_Orders'.","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Add Databricks Activity Drag 'Databricks Notebook' activity from Activities panel into the","type":"concept","a":"• pipeline\n• Configure: Linked Service, Notebook path (e.g. /Shared/ETL/orders_pipeline), cluster settings","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Add Trigger Click 'Add Trigger' → 'New/Edit'. Choose type: Schedule (cron),","type":"concept","a":"Tumbling Window, or Event-based (file arrives in ADLS).","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Publish & Monitor Click Publish All. Monitor runs in ADF → Monitor → Pipeline Runs.","type":"concept","a":"• Trigger Types Trigger Type Use Case Schedule Trigger Run at fixed times: daily at 2 AM, hourly, weekly\n• Most common for batch ETL\n• Tumbling Window Non-overlapping time windows\n• Good for backfills and reprocessing historical data\n• Event Trigger Fires when a file arrives/is deleted in ADLS\n• Good for event-driven pipelines\n• Manual Trigger One-off runs triggered manually or by another pipeline. # Pass parameters from ADF to Databricks notebook # In ADF notebook activity → Base Parameters: # { 'run_date': '@formatDateTime(pipeline().TriggerTime, \"yyyy-MM-dd\")', # 'env': 'prod' } # In Databricks notebook, receive the parameter: dbutils.widgets.text('run_date', '2024-01-01') dbutils.widgets.text('env', 'dev') run_date = dbutils.widgets.get('run_date') env = dbutils.widgets.get('env') print(f'Processing data for {run_date} in {env} environment')","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Trigger?","type":"concept","a":"• A Trigger in ADF determines WHEN a pipeline runs\n• There are four main types of triggers","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Schedule Trigger","type":"concept","a":"• When to use: Runs a pipeline on a fixed time schedule — like a cron job\n• Example: Run every day at 6 AM, or every Monday at 9 AM. // Schedule Trigger — runs daily at 6 AM UTC Trigger Name : trig_daily_sales_load Type : Schedule Frequency : Day Interval : 1 Start Time : 2024-01-01T06:00:00Z Time Zone : UTC // Pipeline receives: @trigger().scheduledTime => '2024-01-15T06:00:00Z' // Use in pipeline: @formatDateTime(trigger().scheduledTime, 'yyyy-MM-dd')","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tumbling Window Trigger","type":"concept","a":"• When to use: Runs at regular intervals with a fixed, non-overlapping time window\n• ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 1 Each run gets a windowStart and windowEnd — perfect for incremental data loads\n• If a run fails, it can be retried for that exact window — no data gap. // Tumbling Window — processes data in 1-hour windows Trigger Name : trig_hourly_window Type : TumblingWindow Frequency : Hour Interval : 1 Start Time : 2024-01-01T00:00:00Z // Each pipeline run automatically receives: @trigger().outputs.windowStartTime => '2024-01-15T08:00:00Z' @trigger().outputs.windowEndTime => '2024-01-15T09:00:00Z' // Use in Copy Activity source query: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at >= '@{trigger().outputs.windowStartTime}' AND created_at < '@{trigger().outputs.windowEndTime}'","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Custom Event Trigger","type":"concept","a":"• When to use: Fires based on custom events published to Azure Event Grid\n• Used when an external system (not Azure Storage) sends an event\n• ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 2 Example: An IoT device publishes 'data_ready' event — pipeline starts\n• Quick Comparison Trigger Type How It Fires Best Use Case Schedule Trigger Fixed time — daily, weekly, Nightly batch loads, regular hourly reports Tumbling Window Fixed windows, retryable, Incremental loads with sequential guaranteed windows Storage Event File lands in ADLS / Blob Storage File-driven pipelines, real-time file ingestion Custom Event Custom event via Azure Event IoT, external system-triggered Grid pipelines Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'We used Schedule Triggers for nightly batch loads and Tumbling Window Triggers for hourly incremental loads because each window is guaranteed and retryable\n• For file-arrival scenarios, we used Storage Event Triggers that fire the moment a file lands in ADLS Gen2.' Q4 Migrate On-Premises Data to Azure SQL — Pipeline Design High-Level Architecture Flow: On-Premises SQL Server --> Self-hosted IR --> ADF --> ADLS Gen2 (staging) --> Azure SQL Database The Self-Hosted Integration Runtime (SHIR) is installed on-premises to securely connect ADF to the local network\n• Step-by-Step Pipeline Design 1 Install SHIR Install Self-Hosted Integration Runtime on an on-prem Windows server\n• Register it in ADF to create a secure tunnel between on- prem and Azure\n• 2 Linked Services Create two Linked Services: (a) On-Prem SQL Server using SHIR, (b) Azure SQL Database using public/private endpoint\n• 3 Datasets Create source Dataset pointing to on-prem SQL tables\n• Create sink Dataset pointing to Azure SQL tables\n• ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 3 4 Bronze Layer Use Copy Activity to copy raw data from on-prem SQL to ADLS Gen2 as Parquet files (bronze/raw zone)\n• This is the staging layer","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What are major components of Azure Data Factory.","type":"concept","a":"➢ Integration Runtimes: compute infrastructure to execute activities and performing data movements from different networks. ➢ Linked Services: Connection Information to any data source. ➢ Data Sets: Preparation of data to move and transform and load ➢ Pipelines: Designing of ETL process by using different activates and defining execution flow. ➢ Activities: To perform a specific operation like Copy data activity, delete activity, etc ➢ Triggers: Schedule of pipelines on specific time automatically, we need triggers.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of Integration Runtimes available.","type":"concept","a":"➢ Self hosted: it is the compute infrastructure that Azure Data Factory uses to provide data- integration capabilities across different network environments. ➢ Auto Resolve or Azure Integration Services: It is an offering by Microsoft Cloud for performing mission-critical integrations ➢ SSIS Integration Runtime: It is switch to the Manage tab and then switch to the Integration runtimes tab on the Connections pane to view existing integration runtimes in your data factory.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Linked Service and Dataset.","type":"concept","a":"A. Linked services are using to store connection information Datasets are preparing of data using Linked services","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Integration Runtime used to connect on premises Data sources.","type":"concept","a":"A. Self hosted","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Self hosted and Azure Integration runtime","type":"concept","a":"A. Self hosted Integration Runtime is for On premises or Private network data sources Azure integration runtime is for Cloud based services","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Auto resolve and Azure Integration runtime.","type":"concept","a":"A. Auto Resolve is Default Integration Runtime is used to connect cloud Azure Integration runtime is Customized runtime is used to connect cloud","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are Activities used in u r project","type":"concept","a":"A. Get Metadata--To get all list of filename in storage by specifying Childitems property Copy Data activity---Copying of data from any source to destination Lookup Activity--Retrieving of data from data sources supported by data factory and passing that output as input to other activities Ex: Retrieving watermark value while implementing incremental Loading Stored Procedure--Executing stored procedure Ex: We are executing stored procedure, to update watermark value in control table For Each Loop: Looping and executing several activities Ex: Copying of multiple files using single copy data activity If Condition :based on condition true or false activities will be executed Ex: If file exist run copy activity else send email notification to place file using web activity Web Activity: We are using to send email notification by using Logic apps Data Flows: we are using to apply transformations Page | 1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Lookup and Stored Procedures activity.","type":"concept","a":"• Lookup activity: It can retrieve a dataset from any of the data sources supported by data factory and Synapse pipelines\n• You can use it to dynamically determine which objects to operate on in a subsequent activity, instead of hard coding the object name\n• Some object examples are files and table Stored Procedure: You use data transformation activities in a Data Factory or Synapse pipeline to transform and process raw data into predictions and insights\n• The Stored Procedure Activity is one of the transformation activities that pipelines support","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to implement Incremental Loading.","type":"concept","a":"• A. ➢ Select the watermark column: Select one column in the source data store, which can be used to slice the new or updated records for every run\n• Normally, the data in this selected column (for example, last_modify_time or ID) keeps increasing when rows are created or updated\n• The maximum value in this column is used as a watermark. ➢ Prepare a data store to store the watermark value: In this tutorial, you store the watermark value in a SQL database. ➢ Create a pipeline with the following workflow: ➢ The pipeline in this solution has the following activities: o Create two Lookup activities\n• Use the first Lookup activity to retrieve the last watermark value\n• Use the second Lookup activity to retrieve the new watermark value\n• These watermark values are passed to the Copy activity. o Create a Copy activity that copies rows from the source data store with the value of the watermark column greater than the old watermark value and less than the new watermark value\n• Then, it copies the delta data from the source data store to Blob storage as a new file\n• Page | 2 o Create a StoredProcedure activity that updates the watermark value for the pipeline that runs next time","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to send email notifications","type":"concept","a":"Web activity by using Logic apps Alerts on Data factory level Questions 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 Go to Azure Data Factory Syntax","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind triggers scheduling","type":"concept","a":"➢ Schedule Trigger: As per schedule trigger recursively executing pipelines. ➢ Tumbling Window Trigger: It is executing in periodic time interval from specified start time, historical manner, etc ➢ Event-based Trigger: It will executing pipelines based on event specified like file uploaded or deleted blob","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between SSIS and Data Factory","type":"concept","a":"A.SSIS: ➢ It is one of components in MSBI ➢ It is ETL Tool available in On premises ➢ This is not auto scale up and down compute based on workload ➢ Very less number of cloud integrations ➢ SSIS contains more number of transformations Data Factory : ➢ It is cloud based ETL Tool ➢ It is having auto compute scale up and down ➢ It has more cloud integrations ➢ It has all options in one place, monitoring, scheduling ➢ comparing to ssis data factory has less number of transformations \\ Page | 3","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Data factory v1 and Data Factory v2","type":"concept","a":"A. Data Factory V1: ➢ GIT HUB Integration not available ➢ So many Activities and transformations are missing ➢ Triggers are not available for scheduling Data factory V2: ➢ connection information ➢ dataset ➢ Actions or activates ➢ Compute Infrastructure ➢ workflow ➢ scheduling","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is The difference between Parameters and variables","type":"concept","a":"A. Parameter : Passing value to Pipeline from outside at the time of running pipeline Variable: It is Storing or assigning values internally for re using of that values in Pipeline Set Variable---Assigning values to variable of data type as a string or Boolean Append Variable---assigning values to variable of data type as a array list(more then one value)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Flows","type":"concept","a":"• Mapping data flows are visually designed data transformations in Azure Data Factory\n• Data flows allow data engineers to develop data transformation logic without writing code\n• The resulting data flows are executed as activities within Azure Data Factory pipelines that use scaled- out Apache Spark clusters","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Azure Data Factory and Azure Data Bricks","type":"concept","a":"• ADF is primarily used for Data Integration services to perform ETL processes and orchestrate data movements at scale\n• In contrast, Data bricks provides a collaborative platform for Data Engineers and Data Scientists to perform ETL as well as build Machine Learning models under a single platform","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is difference between ADF V1 and V2?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Explicit Cursors:","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ When a user create a memory location to store the tables. ➢ These cursor will access the records in the table row by row Or one by one\n• Step 1.declare the cursor DECLARE @ACODE VARCHAR(10) DECLARE @SEATS INT DECLARE @CCODE VARCHAR(10) DECLARE CRS_RET_VARIABLE CURSOR--creating a cursor FOR SELECT * FROM RES Step 2.open the cursor OPEN CRS_RET_VARIABLE Step 3 fetching data the cursor FETCH NEXT FROM CRS_RET_VARIABLE INTO @ACODE,@SEATS,@CCODE WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS=0--it is holding the data BEGIN PRINT 'AIRCRAFT_CODE :' + @ACODE + ',seat_no:' + CAST(@SEATS AS VARCHAR(10)) +',CLAsess:' + @CCODE FETCH NEXT FROM CRS_RET_VARIABLE INTO @ACODE,@SEATS,@CCODE END Page | 47 (@@ Fetch_status: It is a environmental variable use to check whether cursor variable is holding the record or not\n• If record is there then the is value is’0’ ) Step 4 closing the cursor CLOSE CRS_RET_VARIABLE Step 5 deallocate the cursor DEALLOCATE CRS_RET_VARIABLE --count a number of row in each table DECLARE @TAB_NAME VARCHAR(MAX) DECLARE CURSOR1 CURSOR SCROLL FOR SELECT name FROM sys.tables OPEN CURSOR1 FETCH FIRST FROM CURSOR1 INTO @TAB_NAME WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS=0 BEGIN DECLARE @QUERY NVARCHAR(MAX) SET @QUERY=N'SELECT +''' + @TAB_NAME +''' AS TABLE_NAME,COUNT(*) AS ROW_COUNT FROM ' + @TAB_NAME EXEC sp_executesql @QUERY FETCH next FROM CURSOR1 INTO @TAB_NAME END CLOSE CURSOR1 DEALLOCATE CURSOR1 Page | 48 =======PROJECT ARTICHTERURE==================== Copying the data from On premises to Synapses by using a Azure Data Factory. ➢ Taking the data from On premises files like Oracle db or Some CSV file from FTP site. ➢ Load the data in to Data Lake gen 2 by using a Azure Data Factory with Pipelines and different activities like Copydata activity , get metadata activity, lookup activities for ETL process. ➢ After loading a data use a ploybase in Synapses Data Ware House creating a external tables for implementing slowly changing dimensions . ➢ Loading the data into Dimension and Fact tables which are available in Synapses. ➢ After that will connect to the Powerbi for designing Visualizations and Dashboards\n• Without using a Ploybase how we can copy the data. ➢ In source side there is a Dataset is Delimiter in that we want to change a three options. ➢ Firstly to change Option is Row delimiter keep a Line feed(\\n). ➢ And also there is two options is Escape character and Quota character is should be same\n• So click edit and write a double quotation symbol is “ both the options\n• How to run the Data Bricks Notebooks in Azure Data Factory ➢ In Pipeline there is a Notebook and add a Azure Databricks create a New . ➢ Give the details and remember if already created the cluster click the option is Existing interactive cluster. ➢ There is a option a Access token\n• So to give a Token go to workspace click on workspace name and clock on User settings. ➢ Generate New token give a New Comment Name and Lifetime(days) according to the client. ➢ And there create a new token copy that and paste into a Access Token. ➢ Go to settings and browse the file\n• Page | 49 ================AGILE METHODLOGY======================= With Simple Sentence of explanation of Agile and scrum work process: ➢ Agile it is a Methodology, It’s implement the project and processing be a iterative work(repeating the work) and incremental ➢ Previously there will use a Water Fall but there is a some draw backs like Taking the long time for delivery the project and we can’t add any update feature after few days of completion project. ➢ Mainly in this agile is we can delivery the project in a Specific features within short time period what the customer requirements firstly. ➢ The main Principles are Customer satisfaction ( customer can’t wait long time) and Collaboration( designing person, developer, product owner, scrum master). ➢ Product Backlog, there is a product owner he will collect the all the information like requirements and features of the project from the End User. ➢ Sprint Planning Meeting in this scrum master, production, developer, testing are all involved and in this there discuss of the work in a period of time. ➢ Scrum Master will check the principles of Agile end of the project. ➢ Sprint Backlog, here do the work what discussed in Sprint Planning ➢ Scrum Master will discuss every day with the team 10 to 15 mins of standup meeting for progress of the work and he will prepare the Brumdown chart( means the graph) progress of the work. ➢ Sprint Review , in this the product owner and Scrum master review the Project of the Customer requirements is completed or not. ➢ Sprint Retrespective Meeting, in this all members involved and discuss of progess of the work and also any changes of the work for the next sprint. ➢ And I will explain with example suppose what discuss in Sprint completed 90% remaining 10% it will add next sprint but in Water Fall can’t do like this\n• Page | 50","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Runs unit tests on the pipeline logic","type":"concept","a":"Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 1","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Validates ADF ARM templates (export from ADF Studio)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How to Answer Data Flow Questions in ADF","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Setting Up Alerts in ADF and Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Alerts in Azure Data Factory (ADF) ADF integrates with Azure Monitor to send alerts when pipelines fail, succeed, or take too long\n• Steps to set up an alert in ADF:","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Go to ADF Studio → Monitor → Alerts & Metrics","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Choose Signal — e.g., 'Failed Pipeline Runs' or 'Pipeline run duration'","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Save and enable the rule","type":"concept","a":"• Example: Alert fires when any pipeline fails → email sent to data-team@company.com with pipeline name, error message, and timestamp\n• Common ADF Alert Scenarios: Alert Type When to Use Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 5 Failed Pipeline Run Pipeline threw an error — immediate action needed Pipeline Run Duration > X min Pipeline is running slower than expected Activity Failed A specific activity (copy, data flow) failed Cancelled Pipeline Pipeline was manually cancelled or timed out Alerts in Databricks Databricks has two ways to set up alerts: Option 1 — Databricks SQL Alerts (for query results):","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Select events: On Failure, On Success, On Start","type":"concept","a":"• Option 3 — Custom Alerts via Azure Monitor + Log Analytics: # In your Databricks notebook, send custom metric to Azure Monitor import requests, json def send_alert(message): webhook_url = dbutils.secrets.get('scope', 'teams-webhook') payload = {'text': f'🚨 Pipeline Alert: {message}'} requests.post(webhook_url, data=json.dumps(payload)) try: # your pipeline logic here process_data() except Exception as e: send_alert(f'Sales pipeline failed: {str(e)}') raise 💡 Tip: Say in interview: 'For ADF, we used Azure Monitor Alert Rules connected to an Action Group that notified us via email and Teams\n• In Databricks, we configured job failure notifications and also used custom webhook alerts for business-critical failures.' Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 6","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Passing Variables/Values to a Child Pipeline in ADF","type":"concept","a":"• Parent-Child Pipeline Pattern In ADF, you can call one pipeline (child) from another (parent) using the Execute Pipeline activity\n• You pass values as parameters\n• Step-by-Step: Set Up Parameters in Child Pipeline","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Open the Child Pipeline in ADF Studio","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Use those parameters inside the pipeline with @pipeline().parameters.load_date","type":"concept","a":"Step-by-Step: Pass Values from Parent Pipeline","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Add an 'Execute Pipeline' activity in the Parent Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Select the Child Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Map values — you can pass static values, variables, or expressions","type":"concept","a":"• Example — Parent pipeline passing date and environment: // In Execute Pipeline activity → Parameters section: load_date : @formatDateTime(utcNow(), 'yyyy-MM-dd') environment : 'production' batch_size : 1000 // In Child Pipeline, use the parameter: // SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_date = '@{pipeline().parameters.load_date}' Example: Dynamic Child Pipeline Call Scenario: Parent pipeline runs for each region (North, South, East, West) using ForEach Activity, passing the region name to a child pipeline. // Parent Pipeline setup: // 1\n• Create an Array variable: regions = ['North','South','East','West'] // 2\n• Add ForEach Activity, iterate over @variables('regions') // 3\n• Inside ForEach, add Execute Pipeline activity Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 7 // Execute Pipeline → Parameters: region_name : @item() // current item from ForEach load_date : @pipeline().parameters.load_date // Child Pipeline uses it in Copy Activity source query: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE region = '@{pipeline().parameters.region_name}' AND sale_date = '@{pipeline().parameters.load_date}' Using Output Values from Child Pipeline Child pipelines can also return output back to the parent: // In Parent, after Execute Pipeline activity completes: // Access child output using: @activity('Execute_Child_Pipeline').output.pipelineReturnValue.record_count // In Child Pipeline, set Return Value: // Use Set Variable + pipeline return value to pass data back 💡 Tip: Key interview answer: 'We used the Execute Pipeline activity with parameters to pass the load date, environment, and partition key from parent to child pipeline\n• This made our child pipelines reusable across environments.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Production Pipeline Failed at 2 AM — What Do You Do?","type":"concept","a":"• 🔥 This is a behavioral + technical question\n• Show that you stay calm, follow a process, and communicate clearly\n• Phase 1: Immediate Response (0–5 minutes)","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Open ADF Monitor → check which pipeline failed and at which activity","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Note the exact error message and timestamp","type":"concept","a":"• Phase 2: Diagnose the Failure (5–20 minutes) Check these in order: Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 8 • ADF Activity Run details — click the failed activity, read the full error • Is it a connection failure? → Check if the source/target (SQL DB, ADLS, etc.) is reachable • Is it a data issue? → Bad records, schema mismatch, null in non-nullable column • Is it a timeout? → Pipeline ran longer than expected • Is it a permissions issue? → Managed Identity or Service Principal lost access • Check Databricks cluster logs if a notebook activity failed // Quick diagnostic checklist in ADF Monitor: // 1\n• Go to Monitor → Pipeline Runs → Find failed run // 2\n• Click on the pipeline → click the red (failed) activity // 3\n• Read Error Code and Message // 4\n• Common error codes: // - UserErrorInvalidConnectionString → Connection issue // - UserErrorDataCorruption → Bad source data // - ExecutionTimeout → Cluster/query too slow // - AuthorizationFailed → Permission issue Phase 3: Fix and Recover (20–40 minutes) Root Cause Fix Source system down Wait / coordinate with source team; trigger pipeline once restored Bad/corrupt data file Quarantine the file, fix/reprocess it, re-run pipeline Schema change in Update mapping in ADF Copy Activity or Data Flow; redeploy source Permission denied Re-grant access to Managed Identity via Azure IAM Pipeline timeout Optimize query / increase timeout setting; add partitioning Network/firewall issue Check Private Endpoint / VNet config with network team Phase 4: Rerun the Pipeline","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Once root cause is fixed, trigger a manual rerun from ADF Monitor","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Watch the pipeline live until it completes successfully","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"If the pipeline was part of a chain, ensure downstream pipelines run in correct order","type":"concept","a":"Phase 5: Communicate & Document (After Fix)","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How will you validate all the files are present ?","type":"concept","a":"• \"My approach would be: Step 1: Schedule Pipeline Create a Time Trigger in Azure Data Factory to start at 10 AM\n• Step 2: Check File Arrival Use the Get Metadata ac(cid:415)vity in ADF to list all files in the landing folder\n• Step 3: Validate File Count Expected files: Employee_1.csv Employee_2.csv Employee_3.csv Employee_4.csv Employee_5.csv A(cid:425)endance_1.csv A(cid:425)endance_2.csv A(cid:425)endance_3.csv A(cid:425)endance_4.csv A(cid:425)endance_5.csv If the total count is 10, con(cid:415)nue processing\n• Otherwise, stop the pipeline and send an alert\n• Step 4: Validate File Quality Before processing, validate:  File exists  File size > 0  Correct schema  Correct delimiter  No corrupt records Step 5: Load to Bronze Store the raw files in the Bronze layer\n• Step 6: Transform in Silver Perform:  Remove duplicates  Handle null values  Data type conversions  Standardize formats Step 7: Load to Gold Create business-ready datasets for repor(cid:415)ng.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you check whether files arrived by 6 AM?","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Answer: \"I would use the Get Metadata ac(cid:415)vity to retrieve the Last Modified Time of each file\n• The pipeline compares the (cid:415)mestamp with the expected arrival (cid:415)me (6 AM)\n• If any file is missing or delayed, I would:  Retry a(cid:332)er a configured interval  Send an email alert  Log the issue in the monitoring table  Stop downstream processing un(cid:415)l all required files arrive\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you validate all files are present?","type":"concept","a":"Sample Answer: \"I would validate using mul(cid:415)ple checks:  Expected file count = 10  File naming conven(cid:415)on  File size greater than zero  Schema valida(cid:415)on  No duplicate files Only a(cid:332)er all valida(cid:415)ons pass would the pipeline con(cid:415)nue.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Join (for small datasets)","type":"concept","a":"➢ If one side of the join is small enough, broadcast it to avoid shuffling the large dataset.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How to connect sql server to ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• To connect SQL Server to ADF, I use a Self-Hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me because SQL Server is usually on-prem\n• I install SHIR on a local machine, register it with ADF, and then create a Linked Service using SQL Server connector with SHIR\n• A(cid:332)er tes(cid:415)ng the connec(cid:415)on, I use Copy Ac(cid:415)vity to move data into ADLS or other sinks","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Types of trigger in ADF","type":"concept","a":"• Azure Data Factory supports three primary trigger types: Schedule Trigger, Tumbling Window Trigger, and Event Trigger\n• Schedule triggers run pipelines at predefined (cid:415)mes, Tumbling Window triggers execute pipelines for fixed (cid:415)me intervals with state management and backfill support, and Event triggers start pipelines based on storage events such as file crea(cid:415)on or dele(cid:415)on\n• Pipelines can also be run manually using Debug or Trigger Now for tes(cid:415)ng purposes\n• Trigger Type Runs Based On Common Use Case Schedule Time-based schedule Daily ETL jobs Tumbling Window Fixed (cid:415)me intervals Incremental/hourly processing Event File/storage events File arrival processing Manual User ac(cid:415)on Tes(cid:415)ng and debugging","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"if job stuck in queue what are things need to check","type":"concept","a":"• If an ADF job is stuck in the queue, I first check the Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me status and resource u(cid:415)liza(cid:415)on\n• Then I verify concurrency limits, pipeline dependencies, source and des(cid:415)na(cid:415)on system health, and network connec(cid:415)vity\n• For Mapping Data Flows, I also check whether the Spark cluster is s(cid:415)ll being provisioned\n• Finally, I review ADF monitoring logs and ac(cid:415)vity diagnos(cid:415)cs to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks or service-limit issues","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How do you call one pipeline from another in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Use the Execute Pipeline ac(cid:415)vity\n• Steps:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Create Parent Pipeline.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Add Execute Pipeline ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Select Child Pipeline.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you pass variables to a pipeline in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• There are two concepts: Pipeline Parameters Passed from outside the pipeline\n• Example: LoadDate = 2025-01-01 Access: @pipeline().parameters.LoadDate Pipeline Variables Created inside the pipeline\n• Ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es:  Set Variable  Append Variable Example: @variables('FileName')","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Configura(cid:415)on to call Databricks Workflow from ADF","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Create Azure Databricks Linked Service.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Publish and trigger the pipeline.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is your business problem?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Our client wanted to replace manual repor(cid:415)ng with an automated, scalable analy(cid:415)cs pla(cid:414)orm\n• Data was spread across mul(cid:415)ple opera(cid:415)onal systems, making repor(cid:415)ng slow and inconsistent\n• We built a centralized data pla(cid:414)orm that ingests data, performs transforma(cid:415)ons, stores curated datasets, and serves dashboards through Power BI.\"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How are stored procedures scheduled?","type":"concept","a":"Usually using:  Azure Data Factory  SQL Server Agent (on-prem)  Azure Automa(cid:415)on ADF Lookup or Stored Procedure ac(cid:415)vity invokes them.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Schedule every Monday and Friday","type":"concept","a":"ADF Trigger:  Type: Schedule Trigger  Frequency: Week  Days: o Monday o Friday","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are you wri(cid:415)ng stored procedures?","type":"concept","a":"• A balanced answer: \"Yes\n• I write simple stored procedures for incremental loads, valida(cid:415)ons, and staging opera(cid:415)ons\n• Complex business logic is typically handled by the database team, while I consume those procedures within Azure Data Factory pipelines.\"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What scheduler is used?","type":"concept","a":"Typically:  Azure Data Factory Triggers  SQL Server Agent (legacy jobs)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"CTE or Intermediate Table?","type":"concept","a":"Use CTE when:  Small query  Single use  Be(cid:425)er readability Use intermediate table when:  Reused mul(cid:415)ple (cid:415)mes  Expensive transforma(cid:415)ons  Large datasets  Mul(cid:415)ple downstream jobs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"When materialize data?","type":"concept","a":"Examples:  Heavy joins  Complex aggrega(cid:415)ons  Data reused several (cid:415)mes  Large ETL pipelines Materializing avoids recompu(cid:415)ng expensive transforma(cid:415)ons.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Bring Produc(cid:415)on-like data to Dev","type":"concept","a":"Use:  Masked/anonymized produc(cid:415)on copy  Sample datasets  Synthe(cid:415)c test data  Restore from backup (with sensi(cid:415)ve data removed)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Root cause analysis","type":"concept","a":"Check:  Pipeline logs  Databricks job logs  Spark UI  ADF Monitor  SQL logs  Recent deployments  Data quality changes","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Install a Self-hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me (SHIR) on a server that can access the on-prem","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Register the SHIR with Azure Data Factory.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Create a linked service in ADF using the SHIR.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Mapping Data Flow vs Wrangling Data Flow","type":"concept","a":"Feature Mapping Data Flow Wrangling Data Flow Type GUI ETL pipeline Data prepara(cid:415)on tool Complexity High (full ETL) Low/medium Use case Complex transforma(cid:415)ons Data cleaning Spark usage Yes (behind the scenes) Yes User type Data engineers Analysts","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Dataset vs Linked Service (ADF)","type":"concept","a":"Feature Dataset Linked Service Defini(cid:415)on Data structure (table/file) Connec(cid:415)on to data source Feature Dataset Linked Service Purpose Points to data Points to system Example Table, CSV file SQL Server, ADLS connec(cid:415)on Reusability Less reusable Highly reusable (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Simple:  Linked Service = connec(cid:415)on  Dataset = data reference","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Schedule Notebooks One by One Using ADB and ADF","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — Databricks Jobs (Native) Create a multi-task Job in Databricks Workflows where each notebook is a Task, and tasks run sequentially with dependencies: # In Databricks Workflows UI or via REST API: Job: 'Daily ETL Pipeline' Task 1: Bronze_Ingestion (notebook: /pipelines/bronze) -> No dependency Task 2: Silver_Transform (notebook: /pipelines/silver) -> depends on Task 1 Task 3: Gold_Aggregation (notebook: /pipelines/gold) -> depends on Task 2 # Schedule: 0 2 * * * (2 AM daily, cron expression) Method 2 — ADF Pipeline with Databricks Notebook Activities • In ADF, create a Pipeline with multiple 'Databricks Notebook' activities • Connect them sequentially with success dependency arrows • Set the Linked Service to your Databricks workspace • Schedule using ADF Trigger (Schedule, Tumbling Window, or Event-based) ADF Pipeline: 'ETL_Daily_Pipeline' [Notebook Activity: Bronze] --> [Notebook Activity: Silver] --> [Notebook Activity: Gold] Notebook: /pipelines/bronze Notebook: /pipelines/silver Notebook: /pipelines/gold Page 4 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide On Success -> On Success -> # Pass parameters between notebooks using ADF parameters: Activity Settings > Base Parameters > { 'run_date': '@pipeline().TriggerTime' } Method 3 — dbutils.notebook.run() (Chain from Master Notebook) # Master orchestrator notebook calls child notebooks sequentially result1 = dbutils.notebook.run('/pipelines/bronze', timeout_seconds=3600, arguments={'run_date': '2024-01-01'}) result2 = dbutils.notebook.run('/pipelines/silver', timeout_seconds=3600, arguments={'run_date': '2024-01-01'}) result3 = dbutils.notebook.run('/pipelines/gold', timeout_seconds=3600, arguments={'run_date': '2024-01-01'}) print(f'Bronze: {result1}, Silver: {result2}, Gold: {result3}') Best Practice: Use Databricks Workflows (Jobs) for production scheduling — it provides built-in retry logic, alerting, and monitoring\n• Use ADF when orchestrating across multiple Azure services (ADB + SQL + Blob + Functions)\n• Use dbutils.notebook.run() for simple chaining within a notebook cluster","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Types of Clusters and When to Use Them","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster Type When to Use Auto- Cost Terminate All-Purpose (Interactive) Development, exploration, ad-hoc Optional Highest — analysis in notebooks billed while running Job Cluster Production ETL jobs, scheduled Yes — after job Lowest — pipelines billed only during run High Concurrency Multiple users sharing one cluster, BI Optional Medium tools Single Node Dev/test, small data, local Spark Optional Lowest — no testing worker nodes SQL Warehouse Databricks SQL, Power BI, Tableau, Yes — auto- Per DBU (Serverless/Pro/Classic) BI dashboards suspend Page 15 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Delta Live Tables (DLT) DLT pipelines — Databricks fully Yes — auto- DLT pricing Cluster manages managed Instance Pools Pre-warmed VMs for faster cold start N/A Idle instance fee Best Practices: Job Clusters for all production pipelines (auto-terminate = zero idle cost)\n• SQL Warehouses for BI/reporting\n• All-Purpose only for development\n• Never run production workloads on All- Purpose clusters — it wastes money when idle","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Handling File Delays","type":"concept","a":"In Databricks (Autoloader) • Autoloader continuously monitors the source directory using file-notification or directory- listing mode • If a file is delayed, Autoloader simply waits and picks it up automatically when it arrives • No manual intervention required — the stream stays active • Files are processed exactly once using checkpoint tracking In ADF (Azure Data Factory) • ADF pipelines are typically schedule-triggered and will run even if the file has not yet arrived • If the file doesn't arrive, the pipeline either fails or processes with empty/no data Best practices to handle delays in ADF: Strategy How It Helps Page 2 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Storage Event Trigger Fires the pipeline only when a file lands in storage – no timing issue Tumbling Window Trigger Allows retry policies and dependency conditions between windows Retry Policy on Activities Configure retries with wait time on Copy/Lookup activities Get Metadata + If Condition Check if file exists before processing; loop until found Logic App / Event Grid Event-driven notification when file arrives to kick off pipeline","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Copy Activity and Data Flow in ADF","type":"concept","a":"• Copy Activity Copy Activity is a data movement component in ADF\n• It copies data from a source to a sink (destination) without applying complex transformations. • Supports 90+ connectors (SQL, Blob, ADLS, Salesforce, etc.) • Fast — optimised for high-throughput data movement • Can do basic column mapping and type casting • Does NOT support complex business logic or multi-step transforms Data Flow (Mapping Data Flow) Mapping Data Flow is a visual, code-free transformation component in ADF running on Spark behind the scenes. • Supports joins, aggregations, pivots, lookups, conditional splits • Runs on a Spark cluster — scalable to large datasets • Visual drag-and-drop designer; no coding required • More expensive and slower to start than Copy Activity Aspect Copy Activity Data Flow Purpose Move/copy data Transform data Complexity Simple mapping only Full transformations (joins, aggregations, etc.) Execution engine ADF integration runtime Spark (auto-provisioned cluster) Performance Very fast Slower (Spark startup time) Cost Low Higher (Spark compute) Code required? No No (visual) / Optional (expression language) Page 5 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Connect ADF and SQL Server to Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• A) Connecting ADF to Databricks Azure Data Factory can orchestrate and trigger Databricks notebooks, jars, or Python scripts via a Linked Service\n• Step Action 1 In ADF Studio go to Manage > Linked Services > New 2 Search for 'Azure Databricks' and select it 3 Choose authentication: Access Token, MSI (Managed Identity), or Service Principal 4 Provide Databricks workspace URL (e.g. https://adb-xxxx.azuredatabricks.net) 5 Select existing cluster or configure a new job cluster 6 Test connection and save the Linked Service 7 Use the Databricks Notebook / Python / Jar activity in your pipeline Authentication Options for ADF -> Databricks Method When to Use Page 1 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Access Token Quick dev/testing; generate token in Databricks User Settings Managed Identity (MSI) Production; no secrets stored;\n• ADF identity is granted Contributor role in Databricks Service Principal Production;\n• AAD app registration with client ID + secret stored in Key Vault B) Connecting SQL Server to Databricks Two common approaches: JDBC driver (direct query) or using ADF as an intermediary to stage data\n• Method 1 – JDBC Connection (Direct) jdbcUrl = 'jdbc:sqlserver://<server>.database.windows.net:1433;database=<db>' df = spark.read \\ .format('jdbc') \\ .option('url', jdbcUrl) \\ .option('dbtable', 'dbo.Employees') \\ .option('user', dbutils.secrets.get('scope','sql-user')) \\ .option('password', dbutils.secrets.get('scope','sql-pwd')) \\ .option('driver', 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver') \\ .load() df.show() Method 2 – Write back to SQL Server from Databricks df.write \\ .format('jdbc') \\ .option('url', jdbcUrl) \\ .option('dbtable', 'dbo.Output') \\ .option('user', dbutils.secrets.get('scope','sql-user')) \\ .option('password',dbutils.secrets.get('scope','sql-pwd')) \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save() Method 3 – Via ADF Copy Activity (Staging) • Use ADF Copy Activity: SQL Server -> ADLS (Parquet/CSV) • Databricks reads from ADLS using Autoloader or spark.read • Best for large volumes — avoids direct JDBC load on SQL Server Page 2 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is DLT.?","type":"concept","a":"DLT (Delta Live Tables)  Managed ETL framework in Databricks  Handles pipeline orchestra(cid:415)on + data quality automa(cid:415)cally","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Which is be(cid:425)er running notebooks in ADF or running in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"ADF vs Databricks execu(cid:415)on  ADF → orchestra(cid:415)on only  Databricks → processing engine Best prac(cid:415)ce: Run notebooks in Databricks, trigger via ADF","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is trigger in sql?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Trigger  Executes automa(cid:415)cally on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Which tool you will be using for development?","type":"concept","a":"Tools used for development  Databricks notebooks  ADF pipelines  SQL tools  Git / Azure DevOps  VS Code","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How would you design an incremental data load pipeline in Azure Data Factory?","type":"concept","a":"• I would design the incremental load pipeline using a watermark column such as LastModifiedDate or UpdatedDate\n• Instead of loading the entire dataset every time, the pipeline extracts only records that are new or updated since the last successful run\n• This reduces processing time, network usage, and overall cost\n• Source Database ↓ Lookup Activity (Get Last Watermark) ↓ Copy Activity WHERE ModifiedDate > Watermark ↓ Databricks Transformation ↓ ADLS ↓ Update Watermark Table Methods  Watermark column  LastModifiedDate  Change Data Capture (CDC)  Change Tracking","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me about a time when you faced a major production issue how did you resolve it?","type":"concept","a":"• Structure your answer using STAR\n• Situation: A daily ADF pipeline failed because the source schema changed unexpectedly\n• Task: Restore the production pipeline before the business reporting deadline\n• Action:  Identified the schema mismatch in logs.  Updated the mapping in ADF and modified the Databricks notebook to handle the new column.  Reprocessed only the failed batch using the watermark, avoiding a full reload.  Added schema validation and alerting to prevent similar issues\n• Result: The pipeline was restored within the SLA, reports were delivered on time, and future schema changes were detected automatically","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Azure Data Factory (ADF)","type":"concept","a":"Types of Integration Runtime (IR)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What are the types of Integration Runtime in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Azure IR — fully managed, used to run activities in the Azure cloud (copy data between cloud stores, run Data Flow). • Self-hosted IR — installed on on-premises machines or VMs; used to access data sources inside a private network or on-premises. • Azure-SSIS IR — lifts and shifts SQL Server Integration Services (SSIS) packages to run natively in Azure. Staging & PolyBase","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Have you created a DevOps pipeline to move code from one environment to another?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"No of clusters and worker nodes. What is the size of the worker nodes","type":"concept","a":"Number of clusters + worker nodes Sample strong answer:  Dev environment: 1 all-purpose cluster  Prod: mul(cid:415)ple job clusters per pipeline  Worker nodes: 2–8 nodes based on data volume  Node type: memory op(cid:415)mized / general purpose (Azure VM types like Standard_D3_v2 / D4_v3) Key point:  Smaller data → 2 workers  Large ETL → autoscaling up to 8–16 workers","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Types of Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me (ADF)","type":"concept","a":"1. Azure IR o Cloud-based o Used for cloud-to-cloud copy","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you debug/handle failures in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Check linked service.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Verify dataset path.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Delta Live Tables and Declara(cid:415)ve Pipelines","type":"concept","a":"• This is a newer interview ques(cid:415)on\n• Delta Live Tables Declara(cid:415)ve Pipelines Older name New name (Databricks) Focus on ETL General data pipelines Python/SQL SQL/Python Supports Expecta(cid:415)ons Yes Auto dependency Yes Recommended Declara(cid:415)ve Pipelines Simply say: \"Delta Live Tables has evolved into Databricks Declara(cid:415)ve Pipelines\n• Declara(cid:415)ve Pipelines is the newer and broader framework with the same core concepts plus addi(cid:415)onal capabili(cid:415)es.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Have you handled APIs?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Typical flow: REST API ↓ ADF Web Ac(cid:415)vity ↓ Copy Ac(cid:415)vity ↓ JSON ↓ ADLS ↓ Databricks or API ↓ Python Requests ↓ Databricks ↓ Delta","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Can we call API directly from Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Using Python: import requests url = \"h(cid:425)ps://api.sample.com\" response = requests.get(url) data = response.json() ADF is not mandatory\n• ADF is mainly used for orchestra(cid:415)on","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How can we op(cid:415)mize ADF pipeline?","type":"concept","a":"Methods:  Parallel ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es  ForEach concurrency  Trigger mul(cid:415)ple pipelines  Incremental load  Parameteriza(cid:415)on  Avoid unnecessary copy  Proper Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me  Retry policy  Tumbling window triggers","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Pipeline taking too long. How will you debug?","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Review ADF ac(cid:415)vity dura(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What are different types of compute in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks provides different compute op(cid:415)ons based on the workload\n• Compute Type Purpose All-Purpose Compute Interac(cid:415)ve development, notebooks, data explora(cid:415)on Job Compute (Job Runs scheduled or one-(cid:415)me jobs and terminates automa(cid:415)cally Cluster) SQL Warehouse Executes SQL queries for BI tools like Power BI, Tableau Databricks manages infrastructure automa(cid:415)cally; no cluster Serverless Compute management Model Serving Compute Hosts machine learning models for inference \"In my project, we mainly use All-Purpose Compute for development and debugging notebooks, and Job Compute for produc(cid:415)on ADF-triggered workflows\n• SQL Warehouses are used by analysts for repor(cid:415)ng, while Serverless Compute is available in some environments to simplify infrastructure management.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Brief Introduction (Self Intro)","type":"concept","a":"• Use a structured approach covering your experience, tech stack, and domain expertise\n• Sample Introduction \"I'm [Your Name], a Data Engineer with X years of experience\n• I specialize in building large-scale data pipelines using Apache Spark, PySpark, and Databricks\n• I have worked extensively with Delta Lake for data lakehouse architectures, and have hands-on experience with Azure Data Factory, cloud platforms, and SQL-based analytics.\" Key Skills to Highlight • PySpark & Apache Spark — core distributed processing engine • Databricks — unified analytics platform • Delta Lake — ACID-compliant data lakehouse storage layer • Azure Data Factory (ADF) — data integration and orchestration • SQL, Python — data querying and transformation • Performance tuning — partitioning, caching, broadcast joins","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"If an ADF pipeline fails in the middle, how do you process data without duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• The key concept is idempotent processing and checkpoin(cid:415)ng\n• Answer \"I design the pipeline so it can be rerun safely without crea(cid:415)ng duplicate records.\" Common approaches:  Use watermarking o Store the last successfully processed (cid:415)mestamp or ID in a control table. o On rerun, process only records greater than the last successful watermark.  MERGE (Upsert) instead of INSERT o Use SQL MERGE statement. o Update exis(cid:415)ng records and insert only new ones\n• Example: MERGE INTO Target t USING Source s ON t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.Name = s.Name WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT(CustomerID,Name) VALUES(s.CustomerID,s.Name);  Maintain pipeline checkpoints o Store pipeline execu(cid:415)on status. o Restart from the failed ac(cid:415)vity rather than beginning from scratch.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on-wise processing o Process one date par(cid:415)(cid:415)on at a (cid:415)me. o If one par(cid:415)(cid:415)on fails, rerun only that par(cid:415)(cid:415)on.  ADF Retry o Configure retry policy for transient failures.  Deduplica(cid:415)on o Use primary keys or window func(cid:415)ons to remove duplicates before loading","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Ka(cid:325)a?","type":"concept","a":"• Distributed event streaming pla(cid:414)orm used for real-(cid:415)me data pipelines\n• Components  Producer  Consumer  Broker  Topic  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Offset  Consumer Group","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Install Self-hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me (SHIR) on a machine that can access the on-","type":"concept","a":"prem SQL Server.","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Linked Service for SQL Server using SHIR.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create another Linked Service for ADLS.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Source Dataset poin(cid:415)ng to SQL Server.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Sink Dataset poin(cid:415)ng to ADLS.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Use a Copy Data Ac(cid:415)vity in the pipeline.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"in each folder, how do you move these files from ADLS to ADLS?","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose the source ADLS contains: Main/ Product/ file1.csv file2.csv Orders/ order1.csv Sales/ sales1.csv You want to copy all folders and files to another ADLS while preserving the same structure\n• Solu(cid:415)on Use:  Get Metadata  ForEach  Copy Ac(cid:415)vity Pipeline flow: Get Metadata (Main Folder) ↓ Child Items ↓ ForEach ↓ Copy Ac(cid:415)vity Configura(cid:415)on Get Metadata Dataset path: Main Field list: Child Items Output: [ {\"name\":\"Product\",\"type\":\"Folder\"}, {\"name\":\"Orders\",\"type\":\"Folder\"}, {\"name\":\"Sales\",\"type\":\"Folder\"} ] ForEach Items: @ac(cid:415)vity('Get Metadata').output.childItems Inside ForEach: Copy Ac(cid:415)vity Source folder: @item().name Sink folder: @item().name This copies each folder while preserving the folder names","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"why do u use look up ac(cid:415)vity and what value do u use with lookup while giving dynamic","type":"concept","a":"• parameter? Lookup retrieves data from a source and passes it to downstream ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es\n• Typical uses:  Read a control table.  Get a list of tables to process.  Read configura(cid:415)on values.  Read watermark values.  Drive dynamic pipelines\n• Example control table: TableName LoadType Product Full Orders Incremental Sales Incremental Lookup reads this table\n• Output: [ { \"TableName\":\"Orders\", \"LoadType\":\"Incremental\" } ] Pass the output to a ForEach ac(cid:415)vity","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is the use of Get Metadata Ac(cid:415)vity?","type":"concept","a":"• Get Metadata retrieves informa(cid:415)on about files, folders, or datasets without reading the file contents\n• Example: I use a Get Metadata ac(cid:415)vity to retrieve the child folders from the parent directory, pass the childItems output to a ForEach ac(cid:415)vity, and inside the loop use a Copy ac(cid:415)vity\n• The source and sink paths are parameterized with @item().name, allowing the pipeline to copy each folder dynamically while preserving the folder structure\n• If there are nested folders, I can extend this approach with addi(cid:415)onal Get Metadata and ForEach ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es or use a recursive copy if appropriate.\" Common metadata fields include:  Child Items  Exists  File Name  File Size  Last Modified  Structure (for supported formats)  Column Count (for supported formats) It is commonly used to:  Check whether a file exists before processing.  List files or folders dynamically.  Get file size or modifica(cid:415)on date.  Drive dynamic pipelines","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you create Get Metadata Ac(cid:415)vity?","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose your source is: Main/ Product/ Orders/ Sales/ Step 1 Create an ADLS dataset\n• Folder path: Main Step 2 Add Get Metadata ac(cid:415)vity\n• Choose the dataset\n• Step 3 Under Field List, select: Child Items Step 4 Run the pipeline\n• Output: { \"childItems\":[ { \"name\":\"Product\", \"type\":\"Folder\" }, { \"name\":\"Orders\", \"type\":\"Folder\" }, { \"name\":\"Sales\", \"type\":\"Folder\" } ] } Step 5 Use ForEach\n• Items: @ac(cid:415)vity('Get Metadata').output.childItems Inside the loop, use: @item().name to build dynamic source and sink folder paths","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"We have Azure Subscrip(cid:415)on containing ADF, Databricks, Key Vault, SQL Server (Metadata).","type":"concept","a":"• We need to migrate from one Azure Subscrip(cid:415)on to another\n• What is your migra(cid:415)on checklist? \"Before migra(cid:415)on, I would prepare a detailed checklist to ensure nothing is missed.\" Pre-Migra(cid:415)on  Take backup of Azure SQL metadata database.  Export ADF ARM template (or Bicep/Terraform if Infrastructure as Code is used).  Backup Databricks notebooks, jobs, clusters, libraries, and init scripts.  Export Key Vault secrets (securely).  Document Linked Services, Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)mes, triggers, managed iden(cid:415)(cid:415)es, RBAC roles, and firewall rules.  Verify ADLS data backup if required\n• Migra(cid:415)on  Create the new Azure Subscrip(cid:415)on and Resource Groups.  Deploy ADF using ARM templates.  Deploy Databricks workspace.  Create ADLS storage accounts.  Create Key Vault and import secrets.  Restore SQL metadata database.  Configure Managed Iden(cid:415)ty and RBAC permissions.  Update Linked Services with new resource IDs or endpoints.  Reconfigure Self-hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me (if used)\n• Post-Migra(cid:415)on Valida(cid:415)on  Validate Linked Services.  Test Key Vault access.  Verify Databricks cluster connec(cid:415)vity.  Run sample pipelines.  Validate triggers.  Verify monitoring and alerts.  Compare row counts between source and target.  Confirm downstream applica(cid:415)ons are func(cid:415)oning correctly","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How will you migrate SQL Server using ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would create a Linked Service for SQL Server as the source and another Linked Service for the target SQL Server or Azure SQL Database\n• Then I would create datasets for both source and des(cid:415)na(cid:415)on, use a Copy Data ac(cid:415)vity to move the data, and configure parallel copy and fault tolerance if needed\n• For mul(cid:415)ple tables, I would use a metadata-driven pipeline with a ForEach ac(cid:415)vity.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If I want to write one table to PostgreSQL from ADF, how will you do it?","type":"concept","a":"\"I would:","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a PostgreSQL Linked Service.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a source dataset.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a PostgreSQL sink dataset.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Execute the pipeline and validate the data a(cid:332)er loading.\"","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"One table failed out of 100 tables in an ADF pipeline. What will you do?","type":"concept","a":"\"I would:","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Check the failed ac(cid:415)vity in ADF Monitor.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you send automated no(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons a(cid:332)er pipeline comple(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"\"A(cid:332)er the pipeline completes, I use:  Azure Monitor alerts with Ac(cid:415)on Groups for success or failure no(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons.  Logic Apps to send emails or Microso(cid:332) Teams messages.  Web ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es to call downstream APIs if another applica(cid:415)on needs to be no(cid:415)fied.  Event Grid or Service Bus if downstream systems consume events.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Apart from inges(cid:415)on, what did you work on?","type":"concept","a":"Answer \"Apart from data inges(cid:415)on, I worked on data transforma(cid:415)on, op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on, orchestra(cid:415)on, monitoring, and repor(cid:415)ng.\" For example:  Built transforma(cid:415)on logic using Databricks (PySpark/Spark SQL).  Cleaned and validated data.  Removed duplicates.  Created Delta tables.  Implemented incremental loads.  Op(cid:415)mized Spark jobs using par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and caching where appropriate.  Scheduled pipelines using Azure Data Factory.  Monitored failures and resolved produc(cid:415)on issues.  Worked with business users to validate data.","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Clusters Exist in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster Type Use Case Auto- Cost Profile Terminate? All-Purpose (Interactive) Development, ad-hoc Optional Higher — charged while exploration, notebooks running Job Cluster Production pipelines and Yes (after job) Lower — only during job scheduled jobs run High Concurrency Multiple users sharing one Optional Medium — optimized for cluster sharing Single Node Development, local testing, Optional Lowest — no worker nodes small datasets SQL Warehouse Databricks SQL, BI Yes (auto- Per DBU, serverless option dashboards, analytics suspend) DLT Cluster (managed) Delta Live Tables pipelines Yes (auto- Managed automatically by only managed) DLT Best Practice: Job Clusters for all production runs (auto-terminate = zero idle cost)\n• All-Purpose only for development\n• SQL Warehouses for BI/reporting\n• Never run long production jobs on interactive clusters","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• How large is the data engineering team and how is work distributed across domains?","type":"concept","a":"Questions about technology and growth • Are you on Unity Catalog yet, or is that on the roadmap? I have experience with it and would contribute to the migration. • What does the CI/CD and testing process look like for data pipelines — dbt tests, Great","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"You are given a sales dataset containing product ID, transac(cid:415)on date, and sales amount.","type":"concept","a":"data = [ (\"P001\", \"2025-01-01\", 100), (\"P001\", \"2025-01-02\", 150), (\"P001\", \"2025-01-03\", 200), (\"P002\", \"2025-01-01\", 80), (\"P002\", \"2025-01-03\", 120), (\"P002\", \"2025-01-04\", 160), (\"P003\", \"2025-01-02\", 90), (\"P003\", \"2025-01-03\", 110), (\"P003\", \"2025-01-04\", 130), ] Using PySpark, perform the following tasks:","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"You are given a dataset where each record contains a product name and a nested structure","type":"concept","a":"holding pricing details. data = [ (\"product1\", {\"price\": 100, \"quan(cid:415)ty\": 2}), (\"product2\", {\"price\": 200, \"quan(cid:415)ty\": 3}) ] Using PySpark, perform the following tasks:","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"enabled alerts, messaging, and triggers in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• \"In my project, we implemented several op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in Databricks to improve job performance and reduce execu(cid:415)on (cid:415)me\n• Databricks Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques  Used Broadcast Join for joining small lookup tables with large fact tables.  Applied repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on() before large joins to distribute data evenly.  Used cache() for DataFrames that were reused mul(cid:415)ple (cid:415)mes.  Performed column pruning by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns.  Applied predicate pushdown by filtering data early.  Used Delta Lake OPTIMIZE to compact small files.  Used ZORDER on frequently filtered columns to improve data skipping.  Enabled Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) to op(cid:415)mize joins and shuffle par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons dynamically.  Used par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning on high-cardinality columns where appropriate.  Tuned the number of shuffle par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons based on data volume\n• Alerts and Messaging in ADF For monitoring pipelines:  Configured Schedule Triggers for daily execu(cid:415)on.  Used Event Triggers for file arrival scenarios.  Configured Azure Monitor alerts for pipeline failures.  Enabled email no(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons through Ac(cid:415)on Groups.  For business no(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons, we integrated Logic Apps to send emails or Microso(cid:332) Teams messages a(cid:332)er pipeline comple(cid:415)on or failure.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es used in project","type":"concept","a":"In a typical Azure + Databricks project:  Data inges(cid:415)on using Azure Data Factory (ADF)  Data processing using Databricks (PySpark)  Data storage in ADLS (Bronze/Silver/Gold layers)  Data transforma(cid:415)on using Spark SQL / PySpark  Scheduling pipelines in ADF  Monitoring jobs using ADF Monitor / Databricks Jobs  Data quality checks and logging  Incremental load handling (SCD, watermarking)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Limita(cid:415)on of Lookup Ac(cid:415)vity (ADF)","type":"concept","a":" Can return only up to 5,000 rows  Payload size limit (~4 MB)  Not suitable for large datasets  No pagina(cid:415)on support  Only meant for small reference data or control tables","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Loading data: Snowflake ↔ ADLS","type":"concept","a":"ADLS → Snowflake:  Use ADF Copy Ac(cid:415)vity  Source: ADLS (Parquet/CSV/Delta)  Sink: Snowflake connector  Uses staging (internal/external stage) in Snowflake Snowflake → ADLS:  Use ADF Copy Ac(cid:415)vity  Or Snowflake COPY INTO → external stage (ADLS)  Then ADF reads from ADLS if needed","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Different types of clusters (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":" All-purpose cluster (interac(cid:415)ve development)  Job cluster (for scheduled pipelines)  High concurrency cluster (shared BI workloads)  Single node cluster (small dev/tes(cid:415)ng)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me in ADF","type":"concept","a":"• Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me (IR) is the compute infrastructure used by ADF to move and transform data\n• Types:  Azure IR → cloud data movement  Self-hosted IR → on-prem to cloud data transfer  SSIS IR → runs SSIS packages in Azure TCS Technical Round","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Daily data load basis","type":"concept","a":"•  Usually based on: o Incremental load (CDC / watermark column) o Timestamp column (modified_date) o Event-based inges(cid:415)on  Scheduled via ADF triggers (daily/hourly) 7\n• SCD1 vs SCD2 SCD Type 1:  Overwrites old data  No history maintained  Used for correc(cid:415)ons SCD Type 2:  Maintains history  Adds new row with: o start_date, end_date o ac(cid:415)ve flag  Used for tracking changes over (cid:415)me","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Job vs Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"ADF Pipeline:  Logical grouping of ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es  Orchestra(cid:415)on layer Databricks Job:  Executes notebooks/code  Runs compute workloads Key difference:  Pipeline = orchestra(cid:415)on  Job = execu(cid:415)on engine Manager Round","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Daily ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es","type":"concept","a":" Monitor pipelines and fix failures  Develop and op(cid:415)mize ETL workflows  Handle data issues and support produc(cid:415)on  Code reviews and deployment  Work on enhancements and new pipelines","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Daily volume of data in your project","type":"concept","a":"• A sample answer: \"Our pipelines process approximately 100–200 GB of data daily, with around 5–10 million records depending on the source system\n• Incremental loads are used to minimize processing (cid:415)me.\" Adjust the numbers to match your actual experience","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"ADF Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":" Parallel Copy  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oned Copy  Incremental loading  Parameterized pipelines  Trigger scheduling  Self-hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me tuning  Retry policies  Avoid unnecessary ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Generator and Decorator","type":"concept","a":"• Generator Produces values lazily using yield. def gen(): yield 1 yield 2 Benefits:  Memory efficient.  Useful for large datasets\n• Decorator Adds func(cid:415)onality to a func(cid:415)on without changing its code. def log(func): def wrapper(): print(\"Start\") func() return wrapper","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"How do you know a job is op(cid:415)mized in Databricks and ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• In Databricks  Reduced execu(cid:415)on (cid:415)me.  Fewer shuffle opera(cid:415)ons.  Balanced task execu(cid:415)on.  Improved cluster u(cid:415)liza(cid:415)on.  Spark UI shows fewer bo(cid:425)lenecks.  Physical plan (explain()) uses efficient strategies (e.g., broadcast joins where appropriate)\n• In ADF  Pipeline completes faster.  Lower Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me usage.  Fewer ac(cid:415)vity retries or failures.  Be(cid:425)er throughput in Copy ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es.  Reduced overall execu(cid:415)on cost","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"How will you retrieve 10 files from an SFTP server to ADLS?","type":"concept","a":"\"I would create an Azure Data Factory pipeline.","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Configure a Linked Service for the SFTP server.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create datasets for both the source and des(cid:415)na(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How will you connect a SaaS applica(cid:415)on with Azure Data Factory?","type":"concept","a":"• \"ADF provides built-in connectors for many SaaS applica(cid:415)ons such as Salesforce, SAP, Dynamics 365, ServiceNow, and Snowflake\n• The process is:","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Linked Service using the SaaS connector.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create source and sink datasets.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Schedule the pipeline or trigger it based on events.\"","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How can ADF execute a Databricks notebook?","type":"concept","a":"• \"ADF integrates directly with Azure Databricks using the Databricks Notebook Ac(cid:415)vity\n• Steps:","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Linked Service connec(cid:415)ng ADF to the Databricks workspace.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Add a Databricks Notebook ac(cid:415)vity to the pipeline.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"During pipeline execu(cid:415)on, ADF triggers the notebook, waits for comple(cid:415)on, and","type":"concept","a":"captures the execu(cid:415)on status for monitoring and error handling.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Batch Ingestion","type":"concept","a":"• Data is collected and processed at scheduled intervals (hourly, daily, etc.) • Tools: ADF pipelines, Spark batch jobs, SSIS • Use case: End-of-day reports, historical data loads","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Change Data Capture (CDC)","type":"concept","a":"• Captures changes (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) from source databases • Tools: Debezium, Azure Data Factory, Oracle GoldenGate","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"API / Webhook Ingestion","type":"concept","a":"• Data pulled via REST APIs or pushed via webhooks • Tools: ADF REST connector, custom Python scripts","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"File-based Ingestion","type":"concept","a":"• CSV, JSON, Parquet, Avro files dropped into landing zones (ADLS, S3) • Picked up via ADF trigger or Auto Loader in Databricks","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What is Data Mapping?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Mapping is the process of defining how data from a source system corresponds to data in a target system\n• It involves: • Field-to-field mapping: source_column → target_column • Transformation logic: UPPER(first_name) → full_name • Data type conversions: VARCHAR → INT • Lookup/reference data mapping: 'M' → 'Male' • Handling nulls: NULL → 'Unknown' In ADF: Handled via Mapping Data Flows\n• In PySpark: via withColumnRenamed(), cast(), withColumn()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Create an ADF Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"• Steps to Create an ADF Pipeline: • 1\n• Open Azure Data Factory Studio → Author tab • 2\n• Click '+' → Pipeline → New Pipeline • 3\n• Add Activities from the activity panel: ◦ Copy Activity: Move data between source & sink ◦ Data Flow: Transformations (filter, join, aggregate) ◦ Execute Pipeline: Trigger child pipelines ◦ Lookup / Get Metadata: Fetch config/metadata ◦ ForEach / If Condition: Control flow • 4\n• Configure Linked Services (connection strings) • 5\n• Configure Datasets (source and sink schema) • 6\n• Set Parameters & Variables for dynamic pipelines • 7\n• Add Triggers: Schedule / Event-based / Tumbling Window • 8\n• Debug → Publish → Monitor Example: Incremental Copy Pipeline • Use Lookup to get last watermark from control table • Use Copy Activity with dynamic query filtering by watermark • Use Stored Procedure to update watermark after successful load","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Job Cluster (Automated Cluster)","type":"concept","a":"• Created for a specific job run, terminated when job completes • Cost-efficient: No idle charges • Used for production pipelines and scheduled jobs","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Identify ADF Pipeline Issues","type":"concept","a":"• Monitoring & Debugging Steps: • 1\n• ADF Monitor Tab: View pipeline runs, failed activities, error messages • 2\n• Activity Run Details: Click failed activity for input/output/error details • 3\n• Diagnostic Logs: Enable Azure Monitor / Log Analytics for detailed logs • 4\n• Integration Runtime: Check if IR is running and accessible • 5\n• Linked Service / Dataset: Validate connection strings and credentials • 6\n• Data Flow Debug Mode: Step through transformations to inspect data • 7\n• Alerts: Set up email/SMS alerts on pipeline failure via Azure Monitor Common Issues: • Connection timeout: Firewall rules, wrong credentials • Schema mismatch: Source schema changed • Data type errors: Data doesn't match expected type • Concurrency limits: Too many parallel pipelines • File not found: Wrong path or missing trigger file","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Challenges/Issues Faced in Pipelines","type":"concept","a":"• Common Pipeline Challenges: • 1\n• Data Skew: Uneven partition distribution causing slow tasks — Fixed with AQE/salting • 2\n• Schema Drift: Source schema changes break pipelines — Fixed with mergeSchema, DLT • 3\n• Duplicate Records: Source system sends duplicates — Fixed with deduplication and MERGE INTO • 4\n• Memory Issues / OOM: Large datasets exceed executor memory — Fixed with partitioning, proper cluster sizing, persist() • 5\n• Small File Problem: Thousands of tiny files degrade read performance — Fixed with OPTIMIZE in Delta • 6\n• Slow Joins: Large-large joins causing excessive shuffling — Fixed with broadcast hints, bucketing • 7\n• Pipeline Failures due to Connection Timeout: Network issues to source — Fixed with retry logic, connection pooling • 8\n• Data Type Mismatches: Source sends unexpected types — Fixed with explicit schema definition and validation • 9\n• Long-running Jobs: Jobs taking too long — Fixed with query optimization, cluster scaling, Z- ordering • 10\n• Incremental Load Failures: Watermark not updated correctly causing re-processing — Fixed with reliable watermark tracking in control table Pipeline Monitoring Best Practices: • Enable Azure Monitor alerts for ADF pipeline failures • Use Databricks job notification policies • Implement audit tables to track load history • Add DQ checks and fail fast on critical violations — End of Document — Data Engineering Interview Reference Guide —","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Challenges faced in dev environment","type":"concept","a":"“In dev environments, challenges are mostly related to data quality, performance mismatch, and pipeline instability.” (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Common challenges: (cid:48132) a) Data inconsistency  Missing or fake data in dev (cid:48131) b) Schema mismatch  Dev schema differs from prod (cid:44931) c) Small file issue  Frequent test runs create many small files (cid:48130) d) Performance not realis(cid:415)c  Dev cluster smaller than prod → misleading performance (cid:48133) e) Dependency issues  Downstream tables not always available","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"azure","name":"Azure Services","icon":"•","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"How will you connect ADLS and Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• In my project, we connected Azure Databricks to ADLS Gen2 using a Service Principal\n• We created an Azure AD App Registra(cid:415)on, generated a client secret, assigned the Storage Blob Data Contributor role on the storage account, and stored the creden(cid:415)als securely in Azure Key Vault or Databricks Secret Scopes\n• In Databricks, we configured Spark with OAuth authen(cid:415)ca(cid:415)on and accessed the data using the abfss:// path\n• For produc(cid:415)on environments, we avoid storage account keys and prefer Service Principal or Managed Iden(cid:415)ty for be(cid:425)er security","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Event-Based Trigger (Storage Event Trigger)","type":"concept","a":"• When to use: Fires automatically when a file arrives in Azure Blob Storage or ADLS Gen2\n• Example: Trigger pipeline the moment a new CSV file lands in a container\n• Can filter by blob path prefix and suffix (e.g., only .csv files in /raw/sales/). // Storage Event Trigger — fires when a .csv file lands in /raw/sales/ Trigger Name : trig_on_file_arrival Type : BlobEventsTrigger Storage Account : mystorageaccount Container : raw Blob path begins : /sales/ Blob path ends : .csv Event : Microsoft.Storage.BlobCreated // Pipeline receives the file path: @trigger().outputs.body.folderPath => 'sales' @trigger().outputs.body.fileName => 'sales_2024_01_15.csv'","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to implement security mechanism","type":"concept","a":"Azure Key vault","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to create mounting point to Datalake gen2","type":"concept","a":"• Step 1: Create a container in Azure Data Lake Gen2 Storage\n• Here, creating a container named blob-container. \n• Step 2: Get ADLS Gen2 Access Key. \n• Step 3: Create Secret for Access Key in Azure Key Vault. \n• Step 4: Create Mount in Azure Databricks. \n• Step 5: List Created Mount Point","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Cloud or Cloud Computing?","type":"concept","a":"• Cloud computing is renting & Accessing resources through internet browser, like storage space and CPU, Software’s & hard wares You only pay running on another company's computers\n• You only pay for you use\n• Or Accessing services remotely provided by third party vendors through web browsers from data centres\n• Examples of cloud providers Microsoft (Azure), Amazon (AWS), Google (GCP)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is storage account, different kind of storage accounts?","type":"concept","a":"Azure storage account is cloud based storage solution to store any kind of data like structured, Un structured & semi structured","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Containers or blob storage","type":"concept","a":"2. Tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is blob storage or containers what are the different types?","type":"concept","a":"Blob stands for Binary large objective file It is basically general purpose storage for unstructured data that include pictures, videos, Music files, documents, raw data, and log data…..","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Block blob: You can use block blobs for documents, image files, and video file storage.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Append blob: Append blob is similar to block blobs, but more often used append","type":"concept","a":"operations like logging","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Page blob: Page blobs are used for objects meant for frequent read, write","type":"concept","a":"operations. therefore used in Azure VMs to store OS & data disks","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is data lake and what is difference between data lake and blob storage?","type":"concept","a":"• Azure Data Lake is big data storage system used to store high volume, velocity, varities of data\n• It will store metadata hierarchal format.It is compatibility with Hadoop HDFS Frame work\n• Azure Blob Storage Azure Data Lake Storage General purpose Storage Optimized storage for Big data analytics purpose Object based model with container Hierarchical name space model Not Compatible with Hadoop compatible with Hadoop Less Cost of storage Cost is expensive High cost for analytics Low cost for analytics","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Difference between Azure SQL and Synapse DWH ?","type":"concept","a":"Azure SQL database Azure Synapse DW Insert, update, delete options mostly Selecting data from reporting OLTP OLAP Dynamic masking possible Dynamic masking not possible Polybase will not support to blob storage or Polybase will support to blob storage or Datalake datalake 4TB 1PB 30,000 concurrent connections 1024 concurrent quires 6400 32 not support 3 part naming not support 3 part naming Not support MPP Support MPP","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is difference between server less pool and dedicated SQL pool?","type":"concept","a":"Dedicated Server less pool pool Dedicated pool needs of providing of 'DWU' at Server less pool no need to provide, it is auto the scale up and down time of creating Dedicated pool used storage of synapse SQL Server less pool used to store data lake Server less pool charges only for executing Appling charges hourly queries","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"advantage of polybase?","type":"concept","a":"Querying the data from external data sources like blob, Data Lake into SQL synapse Data warehouse by creating external data source and external tables is called Poly base.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create external tables","type":"concept","a":"• Advantages: ➢ Azure blob storage is convenient place to store data for use by Azure service\n• Polybase it easy to Access the data by using T-SQL. ➢ Polybase makes it easy to query the data by using T-SQL\n• Query data stored in Azure Blob storage\n• Page | 27","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Flow in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Flows in ADF allow you to build data transformation logic visually (no-code/low-code)\n• They run on Apache Spark clusters behind the scenes\n• Key Components to Explain Component What to Say Source Where data comes from — e.g., Azure Blob Storage, SQL Database, ADLS Gen2 Transformation Steps like Filter, Join, Aggregate, Derived Column, Select, Lookup Sink Where processed data goes — e.g., Azure SQL DB, Synapse, Delta Lake Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 2 Debug Mode Run small samples to test transformations without full Spark cluster Data Preview View data at each step to verify your logic is correct Real Example: Joining Sales + Customer Data Scenario: Join a daily sales file (CSV in ADLS) with a Customer table in Azure SQL DB, and load the result into Synapse\n• Data Flow steps:","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Source 1 — Read sales CSV from ADLS Gen2","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Write a brief post-mortem: root cause, impact, fix, prevention","type":"concept","a":"Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 9","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your project in Azure.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Assign Storage Blob Data Contributor","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Store creden(cid:415)als in Key Vault","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Mount or directly access ADLS","type":"concept","a":"Example: spark.conf.set( \"fs.azure.account.auth.type.storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net\",\"OAuth\") spark.conf.set( \"fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type.storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net\", \"org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider\") spark.conf.set( \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id.storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net\", client_id) spark.conf.set( \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret.storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net\", secret) spark.conf.set( \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint.storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net\", endpoint) Then df = spark.read.csv(\"abfss://container@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/file.csv\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Have you migrated on-prem to cloud?","type":"concept","a":"• If Yes: \"Yes\n• We migrated data from on-premises SQL Server to Azure using Azure Data Factory\n• Ini(cid:415)al full loads were followed by incremental loads based on watermark columns or Change Data Capture (CDC)\n• The data was stored in ADLS, transformed using Databricks, and published to Synapse for repor(cid:415)ng.\" If No: \"I haven't led a full migra(cid:415)on project, but I understand the approach: assess source systems, choose migra(cid:415)on strategy (full/incremental), establish secure connec(cid:415)vity, load data into ADLS, validate data quality, and cut over a(cid:332)er successful tes(cid:415)ng.\"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What parts are on-prem and what parts are in Azure?","type":"concept","a":"On-premises:  SQL Server  ERP systems  Legacy applica(cid:415)ons Azure:  Azure Data Factory  ADLS Gen2  Azure Databricks  Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs  Azure Key Vault  Power BI","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Use Copy Ac(cid:415)vity or other ADF ac(cid:415)vi(cid:415)es to move data securely from SQL Server to ADLS","type":"concept","a":"• or directly to other Azure services\n• This setup provides secure communica(cid:415)on without exposing the on-premises database directly to the internet","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Authentication Methods","type":"concept","a":"• Service Principal (App Registration) — register an app in Azure AD; assign Storage Blob Data Contributor role on ADLS Page 8 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide • Managed Identity — assign a system/user-assigned managed identity to ADB cluster; grant RBAC roles on storage • Azure Key Vault + Databricks Secret Scope — store storage keys/SPN secrets in Key Vault; access via dbutils.secrets # Mount ADLS Gen2 using Service Principal + Key Vault secret scope configs = { 'fs.azure.account.auth.type': 'OAuth', 'fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type': 'org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider', 'fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id': dbutils.secrets.get('kv-scope','sp-client-id'), 'fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret': dbutils.secrets.get('kv-scope','sp-client- secret'), 'fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint': f'https://login.microsoftonline.com/{dbutils.secrets.get(\"kv-scope\",\"tenant- id\")}/oauth2/token' } dbutils.fs.mount( source = 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/', mount_point = '/mnt/datalake', extra_configs = configs )","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Network Security","type":"concept","a":"• Private Endpoints — restrict ADLS to VNet-only access • Service Endpoints — route traffic within Azure backbone • Firewall rules — whitelist specific IP ranges or VNet subnets 6. Encryption • Data at rest — Azure Storage Service Encryption (SSE) with Microsoft-managed or customer- managed keys (CMK) • Data in transit — TLS 1.2+ enforced on all ADLS connections","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Difference Between ADLS Gen2 and Azure Blob Storage","type":"concept","a":"• Feature ADLS Gen2 Azure Blob Storage Filesystem Hierarchical Namespace (HNS) — true Flat namespace — no real directories directories (virtual paths) ACL Support POSIX-compatible ACLs at file/folder level Only container-level RBAC Analytics Optimized for big data analytics General-purpose object storage (Hadoop/Spark) Performance Higher throughput for analytics workloads Slightly lower for analytics Protocol ABFSS (Azure Blob File System Secure) Wasbs (Windows Azure Storage Blob Secure) Renaming Atomic rename (metadata only — fast) Copy + delete (slow — copies bytes) Page 17 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Compatibility Hadoop, Spark, Hive, Databricks All Azure services, general HTTP Cost Slightly higher (namespace overhead) Lower — simpler storage Best for Data Lake, Databricks, Synapse Analytics Backups, static files, web assets, archives Tiering Hot, Cool, Archive Hot, Cool, Archive (same) For Databricks + big data workloads, always use ADLS Gen2 (not plain Blob)\n• The hierarchical namespace enables efficient directory operations and fine-grained ACL security at the folder level — critical for multi-team data lakes","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Core Technologies","type":"concept","a":"The following technologies form the backbone of a modern cloud data platform: Technology Purpose Apache Spark Distributed computing engine for big data processing Databricks Unified analytics platform built on Spark (notebooks, jobs, MLflow) Delta Lake Open-source storage layer – ACID transactions on data lakes Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS) / S3 / GCS Cloud object storage for raw and processed data Azure Data Factory (ADF) Cloud-based ETL/orchestration tool for data movement Unity Catalog Governance, lineage, and metadata management in Databricks Power BI / Tableau Visualization and reporting layer Azure Key Vault / Secrets Manager Secure credential and secret management","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Warehouse and Lakehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Warehouse: • A structured repository of historical data optimized for query performance and reporting. • Stores only structured data in predefined schemas (star/snowflake schema). • ETL process: data is transformed before loading. • Examples: Snowflake, Amazon Redshift, Azure Synapse, Google BigQuery. • Best for: BI dashboards, structured reporting, SQL analytics\n• Lakehouse: • Combines the best of Data Lakes (cheap storage, raw data, all formats) and Data Warehouses (ACID transactions, schema enforcement, performance). • Stores raw, semi-structured, and structured data on cheap object storage (S3, ADLS). • Uses open formats like Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, or Apache Hudi. • Supports both ELT and ETL patterns. • Examples: Databricks Lakehouse, Delta Lake on Azure, Apache Iceberg on AWS. • Best for: Unified analytics — ML, streaming, SQL, and BI on the same platform","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is managed and external table. What will happen if it is deleted?","type":"concept","a":"Managed vs External Table Type Behavior Managed Databricks manages data + metadata External Only metadata managed, data in ADLS If deleted:  Managed → data + metadata deleted  External → only metadata deleted, data remains safe","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"table. What will happen If you do any column data type changes or DDL changes?","type":"concept","a":"External table deleted + DDL changes If table deleted:  Data s(cid:415)ll exists in ADLS  Table can be recreated using same loca(cid:415)on If DDL changes:  Only metadata changes  Data remains unchanged  But schema mismatch may cause query failure","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the use of Staging and PolyBase in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Staging: When copying data into Azure Synapse Analytics (formerly SQL DW), ADF can stage data in Azure Blob Storage or ADLS first, then bulk-load it using PolyBase\n• This is far faster than row-by-row inserts\n• PolyBase: A technology that lets Synapse Analytics read data directly from external storage (Blob/ADLS) as if it were a table, enabling massively parallel, high-throughput bulk loads. • Benefit: Avoids single-node bottleneck — data is loaded in parallel across all Synapse distributions. • Requirement: Data must be in a supported format (Parquet, ORC, delimited text) in the staging area\n• Optimizing Mapping Data Flow","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"How will you mount Databricks and what are the other ways to connect to ADLS","type":"concept","a":"container? Databricks Mount + ADLS connec(cid:415)on methods (cid:44934) Mount ADLS in Databricks (using Service Principal) dbu(cid:415)ls.fs.mount( source = \"abfss://container@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/\", mount_point = \"/mnt/data\", extra_configs = { \"fs.azure.account.auth.type.storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net\": \"OAuth\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type\": \"org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id\": \"<client-id>\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret\": \"<secret>\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint\": \"h(cid:425)ps://login.microso(cid:332)online.com/<tenant- id>/oauth2/token\" } ) (cid:44934) Other ways to connect ADLS:  Direct ABFS path (abfss://)  Service Principal authen(cid:415)ca(cid:415)on  Managed Iden(cid:415)ty (best prac(cid:415)ce)  Unity Catalog external loca(cid:415)ons","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Azure SSIS IR","type":"concept","a":"o Runs SSIS packages in Azure","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How do you move data from SQL Server On-Premises to ADLS?","type":"concept","a":"Answer \"To move data from an on-premises SQL Server to Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS), I use Azure Data Factory with a Self-hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me.\" Steps","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Types of sources in project","type":"concept","a":" Azure SQL Database  On-prem SQL Server (via Self-hosted IR)  ADLS Gen2  Snowflake  APIs (REST)  Flat files (CSV, JSON, Parquet)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Different sources in your project","type":"concept","a":"Typical sources:  SQL Server  Oracle  SAP  REST APIs  CSV/Excel files  Azure Blob Storage  ADLS Gen2  Salesforce (if applicable)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Configure another Linked Service for Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Use the Copy Data ac(cid:415)vity to transfer files from SFTP to ADLS.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Have you started your AI journey?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes\n• I have started learning Genera(cid:415)ve AI concepts such as Large Language Models (LLMs), Retrieval-Augmented Genera(cid:415)on (RAG), prompt engineering, embeddings, and vector databases\n• I am also exploring how AI integrates with Azure services to build intelligent applica(cid:415)ons.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Use a Copy Ac(cid:415)vity or Mapping Data Flow to move the data into Azure Data Lake or","type":"concept","a":"Azure SQL Database.","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How will you share Databricks tables that are not accessible to other teams?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If another team does not have direct access to Databricks, we can:  Export the data as Delta, Parquet, or CSV files to ADLS.  Provide access through Azure SQL Database or Synapse if appropriate.  Share data using Delta Sharing (if enabled).  Expose the data through Power BI or APIs based on business requirements\n• The approach depends on the organiza(cid:415)on's security and governance policies.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"}]},{"id":"spark","name":"Apache Spark & Databricks Fundamentals","icon":"⚡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the Apache Spark architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Spark uses a master-worker (driver-executor) model:\n• Driver — runs the user code, builds the DAG (logical plan), creates SparkContext, schedules tasks, collects results.\n• Cluster Manager — allocates resources (Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos). In Databricks it's managed.\n• Executors — JVM processes on worker nodes that run tasks and cache partitions in memory/disk.\n• Task — smallest unit of work (one per partition).\n• Stage — set of tasks with no shuffle boundary. Job — triggered by an action.\nFlow: action → Driver → DAG Scheduler splits into stages at shuffles → Task Scheduler sends tasks to executors → results returned."},{"q":"Explain DAG, Jobs, Stages, and Tasks in Spark.","type":"concept","a":"• DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) — the logical execution plan of transformations; built lazily.\n• Job — created when an action is called; a DAG broken at shuffle boundaries.\n• Stage — a set of transformations that can run without a shuffle (narrow pipeline); shuffle starts a new stage.\n• Task — one task per partition per stage; executes on an executor core.\nSo: 1 action → 1+ Jobs → each Job has Stages → each Stage has Tasks (= number of partitions)."},{"q":"What is the difference between narrow and wide transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow — each input partition contributes to exactly one output partition; no shuffle. Examples: map, filter, select, withColumn, union, mapPartitions. Data stays local → fast.\n• Wide — data from many input partitions must be reorganized into output partitions via a shuffle. Examples: groupBy/aggregate, join (non-broadcast), distinct, repartition, orderBy, sort.\nRule: wide transformations cause shuffles → new stages → usually the performance bottleneck."},{"q":"What is lazy evaluation in Spark and why is it useful?","type":"concept","a":"Spark does not execute transformations immediately; it records them and builds a logical plan (DAG). Execution happens only when an action is called.\n• Transformations (lazy): filter, select, join, groupBy, withColumn.\n• Actions (trigger execution): count, collect, show, write, save, take.\nBenefits: the Catalyst optimizer can optimize the whole plan (predicate pushdown, column pruning, join reordering), fewer passes over data, and avoids unnecessary computation."},{"q":"On what basis does Spark create stages?","type":"concept","a":"Stages are split at shuffle boundaries.\n• A stage = a sequence of narrow transformations that can be pipelined together without moving data across the network.\n• A new stage begins whenever a wide transformation (shuffle) is needed — e.g., groupBy, sort, non-broadcast join, repartition.\n• DAG Scheduler creates stages; each stage is submitted as a set of tasks (one per partition) to the Task Scheduler.\nMinimizing shuffles (fewer stages) is a key optimization goal."},{"q":"What is the Catalyst optimizer and how does it optimize queries?","type":"concept","a":"Catalyst is Spark's rule-based query optimizer for SQL/DataFrames. Phases:\n1. Analysis — resolves columns/tables and validates schema.\n2. Logical optimization — rule-based: predicate pushdown, constant folding, column pruning, filter reordering, join reorder.\n3. Physical planning — chooses join strategy (broadcast/sort-merge) and operators based on cost/stats.\n4. Code generation — Whole-Stage CodeGen / Tungsten emits optimized JVM bytecode.\nInspect with df.explain(True) or explain('formatted'). Using built-in functions (not UDFs) lets Catalyst optimize them."},{"q":"What is Tungsten in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"Tungsten is Spark's execution engine focused on CPU/memory efficiency:\n• Off-heap binary memory management (reduces GC overhead).\n• Cache-aware computation and explicit memory layout.\n• Whole-Stage Code Generation — fuses operators into a single optimized function, avoiding virtual function calls.\nResult: much faster execution and lower memory usage than the original Spark engine."},{"q":"What is the difference between RDD, DataFrame, and Dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD — low-level, untyped distributed collection; full control, no schema; no Catalyst optimization; slowest but most flexible.\n• DataFrame — distributed table with named columns + schema; runs through Catalyst + Tungsten; best for structured data (Python/Scala/Java/R).\n• Dataset — typed JVM objects (Scala/Java only); compile-time type safety + Catalyst; Python has no Dataset (uses DataFrame).\nPrefer DataFrames/Datasets over RDDs for performance; use RDDs only for fine-grained control."},{"q":"What are the main Databricks cluster types?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose (interactive) — development, notebooks, ad-hoc; shared; billed while running.\n• Job (automated) — created for a job, terminates on completion; cheapest for production pipelines.\n• High Concurrency — many concurrent users/BI; user isolation; SQL/Python/R (not Scala).\n• Single Node — driver only, no workers; dev/testing/small data.\n• SQL Warehouse — optimized for Databricks SQL/BI (Power BI, Tableau); serverless/pro/classic.\n• Serverless — fully managed compute, no cluster config.\nBest practice: Job clusters for production, All-Purpose for dev, SQL Warehouse for BI."},{"q":"Difference between All-Purpose Cluster and Job Cluster?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose: manually started, stays up (auto-terminate 30–120 min), shared by multiple users/notebooks, warm, higher cost. For interactive development.\n• Job: created automatically from job config, fresh environment each run, isolated, terminated after completion (no idle cost). For scheduled/production jobs.\nUse Job clusters for all production pipelines to control cost and ensure clean, isolated runs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"scenario","a":"• No distributed processing — one machine is both driver and worker.\n• Driver bottleneck — it coordinates AND executes, so it's overloaded.\n• Memory pressure — large datasets/joins can exceed node memory (OOM/spill).\n• CPU saturation — all tasks compete for the same 16 cores; long runtimes.\n• No fault tolerance — if the node fails, the whole workload fails.\nSuitable only for dev/testing/small data — not for production ETL."},{"q":"What is the Databricks Runtime (DBR)?","type":"concept","a":"DBR is Databricks' optimized, curated software stack for clusters. It bundles:\n• Apache Spark (specific version), Delta Lake, and performance optimizations (Photon).\n• Language runtimes: Python, Scala, Java, R, SQL.\n• Optimized libraries and security patches.\nVariants: Standard, ML, GPU, Photon, and LTS (Long-Term Support). Prefer LTS in production for stability/longer support."},{"q":"Difference between repartition and coalesce?","type":"concept","a":"• repartition(n) — full shuffle; can increase OR decrease partitions; even distribution; expensive. Use to fix skew or before heavy joins/aggregations.\n• coalesce(n) — reduces partitions WITHOUT a full shuffle (merges local partitions); cheap; used after filtering to reduce small files. Cannot increase partitions.\n• repartition(n, col) — hash-partition by column to co-locate join keys.\nExample: df.repartition(200, 'customer_id'); df.coalesce(4)."},{"q":"What is the default partition size and default number of shuffle partitions?","type":"concept","a":"• Target partition/file size in Spark/Delta — ~128 MB (block/file size).\n• Default shuffle partitions — spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200.\n• Default parallelism — equals the number of executor cores.\nTune shuffle partitions to data size (too few → huge tasks/OOM; too many → tiny tasks/overhead). AQE can coalesce partitions automatically at runtime."},{"q":"Difference between cache() and persist()?","type":"concept","a":"• cache() — stores the DataFrame/RDD using MEMORY_AND_DISK storage level; lazy (materializes on first action).\n• persist(level) — same but you choose the storage level: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK_SER, etc.\nBoth are lazy transformations; trigger with an action (count/write). Use when a DataFrame is reused 2+ times (iterative ML, repeated joins).\nAlways unpersist() when done to free memory."},{"q":"What is a broadcast join and when is it used?","type":"concept","a":"A broadcast join replicates a SMALL table to every executor so the large table doesn't need to be shuffled.\n• Use when one side is small (< ~10 MB default autoBroadcastJoinThreshold, up to ~200 MB feasible).\n• Eliminates the shuffle of the big table → big performance gain and also mitigates skew.\nPySpark: large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id'); SQL: /*+ BROADCAST(small) */.\nTypical for joining a fact with a small lookup/dimension table."},{"q":"What is data skew and how do you detect and fix it?","type":"concept","a":"Skew = uneven data distribution across partitions so a few tasks process far more data (stragglers).\nDetect: Spark UI shows one/few tasks much longer, uneven shuffle read sizes, one executor overloaded while others idle.\nFix:\n• Salting — add a random prefix/suffix to the skewed key to spread it.\n• Enable AQE skew join (spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true) — splits skewed partitions automatically.\n• Broadcast join for the small side.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key; filter early.\n• Isolate and process the hot key separately.","code":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\nspark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true')\n# Salting\ndf = df.withColumn('salt', (F.rand()*10).cast('int'))\ndf = df.withColumn('salted_key', F.concat(col('key'), F.lit('_'), col('salt')))"},{"q":"What is bucketing and when would you use it?","type":"concept","a":"Bucketing pre-shuffles data into a fixed number of buckets by hash of a column, persisted with the table (e.g., bucketBy(50,'customer_id').saveAsTable(...)).\n• Benefit: repeated joins/aggregations on the bucketed key avoid shuffles (sort-merge join becomes shuffle-free).\n• Differs from partitioning: partitioning creates directories (low cardinality); bucketing creates fixed files (higher cardinality, even distribution).\nUse for large, frequently joined tables on the same key. In Databricks, Liquid Clustering is now often preferred over manual bucketing/partitioning."},{"q":"What is a shuffle and how do you reduce it?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffle = redistribution of data across executors (network + disk I/O) for wide transformations like join, groupBy, distinct, sort.\nReduce it by: broadcast joins for small tables, filtering/aggregating before joins, bucketing on join keys, using map-side partial aggregation (reduceByKey over groupByKey), avoiding unnecessary repartition/distinct, and enabling AQE.\nWatch shuffle read/write metrics in the Spark UI — it's usually the top cost."},{"q":"What is Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) and its benefits?","type":"concept","a":"AQE re-optimizes the physical plan at runtime using actual statistics gathered after each stage. Benefits:\n• Dynamically coalesces shuffle partitions (reduces tiny tasks).\n• Switches join strategies (e.g., sort-merge → broadcast) based on actual sizes.\n• Detects and splits skewed partitions (skew join handling).\nEnable: spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true (plus skewJoin + coalescePartitions). Default on in Spark 3.x/Databricks runtime."},{"q":"What is Photon?","type":"concept","a":"Photon is Databricks' vectorized C++ query engine. It replaces the JVM execution for many SQL/DataFrame operators with native, columnar, vectorized execution.\n• Delivers ~3–10x speedups for SQL/scan/aggregation/join-heavy workloads.\n• Drop-in: enable in cluster config; no code changes.\n• Best for SQL and Delta workloads; some UDF-heavy or non-supported ops fall back to the JVM."},{"q":"How do you use the Spark UI to troubleshoot performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Jobs/Stages tabs — find slow stages; check task durations and counts.\n• Tasks — spot skew (one task far longer), spill to disk, retries.\n• SQL/DataFrame tab — inspect the physical plan, exchanges (shuffles), and per-operator metrics.\n• Storage tab — what's cached and its size.\n• Executors tab — memory/GC, shuffle read/write, disk spill.\nUse these to decide: broadcast, repartition, AQE, more partitions, or bigger cluster."},{"q":"How do you decide the size of a Databricks cluster?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster sizing depends on data volume, number of files, transformation complexity, SLA, and concurrency — it can't be predicted, so iterate:\n1. Start with a medium cluster (2–8 nodes) matching the workload class (F/DSv2 for ETL, DSv2 for ML, L for analytics).\n2. Run end-to-end on representative data; measure CPU, memory, I/O, shuffle.\n3. Remove bottlenecks: CPU-bound → add nodes/cores; network-bound → fewer bigger SSD machines; disk/spill-bound → more memory.\n4. Repeat 3–4 times on increasing data sizes; prefer fewer big instances over many small (1 executor/machine) for batch ETL, enable autoscaling.\nFirst guess: scale linearly from a small test (e.g., 5 nodes for 100 TB ≈ 50 nodes for 1 PB), then refine."},{"q":"What are the key cluster configuration options in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster mode: Single Node / Standard / High Concurrency (or Serverless).\n• Runtime version (prefer LTS, e.g., 15.4 LTS).\n• Driver + worker VM types (workers can be sized independently).\n• Worker count with autoscaling (min/max) or fixed.\n• Photon on/off; instance pools for fast start.\n• Auto-termination (idle timeout).\n• Access mode / Unity Catalog mode (Single user, Shared, No isolation).\n• Cluster policies, tags, init scripts, and Spark configs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks job that normally finishes quickly is now taking far longer. How do you investigate?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the job run → Spark UI; find the slow stage and inspect task times.\n• Check for data skew (one task much longer), shuffle size, and spill-to-disk in the Tasks/Executors tabs.\n• Check input data volume/growth and partition count; look for small-file explosion.\n• Check cluster health — resource contention (shared cluster), GC, executor loss, autoscaling lag.\n• Compare the current physical plan vs a known-good run; check join strategy changes (broadcast vs sort-merge).\n• Look for recently changed code (new joins/UDFs), source schema changes, or upstream backlog.\n• Remedy: tune partitions, enable AQE/Photon, broadcast small tables, salt skewed keys, scale cluster, OPTIMIZE tables."},{"q":"What is the difference between Spark SQL, DataFrame API, and RDD API?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD API — lowest level, no schema, no Catalyst; maximum control but least optimization.\n• DataFrame API — structured, schema-aware, optimized by Catalyst/Tungsten; expressive transforms.\n• Spark SQL — declarative SQL strings over the same engine (also Catalyst-optimized); easy for analysts and interop.\nAll compile to the same optimized plan; use DataFrames/SQL for day-to-day, RDDs only for niche low-level needs."},{"q":"Why is PySpark used and how does it interface with the JVM?","type":"concept","a":"PySpark is Spark's Python API. Spark is written in Scala and runs on the JVM; PySpark uses Py4J to let Python code call JVM objects.\n• Benefits: Python ecosystem (pandas, ML libs), ease of use, distributed processing across a cluster.\n• Note: Python UDFs incur serialization overhead (row-by-row), so prefer Spark built-in functions / pandas UDFs (vectorized) for performance.\n• Needs Java installed alongside Python and Spark for local development."},{"q":"What is SCD Type 1? How will you implement it?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 1 overwrites exis(cid:415)ng records and does not preserve history\n• Example: Old 101 John Chennai New 101 John Bangalore Result 101 John Bangalore History is lost\n• PySpark MERGE Example Requirement: If Selling_Period is NULL or 0, do not insert or update. from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"customer\") ( target.alias(\"t\") .merge( source.alias(\"s\"), \"t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID\" ) .whenMatchedUpdate( condi(cid:415)on=\"s.Selling_Period IS NOT NULL AND s.Selling_Period <> 0\", set={ \"CustomerName\":\"s.CustomerName\", \"City\":\"s.City\", \"Selling_Period\":\"s.Selling_Period\" } ) .whenNotMatchedInsert( condi(cid:415)on=\"s.Selling_Period IS NOT NULL AND s.Selling_Period <> 0\", values={ \"CustomerID\":\"s.CustomerID\", \"CustomerName\":\"s.CustomerName\", \"City\":\"s.City\", \"Selling_Period\":\"s.Selling_Period\" } ) .execute() This sa(cid:415)sfies the condi(cid:415)on that records with Selling_Period equal to NULL or 0 are ignored for both updates and inserts","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Small Table? How does Auto Loader know which files are already processed?","type":"concept","a":"• Small Table A small table is a table that is small enough to fit in the memory of each executor\n• Spark uses Broadcast Join for small tables\n• Example:  Customer table = 20 MB  Sales table = 500 GB Spark broadcasts the customer table to all workers instead of shuffling the huge sales table. from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast sales.join(broadcast(customers), \"customer_id\") How Auto Loader tracks processed files Auto Loader maintains a checkpoint\n• The checkpoint stores metadata about:  Files already processed  Offsets  Schema informa(cid:415)on  Progress When new files arrive:","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning? How does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning organizes data into separate folders based on one or more columns\n• Example: Sales Country=USA Country=India Country=Canada df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"Country\").saveAsTable(\"sales\") Benefits Suppose the table has 1 TB of data\n• Query: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE Country='India';\n• Without par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning:  Spark scans the en(cid:415)re 1 TB\n• With par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning:  Spark scans only the Country=India par(cid:415)(cid:415)on, greatly reducing I/O and improving performance\n• Choose par(cid:415)(cid:415)on columns that:  Are frequently used in filters.  Have moderate cardinality (not too few or too many dis(cid:415)nct values)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Spark","type":"concept","a":"A. Spark it is Inmemory parallel space and it will use ram inmemory for read & write operations","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Map reduce and Spark","type":"concept","a":"• S.No\n• Map Reduce Spark","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is having a very slow speed as It is much faster than Map Reduce.","type":"concept","a":"compared to Apache Spark.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It actually needs other queries to It has Spark SQL as its very own query","type":"concept","a":"perform the task. language.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the architecture of spark","type":"concept","a":"➢ Driver Node ➢ Cluster Manager ➢ Worker Node: In This, there is a Executor and Task","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what are different languages supported in Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Python or Pyspark ➢ Scala ➢ Sql ➢ R-Language 9.What is DAG A\n• A directed acyclic graph (DAG) is a conceptual representation of a series of activities\n• The order of the activities is depicted by a graph, which is visually presented as a set of circles, each one representing an activity, some of which are connected by lines, which represent the flow from one activity to another","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Languages using in your project","type":"concept","a":"A. Pyspark and Scala","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is RDD, Data frame, Data Set","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ RDD: A Resilient Distributed Dataset (RDD), the basic abstraction in Spark\n• Represents an immutable, partitioned collection of elements that can be operated on in parallel. ➢ Data Frame: A Data Frame is equivalent to a relational table in Spark SQL, and can be created using various functions ➢ Dataset: A Dataset is a distributed collection of data\n• Dataset is a new interface added in Spark 1.6 that provides the benefits of RDDs","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to convert RDD into Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"• Convert Spark RDD to Data Frame and Dataset as these provide more advantages over RDD\n• For instance, Data Frame is a distributed collection of data organized into named columns similar to Database tables and provides optimization and performance improvement\n• Syntax: val dfFromRDD1 = rdd.toDF() dfFromRDD1.printSchema()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Transformation and Action","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Transformation: Transformation refers to the operation applied on a RDD to create new RDD\n• Filter, groupBy and map are the examples of transformations. ➢ Actions: Actions refer to an operation which also applies on RDD, that instructs Spark to perform computation and send the result back to driver\n• Page | 13","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to create Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"A. # Create DataFrame. df = pd.DataFrame(data) # Print the output. print(df)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to read Data from csv file to create data frame","type":"concept","a":"A. usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to remove duplicate values from Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. dropdf = df.dropDuplicates([\"department\",\"salary\"]) print(\"Distinct count of department & salary :\"+ str(dropdf.count()))","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to add new column into Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to write sql commands on Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. %sql select * from user","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to select only few columns from dataframe","type":"concept","a":"• In PySpark we can select columns using the select() function\n• The select() function allows us to select single or multiple columns in different formats\n• Syntax: df.select(\"firstname\",\"lastname\").show() df.select(df.firstname,df.lastname).show() df.select(df[\"firstname\"],df[\"lastname\"]).show() #By using col() function from pyspark.sql.functions import col df.select(col(\"firstname\"),col(\"lastname\")).show() #Select columns by regular expression df.select(df.colRegex(\"`^.*name*`\")).show()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to drop columns from dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. \"\" import col is required \"\"\" df.drop(col(\"firstname\")) \\ .printSchema() Page | 14","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is diff between repartition and coalesce","type":"concept","a":"A. The repartition() the number of partitions can be increased/decreased, but with coalesce() the number of partitions can only be decreased and The repartition algorithm does a full shuffle of the data and creates equal sized partitions of data. coalesce combines existing partitions to avoid a full shuffle.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is caching and persistence","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ A\n• Caching or persistence are optimization techniques for (iterative and interactive) Spark computations\n• They help saving interim partial results so they can be reused in subsequent stages\n• These interim results as RDDs are thus kept in memory (default) or more solid storage like disk and/or replicated. ➢ These functions can be used to adjust the storage level of a RDD\n• When freeing up memory, Spark will use the storage level identifier to decide which partitions should be kept\n• The parameter less variants persist() and cache() are just abbreviations","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what are broad cast variables and broadcast joins","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Broadcast variables allow the programmer to keep a read-only variable cached on each machine rather than shipping a copy of it with tasks. ➢ Broadcast join is an important part of Spark SQL's execution engine\n• When used, it performs a join on two relations by first broadcasting the smaller one to all Spark executors, then evaluating the join criteria with each executor's partitions of the other relation","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Task Dura(cid:415)on Imbalance:","type":"concept","a":"➢ Use the Spark UI (h(cid:425)p://<spark-driver>:4040) → Stages tab. ➢ Look for stages where a few tasks take significantly longer than others. ➢ If most tasks finish in seconds while a few take minutes, it strongly indicates data skew.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Skewed Shuffle Par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:","type":"concept","a":"➢ Check for stages involving wide transforma(cid:415)ons like groupByKey(), join(), or reduceByKey(). ➢ Under the \"Tasks\" tab, look at the input size and run (cid:415)me of each task. ➢ Skewed keys will show dispropor(cid:415)onate data sizes for specific par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Logs and Metrics:","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Enable detailed logging (especially for jobs involving joins or aggrega(cid:415)ons). ➢ Use .rdd.getNumPar(cid:415)(cid:415)ons() or .rdd.glom().map(len).collect() to inspect distribu(cid:415)on manually\n• How to Resolve Data Skew in PySpark","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid groupByKey()","type":"concept","a":"➢Prefer reduceByKey() or aggregateByKey() which are more efficient and minimizeshuffles.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Skew Join Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on (in Spark 3+)","type":"concept","a":"➢If enabled, Spark can automa(cid:415)cally handle skew joins using adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on (AQE).","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"read employees.csv (schema same as employees table) into a dataframe df_emp","type":"concept","a":"add a column grade in df_emp, if salary > 10000 then A else B from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import when, col # Read CSV into DataFrame df_emp = spark.read \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"header\", \"true\") \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", \"true\") \\ .csv(\"employees.csv\") # Add grade column df_emp = df_emp.withColumn( \"grade\", when(col(\"salary\") > 10000, \"A\").otherwise(\"B\") )","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are the read modes available in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• When reading data, Spark supports different modes for handling malformed records\n• Mode Descrip(cid:415)on PERMISSIVE (Default) Invalid records are set to null, malformed records are captured\n• DROPMALFORMED Drops malformed records\n• FAILFAST Stops execu(cid:415)on if a malformed record is found\n• Example: df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"mode\", \"PERMISSIVE\").csv(\"/input/data.csv\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How are Jobs, Stages, and Tasks created in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Execu(cid:415)on hierarchy: Applica(cid:415)on │ ▼ Job │ ▼ Stage │ ▼ Task  Applica(cid:415)on: Spark program.  Job: Created when an ac(cid:415)on (show(), count(), collect(), write()) is executed.  Stage: Created based on shuffle boundaries.  Task: Smallest execu(cid:415)on unit, one task per par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Example: df = spark.read.csv(\"sales.csv\") df.filter(\"amount>100\").groupBy(\"city\").sum().show()  show() → 1 Job  groupBy() causes a shuffle → mul(cid:415)ple stages  Each par(cid:415)(cid:415)on becomes a task","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Read CSV, create DataFrame, and write Delta to ADLS","type":"concept","a":"df = spark.read \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True) \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", True) \\ .csv(\"abfss://raw@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/customer.csv\") Write to Delta: df.write \\ .format(\"delta\") \\ .mode(\"overwrite\") \\ .save(\"abfss://silver@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/customer\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Spark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"Driver Program │ Cluster Manager │ Executors │ Tasks Driver  Creates SparkSession  Creates DAG  Schedules jobs  Coordinates executors Cluster Manager  Allocates resources Examples:  Standalone  YARN  Kubernetes Executors  Execute tasks  Store cached data  Return results Worker Nodes Machines where executors run.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Managed and External Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Managed Table External Table Spark manages data and metadata Spark manages only metadata Dropping table deletes data Dropping table keeps data Default storage loca(cid:415)on User specifies loca(cid:415)on Suitable for internal data Suitable for shared/external data Example: Managed: CREATE TABLE sales (...);\n• External: CREATE TABLE sales USING DELTA LOCATION '/mnt/sales';","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Spark Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques","type":"concept","a":"• Common op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques include:  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning – Improve parallel processing.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on vs\n• Coalesce – Increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons efficiently.  Cache/Persist – Reuse frequently accessed DataFrames.  Broadcast Join – Broadcast small tables to avoid shuffles.  Predicate Pushdown – Filter data at the source.  Column Pruning – Read only required columns.  Avoid collect() – Prevent moving large datasets to the driver.  Use Delta Lake – Leverage transac(cid:415)on support and performance op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons.  Op(cid:415)mize file sizes – Avoid too many small files.  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) – Dynamically op(cid:415)mize joins and shuffle par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons at run(cid:415)me.  Op(cid:415)mize Z-Ordering (Delta Lake) – Improve data skipping for frequently filtered columns.  Use appropriate file formats – Prefer Parquet or Delta over CSV for analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Check Spark UI","type":"concept","a":" Iden(cid:415)fy slow stages / skewed tasks","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Check shuffle","type":"concept","a":"•  Reduce wide transforma(cid:415)ons (groupBy, join) 4\n• Use caching df.cache()","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Use AQE op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":" Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain PySpark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"(cid:44934) Components:  Driver o Creates SparkContext o Converts code into DAG  Cluster Manager o Allocates resources (YARN / Databricks)  Executors o Run tasks on par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons (cid:44934) Flow:","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Code → Driver","type":"concept","a":"2. DAG created","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Stages formed","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Tasks sent to executors","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Transformation in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• In Apache Spark (used by Databricks), every transformation is either Narrow or Wide\n• This classification directly affects how fast your pipeline runs\n• Narrow Transformations Definition: Each partition of input produces exactly one partition of output\n• No data movement between nodes\n• Fast and efficient\n• Examples: • filter() / where() — Remove rows based on a condition • select() — Choose specific columns • map() — Apply a function to each row • withColumn() — Add or modify a column • union() — Combine two DataFrames with same schema # Narrow transformation example — no shuffle at all from pyspark.sql.functions import col, upper # filter: each partition processed independently, no movement df_filtered = df.filter(col('sales_amount') > 1000) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 1 # select: columns picked per partition, no shuffle df_small = df_filtered.select('customer_id','region','sales_amount') # withColumn: computed row-by-row within the same partition df_final = df_small.withColumn('region_upper', upper(col('region'))) Wide Transformations Definition: Data from multiple partitions is combined and redistributed across nodes\n• Causes a SHUFFLE — most expensive operation in Spark\n• Creates a new stage boundary in the execution plan\n• Examples: • groupBy() + agg() — Group data across all partitions • join() — Match rows from two separate DataFrames • distinct() — Remove duplicates (needs to see all rows globally) • orderBy() / sort() — Sort across all partitions globally • repartition() — Redistribute into new number of partitions # Wide transformation example — SHUFFLE happens here from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, count # groupBy: Spark must move all rows with same 'region' # to the same partition before aggregating df_summary = df.groupBy('region').agg( sum('sales_amount').alias('total_sales'), count('customer_id').alias('num_customers') ) # join: both DataFrames shuffled to align matching keys df_joined = df_sales.join(df_customers, on='customer_id', how='inner') Side-by-Side Comparison Feature Narrow Transformation Wide Transformation Data Movement No shuffle — stays in same Full shuffle — data moves across partition all nodes Performance Very fast Slower — network + disk I/O overhead Examples filter, select, map, withColumn, groupBy, join, distinct, orderBy, union repartition Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 2 Stage Boundary No — same Spark stage Yes — creates a new stage When to use Column ops, filtering, cleaning Aggregations, joins, deduplication Interview Tip: Say in interview: 'Narrow transformations stay within a single partition — no shuffle\n• Wide transformations require shuffling data across the network and always create a stage boundary\n• We minimize wide transformations and use broadcast joins for small lookup tables to avoid expensive shuffles.' Q3 Coalesce vs Repartition","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Why Do Partitions Matter?","type":"concept","a":"• Spark splits data into partitions and processes them in parallel\n• Too many small partitions = scheduler overhead\n• Too few large partitions = memory pressure and slow tasks\n• Both coalesce and repartition change the partition count, but very differently. coalesce(n) Key Facts: Reduces partitions by merging existing ones together\n• Does NOT trigger a full shuffle — very efficient\n• Can only REDUCE partition count, not increase it\n• May result in slightly uneven partition sizes. # Before: 200 partitions (created from large file read) print(df.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 200 # coalesce: merge down to 10 — no shuffle needed df_coalesced = df.coalesce(10) print(df_coalesced.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 10 # Best use case: reduce output file count before writing df_coalesced.write.mode('overwrite').parquet('/mnt/output/sales/') # Writes 10 parquet files instead of 200 tiny ones # WARNING: coalesce does not rebalance — some partitions # may be larger than others (slight skew is OK here) repartition(n) Key Facts: Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 3 Redistributes data EVENLY across n new partitions\n• Always triggers a full shuffle — more expensive\n• Can both INCREASE and DECREASE partition count\n• Can also partition by a specific column (hash partitioning). # repartition: even redistribution — triggers full shuffle df_rep = df.repartition(50) print(df_rep.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 50 # repartition by column — all rows with same key go to same partition # Great before a join to avoid double-shuffle df_by_region = df.repartition(20, 'region') # Pre-partition both sides of a join on the join key: df_sales_r = df_sales.repartition(50, 'customer_id') df_cust_r = df_customers.repartition(50, 'customer_id') df_joined = df_sales_r.join(df_cust_r, 'customer_id') # Result: join is much faster — data already co-located!","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"When to Use Which?","type":"concept","a":"Scenario Use Reduce partitions before writing output coalesce — no shuffle, fast Increase partitions for more parallelism repartition — only option that can increase Fix data skew (uneven partition sizes) repartition — even redistribution Pre-partition before a join on a key repartition(n, 'key') — co-locate data After heavy filter reduced data volume coalesce — fewer tasks, no shuffle Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'coalesce reduces partitions without a shuffle — I use it before writing output files. repartition does a full shuffle to evenly redistribute data — I use it when I need to increase parallelism, fix data skew, or co-locate data by a join key.' Q4 Schema Enforcement vs Schema Evolution","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Spark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Apache Spark uses a Master-Worker (Driver-Executor) distributed computing model with the following components: Component Role Key Detail Driver Runs user code; builds DAG;\n• Creates SparkContext; schedules coordinates execution tasks Cluster Manager Resource allocation across nodes Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos Executor Runs tasks; caches partitions in JVM processes on worker nodes memory or disk Task Smallest unit of work (one per Sent from Task Scheduler to Executors partition) Stage Set of tasks with no shuffle boundary Created by DAG Scheduler Job Triggered by an action (collect, write, Broken into Stages count) DAG Logical execution plan Built lazily; executed only on action Execution Flow • User calls an action (df.write(), df.count()) • Driver sends job to DAG Scheduler • DAG Scheduler splits into Stages at shuffle boundaries • Task Scheduler sends Tasks to Executors • Executors run tasks, return results to Driver Key concept: Lazy Evaluation — transformations (filter, select, join) build the DAG but do NOT execute\n• Only when an action is called does Spark execute the full optimized plan through the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten engine\n• Page 1 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Join","type":"concept","a":"• Broadcast small tables (under 200 MB) to avoid shuffle in joins result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"AQE — Adaptive Query Execution","type":"concept","a":"• Dynamically re-optimizes query plan at runtime based on actual data stats spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled', 'true')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Photon Engine","type":"concept","a":"• Enable Photon in Databricks cluster config — 3-10x faster SQL and Delta operations 6. Caching • Cache frequently reused DataFrames: df.cache() or df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Filter Pushdown — Apply filters early","type":"concept","a":"# Apply filter BEFORE join — reduces shuffle data df_filtered = df.filter(col('status') == 'ACTIVE') result = df_filtered.join(df_other, 'id')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tune Shuffle Partitions","type":"concept","a":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '200') # default 200; tune to data size","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• Technique How When to Use Broadcast Join broadcast(small_df) One table < 200 MB — eliminates shuffle Predicate Pushdown filter() before join/groupBy Reduce data early before expensive ops Column Pruning select() only needed columns Avoid reading unused columns from Parquet Caching df.cache() or df.persist() DataFrame reused 2+ times in same job Repartition df.repartition(n, col) Before heavy groupBy/join on skewed data Coalesce df.coalesce(n) Reduce partitions after filter (no shuffle) reduceByKey vs groupByKey reduceByKey does partial local Aggregation on RDDs — less shuffle aggregation AQE spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true Runtime query plan re-optimization Avoid UDFs Use built-in Spark functions UDFs bypass Catalyst optimizer Bucketing bucketBy(n, col).saveAsTable() Eliminate shuffle for repeated large joins from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast, col from pyspark.sql import functions as F Page 7 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide # 1\n• Broadcast join result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id') # 2\n• Filter early + column pruning df = df.filter(col('status')=='ACTIVE').select('id','name','amount') # 3\n• Cache for reuse df.cache() count1 = df.count() # triggers cache result = df.groupBy('dept').sum('salary') # 4\n• Coalesce (no shuffle) vs repartition (with shuffle) df_small = df.filter(col('year')==2024).coalesce(4) # after big filter df_even = df.repartition(200, col('customer_id')) # even distribution # 5\n• AQE + Photon config spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '200')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Remove / Handle Nulls Using PySpark","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Drop rows with any null","type":"concept","a":"Page 14 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide df_clean = df.dropna() # drop row if ANY column is null","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"External Tables vs Managed Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Aspect Managed Table External Table Data location Databricks/Spark manages the YOU specify the data location (ADLS/S3) storage location DROP TABLE behavior Deletes metadata AND the data Deletes only metadata — data files files remain Data ownership Owned by Databricks/Spark Owned by external storage (ADLS Gen2) Use case Temporary/intermediate tables;\n• Shared data accessed by multiple tools DW tables Schema evolution Managed by Databricks You manage schema;\n• Spark reads it Performance Slightly faster (local storage Depends on network/storage optimized) performance Typical format Delta (default in Databricks) Parquet, Delta, CSV, ORC, JSON Page 20 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Multi-tool access Databricks only (unless Unity Any tool that can read the storage path Catalog) Create Managed Table -- Managed Table (Databricks controls storage location) CREATE TABLE silver.customers ( customer_id INT, name STRING, email STRING, created_date DATE ); -- Or from DataFrame df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('silver.customers') -- Data stored at: /user/hive/warehouse/silver.db/customers/ Create External Table -- External Table (you specify the ADLS path) CREATE TABLE bronze.raw_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/'; -- Or from DataFrame df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/') -- Register as external table spark.sql(''' CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bronze.raw_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/' ''') -- DROP TABLE only removes catalog entry; files remain in ADLS DROP TABLE bronze.raw_orders; -- data still in ADLS! Critical difference: DROP TABLE on a Managed table permanently deletes your data files\n• Always use External Tables in production for data stored on ADLS — this protects your data from accidental deletion\n• Quick Reference — All 25 Topics # Topic Key Point 1 Spark Architecture Driver + Cluster Manager + Executors + DAG + Stages + Lazy Eval 2 CDC & CDF CDC=source change capture;\n• CDF=Delta row-level change feed (_change_type) 3 Query Optimization Broadcast, Z-ORDER, AQE, Photon, cache, filter early, partition 4 Parquet vs JSON Parquet=columnar+compressed+schema;\n• JSON=text+row+nested; use Parquet for analytics 5 Schedule Notebooks Databricks Jobs (Workflows) OR ADF Notebook Activity OR dbutils.notebook.run() Page 21 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide 6 SCD Types Type1=overwrite;\n• Type2=new row+expire;\n• Type3=add column;\n• Type2 most common 7 SCD SQL Code MERGE INTO for Type1; expire+insert pattern for Type2;\n• UPDATE prev column for Type3 8 PySpark Optimization Broadcast, filter early, cache, coalesce, repartition, avoid UDFs, AQE 9 Databricks Optimization OPTIMIZE+ZORDER, Liquid Cluster, Photon, Delta Cache, AQE, Materialized Views 10 ADLS Security Service Principal + Key Vault secrets + RBAC roles + ACLs + Private Endpoints 11 Load from External abfss:// direct read, Mount, Auto Loader, COPY INTO, External Location 12 Left Anti Join Returns LEFT rows with NO match in RIGHT; how='left_anti' 13 2nd Highest Salary dense_rank() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) WHERE rnk=2 14 Split Col3 into rows split(col, ',') + explode() in PySpark;\n• LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT()) in SQL 15 Notebook in Notebook dbutils.notebook.run() for isolated exec with params; %run to share scope 16 Validation Checks Schema, null, duplicate, type, range, referential integrity, count, business rules 17 Remove Nulls dropna(), fillna(), when().isNull(), forward fill with Window last() 18 Cluster Types All-Purpose(dev), Job(prod), Single Node, SQL Warehouse(BI), DLT(managed) 19 Store after Transform Delta tables in Bronze/Silver/Gold layers on ADLS partitioned by date 20 Load Data into ADB spark.read.jdbc(), spark.read.csv(), Auto Loader (cloudFiles), COPY INTO 21 ADLS Gen2 vs Blob Gen2=HNS+POSIX ACL+analytics optimized;","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Table Has 3 Columns But Client Sends Only 1","type":"concept","a":"When the source data has fewer columns than the target table, there are several approaches: Approach Description Insert with NULLs for Insert the available column; let missing columns default to NULL (if missing columns nullable) Provide DEFAULT values Define DEFAULT constraints on the missing columns in the table schema Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects mismatched schemas by default — schema Delta evolution must be enabled Transform before writing Add the missing columns with NULL or derived values in the DataFrame before writing Communicate with client Raise a data quality issue and request the complete dataset as per the agreed contract PySpark Example — Add Missing Columns from pyspark.sql.functions import lit # Client sent only 'emp_id'; table expects emp_id, emp_name, salary df = df.withColumn('emp_name', lit(None).cast('string')) \\ .withColumn('salary', lit(None).cast('integer'))","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark – Salary Classification","type":"concept","a":"Input Data 1, 'AAA', 500, 'HYD' 2, 'BBB', 100, 'CHI' 3, NULL, 1000, 'BANG' 4, 'DDD', NULL, 'KOL' 5, 'EEE', 2500, NULL PySpark Code from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, IntegerType, StringType spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SalaryClassification').getOrCreate() data = [ (1, 'AAA', 500, 'HYD'), (2, 'BBB', 100, 'CHI'), (3, None, 1000, 'BANG'), (4, 'DDD', None, 'KOL'), (5, 'EEE', 2500, None) ] schema = StructType([ StructField('emp_id', IntegerType(), True), StructField('emp_name', StringType(), True), StructField('salary', IntegerType(), True), StructField('location', StringType(), True) ]) df = spark.createDataFrame(data, schema) df = df.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary').isNull(), 'Unknown') .when(col('salary') < 500, 'Low') .when((col('salary') >= 1000) & Page 8 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A (col('salary') < 1500), 'Mid') .when(col('salary') >= 1500, 'High') .otherwise('No Grade') ) df.show() Expected Output emp_id emp_name salary location salary_grade 1 AAA 500 HYD No Grade (500 is not < 500 nor >= 1000) 2 BBB 100 CHI Low 3 NULL 1000 BANG Mid 4 DDD NULL KOL Unknown 5 EEE 2500 NULL High","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Why a Particular Job Takes Long – Causes & Solutions","type":"concept","a":"• Common Root Causes of Slow Jobs Issue Cause Impact Data Skew One partition has far more Most executors idle while one overloaded data than others executor runs for hours Shuffle Operations Wide transformations Network I/O and disk spill dominate job time (groupBy, join, distinct) cause expensive data shuffles Too Many Small Files Thousands of tiny Parquet Driver and executors spend excessive time on files instead of a few large file metadata lookups ones Insufficient Cluster Not enough cores/memory Tasks queue up;\n• GC pressure;\n• OOM errors Resources for the data volume Missing Partitioning Full table scan on every Reads entire dataset instead of pruning query irrelevant partitions Cartesian / Broadcast Large table joined without Generates massive intermediate datasets Join Issues broadcast hint or wrong join strategy UDF (Python) Usage Row-by-row Python UDFs Dramatically slower than native Spark SQL bypass Spark functions optimizations No Caching Recomputing the same Redundant reads and transformations repeated DataFrame multiple times for each action in a DAG Solutions & Optimizations Problem Solution Code / Config Data Skew Salting technique — df.withColumn('salt_key', (rand()*10).cast('int')) add random key prefix to distribute skewed key Data Skew Skew hint in Spark spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true') 3.x AQE Shuffle Enable Adaptive spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true') Query Execution (AQE) Shuffle Use broadcast join df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id') for small tables Small Files Run OPTIMIZE on OPTIMIZE tablename ZORDER BY (col) Delta tables Page 8 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Small Files Coalesce or df.coalesce(10).write.format('delta').save(path) repartition before write Missing Partition Partition table on filter df.write.partitionBy('year','month').save(path) Pruning column Python UDFs Replace with Spark Use F.col(), F.when(), Pandas UDF with @pandas_udf SQL / Pandas UDF No Caching Cache reused df.cache() or DataFrames df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Cluster Sizing Right-size cluster or Set min/max workers; use photon runtime for SQL-heavy enable autoscaling jobs Diagnostic Tools in Databricks • Spark UI -> Stages tab: identify slow stages and skew via task duration distribution • Ganglia Metrics: CPU, memory, network I/O per executor • Event Timeline: spot executor idle time • EXPLAIN plan: check query plan for missing partition pruning or cartesian joins Page 9 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"Partitioning and Caching – Concepts & Advantages","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partitioning is the process of dividing a large dataset into smaller, manageable chunks (partitions) based on the values of one or more columns\n• This allows Spark to skip reading irrelevant partitions during queries — a technique called partition pruning\n• Two Types of Partitioning Type Description When to Use Storage Partitioning Data physically written into folder When tables are large and frequently (Write) hierarchy by column value (e.g., filtered by specific columns (date, region, /year=2024/month=01/) dept) Spark Partitioning (In- Number of in-memory partitions Control parallelism; repartition before memory) that Spark distributes across wide transformations or writes executors Storage Partitioning – Code # Write partitioned Delta table df.write \\ .format('delta') \\ .partitionBy('year', 'month') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/sales/') # Query benefits from partition pruning df_filtered = spark.read.format('delta').load('/mnt/delta/sales/') \\ .filter('year = 2024 AND month = 6') # Spark reads ONLY year=2024/month=6 folder -- skips all others Spark In-Memory Partitioning – Code # Repartition -- full shuffle, use for even distribution df = df.repartition(200, 'department_id') # Coalesce -- no shuffle, only reduces partitions (for writing fewer files) df = df.coalesce(10) # Check current number of partitions print(df.rdd.getNumPartitions()) Advantages of Partitioning Advantage Explanation Partition Pruning Spark skips irrelevant partitions entirely — massive read reduction Page 14 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Parallel Processing Each partition processed by a separate task — full cluster parallelism Faster Queries WHERE/filter on partition column can reduce scan from TBs to MBs Efficient Writes Large data written in parallel across many executors Better Maintenance VACUUM, OPTIMIZE, and MERGE can target specific partitions Caching Caching (persisting) stores a DataFrame or RDD in memory (and optionally disk) so that it does not need to be recomputed from scratch each time it is used in subsequent actions within the same Spark session\n• Cache vs Persist Method Storage Level Description df.cache() MEMORY_AND_DISK Stores in memory; spills to disk if (default) memory runs out df.persist() Configurable Allows specifying exact storage level StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY RAM only Fastest; data lost if evicted (recomputed) StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK RAM + Disk Balanced — no recomputation on eviction StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY Disk only Useful for DataFrames too large for RAM StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY_SER Serialized RAM Less memory; slight CPU overhead to deserialize Caching – Code Example from pyspark import StorageLevel # Cache a frequently reused DataFrame df_clean = df.filter(df.salary > 0).dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) df_clean.cache() # or df_clean.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) # Trigger caching (lazy evaluation -- first action materialises the cache) df_clean.count() # Reuse -- reads from cache, not original source dept_summary = df_clean.groupBy('department').agg(F.avg('salary')) top_earners = df_clean.filter(df_clean.salary > 100000) # Always unpersist when done to free memory df_clean.unpersist() Page 15 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Advantages of Caching Advantage Explanation Eliminates Recomputation DataFrame computed once; reused many times without re-reading source Faster Iterative Algorithms ML training loops (10–100 iterations) benefit enormously from caching input data Reduces I/O Avoids repeated reads from slow storage (ADLS, S3) for intermediate results Speeds Up Interactive Notebook queries on the same DataFrame return results instantly after Analysis first cache Pipeline Optimization Cache after expensive joins/aggregations shared across multiple downstream steps Page 16 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is Compute in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Compute in Databricks refers to the cluster of virtual machines (VMs) used to run Spark jobs, notebooks, and SQL queries\n• It is the processing engine behind all workloads\n• Types of Compute in Databricks: • All-Purpose Clusters: Long-running, interactive clusters for notebooks and development\n• Can be shared among multiple users. • Job Clusters: Ephemeral clusters created for a specific job run and terminated automatically after completion — cost-efficient for production pipelines. • SQL Warehouses (formerly SQL Endpoints): Optimized for running SQL queries via Databricks SQL\n• Uses Photon engine for faster performance. • Instance Pools: Pre-warmed VMs to reduce cluster start time\n• Cluster Configuration Includes: • Driver node: Orchestrates the Spark job, maintains SparkContext. • Worker nodes: Execute the actual data processing tasks. • Autoscaling: Automatically adds/removes workers based on workload","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Cache and Persisting?","type":"concept","a":"Both cache() and persist() are used to store intermediate Spark DataFrames/RDDs in memory to avoid recomputation. cache(): • Shortcut for persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK). • Stores data in memory; if memory is insufficient, spills to disk. • Syntax: df.cache() persist(): • Provides fine-grained control over storage level. • Storage levels: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_ONLY_SER, OFF_HEAP, etc. • Syntax: df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) When to Use: • Use when a DataFrame is used multiple times in the same job — avoids re-reading from disk/source. • Always unpersist() after use to free memory: df.unpersist()","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Your Spark job is running slow. What will you check to identify the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• Step-by-step Diagnosis: • Check Spark UI: Review the Jobs, Stages, and Tasks tabs\n• Look for long-running stages, failed tasks, and skewed partitions. • Data Skew: Check if one partition has significantly more data than others (one task takes 10x longer)\n• Fix with salting or repartitioning. • Shuffles: Wide transformations like groupBy, join, and distinct trigger expensive shuffles\n• Check shuffle read/write sizes in Spark UI. • Number of Partitions: Too few = underutilized executors\n• Too many = overhead of task scheduling\n• Ideal: 2-4x the number of CPU cores. • Spill to Disk: Memory spill means executors don't have enough memory\n• Increase executor memory or reduce partition size. • Broadcast Joins: If joining a large table with a small table, ensure the small table is broadcast to avoid shuffle. • Data Format & Compression: Are you reading CSV instead of Parquet? CSV lacks predicate pushdown\n• Switch to Parquet/Delta. • Predicate Pushdown & Partition Pruning: Ensure filters are applied early and partition columns are used in WHERE clauses. • GC Overhead: High Garbage Collection time in executor logs indicates memory pressure. • Driver Bottleneck: If Driver is doing too much work (e.g., collect() on large data), it becomes a bottleneck","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Write PySpark code to get the above output using Window function?","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, max as spark_max from pyspark.sql.window import Window spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SalesMax').getOrCreate() # Sample Data data = [('cust1', '2023-12-01', 100), ('cust2', '2023-12-02', 150), ('cust1', '2023-12-03', 200), ('cust2', '2023-12-04', 250)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['customer_id', 'transaction_date', 'sales']) # Define Window partitioned by customer_id window_spec = Window.partitionBy('customer_id') # Apply max() over the window df_with_max = df.withColumn('sales_max', spark_max('sales').over(window_spec)) # Drop duplicates to get one row per customer result = df_with_max.select('customer_id', 'sales_max').distinct() result.show() # Alternative: Using groupBy (simpler if window not required) result_alt = df.groupBy('customer_id').agg(spark_max('sales').alias('sales_max')) result_alt.show()","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Date type using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_date, to_timestamp # Method 1: to_date() with format pattern df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_date(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd')) # Method 2: to_timestamp() if time component exists df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_timestamp(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss')) # Method 3: Using cast() df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', col('transaction_date').cast('date')) # Verify schema df.printSchema() df.show() Note: Always match the format string to the actual string format in your data\n• Mismatched format returns null values","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"customer_ids that are NOT in Sales table using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• This is a classic anti-join / left anti join scenario. from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('AntiJoin').getOrCreate() # Sample Data customers_data = [('cust1',), ('cust2',), ('cust3',), ('cust4',)] sales_data = [('cust1', '2023-12-01', 100), ('cust2', '2023-12-02', 150)] customers_df = spark.createDataFrame(customers_data, ['customer_id']) sales_df = spark.createDataFrame(sales_data, ['customer_id', 'date', 'sales']) # ---- Method 1: Left Anti Join (Most Efficient) ---- result = customers_df.join(sales_df, on='customer_id', how='left_anti') result.show() # Output: cust3, cust4 # ---- Method 2: Left Join + Filter Nulls ---- result2 = (customers_df .join(sales_df, on='customer_id', how='left') .filter(sales_df['customer_id'].isNull()) .select(customers_df['customer_id'])) result2.show() # ---- Method 3: Using SQL ---- customers_df.createOrReplaceTempView('customers') sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') result3 = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM sales) ''') result3.show() # ---- Method 4: subtract() on RDD level ---- result4 = customers_df.select('customer_id').subtract( sales_df.select('customer_id')) result4.show() Best Practice: Use Method 1 (left_anti join) — it is the most efficient and idiomatic PySpark approach\n• It avoids shuffling unnecessary data and clearly expresses the intent\n• End of Interview Q&A Document","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is shuffling. Why it is expensive?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffling & why expensive  Data movement between executors Expensive due to:  Network transfer  Disk I/O  Serializa(cid:415)on overhead","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Consider you are having 1TB and 10MB file. How will spark execute?","type":"concept","a":"1TB + 10MB file execu(cid:415)on  Spark splits 1TB into par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons  10MB file may cause small-file problem  Uneven task distribu(cid:415)on → performance issues","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Databricks Run(cid:415)me?","type":"concept","a":"Databricks Run(cid:415)me  Op(cid:415)mized Spark environment  Includes Spark + Delta Lake + ML libraries","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is UDF in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"UDF in Databricks  User Defined Func(cid:415)on for custom logic (cid:57523)(cid:57524)(cid:57525) slower than built-in Spark func(cid:415)ons","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"24. What is AQE?","type":"concept","a":"AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on)  Run(cid:415)me op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on in Spark  Improves joins, par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, skew handling","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Lazy Evalua(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Spark does not execute transforma(cid:415)ons immediately\n• Execu(cid:415)on starts only when an ac(cid:415)on (such as count() or show()) is called","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cache vs Persist","type":"concept","a":"• Cache df.cache() Persist df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Use persist() when you need a storage level other than the default\n• Spark Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques Interviewers o(cid:332)en ask you to explain at least 8–10 techniques\n• Cache df.cache() Persist df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Broadcast Join from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2),\"id\") Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(10) Coalesce df.coalesce(2) Filter Early df.filter(df.salary>50000) Select Required Columns df.select(\"id\",\"name\") Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Data df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"country\") Bucke(cid:415)ng df.write.bucketBy(10,\"id\") AQE spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.adap(cid:415)ve.enabled\",\"true\") Explain Plan df.explain(True) Join Strategies in Spark","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Hash Join","type":"concept","a":"Used when one table is small. large.join(broadcast(small),\"id\") No shuffle for the small table.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Sort Merge Join","type":"concept","a":"• Default join for large tables\n• Shuffle ↓ Sort ↓ Merge","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Shuffle Hash Join","type":"concept","a":" Shuffle both tables.  Build a hash table for the smaller par(cid:415)(cid:415)on.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Shuffle-and-Replicate Nested Loop Join","type":"concept","a":"Used for complex join condi(cid:415)ons when Spark cannot use a more efficient join strategy.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you handle data skew and optimize Spark transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Common techniques: Identify skewed keys  Analyze the data distribution using groupBy() and count().  Review the Spark UI to identify tasks that take significantly longer than others\n• Use Broadcast Joins  If one table is small (lookup or dimension table), broadcast it to all executors to avoid expensive shuffles. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast result = largeDF.join( broadcast(smallDF), \"CustomerID\", \"inner\") Repartition Data  Redistribute data evenly before joins or aggregations. df = df.repartition(200, \"CustomerID\") Apply Salting  For heavily skewed keys, add a random salt to spread records across multiple partitions, then remove the salt after processing\n• Enable Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)  AQE automatically detects skewed partitions and optimizes shuffle execution at runtime\n• Filter Early  Remove unnecessary rows before joins to reduce the amount of data shuffled\n• Partition Data Properly  Store large datasets partitioned by frequently filtered columns, such as date or region, to minimize data scanning\n• Example answer: In one project, we noticed a join between transaction data and customer data was taking about 45 minutes\n• Spark UI showed that one partition contained most of the records because a few customer IDs had a very high number of transactions\n• We solved the issue by broadcasting the small customer table, repartitioning the transaction data, and enabling Adaptive Query Execution\n• The job execution time reduced to around 12 minutes, and cluster resource utilization became much more balanced","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Insert new version","type":"concept","a":"CurrentFlag='Y' StartDate=current_date() EndDate=NULL Columns typically used CustomerID Address StartDate EndDate CurrentFlag Pyspark Code: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, \"/target\") deltaTable.alias(\"t\").merge( sourceDF.alias(\"s\"), \"t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID AND t.CurrentFlag = 'Y'\" ).whenMatchedUpdate(condition=\"t.City <> s.City\", set={ \"CurrentFlag\": \"'N'\", \"EndDate\": \"current_date()\" } ).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ \"CustomerID\": \"s.CustomerID\", \"Name\": \"s.Name\", \"City\": \"s.City\", \"StartDate\": \"current_date()\", \"EndDate\": \"NULL\", \"CurrentFlag\": \"'Y'\" }).execute()","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"What are the most common performance bottlenecks you’ve faced in Spark and how","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"did you fix them?","type":"concept","a":"• Mention multiple points\n• Small Files Solution coalesce() repartition() Data Skew Solution Broadcast joins AQE Salting Shuffle Reduce unnecessary joins Filter early Partition correctly Expensive UDFs Use built-in Spark functions instead Repeated Computation Use cache() persist() Managerial Round","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you communicate technical issues to non-technical managers?","type":"concept","a":"• Instead of saying \"Spark executor memory exceeded\" Say The data processing job ran out of resources due to a significant increase in data volume\n• We increased processing capacity and optimized the job to ensure future runs complete within the expected time","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you mentor junior team members or help upskill the team?","type":"concept","a":"Mention  Code reviews  Pair programming  Documentation  SQL optimization sessions  Spark best practices  Knowledge-sharing meetings","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you resolve the data skew issue in Databricks / Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Data skew occurs when certain partition keys have far more data than others, causing some tasks to take much longer. • Salting: Add a random prefix (0-N) to the skewed key; join on the salted key, then aggregate results back. • Broadcast Join: If one side is small (<= 10 MB or tuned threshold), use broadcast to avoid shuffle altogether. • AQE (Adaptive Query Execution): Enable spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true — Spark automatically coalesces skewed partitions at runtime. • Skew Join Optimization (AQE): spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true splits skewed partitions into smaller ones. • Repartition: Use repartition(n, col) to manually redistribute data more evenly. • Increase spark.sql.shuffle.partitions from default 200 to a higher value for large datasets\n• DevOps Pipeline – Code Promotion & Debugging","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the cluster configura(cid:415)on you have done in your recent project","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster Configura(cid:415)on (Databricks) You can say:  We used Databricks Job Clusters in produc(cid:415)on  Cluster type: autoscaling enabled  Run(cid:415)me: DBR (Databricks Run(cid:415)me latest stable)  Mode: Standard cluster Example answer: “We used job clusters with autoscaling enabled between 2–8 workers depending on workload\n• We selected standard run(cid:415)me with op(cid:415)mized Spark configura(cid:415)ons for performance.”","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Check stage execu(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Are you familiar with cluster configura(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Typical configura(cid:415)on includes: Cluster Mode  Single Node  Standard  High Concurrency (legacy;\n• Unity Catalog environments now commonly use Standard or Serverless depending on the workload) Worker Type Example: Standard_D8s_v5 Driver Type Can be same or larger than workers\n• Worker Count Min = 2 Max = 8 Autoscaling enabled\n• Run(cid:415)me Version Example: 15.4 LTS Photon Enabled for SQL-heavy workloads\n• Auto Termina(cid:415)on 30 Minutes Unity Catalog Access Mode  Single User  Shared Interview Answer \"When configuring clusters, I select the run(cid:415)me version, VM size, worker count or autoscaling, access mode, auto termina(cid:415)on, Photon, and instance availability\n• In produc(cid:415)on we typically use autoscaling Job Clusters with LTS run(cid:415)mes.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"concept","a":"• A single-node cluster has one machine ac(cid:415)ng as both driver and worker\n• Possible issues: No Distributed Compu(cid:415)ng Spark cannot distribute work across mul(cid:415)ple machines\n• Driver Bo(cid:425)leneck Driver performs both coordina(cid:415)on and execu(cid:415)on\n• Memory Pressure Large datasets may exceed the memory available on the single node\n• CPU Satura(cid:415)on All tasks compete for the same 16 cores\n• Long Execu(cid:415)on Time Large joins and aggrega(cid:415)ons take longer because no addi(cid:415)onal workers are available\n• Fault Tolerance If the single node fails, the en(cid:415)re workload fails\n• Suitable For  Development  Tes(cid:415)ng  Small datasets  Learning Not recommended for produc(cid:415)on ETL workloads","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"For Dev and Test workspaces, should we create one metastore or different metastores?","type":"concept","a":"• Typical recommenda(cid:415)on: One metastore per region, shared across mul(cid:415)ple workspaces (Dev, Test, Prod), while keeping environments isolated using catalogs, schemas, and permissions\n• Example: Metastore ↓ Dev Workspace ↓ dev_catalog ------------------- Test Workspace ↓ test_catalog ------------------- Prod Workspace ↓ prod_catalog Different metastores are generally used when there are strong isola(cid:415)on requirements, such as:  Different cloud regions  Different legal en(cid:415)(cid:415)es or business units  Strict compliance or regulatory boundaries  Separate organiza(cid:415)ons \"In most enterprise implementa(cid:415)ons, we use a single metastore per region and a(cid:425)ach mul(cid:415)ple workspaces to it\n• Environment isola(cid:415)on is achieved using separate catalogs, schemas, and access controls\n• Separate metastores are typically reserved for cases where stronger isola(cid:415)on is required, such as different regions or compliance boundaries.\" Quick Revision (Most Important Points) Ques(cid:415)on Key Answer Compute Types All-Purpose, Job, SQL Warehouse, Serverless, Model Serving Job = produc(cid:415)on & auto-terminate;\n• All-Purpose = interac(cid:415)ve Job vs All-Purpose development Databricks Run(cid:415)me Op(cid:415)mized Spark + Delta Lake + Photon + language run(cid:415)mes Cluster Config Run(cid:415)me, VM size, autoscaling, access mode, Photon, auto-termina(cid:415)on No distribu(cid:415)on, driver bo(cid:425)leneck, limited scalability, memory/CPU Single Node Issues constraints Data Lake vs Delta Delta adds ACID, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, transac(cid:415)on log Deleted Managed Cannot recover without backups or source data once files/logs are Table permanently removed Managed vs External Managed = Databricks-managed storage;\n• External = customer-managed Volume cloud storage Unity Catalog Metastore → Catalog → Schema → Tables/Views/Volumes Usually one metastore per region with separate catalogs and Dev/Test Metastore permissions for each environment","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Read a text file with custom delimiter","type":"concept","a":"Input file id~!name~!=age 1~!Viswa~!=25 Desired Output id name age 1 Viswa 25 Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import split df = spark.read.text(\"input.txt\") result = df.select( split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(0).alias(\"id\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(1).alias(\"name\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(2).alias(\"age\") ) result.show() If the file has a header row that should be skipped: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import monotonically_increasing_id df = spark.read.text(\"input.txt\") data = ( df.withColumn(\"rn\", monotonically_increasing_id()) .filter(\"rn > 0\") ) result = data.select( split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(0).alias(\"id\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(1).alias(\"name\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(2).alias(\"age\") ) result.show()","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Q3. What is DAG?","type":"concept","a":"• DAG stands for Directed Acyclic Graph\n• In Apache Spark, a DAG represents the logical execution plan — a sequence of stages and tasks derived from transformations on RDDs or DataFrames\n• Three Key Properties • Directed — data flows in one direction (parent to child node) • Acyclic — no circular dependencies; no node can be its own ancestor • Graph — nodes represent RDDs/DataFrames; edges represent transformations How Spark Uses DAG • An action (collect, count, write) triggers job submission to the DAG Scheduler • DAG Scheduler divides the DAG into Stages based on shuffle boundaries • Each Stage splits into Tasks — one task per partition • Task Scheduler assigns tasks to available executors Lazy Evaluation: Transformations are NOT executed immediately\n• They build the DAG plan\n• Execution only happens when an action is triggered, allowing Spark to optimize the full plan first\n• Interview Tip: Mention the DAG can be viewed in Spark UI under the SQL/Jobs tab — useful for debugging performance and identifying shuffle stages","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow vs Wide Transformations","type":"concept","a":"Aspect Narrow Transformation Wide Transformation Shuffle No shuffle Shuffle occurs Performance Fast Slower (network I/O) Stage Boundary Same stage New stage is created Examples map, filter, select, union, flatMap groupBy, join, distinct, repartition, orderBy Fault Tolerance Re-compute from parent partition Must re-shuffle on node failure Best Practices • Use filter() early to reduce data before any shuffle operation • Use reduceByKey() instead of groupByKey() — performs partial aggregation locally before shuffle • Cache DataFrames before wide transformations if the result is reused • Broadcast small tables in joins to avoid shuffle entirely Page 2 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Additional Techniques","type":"concept","a":"• Data Skipping — Delta auto-collects min/max stats for first 32 columns • Caching — df.cache() for DataFrames reused multiple times in the same job • Broadcast Joins — broadcast hint for small dimension tables • AQE — spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true for runtime optimizations","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Skew — What it is and How to Handle It","type":"concept","a":"• Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across partitions\n• Some tasks (stragglers) take much longer and the entire job waits for them\n• Example: Joining on customer_id where 1 customer has 80% of all records\n• One executor processes millions of rows while others sit idle\n• Detect Skew • Spark UI: Stage details, Task duration histogram — few tasks taking 10x longer df.groupBy('key').count().orderBy(desc('count')).show() Solution 1: Salting from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, col, lit, floor, rand, explode, array salted = skewed_df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('key'), lit('_'), (floor(rand()*10)).cast('string'))) other_exp = other_df.withColumn('salt', explode(array([lit(i) for i in range(10)]))) other_exp = other_exp.withColumn('salted_key', Page 4 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide concat(col('key'), lit('_'), col('salt').cast('string'))) Solution 2: AQE — Recommended for Spark 3+ spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') AQE detects skewed partitions at runtime and automatically splits them\n• Available in Spark 3.0+\n• Easiest modern solution","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Joins — Inner, Left, Right","type":"concept","a":"Table A — Employees EmpID Name 1 Alice 2 Bob 3 Carol 4 Dave Table B — Departments EmpID Dept 1 Engineering 2 Marketing 5 Finance Inner Join — Only Matching Rows SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a INNER JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing Left Join — All from A, Matching from B (NULLs for unmatched) SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a LEFT JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing 3 Carol NULL 4 Dave NULL Right Join — All from B, Matching from A (NULLs for unmatched) SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a RIGHT JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing 5 NULL Finance Page 7 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide PySpark: df_a.join(df_b, 'EmpID', 'inner') | 'left' | 'right' | 'outer' | 'left_semi' | 'left_anti' Quick Reference Summary Topic Key Takeaway DAG Logical plan built lazily; executed on action trigger Narrow Transform No shuffle — same stage (map, filter, select) Wide Transform Shuffle — new stage (groupBy, join, distinct) Broadcast Join Replicate small table to all executors; eliminates shuffle OPTIMIZE Compact small Delta files for faster scans Z-ORDER Cluster data by column for file skipping on filters Data Skew Uneven partitions — fix with AQE (Spark 3+) or salting Delta Lake Data lake + ACID + Time Travel + MERGE on Parquet Auto Loader cloudFiles format — incremental ingestion with checkpointing dense_rank() Best for Top-N salary — handles ties without gaps Left Join All from left + NULL for unmatched right rows Right Join All from right + NULL for unmatched left rows Page 8 of 8","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on vs Wide Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on  No data shuffle.  Child par(cid:415)(cid:415)on depends on one parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  map()  filter()  union()  flatMap() Very fast","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is MongoDB?","type":"concept","a":"• NoSQL document database\n• Stores data as JSON-like BSON documents\n• Example: { \"empId\":101, \"name\":\"John\", \"skills\":[\"Python\",\"Spark\"] } Round 2:","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Spark job gives an Out Of Memory (OOM) error, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would check the Spark UI to iden(cid:415)fy which stage failed\n• Then I would:  Increase executor memory.  Increase the number of executors.  Reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)on size using repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on().  Avoid collect() on large datasets.  Use broadcast joins for small lookup tables.  Cache only when necessary.  Filter data before joins.  Inves(cid:415)gate data skew if one par(cid:415)(cid:415)on is much larger than the others.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Join a Huge Table with a Small Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Use a Broadcast Join\n• The small table is replicated to every executor so the join happens locally on each partition of the large table — no shuffle. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Explicit broadcast hint result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), on='customer_id', how='inner') # Configure auto-broadcast threshold (default 10 MB) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', '100mb') Page 2 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Aspect Broadcast Join Sort-Merge Join Shuffle None — no data movement Both tables shuffled Use when One table < ~200 MB Both tables are large Memory impact Small table on every executor Standard memory usage Performance Very fast Slower but scalable to any size Never broadcast a large table — it causes Out of Memory (OOM) on executors\n• Disable auto- broadcast: spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', '-1')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Join Two Large Tables in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Spark uses Sort-Merge Join (SMJ) by default for large-large joins\n• Additional strategies: Option 1 — Sort-Merge Join (default, Spark 2.3+) • Both tables are shuffled by the join key, sorted, then merged • Spark automatically picks SMJ when both tables exceed the broadcast threshold # SMJ happens automatically; or hint explicitly: result = df_orders.hint('merge').join(df_customers.hint('merge'), on='customer_id') Option 2 — Pre-bucket Both Tables (eliminates shuffle permanently) df_orders.write.bucketBy(64,'customer_id').sortBy('customer_id').saveAsTable('orders_bkt') df_customers.write.bucketBy(64,'customer_id').sortBy('customer_id').saveAsTable('cust_bkt') # All subsequent joins skip shuffle entirely result = spark.table('orders_bkt').join(spark.table('cust_bkt'), 'customer_id') Option 3 — Enable AQE (Adaptive Query Execution) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled', 'true') AQE dynamically adjusts join strategy at runtime\n• If one side turns out smaller than expected, it can switch from SMJ to Broadcast Join automatically","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Broadcast Join?","type":"concept","a":"• A Broadcast Join (map-side join) sends a complete copy of the smaller table to every executor node\n• The large table is never moved — each executor joins its local partition against the in-memory copy of the small table\n• No shuffle at all. • Eliminates shuffle — the most expensive Spark operation (network I/O, disk spill) • Best for: small lookup/dimension tables (under 10 MB default, up to ~200 MB practical) • Ideal pattern: large fact table JOIN broadcast(small dimension table) result = fact_orders.join(broadcast(dim_product), 'product_id') Section 3 | Delta Lake Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Cluster Configuration Would You Use for TB-Level Data?","type":"concept","a":"Aspect Recommendation Notes Cluster type Job Cluster (auto-terminate) Never use All-Purpose in production Driver size 16 cores, 64 GB RAM Larger driver for collect/broadcast ops Page 12 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Worker size 16 cores, 64 GB RAM per node 8-16 workers for 1 TB workloads Auto-scaling Min 4, Max 16 workers Scales with data volume dynamically Runtime Databricks Runtime + Photon Photon gives 3-10x speedup on SQL ops Spot workers Workers on Spot, Driver on On- Up to 70% cost savings Demand Delta Cache Enabled (SSD caching) Critical for repeated Delta table reads # Example Databricks cluster config { 'autoscale': { 'min_workers': 4, 'max_workers': 16 }, 'node_type_id': 'Standard_DS5_v2', # 16 cores, 56 GB RAM 'driver_node_type_id': 'Standard_DS5_v2', 'spark_conf': { 'spark.sql.adaptive.enabled': 'true', 'spark.sql.shuffle.partitions': '400' }, 'runtime_engine': 'PHOTON' }","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Executors Would You Use for Processing 1 TB of Data?","type":"concept","a":"• Default partition size: 128 MB (spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes) • 1 TB / 128 MB = ~8192 partitions (maximum concurrent tasks) • Practical executor config: 4-8 cores per executor, 16-32 GB RAM each • With 64 executors x 8 cores = 512 cores total — can run 512 tasks in parallel • Set shuffle partitions to 200-400: spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '400') Page 13 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Config Recommended Value Reasoning Executors 32-64 Balances parallelism and coordination overhead Cores per executor 4-8 (5 is sweet spot) Above 5 increases GC pressure RAM per executor 16-32 GB Covers 128-256 MB partitions with 4x overhead Total cores 128-512 Sufficient parallelism for 1 TB Shuffle partitions 200-400 Avoid 200 (too few) or 8192 (too many small tasks) Rule of thumb: 5 cores and 16 GB RAM per executor is a well-balanced starting point. Too many small executors = high task overhead. Too few large executors = GC pressure. Use auto-scaling with min/max bounds for production. Section 10 | Behavioral / Closing","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What skills or experiences have made previous hires most successful in this role?","type":"concept","a":"• Never say 'I have no questions' — it signals disinterest\n• Avoid asking about salary, leave policy, or working hours in a technical round\n• Save those for the HR discussion\n• Quick Reference — All 30 Questions at a Glance Page 14 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) # Question (Short) Key Answer / Keyword 1 Spark Architecture Driver + Cluster Manager + Executors + Stages + DAG + Lazy Eval 2 Notebooks per year 10-40 typical; organized by Bronze/Silver/Gold layers 3 Daily data volume Tailor to project;\n• 500 GB-2 TB is typical mid-scale 4 File types CSV, JSON, Parquet, Delta, Avro, ORC, XML 5 Huge + small table join Broadcast Join — no shuffle, small table to all executors 6 Two large tables join Sort-Merge Join (default) OR pre-bucket both tables 7 Broadcast Join Copy small table to every executor; eliminates shuffle entirely 8 Z-ORDER for transactions transaction_date + customer_id (high cardinality, frequent filters) 9 Default Z-ORDER column None — must be explicit;\n• Delta auto-stats on first 32 cols 10 Z-Order indexes Z-curve clustering; co-locates related rows; enables file skipping 11 CSV to Parquet Columnar, compressed, schema-aware, predicate pushdown, 5-20x faster 12 JSON to structured table from_json() + schema + explode() for nested/array fields 13 Remove duplicates dropDuplicates() / distinct() / Window row_number() / MERGE 14 Duplicate handling methods 7 methods: dropDuplicates, distinct, window rank, MERGE, GROUP BY\n• 15 Gender count per city GROUP BY city + COUNT(CASE WHEN gender=X) or PIVOT or PySpark agg 16 Liquid Clustering Replaces partition+ZORDER; dynamic, incremental, change columns anytime 17 View types Temp, Global Temp, Standard (metastore), Materialized (pre- computed) 18 Materialized Views Pre-computed Delta table; auto-refreshed; fast for heavy aggregations 19 Thousands of CSV files Auto Loader (cloudFiles) + checkpoint — incremental, scalable 20 Most efficient file loading Auto Loader for incremental;\n• COPY INTO for SQL batch; spark.read for one-time 21 Auto Loader incremental Directory listing OR file notifications (Event Grid/SNS) + checkpoint 22 Schedule Auto Loader Databricks Jobs, DLT pipelines, ADF triggers, Databricks Workflows 23 Auto Loader triggers availableNow, processingTime, continuous, once (deprecated) 24 Auto Loader file detection Directory scan vs checkpoint OR cloud file notification queue 25 Corrupted CSV rows PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + quarantine bad rows to Delta table 26 Bronze layer from CSV files Auto Loader + _ingestion_time + _source_file + mergeSchema=true 27 TB-level cluster config 16+ workers, Photon, auto-scale, Spot workers, AQE enabled Page 15 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) 28 Cluster types in Databricks All-Purpose, Job, High Concurrency, Single Node, SQL Warehouse, DLT 29 Executors for 1 TB 32-64 executors, 4-8 cores each, 16-32 GB RAM, shuffle.partitions=400 30 Questions for interviewer Ask about architecture, challenges, CI/CD, streaming, first 90 days Page 16 of 16","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Spark DataFrame from the given data.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Display the final DataFrame.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Convert the nested structure into a Spark StructType (if needed).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produce a final DataFrame with the following schema:","type":"concept","a":"product | price | quan(cid:415)ty","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"How will you join two large DataFrames effec(cid:415)vely in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• \"When both DataFrames are large, I avoid broadcast joins because broadcas(cid:415)ng large datasets can cause memory issues\n• Instead, I:  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on both DataFrames on the join key.  Filter unnecessary records before joining.  Select only required columns.  Enable Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE).  Ensure both DataFrames have a balanced par(cid:415)(cid:415)on distribu(cid:415)on to minimize shuffle.\" Example df1 = df1.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(\"user_id\") df2 = df2.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(\"user_id\") result = df1.join(df2, \"user_id\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark coding: Large table is skewed, join with a small table.","type":"concept","a":"• Interviewer Large table is skewed\n• Small table is normal\n• Join Key: user_id \"Since the large DataFrame is skewed and the second DataFrame is small, I would use a Broadcast Join\n• Broadcas(cid:415)ng the small table eliminates shuffle for that table and significantly improves performance\n• If skew is severe, I may also use key sal(cid:415)ng.\" Broadcast Join from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"user_id\", \"le(cid:332)\" ) If skew is extremely high (Sal(cid:415)ng) from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import floor, rand large_df = large_df.withColumn( \"salt\", floor(rand()*5) ) small_df = small_df.crossJoin( spark.range(5).toDF(\"salt\") ) result = large_df.join( small_df, [\"user_id\",\"salt\"] )","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Read CSV → Filter inac(cid:415)ve → Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on by Region → Write Delta to S3","type":"concept","a":"PySpark Code from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on( \"header\", True ).csv(\"s3://input/customer.csv\") inac(cid:415)ve = df.filter( col(\"status\") == \"inac(cid:415)ve\" ) inac(cid:415)ve.write \\ .format(\"delta\") \\ .par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"region\") \\ .mode(\"overwrite\") \\ .save(\"s3://output/customer_delta/\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you install a third-party JAR in Python?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the JAR is required for Spark, I a(cid:425)ach it to the Databricks cluster:  Go to the cluster configura(cid:415)on.  Select Libraries.  Install the JAR from Workspace, DBFS, Maven, or cloud storage.  Restart the cluster if required\n• If I need a Python package instead of a JAR, I use %pip install in Databricks notebooks or install it as a cluster library.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you explain technical strategies to non-technical stakeholders?","type":"concept","a":"• \"When speaking with non-technical stakeholders, I avoid technical jargon and focus on business outcomes\n• For example, instead of saying, 'We op(cid:415)mized Spark jobs using broadcast joins and par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning,' I would say: 'We improved the data processing process so reports are generated faster and more reliably, reducing wai(cid:415)ng (cid:415)me for business users.' I also use simple diagrams, real-world analogies, and dashboards to explain the impact of the solu(cid:415)on\n• My goal is to help stakeholders understand how the changes improve performance, reduce costs, or support be(cid:425)er decision-making without requiring technical knowledge.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark code (min salary by dept, cast emp id, null salary check)","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col, min df_clean = df \\ .withColumn(\"emp_id\", col(\"emp_id\").cast(\"int\")) \\ .filter(col(\"salary\").isNotNull()) df_result = df_clean.groupBy(\"dept_id\") \\ .agg(min(\"salary\").alias(\"min_salary\")) df_result.show()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If column name changed in withColumn, do we rerun both cells?","type":"concept","a":" Yes, you must rerun dependent cells  Because Spark is lazy evalua(cid:415)on, but notebook cells depend on execu(cid:415)on order  So if upstream transforma(cid:415)on changes column name, downstream cells must be re- executed","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Spark architecture + stages + cluster used","type":"concept","a":"Architecture:  Driver  Executors  Cluster Manager Stages:  Spark divides job into stages based on shuffle boundaries  Each stage has mul(cid:415)ple tasks  Example: groupBy or join causes shuffle → new stage Cluster used:  Typically Databricks job cluster / all-purpose cluster  Auto-scaling enabled in produc(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Upskilling & cer(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":" Learning PySpark, Azure Data Engineering  Microso(cid:332) cer(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons (DP-203)  Prac(cid:415)cing Databricks, Delta Lake  Hands-on projects","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Check Spark UI for stages and tasks.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Check shuffle size.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Cache reused DataFrames.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Use AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Wide vs. Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Narrow Wide No shuffle Shuffle required Faster Slower One parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Mul(cid:415)ple parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Examples: Narrow df.filter() df.map() Wide df.groupBy() df.join() df.dis(cid:415)nct()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Coalesce vs. Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Coalesce Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on Reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons No full shuffle (usually) Full shuffle Faster for reducing More expensive","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"You have 5 million records. How will you op(cid:415)mize?","type":"concept","a":" Read only required columns.  Filter early.  Use Parquet or Delta.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on appropriately.  Broadcast small lookup tables.  Cache reused DataFrames.  Avoid unnecessary shuffles.  Enable AQE.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"How do you see PySpark op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":" Spark UI  Physical execu(cid:415)on plan: df.explain(True) Check for:  Broadcast joins  Shuffle opera(cid:415)ons  Predicate pushdown  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on pruning","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Improvement using GenAI in your project","type":"concept","a":"• Example answer: \"We used GenAI tools such as ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot to improve developer produc(cid:415)vity\n• They helped generate PySpark transforma(cid:415)ons, SQL queries, unit tests, documenta(cid:415)on, and code explana(cid:415)ons\n• This reduced development (cid:415)me, improved consistency, and accelerated troubleshoo(cid:415)ng\n• We always reviewed and validated AI-generated code before using it.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Transforma(cid:415)on and Ac(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Transforma(cid:415)on Ac(cid:415)on Creates a new DataFrame Triggers execu(cid:415)on Lazy Eager Builds the DAG Executes the DAG Examples: Transforma(cid:415)ons filter() select() join() groupBy() withColumn() Ac(cid:415)ons show() count() collect() write() first()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"A Spark job processing 3 joined tables increased from 1 million to 50 million records. The","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"job now takes much longer. How would you debug it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would first analyze the Spark UI to iden(cid:415)fy the bo(cid:425)leneck, then check for data skew, shuffle size, join strategy, par(cid:415)(cid:415)on count, execu(cid:415)on plan, and executor resource usage\n• Based on the findings, I would op(cid:415)mize using broadcast joins, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, filtering before joins, selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, caching reusable DataFrames, or handling skew.\" Step-by-step approach Step 1: Check the Spark UI Look at:","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":" Which stage is taking the most (cid:415)me?","type":"concept","a":"•  Number of tasks  Shuffle Read/Write size  Executor CPU u(cid:415)liza(cid:415)on  Spill to disk  Skewed tasks (one task taking much longer than others) Step 2: Check Data Skew One of the join keys may have become highly skewed\n• Example: Customer_ID 1 -> 20 million records 2 -> 10 records 3 -> 5 records One executor processes huge data while others stay idle\n• Check: df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count().orderBy(\"count\", ascending=False).show() Possible solu(cid:415)ons:  Sal(cid:415)ng  Broadcast join (if one table is small)  AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on) Step 3: Check Join Strategy Run df.explain(True) Look for:  BroadcastHashJoin  SortMergeJoin  ShuffleHashJoin If Spark is doing SortMergeJoin because the table became larger, shuffle cost increases\n• If one table is small: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\") Step 4: Check Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Count Too few par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:  Executors become idle\n• Too many par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:  Scheduling overhead increases\n• Check: df.rdd.getNumPar(cid:415)(cid:415)ons() Adjust if necessary: df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(400) or spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.shuffle.par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons\", 400) (Default is o(cid:332)en 200.) Step 5: Check Shuffle Size Large shuffle is a common bo(cid:425)leneck\n• Spark UI → Shuffle Read/Write If shuffle is very high:  Filter data before join  Select only required columns  Broadcast small table Step 6: Filter Early Bad: Join ↓ Filter Be(cid:425)er: Filter ↓ Join This reduces data movement\n• Step 7: Remove Unnecessary Columns Instead of select(\"*\") Use select(\"id\", \"name\", \"amount\") Less data is shuffled\n• Step 8: Check Caching If the same DataFrame is reused mul(cid:415)ple (cid:415)mes: df.cache() Otherwise Spark recomputes it\n• Step 9: Check Executor Configura(cid:415)on Review:  Executor Memory  Executor Cores  Driver Memory Insufficient memory can lead to disk spilling\n• Step 10: Compare Execu(cid:415)on Plans df.explain(True) Look for:  Extra exchanges  Mul(cid:415)ple shuffles  Cartesian joins  Broadcast not happening","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Coalesce and Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Coalesce Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on Reduces par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Increases or decreases par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Avoids full shuffle (mostly) Always performs a full shuffle Faster Slower Used a(cid:332)er filtering Used before joins or for balancing data Cannot efficiently increase par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Can increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Example: df.coalesce(5) df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(20)","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"May create skewed par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Some executors process much more data.","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques have you used in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"\"The op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques I have used include:  Broadcast joins for small lookup tables.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning to balance data distribu(cid:415)on.  Caching frequently used DataFrames.  Filtering data early to reduce processing.  Using column pruning instead of SELECT *.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning large datasets.  Enabling Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE).  Op(cid:415)mizing Delta tables using OPTIMIZE and ZORDER.  Avoiding unnecessary shuffle opera(cid:415)ons.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How will you join a small table with a large dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If one table is significantly smaller than the other, I use a Broadcast Join\n• Spark sends the small table to every executor, allowing the large table to be processed locally without a shuffle\n• This improves performance by reducing network overhead.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Write PySpark code to create a new column as the sum of two columns.","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col df = df.withColumn( \"Total\", col(\"Salary\") + col(\"Bonus\") ) df.show()","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in PySpark.","type":"concept","a":"Men(cid:415)on:  Broadcast Join  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Cache/Persist  Filter Early  Column Pruning  AQE  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Delta Op(cid:415)mize  ZORDER  Avoid Shuffle","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Volume?","type":"concept","a":"• Volume refers to the amount/size of data being stored or processed\n• It is one of the 3 Vs of Big Data (Volume, Velocity, Variety). • Small scale: GBs - can fit on a single machine • Medium scale: TBs - requires distributed systems • Large scale: PBs/EBs - requires cloud-native distributed architectures (e.g., HDFS, S3, ADLS) In Databricks/Spark, volume impacts partitioning strategy, cluster sizing, and shuffle operations","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What to do when the Dataset is Slow?","type":"concept","a":"Diagnosis & Optimization Strategies: • Check Spark UI: Identify slow stages, skewed partitions, spill to disk • Partitioning: Repartition/coalesce to right number of partitions • Caching: cache() or persist() frequently accessed DataFrames • Broadcast Join: Use for small tables (< 10MB by default) • Predicate Pushdown: Filter early to reduce data scanned • File format: Use Parquet/Delta instead of CSV/JSON • Z-ordering: Co-locate related data for faster reads • AQE: Enable Adaptive Query Execution • Columnar pruning: Select only needed columns • Data skew: Apply salting or AQE skew hints • Cluster sizing: Increase worker nodes or use larger instance type SECTION 2: Data Modeling","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What is Partitioning?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning divides data into smaller, manageable chunks to improve query performance and parallelism\n• Types: • Hive-style Partitioning: Folders on disk (e.g., /data/year=2024/month=01/) • Spark RDD/DataFrame Partitioning: In-memory distribution across executors • Hash Partitioning: Distribute by hash of a column • Range Partitioning: Distribute by value ranges Best Practices: • Choose low-cardinality columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning (too many small files) • Use OPTIMIZE to compact small files in Delta # Write partitioned Delta table df.write.format('delta') \\ .partitionBy('year', 'month') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save('/delta/sales') # Repartition in Spark df = df.repartition(200, col('customer_id')) df = df.coalesce(10) # Reduce partitions without full shuffle SECTION 3: Azure Data Factory (ADF)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Single Node Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Driver only, no workers — for small data / development • No distributed processing Cluster Modes: • Standard Mode: Full Spark — Python, Scala, R, SQL • Enhanced Autoscaling: Dynamic worker count based on workload • Photon Engine: Vectorized query engine for faster SQL SECTION 4: CI/CD, Delta Lake & Architecture","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Lazy Evaluation in Spark","type":"concept","a":"• Lazy Evaluation means Spark does NOT execute transformations immediately\n• It builds a logical plan (DAG) and only executes when an action is called\n• Transformations (Lazy — build the plan): • filter(), select(), join(), groupBy(), withColumn(), map() Actions (Eager — trigger execution): • count(), collect(), show(), write(), save(), take() # None of these execute yet: df = spark.read.csv('/data/sales.csv', header=True) df = df.filter(col('amount') > 1000) # Lazy df = df.groupBy('region').sum('amount') # Lazy # Execution happens HERE: df.show() # Action — triggers the full plan Benefits: • Query optimization via Catalyst Optimizer before execution • Pipelining of transformations to avoid redundant passes • Spark can reorder/skip operations for efficiency","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Catalyst Optimizer in Spark","type":"concept","a":"• Catalyst Optimizer is Spark's built-in query optimizer that automatically optimizes SQL/DataFrame operations using a tree-based rule system\n• Optimization Phases: • 1\n• Analysis: Resolves column references, validates schema • 2\n• Logical Optimization: Applies rule-based optimizations: ◦ Predicate Pushdown: Push filters as close to source as possible ◦ Constant Folding: Pre-compute constant expressions ◦ Column Pruning: Drop unused columns early • 3\n• Physical Planning: Converts logical plan to physical plan, chooses join strategy • 4\n• Code Generation: Generates JVM bytecode (Whole-Stage CodeGen) # Catalyst auto-applies pushdown — you benefit automatically # To inspect the plan: df.explain(True) # Shows analyzed, optimized, physical plans # Extended explain df.explain(mode='formatted')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark — Process 100M Row CSV","type":"concept","a":"• from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, sum as spark_sum from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType # Initialize Spark with optimized config for 100M rows spark = SparkSession.builder \\ .appName('LargeCSVProcessor') \\ .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') \\ .config('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '400') \\ .config('spark.executor.memory', '8g') \\ .getOrCreate() # Define schema explicitly (faster than inferSchema for large files) schema = StructType([ StructField('transaction_id', StringType(), True), StructField('customer_id', StringType(), True), StructField('transaction_date', StringType(), True), StructField('amount', DoubleType(), True), StructField('category', StringType(), True), ]) # Step 1: Read large CSV df = spark.read \\ .option('header', 'true') \\ .option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED') \\ .schema(schema) \\ .csv('/data/large_transactions.csv') print(f'Total rows: {df.count()}') # Step 2: Filter out rows with missing values df_clean = df.dropna(subset=['transaction_id', 'customer_id', 'transaction_date', 'amount']) df_clean = df_clean.filter( col('transaction_id').isNotNull() & col('customer_id').isNotNull() & col('amount').isNotNull() ) print(f'Rows after null filter: {df_clean.count()}') # Step 3: Convert transaction_date to datetime df_clean = df_clean.withColumn( 'transaction_date', to_timestamp(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss') ) # Step 4: Group by category and calculate sum of amount df_aggregated = df_clean \\ .groupBy('category') \\ .agg(spark_sum('amount').alias('total_amount')) \\ .orderBy(col('total_amount').desc()) df_aggregated.show() # Step 5: Write result to new CSV file df_aggregated.coalesce(1) \\ .write \\ .option('header', 'true') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .csv('/output/revenue_by_category') print('Processing complete\n• Results written to /output/revenue_by_category') spark.stop() SECTION 7: Challenges & Issues in Pipelines","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Task is taking 45 mins, as an experience data engineer what can be the issue and how","type":"concept","a":"• will you rec(cid:415)fy\n• If a pipeline task is taking 45 minutes, I would first analyze logs and execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy the bo(cid:425)leneck stage—whether it's extrac(cid:415)on, transforma(cid:415)on, or load\n• Common causes include full table scans, missing indexes, lack of incremental load, inefficient joins, data skew, or small file issues\n• I would op(cid:415)mize by implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loading using watermark or CDC, op(cid:415)mizing joins using broadcast or par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, improving cluster configura(cid:415)on, and par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data properly in storage\n• I would also reduce shuffles and unnecessary transforma(cid:415)ons and monitor performance a(cid:332)er each change","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"If there are 100 columns, how will you create schema?","type":"concept","a":"“I prefer schema defini(cid:415)on over schema inference for be(cid:425)er control and performance.” (cid:48132) Approach 1: Explicit schema (best prac(cid:415)ce) from pyspark.sql.types import * schema = StructType([ StructField(\"col1\", StringType(), True), StructField(\"col2\", IntegerType(), True), ... ]) (cid:48131) Approach 2: Read from source with schema inference (not preferred in prod) df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", True).csv(path) (cid:44931) Approach 3: Schema registry / metadata-driven (enterprise approach)  Store schema in: o JSON o Control table  Dynamically generate schema “For 100 columns, I prefer metadata-driven schema genera(cid:415)on or explicit schema defini(cid:415)on to avoid type mismatches and improve performance.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"(cid:44869)(cid:44870) What happens if broadcast is NOT used?","type":"concept","a":"• “Spark automa(cid:415)cally decides the join type based on size and configura(cid:415)on.” Default behavior:  Small + large table → Broadcast join (auto)  Large + large → Sort-Merge Join (cid:55982)(cid:55983)(cid:55984) Join selec(cid:415)on logic: Scenario Join Type Small table Broadcast Join Large tables Sort Merge Join Skewed data May cause shuffle skew “If broadcast is not explicitly specified, Spark’s op(cid:415)mizer (Catalyst + AQE) decides the join strategy\n• If one table is small enough, it automa(cid:415)cally performs a broadcast join; otherwise it uses sort-merge join for large datasets.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"delta","name":"Delta Lake","icon":"🗄️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Delta Lake / a Delta table and what problems does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that sits on top of Parquet in cloud storage and adds: ACID transactions, a transaction log (_delta_log), schema enforcement & evolution, time travel, UPSERT/MERGE, and unified batch + streaming reads/writes.\nIt brings reliability and warehouse-like guarantees to a data lake (the 'lakehouse'), fixing typical data-lake issues: no ACID, no schema control, slow updates, and small-file problems."},{"q":"Explain ACID properties in Delta Lake and the transaction log.","type":"concept","a":"• Atomicity — a commit fully succeeds or fully fails (no partial writes).\n• Consistency — schema enforcement keeps data valid.\n• Isolation — snapshot isolation for concurrent reads/writes.\n• Durability — committed data persists even after failures.\nThe _delta_log is an ordered set of JSON commits recording every change (add/remove files, metadata, protocol). Each read reconstructs the table state from the log; the log is the source of truth and enables time travel and atomic operations."},{"q":"What are the file types inside the _delta_log folder?","type":"concept","a":"• .json — incremental transaction files, one per commit (AddFile, RemoveFile, metadata, protocol). No actual data.\n• .checkpoint.parquet — a snapshot of table state every ~10 commits so readers don't replay all JSON files.\n• .compacted.json — combines multiple small JSON commits to speed up log reads.\n• .crc — checksum/integrity file for checkpoints; on corruption Spark falls back to the previous valid checkpoint.\nTogether they keep metadata reads fast and reliable."},{"q":"What is Delta table metadata log management and how does it become a bottleneck?","type":"concept","a":"Every commit adds a JSON file to _delta_log; over time this grows and slows query planning. Mitigation:\n• Checkpoints (every 10 commits) collapse state so readers load 1 checkpoint + recent JSONs.\n• delta.logRetentionDuration (default 30 days) — old JSON commits are pruned once a checkpoint exists.\n• Compacted JSON files reduce the number of individual commits.\n• OPTIMIZE compacts the underlying Parquet data files (not the log).\nNote: copying the log elsewhere doesn't fix performance — checkpointing + retention does."},{"q":"How does time travel work in Delta Lake?","type":"concept","a":"Delta keeps every version in the log, so you can query historical snapshots:\n• By version: SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 5 (or option('versionAsOf',5)).\n• By timestamp: SELECT * FROM t TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01'.\n• DESCRIBE HISTORY t shows the commit history (operations, versions, timestamps).\nUse cases: audit, reproduce past results, recover from bad writes (RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF n).\nLimitation: time travel is bounded by VACUUM retention — once old files are removed, those versions are gone."},{"q":"Difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema enforcement (validation) — by default Delta rejects writes whose schema doesn't match the table, protecting data integrity.\n• Schema evolution — you can intentionally allow new/changed columns:\n  – df.write.option('mergeSchema','true').mode('append')\n  – SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true\n  – ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ...\n  – COPY INTO ... COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')\n• overwriteSchema replaces the whole schema (use with caution)."},{"q":"How do you implement MERGE (UPSERT) in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"MERGE matches source to target and conditionally updates/inserts/deletes — the standard upsert pattern for incremental/CDC loads.\nExample:\ntarget.alias('t').merge(incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.id = s.id').whenMatchedUpdateAll().whenNotMatchedInsertAll().execute()\nSQL: MERGE INTO t USING s ON t.id=s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\nSupports whenMatchedDelete, conditional updates, and partial column updates. Requires the join condition to be on key columns for efficiency."},{"q":"What does OPTIMIZE do?","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE compacts many small Parquet files into fewer, larger (~1 GB) files to improve read performance and reduce metadata overhead (fixes the small-file problem).\n• OPTIMIZE table; or OPTIMIZE table WHERE date >= '2026-01-01' (target recent partitions).\n• OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY (col) adds clustering for data skipping.\nRun after bulk loads / daily ETL. Target file size is configurable via delta.optimize.maxFileSize. Auto Optimize can do this automatically."},{"q":"What is Z-ORDER and how does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"Z-ORDER co-locates related rows into the same files by reordering data across multiple columns (multi-dimensional clustering).\n• Delta's data-skipping uses file min/max stats to skip files that can't match a filter; good clustering means fewer files to scan.\n• Best on HIGH-cardinality columns used frequently in WHERE/JOIN (customer_id, transaction_id, date).\n• Syntax: OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date).\n• It's expensive — apply to the most queried columns only. Don't combine with auto compaction."},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compacts small files into bigger files; no sorting; always useful for small-file cleanup.\n• Z-ORDER — does the compaction AND sorts/clusters data by column values within files for data skipping; best for high-cardinality filter columns; requires re-running to stay fresh.\n• Liquid Clustering — dynamic, incremental clustering on chosen keys; Databricks re-clusters new data automatically, no partition maintenance, works with evolving workloads; replaces partition+z-order.\nModern guidance: prefer Liquid Clustering; use OPTIMIZE (with ZORDER) on legacy partitioned tables."},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"Liquid Clustering is a Databricks Delta feature that organizes data by clustering keys without traditional Hive partitioning.\n• Define keys once: ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date) (or CLUSTER BY AUTO).\n• Databricks incrementally re-clusters new data; keys can change without rewriting the whole table.\n• Removes partition-column cardinality pitfalls and small-file issues; improves data skipping.\n• No need to run Z-ORDER manually; useful for evolving/changing query patterns."},{"q":"What is VACUUM and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM permanently deletes data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta log and are past the retention period (default 7 days).\n• Reduces storage cost and scan overhead.\n• VACUUM t RETAIN 168 HOURS; add DRY RUN to see what would be deleted.\n• Warning: vacuuming deletes old versions, so you LOSE time travel beyond the retention. Lowering retention below 7 days requires disabling the safety check (risky — can corrupt concurrent readers)."},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed (CDF) and how does it differ from CDC?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF (Delta feature) — records row-level changes (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) in a Delta table so downstream can read changes incrementally.\n  Enable: ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed=true); read with readChangeFeed=true + startingVersion.\n  Extra columns: _change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp.\n• CDC — the broader process of capturing INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from a SOURCE system (via log-based tools like Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, triggers, or timestamp polling).\nIn short: CDC captures changes at the source; CDF propagates Delta table changes downstream. CDF is a Delta-native way to consume changes."},{"q":"What are Deletion Vectors?","type":"concept","a":"Deletion Vectors mark deleted rows in a bitmap rather than immediately rewriting the entire Parquet file.\n• Makes DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE much faster (avoids rewriting whole files).\n• Readers transparently respect the deletion vector; compacted later by OPTIMIZE.\n• Enable via table property delta.enableDeletionVectors=true. Trade-off: slight read overhead until optimized."},{"q":"Difference between DEEP CLONE and SHALLOW CLONE?","type":"concept","a":"• DEEP CLONE — copies the data files AND the metadata/history to a new location; fully independent; supports time travel on the clone; used for migration/backup/experimentation.\n• SHALLOW CLONE — copies only the metadata and references the original data files (no data copy); fast/cheap; if the source files are vacuumed or changed, the clone breaks (depends on source).\nUse deep clone for independent copies; shallow clone for quick test/dev without data duplication.\nSyntax: CREATE TABLE dest DEEP|SHALLOW CLONE source."},{"q":"Difference between managed (internal) and external tables in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — Databricks manages the storage location and lifecycle; DROP TABLE deletes BOTH metadata AND data files. Default format is Delta. Best for lakehouse/DW tables.\n• External table — you specify the storage location (ADLS/S3); DROP TABLE deletes only metadata, data files REMAIN. Shared across tools.\nProduction rule: use external tables (or Unity Catalog managed with careful governance) on ADLS so a drop can't accidentally destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move/copy a Delta table to another location. What options do you have?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read + write new location: spark.read.format('delta').load(src).write.format('delta').save(dest).\n• DEEP CLONE — preserves data + history/metadata to a new path (independent).\n• SHALLOW CLONE — metadata-only, references original files (fast/cheap).\n• Export to Parquet/CSV for external consumption.\nChoose deep clone for a full copy, shallow clone for a quick reference."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A managed table was dropped and, after the retention period, the files were permanently removed. Can you recreate it?","type":"scenario","a":"No — not from Delta alone. Time travel and RESTORE depend on the transaction log AND the underlying data files still existing. Once both are permanently deleted, recovery options are:\n• Restore from backup/DR copy.\n• Reload from the source system and reprocess.\n• Recover via cloud storage versioning/soft-delete if enabled.\nLesson: use external tables (or backups) in production so a DROP doesn't destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The CRC file is corrupted. What happens?","type":"scenario","a":"• The .crc file validates checkpoint integrity. If the CRC check fails, Spark gracefully falls back to the PREVIOUS valid checkpoint and uses the recent JSON commits to rebuild current state.\n• So reads typically still succeed — the corrupted checkpoint is simply skipped.\n• Fix root cause (e.g., partial/interrupted write). Delta is resilient because the log + checkpoints are redundant; you can also force a new checkpoint with OPTIMIZE or Spark actions."},{"q":"What are Delta table optimization techniques?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compact small files.\n• Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering — co-locate related data for data skipping.\n• Partitioning — on low-cardinality columns (date/region) where partitions are ~1 GB+.\n• Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) — automatic file sizing.\n• VACUUM — remove stale files.\n• Deletion Vectors — fast deletes/updates.\n• Predictive Optimization (UC) — automatic maintenance.\n• Right file size (~128 MB–1 GB), avoid over-partitioning, use Photon + AQE, and keep statistics for data skipping."},{"q":"What is the small-file problem and how do you fix it in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"Streaming/micro-batch or many small writes create thousands of tiny files, causing high metadata/listing overhead and slow reads.\nFix:\n• OPTIMIZE to compact into larger files; run on a schedule.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) for automatic compaction.\n• Use trigger(availableNow=True) or larger micro-batches in streaming; consider foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy.\n• Set a target file size and coalesce before writes."},{"q":"How do COPY INTO and Auto Loader differ for ingestion?","type":"concept","a":"• COPY INTO — SQL-based, idempotent batch ingestion of files from cloud storage into a Delta table; tracks already-loaded files; good for scheduled/batch loads.\n• Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — Structured Streaming source that incrementally and continuously discovers new files (file notification or directory listing); scalable, handles schema inference/evolution and rescued data; best for high-volume, near-real-time ingestion.\nUse COPY INTO for simple batch; Auto Loader for continuous/scalable incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is Time Travel? What is the reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• Time Travel allows querying previous versions of a Delta table\n• Example: SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5; or SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01';\n• Default reten(cid:415)on:  Transac(cid:415)on Log = 30 days  Deleted data (VACUUM) reten(cid:415)on = 7 days","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore a table beyond the default reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• If the data has already been removed by VACUUM, it cannot be restored using Delta Time Travel because the underlying files have been permanently deleted\n• Possible recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from an ADLS backup or snapshot.  Recreate the table from the original source.  Use an external backup or disaster recovery solu(cid:415)on\n• If the files are s(cid:415)ll available (for example, because VACUUM has not removed them), you can restore with: RESTORE TABLE sales TO VERSION AS OF 10; or RESTORE TABLE sales TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-05-01';","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you increase reten(cid:415)on period for one table?","type":"concept","a":"• Example: ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.deletedFileReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='30 days' ); or ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.logReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='90 days' );\n• This changes reten(cid:415)on only for that specific Delta table","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE Over (cid:415)me, many small files can be created due to frequent writes\n• OPTIMIZE compacts these small files into fewer, larger files\n• Example: Before: 1000 files × 5 MB A(cid:332)er: 10 files × 500 MB OPTIMIZE sales;\n• Benefits:  Fewer files to open.  Faster reads.  Be(cid:425)er query performance\n• Z-ORDER ZORDER physically reorganizes data within the op(cid:415)mized files so that rows with similar values are stored close together\n• OPTIMIZE sales ZORDER BY (customer_id);\n• If you frequently run: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE customer_id = 101;\n• Z-Ordering helps Spark read only the relevant por(cid:415)ons of the data, reducing the amount scanned\n• Difference between OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Compacts small files into larger ones Reorders data within files based on specified columns Reduces file management overhead Improves data skipping for filtered queries Improves general read performance Improves queries filtering on Z-Ordered columns Can be run alone Used as part of OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER BY (...)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delta Lake Architecture","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE, ZORDER & VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Time Travel in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Why Is This Useful?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Case Example Debugging A bug caused wrong data to be written yesterday — query v10 to see the clean state Rollback Accidentally deleted rows — restore from a previous version Auditing Regulatory requirement to show what data looked like on Jan 1st Reproducibility ML model trained on v5 of the data — reproduce by reading that version Time Travel Code # ── Query by VERSION NUMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────── # SQL spark.sql('SELECT * FROM silver.orders VERSION AS OF 5') # Python df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', '5') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── Query by TIMESTAMP ──────────────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM silver.orders TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-15 10:00:00'\") df_yesterday = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── See all versions (history) ──────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql('DESCRIBE HISTORY silver.orders').show(truncate=False) # Shows: version, timestamp, operation (WRITE/DELETE/MERGE), user, etc. # ── RESTORE a table to a previous version ──────────────────────────── spark.sql('RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 3') # Or by timestamp: spark.sql(\"RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-14'\") ⚠️ Time Travel Limitation Time travel only works as long as the old Parquet files exist\n• Running VACUUM deletes those files permanently\n• Default VACUUM retention = 7 days → time travel available for the last 7 days by default\n• Increase retention period if you need longer time travel: ALTER TABLE \n• SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days')","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• Azure Data bricks is a data analytics platform optimized for the Microsoft Azure cloud services platform\n• Azure Data bricks offers three environments for developing data intensive applications: Data bricks SQL, Data bricks Data Science & Engineering, and Data bricks Machine Learning","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of clusters in Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ Single Node: Doesn’t have any worker nodes and recommended for the single user cluster the small data volume. ➢ Standard: Recommend for a Single user cluster and can run SQL, Python, R, SCALA workload . ➢ High Concurrency: Optimized to run concurrency SQL, Python, R workloads rund and scala is not supported.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is delta Lake, How to create delta lake tables","type":"concept","a":"• Delta lake is an open-source data format that provides ACID transactions, data reliability, query performance, data caching and indexing, and many other benefits\n• Delta lake can be thought of as an extension of existing data lakes and can be configured per the data requirements","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Difference Between Data Lake and Delta lake","type":"concept","a":"Page | 15 Page | 16 =============AZURE DATA BRICKS TABLES AND SYNTAX==================== https://sparkbyexamples.com/pyspark/pyspark-withcolumn/ --- This link is reference of any commands CREATE A DATAFRAME BY USING A SPARK It means that Output will be visual in Table Syntax : DF=spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema=colunm) Joins: There is a Different joins like Inner, Left Join(left outer, left semi), Right Outer Join, Semi Table Syntax: emp = [(1,\"Smith\",-1,\"2018\",\"10\",\"M\",3000), \\ (2,\"Rose\",1,\"2010\",\"20\",\"M\",4000), \\ (3,\"Williams\",1,\"2010\",\"10\",\"M\",1000), \\ (4,\"Jones\",2,\"2005\",\"10\",\"F\",2000), \\ (5,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"40\",\"\",-1), \\ (6,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"50\",\"\",-1) \\ ] empColumns = [\"emp_id\",\"name\",\"superior_emp_id\",\"year_joined\", \\ \"emp_dept_id\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] empDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=emp, schema = empColumns) empDF.show() --- Creating a Data Frame dept = [(\"Finance\",10), \\ (\"Marketing\",20), \\ (\"Sales\",30), \\ (\"IT\",40) \\ ] deptColumns = [\"dept_name\",\"dept_id\"] deptDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=dept, schema = deptColumns) deptDF.show() ---- Creating a Data Frame and show Syntax: Page | 17 empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"Inner\" ).show() --- it is a Inner join empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"leftsemi\" ).show() -- it is a Left join SELFJOIN from pyspark.sql.functions import col --- (If col is not define on that we define this is statement) empDF.alias(\"emp1\").join(empDF.alias(\"emp2\"), \\ col(\"emp1.superior_emp_id\") == col(\"emp2.emp_id\"),\"inner\") \\ .select ((\"emp1.emp_id\"), (\"emp1.name\"), \\ col(\"emp2.emp_id\").alias(\"superid\"), \\ col(\"emp2.name\").alias(\"supername\")).show() WTIH COLUMN It menas to define a New colunm and add a any expression like add, multiple etc\\ Table: data = [('James','','Smith','1991-04-01','M',3000), ('Michael','Rose','','2000-05-19','M',4000), ('Robert','','Williams','1978-09-05','M',4000), ('Maria','Anne','Jones','1967-12-01','F',4000), ('Jen','Mary','Brown','1980-02-17','F',-1) ] columns = [\"firstname\",\"middlename\",\"lastname\",\"dob\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] from pyspark.sql import SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SparkByExamples.com').getOrCreate() df = spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema = columns) df.show() Syntax : df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show() HOW TO LOAD THE IN DATA BRICKS Go to Data and click on create table and Upload the data Syntax: loaddf=spark.read.parquet(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.parquet\") DROP THE COLUNM Syntax: userdf=loaddf.drop(\"ip_address,cc\") Page | 18 CONVERT THE PARQUET TO CSV FILE Syntax: csvdf=userdf.write.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) -- it will convert and store a New file usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) --- it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe usercsvdf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"user\") --- it will convert the filename into Table name %sql --- it is important because there is a default is python, so by using a SQl Command to use (if R language %R) select * from user MOUNT FROM DATA LAKE GEN2 TO DATABRICKS ➢ create a data lake gen2 and upload a parquet file ➢ create a Azure Active Dictory and create a New App Registration and also create a new cerfitications and secret ➢ Remember three things to copy id's is cilent.id(Application id), client.secret(value of cerfitications and secret) and client.endpoint(tenant id) ➢ Give a API permission and add the permission is Azure blob storage, Azure data lake ➢ Once go back to data lake gen2 and give Access Control(IAM) and ➢ add Role assignment in that Give role is Storage blob data reader(for to read the data in databaricks) , ➢ add memebers give the App Name (when you create a Azure Activity) ➢ Create New Notebook and remember select the language is Scala for understanding purpose ➢ syntax: ➢ This is all id of datalake gen2 and azure active dictory for to load the data val configs = Map( \"fs.azure.account.auth.type\" -> \"OAuth\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type\" -> \"org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id\" -> \"b5e7b223-6ccb-4aab-86b2-fd9773f11fe1\", --(Application id) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret\" -> \"_KI8Q~FStvRvFTPguBS~wwAVthPsxQ4zy4sxndnX\", - --(value of certificate and secret) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint\" -> \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/dee06880-2584- 4c56-bf98-b4023454c861/oauth2/token\"); --- (tenant id) dbutils.fs.mount( -------------This is For Mount Purpose source = \"abfss://employee@storimgdatalakr.dfs.core.windows.net/\", mountPoint = \"/mnt/storingthedata\", extraConfigs = configs) Page | 19 %python df=spark.read.parquet(\"/mnt/storingthedata\") ---it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe %python display(df) -- it will the display dbutils.fs.unmount(\"/mnt/eclasessbatch112\") ----- it will unmount the data Delta Lake Delta lake it is like a Data Lake but in this Any changes in existing file it will store the data . ➢ schema enforcements ➢ Time travel /versions ➢ update ➢ delete ➢ merge create a Dataframe Syntax: df=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000),(200,'mani',2000),(300,'siva',3000)],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sa l\"]) df.show() df.write.format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") --- coverting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]).show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") -- it is to overwrite the data ---But it will not happend in Delta---------- df.write.format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") --- converting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]) df1.show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") – (it will get error so change the syntax) Page | 20 df1.write.mode(\"append\").format('delta').option(\"mergeschema\",True).save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingd elta/filedelta\") --- it will the update the table spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",timestampAsof=\"2022-06-21","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"MERGERS: It is a Comparing the table and dimension table if dimension table is not existiong the vale it","type":"concept","a":"will update , insert delete. create table customer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), price int) insert into customer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600),(4,'oiu',500) select * from customer create table dimcustomer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), Page | 46 price int) insert into dimcustomer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600) update customer set price=800 where price=400 delete customer where price=900 merge dimcustomer as target using customer as source on source.cno=target.cno when not matched by target then --- it will be insert insert(cno,cname,price) values(source.cno,source.cname,source.cno) ii ti will be update when matched and target.cno=2 then update set target.cname=source.cname, target.price=source.price when not matched by source then ---- it will delete . delete;","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"05 AM — it missed the window. This is late arriving data.","type":"concept","a":"• Strategy 1: Watermark with a Grace/Tolerance Period Add a buffer period to your WHERE clause to catch late data\n• Example — Pipeline picks up last 2 hours as buffer: -- Standard incremental load SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= '2024-01-15 00:00:00' AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' -- With 2-hour grace period to catch late arrivals SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= DATEADD(HOUR, -2, '2024-01-15 00:00:00') AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' Strategy 2: Upsert (Merge) Instead of Insert Only Instead of just inserting new rows, use MERGE so late data updates existing records without creating duplicates. -- Using MERGE in Azure Synapse / SQL MERGE INTO target_sales AS T USING source_sales AS S ON T.order_id = S.order_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET T.status = S.status, T.amount = S.amount WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, status, amount, created_at) VALUES (S.order_id, S.status, S.amount, S.created_at);\n• Strategy 3: Delta Lake (Databricks) — Time Travel Delta Lake keeps history of all changes\n• If late data arrives, you can reprocess a specific date partition. # Read Delta table as of a specific time (Time Travel) df = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('/mnt/sales/delta') # Upsert late data into Delta table Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 4 from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/sales/delta') deltaTable.alias('T').merge( late_df.alias('S'), 'T.order_id = S.order_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute() Strategy 4: Partition Reprocessing If late data arrives for an old date, re-run the pipeline only for that partition rather than the whole table. • Use ADF pipeline parameters to pass a specific date/partition • Overwrite only that partition in the target (e.g., ADLS partition: year=2024/month=01/day=15) • This avoids full reload and is much faster 💡 Tip: In an interview say: 'We handled late arriving data using a combination of a grace period watermark and MERGE/Upsert in Synapse\n• For Delta Lake, we used merge operations and partition reprocessing when needed.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How to handle incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• I implement incremental load using watermark columns like ModifiedDate or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table, and in each run I extract only new or updated records\n• Then I load data into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• A(cid:332)er successful load, I update the watermark to ensure no data duplica(cid:415)on","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If unchanged:","type":"concept","a":"o Do nothing Use Delta MERGE with update and insert logic.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A schema defines the structure of your data: column names, data types, and nullability\n• When new data arrives, it may or may not match the existing table schema\n• Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 4 Schema Enforcement (Default Behavior in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake validates all incoming data against the current table schema\n• If new data has extra columns or mismatched types — the write FAILS with a clear error\n• This is the DEFAULT — protects data quality in production. # Existing Delta table schema: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE # New data has an extra column 'discount' new_df = spark.createDataFrame( [(1001, 501, 99.99, 0.10)], ['order_id', 'customer_id', 'amount', 'discount'] ) # Schema Enforcement in action — this FAILS new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # AnalysisException: A schema mismatch detected when writing. # Column 'discount' is not defined in the table\n• When to enforce schema: • Production financial or compliance tables • When schema changes must go through a change control review • When you want the pipeline to fail loudly rather than silently corrupt data Schema Evolution (Opt-in in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake auto-updates the table schema when mergeSchema=true\n• New columns are added; existing columns are untouched\n• Old rows will show NULL for newly added columns. # Enable Schema Evolution — write succeeds, new column is added new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # Table schema is now: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE, discount DOUBLE # # Old rows: discount = NULL # New rows: discount = 0.10 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 5 # Enable globally at Spark session level: spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled','true') Comparison Table Aspect Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default? YES — Delta default No — opt-in with mergeSchema=true Extra column Write FAILS with error Column added automatically to arrives table Wrong data type Write FAILS Write FAILS (type mismatch not auto-evolved) Old rows for new N/A NULL value column Best for Prod, financial, compliance data Rapidly changing source systems Interview Tip: Say: 'Delta enforces schema by default to protect data quality\n• For sources that evolve their schema (add new columns), we enable mergeSchema=true\n• Data type changes always need a manual ALTER TABLE — Delta never auto-changes types.' Q5 Function to Insert Newly Arriving Data — MERGE / Upsert The Answer: Use MERGE (Upsert) When new data arrives, you typically need to: INSERT rows that do not exist yet, and UPDATE rows that already exist\n• The function for this is MERGE — also called Upsert (Update + Insert)\n• Delta Lake MERGE — Python API from delta.tables import DeltaTable # Existing Delta table (destination) delta_table = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/customers') # Incoming new data (source) new_data = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/raw/customers_today/') # MERGE: update matching rows, insert new rows Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 6 delta_table.alias('target').merge( new_data.alias('source'), 'target.customer_id = source.customer_id' # match key ) \\ .whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ # update all columns if key found .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ # insert full row if key not found .execute() print('MERGE complete — no duplicates, no data loss.') MERGE with SQL Syntax (Databricks SQL) MERGE INTO customers AS target USING new_customers AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND source.updated_at > target.updated_at THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = source.updated_at WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, source.created_at, source.updated_at) -- Optional: remove records deleted at source WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE Real-World Example: Daily Customer Incremental Load # Step 1: Read only changed records (incremental extract) new_customers = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM source_db.customers WHERE updated_at >= current_date() - INTERVAL 1 DAY ''') # Step 2: Merge into Delta dimension table delta_cust = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/dim_customers') delta_cust.alias('t').merge( new_customers.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdate(set={ 'name': 's.name', Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 7 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' )).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ 'customer_id': 's.customer_id', 'name': 's.name', 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'created_at': 's.created_at', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' }).execute() print('Incremental load complete.') Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'To insert newly arriving data into an existing Delta table, we use MERGE (upsert)","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Simple Definition A Delta Table is a table format built on Parquet files with an added transaction log (_delta_log)\n• It gives you ACID transactions, time travel, schema enforcement, and MERGE support\n• It is the standard table type in Databricks (open-source Delta Lake project)\n• Core Features Feature What It Means Example ACID Transactions All-or-nothing writes — no Pipeline fails mid-write? Table stays partial data clean Time Travel Query older versions of data SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 MERGE / Upsert Insert + Update in one atomic Daily incremental customer load step Schema Rejects wrong schema by Extra column fails the write Enforcement default Schema Evolution Auto-adds new columns (opt- mergeSchema=true in) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 8 OPTIMIZE Compacts small files Speeds up future reads automatically Z-Ordering Collocates related data in ZORDER BY date for fast range same files queries Streaming Support Works with Structured Real-time events land into Delta Streaming table How Delta Table Storage Works # Delta table = Parquet files + _delta_log folder # Folder structure on ADLS / S3: '/mnt/delta/sales/' ' _delta_log/' ' 00000000000000000000.json -- table created' ' 00000000000000000001.json -- first batch loaded' ' 00000000000000000002.json -- merge operation ran' ' 00000000000000000003.json -- OPTIMIZE ran' ' part-00000-abc123.snappy.parquet' ' part-00001-def456.snappy.parquet' ' ...' # Every write is an entry in _delta_log # This is what enables Time Travel and ACID Time Travel Examples # Read table as of 7 days ago (useful for debugging) df_old = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-08') \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # Read a specific version df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', 5) \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # View full history from delta.tables import DeltaTable dt = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/sales') dt.history(10).show(truncate=False) # SQL versions # SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-08' # SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 9 Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'A Delta Table is Parquet + a transaction log\n• The transaction log is what gives us ACID guarantees, time travel to query historical versions, and the ability to do MERGE operations\n• It is the foundation of the Lakehouse architecture in Databricks — data warehouse reliability with data lake scalability.' Q7 Pipeline Running Slowly — Causes and Fixes Step 1: Where to Look First • Databricks Spark UI: Job Run page -> Stages tab -> look for the slowest stage • ADF Monitor: Activity Runs -> check which activity consumes the most time • Compare to previous runs — if it was faster before, something changed Cause 1: Data Skew One partition has far more data than others\n• One task takes 30 minutes while all others finish in 2 minutes. # Symptom: Spark UI shows one task dramatically slower than others # Diagnose: check partition sizes from pyspark.sql.functions import spark_partition_id df.groupBy(spark_partition_id()).count().orderBy('count(1)',ascending=False).show() # Fix 1: Enable Adaptive Query Execution (Databricks default ON) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') # Fix 2: Salt the skewed join key from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, lit, rand SALT = 10 df_salted = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('customer_id'), lit('_'), (rand()*SALT).cast('int'))) Cause 2: Small File Problem Thousands of tiny Parquet files = thousands of file-open operations\n• Reads become very slow. # Diagnose: list files in Delta table folder files = dbutils.fs.ls('/mnt/delta/sales/') print(f'File count: {len(files)}') # > 1000 = problem # Fix: Run OPTIMIZE to compact small files into larger ones spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales`') Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 10 # Fix with Z-Ordering: collocate data by the most-queried column spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales` ZORDER BY (sale_date)') # Fix: Write fewer files by coalescing before write df.coalesce(20).write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/mnt/delta/sales') Cause 3: Missing Broadcast Join for Small Tables Default joins shuffle BOTH sides\n• If one table is small, broadcast it to avoid shuffle entirely. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Slow: both DataFrames shuffled df_result = df_large_sales.join(df_dim_region, 'region_id') # Fast: small table sent to all nodes — zero shuffle df_result = df_large_sales.join(broadcast(df_dim_region), 'region_id') # Raise auto-broadcast threshold from 10MB to 50MB spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', str(50*1024*1024)) Cause 4: Full Table Scan (No Partition Pruning) Reading the entire table when only a small date range is needed wastes time. # Slow: reads ALL partitions then filters in memory df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/') df_filtered = df.filter(col('sale_date') == '2024-01-15') # Fast: only reads the matching partition folder df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/year=2024/month=01/day=15/') # For Delta tables — use predicate pushdown in SQL queries df = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM sales WHERE sale_date = '2024-01-15' -- Spark prunes to this partition AND region = 'North' ''') Cause 5: Shuffle Partitions Too High or Too Low # Default spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200 # For 1GB data, 200 partitions = 5MB each — too many small tasks # Enable AQE to auto-tune (Databricks default) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') # AQE merges small shuffle partitions automatically Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 11 # Manual rule of thumb: aim for ~128MB per partition # 10 GB data / 128 MB = 80 partitions spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '80') Summary Checklist Problem Symptom Fix Data Skew 1 task much slower than AQE skewJoin + salting others Small Files Slow reads, many tiny files OPTIMIZE + ZORDER on Delta Missing broadcast Large shuffle for small broadcast() hint or raise threshold join lookup table Full table scan Reading GB/TB for MB Partition pruning, predicate pushdown result Too many shuffle 200 tiny tasks for small data Enable AQE or lower manually partitions Cluster too small Spill to disk, OOM errors Scale up memory, add more workers Interview Tip: Structure your answer: 'I open the Spark UI first to find which stage is slow and look for signs of skew, shuffle, or spill\n• The most common causes are data skew, small file problem, missing broadcast joins, or full table scans\n• I fix each systematically using AQE, OPTIMIZE, broadcast hints, and partition filters.' Q8 Python: First Occurrence of Values from a List with Duplicates Problem Given a list with duplicates, print or return only the FIRST occurrence of each value in order\n• Input: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 1: Using a Set (Most Common Interview Answer) # Method 1 — Set to track already-seen values # Time: O(n) | Space: O(n) | Order preserved: YES Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 12 def first_occurrences(lst): seen = set() # remembers values we have already added result = [] for item in lst: if item not in seen: result.append(item) seen.add(item) return result # Test data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] print(first_occurrences(data)) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 2: dict.fromkeys() — Python One-Liner # Method 2 — dict.fromkeys removes duplicates and preserves order # Python 3.7+ dicts are ordered by insertion data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = list(dict.fromkeys(data)) print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # How it works: # dict.fromkeys(data) => {3:None, 1:None, 4:None, 5:None, 9:None, 2:None, 6:None} # Keys are inherently unique and insertion-ordered # list() converts back to a plain list Method 3: Print Directly data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] seen = set() for item in data: if item not in seen: print(item, end=' ') seen.add(item) # Output: 3 1 4 5 9 2 6 Method 4: For Strings names = ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Alice', 'Charlie', 'Bob', 'David'] Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 13 seen = set() for name in names: if name not in seen: print(name) seen.add(name) # Output: # Alice # Bob # Charlie # David Method 5: Using pandas / PySpark (Data Engineering) # pandas import pandas as pd data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = pd.Series(data).drop_duplicates(keep='first').tolist() print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # PySpark — for large distributed datasets # df.dropDuplicates(['column_name']) # drops duplicate rows by column # df.dropDuplicates() # drops fully duplicate rows Complexity Comparison Method Time Space Order? Best For Set tracking (Method 1) O(n) O(n) Yes Most readable, interview default dict.fromkeys (Method O(n) O(n) Yes Shortest code — Python 3.7+ 2) pandas O(n) O(n) Yes DataFrames, data engineering drop_duplicates PySpark O(n) Low No Big data on cluster dropDuplicates dist. (unless sorted) Interview Tip: In interview: Start with Method 1 — explain the set-based logic step by step","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"CDC (Change Data Capture) and CDF (Change Data Feed)","type":"concept","a":"• CDC — Change Data Capture CDC is the process of identifying and capturing changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) made to a source database and propagating those changes to a target system — without doing a full reload every time\n• CDC Methods • Log-based CDC — reads the database transaction log (WAL/binlog)\n• Tools: Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, Oracle GoldenGate • Trigger-based CDC — DB triggers write change records to a shadow table • Timestamp-based CDC — query source using last_modified > watermark • Diff-based CDC — compare full snapshots to identify changes (slow, expensive) -- Timestamp-based CDC example (SQL Server / ADB) SELECT * FROM source_table WHERE last_modified_date > '2024-01-01 00:00:00' AND last_modified_date <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP CDF — Change Data Feed (Delta Lake Feature) CDF is a Delta Lake feature that tracks row-level changes (insert, update, delete, update_preimage) in a Delta table\n• It creates a built-in change log that downstream consumers can read incrementally\n• Enable and Use CDF -- Enable CDF on a Delta table ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Or at creation time CREATE TABLE my_table (id INT, name STRING) TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Read changes from version 5 onwards df = spark.read.format('delta') .option('readChangeFeed', 'true') .option('startingVersion', 5) .table('my_table') -- Columns added by CDF: -- _change_type: insert | update_preimage | update_postimage | delete -- _commit_version: Delta log version -- _commit_timestamp: When the change was committed Feature CDC CDF (Delta) Scope Source database changes Delta table row-level changes Granularity Row-level (I/U/D) Row-level with pre/post image for updates Setup External tool (Debezium) or DB ALTER TABLE + TBLPROPERTIES config Streaming Yes — Kafka/Event Hub Yes — readStream with readChangeFeed Use case Sync source DB to lakehouse Propagate Delta changes downstream Page 2 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Optimize Queries in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"1. Partitioning • Partition tables on low-cardinality filter columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning — too many small partitions increases metadata overhead df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save(path)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• Z-ORDER on high-cardinality columns used in WHERE clauses (customer_id, transaction_id) • Use Liquid Clustering for dynamic, incremental clustering without partition rewrites OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) -- compact + cluster VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- remove stale files","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Parquet Format vs JSON Format","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Feature Parquet JSON Storage layout Columnar — stores each column Row-based — stores each record together together Compression Snappy/GZIP — typically 75-90% Minimal — largely uncompressed smaller text Schema Embedded in file footer (strongly typed) No schema — inferred at read time Query speed 5-20x faster — column pruning + Slow — must read all fields per row predicate pushdown Predicate pushdown Yes — skips row groups outside filter Not supported range Splittable Yes — parallel reads Only with JSON Lines format Null handling Efficient native encoding String 'null' or missing key Nested types Supported natively (struct, array, map) Natural — JSON is inherently nested Human readable No — binary format Yes — text based Best for Analytics, large-scale processing, Delta APIs, config files, event messages Write speed Slower (encoding overhead) Faster (plain text write) Rule of thumb: Always convert landing-zone JSON/CSV files to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Parquet gives columnar compression and predicate pushdown\n• Delta adds ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement on top","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE + ZORDER — compact small Delta files and cluster data for file skipping • Liquid Clustering — dynamic incremental clustering; replaces partition + ZORDER • Photon Engine — vectorized C++ query engine; enable in cluster config for 3-10x speedup • Delta Caching — SSD-level caching of remote Parquet data on worker nodes • Auto Optimize — enable optimizeWrite and autoCompact for automatic file sizing • AQE — Adaptive Query Execution for runtime plan re-optimization • VACUUM — remove stale/orphan files to reduce storage scan overhead • Partition pruning — query only relevant partitions using WHERE on partition columns • Data skipping — Delta min/max stats skip irrelevant files automatically • Materialized Views — pre-compute expensive aggregations for fast dashboard reads -- Databricks-specific optimizations OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date); VACUUM transactions RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Auto Optimize properties ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' ); -- Liquid Clustering ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date);","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evaluation and Enforcement","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Enforcement (Schema Validation) Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects any writes where the incoming data does not match the existing table schema\n• It protects data quality by preventing corrupt or mismatched data from entering the table. • Throws an AnalysisException if new columns are added or data types mismatch • Enabled by default in Delta Lake Schema Evolution (mergeSchema) Schema evolution allows the table schema to be updated automatically when new columns are added to incoming data. # Allow new columns to be added automatically df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/table/') Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Feature Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default behaviour ON (strict) OFF (must opt-in) New columns in source Write fails Columns added automatically Data type mismatch Write fails Still fails (type safety) Use case Protect prod tables from bad Accommodate evolving source data schemas","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Tables Available in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Table Type Description Managed Table Both metadata AND data are managed by Databricks/Unity Catalog\n• Dropping the table deletes all data\n• External Table Only metadata is managed\n• Data resides at a specified external path\n• Dropping the table does NOT delete data\n• Delta Table Table backed by Delta Lake format\n• Supports ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement\n• Hive Metastore Table Legacy table registered in the Hive Metastore (available before Unity Catalog)\n• View A virtual table based on a query\n• No data stored; computed on-the-fly\n• Temporary View Session-scoped view that disappears when the session ends\n• Global Temporary View Cluster-scoped temp view; accessible across sessions but dropped when cluster restarts","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table? Advantages of Using Delta Table","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Table is a table stored in Delta Lake format — an open-source storage layer built on top of Parquet files with a transaction log (_delta_log) that brings ACID guarantees and advanced features to data lake storage\n• How Delta Table Works Internally • Data is stored as Parquet files in cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) • A transaction log directory (_delta_log) records every change as a JSON/Parquet checkpoint • Each write creates a new versioned set of Parquet files — old files are retained for time travel • Compaction (OPTIMIZE) merges small files into larger ones for query performance Advantages of Delta Table Feature Description ACID Transactions Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — concurrent reads/writes are safe Time Travel Query any previous version: SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 or TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' Schema Enforcement Rejects writes that don't match the table schema — prevents corrupt data Schema Evolution New columns can be added via mergeSchema without rewriting the table Upserts (MERGE) Efficient insert/update/delete using MERGE INTO — enables SCD and CDC patterns Unified Batch & Streaming Same Delta table can be written by a batch job and read as a stream simultaneously Scalable Metadata Z-ordering and data skipping speed up queries on large datasets OPTIMIZE & VACUUM OPTIMIZE compacts small files;\n• VACUUM removes old files saving storage costs Open Format Based on Parquet — readable by Spark, Hive, Presto, Trino, and other engines Useful Delta Table Commands -- Time travel SELECT * FROM employees VERSION AS OF 3;\n• SELECT * FROM employees TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-06-01'; -- Restore to previous version RESTORE TABLE employees TO VERSION AS OF 5;\n• Page 6 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 -- Compact small files OPTIMIZE employees ZORDER BY (department_id); -- Remove files older than 7 days VACUUM employees RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- View history DESCRIBE HISTORY employees;\n• Page 7 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"How to Build a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"• A fault-tolerant ETL pipeline continues to operate correctly even when individual components fail, ensuring no data loss, no duplicate processing, and automatic recovery — without manual intervention\n• Key Principles of Fault Tolerance Principle Implementation Idempotency Re-running the pipeline produces the same result — no duplicates, no data loss Exactly-Once Processing Each record processed exactly once using checkpoints and unique keys Checkpointing Stream state saved to durable storage so recovery resumes from last checkpoint Retry Logic Automatic retries on transient failures (network, API timeout, cluster preemption) Dead Letter Queue Bad records quarantined separately for later inspection instead of failing the job Monitoring & Alerting Proactive alerts on failures, SLA breaches, and data quality violations Schema Validation Reject or quarantine records that violate schema before writing to target ACID Writes Use Delta Lake MERGE for upserts — safe for partial failures and re-runs Architecture of a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline • Layer 1 – Ingest: Autoloader with checkpoints ensures no file is missed or processed twice • Layer 2 – Bronze: Raw Delta table written with ACID guarantees; idempotent via COPY INTO • Layer 3 – Silver: Cleansing + MERGE INTO Delta for upserts; bad records to quarantine table • Layer 4 – Gold: Aggregation; only runs after Silver passes data quality Expectations • Layer 5 – Orchestration: Databricks Workflows with retry policies and failure alerts Full PySpark Fault-Tolerant Pipeline Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F from delta.tables import DeltaTable import logging # ── CONFIG ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── SOURCE_PATH = '/mnt/landing/employees/' SCHEMA_PATH = '/mnt/schema/employees/' CHECKPOINT_PATH = '/mnt/checkpoint/employees/' BRONZE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/bronze/employees/' SILVER_PATH = '/mnt/delta/silver/employees/' QUARANTINE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/quarantine/employees/' Page 17 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # ── STEP 1: INGEST with Autoloader (Fault-Tolerant Streaming) ─── df_raw = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', SCHEMA_PATH) .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true') .load(SOURCE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 2: WRITE TO BRONZE (Idempotent via checkpoint) ───────── (df_raw.writeStream .format('delta') .outputMode('append') .option('checkpointLocation', CHECKPOINT_PATH + 'bronze/') .trigger(availableNow=True) # process all pending files once .start(BRONZE_PATH) .awaitTermination() ) # ── STEP 3: CLEANSE + QUARANTINE BAD RECORDS ──────────────────── df_bronze = spark.read.format('delta').load(BRONZE_PATH) df_valid = df_bronze.filter( F.col('emp_id').isNotNull() & F.col('salary').cast('integer').isNotNull() & (F.col('salary').cast('integer') > 0) ) df_invalid = df_bronze.subtract(df_valid) # Write bad records to quarantine for investigation (df_invalid .withColumn('quarantine_reason', F.lit('Failed validation: null emp_id or invalid salary')) .withColumn('quarantine_ts', F.current_timestamp()) .write.format('delta').mode('append').save(QUARANTINE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 4: UPSERT TO SILVER (Idempotent MERGE) ───────────────── df_clean = (df_valid .dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) .withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) .withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) .withColumn('updated_at', F.current_timestamp()) ) if DeltaTable.isDeltaTable(spark, SILVER_PATH): silver = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, SILVER_PATH) (silver.alias('tgt') .merge(df_clean.alias('src'), 'tgt.emp_id = src.emp_id') .whenMatchedUpdateAll() .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() .execute() Page 18 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 ) else: df_clean.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save(SILVER_PATH) # ── STEP 5: GOLD AGGREGATION ──────────────────────────────────── df_silver = spark.read.format('delta').load(SILVER_PATH) df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('department').agg( F.count('emp_id').alias('headcount'), F.avg('salary').alias('avg_salary'), F.max('salary').alias('max_salary') ) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/mnt/delta/gold/dept_summary /') print('Pipeline completed successfully.') Retry & Alerting in Databricks Workflows Feature How to Configure Job Retries Set Max Retries = 3 and Retry Interval on each task in Databricks Workflows UI Depends On Task B runs only if Task A succeeds — dependency chain stops cascade failures On Failure Alerting Configure email/Slack/Teams alerts in Workflows notification settings Timeout Set per-task timeout to detect hung jobs and trigger retry/failure handling Run If Condition Conditional task execution based on upstream task outcome (Success/Failed/All) DLT Expectations @dlt.expect_or_quarantine() routes bad records without failing the pipeline Summary Checklist for Fault-Tolerant ETL • Use Autoloader with checkpointing for idempotent file ingestion • Write all layers as Delta tables for ACID guarantees • Use MERGE INTO for upserts — safe to re-run without duplicates • Separate valid vs invalid records — quarantine bad data instead of failing • Validate schema and data quality at Silver layer before Gold • Configure retry policies and timeout in Databricks Workflows • Set up monitoring alerts on job failure, SLA breach, and quarantine record spike • Log pipeline metrics (record counts, error counts, durations) to a monitoring table • Test failure scenarios: missing file, schema change, duplicate keys, corrupt data Page 19","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"While migrating the data, did you just copy the data or did you use incremental","type":"concept","a":"• load? We used a combination of both approaches: Full Load (Initial Load): • For the first migration, we did a full historical load — extracting all records from the source and loading them into the Bronze layer as-is. • This was a one-time bulk load to establish the baseline dataset\n• Incremental Load (Ongoing Sync): • After the initial load, we switched to incremental loads to capture only new or changed records. • We used watermark columns (e.g., updated_at, created_date) or Change Data Capture (CDC) to identify changed rows. • In Databricks, we used Auto Loader for file-based incremental ingestion and MERGE INTO (Delta Lake) for upsert operations\n• This approach minimized load time, reduced compute costs, and kept the target data fresh without full re- processing\n• Section 2: Architecture Concepts","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"metadata stored?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Format: • By default, tables created in Databricks are stored in Delta Lake format (.parquet files + _delta_log transaction log). • Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that brings ACID transactions to data lakes. • Physically, data is stored as columnar Parquet files on cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS)\n• Metadata Storage: • Delta Transaction Log (_delta_log): JSON files stored alongside data files in the table directory\n• Records every operation (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE) — used for time travel and ACID guarantees. • Hive Metastore / Unity Catalog: Table-level metadata (table name, schema, location, partitioning) is stored in the Databricks Metastore (by default, a managed Hive Metastore or Unity Catalog in newer workspaces)\n• So metadata lives in two places: the _delta_log for data-level history and the Metastore for table-level schema/location information","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is VACUUM and when do we use it?","type":"concept","a":"• VACUUM is a Delta Lake command that removes old data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta transaction log\n• Why Vacuum is Needed: • Delta Lake's ACID support and time travel retain old file versions, which accumulate over time and consume storage. • VACUUM cleans up these obsolete files to reclaim storage space\n• Syntax: VACUUM table_name RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Default: 7 days -- Or in PySpark: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forName(spark, 'table_name') deltaTable.vacuum(168) # hours Important Notes: • Default retention period is 7 days (168 hours)\n• Setting it lower than 7 days requires disabling a safety check. • After VACUUM, you cannot time travel to versions older than the retention period. • Use it periodically in production to control storage costs\n• Section 4: Performance Optimization","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Is MERGE SCD-1 or SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• MERGE is primarily SCD-1 by default behavior. • SCD-1 (Overwrite): MERGE with WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE overwrites existing records — no history is kept. • SCD-2 (History): MERGE can implement SCD-2 but requires a more complex query — on match, you UPDATE the old row (close it) and INSERT a new row with the changed values\n• In Delta Lake, you can implement SCD-2 using MERGE with both WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE and WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT clauses, combined with is_current and date columns\n• Section 7: PySpark Coding","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What will be SCD2 logic and columns you will be using for your project. How will you","type":"concept","a":"implement? SCD Type 2 Logic + Columns Columns used:  business_key (emp_id)  start_date  end_date  is_ac(cid:415)ve  hash_diff (for change detec(cid:415)on) Logic:  If record changes → expire old record (end_date + is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  Insert new record with updated values  Maintain history Implementa(cid:415)on:  Delta MERGE INTO OR DLT apply_changes","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed?","type":"concept","a":"• Change Data Feed (CDF)  Tracks row-level changes in Delta tables  Captures INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE Used for:  incremental pipelines  CDC processing 7\n• Auto Loader  Incremental file inges(cid:415)on in Databricks  Automa(cid:415)cally detects new files  Handles schema evolu(cid:415)on Best for streaming inges(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore the table if data is corrupted?","type":"concept","a":"Restore corrupted table Using Delta Time Travel: RESTORE TABLE table_name TO VERSION AS OF 5;","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the use of SQL Warehouse in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Warehouse (previously called SQL Analytics Endpoint) is a serverless/dedicated compute resource optimized for running SQL queries on data stored in the Lakehouse (Delta Lake). • Enables BI tools (Tableau, Power BI) to connect via JDBC/ODBC. • Supports auto-stop and auto-scaling to control costs. • Serverless option eliminates cluster management; compute spins up in seconds. • Photon engine (vectorized query processing) dramatically speeds up SQL workloads\n• Change Data Feed (CDF)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is CDF (Change Data Feed) in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF is a Delta Lake feature that records row-level changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to a Delta table, producing a changelog that can be consumed by downstream processes. • Enable with: ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true) • Read changes: spark.readStream.format('delta').option('readChangeFeed','true').table('my_table') • Use cases: CDC pipelines, incremental ETL, audit trails, real-time aggregations. • Change types exposed: insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete\n• Efficient Model for Daily Streaming Data","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"We are getting streaming data daily — how do you design an efficient model for it?","type":"concept","a":"• Recommended architecture: Medallion (Bronze → Silver → Gold) with Delta Lake + Structured Streaming. • Bronze layer: Ingest raw streaming data with Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — schema evolution, exactly-once, checkpointing. • Silver layer: Parse, clean, deduplicate (use MERGE INTO for upserts via CDF or watermarking). • Gold layer: Aggregated, business-ready tables updated incrementally. • Use trigger(availableNow=True) for micro-batch on schedule, or trigger(processingTime='X seconds') for near-real-time. • Partition Delta tables by date (year/month/day) to enable partition pruning on queries. • Enable optimize & ZORDER on high-cardinality filter columns. • Monitor with Spark UI, Delta transaction log, and Databricks cluster metrics\n• Resolving Data Skew","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write the code for SCD2","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 Code (PySpark / Databricks) (cid:44934) Using Delta Lake MERGE INTO (most asked in interviews) MERGE INTO target_table t USING source_table s ON t.emp_id = s.emp_id AND t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = true WHEN MATCHED AND ( t.salary <> s.salary OR t.dept_id <> s.dept_id ) THEN UPDATE SET t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = false, t.end_date = current_date() WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, start_date, end_date, is_ac(cid:415)ve ) VALUES ( s.emp_id, s.name, s.dept_id, s.salary, current_date(), null, true ); (cid:44934) Key idea:  Old record → expired (is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  New record → inserted with new version  History preserved","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on strategies in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"Common answers:  Cache frequently used DataFrames  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Broadcast Join  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE)  Delta Cache  OPTIMIZE  ZORDER  Liquid Clustering  Predicate Pushdown  Column Pruning  Avoid UDFs  Auto Op(cid:415)mize","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, ZORDER and Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE ZORDER Liquid Clustering Compacts small files Organizes data by column values Automa(cid:415)cally manages clustering Improves file size Improves data skipping No manual par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning Reduces file count Faster filtering Be(cid:425)er for evolving workloads Doesn't sort Sorts within files Re-clusters automa(cid:415)cally Example: OPTIMIZE 1000 small files ↓ 10 large files ZORDER OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY(customer_id) Improves filtering: WHERE customer_id=100 Liquid Clustering No par(cid:415)(cid:415)on maintenance\n• Databricks automa(cid:415)cally reclusters data","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Data Lake and Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• Data Lake Delta Lake Storage layer Storage layer + transac(cid:415)on layer No ACID ACID transac(cid:415)ons No versioning Time Travel No schema enforcement Schema enforcement & evolu(cid:415)on Slow updates MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE No transac(cid:415)on log _delta_log maintains metadata Interview Answer \"A Data Lake stores files such as Parquet, CSV, or JSON, whereas Delta Lake builds on top of a Data Lake by adding ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, and a transac(cid:415)on log\n• Delta Lake makes lake storage more reliable for produc(cid:415)on data engineering.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Managed table deleted a(cid:332)er reten(cid:415)on and all files removed. How do you recreate it?","type":"concept","a":"• If:  Managed table is dropped  Reten(cid:415)on period has passed  Underlying data files are permanently removed  All versions are gone You cannot recover it using Delta Lake features\n• Time Travel and RESTORE depend on the transac(cid:415)on log and data files s(cid:415)ll exis(cid:415)ng\n• Recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from backup  Reload from the source system  Recover using cloud storage versioning (if enabled)  Use disaster recovery copies Interview Answer \"If both the Delta log and data files have been permanently deleted a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period, the table cannot be recreated from Delta metadata alone\n• Recovery would require restoring from backup, cloud storage versioning, or reloading the data from the source.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — File Compaction Delta tables accumulate many small files over time\n• OPTIMIZE compacts them into larger Parquet files for faster reads\n• OPTIMIZE my_table OPTIMIZE my_table WHERE date = '2024-01-01' -- with partition filter Z-ORDER — Co-locate Related Data Z-ORDER clusters related data within the same files\n• Queries on Z-ordered columns can skip irrelevant files automatically (data skipping)\n• OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_date) Feature OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Purpose Compact small files Cluster data by column values Benefit Faster scans, less overhead Data skipping on filter queries Best For After many small writes/streaming High-cardinality filter columns Used Together Yes — in a single OPTIMIZE ZORDER BY command Page 3 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide Best Z-ORDER columns: those appearing frequently in WHERE clauses with high cardinality (customer_id, event_timestamp)\n• Avoid low-cardinality columns like boolean flags","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize Delta Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partition on low-cardinality columns used in filters\n• Avoid over-partitioning. df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year', 'month').save(path)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + ZORDER","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"VACUUM — Remove Stale Files","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- 7 days default","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Optimize (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' )","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Lake vs Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"Feature Data Lake (Plain) Delta Lake ACID Transactions No Yes — full ACID support Schema Enforcement No Yes — schema-on-write Time Travel No Yes — VERSION AS OF Upserts / Deletes Complex or not supported Yes — MERGE INTO Streaming + Batch Separate pipelines needed Unified — same table File Format Parquet / CSV / JSON Parquet + _delta_log Data Quality Manual checks required Built-in constraints Key Delta Lake Commands -- Time Travel SELECT * FROM my_table VERSION AS OF 5 SELECT * FROM my_table TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' -- MERGE (Upsert) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Live Table?","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Live Table (DLT) is a managed ETL framework in Databricks used to build reliable data pipelines\n• Instead of manually crea(cid:415)ng jobs, DLT:  Reads data  Applies transforma(cid:415)ons  Loads data  Handles dependencies  Monitors pipeline health  Enforces data quality rules Example: CREATE OR REFRESH LIVE TABLE sales_clean AS SELECT * FROM LIVE.sales_raw WHERE amount > 0;\n• Data Quality in Delta Live Tables One of the biggest advantages is built-in expecta(cid:415)ons\n• Example: CONSTRAINT valid_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW;\n• This automa(cid:415)cally removes invalid records from the pipeline\n• Interview Answer If the interviewer asks: \"What is the difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table?\" \"A Delta Table is a storage layer based on Delta Lake that provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and (cid:415)me travel\n• A Delta Live Table is a managed ETL framework that builds and orchestrates data pipelines using Delta Tables\n• DLT adds features like automa(cid:415)c dependency management, scheduling, monitoring, lineage, and built-in data quality expecta(cid:415)ons, whereas with Delta Tables these aspects are managed manually.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"MERGE with UPDATE gives a duplicate rows error. What is the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• \"This usually means that the MERGE condi(cid:415)on is matching mul(cid:415)ple source rows to the same target row\n• Common causes are:  Duplicate business keys in the source.  Duplicate records in the target.  An incorrect ON condi(cid:415)on that is not unique\n• The solu(cid:415)on is to remove duplicates using ROW_NUMBER() or ensure the join key is unique before execu(cid:415)ng the MERGE.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Databricks job fails a(cid:332)er 5 minutes, what happens in Parquet and Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Parquet \"Parquet files do not support transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails while wri(cid:415)ng, par(cid:415)ally wri(cid:425)en files may remain in the storage loca(cid:415)on, requiring manual cleanup.\" Delta \"Delta Lake provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails before the transac(cid:415)on commits, the Delta transac(cid:415)on log is not updated, and readers con(cid:415)nue to see the previous consistent version\n• This prevents par(cid:415)al or corrupted data from being visible.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If ADLS storage is full, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would verify whether the issue is storage capacity, account limits, or quotas\n• Then I would:  Remove temporary or intermediate files.  Run VACUUM on Delta tables to delete obsolete files a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period.  Archive historical data to a lower-cost storage (cid:415)er.  Delete unused datasets a(cid:332)er confirming reten(cid:415)on policies.  Review lifecycle management rules to automa(cid:415)cally move or delete old data.  If business growth requires it, increase storage capacity or distribute data across addi(cid:415)onal storage accounts.\" Round 3 : (Client Round)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Files Do You Usually Work With?","type":"concept","a":"• CSV — legacy systems, flat files, exports from operational systems • JSON — API responses, event streams, semi-structured data • Parquet — preferred columnar format for analytics; compressed, schema-embedded • Delta — Parquet + transaction log; used for all curated/processed layers • Avro — schema-embedded binary format; common in Kafka/streaming pipelines • ORC — columnar format used in Hive-based ecosystems • XML — legacy enterprise and EDI data sources Best Practice: Always convert raw CSV/JSON to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer. Parquet provides columnar compression and predicate pushdown. Delta adds ACID transactions and time travel. Section 2 | Spark Joins & Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"In a Transaction Table, Which Column Would You Use for Z-Ordering?","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Choose columns that are: (a) frequently used in WHERE clauses, and (b) have high cardinality. • transaction_date / event_timestamp — most queries filter by date range • customer_id — if queries frequently look up a specific customer • store_id / region_id — if queries filter by location Best Answer: \"I would Z-ORDER on transaction_date combined with customer_id\n• Most business queries filter by a date range AND a specific customer, so co-locating transactions for the same customer within the same date files maximizes file skipping.\" Avoid Z-ORDERing on low-cardinality columns like status (Active/Inactive) or boolean flags — data skipping will be minimal since files will contain both values anyway","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Default Column for Z-Order Indexing?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Lake has NO default Z-ORDER column\n• Z-ORDER must always be explicitly specified: OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date) OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date, customer_id) However, Delta Lake automatically collects min/max statistics for the first 32 columns\n• These enable automatic data skipping (file-level pruning) even without explicit Z-ORDER\n• You can control this: ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.dataSkippingNumIndexedCols' = '5' -- collect stats for first 5 columns only )","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Z-Order Indexes","type":"concept","a":"• Z-Ordering (Z-curve/space-filling curve) is a multi-dimensional data clustering technique that maps multi- column data to a single linear order while preserving proximity — values close in N-dimensional space end up in the same files\n• How it works step-by-step • OPTIMIZE interleaves the bit representations of selected column values using a Z-curve algorithm • Related rows (same customer, same date) are co-located into the same Parquet files • Delta records min/max statistics per file for each column • When you query with WHERE clause on Z-ORDERed columns, Spark compares query predicates against file stats • Files whose min/max range does not include the query value are skipped entirely (data skipping) Aspect Partitioning Z-ORDER Best Together Cardinality Low (date, region) High (customer_id) Both Structure Physical folders per Co-locates within files Partition + ZORDER value Over-partition risk Yes — too many small No Partition on date files Changes needed Hard to change after Re-run OPTIMIZE Combine both creation Command partitionBy(col) OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER Same table, both applied BY -- Best practice: partition by date + Z-order by high-cardinality column Page 4 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save('/delta/transactions') OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_type)","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Liquid Clustering (introduced in Databricks Runtime 13.3+) is a dynamic data layout feature that replaces the need for partitioning + Z-ORDER\n• It automatically reorganizes data based on clustering columns without requiring manual partition planning or full table rewrites\n• Key advantages • No upfront partition column planning — clustering columns can be changed anytime with ALTER TABLE • Incremental: only newly written data is clustered — no full table rewrites Page 7 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Avoids partition explosion from high-cardinality columns • Auto-clustering runs in the background (Databricks-managed) -- Create table with Liquid Clustering CREATE TABLE transactions (id BIGINT, customer_id STRING, amount DOUBLE, txn_date DATE) CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date); -- Change clustering columns anytime ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id); -- Manually trigger clustering OPTIMIZE transactions;\n• Feature Partitioning Z-ORDER Liquid Clustering Cardinality Low only High Any Column flexibility Hard to change Re-run OPTIMIZE Change anytime Incremental No No Yes File management Manual VACUUM Manual OPTIMIZE Automatic Partition explosion Yes No No","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What are Materialized Views?","type":"concept","a":"• A Materialized View (MV) is a view whose results are pre-computed and stored as a physical Delta table\n• Unlike standard views that re-run the query on every read, MVs read from the stored result — fast and low-cost at query time. • Results stored physically — reads are as fast as a regular Delta table scan • Auto-refreshed when source tables change (Unity Catalog manages this) • Supports incremental refresh — only processes changed/new data, not full recompute • Ideal for expensive aggregations, multi-table joins, and frequently-queried reports Page 8 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales AS SELECT txn_date, store_id, SUM(amount) AS total_sales, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY txn_date, store_id;\n• REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales;\n• Aspect Standard View Materialized View Data stored No Yes — physical Delta table Query speed Depends on source tables Fast — reads pre-computed result Freshness Always up-to-date Refreshed on schedule or source change Cost at query time Full query recomputed Just a Delta table scan Best use case Simple aliasing, simple selects Heavy aggregations, dashboards, reports Section 7 | File Ingestion & Auto Loader","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"CSV Files Contain Corrupted Rows with Extra Columns — How Do You Handle","type":"concept","a":"• Them? Spark provides three read modes for handling malformed records in CSV/JSON: Mode 1 — PERMISSIVE (default, recommended for production) Null-fills mismatched columns and captures the raw corrupt record in a special column. df = (spark.read .option('mode', 'PERMISSIVE') .option('columnNameOfCorruptRecord', '_corrupt_record') .schema(defined_schema) # schema required to use _corrupt_record .csv('/data/raw/*.csv') ) good_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNull()) bad_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNotNull()) # Write bad records to quarantine Delta table Page 11 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) bad_df.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/delta/quarantine/csv_errors') Mode 2 — DROPMALFORMED (silent drop) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED').csv('/data/*.csv') # Bad rows silently discarded — use only if data loss is acceptable Mode 3 — FAILFAST (dev/test only) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'FAILFAST').csv('/data/*.csv') # Throws exception on first bad row — useful during development Mode 4 — Delta Live Tables Expectations (modern approach) @dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_row_count', 'col_count = expected') @dlt.expect_or_quarantine('no_null_id', 'id IS NOT NULL') Best Practice: Use PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + write bad rows to a quarantine Delta table\n• This keeps all data visible (no silent losses), enables root-cause analysis, and supports reprocessing after the source issue is fixed\n• Section 9 | Architecture & Cluster","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you load incremental data if the source has no Last Modified (cid:415)mestamp?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the source does not have a Last Modified or watermark column, there are several approaches depending on the business scenario:  Use the primary key and compare source and target records.  Perform a Delta MERGE to insert new records and update changed records.  Use Change Data Capture (CDC) if supported by the source system.  Generate a hash of the business columns and compare hashes to detect changes.  As a last resort, perform a full load if no reliable change detec(cid:415)on mechanism exists\n• In my projects, using a business key with hash comparison and a Delta MERGE has been an effec(cid:415)ve approach.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Apply Changes in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Yes — using:  Delta Live Tables (DLT) APPLY CHANGES  Or manually using MERGE INTO Used for:  SCD handling (Type 1/2)  CDC inges(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Pipeline re-run: overwrite or mul(cid:415)ple files? Delta log?","type":"concept","a":"Behavior:  In ADLS: o Usually creates mul(cid:415)ple part files  In Delta Lake: o Uses transac(cid:415)on log (_delta_log) Delta Log:  Stores metadata of all transac(cid:415)ons  Enables: o Time travel o ACID compliance o Rollback Format:  Parquet + _delta_log (JSON logs)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks components used","type":"concept","a":" Notebooks  Jobs  Clusters  Delta Lake  DBFS  Repos  Workflows","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize Delta tables (OPTIMIZE, ZORDER).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"Common methods:  Watermark column (LastModifiedDate)  Timestamp  CDC (Change Data Capture)  Delta MERGE Example: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE LastModifiedDate > ( SELECT MAX(LastLoadedDate) FROM AuditTable );","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Data format in your project","type":"concept","a":"Typical formats:  CSV (landing)  JSON (APIs)  Parquet (op(cid:415)mized storage)  Delta (curated layers) Interview (cid:415)p: \"We primarily store curated data in Delta format because it provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and be(cid:425)er performance.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What are Delta Tables?","type":"concept","a":"Delta tables are built on top of Parquet and provide:  ACID transac(cid:415)ons  Time Travel  Schema Enforcement  Schema Evolu(cid:415)on  MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE support  Be(cid:425)er performance with OPTIMIZE and ZORDER","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Write a MERGE statement.","type":"concept","a":"MERGE INTO Target t USING Source s ON t.EmpID = s.EmpID WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.Name = s.Name, t.Salary = s.Salary WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( EmpID, Name, Salary ) VALUES ( s.EmpID, s.Name, s.Salary );","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental Loading loads only new or changed data since the last load, instead of reloading everything (full load)\n• Common Approaches: • Timestamp-based: Load records where updated_at > last_run_timestamp • Watermark-based: Track max ID or timestamp as high watermark • CDC (Change Data Capture): Capture inserts/updates/deletes from source • Delta Lake MERGE (UPSERT): Merge changes into target table Benefits: Faster, cheaper, reduces source load # PySpark incremental load using watermark last_load_time = '2025-01-01 00:00:00' incremental_df = spark.read.jdbc(...) \\ .filter(col('updated_at') > last_load_time) # Delta MERGE (UPSERT) from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/delta/customers') target.alias('t').merge( incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE vs Z-ORDER","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE: Compacts small Delta files into larger files to improve read performance and reduce file count\n• OPTIMIZE delta.`/delta/sales` -- or OPTIMIZE sales_table Z-ORDER: Co-locates related data in the same set of files by reordering data based on specified columns\n• Best for high-cardinality columns used in filters\n• OPTIMIZE sales_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, order_date); -- Difference: -- OPTIMIZE alone: compacts files (smaller → bigger) -- ZORDER: compacts AND reorders data within files for faster skipping • OPTIMIZE: Use always for compacting small files • ZORDER: Use on frequently filtered/joined columns • Together: Maximum benefit — compact + co-locate","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"High Concurrency Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Optimized for multiple concurrent users • Supports Python, SQL, R — NOT Scala • Enforces user isolation (each user's code is isolated) • Best for BI tools and SQL analytics","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evolution","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Evolution allows changes to a Delta table's schema (adding/modifying columns) without breaking existing pipelines\n• Options: • mergeSchema: Add new columns from incoming data • overwriteSchema: Replace the entire schema (use with caution) # Enable schema evolution on write df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/delta/customers') # In SQL - enable auto merge SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true; # Alter table to add column manually ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN (loyalty_tier STRING);","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Transaction Log & ACID Properties","type":"concept","a":"• Transaction Log (_delta_log): The Delta transaction log is a directory of JSON files that records every change made to a Delta table\n• Each commit produces a new JSON file\n• ACID Properties in Delta: • Atomicity: Each transaction either fully succeeds or fully fails — no partial writes • Consistency: Schema enforcement ensures valid data at all times • Isolation: Concurrent reads/writes are isolated — snapshot isolation by default • Durability: Once committed, data is permanently stored even after failure # View transaction log DESCRIBE HISTORY orders_table; # Each JSON in _delta_log contains: # - add: files added # - remove: files removed/deleted # - metaData: schema changes # - protocol: reader/writer versions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Move Data from Delta Table to Another Location","type":"concept","a":"# Option 1: Write to new Delta location df = spark.read.format('delta').load('/delta/source_table') df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/delta/destination_table') # Option 2: DEEP CLONE (preserves history & metadata) CREATE TABLE destination_table DEEP CLONE source_table LOCATION '/delta/destination_table'; # Option 3: SHALLOW CLONE (references original files) CREATE TABLE destination_table SHALLOW CLONE source_table; # Option 4: Export to CSV/Parquet df.write.format('parquet').save('/output/parquet_export') df.write.format('csv').option('header', 'true').save('/output/csv_export')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental load","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load is a technique where we load only new or updated data instead of full refresh\n• I implement it using watermark columns like last_updated or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table and extract only delta data in each run\n• Then I load it into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• Finally, I update the watermark a(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on to ensure no duplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"What are the few small differences you see in databricks now comparing with 3 years","type":"concept","a":"back (only small differences not like unity catalog) “Over the last few years, Databricks has improved mainly in performance, automa(cid:415)on, and governance rather than just core func(cid:415)onality.” (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Key small differences: (cid:48132) a) Performance improvements  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) is much be(cid:425)er now  Faster shuffle handling  Be(cid:425)er auto-op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on of joins (cid:48131) b) Photon engine maturity  Much faster SQL execu(cid:415)on compared to earlier versions  Be(cid:425)er vectorized processing (cid:44931) c) Auto op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on features  Auto compac(cid:415)on of small files  Improved OPTIMIZE + ZORDER efficiency (cid:48130) d) Improved job orchestra(cid:415)on  Be(cid:425)er Workflows UI  Retry policies, task dependencies improved (cid:48133) e) Be(cid:425)er Delta Lake stability  More stable MERGE opera(cid:415)ons  Be(cid:425)er schema evolu(cid:415)on handling","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"“Yes, I have used both OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on environments in Delta Lake tables.” (cid:48132) OPTIMIZE  Used to compact small files  Improves read performance OPTIMIZE table_name; (cid:48131) ZORDER (with OPTIMIZE)  Improves query filtering OPTIMIZE table_name ZORDER BY (customer_id); (cid:44931) VACUUM  Removes old/unnecessary files VACUUM table_name; “We ensure reten(cid:415)on period is respected before running VACUUM to avoid dele(cid:415)ng files needed for (cid:415)me travel.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"pyspark","name":"PySpark & Coding","icon":"🐍","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the difference between transformations and actions in PySpark.","type":"concept","a":"• Transformations — lazy; build a new DataFrame/plan. Narrow: select, filter, withColumn, union. Wide (shuffle): groupBy, join, distinct, repartition, orderBy.\n• Actions — eager; trigger execution and return results/driver data or write output: count, show, collect, take, write, save, first.\nOnly actions trigger computation; the physical plan is optimized by Catalyst before execution."},{"q":"What is the difference between Python UDFs and Spark built-in functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Built-in functions (pyspark.sql.functions) run natively in the JVM → Catalyst can optimize them, and they work with Tungsten/Photon.\n• Python UDFs run row-by-row, serialize data between JVM and Python → large overhead, can't be optimized by Catalyst, slower.\nPrefer built-ins; when Python logic is unavoidable use pandas UDFs (vectorized, Arrow-based) which are much faster.\nRegister: @udf(StringType()) or F.udf(f, StringType())."},{"q":"Explain window functions in PySpark/SQL with an example.","type":"concept","a":"Window functions compute values across a set of related rows without collapsing them.\n• Ranking: row_number(), rank(), dense_rank().\n• Analytic: lead()/lag() for previous/next rows, running totals with sum() over unbounded window.\nSpec = partitionBy + orderBy (+ rowsBetween).\nUse case: find the latest/max record per group, top-N per category, running totals, month-over-month change.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter('rn = 1').show()"},{"q":"Difference between ROW_NUMBER, RANK, and DENSE_RANK?","type":"concept","a":"• ROW_NUMBER — unique sequential number; ties broken arbitrarily; no gaps.\n• RANK — same rank for ties, then SKIPS numbers (1,2,2,4).\n• DENSE_RANK — same rank for ties, NO gaps (1,2,2,3).\nFor 'top N per group' or finding the Nth salary, DENSE_RANK is usually the correct choice; ROW_NUMBER when you need exactly one row."},{"q":"CODING: Find the highest salary per designation (PySpark).","type":"coding","a":"Use a window ranked by salary descending per designation and filter rank=1. Two common approaches below.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank, max as spark_max\n\n# Method 1: window\nw = Window.partitionBy('designation').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rnk', rank().over(w)).filter(col('rnk')==1).show()\n\n# Method 2: self-join with max\nmx = df.groupBy('designation').agg(spark_max('salary').alias('m'))\ndf.join(mx, (df.designation==mx.designation) & (df.salary==mx.m)).select(df['*']).show()"},{"q":"CODING: Find the second highest salary by department.","type":"coding","a":"dense_rank() = 2 per department. Subquery/self-join alternative also works.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rnk', dense_rank().over(w)).filter(col('rnk')==2).select('dept','name','salary').show()"},{"q":"CODING: Find patients undergoing more than 2 different treatment types.","type":"coding","a":"Group by patient id, count DISTINCT treatments, filter > 2.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import countDistinct, col\nresult = (df.groupBy('pid')\n            .agg(countDistinct('treatment').alias('treatment_count'))\n            .filter(col('treatment_count') > 2)\n            .select('pid'))\nresult.show()"},{"q":"CODING: Get the manager of each employee (self join, handle null manager).","type":"coding","a":"Alias the employee table twice; LEFT join employee to its manager so top-level employees (mgrid null) still appear.","code":"emp = df.alias('emp')\nmgr = df.alias('mgr')\nresult = (emp.join(mgr, col('emp.mgrid')==col('mgr.empid'), 'left')\n            .select(col('emp.empid').alias('employee_id'),\n                    col('emp.empname').alias('employee_name'),\n                    col('emp.mgrid').alias('manager_id'),\n                    col('mgr.empname').alias('manager_name')))\nresult.show()"},{"q":"CODING: Split a comma-separated column into multiple rows.","type":"coding","a":"split() the string into an array, then explode() it into rows.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import split, explode, col\n# Input Col3 = 'G,Y,U' -> rows G, Y, U\nresult = df.withColumn('Col3', explode(split(col('Col3'), ',')))\nresult.show()\n\n# SQL\n# SELECT Col1, Col2, exploded_val AS Col3\n# FROM t LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) tmp AS exploded_val"},{"q":"CODING: Remove duplicate records (keep the latest).","type":"coding","a":"Use ROW_NUMBER over the business key ordered by a recency column (or a dedupe key set) and keep rn=1.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('customer_id','name','email_id','order_date').orderBy(col('load_ts').desc())\ndf2 = df.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter('rn = 1').drop('rn')\ndf2.show()\n\n# Simplest form when rows are identical:\n# df.dropDuplicates(['customer_id','order_date'])"},{"q":"CODING: Calculate the total purchase amount for each customer.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and sum the purchase amount.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import sum as _sum, col\ndf.groupBy('customer_id').agg(_sum('purchase_amount').alias('total_amount')).orderBy(col('total_amount').desc()).show()"},{"q":"CODING: Process a 100M-row CSV efficiently.","type":"coding","a":"Key points: define schema explicitly (avoid inferSchema), use Parquet/Delta, filter early, tune shuffle partitions + AQE, coalesce output.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, sum as _sum\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType\n\nspark = (SparkSession.builder.appName('LargeCSV')\n         .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\n         .config('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions','400').getOrCreate())\n\nschema = StructType([StructField('transaction_id',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('customer_id',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('transaction_date',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('amount',DoubleType(),True),\n                     StructField('category',StringType(),True)])\n\ndf = (spark.read.option('header','true').option('mode','DROPMALFORMED')\n      .schema(schema).csv('/data/large_transactions.csv'))\ndf = df.dropna(subset=['transaction_id','customer_id','amount'])\ndf = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_timestamp('transaction_date','yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss'))\nagg = df.groupBy('category').agg(_sum('amount').alias('total_amount')).orderBy(col('total_amount').desc())\nagg.coalesce(1).write.option('header','true').mode('overwrite').csv('/output/revenue_by_category')"},{"q":"CODING: Convert a Parquet file/folder into a Delta table.","type":"coding","a":"Read Parquet and write as Delta. Use CONVERT TO DELTA (SQL) to convert in place.","code":"# PySpark\ndf = spark.read.format('parquet').load('abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/')\ndf.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_table')\n\n# In-place conversion (SQL)\n# CONVERT TO DELTA parquet.`abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/`;"},{"q":"CODING: Read from Azure SQL via JDBC with an incremental predicate.","type":"coding","a":"Use spark.read.jdbc with a predicates list (pushed down to the source) and write to Delta with MERGE.","code":"jdbc = 'jdbc:sqlserver://srv.database.windows.net:1433;database=db'\nprops = {'user': dbutils.secrets.get('kv','sql-user'),\n         'password': dbutils.secrets.get('kv','sql-pwd'),\n         'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'}\nlast_wm = spark.sql(\"SELECT MAX(last_load_ts) FROM ctrl.watermark WHERE table_name='Customers'\").first()[0]\ndf = spark.read.jdbc(jdbc, 'dbo.Customers',\n        predicates=[f\"ModifiedDate > '{last_wm}'\"], properties=props)\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView('incr')\nspark.sql('''MERGE INTO silver.customers t USING incr s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\n             WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *''')"},{"q":"CODING: Compute a running total (cumulative sum) per group.","type":"coding","a":"Window with partitionBy group and orderBy date, cumulating from unbounded preceding to current row.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import sum as _sum, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('customer_id').orderBy('order_date').rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)\ndf.withColumn('running_total', _sum('amount').over(w)).show()"},{"q":"How do you handle NULLs in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• Drop: df.dropna(); df.dropna(subset=['id','name']); df.dropna(how='all').\n• Fill: df.fillna(0); df.fillna({'salary':0,'dept':'N/A'}); fill with mean/median.\n• Conditional: withColumn('x', when(col('x').isNull(), lit(0)).otherwise(col('x'))).\n• Forward fill: last('col', ignorenulls=True) over a window.\n• Filter: df.filter(col('id').isNotNull()).\n• coalesce(col1, col2) to pick the first non-null."},{"q":"How do you read and flatten nested JSON / structs and arrays?","type":"concept","a":"• Access struct fields: df.select('col.field') or col('col')['field'].\n• Explode arrays: explode(col('arr')) (one row per element) or explode_outer (keeps nulls).\n• Flatten structs: select col('address.*') to promote nested fields.\n• explode map: explode(map_col) → key/value rows.\n• Define schema for nested reads, or let Auto Loader infer; use from_json() for JSON strings."},{"q":"When do you use left anti join and left semi join?","type":"concept","a":"• LEFT ANTI — returns LEFT rows with NO match in the right table (like NOT EXISTS). Use for finding orphans/unmatched records. how='left_anti'.\n• LEFT SEMI — returns LEFT rows that DO have a match in the right (no right columns returned). how='left_semi'. Use as an efficient EXISTS.\nBoth avoid duplicate rows from the right table (unlike a normal join).","code":"unmatched = orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\nexisting  = orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_semi')"},{"q":"How do you pivot and unpivot data in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• Pivot: df.groupBy('dept').pivot('year').sum('salary') — rows → columns.\n• Unpivot/stack: use stack() in SQL or create arrays/maps and explode, or use melt pattern:\n  df.selectExpr(\"id\", \"stack(3,'A',A,'B',B,'C',C) as (key,value)\").\nCommon for turning wide reports into long format for aggregation."},{"q":"What is the difference between union and unionByName?","type":"concept","a":"• union — combines DataFrames by POSITION; schemas must have the same column order/types or you get wrong/NULL results.\n• unionByName — combines by column NAME; align columns correctly regardless of order; use allowMissingColumns=True to fill missing columns with null.\nPrefer unionByName for safety when schemas may differ slightly."},{"q":"How do you create a Spark session and read a DataFrame?","type":"coding","a":"Standard SparkSession pattern; in Databricks 'spark' already exists.","code":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession\nspark = (SparkSession.builder.appName('App')\n         .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true').getOrCreate())\ndf = spark.read.format('parquet').option('header',True).load('/path')\ndf.printSchema(); df.show(5, truncate=False)"},{"q":"Which services have you used in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Men(cid:415)on:  Databricks Workspace  Clusters  Job Clusters  Interac(cid:415)ve Clusters  Notebooks (Python, SQL, PySpark)  Delta Lake  Unity Catalog (if used)  Repos  Workflows  Secrets  Auto Loader","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Joins in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Inner / Outer / Left Join — Sample Query","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of joins available in Pyspark","type":"concept","a":"➢ INNER JOIN. ➢ CROSS JOIN. ➢ LEFT OUTER JOIN. ➢ RIGHT OUTER JOIN. ➢ FULL OUTER JOIN. ➢ LEFT SEMI JOIN. ➢ LEFT ANTI JOIN.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to write sql or any other langue in python Notebook","type":"concept","a":"A. %Scala & % R","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me how many records will come for","type":"concept","a":"INNER JOIN ---solution1 select * from dbo.table1 as a inner join table2 b on a.id=b.id ---solution2 SELECT A.id FROM table1 A INNER JOIN Table2 B ON A.id=B.id LEFT JOIN SELECT A.id FROM table1 A left join Table2 B ON A.id=B.id RIGHT JOIN SELECT A.id FROM table1 A right join Table2 B ON A.id=B.id FULL JOIN SELECT * FROM table1 A full join Table2 B ON A.id=B.id CROSS JOIN select * from table1, table2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Join — Join on CustomerID (Inner Join)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How will you calculate Total Hours Worked?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would calculate the difference between LogoutTime and LoginTime\n• Total Hours = LogoutTime - LoginTime The result is converted into hours\n• If an employee worked in two loca(cid:415)ons (L1 and L2), I would keep both records separately because each loca(cid:415)on represents a different a(cid:425)endance entry.\" Write the PySpark code. from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import * emp = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True).csv(\"/employee\") a(cid:425)endance = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True).csv(\"/a(cid:425)endance\") # Remove duplicate employees emp = emp.dropDuplicates([\"EmpID\"]) # Handle null employee names emp = emp.fillna({\"EmpName\": \"Unknown\"}) # Reject invalid a(cid:425)endance reject_df = a(cid:425)endance.filter( col(\"LoginTime\").isNull() & col(\"LogoutTime\").isNull() ) # Valid a(cid:425)endance a(cid:425)endance = a(cid:425)endance.filter( ~(col(\"LoginTime\").isNull() & col(\"LogoutTime\").isNull()) ) # Calculate total hours a(cid:425)endance = a(cid:425)endance.withColumn( \"TotalHours\", (unix_(cid:415)mestamp(\"LogoutTime\") - unix_(cid:415)mestamp(\"LoginTime\")) / 3600 ) gold = a(cid:425)endance.join(emp, \"EmpID\", \"le(cid:332)\") gold.select( \"EmpID\", emp.EmpName, \"Loca(cid:415)on\", \"Date\", \"TotalHours\" ).show()","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Sal(cid:415)ng the Skewed Key","type":"concept","a":"➢Add a random number (salt) to the skewed key before the join or aggrega(cid:415)on. ➢Helps distribute the records more evenly.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Use Databricks Notebook, Python, JAR, or Run Job ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on and Bucke(cid:415)ng","type":"concept","a":"Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Bucke(cid:415)ng Splits data into folders Splits data into fixed buckets Based on column values Based on hash func(cid:415)on Good for filtering Good for joins Number of par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons depends on data Number of buckets is fixed Creates directory structure Stores files in bucket files Example: df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"country\") df.write.bucketBy(8, \"customer_id\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Join 50 GB table with 3 GB table","type":"concept","a":"Use a Broadcast Join if the smaller table fits within Spark's broadcast threshold (or if explicitly broadcas(cid:415)ng is appropriate). from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"customer_id\" ) This avoids shuffling the large table across the cluster.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Use broadcast join (if small table)","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\")","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"We have added the credit amount and subtract the debit amount for a customer in","type":"concept","a":"python(pyspark) code Credit - Debit logic in PySpark Requirement:  Add credit  Subtract debit per customer Code: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import sum, when, col df_result = df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").agg( sum(when(col(\"type\") == \"credit\", col(\"amount\")).otherwise(0)).alias(\"total_credit\"), sum(when(col(\"type\") == \"debit\", col(\"amount\")).otherwise(0)).alias(\"total_debit\") ).withColumn( \"net_balance\", col(\"total_credit\") - col(\"total_debit\") ) df_result.show()","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Col1 a a b b col2 has Aa aa bb bb col3 has 1 2 3 4","type":"concept","a":"how to get output col1 a col2 b col3 [1,2] [3,4] in python(pyspark) code Grouping + array output problem (PySpark) Input: col1 col2 col3 a Aa 1 a aa 2 b bb 3 b bb 4 Output:  col1 = a → col3 = [1,2]  col1 = b → col3 = [3,4] (cid:33158)(cid:57393) PySpark Solu(cid:415)on: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_list df_result = df.groupBy(\"col1\").agg( collect_list(\"col3\").alias(\"col3_list\") ) df_result.show() If you also want col2 grouping logic: df.groupBy(\"col1\", \"col2\").agg( collect_list(\"col3\").alias(\"col3_list\") )","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Split Comma-Separated Column into Rows","type":"concept","a":"• Input: Col1 Col2 Col3 A 1 G,Y,U B 2 U,X,C Expected Output: Col1 Col2 Col3 A 1 G A 1 Y A 1 U B 2 U B 2 X B 2 C SQL — using EXPLODE + SPLIT -- Spark SQL SELECT Col1, Col2, exploded_val AS Col3 FROM my_table LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) tmp AS exploded_val; -- Alternative using inline EXPLODE SELECT Col1, Col2, Col3_item AS Col3 FROM my_table CROSS JOIN LATERAL (SELECT EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) AS Col3_item);\n• PySpark — split() + explode() from pyspark.sql.functions import split, explode, col data = [('A', 1, 'G,Y,U'), ('B', 2, 'U,X,C')] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['Col1','Col2','Col3']) Page 12 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide # Split the comma-separated string into an array, then explode result = df.withColumn('Col3', explode(split(col('Col3'), ','))) result.show() # Output: # +----+----+----+ # |Col1|Col2|Col3| # +----+----+----+ # | A| 1| G| # | A| 1| Y| # | A| 1| U| # | B| 2| U| # | B| 2| X| # | B| 2| C| # +----+----+----+","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Difference Between Left and Right Joins","type":"concept","a":"Aspect LEFT JOIN RIGHT JOIN Definition All rows from LEFT table + matching All rows from RIGHT table + rows from RIGHT matching rows from LEFT Unmatched rows Unmatched RIGHT rows become NULL Unmatched LEFT rows become NULL Use case Keep all records from left (driving) table Keep all records from right (lookup) table Common use Find all orders including those without Find all customers including those customers without orders NULL side NULLs appear in RIGHT table columns NULLs appear in LEFT table columns Interchangeable YES — LEFT JOIN A,B = RIGHT JOIN Same relationship, different table B,A order Sample Data Table A — Orders: (order_id: 1,2,3,4) | Table B — Customers: (customer_id: 1,2,5) -- LEFT JOIN: all orders, customer info if exists SELECT o.order_id, c.name FROM orders o LEFT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id; -- Result: rows 1,2 have customer name | rows 3,4 have NULL for name -- RIGHT JOIN: all customers, order info if exists SELECT o.order_id, c.name FROM orders o RIGHT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id; -- Result: rows 1,2 have order_id | row 5 has NULL for order_id -- PySpark left_result = orders_df.join(customers_df, 'customer_id', 'left') right_result = orders_df.join(customers_df, 'customer_id', 'right')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How do you optimize if data is unevenly distributed?","type":"concept","a":"• Uneven data distribution (data skew) is one of the most common Spark performance problems\n• Solutions: 1\n• Salting: Add a random salt key to the join/group key to break the skewed key into multiple smaller partitions. import random from pyspark.sql.functions import col, lit, concat, floor, rand # Add salt to skewed key num_salts = 10 df_salted = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('skewed_key'), lit('_'), (rand() * num_salts).cast('int').cast('string')))","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"(one has more columns, one has less). How do you handle this?","type":"concept","a":"• Approach 1: Schema Merge with mergeSchema option df = (spark.read .option('header', 'true') .option('mergeSchema', 'true') .csv('/mnt/landing/csv_files/')) # Columns missing in a file will be filled with null Approach 2: Auto Loader with Schema Evolution df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/schema/') .load('/mnt/csv/')) Approach 3: Define an Explicit Schema Define a superset schema (all possible columns) and use it during read\n• Missing columns will be null. from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType schema = StructType([ StructField('col1', StringType(), True), StructField('col2', StringType(), True), StructField('col3', DoubleType(), True) # May be missing in some files ]) df = spark.read.schema(schema).option('header', 'true').csv('/mnt/csv/') Section 6: SCD & Merge Operations","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Given the transaction table, write PySpark code using Window functions to get","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Transforma(cid:415)ons vs Ac(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Transforma(cid:415)ons  filter  select  join  groupBy  map Ac(cid:415)ons  show  count  collect  first  take  write","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Nested Loop Join","type":"concept","a":"Used for non-equality joins.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cartesian Join","type":"concept","a":"df1.crossJoin(df2) Produces every possible combina(cid:415)on of rows.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Write the output for le(cid:332) join, an(cid:415)-le(cid:332) join & inner join for a given table","type":"concept","a":"• Output of joins (LEFT, INNER, LEFT ANTI) Assume: Table A (Employees) id name 1 A 2 B 3 C Table B (Departments) id dept 1 IT 3 HR (cid:44934) INNER JOIN SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;\n• Output: id name dept 1 A IT 3 C HR (cid:44934) LEFT JOIN SELECT * FROM A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;\n• Output: id name dept 1 A IT 2 B NULL 3 C HR (cid:44934) LEFT ANTI JOIN SELECT * FROM A LEFT ANTI JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;\n• Output: id name 2 B Meaning:  Rows in A NOT present in B","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Can we implement schema enforcement?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Methods:  Explicit Column Mapping  Data Flow schema valida(cid:415)on  Databricks schema comparison  ValidateMetadata ac(cid:415)vity  Custom Python/PySpark valida(cid:415)on Example: Source Columns = Target Columns AND Source Count = Target Count If mismatch: Fail Ac(cid:415)vity","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Check par(cid:415)(cid:415)on count.","type":"concept","a":"7. Check joins.","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Rate yourself in Python, PySpark, SQL","type":"concept","a":"• Example: Python: 7.5/10 PySpark: 8.5/10 SQL: 9/10 Explain: \"I use SQL daily for transforma(cid:415)ons and valida(cid:415)ons\n• PySpark is my primary transforma(cid:415)on language in Databricks, and I use Python mainly for scrip(cid:415)ng, API integra(cid:415)on, and u(cid:415)lity func(cid:415)ons.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Join ques(cid:415)on , How many rows will be returned for inner,le(cid:332),right?","type":"concept","a":"Table 1 a b 0 1 0 2 1 4 1 5 Rows = 4 Table 2 a d 0 bat 0 net 0 ball 1 cat 2 dog Rows = 5 Inner Join For a = 0:  2 rows in Table 1 × 3 rows in Table 2 = 6 For a = 1:  2 × 1 = 2 For a = 2:  No match Total = 8 rows Le(cid:332) Join All matching rows plus unmatched rows from the le(cid:332) table.  Matches for a = 0: 6  Matches for a = 1: 2  No unmatched rows in Table 1 Total = 8 rows Right Join All matching rows plus unmatched rows from the right table.  Matches: 8  Unmatched a = 2 row from Table 2: 1 Total = 9 rows Final Answer Join Type Rows Inner Join 8 Le(cid:332) Join 8 Right Join 9 Round 2 :","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• \"For SCD Type 2, I compare the source and target using the business key (Customer_ID)\n• If a(cid:425)ributes like Address have changed, I update the exis(cid:415)ng record by se(cid:427)ng End_Date and Is_Current = 'N', then insert a new row with the updated values, a new Start_Date, End_Date = NULL, and Is_Current = 'Y'\n• This preserves the full history.\" Exis(cid:415)ng Table | Customer_ID | Name | Address | Start_Date | End_Date | Is_Current | Incoming Data | Customer_ID | Name | Address | Goal If the customer's address changes:  Expire the old record  Insert a new record  Maintain history Step 1: Join source and target joined = source.alias(\"s\").join( target.alias(\"t\"), \"Customer_ID\", \"le(cid:332)\" ) Step 2: Find changed records changed = joined.filter( (joined.Address != joined[\"t.Address\"]) & (joined[\"t.Is_Current\"] == \"Y\") ) Step 3: Expire old records expired = changed.select( \"Customer_ID\", \"t.Name\", \"t.Address\", \"t.Start_Date\" ).withColumn( \"End_Date\", current_date() ).withColumn( \"Is_Current\", lit(\"N\") ) Step 4: Insert new records new_records = changed.select( \"Customer_ID\", \"s.Name\", \"s.Address\" ).withColumn( \"Start_Date\", current_date() ).withColumn( \"End_Date\", lit(None) ).withColumn( \"Is_Current\", lit(\"Y\") ) Step 5: Keep unchanged records unchanged = target.filter(target.Is_Current == \"Y\") Step 6: Final output final_df = unchanged.unionByName(expired).unionByName(new_records)","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Convert JSON Data into a Structured Table?","type":"concept","a":"Flat JSON df = spark.read.format('json').load('/data/events.json') df.printSchema() # Spark auto-infers schema df.show() Nested JSON with explicit schema from pyspark.sql.functions import col, explode, from_json from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, IntegerType, ArrayType schema = StructType([ StructField('order_id', StringType()), StructField('customer', StructType([ StructField('id', StringType()), StructField('name', StringType()) ])), StructField('items', ArrayType(StructType([ StructField('product_id', StringType()), StructField('qty', IntegerType()) ]))) ]) df = spark.read.schema(schema).json('/data/orders.json') Page 5 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) # Flatten nested struct df_flat = df.select('order_id', col('customer.id').alias('cust_id'), col('customer.name').alias('name')) # Explode array column df_items = df.select('order_id', explode(col('items')).alias('item')) df_items = df_items.select('order_id', col('item.product_id'), col('item.qty')) # JSON stored as a string column df = df.withColumn('parsed', from_json(col('json_col'), schema)).select('parsed.*')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What do you do in Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":" Data cleansing: o Remove duplicates o Handle nulls o Standardize schema  Apply business rules  Join reference/master data  Data is validated and structured","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize joins (Broadcast Join if applicable).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Python Problem","type":"concept","a":"Example: A = [1,2,3] B = A A = A + [4] print(A, B) Output: [1,2,3,4] [1,2,3] Reason: + creates a new list, so A references a new object while B s(cid:415)ll points to the original list.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"22 & 23. What is Pandas?","type":"concept","a":"Pandas is a Python library for:  Data analysis  Data cleaning  Small to medium-sized datasets  DataFrames  CSV/Excel processing Interview point: \"Pandas works on a single machine, whereas PySpark is distributed and designed for big data.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Sets in Python – Why Sets?","type":"concept","a":"A set is an unordered collec(cid:415)on of unique elements.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used Broadcast Join in your project?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes\n• In my project, we join a large employee transac(cid:415)on table with a small department lookup table\n• Since the department table is small, we use a broadcast join to avoid shuffling the large dataset and improve performance.\" PySpark Syntax from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = employee_df.join( broadcast(department_df), \"DeptID\", \"le(cid:332)\" )","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Write Broadcast Join Syntax.","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"EmpID\" ) Client Round 1","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"External Logic in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• External logic refers to code/functions that are defined outside of Databricks but invoked within pipelines: Ways to use External Logic: • UDFs (User Defined Functions): Python/Scala functions registered in Spark • Python libraries: pip install custom packages via %pip or cluster init scripts • Azure Functions / REST APIs: Called from notebooks via HTTP requests • External JARs: Custom Java/Scala libraries added to cluster • MLflow models: External ML models called for inference in pipelines # Registering a Python UDF from pyspark.sql.functions import udf from pyspark.sql.types import StringType @udf(StringType()) def clean_phone(phone): return phone.replace('-', '').strip() if phone else None df = df.withColumn('clean_phone', clean_phone(col('phone_number'))) Q6\n• Data Skew Data Skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across partitions in a Spark cluster, causing some tasks to take much longer than others (stragglers)\n• Causes: • High cardinality or NULL keys in joins/group-bys • Hot keys (e.g., one customer ID with millions of records) Solutions: • Salting: Add a random prefix to keys to spread load • AQE (Adaptive Query Execution): Spark 3.x automatically handles skew • Broadcast Join: Broadcast smaller table to avoid shuffle • Skew hints: Use SKEW hint in SQL • Repartition: Manually repartition on a more uniform key # Enable AQE (Spark 3.x) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') # Salting example import random salt_udf = udf(lambda: random.randint(0, 9), IntegerType()) df = df.withColumn('salt', salt_udf()) df = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('key'), lit('_'), col('salt')))","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"In which case will you prefer Data flow Gen2 and Notebook for pyspark.","type":"concept","a":"• I prefer Dataflow Gen2 when the transforma(cid:415)ons are simple, low-code, and business- oriented like filtering, joins, and basic aggrega(cid:415)ons\n• It is faster to implement and ideal for standard ETL pipelines\n• I prefer PySpark notebooks when dealing with large-scale data, complex transforma(cid:415)ons, performance tuning, or advanced logic like window func(cid:415)ons, incremental processing, and Delta Lake opera(cid:415)ons\n• PySpark gives more flexibility and control over distributed processing","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast join + default join behavior","type":"concept","a":"","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"(cid:48132) How to write broadcast join?","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\") OR SQL style hint: SELECT /*+ BROADCAST(t2) */ * FROM t1 JOIN t2 ON t1.id = t2.id;","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"sql","name":"SQL & Query Optimization","icon":"🗃️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows BEFORE grouping/aggregation; cannot use aggregate functions.\n• HAVING filters groups AFTER GROUP BY; can use aggregates.\n• A query can have both: WHERE to reduce rows, then GROUP BY, then HAVING to filter groups.\nExample: SELECT emp, SUM(sal) FROM t WHERE dept='IT' GROUP BY emp HAVING SUM(sal) > 5000."},{"q":"Explain INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, CROSS, LEFT SEMI, and LEFT ANTI joins.","type":"concept","a":"• INNER — only matching rows from both tables.\n• LEFT — all left rows + matches; unmatched right = NULL.\n• RIGHT — all right rows + matches; unmatched left = NULL.\n• FULL OUTER — all rows from both; NULLs where no match.\n• CROSS — cartesian product (every combination).\n• LEFT SEMI — left rows with a match in right (no right columns).\n• LEFT ANTI — left rows with NO match in right.\nLEFT JOIN A,B = RIGHT JOIN B,A."},{"q":"CODING: Total revenue per month.","type":"coding","a":"Group by the formatted month and sum amounts.","code":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM') AS year_month,\n       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM')\nORDER BY year_month;"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in both January AND February 2025.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and count distinct months; keep those with 2. INTERSECT is an alternative.","code":"SELECT customer_id\nFROM orders\nWHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28'\n  AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1,2)\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2;\n\n-- Alternative\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01'\nINTERSECT\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in 3 consecutive months.","type":"coding","a":"Use LAG to look back two months and compare the dates.","code":"WITH m AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) AS mth\n  FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)\n),\nl AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, mth,\n         LAG(mth,1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev1,\n         LAG(mth,2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev2\n  FROM m\n)\nSELECT DISTINCT customer_id\nFROM l\nWHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2,1) = prev1 AND ADD_MONTHS(prev1,1) = mth;"},{"q":"CODING: Total order amount per customer (join + aggregation).","type":"coding","a":"Inner join customers to orders and sum.","code":"SELECT c.CustomerID, c.Name, SUM(o.Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Customer c\nINNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID\nGROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.Name;"},{"q":"CODING: Delete duplicate records keeping one row.","type":"coding","a":"Assign row numbers per duplicate key and delete rn>1 (or rebuild the table).","code":"WITH cte AS (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer\n)\nDELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n\n-- Delta-friendly rebuild\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n     PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer) WHERE rn = 1;"},{"q":"How would you optimize a slow SQL query?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read the execution plan (EXPLAIN) to find the bottleneck (scans, shuffles, spills).\n• Filter as early as possible; avoid SELECT * (select needed columns only).\n• Ensure joins/filters use indexed or partition columns.\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE (e.g., YEAR(date)=2025) — use range predicates for partition pruning.\n• Use proper join types and broadcast hints for small tables.\n• Replace correlated subqueries with joins; use CTEs to avoid repeated work.\n• Update statistics; partition large tables; avoid unnecessary DISTINCT.\n• Compare plans/times before and after."},{"q":"How does partition pruning work and how do you enable it?","type":"concept","a":"Partition pruning means the engine reads only the partitions matching the filter, skipping the rest.\n• Filter directly on the partition column with range/equality predicates (WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31').\n• Avoid wrapping the partition column in functions (WHERE YEAR(order_date)=2025 disables pruning).\n• In Spark/Delta, this avoids reading irrelevant files/folders → big speedup."},{"q":"What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?","type":"concept","a":"• UNION — removes duplicate rows (implies a distinct/sort → more expensive).\n• UNION ALL — keeps all rows including duplicates (faster).\nUse UNION ALL by default and de-duplicate only when needed."},{"q":"What is a CTE and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"A Common Table Expression (WITH clause) is a named temporary result set that makes complex queries readable and avoids repeating subqueries. It's not materialized by default in many engines (each reference may re-execute), but improves clarity and can be chained. Use for multi-step logic: dedupe → join → aggregate."},{"q":"CODING: Find the manager of each employee using a self join (SQL).","type":"coding","a":"Self join employee to itself; LEFT join so top-level employees (null manager) are kept.","code":"SELECT e.empid AS employee_id, e.empname AS employee_name,\n       e.mgrid AS manager_id, m.empname AS manager_name\nFROM employee e\nLEFT JOIN employee m ON e.mgrid = m.empid;"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions and how do they differ from window functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Aggregates (SUM/COUNT/AVG/MIN/MAX) collapse multiple rows into one row per group — you lose individual rows.\n• Window functions compute across a set of rows but KEEP every row (add a computed column).\nAggregate example: SUM(sal) per dept. Window example: SUM(sal) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) alongside each row."},{"q":"How do you implement SCD Type 1 and Type 2 in SQL?","type":"coding","a":"Type 1 overwrites; Type 2 expires the old row and inserts a new version with effective dates + current flag.","code":"-- SCD Type 1 (overwrite)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.name=s.name, t.email=s.email, t.updated_at=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id,name,email,updated_at) VALUES (s.customer_id,s.name,s.email,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n\n-- SCD Type 2 (expire + insert)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s\nON t.customer_id = s.customer_id AND t.is_current = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (t.name<>s.name OR t.city<>s.city) THEN\n  UPDATE SET t.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, t.is_current = 0;\n\nINSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id,name,city,start_date,end_date,is_current)\nSELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1\nFROM staging s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id=s.customer_id AND d.is_current=1);"},{"q":"How would you find the Nth highest salary?","type":"concept","a":"Use DENSE_RANK (handles ties) or a correlated subquery.\n• Window: SELECT * FROM (SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) rk FROM emp) WHERE rk = N.\n• Subquery: SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp) [repeat for Nth].\nDENSE_RANK is cleaner and scales to any N."},{"q":"Window Func(cid:415)ons (ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK)","type":"concept","a":"• Window func(cid:415)ons perform calcula(cid:415)ons across related rows without collapsing the data\n• Example data: Employee Dept Salary A IT 10000 B IT 9000 C IT 9000 D IT 8000 ROW_NUMBER() Assigns a unique number to each row\n• Salary Row_Number 10000 1 9000 2 9000 3 8000 4 from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import row_number window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"Dept\").orderBy(\"Salary\") df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(window)) RANK() Duplicate values receive the same rank, and the next rank is skipped\n• Salary Rank 10000 1 9000 2 9000 2 Salary Rank 8000 4 DENSE_RANK() Duplicate values receive the same rank, but there are no gaps\n• Salary Dense Rank 10000 1 9000 2 9000 2 8000 3","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Inner, Left & Outer Join — Worked Example","type":"concept","a":"Sample Tables Table A: Orders order_id customer_id amount 101 C1 500 102 C2 300 103 C3 800 104 C5 200 Table B: Customers customer_id customer_name C1 Alice C2 Bob C4 Charlie INNER JOIN Result INNER JOIN: A.customer_id = B.customer_id Returns ONLY rows where customer_id exists in BOTH tables. order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob Excluded: Order 103 (C3 not in Customers), Order 104 (C5 not in Customers), C4 Charlie (no orders) LEFT JOIN Result LEFT JOIN: All rows from Orders, match from Customers (NULL if none) order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob 103 | C3 | 800 | NULL ← C3 not in Customers 104 | C5 | 200 | NULL ← C5 not in Customers FULL OUTER JOIN Result FULL OUTER JOIN: All rows from BOTH tables order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob 103 | C3 | 800 | NULL ← C3 has no matching customer 104 | C5 | 200 | NULL ← C5 has no matching customer NULL | C4 | NULL | Charlie ← C4 has no orders","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"❌ What Breaks?","type":"concept","a":"• Source sends: { order_id, customer_id, amount, discount_code } ← new column added Your Delta table has: order_id, customer_id, amount Spark throws: AnalysisException: Column 'discount_code' not found in schema Solution 1: mergeSchema (Easiest) # In your writeStream, add mergeSchema option df_stream.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/orders/') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ # ← This is the fix! .outputMode('append') \\ .toTable('silver.orders') # mergeSchema automatically adds the new column 'discount_code' to # the Delta table\n• Old rows will have NULL for this new column\n• Solution 2: autoMerge (Spark Config) # Enable schema auto-merge globally spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled', 'true') # Now any write (streaming or batch) will automatically merge new columns Solution 3: Explicit Schema Evolution with Autoloader # With Autoloader, use schemaEvolutionMode df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/schema/') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') \\ # Options: 'addNewColumns' | 'rescue' | 'failOnNewColumns' | 'none' .load('/mnt/raw/orders/') # 'rescue': Puts unexpected columns into a _rescued_data JSON column # 'failOnNewColumns': Deliberately fail and alert when schema changes # 'addNewColumns': Automatically expand schema to include new columns 💡 Best Practice for Production Use 'failOnNewColumns' in strict environments to detect unexpected schema changes early\n• Use 'addNewColumns' or mergeSchema='true' in flexible pipelines where schema drift is expected\n• Always log schema changes and alert the data team when new columns appear\n• Schema Drift Schema drift refers to the phenomenon where the structure of your incoming data changes over time — new columns are added, columns are removed, data types change\n• This is one of the most common challenges in real data engineering projects\n• Types of Schema Drift Type Example & Impact New column added Source adds 'loyalty_points' column\n• Pipeline fails if not handled — breaks schema validation\n• Column removed Source stops sending 'middle_name'\n• Downstream queries on that column will return errors\n• Data type change 'order_amount' changes from INT to DECIMAL","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are Transformations used in Data Flows.","type":"concept","a":"A. Derived Column--Deriving new columns by writing expressions Lookup---Comparing two inputs finding matching non matching based on common column(left outer join) Alter row---categorizing the rows to perform upsert operations, which row needs to insert and update and delete Join---Joining of two inputs like inner, left, fullouter, customer Conditional Split---base don condition data will be spitted Ex: Male and female two outputs Filter: filtering of data by putting filter condition Assertions--Performing some data validations by specifying data rules Windows--to find duplicate or [performing rank, rownumber, running total Aggregate: performing aggregate and group by Union: appending of data into one output","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the Transformations used in Data Flows","type":"concept","a":"• DERIVED COLUMN: Deriving New Columns or adding new columns in Dataflow by writing of Expressions Ex: Firstname,LastName column,we can derive new column Full Name by Concat both firstname,lastname JOIN: If you want to perform all kind of join Inner join, Left outer Join, Right Outer Join..customer join in between two tables Ex:-Customer, Customer Address join both based on Location ID SELECT: Limiting Number of columns in Dataflow Ex: 10 columns from source, But want to load only 5 columns, Then use select transformation Limit only required columns FILTER: Applying filter on data coming from source Ex: Load only city= hyderabad customers into target Page | 4 CONDITIONAL SPLIT: I have one input customer data Male and female data and transgender data I want to load above customer data into 3 tables Male customer Female Customer Trans gender UNION: Appending data from multiple inputs into one output ex: Emphyd EmpBang combine all data from both data sources WINDOWS: If you want to apply Row_Number, Rank , Dense_Rank functionalities use this windows Ex: Finding duplicates using rownumber partition and order by and filtering using Filter Transformation AGGREGATE: Performing Aggregate operations includes Min , Max, Sum, Avg\n• Ex: Each gender wise yearly income, avg income\n• SORT: Just sorting order of data in acesending or descending based on column Ex :Sort data based on productid ASSERTION: Applying data validation rules on incoming data to identify unique, duplicates, condition met or not Ex: Phone number contains 10 digits or not LOOKUP: comparing two inputs based on common column and finding matched and non matched records(Left Outer Join) Ex: Comparing customer source data Destination Dimcustomer based on customerid ALTER ROW: categorizing records to perform upsert operations it includes insert, update, delete BRANCHING: It will take one input and giving multiple outputs of data customer data source table contains 10 rows, i want to load same data into multiple tables customer---dimcustomer customer---dimcustomerbackup customer---dimcustomercopy EXIST: Comparing two source inputs and verifying a record from first source input to second source input a custid in customer table exist or not exist in other table dimcustomer Page | 5","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Mapping Dataflows and Warngling Datflows","type":"concept","a":"• Mapping Data Flows: Use Mapping Data Flows to visually transform data without having to write any code\n• You can focus on the transformations and logic, while Azure Data Factory does the heavy lifting behind the scenes\n• It translates your transformations and logic to code that runs on scaled-out Azure Databricks clusters for maximum performance\n• A Mapping Data Flow can look something like this: The focus in this interface is on the flow\n• You can quickly identify your sources, transformations, branches, joins, and sinks\n• To see the actual data, you need to enable Data Flow Debug and preview the data per transformation\n• Wrangling Data Flows: Use Wrangling Data Flows to visually explore and prepare datasets using the Power Query Online mashup editor\n• You can focus on the modeling and logic, while Azure Data Factory does the heavy lifting behind the scenes\n• It translates the underlying M code to code that runs on a managed Spark environment for maximum performance\n• A Wrangling Data Flow can look something like this: The focus in this interface is on the data\n• You can quickly see what the final dataset will look like\n• To see the actual sources, transformations, and joins, you need to go through the list of Applied Steps","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Its scalability is good as you can add It is having low scalability as compared to","type":"concept","a":"up to n different nodes Map Reduce","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data Bricks Runtime Integration","type":"concept","a":"• Data bricks Runtime ML is a variant of Data bricks Runtime that adds multiple popular machine learning libraries, including Tensor Flow, Keras, PyTorch, and XGBoost\n• Photon\n• Photon is the Azure Data bricks native vectorized query engine that runs SQL workloads faster and reduces your total cost per workload\n• Page | 12","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"PAAS--- Platform as a service {Operating system, SQL server….}","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Elastic pool, what are different compute Tiers?","type":"concept","a":"Group of azure SQL data bases to share resources among all","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Distributions?","type":"concept","a":"• Application connect issue T-SQL commands a control node\n• The control node hosts the MPP Engine, which optimise queries for parallel processing and then passes operations to compute node to do their work in parallel\n• The compute nodes store all users data in Azure storage and run the parallel Queries Control Node: It will act lick human brain it will take request from application and passing to MPP MPP (Massive Parallel Processing): Splitting of Query into multiple smaller queries and execute queries parallel\n• Compute node: It is place where each query will execute\n• 0-60 compute nodes Page | 26 Distribution Method: Based on Distribution method created tables, data will be distributed across nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"One Database to Another Database","type":"concept","a":"create master key encryption by password='Eclasess@456' --Creating a New password create database scoped credential ploybase --Creating a New Database with identity='eclasessgrp', ---This is the Username and password from Source side secret='Eclasess@123' create external data source ploy -----Creating a New external Datasource with ( type=rdbms, location='eclasess1.database.windows.net', -----This is Server of the Source side database_name='source1', credential=ploybase) create external table empext -----Creating a new External tables ( id int, person varchar(100), salary float) with( data_source=ploy, schema_name='dbo', object_name='emp' ------The table from the source side ) select * from empext Ploybase from Blob storage to SQL Database create master key encryption by password='eclasess@123' --- Creating a New Password create database scoped credential ploybase ---Creating a New Database with identity='avinash', -----You can give a any name secret='XabXvCkBrzcio24wEMxhyHkj1ZX7pK19SfUe3/Ux1xj65tr8LKA+HFSCOurjaDgT0EE1mP KcyYlM+AStQwpk+Q==' --------it is of secrete of blob storage available in the ACCESS KEY create external data source ploy_source ---creating a external data source with ( type=hadoop, location='wasbs://eclasess456@eclasess123.blob.core.windows.net', -----(in this eclasess456 is a Container name and eclasess123 is a Blob storage name) credential=ploybase ) Page | 28 create external file format file1 -- creating a external format with( format_type=delimitedtext, format_options(field_terminator=',',first_row=2)) create external table product1 ( eno int, enmae varchar(10), epro varchar(10)) with( data_source=ploy_source, file_format=file1, location='/product/') select * from product1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is master and transaction tables?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Transaction table: This tables gets impacted with insert or update delete we perform business transactions Master table: This will be read and used during transactions, but this table will not get impacted for inset and update or delete\n• A transaction is an activity performed by entities (master tables) within the system\n• These activities are captured in transaction tables and usually, these transaction entries have foreign keys to master records\n• Transaction tables are designed to store events in the system\n• Page | 29","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between where and having clause?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ WHERE: Where clause is used to filter records. where clause cannot be used in aggregate function. where clause used to filter the records from table or used while joining more than one table only those records will be extracted who are satisfying the specified condition in WHERE clause\n• It can be used with select, update, delete statement. ➢ HAVING: Having clause is used to filter the records from the groups based on the given condition in the having clause those groups who will satisfy the given condition will appear in the final result having clause can only be used with select statement","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are distinct and top key word in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Distinct statement: It is used to return only distinct or different values inside a table, a column contains many duplicate values and sometime you only want to list the different values ➢ Top keyword: Advertisements\n• The SQL TOP clause is used to fetch a TOP N number or X percent records from a table\n• Note − All the databases do not support the TOP clause\n• For example MySQL supports the LIMIT clause to fetch limited number of records while Oracle uses the ROWNUM command to fetch a limited number of records","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is sql query order of writing","type":"concept","a":"• Six Operations to order of Writing: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, and ORDER BY\n• By using examples, we will explain the execution order of the six most common operations or pieces in an SQL query\n• Page | 31","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is order of execution of SQL query?","type":"concept","a":"• Six Operations to Order: to order of executing: FROM,WHERE,GROUPBY,HAVING,SELECT,ORDERBY By using examples, we will explain the execution order of the six most common operations or pieces in an SQL query\n• Because the database executes query components in a specific order, it's helpful for the developer to know this order","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"• COUNT Function: COUNT function is used to Count the number of rows in a database table. \n• SUM Function: Sum function is used to calculate the sum of all selected columns. \n• AVG Function: The AVG function is used to calculate the average value of the numeric type. ","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are constrains available in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server contains the following 6 types of constraints:","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is difference between the primary key and unique key?","type":"concept","a":"• Both Primary key and Unique Key are used to uniquely define of a row in a table\n• Primary Key creates a clustered index of the column whereas a Unique creates an unclustered index of the column \n• Primary Key doesn't allow NULL value, however a Unique Key does allow one NULL value","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is view and materilized view?","type":"concept","a":"• A view uses a query to pull data from the underlying tables\n• A materialized view is a table on disk that contains the result set of a query\n• Materialized views are primarily used to increase application performance when it isn't feasible or desirable to use a standard view with indexes applied to it","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are difference between table variable and temp tables, tables and cte?","type":"concept","a":"• A. . \n• This biggest difference is that a CTE can only be used in the current query scope whereas a temporary table or table variable can exist for the entire duration of the session allowing you to perform many different DML operations against them","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is the difference between storaged procedure and and functions?","type":"concept","a":"• A: The function must return a value but in Stored Procedure it is optional\n• Even a procedure can return zero or n values\n• Functions can have only input parameters for it whereas Procedures can have input or output parameters\n• Functions can be called from Procedure whereas Procedures cannot be called from a Function Page | 32","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of index available and difference between those?","type":"concept","a":"• Expression-based indexes efficiently evaluate queries with the indexed Expression. ➢ Unique and Non-Unique indexes ➢ Clustered and Non- Clustered indexes ➢ Partitioned and Non Partitioned indexes ➢ Expression-Based indexes Difference between those: • In addition to enforcing the uniqueness of data values, • A unique index can also be used to improve data retrieval performance during query processing\n• Non-unique indexes are not used to enforce constraints on the tables with which they are associated. • In Non-Clustered index leaf nodes are not the actual data itself rather they only contains included columns • In Clustered index leaf nodes are actual data itself • A non partitioned index is a single index object that refers to all rows in a partitioned table\n• Non partitioned indexes are always created as independent index objects in a single table space, even if the table data partitions span multiple table spaces. • When you create an index for a partitioned table, the index is a partitioned index by default unless you create one of the following types of indexes: • A unique index where the index key does not include all of the table- partitioning columns A spatial index","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of join available and syntax difference between each one?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Join statement is used to combine data or rows from two or more tables based on a common field between them\n• Different types of Joins are INNER JOIN: The INNER JOIN keyword selects all rows from both the tables as long as the condition satisfies.This keyword will create the result-set by combining all rows from both the tables where the condition satisfies i.e value of the common field will be same. syn: SELECT table1.column1,table1.column2,table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 INNER JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column LEFT JOIN: This join returns all the rows of the table on the left side of the join and matching rows for the table on the right side of join\n• The rows for which there is no matching row on right side, the result-set will contain null\n• LEFT JOIN is also known as LEFT OUTER JOIN Syn: SELECT table1.column1, table1.column2, table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 LEFT JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column;\n• RIGHT JOIN: RIGHT JOIN is similar to LEFT JOIN\n• This join returns all the rows of the table on the right side of the join and matching rows for the table on the left side of join\n• The rows for which there is no matching row on left side, the result-set will contain null\n• Page | 33 RIGHT JOIN is also known as RIGHT OUTER JOIN syn: SELECT table1.column1,table1.column2,table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 RIGHT JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column FULL JOIN: FULL JOIN creates the result-set by combining result of both LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN\n• The result-set will contain all the rows from both the tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to find Duplicate records in Employee Table","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 SELECT empno,ename,sal, count(*)FROM employee GROUP BY empno,ename,sal having count(*)>1 ---Solution2 select *, row_number() over (partition by empno order by empno) as rownumber from employee","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to remove duplicate Records in Employee Table using CTE","type":"concept","a":"with ABC as ( SELECT*, row_number() over (PARTITION BY empno ORDER BY empno) as RowNumber FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM ABC WHERE RowNumber>1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Find 2nd highest Salary employees in emp Table using CTE","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT SAL, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SAL DESC) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT TOP 2 SAL FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2 ---solution2 WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT *, Page | 34 DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SAL desc) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT ename,sal FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to find 2nd Highest salary Employees in emp Table with out using rank and cte","type":"concept","a":"use subquery ---Solution1 SELECT ename,sal FROM employee e1 WHERE 1=(SELECT COUNT (distinct sal) FROM employee e2 WHERE e2.sal > e1.sal) ---Solution2 SELECT TOP 1 sal FROM ( SELECT DISTINCT TOP 2 SAL FROM Employee ORDER BY SAL DESC ) result ORDER BY SAL ---Solution3 SELECT MAX(Sal) From Employee WHERE Sal < ( SELECT Max(Sal) FROM Employee) ---Solution4 find two top salaries select distinct top 2 sal from EMPLOYEE order by Sal desc","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Find 2nd highest Salary employees by each department using CTE","type":"concept","a":"WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (partition by deptno ORDER BY SAL desc) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT * FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Find Employees from employees tables, doen’t belongs to any department in dept","type":"concept","a":"table ---Solutio1 select * from employee e right outer join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where e.deptno is null ---Solution2 select e.ename,dname,loc from employee e right join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where e.deptno is null ---Solution3 select e.deptno,ename,dname,loc from employee e left join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where d.deptno is null","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to make combination of cricket match between below countries","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 select a.team+' Vs '+b.team from abc a,abc b where a.team<b.team order by a.team ---Solution2 SELECT * FROM abc A JOIN abc B on A.team<>B.team","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to find all employee having sal greater then the average salary of each","type":"concept","a":"dept select e.ename,e.sal,e.deptno,s.salavg from employee e, (select deptno,avg(sal) salavg from employee group by deptno)s where e.deptno=s.deptno and e.sal>s.salavg","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to find employee and manager name in below table","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 select e.eno,e.ename,m.ename managername from employee_test e,employee_test Page | 36 m where e.mgrid=m.eno ---Solution2 select e.ename,m.ename as manager from employee_test e left join employee_test m on e.mgrid=m.eno https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/samples/adventureworks-install-configure?view=sql- server-ver16&tabs=ssms ----Adventure scripts link.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Connecting the multiples files.","type":"concept","a":"• ======Adventure scripts =============== select * from dimproductsubcategory select * from dimproductcategory select * from FactInternetSales select * from dimproduct select * from DimProductSubcategory ---1.Display each category wise totalsalesamount select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName ------2.Display 2nd highest sales product with cte as ( select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount, DENSE_RANK() over (order by sum(salesamount) desc) as rank from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName ) select * from cte where rank =2 ----3.display product names dont have any sales select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey left join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName having sum(salesamount) is null ------4.display list of products belongs to categoryname is \"BIkes\" select p.EnglishProductName, c.EnglishProductCategoryName from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey Page | 37 where EnglishProductCategoryName='Bikes' To Know common column of the two tables select name from sys.columns where object_id=object_id('dbo.tablename1') intersect select name from sys.columns where object_id = object_id('dbo.tablename2') 44a4bxedxPwGQtSnEzGzdejnCdtrDJqkJ6uqWwGbXmdx5zrjGdHivmr9pyd7x8rVQMgumw GPCfuwseieCg8tuN5jNNeMJPU – This is the link of Emp and Dept scripts\n• SELECT VIEW: select * from sys.databases --display all database select * from sys.tables --display all tables select * from cons.sys.tables --display only given database name select * from sys.views -- display all the views select * from sys.columns --display all the coloumsin all database select * from sys.columns where name like '%emp%' --display only given table name","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"STORED PROCEDURE: A group of statements that to be existing.","type":"concept","a":"-- INNER PARAMETERS create procedure int_par1(@ename varchar(100),@var int)-- create a proce name as begin select empno,ename from emp where @ename=ename And @var=empno--on what table values you want end exec int_par1 'smith',7369 OUTPUT PARAMETER create procedure out_Par6(@ename varchar(100),@var int output)-- give name output as begin select @var=count(Empno) from emp where @ename=job end declare @totalcount2 varchar(100)-- give the specfic name of the output execute out_par6 'clerk', @totalcount2 out print @totalcount2 RETURN PARAMETERS create procedure out_Par7 as begin return (select count(Empno) from emp) end declare @totalcount3 int execute @totalcount3 = out_Par7 print @totalcount3 Page | 38 select * from emp create procedure sum2(@var1 int,@var2 int,@var3 int) as begin declare @sum int set @sum=(@var1+@var2+@var3) select @sum as colunm end exec sum2 1000,2000,3000","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SET OPREATORS: Set operators are used to combine the result of 2 or more tables as a single","type":"concept","a":"• set of values. create table emp_hyd( empid int, personname varchar(100), salary float ) insert into emp_hyd values(100,'name1',1000),(102,'name2',1000),(103,'name3',3000),(104,'name4',4000) select * from emp_hyd create table emp_pune( empid int, personname varchar(100), salary float ) insert into emp_pune values(100,'name1',1000),(107,'name7',7000),(108,'name8',8000),(109,'name9',9000) select * from emp_pune --operators --union: it will return the values but duplicate will not execute select * from emp_hyd union select * from emp_pune -- union all: it will return values and it will execute the duplicates also select * from emp_hyd union all select * from emp_pune -- intersect: it will execute only the common value select * from emp_hyd intersect select * from emp_pune -- EXPECT :it will return values from left hand table which are not right hand table select * from emp_hyd except select * from emp_pune select * from emp_pune except select * from emp_hyd Page | 39 16\n• TRIGGERS: --trigger; it is automatically perform --dml tigger:in response to upadte, insert, delete select * from res create trigger res_trig on res for insert as print 'hi' insert into res values('ai05',54,'ytre') --two magic create trigger res_trig2 on res for insert,update,delete as begin select * from inserted-- it will consider both update,insert select * from deleted-- it will consider delete end insert into res values ('aio6',45,'poiu') insert into res values ('aio8',45,'poiu') delete from res where aircrafe_code='aio6' create table res1( aircrafe_code varchar(100), seat_no int, clasess varchar(100)) -- if seat>25 fro other new table alter trigger res_trig2 on res for insert, update,delete as begin declare @seat_no int select @seat_no=seat_no from inserted if @seat_no>25 begin insert into res1 select * from inserted print 'it is suceed' end else print ' it is not' end insert into res values('aio10',21,'poiuy') insert into res values('aio10',29,'poiuy') select * from res1 --delete triggers create trigger trig_del1 on res1 instead of delete Page | 40 as print 'it have been deleted' set nocount on delete from res1 --ddl trigger-- in respones yo create,alter,drop --database scoped ddl trigger create trigger table_trig--it is a creating a database on database for create_table,alter_table,drop_procedure as print 'table is created' create table t1 ( col1 int, col2 int) create trigger table_drop on database for drop_table,alter_table as print 'table is deleted' rollback drop table t1 --server scope ddl trigger -- it is a servers create trigger server_trig on all server for create_database,drop_database as print 'server has been created' create database data_base drop trigger res_trig drop trigger table_trig on database drop trigger data_base on all server","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SUBQUERY: A sub query is a form of a SQL Statement that appears inside another sql","type":"concept","a":"• statement\n• It is also treate as nested query\n• The statement containing a Subquery is called Parent statements \n• The parent statement uses the rows returned by the subquery. create table sales_orders( order_no varchar(10), client_no varchar (10)) insert into sales_orders values('poo1','coo1'), ('poo2','coo2'),('poo3','coo1'),('poo4','coo3'),('poo5','coo3'), ('poo6','coo3'),('poo7','coo4') create table cilents( client_no varchar(20), person_name varchar(10), quality numeric (10)) insert into cilents values('coo1','name1',1),('coo2','name2',1), Page | 41 ('coo3','name3',1),('coo4','name4',1), ('coo5','name1',1),('coo6','name6',1) select * from sales_orders select * from cilents select * from cilents where person_name='name3' -- it is form of a sql statement appears inside the another sql statement select * from sales_orders where client_no=(select client_no from cilents where person_name='name3') select * from sales_orders where client_no not in (select client_no from cilents where person_name='name3')","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"GROUPING AND HAVING :","type":"concept","a":"• Group By: It is used to group rows based on distinct values that exist for specified columns ie it creates a data set containing several set of records grouped together based on a condition\n• Having : It is always used in conjuction with group by, It is imposes a condition on the group by clause which further filters the group created by the group by clause. --groups by create table sales_pro( product_name varchar(100), product_num varchar(10), quality_num int, quality_dis int ) insert into sales_pro values('pd1','pooo1',10,10), ('pd1','pooo1',9,9),('pd1','pooo1',8,8), ('pd2','pooo2',7,7), ('pd2','pooo2',8,8), ('pd3','pooo3',10,10), ('pd4','pooo4',5,5), ('pd4','pooo4',6,6), ('pd5','pooo5',9,9), ('pd5','pooo5',8,8), ('pd1','pooo1',10,10) select *from sales_pro select product_num,sum(quality_num) as \"total quality\" from sales_pro group by product_num -- group by it add rows having a same values in a colunm -- having condition select product_num,sum(quality_num) as \"total quality\" from sales_pro group by product_num having product_num in('pooo1','pooo5')-- this HAVING gives the filiters 19.FUNCTIONS: --functions: it is a return values --scaler function:it is a single value create function fun_sum(@var1 int,@var2 int,@var3 int) returns int as begin declare @sum int set @sum=@var1+@var2+@var3 Page | 42 return @sum end select dbo. fun_sum (100,200,300) as total alter function nrt_fun(@customerid varchar(100)) returns varchar(100) as begin declare @nrt_fun1 varchar(100) select @nrt_fun1=country from Customers where customerid=@customerid return @nrt_fun1 end select dbo.nrt_fun('usa') --table function retuen values select * from res create function tab_fun(@seat_no int) returns table as return (select * from res where @seat_no=seat_no) select * from tab_fun(87)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"TRANSACTIONS: A SET OF T-SQL STATEMENTS TO BE EXECUTED AS A SINGLE","type":"concept","a":"UNIT create table emp_tran( empid int, person_name varchar(100), salary int) select * from emp_tran --explicit transaction: it will commit and rollback begin tran-- only in this explicit insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) declare @row_count int select @row_count=count(*) from emp_tran if @row_count <=10 commit tran else rollback tran select count(*) from emp_tran truncate table emp_tran --implict tran: it is like explicit set implicit_transactions on--: imp command insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) declare @row_count int select @row_count=count(*) from emp_tran if @row_count <=10 commit tran else rollback tran Page | 44 select count(*) from emp_tran truncate table emp_tran --autocomtt tran & open transbegin tran insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) --isolation level --isolation level read and uncommitted set transaction ISOLATION level READ UNCOMMITTED insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) set transaction ISOLATION level READ COMMITTED insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) set transaction ISOLATION level SERIALIZABLE insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) --snapshot use master alter database [proce] set allow_snapshot_isolation on use [proce] set transaction isolation level snapshot begin tran insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) select * from emp_tran","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"QUEREY OPEATORS","type":"concept","a":"• -- TO HELP T-QUERIES PERTAINING TO SEARCHING AND SORTING ACTIVITIES, -- WE GENERALLY CREATE INDEXES CREATE TABLE EMP_DETAILS ( EMP_ID INT, EMP_NAME VARCHAR(20), EMP_SAL INT ) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1001,'SUNNY',1000) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1002,'PRIYANKA',1200) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1003,'RAVI',1060) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1004,'JEFF',2000) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1005,'JONATHAN',1220) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1006,'PETER',1044) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1007,'AMITABH',1456) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1008,'RAJENDRA',2100) Page | 45 INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1009,'ANUSHKA',1220) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(2001,'KAREENA',1880) SELECT * FROM EMP_DETAILS Index: It is used by queries to find data form table quickly\n• Indexs are created on tables & view , is very similar to an index that we find in a book. create index emp_details_index on emp_details(emp_name) SELECT EMP_NAME FROM EMP_DETAILS--index scan create nonclustered index emp_detalis_index_v2 on emp_details(emp_name) include(EMP_ID,EMP_SAL) select * from EMP_DETAILS where EMP_NAME='sunny'--index seek select * from EMP_DETAILS where EMP_NAME='xyz'--index seek","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Implicit Cursors: These cursor created by SQL server by default when select statement is","type":"concept","a":"executed.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Source 2 — Read Customer table from Azure SQL DB","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Filter — Remove rows where TotalRevenue < 0","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Aggregate — Group by Region, sum TotalRevenue","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Sink — Write results into Synapse Analytics table","type":"concept","a":"• 💡 Tip: When asked about Data Flow: always walk through Source → Transform → Sink\n• Mention that you used Debug mode to test, and that Data Flows run on auto-scaling Spark clusters\n• Common Data Flow Transformations to Know • Derived Column — Add or modify columns using expressions • Aggregate — GROUP BY with SUM, COUNT, AVG • Join — INNER, LEFT OUTER, RIGHT OUTER between two streams • Lookup — Similar to join but optimized for reference/dimension tables • Filter — Remove rows based on a condition • Select — Choose which columns to keep or rename • Conditional Split — Like a WHERE clause that routes rows to different paths • Flatten — Expand arrays/nested JSON into rows • Window — Rank, Row Number, Lead/Lag functions","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"What is Late Arriving Data?","type":"concept","a":"• Late arriving data means records that arrive after the expected processing window\n• For example: a transaction that happened on Monday but arrives in your system on Wednesday\n• Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 3 Example: You run a daily pipeline at midnight\n• A payment event from 11:58 PM arrives at","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Go to Databricks SQL → Alerts","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Create a query (e.g., SELECT COUNT(*) as failed_records FROM errors WHERE date =","type":"concept","a":"current_date())","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Employee and A(cid:425)endance Tables","type":"concept","a":"• \"I have Employee and A(cid:425)endance tables\n• How will you cleanse the data?\" Employee EmpID EmpName Role Manager A(cid:425)endance EmpID EmpName Date Loca(cid:415)on LoginTime LogoutTime Sample Answer: \"For the Employee table:  Remove duplicate Employee IDs using dropDuplicates()  Replace null employee names with 'Unknown' or another business-approved default  Validate mandatory fields For the A(cid:425)endance table:  Remove records where both LoginTime and LogoutTime are null  Instead of dele(cid:415)ng them permanently, store them in a Reject or Quaran(cid:415)ne table for future inves(cid:415)ga(cid:415)on\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Customers with no orders or whose latest order is older than 30 days should be","type":"concept","a":"returned.\" SELECT c.CustomerID, c.CustomerName FROM Customer c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID GROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.CustomerName HAVING MAX(o.OrderDate) IS NULL OR MAX(o.OrderDate) < DATEADD(DAY, -30, GETDATE());","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Joins in sql","type":"concept","a":"Returns Join Type Matching rows only INNER JOIN All le(cid:332) rows + matching right rows LEFT JOIN All right rows + matching le(cid:332) rows RIGHT JOIN All rows from both tables FULL OUTER JOIN Cartesian product CROSS JOIN Table joined with itself SELF JOIN","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize sql queries","type":"concept","a":"• To op(cid:415)mize SQL queries, I reduce data scanning by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, use indexing on join and filter columns, avoid func(cid:415)ons on indexed fields, push filters early, prefer EXISTS over IN for large datasets, and analyze execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks\n• I also use par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and avoid unnecessary opera(cid:415)ons like DISTINCT or correlated subqueries when possible a)\n• Select only required columns (avoid SELECT *) (cid:57872) Bad: SELECT * FROM Employee; ✔ Good: SELECT emp_id, name FROM Employee; (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Reduces I/O and memory usage b)\n• Use proper indexing  Create indexes on: o JOIN keys o WHERE clause columns o ORDER BY columns Example: CREATE INDEX idx_emp_dept ON Employee(dept_id); c).Avoid func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns (cid:57872) Bad: WHERE YEAR(join_date) = 2024; ✔ Good: WHERE join_date >= '2024-01-01' AND join_date < '2025-01-01'; (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Allows index usage d)\n• Filter data early (push filters) (cid:57872) Bad: SELECT * FROM A JOIN B ON A.id = B.id; ✔ Good: SELECT * FROM ( SELECT * FROM A WHERE status = 'ac(cid:415)ve' ) A JOIN B ON A.id = B.id; e)\n• Use EXISTS instead of IN (for large datasets) (cid:57872) IN: SELECT * FROM Employee WHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM Dept); ✔ EXISTS: SELECT * FROM Employee e WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM Dept d WHERE d.dept_id = e.dept_id ); f)\n• Op(cid:415)mize JOINs  Ensure join columns are indexed  Prefer INNER JOIN over OUTER JOIN when possible g)\n• Avoid unnecessary DISTINCT / ORDER BY  DISTINCT adds sor(cid:415)ng overhead  ORDER BY should be used only if needed h)\n• Use LIMIT / TOP for large datasets SELECT * FROM Orders LIMIT 100; i)\n• Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on large tables  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on by date / region / key column  Reduces scan (cid:415)me dras(cid:415)cally Example: PARTITION BY (order_date) j)\n• Use execu(cid:415)on plan analysis  Use: EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM Employee;\n• Check:  Full table scan  Index usage  Join strategy k)","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"write a sql query to get emp id, first name, last name, dept name for those employees","type":"concept","a":"who get the 2nd highest salary Assume table: Employee  emp_id  first_name  last_name  dept_id  salary Department  dept_id  dept_name (cid:33158)(cid:57393) SQL Query (2nd highest salary employees) WITH ranked_emp AS ( SELECT e.emp_id, e.first_name, e.last_name, d.dept_name, e.salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id ) SELECT emp_id, first_name, last_name, dept_name FROM ranked_emp WHERE rnk = 2;","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Why use HAVING instead of WHERE?","type":"concept","a":"•  WHERE filters rows before aggrega(cid:415)on.  HAVING filters groups a(cid:332)er aggrega(cid:415)on\n• For example, this is incorrect: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE SUM(amount) > 500; -- (cid:57872) Invalid This is correct: SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total_spending FROM Orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(amount) > 500;\n• This dis(cid:415)nc(cid:415)on is a frequent SQL interview topic\n• What are the read modes available in spark? How stages, tasks,jobs created in spark? How to call one pipeline from another pipeline in ADF? How to pass variables to pipeline in ADF? What are all the configura(cid:415)on se(cid:427)ngs have to provided to call the databricks workflow via ADF? How to read csv files and create and create a dataframe and write to delta format in ADLS? Explain me the spark architecture? what are all the indexes present in sql and types of it? create a dataframe using [(1,),(3,),(5,)] and find out the missing values in it? A=[1,2,3] and B=[1,2,3] what is the output of A==B,B==A,A is B What is the difference between par(cid:415)(cid:415)on and bucke(cid:415)ng in spark? Difference between managed and external tables in databricks? op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in spark? Below are interview-ready answers for each ques(cid:415)on, suitable for Azure Data Engineer (Azure Databricks + ADF + PySpark + SQL) interviews","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Indexes and Types","type":"concept","a":"• Clustered Index  Physically sorts table data.  Only one clustered index per table\n• Non-Clustered Index  Separate structure storing key + pointer.  Mul(cid:415)ple allowed\n• Unique Index  Prevents duplicate values\n• Composite Index  Built on mul(cid:415)ple columns\n• Filtered Index  Indexes only rows matching a condi(cid:415)on\n• Full-Text Index  Op(cid:415)mized for text searching","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Create DataFrame and find missing values","type":"concept","a":"• Input: data = [(1,), (3,), (5,)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, [\"num\"]) Expected sequence: 1 2 3 4 5 Solu(cid:415)on: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col expected = spark.range(1, 6).toDF(\"num\") missing = expected.join(df, \"num\", \"le(cid:332)_an(cid:415)\") missing.show() Output: 2 4 10\n• Output of A = [1,2,3] B = [1,2,3] print(A == B) print(B == A) print(A is B) Output: True True False Explana(cid:415)on:  == compares values.  is compares object iden(cid:415)ty (memory address)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What are the source systems?","type":"concept","a":"Examples:  Web Applica(cid:415)ons  SQL Server  Oracle  SAP  REST APIs  CSV files  Excel files","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How is data flowing from web applica(cid:415)ons to SQL Server?","type":"concept","a":"Users ↓ Web Applica(cid:415)on ↓ Applica(cid:415)on Server ↓ SQL Server The applica(cid:415)on writes transac(cid:415)onal data into SQL Server using applica(cid:415)on APIs or ORM frameworks.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Who loads web applica(cid:415)on data into SQL Server?","type":"concept","a":"• The applica(cid:415)on layer is responsible for inser(cid:415)ng and upda(cid:415)ng data\n• Data engineers consume that data for analy(cid:415)cs; they do not typically load it into SQL Server","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What transforma(cid:415)ons happen where?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server  Stored procedures  Basic filtering  Simple joins  Incremental extrac(cid:415)on Databricks  Data cleansing  Null handling  Deduplica(cid:415)on  Business rules  SCD implementa(cid:415)on  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  Delta tables Synapse  Repor(cid:415)ng views  Star schema  Aggregated datasets","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find customers who never placed an order","type":"concept","a":"SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID=o.CustomerID WHERE o.CustomerID IS NULL;","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Today's temperature higher than yesterday","type":"concept","a":"SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, LAG(Temperature) OVER(ORDER BY Date) AS PrevTemp FROM Weather )t WHERE Temperature>PrevTemp;","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid recomputa(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Use:  Cache  Persist  Delta intermediate tables  Materialized views (where applicable)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produc(cid:415)on issue when Dev differs","type":"concept","a":" Recreate the scenario using representa(cid:415)ve data.  Align configura(cid:415)on where possible.  Test in QA/UAT before Produc(cid:415)on deployment.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you connect on-prem SQL Server to Azure?","type":"concept","a":"A common approach is:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"SCD Type 1, 2, and 3 — SQL Code","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 1 — Overwrite (Simple UPDATE) -- SCD Type 1: Just overwrite the existing record MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND (target.name <> source.name OR target.email <> source.email) THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n• SCD Type 2 — Full History with Effective Dates -- Step 1: Expire existing active records that have changed MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id AND target.is_current = 1 WHEN MATCHED AND (target.name <> source.name OR target.city <> source.city) THEN UPDATE SET target.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, target.is_current = 0; -- Step 2: Insert new active rows for changed records INSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current) SELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE AS start_date, NULL AS end_date, 1 AS is_current FROM staging_customer s INNER JOIN dim_customer d ON s.customer_id = d.customer_id AND d.is_current = 0 AND d.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY UNION ALL -- Insert brand new customers SELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1 FROM staging_customer s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id = s.customer_id);\n• SCD Type 2 using Delta Lake MERGE (Recommended) -- Cleaner approach using Delta Lake MERGE with CDF MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING ( -- Changed records: expire old row SELECT d.surrogate_key, d.customer_id, d.name, d.city, d.start_date, CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY AS end_date, 0 AS is_current FROM dim_customer d INNER JOIN staging_customer s ON d.customer_id = s.customer_id WHERE d.is_current = 1 Page 6 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide AND (d.name <> s.name OR d.city <> s.city) UNION ALL -- New rows for changed + new customers SELECT NULL, s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1 FROM staging_customer s ) AS source ON target.surrogate_key = source.surrogate_key WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET target.end_date = source.end_date, target.is_current = 0 WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.city, source.start_date, source.end_date, source.is_current);\n• SCD Type 3 — Previous Value Column -- SCD Type 3: Keep current and one previous value MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND target.city <> source.city THEN UPDATE SET target.prev_city = target.city, target.city = source.city, target.updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unmatched Records from Left Table Only — Left Anti Join","type":"concept","a":"• A LEFT ANTI JOIN returns rows from the LEFT table that have NO matching row in the RIGHT table\n• It is the opposite of an INNER JOIN\n• SQL -- Get all orders that have NO matching customer in the customer table SELECT o.* FROM orders o LEFT ANTI JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id;\n• Page 10 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide -- Alternative using NOT EXISTS SELECT * FROM orders o WHERE NOT EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM customers c WHERE c.customer_id = o.customer_id ); -- Alternative using NOT IN (caution: behaves differently with NULLs) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id IS NOT NULL);\n• PySpark # Left Anti Join in PySpark unmatched = orders_df.join(customers_df, on='customer_id', how='left_anti') unmatched.show() # Equivalent using filter + left join unmatched = orders_df.join(customers_df, on='customer_id', how='left') .filter(col('customers_df.customer_id').isNull()) Join Type Returns INNER JOIN Only matching rows from both tables LEFT JOIN All rows from left + NULLs for unmatched right RIGHT JOIN All rows from right + NULLs for unmatched left LEFT ANTI JOIN Only left rows with NO match in right LEFT SEMI JOIN Only left rows that DO have a match in right (no right columns) FULL OUTER JOIN All rows from both — NULLs where no match","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Second Highest Salary by Department","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — dense_rank() Window Function (Recommended) SELECT dept, name, salary FROM ( SELECT dept, name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees ) ranked WHERE rnk = 2;\n• Method 2 — PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) df_ranked = df.withColumn('rnk', dense_rank().over(w)) df_ranked.filter(col('rnk') == 2).select('dept','name','salary').show() Page 11 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Method 3 — Subquery (without window function) SELECT dept, MAX(salary) AS second_highest_salary FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e2.dept = employees.dept) GROUP BY dept;\n• Function Ties Behavior Best for dense_rank() Same rank for ties; no gaps Top-N salary — most common interview answer rank() Same rank for ties; gaps after ties Ranking where gaps matter row_number() Unique number always; no ties When you need exactly one row per rank","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Validation Checks Before Moving Data to the Next Layer","type":"concept","a":"• Page 13 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Validation What to Check PySpark / SQL Example Schema Validation Column names, data types df.schema == expected_schema match expected schema Null Checks Critical columns must not be df.filter(col('id').isNull()).count() == 0 null Duplicate Checks No duplicate records on df.count() == primary key df.dropDuplicates(['id']).count() Data Type Validation Values match expected type Try cast; check for null after cast (int, date, etc.) Range / Domain Validation Numeric ranges, valid enum df.filter((col('age')<0)|(col('age')>150)) values Referential Integrity Foreign key exists in parent Left anti join between child and parent table Record Count Validation Row count matches source or abs(source_count - target_count) <= expected threshold threshold Business Rule Validation Custom business logic (e.g., df.filter(col('end_date') < col('start_date')) end_date > start_date) # Example: Validation framework in PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import col def validate_layer(df, layer_name): errors = [] # 1\n• Null check on key columns null_count = df.filter(col('id').isNull()).count() if null_count > 0: errors.append(f'NULL id count: {null_count}') # 2\n• Duplicate check total = df.count() dedup = df.dropDuplicates(['id']).count() if total != dedup: errors.append(f'Duplicates found: {total - dedup}') # 3\n• Range check invalid_age = df.filter((col('age') < 0) | (col('age') > 150)).count() if invalid_age > 0: errors.append(f'Invalid age values: {invalid_age}') if errors: raise Exception(f'{layer_name} validation FAILED: {errors}') else: print(f'{layer_name} validation PASSED ({total} rows)') validate_layer(silver_df, 'Silver')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Fill nulls with derived values","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, mean, lit # Fill with column mean avg_salary = df.select(mean('salary')).first()[0] df_clean = df.fillna({'salary': avg_salary}) # Conditional fill using when() df_clean = df.withColumn('salary', when(col('salary').isNull(), 0).otherwise(col('salary'))) # Fill with previous value (forward fill using Window) from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import last w = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy('date').rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, 0) df_clean = df.withColumn('salary', last('salary', ignorenulls=True).over(w))","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Filter out nulls","type":"concept","a":"df_clean = df.filter(col('id').isNotNull()) df_clean = df.where(col('name').isNotNull() & col('email').isNotNull())","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Full Load vs Incremental Load from SQL Server","type":"concept","a":"Page 18 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Aspect Full Load Incremental Load What Load ALL data every run Load only NEW or CHANGED data since last run When to use First-time historical load; small Large tables; daily ETL; tables with tables; no CDC support last_modified column Source impact High — full table scan every run Low — only reads delta records Processing time Long Short Complexity Simple Needs watermark tracking Risk Overwrites may cause data loss if Must handle late-arriving data not careful Full Load Code # Full load from SQL Server df_full = spark.read.jdbc( url='jdbc:sqlserver://server.db.windows.net:1433;database=db', table='dbo.Customers', properties={'user': 'user', 'password': 'pwd', 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'} ) df_full.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.customers') Incremental Load Code (Watermark-based) # Read last watermark from control table last_wm = spark.sql(\"SELECT MAX(last_load_ts) FROM ctrl.watermark WHERE table_name='Customers'\").first()[0] # Load only changed records df_incr = spark.read.jdbc( url='jdbc:sqlserver://server.db.windows.net:1433;database=db', table='dbo.Customers', predicates=[f\"ModifiedDate > '{last_wm}'\"], properties={'user': 'user', 'password': 'pwd', 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'} ) # Merge into target (upsert) df_incr.createOrReplaceTempView('incr') spark.sql(''' MERGE INTO silver.customers t USING incr s ON t.customer_id = s.customer_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT * ''') # Update watermark spark.sql(f\"UPDATE ctrl.watermark SET last_load_ts=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE table_name='Customers'\")","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL – Same Emp Name, Different Department (No JOIN)","type":"concept","a":"• Schema EMP (Emp_id, E_name, E_mid, E_mname, E_loc) DEPT (D_id, D_name, D_mid, D_loc) Approach — Using EXISTS / Subquery (No JOIN Keyword) -- Find employees who share the same name but belong to different departments SELECT e1.Emp_id, e1.E_name, e1.E_mid AS dept1, e2.E_mid AS dept2 FROM EMP e1, EMP e2 WHERE e1.E_name = e2.E_name AND e1.E_mid <> e2.E_mid AND e1.Emp_id < e2.Emp_id; -- avoid duplicate pairs Alternative — Using EXISTS SELECT DISTINCT e1.Emp_id, e1.E_name, e1.E_mid AS department FROM EMP e1 WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM EMP e2 WHERE e2.E_name = e1.E_name AND e2.E_mid <> e1.E_mid Page 9 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A );\n• Note: Both solutions use comma-separated FROM (implicit cross join notation) or subquery — NO explicit JOIN keyword is used","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Join Counts – Inner, Left, Right","type":"concept","a":"• Given Data Table A – ID Table B – ID 1 1 1 3 2 1 NULL 4 NULL 5 3 Join Results Join Type Logic Count Explanation INNER JOIN Only matching rows 3 IDs 1,1,1 (A has two 1s;\n• B has one 1) → 2 (A.ID = B.ID) rows for ID=1, plus ID=3 → total 3 rows\n• NULLs never match\n• LEFT JOIN All rows from A + 6 All 6 rows from A are kept\n• Matching B rows matches from B filled in;\n• NULLs from A and unmatched rows get NULL on B side\n• RIGHT JOIN All rows from B + 7 All 5 rows from B are kept\n• ID=1 in B matches from A matches 2 rows in A → 2 rows\n• IDs 3,4,5 from B each get 1 row; total 7\n• Detailed Breakdown Table A: [1, 1, 2, NULL, NULL, 3] → 6 rows Table B: [1, 3, 1, 4, 5] → 5 rows • INNER JOIN: Match A.ID=1 (×2) with B.ID=1 (×1) = 2 rows;\n• A.ID=3 with B.ID=3 = 1 row → Total = 3 • LEFT JOIN: All 6 A rows kept;\n• B columns NULL where no match (A.ID=2, NULL, NULL have no B match) → Total = 6 Page 10 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A • RIGHT JOIN: All 5 B rows kept;","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Warehouse (DWH) is a centralized, structured repository that stores large volumes of historical, integrated data from multiple source systems\n• It is optimized for analytical querying and reporting rather than transactional processing\n• Key Characteristics Property Description Subject-Oriented Organized around key business subjects: Sales, Finance, HR — not by application Integrated Data consolidated from multiple heterogeneous sources (CRM, ERP, flat files) Non-Volatile Once loaded, data is not updated or deleted — historical record is preserved Time-Variant Maintains historical snapshots; every record has a time dimension Read-Optimized Schema and indexes designed for fast analytical reads (not transactional writes) DWH vs OLTP vs Data Lake Aspect OLTP (Operational Data Warehouse Data Lake DB) Purpose Day-to-day transactions Analytics & reporting Raw data storage for all types Data type Current, real-time Historical, structured Raw — structured + unstructured Schema Schema-on-write (strict) Schema-on-write Schema-on-read (flexible) Query speed Fast for single rows Fast for aggregations Slower without optimization Example SQL Server, MySQL Snowflake, Synapse ADLS, S3, Databricks Lakehouse Common Schemas in a Data Warehouse • Star Schema: One central Fact table linked to multiple Dimension tables — simple, fast queries • Snowflake Schema: Dimensions are further normalized into sub-dimensions — saves space, more joins • Galaxy Schema: Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables — for complex enterprises Page 3 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is Data Cleansing and How is it Done?","type":"concept","a":"• Data cleansing (also called data cleaning or data scrubbing) is the process of detecting and correcting inaccurate, incomplete, duplicate, or irrelevant records in a dataset to improve data quality before analysis or loading into a warehouse\n• Common Data Quality Issues Issue Example Fix Missing / NULL Salary = NULL, Email = Fill with default, mean/median, or flag as values NULL unknown Duplicate records Same customer appears Deduplicate using dropDuplicates() or twice with same ID ROW_NUMBER() Incorrect data types Salary stored as '2500' Cast to correct type; handle parse errors (string) instead of integer Inconsistent formats Date as '01-Jan-2024' in one Standardize using to_date() with format file, '2024-01-01' in another pattern Outliers / Invalid Age = -5, Salary = 99999999 Apply business-rule filters; flag or exclude values outliers Whitespace / Case ' John ' vs 'john' vs 'JOHN' trim() + lower() / upper() for normalization issues Referential integrity Order has Customer_ID that Left join + filter nulls to find orphan records doesn't exist in Customer table Special characters Name = 'O\\u2019Brien' vs regexp_replace() to clean special chars 'OBrien' PySpark Data Cleansing Code Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F # 1\n• Remove duplicates df = df.dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) # 2\n• Handle NULL values df = df.fillna({'salary': 0, 'emp_name': 'Unknown', 'location': 'N/A'}) # 3\n• Fix data types df = df.withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) df = df.withColumn('join_date', F.to_date(F.col('join_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd')) # 4\n• Trim whitespace and standardize case df = df.withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) # 5\n• Remove outliers (salary must be between 1000 and 500000) df = df.filter(F.col('salary').between(1000, 500000)) Page 10 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # 6\n• Remove special characters from name df = df.withColumn('emp_name', F.regexp_replace(F.col('emp_name'), '[^a-zA-Z ]', '')) # 7\n• Flag records with remaining NULLs df = df.withColumn('has_nulls', F.when(F.col('emp_id').isNull() | F.col('emp_name').isNull(), True).otherwise(False)) df.show() Page 11 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What are Window Functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Window functions perform calculations across a set of rows that are related to the current row — similar to aggregate functions but WITHOUT collapsing rows into a single result\n• Each row retains its identity while gaining access to aggregated or ranked values from a defined window (partition)\n• Syntax Structure function_name() OVER ( PARTITION BY column1, column2 -- defines the group/window ORDER BY column3 [ASC|DESC] -- defines row ordering within window ROWS/RANGE BETWEEN \n• AND ... -- optional frame specification ) Types of Window Functions Category Function Description Ranking ROW_NUMBER() Unique sequential number per row within partition — no ties Ranking RANK() Same rank for ties; gaps in sequence after ties (1,1,3) Ranking DENSE_RANK() Same rank for ties;\n• NO gaps (1,1,2) Ranking NTILE(n) Divides rows into n equal buckets Ranking PERCENT_RANK() Relative rank as a percentage (0 to 1) Aggregate SUM() OVER(...) Running or partitioned sum without collapsing rows Aggregate AVG() OVER(...) Running or partitioned average Aggregate COUNT() OVER(...) Count per window — useful for % of total Aggregate MIN() / MAX() Min/max within the window OVER(...) Navigation LAG(col, n) Access value from n rows BEFORE current row Navigation LEAD(col, n) Access value from n rows AFTER current row Navigation FIRST_VALUE(col) First value in the window frame Navigation LAST_VALUE(col) Last value in the window frame PySpark Window Function Examples from pyspark.sql import Window from pyspark.sql import functions as F # Define window w = Window.partitionBy('department').orderBy(F.desc('salary')) # ROW_NUMBER -- unique rank per department df = df.withColumn('row_num', F.row_number().over(w)) Page 12 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # RANK -- same rank for ties, gaps after ties df = df.withColumn('rank', F.rank().over(w)) # DENSE_RANK -- same rank for ties, no gaps df = df.withColumn('dense_rank', F.dense_rank().over(w)) # Running total salary within department w2 = Window.partitionBy('department').orderBy('join_date') \\ .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow) df = df.withColumn('running_salary', F.sum('salary').over(w2)) # LAG -- previous row's salary df = df.withColumn('prev_salary', F.lag('salary', 1).over(w)) # Get top 1 earner per department top_earners = df.filter(F.col('row_num') == 1) Page 13 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You stored a table in Databricks — what format is it likely to be and where is the","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"max sales per customer?","type":"concept","a":"Input Data: customer_id | transaction_date | sales cust1 | 2023-12-01 | 100 cust2 | 2023-12-02 | 150 cust1 | 2023-12-03 | 200 cust2 | 2023-12-04 | 250 Expected Output: cust_id | sales_max cust1 | 200 cust2 | 250","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used databricks sql warehouse?","type":"concept","a":"Databricks SQL Warehouse  Serverless compute for BI queries  Used for Power BI / Tableau dashboards","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Diff between rank and dense rank","type":"concept","a":"Rank vs Dense Rank Func(cid:415)on Output RANK 1,1,3 DENSE_RANK 1,1,2","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you op(cid:415)mize sql query?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on  Indexing  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Avoid SELECT *  Filter early  Proper joins","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you do the par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in sql?","type":"concept","a":"Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in SQL  Splits data physically for performance  Usually date-based par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Transac(cid:415)ons table:","type":"concept","a":"+ — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — | id | dept | state | amount | date | + — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — | 1 | IT | approved | 1000 | 2018–12–18 | | 2 | IT | declined | 2000 | 2018–12–19 | | 3 | IT | approved | 2000 | 2019–01–01 | | 4 | Fin | approved | 2000 | 2019–01–07 | + — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — Output: + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — — --------------+ | month | dept | trans_count | approved_trans_cnt | total_amount | approved_total_amount | + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — -------------— -+ | 2018–12 | IT | 2 | 1 | 3000 | 1000 | | 2019–01 | IT | 1 | 1 | 2000 | 2000 | | 2019–01 | Fin | 1 | 1 | 2000 | 2000 | + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — — --------------+ Write a SQL query to find:  Month  Department  Total transac(cid:415)ons  Approved transac(cid:415)on count  Total amount  Approved amount Concepts Tested  GROUP BY  CASE WHEN  Aggregate func(cid:415)ons  DATE_FORMAT SELECT DATE_FORMAT(date,'%Y-%m') AS month, dept, COUNT(*) AS trans_count, SUM(CASE WHEN state='approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) approved_trans_cnt, SUM(amount) total_amount, SUM(CASE WHEN state='approved' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) approved_total_amount FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(date,'%Y-%m'), dept;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Reverse Route Distance","type":"concept","a":"Id | Place1 |Place2 |Distance | ------------------------------------| 1 |Hyd | Mumbai | 1200 | 2 |Banglore | Pune | 400 | 3 |Mumbai | Hyd | 1400 | 4 |Pune | Bangalore | 400 | 5 |Chennai |Hyd | 1200 | ------------------------------------| Output: -------------------------------- Place1 |Place2 |Distance | -------------------------------| Hyd | Mumbai | 2600 | Banglore | Pune | 800 | Chennai | Hyd | 1200 | -------------------------------- Input Hyd → Mumbai Mumbai → Hyd Output Hyd Mumbai 2600 SELECT LEAST(place1,place2) AS place1, GREATEST(place1,place2) AS place2, SUM(distance) total_distance FROM Routes GROUP BY LEAST(place1,place2), GREATEST(place1,place2);","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find Second Highest Salary","type":"concept","a":"SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee WHERE salary < ( SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee );","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Highest Salary in Each Department","type":"concept","a":"SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) rnk FROM Employee )t WHERE rnk=1;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Remove Duplicate Records","type":"concept","a":"• SELECT DISTINCT * FROM Employee;\n• Using ROW_NUMBER WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY id ORDER BY id) rn FROM Employee ) SELECT * FROM cte WHERE rn=1;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between UNION and UNION ALL","type":"concept","a":"UNION UNION ALL Removes duplicates Keeps duplicates Slower Faster","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between WHERE and HAVING","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows before aggrega(cid:415)on\n• HAVING filters groups a(cid:332)er aggrega(cid:415)on\n• PySpark Ques(cid:415)ons","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow vs Wide Transforma(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow No shuffle\n• Examples  map  filter  union Wide Shuffle happens\n• Examples  join  groupBy  reduceByKey  dis(cid:415)nct","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Top 2 items per category based on sales","type":"concept","a":"• sales itemId,categoryId,amount apple,fruit,100 apple,fruit,100 apple,fruit,100 mango,fruit,200 pear,fruit,150 shirt,fashion,5000 jeans,fashion,3000 jeans,fashion,3000 (cid:415)e,fashion,1000 apple,mobile,100000 Answer: SELECT itemId, categoryId, SUM(amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY itemId, categoryId ), ranked_sales AS ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY categoryId ORDER BY total_sales DESC ) AS rnk FROM item_sales ) SELECT itemId, categoryId, total_sales FROM ranked_sales WHERE rnk <= 2;\n• Pyspark: from pyspark.sql import func(cid:415)ons as F from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Step 1: aggregate df_agg = df.groupBy(\"itemId\", \"categoryId\") \\ .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total_sales\")) # Step 2: window ranking window_spec = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"categoryId\").orderBy(F.desc(\"total_sales\")) df_ranked = df_agg.withColumn(\"rnk\", F.dense_rank().over(window_spec)) # Step 3: filter top 2 result = df_ranked.filter(F.col(\"rnk\") <= 2) result.show()","source":"Fractal Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to find customers who spent more than ₹1000 in the last month","type":"concept","a":"using Orders and Order_Items tables Assume: Orders |OrderID|CustomerID|OrderDate| Order_Items |OrderID|Quantity|UnitPrice| SELECT o.CustomerID, SUM(oi.Quantity * oi.UnitPrice) AS TotalSpent FROM Orders o JOIN Order_Items oi ON o.OrderID = oi.OrderID WHERE o.OrderDate >= DATEADD(month, -1, GETDATE()) GROUP BY o.CustomerID HAVING SUM(oi.Quantity * oi.UnitPrice) > 1000;","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Follow-up: Why use HAVING instead of WHERE?","type":"concept","a":"Answer: HAVING filters aggregated results after GROUP BY, whereas WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation.","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Find the top 3 customers by total purchase amount per month using window","type":"concept","a":"functions? WITH MonthlySales AS ( SELECT CustomerID, YEAR(OrderDate) AS Year, MONTH(OrderDate) AS Month, SUM(TotalAmount) AS TotalPurchase FROM Orders GROUP BY CustomerID, YEAR(OrderDate), MONTH(OrderDate) ) SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY Year,Month ORDER BY TotalPurchase DESC ) RankNo FROM MonthlySales )t WHERE RankNo<=3;","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a query to calculate net revenue per day considering returns data?","type":"concept","a":"• Tables Orders |OrderID|OrderDate|Amount| Returns |OrderID|ReturnAmount| SELECT o.OrderDate, SUM(o.Amount)-COALESCE(SUM(r.ReturnAmount),0) AS NetRevenue FROM Orders o LEFT JOIN Returns r ON o.OrderID=r.OrderID GROUP BY o.OrderDate;\n• If no returns exist, COALESCE() avoids NULL","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark: Join two DataFrames using row numbers","type":"concept","a":"Suppose IDs don't match. from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number windowSpec = Window.orderBy(\"Name\") df1 = df1.withColumn( \"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec) ) df2 = df2.withColumn( \"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec) ) result = df1.join(df2,\"rn\").drop(\"rn\")","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a PySpark job to remove duplicate records from a large dataset efficiently.","type":"concept","a":"• Simple approach df = df.dropDuplicates() For selected columns df = df.dropDuplicates([\"CustomerID\"]) For large datasets from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number,col windowSpec = Window.partitionBy(\"CustomerID\") \\ .orderBy(col(\"UpdatedDate\").desc()) df = df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec)) df = df.filter(\"rn=1\").drop(\"rn\") This retains the latest record per customer\n• Round 2 – Technical Coding","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Describe a situation where you had to balance delivery speed with data quality","type":"concept","a":"\"I never compromise data accuracy for speed.\" Instead  Deliver critical fields first  Run validations  Automate testing  Release incrementally","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a query to get employees whose salary increased compared to the previous","type":"concept","a":"• year\n• Use the LAG() window function to fetch each employee's salary from the previous year, then filter where the current salary is greater\n• WITH cte AS ( SELECT emp_id, salary, year, LAG(salary, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY emp_id ORDER BY year) AS prev_sal FROM employee ) SELECT * FROM cte WHERE salary > prev_sal;\n• How it works: • PARTITION BY emp_id — computes LAG independently for each employee. • ORDER BY year — ensures LAG looks at the immediately preceding year. • The outer WHERE salary > prev_sal filters only those whose pay went up\n• TRUNCATE vs DELETE vs DROP","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between TRUNCATE, DELETE, and DROP?","type":"concept","a":"Feature TRUNCATE DELETE DROP Removes Data All rows Selected/all rows Entire table Structure Kept Kept Removed Rollback (TXN) No (DDL) Yes (DML) No (DDL) WHERE clause Not allowed Allowed N/A Triggers fired No Yes No Speed Fastest Slower Instant WHERE Clause vs HAVING Clause","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between GROUP BY and PARTITION BY?","type":"concept","a":"• GROUP BY collapses rows into one row per group — it reduces the result set. • PARTITION BY is used inside window functions (OVER clause) — it divides rows into partitions but DOES NOT reduce the result set; all original rows are returned. Example: -- GROUP BY: returns 1 row per dept SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept; -- PARTITION BY: returns all rows, adds avg per dept as a column SELECT emp_id, dept, salary, AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) AS dept_avg FROM emp;","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"How do you optimize a Mapping Data Flow in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Partitioning wisely — choose Round Robin for uniform distribution; use Hash partition on join keys to reduce data shuffle. • Enable staging — use PolyBase for Synapse sinks to speed up loading. • Push down transformations — filter and select only required columns early in the flow to reduce data volume. • Use Broadcast joins — for small lookup datasets, broadcast the smaller side to avoid shuffle joins. • Increase cluster size (Compute Optimized or Memory Optimized) for heavy transformations. • Avoid unnecessary sorts — sorts are expensive; only sort when required (e.g., for rank or window functions). • Cache sink — use in-memory lookup cache for repeated lookups on the same small dataset. • Monitor with Debug mode first, then use activity run metrics (rows processed, time per stage) to identify bottlenecks. 3. Databricks Standard vs Premium vs Enterprise Tiers","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to get employees that earn more than their managers","type":"concept","a":"• SQL: Employees earning more than their managers SELECT e.emp_id, e.emp_name, e.salary FROM employee e JOIN employee m ON e.manager_id = m.emp_id WHERE e.salary > m.salary;\n• Logic:  Self join on employee table  Compare employee salary with manager salary","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delete duplicate records","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose duplicates are based on: customer_id name email_id order_date SQL WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id ) rn FROM customer ) DELETE FROM customer WHERE (customer_id,name,email_id,order_date) IN ( SELECT customer_id,name,email_id,order_date FROM cte WHERE rn>1 );\n• If using Delta Lake, a more typical approach is: CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id ) rn FROM customer ) WHERE rn = 1;\n• PySpark from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import row_number window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy( \"customer_id\", \"name\", \"email_id\", \"order_date\" ).orderBy(\"customer_id\") df = df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(window)) df = df.filter(\"rn=1\").drop(\"rn\")","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Find the most frequently occurring item for each date.","type":"concept","a":"• -- schema CREATE TABLE Items ( Date date NOT NULL, Item INT NOT NULL ); -- data INSERT INTO Items (Date, Item) VALUES ('2022-09-01', 1), ('2022-09-01', 3), ('2022-09-01', 2), ('2022-09-01', 6), ('2022-09-01', 1), ('2022-09-02', 2), ('2022-09-02', 2), ('2022-09-02', 4) ;\n• Use this query: WITH Item_CTE AS ( SELECT Date, Item, COUNT(*) AS Cnt FROM Items GROUP BY Date, Item ), Rank_Item AS ( SELECT Date, Item, Cnt, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY Date ORDER BY Cnt DESC ) AS Rank_I FROM Item_CTE ) SELECT Date, Item, Cnt FROM Rank_Item WHERE Rank_I = 1;\n• Output Date Item Cnt 2022-09-01 1 2 2022-09-02 2 2","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Write a func(cid:415)on that takes in an array of at least three integers and, without sor(cid:415)ng the","type":"concept","a":"• input array, returns a sorted array of the three largest integers in the input array\n• The func(cid:415)on should return duplicate integers if necessary; for example, it should return [10, 10, 12] for an input array of [10, 5, 9, 10, 12]. def find_three_largest_numbers(array): first = second = third = float('-inf') for num in array: if num > first: third = second second = first first = num elif num > second: third = second second = num elif num > third: third = num return [third, second, first] (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Example arr = [10, 5, 9, 10, 12] print(find_three_largest_numbers(arr)) Output: [10, 10, 12] How it works For each number:  If it's bigger than the largest → shi(cid:332) all down  Else if bigger than second → shi(cid:332) second and third  Else if bigger than third → update third Round 2: SQL Ques(cid:415)ons","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Return Top 3 User_id","type":"concept","a":"• Transac(cid:415)on Table: transac(cid:415)on_id,user_id , date;\n• Answer: SELECT user_id, cnt FROM ( SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS cnt, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id ) t WHERE rnk <= 3;\n• OR If interviewer wants strictly top 3 users (no (cid:415)es): SELECT user_id, cnt FROM ( SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS cnt, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id ) t WHERE rnk <= 3;","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Compare transac(cid:415)on counts per user between consecu(cid:415)ve dates and find users whose","type":"concept","a":"ac(cid:415)vity increased” Step 1: Aggregate WITH cte AS ( SELECT user_id, date, COUNT(transac(cid:415)on_id) AS cnt FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id, date ) Step 2: Compare with previous day SELECT c2.user_id FROM cte c1 JOIN cte c2 ON c1.user_id = c2.user_id AND c2.date = DATE_ADD(c1.date, 1) WHERE c2.cnt > c1.cnt; (cid:55982)(cid:55983)(cid:55984) Explana(cid:415)on  Compare same user across 2 consecu(cid:415)ve days  Check if today's transac(cid:415)ons > yesterday Pyspark : from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"user_id\").orderBy(\"date\") df_agg = df.groupBy(\"user_id\", \"date\") \\ .agg(F.count(\"transac(cid:415)on_id\").alias(\"cnt\")) df_final = df_agg.withColumn(\"prev_cnt\", F.lag(\"cnt\").over(w)) result = df_final.filter(F.col(\"cnt\") > F.col(\"prev_cnt\")) \\ .select(\"user_id\", \"date\", \"cnt\", \"prev_cnt\") result.show()","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Split comma-separated sports into rows and group by Sport","type":"concept","a":"Input Name Sport Alice Badminton, Tennis Greg Cricket, Baseball Julie Swimming, Basket ball Alan Tennis, Swimming Xian Badminton, Baseball Expected Output Name Sport Alice Badminton Xian Badminton Alice Tennis Alan Tennis Greg Baseball Xian Baseball Julie Swimming Alan Swimming Greg Cricket Julie Basket ball PySpark Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql import func(cid:415)ons as F # Split the sports into an array df1 = df.withColumn(\"Sport\", F.split(F.col(\"Sport\"), \",\\\\s*\")) # Convert array into mul(cid:415)ple rows df2 = df1.withColumn(\"Sport\", F.explode(\"Sport\")) # Sort by Sport (op(cid:415)onal, to match expected output) result = df2.orderBy(\"Sport\") result.show(truncate=False) Func(cid:415)ons used:  split()  explode()  orderBy()","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Group values into an array","type":"concept","a":"Input Col1 Col2 Col3 a aa 1 a aa 2 b bb 5 b bb 3 b bb 4 Expected Output Col1 Col2 Col3 a aa [1,2] b bb [5,3,4] Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_list result = df.groupBy(\"Col1\",\"Col2\") \\ .agg(collect_list(\"Col3\").alias(\"Col3\")) result.show(truncate=False) If duplicate values should be removed: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_set result = df.groupBy(\"Col1\",\"Col2\") \\ .agg(collect_set(\"Col3\").alias(\"Col3\"))","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark: 1st & 2nd Highest Salary Per Department","type":"concept","a":"• Window Function with dense_rank() — Recommended from pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window data = [('Alice', 'Engineering', 90000), ('Bob', 'Engineering', 85000), ('Carol', 'Engineering', 85000), ('Dave', 'Marketing', 70000), ('Eve', 'Marketing', 65000)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['name', 'dept', 'salary']) window = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) df_ranked = df.withColumn('rank', dense_rank().over(window)) result = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') <= 2) result.show() Function Behavior on Ties Output Example rank() Skips ranks after ties 1, 1, 3, 4 dense_rank() No gaps after ties 1, 1, 2, 3 row_number() Always unique — no ties 1, 2, 3, 4 Use dense_rank() for Top-N salary — handles tied salaries without skipping ranks\n• SQL Approach df.createOrReplaceTempView('employees') spark.sql(''' SELECT name, dept, salary, dense_rank() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees ''').filter('rnk <= 2').show() Page 6 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Ques(cid:415)on Based on Window Func(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• Find latest record for every employee SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EmpID ORDER BY UpdatedDate DESC) rn FROM Employee )t WHERE rn=1;\n• Find second highest salary SELECT * FROM ( SELECT Salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rnk FROM Employee )t WHERE rnk=2;\n• Running Total SELECT OrderDate, Amount, SUM(Amount) OVER(ORDER BY OrderDate) AS RunningTotal FROM Orders;","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ROW_NUMBER(), RANK(), DENSE_RANK()","type":"concept","a":"Func(cid:415)on Duplicate Rank Gap ROW_NUMBER No No RANK Yes Yes DENSE_RANK Yes No","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Wide Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"•  Requires shuffle across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons.  More expensive\n• Examples:  groupBy()  join()  dis(cid:415)nct()  reduceByKey()  orderBy() Narrow transforma(cid:415)ons don't require data movement across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, whereas wide transforma(cid:415)ons involve shuffle, making them more expensive","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"For incremental loads, use:","type":"concept","a":"• o Watermark column (LastModifiedDate) o SQL query with dynamic parameters o Store the last processed watermark in a control table\n• Example SQL: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE ModifiedDate > @LastWatermark A(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on, update the watermark","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Ques(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Table: Stadium +---------------+---------+ | Column Name | Type | +---------------+---------+ | id | int | | visit_date | date | | people | int | +---------------+---------+ visit_date is the column with unique values for this table\n• Each row of this table contains the visit date and visit id to the stadium with the number of people during the visit\n• As the id increases, the date increases as well\n• Write a solu(cid:415)on to display the records with three or more rows with consecu(cid:415)ve id's, and the number of people is greater than or equal to 100 for each\n• Return the result table ordered by visit_date in ascending order\n• The result format is in the following example\n• Input: Stadium table: +------+------------+-----------+ | id | visit_date | people | +------+------------+-----------+ | 1 | 2017-01-01 | 10 | | 2 | 2017-01-02 | 109 | | 3 | 2017-01-03 | 150 | | 4 | 2017-01-04 | 99 | | 5 | 2017-01-05 | 145 | | 6 | 2017-01-06 | 1455 | | 7 | 2017-01-07 | 199 | | 8 | 2017-01-09 | 188 | Output: +------+------------+-----------+ | id | visit_date | people | +------+------------+-----------+ | 5 | 2017-01-05 | 145 | | 6 | 2017-01-06 | 1455 | | 7 | 2017-01-07 | 199 | | 8 | 2017-01-09 | 188 | Explana(cid:415)on: The four rows with ids 5, 6, 7, and 8 have consecu(cid:415)ve ids and each of them has >= 100 people a(cid:425)ended\n• Note that row 8 was included even though the visit_date was not the next day a(cid:332)er row 7\n• The rows with ids 2 and 3 are not included because we need at least three consecu(cid:415)ve ids\n• Answer: Approach","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Filter rows where people >= 100.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Return only groups having 3 or more consecu(cid:415)ve rows.","type":"concept","a":"• SQL Solu(cid:415)on WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id) AS grp FROM Stadium WHERE people >= 100 ), grp_count AS ( SELECT grp, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM cte GROUP BY grp HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3 ) SELECT c.id, c.visit_date, c.people FROM cte c JOIN grp_count g ON c.grp = g.grp ORDER BY c.visit_date;\n• Explana(cid:415)on Given the filtered data (people >= 100): id people ROW_NUMBER id - ROW_NUMBER 2 109 1 1 3 150 2 1 5 145 3 2 6 1455 4 2 7 199 5 2 8 188 6 2 No(cid:415)ce:  IDs 2, 3 have the same group (1) but only 2 rows, so they are excluded.  IDs 5, 6, 7, 8 have the same group (2) and 4 rows, so they are included\n• Output id visit_date people 5 2017-01-05 145 6 2017-01-06 1455 7 2017-01-07 199 8 2017-01-09 188 Client Round 2 :","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If Copy Ac(cid:415)vity is very slow, how will you op(cid:415)mize it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would first iden(cid:415)fy whether the bo(cid:425)leneck is the source, network, or sink\n• Then I would:  Increase the Degree of Copy Parallelism.  Enable parallel copy where supported.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on the source data.  Use staging for large data transfers.  Op(cid:415)mize the Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me size.  Filter unnecessary records.  Copy mul(cid:415)ple files in parallel.  Ensure source and sink indexes are appropriate.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What challenges did you face while doing transforma(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":"• \"One common challenge was handling duplicate records and inconsistent source data.\" Examples:  Duplicate records.  Null or invalid values.  Schema changes.  Large joins causing performance issues.  Data type mismatches\n• Solu(cid:415)ons:  Used dropDuplicates() or SQL window func(cid:415)ons.  Applied null handling and valida(cid:415)on rules.  Used par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and op(cid:415)mized joins.  Used Delta Lake features like MERGE for incremental updates","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Are you connec(cid:415)ng only with the SQL Server team or also with businesspeople?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I worked with both technical and business teams\n• I collaborated with the SQL Server team to understand the source schema, data availability, and any schema changes\n• I also interacted with business analysts and repor(cid:415)ng teams to clarify business rules, validate transformed data, and ensure the reports met their requirements.\" This demonstrates cross-func(cid:415)onal communica(cid:415)on skills","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why Do We Convert CSV Files to Parquet?","type":"concept","a":"• Feature CSV Parquet Storage format Row-based Columnar (each column stored separately) Compression None or minimal Snappy/GZIP — 75-90% smaller typically Schema No schema (inferred at read) Schema embedded in file footer Query speed Reads ALL columns even if Column pruning — reads only needed unused columns Predicate pushdown Not supported Row groups skipped if outside filter range Splittable Only with separate index Yes — parallel reads across tasks Null handling String representation Efficient native null encoding Nested types Flat only Supports nested/complex types natively Rule of thumb: A 10 GB CSV file becomes 1-3 GB Parquet (Snappy)\n• Queries run 5-20x faster on Parquet because only queried columns are read from disk\n• Always convert raw CSV to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Section 4 | Data Processing","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Remove Duplicates from a Table?","type":"concept","a":"Method 1 — dropDuplicates() df_clean = df.dropDuplicates() # all columns df_clean = df.dropDuplicates(['order_id','cust_id']) # key columns only Method 2 — distinct() df_clean = df.distinct() # removes fully identical rows Method 3 — Window row_number() — keep latest record from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.partitionBy('order_id').orderBy(col('updated_at').desc()) df_dedup = df.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter(col('rn')==1).drop('rn') Method 4 — Delta MERGE INTO (upsert on load) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Ways Can You Handle Duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• Method When to Use Key Consideration dropDuplicates() In-memory DataFrame dedup Specify key columns or all columns distinct() Exact row-level duplicates All columns must match Window row_number() Keep latest/earliest record per Partition by key, order by timestamp key Window dense_rank() Rank-based dedup with ties Use when ties should be preserved allowed Delta MERGE INTO Incremental load — avoid dupes Best for production pipelines at ingest SQL ROW_NUMBER() SQL-based in Spark SQL PARTITION BY key ORDER BY ts DESC GROUP BY + aggregation Collapse to summary rows Loses individual row detail Best Practice for production: Use MERGE INTO for Delta tables\n• It handles both new inserts and updates in one atomic operation, preventing duplicates at the table level without reprocessing old data\n• Page 6 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Section 5 | SQL / Transformation","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Calculate Male Count, Female Count, and Total Count per City","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — Conditional Aggregation (SQL) SELECT city, COUNT(CASE WHEN gender = 'Male' THEN 1 END) AS male_count, COUNT(CASE WHEN gender = 'Female' THEN 1 END) AS female_count, COUNT(*) AS total_count FROM people GROUP BY city ORDER BY city;\n• Method 2 — PIVOT (Spark SQL) SELECT city, Male AS male_count, Female AS female_count FROM (SELECT city, gender FROM people) PIVOT (COUNT(gender) FOR gender IN ('Male', 'Female')) ORDER BY city;\n• Method 3 — PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import count, when, col result = df.groupBy('city').agg( count(when(col('gender')=='Male', 1)).alias('male_count'), count(when(col('gender')=='Female', 1)).alias('female_count'), count('*').alias('total_count') ).orderBy('city') City male_count female_count total_count Bangalore 120 95 215 Chennai 80 110 190 Mumbai 200 180 380 Section 6 | Databricks Concepts","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Views Exist in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"View Type Persistence Data Description Stored? Temporary View Session only (lost on No createOrReplaceTempView(); only in restart) current SparkSession Global Temp View App lifetime (all No createOrReplaceGlobalTempView(); sessions) access via global_temp.view_name Standard View Metastore (permanent) No CREATE VIEW; saved in Hive/Unity metastore; no data copy Materialized View Metastore (permanent) Yes Pre-computed Delta table; refreshed on schedule or trigger df.createOrReplaceTempView('temp_v') df.createOrReplaceGlobalTempView('global_v') spark.sql('SELECT * FROM global_temp.global_v') CREATE VIEW gold.customer_summary AS SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY 1;","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Display the fla(cid:425)ened DataFrame.","type":"concept","a":"• (cid:57859) Expected Output +--------+-----+--------+ |product |price|quan(cid:415)ty| +--------+-----+--------+ |product1| 100| 2| |product2| 200| 3| +--------+-----+--------+ SQL:- (cid:57874)(cid:57875) SQL Interview Ques(cid:415)on: Running Total of Sales per Store You are given a table named sales with the following structure: sales ------ store_id VARCHAR sale_date DATE sales_amount INT Sample Data store_id sale_date sales_amount S1 2025-01-01 100 S1 2025-01-02 150 S1 2025-01-03 200 S2 2025-01-01 80 S2 2025-01-02 120 S2 2025-01-03 160 (cid:44472)(cid:44473)(cid:44474)(cid:44475) Problem Statement Write an SQL query to calculate the running total of sales for each store, ordered by sale_date\n• Requirements  The running total should reset for each store  The results should be ordered by store_id and sale_date  Use window func(cid:415)ons (cid:33158)(cid:57393) Expected Output store_id sale_date sales_amount running_total S1 2025-01-01 100 100 S1 2025-01-02 150 250 S1 2025-01-03 200 450 S2 2025-01-01 80 80 S2 2025-01-02 120 200 S2 2025-01-03 160 360 Q4 You are given a table named orders with the following structure: orders ------ customer_id INT order_id INT order_date DATE order_amount INT Sample Data customer_id order_id order_date order_amount 1 101 2025-01-01 100 1 102 2025-01-05 150 1 103 2025-01-10 200 2 201 2025-01-02 120 2 202 2025-01-06 110 3 301 2025-01-03 50 3 302 2025-01-07 80 (cid:44472)(cid:44473)(cid:44474)(cid:44475) Problem Statement Write an SQL query to find customers whose every order value is strictly higher than their previous order, ordered by order_date\n• Requirements  Orders must be compared chronologically  Customers with only one order should be considered valid  Use window func(cid:415)ons  Return dis(cid:415)nct customer IDs (cid:33158)(cid:57393) Expected Output customer_id 1 3","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques","type":"concept","a":" Create appropriate indexes.  Avoid SELECT *.  Filter early with WHERE.  Op(cid:415)mize joins.  Use execu(cid:415)on plans.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on large tables.  Use EXISTS instead of IN when appropriate.  Keep sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs updated.  Avoid unnecessary func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Two large tables. How will you join?","type":"concept","a":"• Typically use a Sort Merge Join because it scales well for large datasets\n• Also consider:  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on both tables on the join key.  Handle data skew if present.  Enable AQE for run(cid:415)me op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons\n• SQL Indexing Types:  Clustered  Non-Clustered  Composite  Unique  Filtered  Full-Text 16\n• SQL Problem Example: Customers with no orders SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL;","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Window Func(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Examples: ROW_NUMBER() RANK() DENSE_RANK() LAG() LEAD() NTILE() FIRST_VALUE() LAST_VALUE() Example: SELECT employee, salary, RANK() OVER(ORDER BY salary DESC) FROM Employee;","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Experience on NoSQL DB","type":"concept","a":"If you have experience: \"Yes, I have worked with MongoDB/Cosmos DB for storing semi-structured JSON data and reading it into Databricks for downstream processing.\" If not: \"I haven't worked extensively with NoSQL databases in produc(cid:415)on, but I understand they are used for schema-flexible, high-volume data such as JSON documents and key-value workloads.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Why use sets?","type":"concept","a":"•  Remove duplicates automa(cid:415)cally.  Fast membership checks (O(1) average (cid:415)me).  Perform set opera(cid:415)ons like union, intersec(cid:415)on, and difference\n• Example: nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4] unique_nums = set(nums) print(unique_nums) Output: {1, 2, 3, 4}","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is LAST_VALUE() Window Func(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"• It returns the last value within a window\n• Example Table Emp Salary A 2000 B 4000 C 6000 Query LAST_VALUE(salary) OVER( ORDER BY salary ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING ) Output Emp Last Salary A 6000 B 6000 C 6000 Without UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING Spark considers rows only up to the current row, so the result can be different\n• PySpark from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import last window = Window.orderBy(\"salary\") \\ .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.unboundedFollowing) df.withColumn(\"last_salary\", last(\"salary\").over(window))","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"For each customer, find the second and third highest order amount in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Data Customer Order A 500 A 700 A 900 A 300 Customer Order B 100 B 600 B 400 Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import dense_rank window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"customer\") \\ .orderBy(col(\"order_amount\").desc()) result = df.withColumn( \"rank\", dense_rank().over(window) ).filter(\"rank IN (2,3)\") result.show() Output Customer Order Rank A 700 2 A 500 3 B 400 2 B 100 3 If duplicate order amounts should receive unique rankings, use row_number() instead of dense_rank()\n• If you want gaps in ranking a(cid:332)er (cid:415)es, use rank()","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find matched and unmatched records between two tables in SQL","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose: Table A ID 1 2 3 Table B ID 2 3 4 Matched Records SELECT a.* FROM tableA a INNER JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id;\n• Output 2 3 Unmatched from Table A SELECT a.* FROM tableA a LEFT JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id WHERE b.id IS NULL;\n• Output 1 Unmatched from Table B SELECT b.* FROM tableB b LEFT JOIN tableA a ON b.id = a.id WHERE a.id IS NULL;\n• Output 4 All Matched and Unmatched (Full Outer Join) SELECT a.id AS tableA_id, b.id AS tableB_id FROM tableA a FULL OUTER JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id;\n• This returns matched rows along with records present only in either table","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Remove duplicate emails.","type":"concept","a":"Example ID Email 1 abc@gmail.com 2 abc@gmail.com 3 xyz@gmail.com SQL WITH CTE AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY Email ORDER BY ID ) rn FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM CTE WHERE rn > 1;","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Complex SQL Join","type":"concept","a":"• Typical interview ques(cid:415)on: Find department-wise total salary\n• SELECT d.DepartmentName, SUM(e.Salary) AS TotalSalary FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.DepartmentID=d.DepartmentID GROUP BY d.DepartmentName;\n• Client Round 3","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Find maximum salary per department name","type":"concept","a":"• Tables:  Employee(emp_id, emp_name, dept_id, salary)  Department(dept_id, dept_name) Answer SELECT d.dept_name, MAX(e.salary) AS max_salary FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id GROUP BY d.dept_name;\n• What if we want employee details also (name, id)?” Then use window func(cid:415)on: WITH ranked AS ( SELECT e.emp_id, e.emp_name, d.dept_name, e.salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC ) AS rnk FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id ) SELECT emp_id, emp_name, dept_name, salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk = 1;\n• Difference (important interview point) Method Output GROUP BY + MAX only salary per department DENSE_RANK full employee details with max salary","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How do you remove duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EMP_ID ORDER BY EMP_ID) AS rn FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n• Round 2:","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Find customers who have transac(cid:415)ons on more than 1 different date.","type":"concept","a":"Table: id, transac(cid:415)on_id, transaca(cid:415)on_date(cid:415)me , customer_id, product_id, net_qty, amount, transaca(cid:415)on_date SELECT customer_id FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT transac(cid:415)on_date) > 1;","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to calculate the total net quan(cid:415)ty for each product and rank the","type":"concept","a":"products based on total sales quan(cid:415)ty.” product_id net_qty 1 5 Answer: WITH CTE_PRODUCT AS ( SELECT product_id, SUM(net_qty) AS total_qty FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY product_id ) SELECT product_id, total_qty, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total_qty DESC) AS rnk_prod FROM CTE_PRODUCT;","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is DQ (Data Quality)?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Quality (DQ) refers to the degree to which data is fit for its intended use\n• Key dimensions: • Completeness: No missing/null values where required • Accuracy: Data correctly represents real-world values • Consistency: Same data looks the same across all systems • Timeliness: Data is available when needed • Uniqueness: No duplicate records • Validity: Data conforms to expected formats and rules DQ Checks in Databricks: # Using Delta Live Tables expectations @dlt.expect('valid_age', 'age >= 0 AND age <= 120') @dlt.expect_or_drop('not_null_id', 'customer_id IS NOT NULL') # Using Great Expectations or custom PySpark checks df.filter(col('customer_id').isNull()).count() # Should be 0","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize SQL Queries","type":"concept","a":"• Key SQL Optimization Techniques: • 1\n• Use WHERE early: Filter data as early as possible • 2\n• Avoid SELECT *: Only select needed columns • 3\n• Use proper JOIN type: INNER > LEFT OUTER > CROSS • 4\n• Index usage: Ensure joins/filters use indexed columns • 5\n• Partition pruning: Filter on partition columns • 6\n• Use CTEs / temp tables: Avoid repeating complex subqueries • 7\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE: f(col) = val kills indexes • 8\n• EXPLAIN PLAN: Analyze execution plan before running • 9\n• Use BROADCAST hint for small table joins • 10\n• Avoid DISTINCT when not needed — use GROUP BY -- Bad: Function on column disables partition pruning WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025 -- Good: Range filter enables partition pruning WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31' -- Broadcast hint for small table SELECT /*+ BROADCAST(d) */ f.*, d.product_name FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product d ON f.product_key = d.product_key SECTION 6: Coding Questions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark — Highest Salary per Designation","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, max as spark_max from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import rank spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('HighestSalary').getOrCreate() # Sample data data = [('Alice', 'Manager', 90000), ('Bob', 'Manager', 85000), ('Charlie', 'Analyst', 60000), ('Dave', 'Analyst', 62000), ('Eve', 'Director', 120000)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['name', 'designation', 'salary']) # Method 1: Window Function (RANK) window_spec = Window.partitionBy('designation').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) result = df.withColumn('rnk', rank().over(window_spec)) \\ .filter(col('rnk') == 1) \\ .select('name', 'designation', 'salary') result.show() # Method 2: Self-join with max salary max_sal = df.groupBy('designation').agg(spark_max('salary').alias('max_salary')) result2 = df.join(max_sal, (df.designation == max_sal.designation) & (df.salary == max_sal.max_salary)) \\ .select(df.name, df.designation, df.salary) result2.show()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"SQL — Revenue, Common Customers, Consecutive Months","type":"concept","a":"Setup: orders(order_id, customer_id, order_date, amount)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Total Revenue per Month","type":"concept","a":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM') AS year_month, SUM(amount) AS total_revenue FROM orders GROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM') ORDER BY year_month;","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Customers who Ordered in Both January AND February 2025","type":"concept","a":"SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28' AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1, 2) GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2; -- Alternative using INTERSECT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01' INTERSECT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Customers who Ordered in Three Consecutive Months","type":"concept","a":"WITH monthly_orders AS ( SELECT customer_id, DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM-01') AS order_month FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM-01') ), with_lag AS ( SELECT customer_id, order_month, LAG(order_month, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_month) AS prev_month, LAG(order_month, 2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_month) AS prev2_month FROM monthly_orders ) SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM with_lag WHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2_month, 1) = prev_month AND ADD_MONTHS(prev_month, 1) = order_month;","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Sql ques(cid:415)ons for 2nd highest salary and also find 4th highest salary","type":"concept","a":"WITH ranked AS ( SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employee ) SELECT salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk in( 2 , 4)","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you perform transforma(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"• I perform transforma(cid:415)ons based on business requirements using SQL or PySpark\n• In Silver layer, I clean and standardize data by removing duplicates, handling nulls, and applying joins\n• In Gold layer, I apply business logic and aggrega(cid:415)ons like SUM, COUNT, and CASE statements to prepare data for repor(cid:415)ng\n• I also use window func(cid:415)ons for advanced transforma(cid:415)ons like ranking and deduplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques.","type":"concept","a":"• Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques include reducing data scanned by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, using proper indexing, avoiding func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns, and pushing filters early\n• In distributed systems like Spark, I use broadcast joins, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching, and skew handling techniques\n• At the pipeline level, I implement incremental loads, par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, and use efficient file formats like Parquet or Delta\n• I also analyze execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks and improve performance","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"modeling","name":"Data Modeling & SCD","icon":"📐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is a data model? Explain star vs snowflake vs galaxy schema.","type":"concept","a":"A data model defines how data is structured, related, and constrained.\n• Star schema — one central FACT table surrounded by denormalized DIMENSION tables. Simple queries, fast aggregations, BI-friendly. Slight redundancy. Most common in data warehouses.\n• Snowflake schema — dimensions normalized into sub-dimensions (less redundancy, more joins, slower).\n• Galaxy / fact constellation — multiple fact tables sharing conformed dimensions.\nExample: Fact_Sales(sale_id,date_key,product_key,customer_key,amount,qty) + Dim_Date, Dim_Product, Dim_Customer."},{"q":"What are the SCD types (0,1,2,3,4,6)?","type":"concept","a":"• Type 0 — never update (static).\n• Type 1 — overwrite the value; no history.\n• Type 2 — add a new row on change; keep full history with start/end dates + current flag (most common in interviews).\n• Type 3 — add a column to store current + previous value only (1 version back).\n• Type 4 — keep current in main table, history in a separate history table.\n• Type 6 — hybrid (1+2+3): full history rows PLUS a current flag and a current-value column."},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 in detail and how you implement it.","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 preserves history by inserting a NEW row whenever a tracked attribute changes; the natural key stays the same, a surrogate key identifies each version.\nColumns: start_date, end_date, is_current (active flag), surrogate key.\nSteps (Delta MERGE):\n1. Expire the current row if tracked columns changed: set end_date = yesterday, is_current = 0.\n2. Insert a new row for the change: start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1.\n3. Insert brand-new keys as-is.\nOnly one active row per business key at any time."},{"q":"CODING: Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark using a hash/flag approach.","type":"coding","a":"Read history + daily data, compute a hash of tracked columns, split into unchanged / updated / new, expire updates, insert new + updates, then union.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, sha2, concat_ws, lit, current_date, when\n\nTRACK = ['name','city']\n\ndef with_hash(df):\n    return df.withColumn('row_hash', sha2(concat_ws('||', *[col(c) for c in TRACK]), 256))\n\nhist = with_hash(history_df)         # has start/end/is_current\nincr = with_hash(daily_df)\n\ncur = hist.filter(col('is_current')==1)\n\n# rows that changed\nupdates = (incr.alias('s').join(cur.alias('t'), 'customer_id')\n           .filter(col('s.row_hash') != col('t.row_hash'))\n           .select('customer_id', *TRACK))\n\n# new keys\nnew = incr.join(cur, 'customer_id', 'left_anti').select('customer_id', *TRACK)\n\n# expire old current rows for changed keys\nexpired = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id').withColumn('end_date', current_date()).withColumn('is_current', lit(0))\nno_change = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\n\n# new versions of changed + brand new\ninserts = (updates.unionByName(new)\n           .withColumn('start_date', current_date())\n           .withColumn('end_date', lit(None).cast('date'))\n           .withColumn('is_current', lit(1)))\n\nfinal = no_change.unionByName(expired).unionByName(inserts)\nfinal.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('dim_customer')"},{"q":"What is incremental loading and what approaches exist?","type":"concept","a":"Incremental loading processes only new/changed data since the last load instead of a full reload.\n• Timestamp/watermark — load where updated_at > last_run_timestamp; track a high-watermark in a control table.\n• CDC — capture inserts/updates/deletes from the source (log-based, e.g., Debezium/Azure SQL CDC).\n• Delta MERGE (UPSERT) — merge changes into the target.\nBenefits: faster, cheaper, less source load. Watch for late-arriving data and reliable watermark updates."},{"q":"Difference between full load and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Full load — reload all data every run; simple; high source impact and long runtime; used for first load/small tables.\n• Incremental — load only new/changed records; needs watermark/CDC; low source impact, fast; for large tables. Risk: must handle late-arriving data and deletes."},{"q":"Difference between CDC and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load — a loading strategy: only bring in new/changed data (often timestamp-based).\n• CDC — a mechanism that captures row-level INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from the source (log-based/trigger-based), so it can also capture deletes and provide before/after images.\nCDC is a superset that feeds richer incremental loads; plain timestamp incremental usually can't detect deletes."},{"q":"Which table loads first, fact or dimension? How do you relate them?","type":"concept","a":"Dimensions load FIRST — facts reference dimension surrogate keys, so the dimensions (and their keys) must exist before the fact rows can resolve those keys.\nRelation: fact tables store foreign keys pointing to dimension surrogate keys; the join is fact.dim_key = dim.surrogate_key.\nOften a 'late-arriving dimension' pattern is used: insert a placeholder (unknown) dimension row if a fact arrives before its dimension."},{"q":"What is the difference between a surrogate key and a natural key?","type":"concept","a":"• Natural (business) key — a meaningful identifier from the source (e.g., customer_id, SSN).\n• Surrogate key — a system-generated integer uniquely identifying each DIMENSION VERSION (used in SCD2 to distinguish versions).\nFacts join to dimensions on surrogate keys; the natural key links versions of the same entity."},{"q":"What are late-arriving dimensions/facts and how do you handle them?","type":"concept","a":"• Late-arriving dimension — a fact arrives referencing a dimension that hasn't loaded yet.\n• Late-arriving fact — a fact arrives after its expected processing window.\nHandling: insert a placeholder/inferred dimension row (unknown key) and update it when the real record arrives; or defer fact loading until the dimension exists; use watermark + reprocessing and idempotent MERGE so late facts are picked up on the next run."},{"q":"What is a slowly changing dimension and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"SCD manages dimension attributes that change slowly over time (e.g., customer address, department). The type chosen controls how (and whether) history is preserved — so reports can reflect either the current value or the value as of a point in time."},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• In a data warehouse, data is organized into Fact Tables (measures/events) and Dimension Tables (descriptive context)\n• Star and Snowflake are two common ways to arrange these tables\n• Star Schema Characteristics: Fact table is in the center\n• Dimension tables surround it like a star\n• Dimension tables are DENORMALIZED — all attributes in one flat table\n• Simple, fast queries — fewer JOINs needed\n• Best for BI tools and reporting (Power BI, Tableau). -- Star Schema Example: Sales Data Warehouse -- Fact Table (center of the star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key, store_key, sale_amount, quantity, discount -- Dimension Tables (flat, denormalized) dim_customer: customer_key, name, city, state, country, email, age_group dim_product: product_key, product_name, category, brand, color, size dim_date: date_key, date, month, quarter, year, weekday, is_holiday dim_store: store_key, store_name, city, state, region, manager ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 9 -- Query: Total sales by product category (only 2 tables needed) SELECT p.category, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key GROUP BY p.category Snowflake Schema Characteristics: Dimension tables are NORMALIZED — broken into sub-dimension tables\n• Looks like a snowflake because dimensions branch into more tables\n• Saves storage (no repeated values)\n• More complex queries with more JOINs\n• Better for data integrity\n• Used when storage is a constraint. -- Snowflake Schema: Same sales warehouse, normalized -- Fact Table (same as star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key ... -- dim_product is now split into sub-tables dim_product: product_key, product_name, category_key, brand_key dim_category: category_key, category_name, department_key dim_department:department_key, department_name dim_brand: brand_key, brand_name, brand_country -- dim_customer split into sub-tables dim_customer: customer_key, name, city_key, age_group dim_city: city_key, city_name, state_key dim_state: state_key, state_name, country_key dim_country: country_key, country_name, region -- Query: Same total sales by category (now 4 JOINs needed) SELECT dc.category_name, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_category dc ON p.category_key = dc.category_key GROUP BY dc.category_name Comparison Table Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension structure Denormalized (flat) Normalized (broken into sub- tables) Number of JOINs Fewer — simple queries More — complex queries ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 10 Query performance Faster reads Slightly slower due to more JOINs Storage More (repeated values) Less (normalized, no repetition) Data integrity Lower — some redundancy Higher — data in one place Complexity Simple to understand More complex to maintain Best for BI/Reporting, Power BI Large DW with storage constraints Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'We used Star Schema for our reporting layer because it gives faster query performance and is simpler to work with in Power BI","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Questions answered How much? How many? What Who? What? Where? When?","type":"concept","a":"• total? Primary key Surrogate key (sale_id) Surrogate key (customer_key) Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD) — Bonus • SCD Type 1 — Overwrite: Update the record directly\n• No history kept. (e.g., fix a typo in name) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 12 • SCD Type 2 — New Row: Add a new row with effective dates\n• Full history preserved. (e.g., customer moves city) • SCD Type 3 — Add Column: Add a 'previous value' column\n• Limited history. (e.g., previous_city column) Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'Fact tables store the measurable events — like each sale transaction with amount, quantity, and foreign keys\n• Dimension tables provide the context — customer name, product category, date details\n• In our warehouse, fact_sales had 500M rows while dim_customer had only 2M rows.' Q9 PySpark: Top 10 Customers by Total Sales Problem Given: DataFrame: sales_df Schema: customer_id (string), product_id (string), sale_amount (double) Task: Total sales per customer, return top 10 by total sales amount Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, desc # Step 1: Group by customer_id and sum their sale_amounts total_sales_df = sales_df.groupBy('customer_id') \\ .agg(sum('sale_amount').alias('total_sales')) # Step 2: Sort by total_sales descending, take top 10 top_10_customers = total_sales_df \\ .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) \\ .limit(10) # Display result top_10_customers.show() # Output: # +-----------+------------+ # |customer_id|total_sales | # +-----------+------------+ # |CUST_0042 | 245780.50 | # |CUST_0017 | 198340.00 | # |CUST_0099 | 176220.75 | ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 13 # |... | ... | # +-----------+------------+ Alternative: Using SQL in Databricks # Register as temp view sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') # SQL approach top_10_sql = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id, SUM(sale_amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY total_sales DESC LIMIT 10 ''') top_10_sql.show() With Rounding (Production Ready) from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, round as spark_round top_10_customers = ( sales_df .groupBy('customer_id') .agg(spark_round(sum('sale_amount'), 2).alias('total_sales')) .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) .limit(10) ) top_10_customers.show(truncate=False) Interview Tip: Key functions: groupBy() + agg(sum()) for totals, orderBy(col().desc()) for sorting, limit(10) for top N\n• For very large data, consider using window functions with rank() if you need ties included\n• Q10 PySpark: Fill Null Salaries and Add Salary Grade Problem ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 14 Given: DataFrame: employees_df Schema: employee_id (int), department (string), salary (int) — may have NULLs Task 1: Fill null salaries with 50000 Task 2: Add salary_grade column: >=100000 -> Executive, 50000-99999 -> Manager, <50000 -> Associate Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, coalesce, lit # Step 1: Fill null salaries with default value 50000 # Method A: fillna (simple, specific column) df_filled = employees_df.fillna({'salary': 50000}) # Method B: coalesce (returns first non-null value) df_filled = employees_df.withColumn( 'salary', coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) ) # Step 2: Add salary_grade column using when/otherwise df_final = df_filled.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary') >= 100000, 'Executive') .when((col('salary') >= 50000) & (col('salary') < 100000), 'Manager') .otherwise('Associate') ) df_final.show() # Output: # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # |employee_id|department|salary|salary_grade| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # | 101| Sales |120000| Executive| # | 102| HR | null| Manager| <- null -> 50000 -> Manager # | 103| Tech | 75000| Manager| # | 104| Sales | 35000| Associate| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ Key Functions Explained Function Purpose Example fillna(value, subset) Replace nulls in specific fillna({'salary': 50000}) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 15 columns coalesce(col, lit(val)) Return first non-null of multiple coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) exprs when(condition, value) If-else logic for new column when(col('salary')>=100000,'Execut ive') .otherwise(value) Default value if no when .otherwise('Associate') matches withColumn(name, Add or replace a column withColumn('salary_grade', expr) when(...)) Interview Tip: The when().when().otherwise() chain is PySpark's equivalent of SQL CASE WHEN\n• Always put the most specific condition first (>=100000 before >=50000)\n• Use fillna before applying when so null-handling is clean\n• Q11 PySpark: Most Recent Transaction Per User (Window Function) Problem Given: DataFrame: transactions_df Schema: user_id (string), transaction_timestamp (timestamp), transaction_amount (double) Task: Find the single MOST RECENT transaction for each user — return all 3 columns Solution 1: Window Function with rank() — Best Approach from pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Define window: partition by user_id, order by timestamp descending window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) # Add rank column — rank 1 = most recent for each user df_ranked = transactions_df.withColumn('rank', rank().over(window_spec)) # Keep only rank 1 (most recent) and drop the rank column most_recent = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') == 1) \\ .drop('rank') ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 16 most_recent.show() # Output: # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |user_id|transaction_timestamp |transaction_amount| # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |U001 |2024-01-15 14:32:00 | 129.99 | # |U002 |2024-01-16 09:15:00 | 49.50 | # |U003 |2024-01-14 21:00:00 | 299.00 | # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ Solution 2: Using row_number() — Guarantees Single Row from pyspark.sql.functions import col, row_number from pyspark.sql.window import Window # row_number() assigns 1 to the latest tx per user (no ties) # rank() would give ties the same number — use row_number to be safe window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) most_recent = ( transactions_df .withColumn('row_num', row_number().over(window_spec)) .filter(col('row_num') == 1) .drop('row_num') ) Solution 3: groupBy + join (Without Window Functions) from pyspark.sql.functions import max # Step 1: Find max timestamp per user max_ts = transactions_df \\ .groupBy('user_id') \\ .agg(max('transaction_timestamp').alias('max_ts')) # Step 2: Join back to original to get full row most_recent = transactions_df.join( max_ts, (transactions_df.user_id == max_ts.user_id) & (transactions_df.transaction_timestamp == max_ts.max_ts), how='inner' ).select( transactions_df.user_id, transactions_df.transaction_timestamp, ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 17 transactions_df.transaction_amount ) rank() vs row_number() vs dense_rank() Function Tie Handling Returns single row Use When per user? row_number() No ties — arbitrary order for Always exactly 1 Need strictly 1 ties row per group rank() Ties get same rank, gap May return >1 if Aware of ties, after timestamp tie want them included dense_rank() Ties get same rank, no gap May return >1 if Want timestamp tie consecutive rank numbers Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'I use a Window function with row_number() partitioned by user_id and ordered by transaction_timestamp descending. row_number() guarantees exactly one row per user even if two transactions have the same timestamp\n• Then I filter where row_num = 1 and drop the helper column.' Quick Reference — One-Line Answers Topic One-Line Answer Schedule Trigger Runs pipeline on fixed time — daily, hourly, weekly Tumbling Window Trigger Fixed non-overlapping windows — retryable, perfect for incremental loads Storage Event Trigger Fires when a file lands in ADLS Gen2 / Blob — event-driven On-Prem Migration SHIR -> ADF -> ADLS Gen2 (staging) -> Transform -> Azure SQL + watermark control table File Routing by Suffix Get Metadata + ForEach + dynamic sink table name using @replace(last(split(name,'_')),'.csv','') CSV-Driven Routing Lookup (read CSV) + ForEach + dynamic Copy Activity using @item().Country_code Star Schema Fact + flat denormalized dims — fewer JOINs, faster queries, best for BI Snowflake Schema Fact + normalized sub-dims — more JOINs, less storage, more data integrity Fact Table Measurable events (sale_amount, quantity) + FK to dimensions — very large ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 18 Dimension Table Descriptive context (name, city, category) — smaller, used for grouping/filtering Top 10 Customers groupBy('customer_id').agg(sum).orderBy(desc).limit(10) Fill Null + Salary Grade fillna({'salary':50000}) then withColumn('grade', when().when().otherwise()) Most Recent Transaction Window(partitionBy, orderBy desc) + row_number() + filter(row_num==1) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 19","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to implement Slowly changing type1","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"00:00.0\").show() -- it will add the Time stamp","type":"concept","a":"• spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",versionAsof=1).show() – if version is 0, it will execute the first table(df) if version is 1, it will execute the updated table ----In sql------- from delta.tables import DeltaTable -- (it will convert the file to Table) spark.sql(\"CREATE TABLE dbtablenew USING DELTA LOCATION '/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta'\") -- (it will create a new table ) %sql select * from dbtablenew %sql -- it will update and insert when mergering the files MERGE INTO dbtablenew USING dbtable ON dbtablenew.eno = dbtable.eno WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET dbtablenew.data = dbtable.data WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (eno, ename, sal) VALUES (eno, ename, sal) Page | 21 =========DATA WAREHOUSE CONCEPTS============ ➢ Data Ware house it is used for the ETL because fro analysis purpose. ➢ We want to design a OLTP system , we should a have modeling is E-R Modeling ( Entity Relational Modeling) and have a Primary key and Foreign Key. ➢ Entity relational is nothing but a tables like customer tables, location tables, category, sub category and making a relational between two tables. ➢ It is an real time object and this tables we can call as Master tables. ➢ There is a Transaction table(sales tables, etc) in that we can see the information but we want use a joins. ➢ OLTP concept it is for any update or insert should be a normalized because there is tables in a Small and performance is less. ➢ In that OLTP want get a information is difficult because there is a different tables like customer table and Details customer table . ➢ We want to implement the business intelligence, so we have to apply to data because it is not a format data\n• Master Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Primary Keys Product ID Product name 1 Honda Bike 2 Tvs Bike Customer ID Customer Name 1 Rama 2 Siva Transaction Table: Product ID Customer ID Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ By using a ETL to loading the data OLTP system to another system, so we called a Data Ware Housing or OLAP. ➢ By Designing a OLAP we have modeling is Dimensional and tables we can called as Dimensional Tables( Dimproduct, dimcustomer, etc) in that we have a Bussiness key. ➢ It will give the information from table will get a combination of Dimcustomer and Dim detalis customer. ➢ In this dimensional it will check the is existing or not, if not existing it will update or insert. ➢ In this Dimensional table will have a Surrogate key, while inserting the data automatically it will give a Unique number and easily we can identify the data. ➢ There is Fact Table it is a connection of all dimensions tables. ➢ We can merge and also we can add a Values like Quantity, amount etc\n• Dimensional Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Business Keys and Pkey and Ckey are called a Surrogate key\n• Page | 22 PKey Product ID Product name 1 1 Honda Bike 2 2 Tvs Bike CKey Customer ID Customer Name 1 1 Rama 2 2 Siva Fact Table: Pkey Ckey Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ In this Dimensional there is two schema 1\n• Star Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snow Flake Schema","type":"concept","a":"S.NO Star Schema Snowflake Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, The fact tables and While in snowflake schema, The fact tables,","type":"concept","a":"the dimension tables are contained. dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema is a top-down model. While it is a bottom-up model.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema uses more space. While it uses less space.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It takes less time for the execution While it takes more time than star schema for the","type":"concept","a":"of queries. execution of queries.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, Normalization is While in this, Both normalization and","type":"concept","a":"not used. denormalization are used.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"The query complexity of star While the query complexity of snowflake schema is","type":"concept","a":"schema is low. higher than star schema.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It has high data redundancy. While it has low data redundancy.","type":"concept","a":"Page | 23 Star Schema Diagram: Snow Flake Diagram: Page | 24 ===================Azure Questions===============================","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Round Robin: It will dis tribute data randomly row by row on each node. Less volume","type":"concept","a":"of data tables , dimension tables(customer, product) Disadvantage is data need to sorted at the time of combining from all nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Hash: It will distributed based on column similar values like same kind of product","type":"concept","a":"• ID will share one node\n• High volume data tables , Fact tables(Sales table) Advantage is no need of sorting while combining of data from nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is dimension modelling?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Dimensional Data Modelling is one of the data modelling techniques used in data warehouse design\n• To improve the data retrieval\n• Dimensional modeling involves the use of fact and dimension tables to maintain a record of historical data in data warehouses","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is star schema and snowflake schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A.In star schema, The fact tables and the dimension tables are contained\n• In this schema fewer foreign- key join is used\n• This schema forms a star with fact table and dimension tables\n• In snowflake schema, The fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained\n• This schema forms a snowflake with fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub- dimension tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is surrogate key?","type":"concept","a":"A.Surrogate key also called a synthetic primary key, is generated when a new record is inserted into a table automatically by a database that can be declared as the primary key of that table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"A.Fact means measures and dimension means description related to facts.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Slowly changing dimension: Changing over a period of time.is nothing but","type":"concept","a":"address These are three types Type1: need to override address, Type2: store all address Type3: need to store only current and previous address only or current previous and before one","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed dimension: A dimension table which can be used multiple fact tables is","type":"concept","a":"nothing but conformed dimension.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Degenerate dimension: Fact table contain dimension table information that is","type":"concept","a":"called as degenerate dimension table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Junk Dimension: If a model has to many small dimension, we can take all this model","type":"concept","a":"in one dimension table is nothing but junk dimensions Ex: ‘True or False’, ‘Yes or No’, ‘Male and Female’, ’Single or married’","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Role-Plying dimension: Fact table is more than two times relationship with dimension","type":"concept","a":"table is nothing but Role-Play dimension","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Static dimension: Something which never change like gender is nothing but static dimension","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is different kind of facts?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Additive facts: All data in the fact can be aggregate, we can apply all aggregate","type":"concept","a":"functions on data example sales.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Semi additive facts: we cannot apply all aggregate function but you will able to apply","type":"concept","a":"some aggregate functions example current account balance.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Non additive facts: we cannot apply any aggregate function like percentage tables","type":"concept","a":"Page | 30","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of fact tables in DWH?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Fact less fact table: It does not contain any measure in it contain only foreign","type":"concept","a":"from dimension table","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snapshot fact table: Store the data in relation to date or time","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Centipede fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What data is moved to Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Only analy(cid:415)cs-ready datasets:  Fact tables  Dimension tables  Aggregated tables  Repor(cid:415)ng datasets Opera(cid:415)onal tables remain in SQL Server.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Implement SCD Type 2","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Fact and Dimension","type":"concept","a":"Dimension:  Customer  Product  Employee Fact:  Sales  Orders  Transac(cid:415)ons SCD Type 2 is generally applied to dimension tables.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"About SCD (Slowly Changing Dimensions)","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) is a data warehousing technique for managing changes to dimension data over time\n• Different types handle historical tracking differently: SCD Type Strategy Keeps History? Storage Impact Type 0 Never update — static dimension No Minimal Type 1 Overwrite — replace old value with No Minimal new Type 2 Add new row — expire old, insert Yes (full history) Grows over time new with dates Type 3 Add new column — current + Partial (1 version Moderate previous value only back) Type 4 History table — current in main, Yes Moderate history in separate table Type 6 Hybrid: Type 1+2+3 combined Yes + current flag Larger SCD Type 2 is most commonly asked in interviews: • A new row is added for each change to a dimension record • The old row is 'expired' by setting end_date and is_current = 0 • The new row gets start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1 • The natural key (e.g., customer_id) remains the same; a surrogate key identifies each version Page 5 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me about yourself and your skill-set?","type":"concept","a":"• I am a Data Engineer with experience in building and optimizing data pipelines, ETL/ELT workflows, and cloud- based data platforms\n• My core skill-set includes: • Big Data Technologies: Apache Spark (PySpark), Databricks, Delta Lake • Cloud Platforms: Azure, AWS, or GCP — cloud storage, compute, and managed services • Data Warehousing: Designing star/snowflake schemas, building fact and dimension tables • Programming: Python, SQL, Scala (basic) • Data Ingestion: Auto Loader, Kafka, Azure Data Factory, or AWS Glue • Orchestration: Apache Airflow, Databricks Workflows, Azure Data Factory pipelines • Version Control: Git, CI/CD pipelines for data • Data Governance: Implementing SCD-1, SCD-2, data quality checks, schema evolution I have worked on end-to-end data platform projects including migrations, real-time ingestion, and building curated analytical layers for BI consumption","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 2 (Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2) tracks the full history of changes to a dimension record by creating a new row for each change rather than overwriting the existing record\n• Extra Columns Required for SCD-2: • start_date / effective_from: Date when the record became active. • end_date / effective_to: Date when the record was superseded (NULL for current record). • is_current / is_active: Boolean flag — TRUE for the latest active record. • (Optional) surrogate_key: A system-generated unique key for each row version\n• How SCD-2 Works: • When a record changes, the existing row is 'closed' by setting end_date = today and is_current = False. • A new row is inserted with the updated values, start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = True. • Historical queries can use the date range to retrieve the state of a record at any point in time","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 implementation using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) Type 2 is used to maintain the complete history of changes in dimension data\n• Instead of updating an existing record, we expire the old record and insert a new one whenever a tracked attribute changes\n• This allows us to preserve historical data for reporting and auditing Steps","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain all the SCD types and difference between them","type":"concept","a":"SCD Types & Differences Type Behavior History Use Case SCD 0 No change allowed No Sta(cid:415)c data SCD 1 Overwrite old data No history Fixing errors SCD 2 New row for changes Full history Tracking changes SCD 3 Adds new column for previous value Limited history Few a(cid:425)ributes only (cid:44934) Example:  Salary change: o SCD1 → overwrite old salary o SCD2 → new row inserted o SCD3 → old salary stored in another column","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Star & Snowflake schema","type":"concept","a":"Star vs Snowflake Schema Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Structure Denormalized Normalized Joins Fewer More Performance Faster Slower Storage More space Op(cid:415)mized Complexity Simple Complex Example:  Star → Fact table + dimension tables  Snowflake → dimension tables further split","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Do you know data modeling? How do you rate yourself out of 5?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes, I have worked with data modeling while designing data warehouse solu(cid:415)ons\n• I'm comfortable with fact and dimension tables, Star Schema, Snowflake Schema, normaliza(cid:415)on, and denormaliza(cid:415)on\n• I would rate myself 4 out of 5 because I have prac(cid:415)cal experience, while I'm s(cid:415)ll expanding my knowledge in advanced modeling techniques like Data Vault.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"We have transac(cid:415)onal data and analy(cid:415)cal data. Which schema do you use?","type":"concept","a":"• \"For transac(cid:415)onal systems (OLTP), I use a normalized schema (3NF) because it minimizes redundancy and maintains data integrity for frequent inserts, updates, and deletes\n• For analy(cid:415)cal systems (OLAP), I generally use a Star Schema because it is op(cid:415)mized for repor(cid:415)ng, aggrega(cid:415)on, and fast query performance.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Star Schema and Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension tables are denormalized Dimension tables are normalized Fewer joins More joins Be(cid:425)er query performance Slightly slower due to addi(cid:415)onal joins Easier to understand More complex More storage required Less storage required Preferred for most data warehouses Used when dimensions have significant redundancy Example: Star Schema Product | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Store Snowflake Schema Product | Product Category | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Geography | Country","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why do you choose Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal data?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I prefer a Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal workloads because repor(cid:415)ng queries usually aggregate millions of records\n• Since the dimension tables are denormalized, fewer joins are required, which improves query performance\n• It's also easier for BI tools like Power BI and Tableau to consume.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"When would you use Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I use a Snowflake Schema when dimension tables contain a lot of redundant data or have natural hierarchical rela(cid:415)onships, such as Country → State → City or Category → Subcategory → Product\n• Normalizing these dimensions reduces redundancy and makes updates easier, although it requires more joins.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What are Dimension and Fact Tables? Explain in detail.","type":"concept","a":"• Fact Table Stores measurable business events\n• Examples:  Sales Amount  Quan(cid:415)ty Sold  Revenue  Profit Contains:  Numeric measures  Foreign keys to dimension tables Example: ProductKey CustomerKey DateKey SalesAmount 101 201 20240101 500 Dimension Table Stores descrip(cid:415)ve informa(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  Customer  Product  Employee  Date  Store Example: ProductKey ProductName Category 101 Laptop Electronics Rela(cid:415)onship Customer Dimension | Product Dimension -- Sales Fact -- Date Dimension | Store Dimension Difference Fact Table Dimension Table Stores business measures Stores descrip(cid:415)ve a(cid:425)ributes Very large Smaller Contains foreign keys Contains primary keys Used for calcula(cid:415)ons Used for filtering and grouping","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Large Fact and Dimension Tables","type":"concept","a":"If both tables are large:  Prefer Sort Merge Join.  Ensure par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning on the join key.  Avoid broadcas(cid:415)ng either table.  Enable AQE to op(cid:415)mize joins dynamically.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Model? Which One Did You Use? (Star Schema)","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Model defines the structure, relationships, and rules for organizing data\n• Common models: Star Schema (Most Common in DW): One central Fact table surrounded by Dimension tables\n• No normalization of dimensions. • Fact Table: Contains measurable, quantitative data (sales_amount, quantity) • Dimension Tables: Descriptive attributes (customer, product, date, location) • Pros: Simple queries, fast aggregations, easy for BI tools • Cons: Data redundancy in dimensions Snowflake Schema: • Extension of star schema where dimensions are normalized into sub-dimensions • Pros: Less redundancy\n• Cons: More joins, slower queries Galaxy/Fact Constellation Schema: • Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables Example Star Schema: Fact_Sales (sale_id, date_key, product_key, customer_key, amount, qty) Dim_Date (date_key, date, month, quarter, year) Dim_Product(product_key, name, category, brand) Dim_Customer(customer_key, name, city, country)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"streaming","name":"Streaming & Ingestion","icon":"📡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Difference between batch and streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — bounded dataset processed on a schedule (hourly/daily); high throughput, higher latency; tools: Spark batch, ADF, SSIS.\n• Streaming — unbounded, continuously arriving data processed incrementally with low latency; tools: Spark Structured Streaming, Kafka, Event Hubs.\nChoose batch for EOD reports/historical loads; streaming for fraud detection, IoT, clickstream, real-time dashboards."},{"q":"What is Spark Structured Streaming?","type":"concept","a":"Structured Streaming is Spark's scalable, fault-tolerant stream engine built on the DataFrame/SQL API.\n• You write the same DataFrame transformations; Spark runs them incrementally as new data arrives.\n• Sources: Kafka, Event Hubs, Auto Loader (cloudFiles), Delta, files, sockets.\n• Sinks: Delta tables, files, foreachBatch, Kafka.\n• Guarantees: exactly-once via checkpointing + idempotent sinks.\nModel: output modes append / update / complete; triggers processingTime or availableNow."},{"q":"What is checkpointing and why is it important in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"Checkpointing persists stream progress (which offsets/data were processed) and state to durable storage (e.g., ADLS) so the stream can recover after failure and continue without reprocessing or losing data.\n• Enables fault tolerance and exactly-once processing.\n• option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/...').\n• Each stream needs its own checkpoint location; changing the query requires a new checkpoint (or careful migration)."},{"q":"What is watermarking in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"A watermark defines how long to wait for late-arriving events (bounded lateness). Spark retains state for aggregations/deduplication until the watermark passes, then drops old state to bound memory.\n• withWatermark('eventTime','10 minutes').\n• Events older than the watermark are treated as late and dropped from stateful ops.\n• Balances correctness (waiting for late data) vs resource usage."},{"q":"Difference between event time and processing time?","type":"concept","a":"• Event time — when the event actually occurred (timestamp in the data).\n• Processing time — when Spark processes/receives it.\nStreaming windows/watermarks should use EVENT time for correct results when data arrives out of order; processing time is simpler but inaccurate for late data."},{"q":"Explain window operations in Structured Streaming.","type":"concept","a":"• Tumbling window — fixed-size, non-overlapping (e.g., every 1 minute): window('ts','1 minute').\n• Sliding window — fixed-size, overlapping (e.g., 10-min window every 5 min): window('ts','10 minutes','5 minutes').\n• Session window — gaps-based (Spark 3.2+).\nUsed with groupBy + watermark for time-based aggregation over event time."},{"q":"What is Auto Loader (cloudFiles)?","type":"concept","a":"Auto Loader is a Structured Streaming source that incrementally and efficiently discovers and ingests NEW files from cloud storage into Delta.\n• Scales to billions of files; processes each file once (tracked via checkpoint + rocksdb state).\n• Schema inference + evolution; rescued data column captures malformed records.\n• Two discovery modes: directory listing (default) or file notification (Event Grid/Queue) for lower cost at scale.\nTypical: ADLS → Auto Loader → Bronze Delta → Silver → Gold.","code":"(spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles')\n  .option('cloudFiles.format','json')\n  .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation','/ckpt/schema')\n  .load('abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/')\n  .writeStream.format('delta')\n  .option('checkpointLocation','/ckpt/bronze')\n  .trigger(availableNow=True)\n  .toTable('bronze.events'))"},{"q":"What is the rescued data column in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"When incoming data doesn't match the expected schema (type mismatch, corrupt record), Auto Loader doesn't lose it — it stores the raw value in a special rescued data column (_rescued_data) as JSON.\n• Enables later triage/quarantine instead of dropping bad records.\n• Set via schemaHints/schemaLocation; default column name _rescued_data (configurable)."},{"q":"File notification vs directory listing in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Directory listing (default) — periodically lists the directory to find new files; simple, no extra Azure setup; cost grows with directory size.\n• File notification — subscribes to cloud events (Azure Event Grid + Storage Queue) to be notified of new files; more scalable and cheaper for high-volume/very large directories.\nChoose notification for scale; listing for simplicity/small volumes."},{"q":"How do you integrate Kafka or Event Hubs with Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Both expose a Kafka-compatible endpoint; use Spark's Kafka source.\n• readStream.format('kafka').option('kafka.bootstrap.servers',...).option('subscribe','topic').\n• For Event Hubs: use the Kafka API with Event Hubs connection string/SASL (or the native Event Hubs connector).\n• Parse the value (JSON) with from_json, then write to Delta with a checkpoint.\n• Deliver exactly-once by using Delta as the sink + checkpointing."},{"q":"How do you deduplicate streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"Use dropDuplicates on the streaming DataFrame with an event-time key, combined with a watermark to bound state:\ndf.withWatermark('event_ts','10 minutes').dropDuplicates(['id']).\nFor unbounded/all-time dedupe, use MERGE into a Delta table (whenNotMatchedInsert) or foreachBatch with a merge on the unique key."},{"q":"What trigger modes does Structured Streaming support?","type":"concept","a":"• Default/processingTime — micro-batches at intervals (e.g., '1 minute', '5 seconds').\n• availableNow — processes all available data as fast as possible then stops (efficient incremental batch; great for Auto Loader/COPY-style loads).\n• once (deprecated) — similar to availableNow.\n• continuous — experimental low-latency (~ms) mode with limited operators.\navailableNow is commonly used for cost-efficient incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is foreachBatch and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"foreachBatch lets you run arbitrary logic on each micro-batch as a batch DataFrame.\n• Use it when the sink isn't natively supported or you need MERGE/upsert, multiple sinks, or custom dedupe.\n• It handles idempotency per batch (use batchId + MERGE).\nSignature: df.writeStream.foreachBatch(lambda df, batch_id: ...)."},{"q":"How do you achieve exactly-once processing in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"• Use checkpointing to track offsets/state.\n• Use an idempotent/transactional sink — Delta Lake writes are atomic, so replay is safe.\n• With foreachBatch, key the MERGE on a stable business key so re-processing the same batch doesn't duplicate.\n• Combine replayable source (Kafka offsets) + atomic sink = exactly-once end-to-end."},{"q":"What is DLT / Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines and what are its key features?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Live Tables (now Databricks Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines) is a declarative framework for building reliable pipelines with dependency management.\n• Define @dlt.table functions; the engine builds the dependency graph automatically (no manual orchestration).\n• Streaming tables (incremental) + materialized views (refreshed).\n• Data quality EXPECTATIONS: expect, expect_or_drop, expect_or_fail.\n• Auto retries, automatic lineage, schema evolution, and managed compute.\nDLT evolved into Declarative Pipelines — same concepts, broader scope.","code":"import dlt\n@dlt.table\n@dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_amount', 'amount > 0')\ndef silver_orders():\n    return dlt.read_stream('bronze_orders').filter('status != \"CANCELLED\"')"},{"q":"Difference between Delta Live Tables and Databricks Declarative Pipelines?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables is the older name; Declarative Pipelines is the newer, broader framework from Databricks.\n• Same core concepts (declarative @dlt tables, expectations, auto dependency, streaming tables + materialized views) with additional capabilities and general data-pipeline scope beyond ETL.\nSimple answer: DLT has evolved into Databricks Declarative Pipelines."},{"q":"How do you design a real-time analytics pipeline (Event Hubs → Databricks → Delta → BI)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Ingest: Spark Structured Streaming reads from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse (from_json), apply watermarks + dedupe, transform aggregations.\n• Write to a Bronze streaming Delta table (or DLT streaming table) with checkpointing for exactly-once.\n• Silver cleans/enriches; Gold aggregates for serving.\n• Serve to Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse, or to Real-Time Analytics for millisecond dashboards.\n• Monitor lag, throughput, and checkpoint health; tune triggers/partitions."},{"q":"What are the modes for writing streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"• Append — only new rows (default; Delta table grows).\n• Update — emit updated aggregation results (no full recompute).\n• Complete — rewrite the entire result table each batch (for aggregations without watermark).\nMost Delta streaming sinks use append; aggregations use update/complete."},{"q":"What is DLT (Delta Live Tables)?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables (DLT) is a Databricks framework for building reliable, maintainable, and automated ETL/ELT pipelines\n• Features:  Automa(cid:415)cally manages pipeline execu(cid:415)on.  Supports batch and streaming data.  Built-in data quality checks using expecta(cid:415)ons.  Automa(cid:415)cally handles dependencies between tables.  Provides monitoring and lineage.  Simplifies produc(cid:415)on pipeline development\n• Example: import dlt @dlt.table def customers(): return spark.read.table(\"bronze.customers\")","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Loader checks the checkpoint.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Only new files are read.","type":"concept","a":"Example: df = (spark.readStream .format(\"cloudFiles\") .op(cid:415)on(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"csv\") .load(\"/input\")) (df.writeStream .op(cid:415)on(\"checkpointLoca(cid:415)on\", \"/checkpoint\") .start(\"/output\"))","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"New Column in Streaming Data","type":"concept","a":"13. Schema Drift","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Autoloader — Auto-Ingest Files as They Arrive","type":"concept","a":"• Autoloader is Databricks' built-in streaming ingestion tool\n• It monitors a cloud storage path and automatically processes new files as they land — without you writing custom file-tracking logic\n• Why Autoloader? (vs. regular spark.read) Feature Regular spark.read vs Autoloader New files You must re-run the job manually each time new files arrive Autoloader Automatically detects and processes ONLY new files (no reprocessing old files) Scale Regular read loads ALL files every time — slow for millions of files Autoloader scale Uses cloud file notifications or incremental listing — handles millions of files efficiently Schema You must define schema manually or re-infer every time Autoloader schema Built-in schema inference + schema evolution support Autoloader Implementation # Basic Autoloader — reads new CSV files as they land df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/schema/') \\ # saves inferred schema .option('header', 'true') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/orders/') # Write the stream to a Delta table query = df_stream.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/orders/') \\ # tracks processed files .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ # handles new columns automatically .outputMode('append') \\ .trigger(availableNow=True) \\ # run once, process all pending files, then stop .toTable('silver.orders') query.awaitTermination() Autoloader with Schema Evolution (Production Pattern) # Production-ready Autoloader with transformations from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, input_file_name df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/events/schema/') \\ .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') \\ # handles new columns .load('abfss://raw@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/events/') # Add audit columns df_enriched = df_stream \\ .withColumn('ingestion_time', current_timestamp()) \\ .withColumn('source_file', input_file_name()) df_enriched.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/events/') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .outputMode('append') \\ .toTable('bronze.events') 🔑 Key Autoloader Concepts checkpointLocation: Remembers which files have been processed\n• NEVER change this path. schemaLocation: Stores the inferred/evolved schema over time. trigger(availableNow=True): Batch mode — process pending files and stop (like a scheduled batch job). trigger(processingTime='5 minutes'): Micro-batch — runs every 5 minutes continuously","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"New Column Added in Streaming — How to Handle","type":"concept","a":"• This is a very common real-world problem\n• Your source system adds a new column to the data (e.g., a new field in Kafka, a new column in the CSV files), and your streaming job fails or ignores it\n• The Problem","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is unable to handle real-time It can deal with real-time processing.","type":"concept","a":"processing.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Load Data into Databricks from an External Location?","type":"concept","a":"• Page 9 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Method 1 — Direct Read via abfss:// path # Read directly using the ABFSS path (no mount needed) df = spark.read.format('csv') .option('header','true') .option('inferSchema','true') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/data.csv') df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_data') Method 2 — Mount ADLS and Read from Mount # After mounting (see Q10), read from mount point df = spark.read.format('parquet').load('/mnt/datalake/raw/') Method 3 — Auto Loader for Incremental File Ingestion df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/schema') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/') ) df.writeStream.format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/bronze') .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.incremental_data') Method 4 — COPY INTO (SQL-based batch load) COPY INTO bronze.raw_orders FROM 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/orders/' FILEFORMAT = CSV FORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true', 'inferSchema' = 'true') COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true');\n• Method 5 — Unity Catalog External Location -- Create external location (Unity Catalog) CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION my_adls_location URL 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/' WITH (STORAGE CREDENTIAL my_storage_credential); -- Create external table pointing to it CREATE TABLE bronze.raw_sales LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/' AS SELECT * FROM read_files('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/');","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Code to Load Data into Azure Databricks","type":"concept","a":"From ADLS Gen2 (CSV) df = (spark.read .format('csv') .option('header', 'true') .option('inferSchema', 'true') .option('mode', 'PERMISSIVE') .option('columnNameOfCorruptRecord', '_corrupt') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/orders.csv') ) df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_orders') Page 16 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide From Azure SQL Database jdbc_url = 'jdbc:sqlserver://server.database.windows.net:1433;database=mydb' sql_props = { 'user': dbutils.secrets.get('kv-scope','sql-user'), 'password': dbutils.secrets.get('kv-scope','sql-password'), 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver' } # Full load df = spark.read.jdbc(url=jdbc_url, table='dbo.Orders', properties=sql_props) # Incremental load with pushdown predicate df = spark.read.jdbc( url=jdbc_url, table='dbo.Orders', predicates=['ModifiedDate > CAST(GETDATE()-1 AS DATE)'], properties=sql_props ) df.write.format('delta').mode('append').saveAsTable('bronze.sql_orders') Auto Loader (Incremental from ADLS) from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, input_file_name (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/ckpt/schema') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/') .withColumn('_loaded_at', current_timestamp()) .withColumn('_file', input_file_name()) .writeStream .format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/bronze') .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.events') )","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Why Databricks Instead of ADF?","type":"concept","a":"Page 1 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A ADF is excellent for orchestration and simple copy/move tasks; however, Databricks is preferred for heavy-duty data engineering for the following reasons: Feature Databricks ADF Processing Power Distributed Spark clusters – Limited compute; not designed for handles TBs easily heavy transforms Complex Transformations Full Python / SQL / Scala / R Only basic via Mapping Data Flow support (GUI-based) ML & Analytics Built-in MLflow, notebooks, Not supported AutoML Delta Lake Support Native first-class support Limited / no native support Real-time Streaming Structured Streaming & Limited streaming capability Autoloader Debugging Interactive notebooks, easy cell- Pipeline debugging is complex by-cell debug Cost at Scale More efficient for heavy workloads Cheaper for simple ETL / copy pipelines Version Control Git integration in Repos ARM templates only Summary: Use ADF for orchestration and scheduling; use Databricks for transformation, streaming, ML, and Delta Lake operations.","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Autoloader and Its Uses?","type":"concept","a":"• Autoloader (cloudFiles) is a Databricks feature that incrementally and efficiently ingests new files arriving in cloud storage (ADLS Gen2, S3, GCS) into Delta tables — without reprocessing old files\n• Key Features • Automatically detects new files — no manual tracking required • Processes files exactly once using checkpointing • Schema inference and evolution support • Scales to billions of files using file notification mode (Event Grid / SNS) • Supports JSON, CSV, Parquet, Avro, ORC, text, binary formats Two Discovery Modes Mode How it works Best for Directory Listing Periodically lists all files and identifies new Small to medium file volumes ones File Notification Uses cloud events (Event Grid/SNS) to High-volume, millions of files receive notifications Basic Autoloader Code df = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/schema/') \\ .load('/mnt/landing/input/') df.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoint/') \\ .start('/mnt/delta/output/') Page 3 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Live Table (DLT)?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables (DLT) is a declarative framework in Databricks for building reliable, scalable, and maintainable data pipelines\n• Instead of writing imperative ETL code, you declare what data transformations you want, and DLT manages the execution, orchestration, error handling, and monitoring automatically\n• Key Benefits • Automatic pipeline orchestration — no need to manage job dependencies manually • Built-in data quality with Expectations (constraints on data) • Automatic schema management and evolution • End-to-end lineage and observability in the DLT UI • Supports both batch and streaming workloads in the same pipeline Basic DLT Example import dlt from pyspark.sql.functions import col @dlt.table(comment='Raw ingested data') Page 6 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A def raw_data(): return spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .load('/mnt/landing/') @dlt.table @dlt.expect('valid_salary', 'salary > 0') def cleaned_data(): return dlt.read_stream('raw_data').filter(col('salary').isNotNull())","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Types of Delta Live Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Type Description Use Case Streaming Table Append-only; processes each row Real-time ingestion from exactly once from a streaming source\n• Autoloader, Kafka, Event Hubs Defined with @dlt.table + readStream\n• Materialized View Pre-computed result of a query, Aggregations, joins, reporting- updated automatically by the pipeline. ready data Can perform aggregations\n• Live Table (batch) Standard DLT table refreshed in full or Batch ETL, slowly changing incrementally on each pipeline run. dimensions","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is an Autoloader? (Detailed)","type":"concept","a":"(See also Q6 above for overview) Additional Details • Autoloader maintains a checkpoint so it never reprocesses the same file twice, even across cluster restarts • Schema inference: on first run, Autoloader samples files and infers the schema automatically • Schema hints: you can manually specify data types for specific columns to override inference • Supports schema evolution — new columns in files can be automatically merged into the target schema • Works seamlessly with Delta Live Tables for production-grade ingestion pipelines File Notification vs Directory Listing — When to Use Scenario Recommended Mode < 10,000 files and simple setup needed Directory Listing Page 7 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Millions of files / high-throughput ingestion File Notification (Event Grid on Azure, SNS/SQS on AWS) Near real-time latency required File Notification Cross-cloud or custom storage Directory Listing","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Auto Loader concept?","type":"concept","a":"• Auto Loader is a Databricks feature (built on Spark Structured Streaming) that efficiently ingests new files arriving in cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) into Delta Lake incrementally\n• Key Features: • Incremental Ingestion: Processes only new files since the last checkpoint — no need to list all files on every run. • Scalable File Detection: Uses cloud notification services (Azure Event Grid / AWS SNS+SQS) instead of listing directory contents, making it efficient at scale. • Schema Inference & Evolution: Can automatically infer schema from incoming files and handle schema changes. • Exactly-once Processing: Uses checkpointing to ensure files are not reprocessed\n• Basic Syntax: df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/schema_checkpoint/') .load('/mnt/landing/raw_data/')) df.writeStream .format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoint/') .outputMode('append') .start('/mnt/delta/bronze_table/')","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Difference b/w Copy into vs Auto loader?","type":"concept","a":"Copy Into vs Auto Loader Feature Copy Into Auto Loader Type Batch Streaming File tracking Manual Automa(cid:415)c Use case One-(cid:415)me load Con(cid:415)nuous inges(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is expecta(cid:415)on in DLT?","type":"concept","a":"Expecta(cid:415)ons in DLT  Define data quality rules: CONSTRAINT valid_salary EXPECT (salary > 0)  Can drop or quaran(cid:415)ne bad records","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What’s your plan for career growth as a Data Engineer in the next few years?","type":"concept","a":"• In the next few years, I want to deepen my expertise in Azure Data Engineering by working on large-scale, cloud-native data platforms\n• I aim to become proficient in advanced Spark optimization, real-time streaming, and data architecture\n• As I gain more experience, I'd like to take on technical leadership responsibilities, mentor junior engineers, and contribute to designing scalable, reliable, and cost-efficient data solutions that support business goals","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Loader in ADF — Latest File Detection","type":"concept","a":"• Auto Loader is a Databricks feature that automatically detects and processes new files arriving in cloud storage using Structured Streaming's cloudFiles format source\n• Auto Loader Code (Databricks) df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/checkpoint/schema') .load('/mnt/landing-zone/') ) df.writeStream .option('checkpointLocation', '/checkpoint/data') Page 5 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.raw_events') How to Know the Latest File Placed • Method 1 — Auto Loader Checkpoint: tracks processed files automatically; new files picked up on next run # Method 2 — Check via dbutils files = dbutils.fs.ls('/mnt/landing-zone/') latest = max(files, key=lambda f: f.modificationTime) print(latest.name, latest.modificationTime) • Method 3 — ADF Get Metadata Activity: use 'Last Modified' field and compare with previous run timestamp -- Method 4 — Delta audit log DESCRIBE HISTORY my_table","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What value do you use from Lookup while giving dynamic parameters?","type":"concept","a":"If First row only = True: Use: @ac(cid:415)vity('Lookup1').output.firstRow.TableName Example: @ac(cid:415)vity('Lookup1').output.firstRow.Watermark If First row only = False: Use: @ac(cid:415)vity('Lookup1').output.value This returns an array, which is commonly passed to a ForEach ac(cid:415)vity.","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If Blob Storage Has Thousands of CSV Files, How Would You Load Them","type":"concept","a":"Efficiently? Use Auto Loader (cloudFiles format) — the Databricks-native solution for large-scale incremental file ingestion: df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('header', 'true') .option('inferSchema', 'true') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/schema') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/csv/') ) df.writeStream .format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/bronze') .option('mergeSchema', 'true') .trigger(availableNow=True) # batch mode: process all new files, then stop .toTable('bronze.raw_csv_data')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Why Auto Loader over plain spark.read?","type":"concept","a":"• spark.read.csv() lists ALL files on every run — slow and redundant for thousands of files • Auto Loader tracks processed files in a checkpoint — only new files are processed each run • File notification mode (Azure Event Grid / AWS SNS) detects new files instantly without directory listing • Schema evolution handled automatically via schemaLocation — new columns merged gracefully","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Most Efficient Way to Load Files in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Method Best For Incremental? Notes Page 9 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Auto Loader (cloudFiles) Thousands of files, cloud Yes Best overall — uses file storage notifications + checkpoint COPY INTO SQL-based batch loads, Yes (tracks Simpler than Auto Loader; idempotent loaded files) SQL syntax spark.readStream (manual) Custom streaming with Yes (manual More complex; need to full control checkpoint) manage state spark.read.format() One-time full/historical No Simplest — not for loads incremental use Auto Loader is the recommended production approach for incremental ingestion\n• COPY INTO is good for simpler SQL-centric workflows. spark.read is fine for one-time historical data loads","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Does Auto Loader Perform Incremental Loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Mode 1 — Directory Listing (default) • Lists the source directory and compares against checkpoint of already-processed files • Simple to set up; suitable for directories with fewer than a few hundred thousand files Mode 2 — File Notification (recommended for scale) • Cloud storage sends event notifications (Azure Event Grid, AWS S3 Events, GCS Pub/Sub) when new files arrive • Auto Loader subscribes to the notification queue and processes only new files • Much faster than directory listing at scale; no full directory scan .option('cloudFiles.useNotifications', 'true') .option('cloudFiles.subscriptionId', '<azure-subscription-id>') .option('cloudFiles.resourceGroup', '<resource-group>') The checkpoint folder on cloud storage records every processed file path and offset\n• On the next run, Auto Loader resumes from the checkpoint and skips already-processed files","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Schedule Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Jobs — create a Job that runs the Auto Loader notebook on a cron schedule (e.g., every 30 minutes or hourly) • Delta Live Tables (DLT) — define Auto Loader as a DLT source; Databricks fully manages scheduling, retries, and monitoring • Azure Data Factory — ADF triggers a Databricks notebook/job on a schedule or via tumbling window triggers • Databricks Workflows — multi-task workflows with dependencies across Bronze/Silver/Gold layers .trigger(availableNow=True) # Scheduled batch: process all pending files then stop .trigger(processingTime='1 hour') # Continuous micro-batch every hour .trigger(continuous='1 minute') # Near real-time, low-latency streaming","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Triggers Are Available in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Trigger Syntax Use Case Available Now trigger(availableNow=True) Process all pending files then stop; best for scheduled batch jobs Page 10 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Processing Time trigger(processingTime='30 Micro-batch every N seconds/minutes; seconds') near real-time Once (deprecated) trigger(once=True) Old version of availableNow; still works but deprecated in Spark 3.3+ Continuous trigger(continuous='1 minute') Ultra-low latency streaming; millisecond processing No trigger (default) Omit trigger option Continuous streaming; runs until manually stopped For scheduled batch jobs: use trigger(availableNow=True)\n• For near real-time: trigger(processingTime='30 seconds'). availableNow is the most common choice in production scheduled pipelines","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Does Auto Loader Detect New Files?","type":"concept","a":"• Directory Listing: scans the source path, compares full file list against checkpoint; files not in checkpoint are new • File Notification: cloud storage fires an event (S3 SNS -> SQS, ADLS -> Event Grid, GCS -> Pub/Sub) on each new file; Auto Loader polls the queue • Checkpoint: stored on cloud storage (similar to Delta log) — records every processed file and stream offset • Schema location: Auto Loader samples new file batches and updates schema if new columns are detected spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.useNotifications', 'false') # directory listing mode .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/ckpt/schema') # schema evolution tracking .option('cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger', '1000') # limit files per micro-batch .load('/mnt/landing/') Section 8 | Data Quality","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How is Data Loaded into the Raw/Bronze Layer from Thousands of CSV Files?","type":"concept","a":"• Auto Loader reads new CSV files from ADLS/S3 incrementally using the cloudFiles format • Minimal transformation in Bronze — raw data written as-is to a Delta table • Add metadata columns: _ingestion_time and _source_file for lineage/audit • Enable mergeSchema to handle new columns arriving in new file batches from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, input_file_name df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/ckpt/bronze_schema') .option('header', 'true') .load('/mnt/landing/csv/') .withColumn('_ingestion_time', current_timestamp()) .withColumn('_source_file', input_file_name()) ) df.writeStream.format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/bronze') .option('mergeSchema', 'true') .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.raw_transactions')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Expectations, DLT expectations?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• Are there opportunities to work on real-time streaming use cases, or is the focus primarily batch?","type":"concept","a":"Questions about the role","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Have you worked on streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"• If your project is batch: \"My current project is primarily batch-based\n• We use Azure Data Factory to schedule daily incremental loads into ADLS, transform them using Databricks, and publish curated data for repor(cid:415)ng\n• Although I haven't implemented streaming in produc(cid:415)on, I'm familiar with Spark Structured Streaming concepts such as micro-batches, checkpoints, and event processing.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Micro-batch / Near Real-time","type":"concept","a":"• Data is processed in small batches at short intervals (seconds to minutes) • Tools: Spark Structured Streaming with trigger intervals","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Streaming / Real-time Ingestion","type":"concept","a":"• Data is ingested and processed as it arrives (continuous) • Tools: Kafka, Azure Event Hubs, Spark Streaming, Kinesis • Use case: Fraud detection, IoT sensor data, clickstream","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Delta Live Tables (DLT)","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables (DLT) is a declarative ETL framework in Databricks for building reliable, maintainable data pipelines with built-in data quality checks\n• Key Concepts: • LIVE TABLE: Materialized view updated by DLT engine • STREAMING LIVE TABLE: For streaming/incremental data • EXPECTATIONS: Data quality constraints import dlt from pyspark.sql.functions import col # Bronze layer - raw ingestion @dlt.table(comment='Raw orders from source') def bronze_orders(): return spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .load('/landing/orders/') # Silver layer - cleaned with DQ checks @dlt.table(comment='Cleaned orders') @dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_amount', 'amount > 0') @dlt.expect('not_null_id', 'order_id IS NOT NULL') def silver_orders(): return dlt.read_stream('bronze_orders') \\ .filter(col('status') != 'CANCELLED') # Gold layer - aggregated @dlt.table(comment='Daily revenue') def gold_daily_revenue(): return dlt.read('silver_orders') \\ .groupBy('order_date') \\ .agg(sum('amount').alias('total_revenue'))","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"governance","name":"Unity Catalog, Governance & Security","icon":"🔐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Unity Catalog and what problem does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance layer for data and AI assets across all workspaces in an account.\n• Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns, rows).\n• 3-level namespace: catalog.schema.table.\n• Built-in data lineage (column-level), audit logs, and Delta Sharing.\n• External locations + storage credentials for governed cloud storage access.\n• Row-level security (row filters) and column masking.\nSolves per-workspace (Hive metastore) silos with account-level governance and fine-grained security."},{"q":"Explain the Unity Catalog object hierarchy.","type":"concept","a":"Metastore (top-level, one per region) → Catalog → Schema (database) → objects (tables, views, volumes, functions, models).\n• Metastore stores metadata + permissions for all workspaces attached to it.\n• Permissions flow downward (grant at a higher level, inherits lower).\n• Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders.\nRelated objects: storage credentials, external locations, shares."},{"q":"Managed vs external tables AND managed vs external volumes in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — UC manages data lifecycle/location; DROP deletes metadata AND data.\n• External table — references a location you own (external location); DROP removes only metadata, files remain.\n• Managed volume — UC-managed storage for non-tabular files (images, PDFs, CSV); deleted with the volume.\n• External volume — references existing cloud storage you manage (via external location); files remain after dropping the volume.\nVolumes are like managed/external tables but for files."},{"q":"What are storage credentials and external locations in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage credential — a UC securable that holds cloud auth (managed identity / service principal / access connector) to access storage; no secrets in code.\n• External location — combines a cloud storage URL (abfss://...) with a storage credential, granting governed access to that path.\n• Access Connector — an Azure resource providing managed-identity access from Databricks to ADLS.\nTogether they let UC govern who can read/write specific storage paths."},{"q":"How do you implement row-level and column-level security in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Column-level: dynamic column masks — a UDF masks sensitive values based on IS_ACCOUNT_GROUP_MEMBER / current_user(): ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_fn.\n• Row-level: row filter functions — ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER filter_fn ON (region); returns only rows the user is allowed to see.\n• Unity Catalog enforces them centrally; also table/column GRANT/REVOKE.\nLegacy: table ACLs / AAD credential passthrough (being replaced by UC)."},{"q":"Difference between Unity Catalog and Hive Metastore?","type":"concept","a":"• Scope: Hive = per-workspace; UC = account-level across all workspaces.\n• Namespace: Hive 2-level (db.table); UC 3-level (catalog.schema.table).\n• Governance: Hive basic ACLs, no column/row security; UC fine-grained (row filters, column masks), lineage, audit logs.\n• Sharing: Hive none; UC Delta Sharing built-in.\n• UC is the current standard; Hive is legacy/being replaced."},{"q":"What is Delta Sharing?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Sharing is an open protocol to securely share data (and AI assets) across organizations/workspaces/business units WITHOUT copying data.\n• A provider shares a subset of tables/volumes; recipients read via the share (Databricks-to-Databricks or open recipients like Power BI/pandas).\n• Governance and audit are maintained; recipients only see what's shared.\nScenario: 3 business units with separate workspaces share data through Delta Sharing instead of duplicating into each workspace."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Three business units have separate workspaces. How do you share data between them?","type":"scenario","a":"Option A — Unity Catalog + Delta Sharing: publish selected tables as a share from the provider workspace; grant recipients access; they read without copying. Centralized governance/audit.\nOption B — one UC metastore per region attached to all workspaces with catalogs per BU and GRANTs (cross-workspace access within the same account).\nChoose Delta Sharing for cross-org/cross-cloud or strict isolation; UC catalogs when all workspaces share an account/metastore."},{"q":"How do you secure access to ADLS from Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Authentication: Service Principal (app registration) + Storage Blob Data Contributor, or Managed Identity / Access Connector (no secrets), or account key (avoid).\n• Secrets: store SPN keys in Azure Key Vault + Databricks secret scope; read via dbutils.secrets.\n• RBAC: Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor/Owner.\n• POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 HNS): file/folder-level rwx with masks + defaults.\n• Unity Catalog: external locations + storage credentials + GRANTs (preferred).\n• Network: private endpoints/service endpoints/firewall.\n• Encryption: SSE at rest (Microsoft-managed or CMK), TLS in transit.\n• Avoid mounting with keys; prefer UC or OAuth."},{"q":"What are service principals, managed identities, and secrets in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Service principal — a non-human identity (app registration) used by pipelines/CI-CD for automated auth.\n• Managed identity — Azure-managed identity so resources authenticate without storing credentials (via Access Connector to ADLS).\n• Secrets — Databricks secret scopes (backed by Azure Key Vault) store passwords/tokens/keys; accessed with dbutils.secrets.get().\nBest practice: least privilege, no secrets in code, rotate regularly."},{"q":"How do you audit who accessed what data in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog audit logs capture user actions (logins, cluster/job runs, permission changes, data access) and column-level lineage.\n• Enable diagnostic logging to a storage account / Log Analytics; query with KQL.\n• Lineage shows how data flows between tables/columns/notebooks/jobs.\n• Combine with Azure Monitor for alerting on suspicious access."},{"q":"What is the difference between RBAC and ACL in the lakehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — permissions granted to roles/principals at the control/service level (e.g., Azure roles on storage, workspace admin roles).\n• ACLs (Access Control Lists) — fine-grained permissions on specific objects (tables, columns, files/folders via POSIX ACLs).\nBoth are used together: RBAC for broad access, UC ACLs/GRANTs for fine-grained data-level control."},{"q":"What is least-privilege design and how do you apply it?","type":"concept","a":"Grant users/apps only the minimum permissions needed.\n• Use groups (not individual users) and grant at the highest sensible level.\n• Use read-only roles where possible; scope service principals to specific storage/workspaces.\n• Apply column masks/row filters for sensitive data.\n• Separate prod/dev credentials; audit and rotate regularly."},{"q":"What are components of Azure data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ User --A unique individual who has access to the system. ➢ Group --- A collection of users ➢ Access control list (ACL) ➢ Notebook ➢ Dashboard ➢ Library ➢ Repo ➢ Experiment","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It supports more security projects Its security is not as good as Map Reduce","type":"concept","a":"and continuously working on its security issues.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of security of Blob or storage account?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is RBAC?","type":"concept","a":"Role back access control Click on Access control (IAM) then click on add role assignment","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Provide Security Over ADLS Storage?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"RBAC — Role-Based Access Control","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Blob Data Reader — read-only access • Storage Blob Data Contributor — read + write access • Storage Blob Data Owner — full control including ACLs","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 hierarchical namespace)","type":"concept","a":"• File/folder level ACLs — grant rwx permissions at directory level • Masks and default ACLs for inheritance","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"• Centralized governance — define External Locations and Storage Credentials • Fine-grained access: GRANT SELECT ON TABLE to specific users or groups • Row-level and column-level security through Delta Lake row filters and column masks","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog vs Hive Metastore","type":"concept","a":"• Feature Hive Metastore Unity Catalog Page 19 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Scope Workspace-level (per ADB Account-level (across all workspaces) workspace) Governance Basic — no column/row security Fine-grained: table, column, row-level Namespace 2-level: database.table 3-level: catalog.schema.table Data lineage Not built-in Built-in — column-level lineage tracking Access control Workspace-level ACLs Centralized — groups, GRANT/REVOKE Audit logs Limited Full — who accessed what and when External tables Supported Supported via External Locations Delta Sharing Not supported Built-in Delta Sharing support Migration path Legacy — being replaced by UC Current standard — preferred Multiple workspaces Each workspace has own One Unity Catalog for all workspaces metastore -- Hive Metastore (2-level namespace) USE DATABASE my_database;\n• SELECT * FROM my_table; -- Unity Catalog (3-level namespace) SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table; -- Grant in Unity Catalog GRANT SELECT ON TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table TO `data_analysts`;\n• GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG my_catalog TO `data_engineers`;\n• Unity Catalog is the recommended governance solution for all new Databricks deployments\n• It provides centralized data access control, lineage, and auditing across the entire organization rather than per- workspace","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you handle PII data in your project?","type":"concept","a":"Handling PII data  Masking / tokeniza(cid:415)on  Encryp(cid:415)on (at rest + transit)  Unity Catalog access control  Row-level security  Restricted columns in Silver/Gold layer","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Standard, Premium, and Enterprise in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Standard — Core Apache Spark, notebooks, jobs, basic collaboration. No RBAC or advanced security. • Premium — Adds Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), Databricks SQL (SQL Warehouses), audit logs, token management, SSO. Suitable for enterprise data teams. • Enterprise — Full Premium features plus compliance certifications (HIPAA, FedRAMP), enhanced security controls, network isolation (Private Link), and dedicated support SLAs. SQL Warehouse (SQL Data Warehouse in Databricks)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Managed Volume vs External Volume","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog introduces Volumes for storing non-tabular files\n• Managed Volume External Volume Databricks manages storage Customer manages storage Files stored in managed loca(cid:415)on Files stored in external cloud storage Deleted with the volume Files remain a(cid:332)er dropping the volume Simpler management Greater flexibility Think of it like managed vs external tables, but for files rather than tables\n• Examples: Managed Volume: Catalog ↓ Schema ↓ Managed Volume ↓ Images PDFs CSV External Volume: ADLS ↓ External Loca(cid:415)on ↓ Unity Catalog Volume","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Are you familiar with Unity Catalog architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Basic architecture: Metastore ↓ Catalog ↓ Schema ↓ Tables Views Volumes Func(cid:415)ons Models Permissions flow downward\n• Example: Metastore ↓ Sales Catalog ↓ Finance Schema ↓ Orders Table Main components:  Metastore  Catalog  Schema  Tables  Views  Volumes  External Loca(cid:415)ons  Storage Creden(cid:415)als Benefits:  Centralized governance  Fine-grained access control  Data lineage  Audi(cid:415)ng  Cross-workspace sharing (when a(cid:425)ached to the same metastore)","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is Predicate Pushdown in Parquet?","type":"concept","a":"• Predicate pushdown means the storage layer filters data before Spark reads it\n• Example: df = spark.read.parquet(\"sales\") df.filter(\"country='US'\") Spark reads only row groups that contain country='US'\n• Benefits:  Less disk I/O  Faster execu(cid:415)on  Lower memory usage Parquet stores:  Min value  Max value  Sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs Spark uses these sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs to skip unnecessary row groups\n• RBAC vs ACL RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) Permissions are assigned to roles\n• Example: Admin Read Write Delete User Read User gets permissions by role\n• Advantages:  Easy management  Enterprise friendly ACL (Access Control List) Permissions are assigned directly to each resource\n• Example: File A John → Read Alice → Write Bob → No Access Useful for object-level permissions\n• Difference RBAC ACL Role based Resource based Easier to manage More granular Scalable Complex with many users Enterprise systems File/object permissions","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance solu(cid:415)on for managing data and AI assets across mul(cid:415)ple workspaces\n• It provides centralized access control, data discovery, audi(cid:415)ng, and lineage for tables, views, files, machine learning models, and other data assets.\" Key Features  Centralized data governance  Fine-grained access control (catalog, schema, table, column, row)  Data lineage  Data discovery  Audit logs  Cross-workspace data sharing  Integra(cid:415)on with cloud storage like ADLS, S3, and GCS Unity Catalog hierarchy Catalog │ Schema │ Table/View Example: Catalog : Sales Schema : Bronze Table : Orders Access example: SELECT * FROM Sales.Bronze.Orders;","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why is Unity Catalog used?","type":"concept","a":"\"Instead of managing permissions separately in each workspace, Unity Catalog provides a single place to manage security, governance, and metadata across the organiza(cid:415)on.\" Difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table (DLT) Delta Table Delta Live Table Storage format based on Delta Lake Framework for building ETL pipelines Stores data Creates and manages data pipelines Manually created Automa(cid:415)cally managed You write SQL or Spark code You define transforma(cid:415)ons declara(cid:415)vely Manual scheduling Built-in orchestra(cid:415)on No built-in data quality Supports data quality expecta(cid:415)ons Manual monitoring Built-in monitoring and lineage","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Unity Catalog? Have you implemented it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance solu(cid:415)on for managing data, AI assets, and permissions across workspaces\n• It provides:  Centralized access control.  Fine-grained permissions at catalog, schema, table, and column levels.  Data lineage to track data movement.  Audit logs for compliance.  Secure sharing through Delta Sharing\n• In my project, I worked with Unity Catalog by storing managed Delta tables in catalogs, assigning role-based permissions to different teams, and ensuring secure access to data.\" (If you haven't implemented it, say: \"I understand its architecture and concepts, and I have worked in environments where it was configured by the pla(cid:414)orm team.\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"AI tools / Unity Catalog","type":"concept","a":" AI tools: ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot  Unity Catalog: o Centralized governance layer in Databricks o Manages access control for data, tables, and models o Enables data lineage + security","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance solution for data and AI assets across all workspaces\n• Three-level namespace: catalog.schema.table -- Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders_table Key Features: • Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns) • Data lineage: Tracks data flow from source to destination • Audit logs: Who accessed what data and when • External locations: Unified access to cloud storage (ADLS, S3) • Delta Sharing: Securely share data across organizations • Column-level & Row-level security -- Grant permissions in Unity Catalog GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders TO `data_analyst@company.com`;\n• GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA prod_catalog.sales_schema TO `data_team`; -- Create external table via Unity Catalog CREATE TABLE prod_catalog.sales_schema.ext_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/orders';\n• Q13\n• Cache() cache() stores a DataFrame in memory (or memory+disk) so it doesn't need to be recomputed for subsequent actions. # cache() = persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) df_cached = df.cache() # Lazy - cached on first action df_cached.count() # Triggers caching # persist() allows specifying storage level from pyspark import StorageLevel df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) # Fastest, needs RAM df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) # Spills to disk if needed df.persist(StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY) # Saves RAM, slower df.unpersist() # Free cached data when done When to use cache(): • DataFrame is used multiple times in the same job • Iterative algorithms (ML training loops) • After expensive transformations (joins, aggregations)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"architecture","name":"Medallion Architecture & Data Platform Design","icon":"🏗️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold)?","type":"concept","a":"Medallion architecture organizes a lakehouse into progressive quality layers:\n• Bronze (raw) — exact copy of source, minimal transformation, append-only, audit trail, Delta format.\n• Silver (cleansed/conformed) — cleaned, validated, deduplicated, standardized, joined; the 'single source of truth' for analytics/DS.\n• Gold (business) — aggregated, enriched, business-ready (KPIs, star schemas) for BI/reporting.\nTypical flow: ADLS → Databricks → Bronze → Silver → Gold → Power BI."},{"q":"What is stored in each Medallion layer and in what format?","type":"concept","a":"• Bronze: raw as-is + metadata columns (load_date, ingestion_timestamp, source_file). Delta (converted from CSV/JSON/Parquet). No business transforms. Path e.g. /bronze/orders.\n• Silver: validated, deduplicated, type-cast, joined, conformed data. Delta. /silver/orders_clean.\n• Gold: business aggregates, KPIs, star-schema fact/dim tables. Delta (or Parquet for serving). /gold/daily_sales.\nAlways store transformed data as Delta for ACID, time travel, schema evolution, and MERGE."},{"q":"Walk through an end-to-end Medallion implementation.","type":"concept","a":"1. Ingest — ADF / Auto Loader / COPY INTO lands raw files into ADLS Bronze as Delta (add metadata columns).\n2. Transform to Silver — PySpark/DLT: cleanse, dedupe, cast types, standardize, apply DQ checks, join reference data; MERGE incrementally.\n3. Build Gold — aggregate to KPI tables / star schema (facts + dims), apply SCD logic, partition by date.\n4. Serve — Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse; ML from Silver.\n5. Govern — Unity Catalog for access, lineage, RLS/CLS; monitor with alerts + audit.\n6. Orchestrate — Databricks Workflows / ADF with retries, parameterization, dev→qa→prod promotion."},{"q":"What data validations do you perform on the Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema/type validation — columns and types match expectations.\n• Null checks on critical columns.\n• Duplicate checks on business/primary keys.\n• Range/domain validation (e.g., age 0–150, valid enums).\n• Referential integrity (child keys exist in parents via left anti join).\n• Record-count reconciliation vs source/threshold.\n• Business rules (end_date >= start_date).\nImplement as DQ framework / DLT expectations; quarantine or fail on critical violations."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Data moves from Silver to Gold. At what stage do you aggregate and why? How do you apply RLS/CLS there?","type":"scenario","a":"• Aggregate in the GOLD layer — Silver keeps granular, conformed data (reusable, single source of truth); Gold is purpose-built for BI with pre-computed aggregates (faster dashboards, smaller scans). Aggregating too early loses reusability.\n• RLS/CLS: apply in Gold (and where sensitive columns exist) using Unity Catalog row filters and column masks so Power BI/Tableau consumers only see permitted rows/columns. Enforce centrally in UC, not in each report.\n• Also restrict access via GRANTs on Gold tables/views."},{"q":"When would you choose batch vs streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — scheduled, high-throughput, latency-tolerant: end-of-day reports, historical/backfill loads, month-end close, large transformations.\n• Streaming — continuous, low-latency: fraud detection, IoT/telemetry, clickstream, real-time alerting/dashboards, CDC propagation.\nMany platforms use a lambda-style hybrid: streaming for speed, batch for corrections/reprocessing."},{"q":"How do you design a pipeline for incremental data loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Track a watermark (max updated_ts/ID) per table in a control table.\n• Extract only records > watermark (predicate pushdown at source).\n• Apply transforms; MERGE into the target (upsert; handle deletes via CDC if available).\n• Update the watermark only after success.\n• Handle late-arriving data (overlap window / reprocess) and ensure idempotency.\n• Schedule/orchestrate via ADF or Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"What is data quality and what are its dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"Data Quality = how fit data is for its intended use. Dimensions:\n• Completeness (no missing required values)\n• Accuracy (reflects reality)\n• Consistency (same across systems)\n• Timeliness (available when needed)\n• Uniqueness (no duplicates)\n• Validity (conforms to formats/rules).\nEnforced via DLT expectations, Great Expectations, or custom PySpark checks; fail fast or quarantine bad records."},{"q":"How do you handle bad/corrupt records?","type":"concept","a":"Never let bad records corrupt trusted layers. Options:\n• Quarantine — write invalid rows to a separate bad-records table/path for triage.\n• Rescue — Auto Loader's _rescued_data column keeps unmatched/corrupt values.\n• Drop/log — expect_or_drop in DLT (log + drop) or filter with logging.\n• Fail fast — expect_or_fail for critical rules (stop the pipeline).\n• Schema handling — mode PERMISSIVE (keeps corrupt as null), DROPMALFORMED (drop), FAILFAST (error)."},{"q":"What is the 'typical architecture' for an Azure data engineering project?","type":"concept","a":"Sources (SQL Server, APIs, CSV, SAP) → ADF (orchestration/ingestion) → ADLS Gen2 → Databricks (PySpark transformations) with Bronze/Silver/Gold Delta layers → serve to Power BI / Synapse.\nCross-cutting: Unity Catalog governance, Key Vault secrets, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics, CI/CD, and orchestration via ADF/Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"How do you decide between partitioning, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning — low-cardinality columns (date, region, country) where each partition is ≥ ~1 GB; enables partition pruning; avoid high cardinality/over-partitioning.\n• Z-ORDER — high-cardinality filter/join columns (customer_id) on existing (often partitioned) tables; re-cluster on OPTIMIZE.\n• Liquid Clustering — modern default for evolving queries; incremental auto re-clustering, no partition maintenance; can replace partition+Z-ORDER.\nDecide by query filter patterns + table size + maintenance overhead."},{"q":"How do you handle schema drift/evolution across the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Detect: compare source vs expected schema (Get Metadata/Validate) or let reads report new columns.\n• ADF: Allow schema drift in data flows; benign schema changes flow through.\n• Auto Loader: schema inference + evolution (_rescued_data for mismatches).\n• Delta: mergeSchema on write / delta.autoMerge; ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN.\n• Design Bronze to be schema-flexible (raw), enforce structure at Silver/Gold.\n• Add alerts on drift so downstream contracts don't break silently."},{"q":"Explain your Project Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Our project follows Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• Flow: Source Systems | | (SAP, SQL Server, APIs, CSV, Oracle) | Azure Data Factory | | Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 | | Databricks Bronze Layer (Raw Data) | Silver Layer (Cleansed & Transformed) | Gold Layer (Business Ready Tables) | Power BI / Synapse / ML Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2  Azure Databricks  Delta Lake  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI  Azure Monitor","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"what is difference between data lake and data warehouse","type":"concept","a":"• Data lakes and data warehouses are both widely used for storing big data, but they are not interchangeable terms\n• A data lake is a vast pool of raw data, the purpose for which is not yet defined\n• A data warehouse is a repository for structured, filtered data that has already been processed for a specific purpose","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It’s design is very simple. While it’s design is complex.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Architecture of Synapse Data ware house? And what are different kind of","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"19.What is DW?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Data ware house is nothing but collect the data, store the data, analysis the data and consume the data\n• A data warehouse is specially designed for data analytics, which involves reading large amounts of data to understand relationships and trends across the data\n• A database is used to capture and store data, such as recording details of a transaction","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is data mart?","type":"concept","a":"A data mart is a simple form of data warehouse focused on a single subject or line of business.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Can you introduce yourself?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Hi, I'm an Azure Data Engineer with experience in building end-to-end data pipelines using Azure Data Factory (ADF), Azure Databricks, ADLS Gen2, Azure SQL Database, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on inges(cid:415)ng data from mul(cid:415)ple source systems into Azure Data Lake, transforming it using PySpark in Databricks, and loading curated data into the Gold layer following the Medallion Architecture\n• I have experience in developing ADF pipelines, crea(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng data valida(cid:415)on and cleansing, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, handling incremental loads, monitoring pipelines, and suppor(cid:415)ng produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Our project is an employee analy(cid:415)cs pla(cid:414)orm\n• Data comes from HR systems, A(cid:425)endance systems, and Payroll applica(cid:415)ons in CSV and SQL formats\n• The architecture is: Source Systems │ Azure Data Factory │ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 │ Bronze Layer │ Azure Databricks (PySpark) │ Silver Layer │ Business Transforma(cid:415)ons │ Gold Layer │ Power BI Repor(cid:415)ng ADF is responsible for orchestra(cid:415)ng the pipelines\n• Databricks performs data cleansing and transforma(cid:415)ons\n• Gold tables are used by business users through Power BI dashboards.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are your roles and responsibili(cid:415)es?","type":"concept","a":"\"My daily responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing Azure Data Factory pipelines  Crea(cid:415)ng Databricks notebooks using PySpark  Reading and wri(cid:415)ng data to ADLS Gen2  Performing data cleansing and valida(cid:415)on  Removing duplicates and handling null values  Implemen(cid:415)ng Delta Lake tables  Loading data into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Monitoring pipeline failures  Fixing produc(cid:415)on issues  Working with Git for version control  Coordina(cid:415)ng with QA and business teams\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ETL and ELT","type":"concept","a":"• ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) and ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) are two approaches for moving and preparing data\n• Aspect ETL ELT Order Extract → Transform → Load Extract → Load → Transform Where Target data Separate ETL tool/server transformations occur warehouse/lakehouse Aspect ETL ELT Data loaded into Already transformed Raw data first, then transformed target Leverages warehouse compute Performance Depends on ETL server capacity power Traditional data warehouses and Modern cloud platforms with Best for strict data quality requirements scalable compute Raw data remains available for Flexibility Less flexible once transformed reprocessing","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your end-to-end project architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Sample Answer \"Our project is an Azure-based data pla(cid:414)orm built using a Medallion Architecture.\" Web Applica(cid:415)ons / ERP / APIs │ ▼ On-Prem SQL Server │ Azure Data Factory │ ▼ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 Bronze Layer (Raw) │ ▼ Azure Databricks (PySpark) Silver Layer (Cleaned) │ ▼ Azure Databricks Gold Layer │ ▼ Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs │ ▼ Power BI Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Databricks  ADLS Gen2  Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Where is the actual data stored?","type":"concept","a":"Different stages:  SQL Server → Opera(cid:415)onal (OLTP)  ADLS Bronze → Raw  ADLS Silver → Cleaned  ADLS Gold → Curated  Synapse → Analy(cid:415)cs/Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are you storing data in SQL Server, ADLS, or Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Yes, but each serves a different purpose: Storage Purpose SQL Server Opera(cid:415)onal data ADLS Data lake (Bronze, Silver, Gold) Synapse Repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Architecture of project (End-to-End pipeline)","type":"concept","a":"We follow Medallion Architecture in Azure Databricks: (cid:44934) Flow: Sources  SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake ADF (Inges(cid:415)on Layer)  Copy Ac(cid:415)vity / Mapping Data Flow  Moves data to ADLS ADLS Gen2 Storage  Bronze (Raw data) Databricks Processing  Bronze → Silver → Gold (cid:44934) Layers:  Bronze → Raw ingested data  Silver → Cleaned + validated + deduplicated  Gold → Business aggregated data Consump(cid:415)on Layer  Power BI / Tableau / SQL Warehouse","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"After Transformation, Where Do You Store the Data?","type":"concept","a":"• After transformation, data is stored in the appropriate Medallion Architecture layer as a Delta table: Layer What is Stored Format Location Example Bronze (Raw) Raw data as-is from source;\n• Delta (from delta/bronze/raw_orders metadata columns added CSV/JSON/Parquet) Silver (Cleansed) Validated, deduplicated, type-cast, Delta delta/silver/orders_clean joined data Gold (Curated) Business-level aggregations, KPIs, Delta or Parquet delta/gold/daily_sales reporting tables # Write transformed Silver data to Delta table silver_df.write .format('delta') .mode('overwrite') # or 'append' / 'merge' for incremental .option('mergeSchema','true') .partitionBy('year','month') .saveAsTable('silver.orders_clean') # Or write to ADLS path silver_df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders/') Always store transformed data as Delta format — it gives ACID transactions, time travel, schema evolution, and efficient MERGE operations for incremental updates","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Architecture in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks follows the Lakehouse Architecture — combining the scalability of a Data Lake with the governance and performance of a Data Warehouse\n• Medallion Architecture (Most Common) Data flows through three progressive layers — each improving quality and structure: Layer Also Called Purpose Format Bronze Raw Layer Ingest raw data as-is from source Delta / Parquet / JSON / — no transformations CSV Silver Cleansed Layer Clean, deduplicate, validate, join Delta Table — trusted, queryable data Gold Aggregated / Business-level aggregations, Delta Table Business Layer KPIs, reporting-ready data Core Databricks Architecture Components Component Description Control Plane Managed by Databricks (Azure/AWS/GCP) — stores metadata, notebooks, job configs, UI Data Plane Customer's cloud account — where Spark clusters run and data is stored in ADLS/S3 Clusters Spark compute nodes — All-Purpose (interactive) or Job Clusters (automated pipelines) DBFS Databricks File System — abstraction over cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) Unity Catalog Unified governance layer — manages tables, schemas, data lineage, access control Delta Lake Storage layer for all tables — provides ACID, versioning, schema enforcement MLflow Built-in ML lifecycle management — experiment tracking, model registry, deployment Workflows / Jobs Orchestration layer — schedule notebooks, DLT pipelines, dbt, Python scripts Autoloader Incremental file ingestion from cloud storage into Delta tables Delta Live Tables Declarative pipeline framework with built-in data quality and monitoring Data Flow in the Architecture • Sources (SQL Server, APIs, flat files, IoT) land raw data in ADLS/S3 Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 • Bronze layer ingests via Autoloader or COPY INTO — raw Delta tables • Silver layer cleanses, deduplicates, and enriches with business rules • Gold layer aggregates for BI tools (Power BI, Tableau) or ML models • Unity Catalog governs access and lineage across all three layers Page 5 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You mentioned in your resume that you worked on a migration project. Explain","type":"concept","a":"• about it\n• In the migration project, we moved our existing on-premises data warehouse (e.g., SQL Server / Oracle) to a cloud-based Lakehouse architecture on Databricks / Azure Synapse\n• Key Phases of the Migration: • Assessment & Planning: Audited existing tables, stored procedures, ETL jobs, and data volumes\n• Identified dependencies and prioritized migration order. • Architecture Design: Designed a Medallion Architecture (Bronze → Silver → Gold) on Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS Gen2) with Delta Lake format. • Data Extraction: Extracted data from the source (on-prem DB) using JDBC connectors or flat file exports. • Transformation & Load: Rewrote SQL-based transformations in PySpark\n• Applied business logic, cleansing, deduplication, and type casting. • Validation: Compared row counts, aggregates, and key metrics between source and target to ensure data integrity. • Cutover: Ran parallel loads, froze the source, did final delta sync, and switched downstream consumers to the new platform","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Project Architecture (End-to-End)","type":"concept","a":"We used Azure + Databricks Medallion Architecture:  Sources: SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake  ADF: Data inges(cid:415)on + orchestra(cid:415)on  ADLS Gen2: Storage layer  Databricks: o Bronze → Raw inges(cid:415)on o Silver → Cleaned + transformed o Gold → Business-ready data  Consump(cid:415)on: Power BI / SQL Warehouse","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How many layers you will be having in your databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Layers in Databricks  Bronze → Raw data  Silver → Cleaned + validated data  Gold → Aggregated business data (Some systems also use Pla(cid:415)num for ML)","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your current data pipeline architecture tools, flow, and data volume.","type":"concept","a":"• Our source systems include SQL Database, REST APIs, and CSV files uploaded to Azure Blob Storage\n• Azure Data Factory orchestrates data ingestion into ADLS Gen2 Bronze storage\n• Azure Databricks performs cleansing, deduplication, joins, aggregations, and business transformations using PySpark\n• Processed data is stored in Delta Lake Silver and Gold layers\n• Power BI consumes the Gold layer for reporting\n• Azure Monitor and Log Analytics are used for monitoring, while Git-based CI/CD manages deployments\n• On average, we process around 80–150 GB of data daily, which translates to several million records depending on the source","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain about yourself.","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Answer (2-3 minutes) \"Hi, I'm a Data Engineer with around X years of experience in designing and developing ETL/ELT data pipelines using Azure Data Factory, Azure Databricks, PySpark, SQL, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on building scalable data inges(cid:415)on frameworks where data is collected from mul(cid:415)ple sources such as SQL databases, APIs, and cloud storage into Azure Data Lake\n• We follow the Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• ADF is mainly used for orchestra(cid:415)on, Databricks performs data transforma(cid:415)on, and Delta Lake stores processed data\n• My responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing ADF pipelines  Wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Data valida(cid:415)on  Incremental loading  Performance tuning  Pipeline monitoring and troubleshoo(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the data framework that you are working on.","type":"concept","a":"A typical architecture: Source Systems (SQL, APIs, CSV, SAP) ↓ Azure Data Factory (Orchestra(cid:415)on) ↓ Azure Data Lake Gen2 ↓ Bronze Layer (Raw Delta Tables) ↓ Databricks ↓ Silver Layer (Cleansed + Standardized) ↓ Gold Layer (Business Aggrega(cid:415)ons) ↓ Power BI / Synapse Explain each layer: Bronze  Raw data  No transforma(cid:415)ons  Append only  Delta format Silver  Data cleaning  Remove duplicates  Null handling  Data type conversion  Standardiza(cid:415)on Gold  Business-ready data  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  KPI tables  Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Delta Live Tables?","type":"concept","a":"• DLT is a Databricks framework used for building reliable ETL pipelines\n• Features:  Automa(cid:415)c dependency management  Incremental processing  Data quality expecta(cid:415)ons  Auto retries  Pipeline monitoring  Automa(cid:415)c lineage  Schema evolu(cid:415)on Example: Bronze ↓ Silver ↓ Gold No need to manually manage execu(cid:415)on order","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How is data stored in Bronze?","type":"concept","a":"Usually:  Delta format  Append only  Raw  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oned (op(cid:415)onal)  Metadata columns Example: customer.csv ↓ Bronze customer_id name city load_date inges(cid:415)on_(cid:415)mestamp source_file No business transforma(cid:415)ons.","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Project Explanation","type":"concept","a":"• Use the STAR framework: Situation, Task, Action, Result\n• Always quantify outcomes. • Situation: Organization needed to process 10TB+ daily transaction data from Kafka, S3, and REST APIs for fraud detection. • Task: Design and implement an end-to-end pipeline using Databricks and Delta Lake, replacing legacy batch jobs. • Action: Built Bronze/Silver/Gold medallion architecture\n• Used Auto Loader for incremental ingestion, Structured Streaming for near-real-time processing, Z-ordering for query optimization, and Delta constraints for data quality. • Result: Reduced data latency from 6 hours to 15 minutes\n• Query performance improved by 70%\n• Storage costs reduced by 40% via Delta compaction\n• Page 1 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How many Delta tables are there in Silver and Gold layers?","type":"concept","a":"• \"In my project, each curated dataset is stored as a separate Delta table\n• The exact number depends on the project\n• For example, we maintain around 20–30 Delta tables in the Silver layer and 10–15 aggregated/business tables in the Gold layer\n• The key point is that each business en(cid:415)ty or repor(cid:415)ng dataset is maintained as its own Delta table.\" (Replace the numbers with your project's actual values if known.)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Notebooks Does a Data Engineer Typically Deal With in a Year?","type":"concept","a":"• This is experience-based\n• Tailor your answer to your actual project, but a typical range: • Small project: 10-20 notebooks per year Page 1 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Mid-size project: 25-50 notebooks (Bronze/Silver/Gold layers, 3-10 notebooks per domain per layer) • Large enterprise: 50-100+ notebooks organized by domain (Finance, Orders, Customers, etc.) Sample Answer: \"In my current project I work with approximately 30-40 notebooks organized by layer\n• We have Bronze ingestion notebooks, Silver cleansing/transformation notebooks, Gold aggregation notebooks for reporting, plus shared utility notebooks for config and common functions.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Daily Volume of Data You Process?","type":"concept","a":"• Scale Volume/Day Typical Cluster Small Less than 10 GB 4-8 cores, 16-32 GB RAM Medium 10 GB - 1 TB 16-64 cores, 128-256 GB RAM Large 1 TB - 100 TB 100+ cores, 500 GB+ RAM, Auto-scaling Enterprise 100 TB+ Hundreds of nodes, streaming pipelines Sample Answer: \"We process approximately 500 GB to 2 TB of raw data daily from 15 upstream systems\n• After transformation the Silver/Gold layers are 200-500 GB\n• Peak loads during month-end hit 5 TB.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What does the current data architecture look like — Medallion/Lakehouse or more traditional ETL?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What do you do in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":" Develop PySpark notebooks  Build ETL pipelines (bronze → silver → gold)  Op(cid:415)mize performance (par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching)  Handle incremental loads (Delta Lake)  Create jobs/workflows  Work with Delta tables","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project from source to repor(cid:415)ng?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I am working on an Employee Analy(cid:415)cs project using Azure Data Engineering services\n• The source systems include SQL Server databases, REST APIs, and CSV files from SFTP\n• Azure Data Factory (ADF) orchestrates the data inges(cid:415)on process\n• The raw data is stored in Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 (Bronze layer)\n• Azure Databricks processes the data using PySpark, where we perform data valida(cid:415)on, duplicate removal, null handling, joins, aggrega(cid:415)ons, and business transforma(cid:415)ons\n• The transformed data is stored in Delta tables in the Silver and Gold layers\n• The Gold layer is consumed by Power BI for dashboards and business repor(cid:415)ng\n• I am responsible for developing ADF pipelines, wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loads, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, monitoring produc(cid:415)on pipelines, and resolving produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Medallion Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold) is a data design pattern that organizes data into progressive quality layers in a Data Lakehouse\n• Bronze Layer (Raw): • Exact copy of source data, minimal transformation • All data lands here (append-only), serves as audit trail • Format: Delta or Parquet Silver Layer (Cleaned/Conformed): • Data is cleaned, deduplicated, validated • Joins applied, schema enforced • Ready for data science and analytics use cases Gold Layer (Business-level Aggregates): • Aggregated, enriched, business-ready data • Star schema models, KPIs, dashboards • Optimized for reporting tools (Power BI, Tableau) # Bronze: Raw ingest df_raw.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/bronze/orders') # Silver: Clean and deduplicate df_silver = df_raw.dropDuplicates(['order_id']).filter(col('amount') > 0) df_silver.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/silver/orders') # Gold: Aggregate for reporting df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('region').agg(sum('amount').alias('total_revenue')) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/gold/revenue_by_region') SECTION 5: Spark Internals & Optimization","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Medallian Architecture ?","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture is a layered data design pa(cid:425)ern used in data lakes where data is organized into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers\n• Bronze stores raw ingested data, Silver contains cleaned and standardized data, and Gold contains aggregated business-ready data for repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs\n• This approach improves data quality, maintainability, and scalability of pipelines","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Why Medallion Architecture?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:33158)(cid:57393) Benefits:  Be(cid:425)er data quality  Easy debugging (trace back to raw data)  Reusable datasets  Scalable pipeline design  Supports incremental processing","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you move on prem data to fabric to delta lake house ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move on-prem data to Microso(cid:332) Fabric Lakehouse, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to connect the source systems securely\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines in Fabric to ingest the data into OneLake in Delta format\n• The data is stored in Bronze layer as raw data, then transformed into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark or CDC-based extrac(cid:415)on to ensure efficiency","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Oracle to fabric ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move data from Oracle to Microso(cid:332) Fabric, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to establish a secure connec(cid:415)on if Oracle is on-prem\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines to extract data from Oracle using SQL queries\n• The data is loaded into OneLake Lakehouse in Delta format, typically in the Bronze layer\n• Later, I transform it into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark columns or Oracle CDC to ensure efficient data movement","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Which layer data is ready to use for presenta(cid:415)on purpose","type":"concept","a":"Gold layer is the final curated layer in Medallion Architecture that is op(cid:415)mized and structured for business repor(cid:415)ng and visualiza(cid:415)on tools.","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"As a team lead you have 300 tables in Oracle database, what will be your approach,","type":"concept","a":"• explain your flow\n• If I have 300 tables in Oracle, I will avoid building individual pipelines\n• Instead, I would design a metadata-driven inges(cid:415)on framework where table details like load type, watermark column, and query are stored in a control table\n• I would create a single dynamic ADF or Fabric pipeline that reads metadata and processes all tables using a ForEach loop\n• Data would be ingested into the Bronze layer, then transformed into silver and gold layers using Spark or SQL\n• I would also implement incremental load using watermark or CDC, enable parallel processing for performance, and add monitoring for failure tracking and retries\n• Data skew Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons in distributed systems like Spark, causing some tasks to take significantly longer than others\n• It usually happens during joins, groupBy, or shuffle opera(cid:415)ons\n• To resolve it, I use techniques like broadcast join for small tables, sal(cid:415)ng for skewed keys, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data, enabling Spark adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on, and pre-aggrega(cid:415)ng data before joins","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"cicd","name":"CI/CD, Orchestration & Production","icon":"🚀","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"How do you implement CI/CD for Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Pipeline (Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions):\n• Source control — notebooks/code in Git (Azure Repos/GitHub) via Databricks Repos/Git folders.\n• CI — run unit tests (pytest/nutter), lint, build artifacts (wheel/JAR).\n• CD — deploy notebooks/jobs via Databricks CLI/REST API or databricks bundle deploy (Asset Bundles); promote DEV→QA→PROD.\n• Use service principals for deployment auth; store secrets in Key Vault."},{"q":"What are Databricks Asset Bundles (DAB)?","type":"concept","a":"DAB is an infrastructure-as-code way to define Databricks resources (jobs, pipelines, notebooks, clusters, permissions) in a databricks.yml bundle.\n• Supports multiple targets (dev/qa/prod) and parameterization.\n• Deploy with: databricks bundle deploy --target prod; run with databricks bundle run.\n• Enables version-controlled, repeatable deployments across environments."},{"q":"How do you orchestrate/schedule Databricks notebooks?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Workflows (Jobs) — native; multi-task jobs with dependencies, retries, alerting, repair-and-rerun; best for production.\n• ADF — pipeline with Databricks Notebook activities chained on success; best when orchestrating across Azure services (ADB+SQL+Functions).\n• dbutils.notebook.run() — simple chaining from a master notebook (pass args, get exit value).\nRecommendation: use Workflows for production scheduling; ADF for cross-service orchestration."},{"q":"Difference between %run and dbutils.notebook.run()?","type":"concept","a":"• %run — runs another notebook INLINE, sharing scope/variables/functions; no parameters (uses widget defaults); no return value. Use to share utility functions.\n• dbutils.notebook.run() — runs in an ISOLATED context; pass a dict of parameters; returns a string via dbutils.notebook.exit(); has a timeout. Use to orchestrate sequential notebooks and pass/return data."},{"q":"How do you promote code across Dev → QA → Prod in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Separate workspaces (or catalogs/schemas) per environment attached to the same UC metastore.\n• Parameterize storage paths, catalog/schema names, and cluster configs per environment.\n• Use Git + CI/CD (Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions) with service principals to deploy bundles/jobs.\n• Environment-specific config files (databricks.yml targets) or ADF global parameters/ARM params.\n• Run tests in QA before promoting to Prod; keep secrets in Key Vault per environment."},{"q":"For Dev/Test/Prod, should you use one metastore or multiple?","type":"concept","a":"Typical: ONE metastore per region, shared across Dev/Test/Prod workspaces; isolate environments using separate CATALOGS (dev_catalog, test_catalog, prod_catalog), schemas, and permissions.\nUse SEPARATE metastores only for strong isolation: different regions, legal entities, compliance boundaries, or separate organizations."},{"q":"How do you optimize cost in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Job clusters (auto-terminate) for production; avoid idle All-Purpose clusters.\n• Enable autoscaling; set auto-termination aggressively.\n• Use instance pools and Spot instances where appropriate.\n• Serverless/SQL Warehouse with auto-suspend for BI.\n• Enable Photon + AQE + predictive optimization to reduce run time.\n• OPTIMIZE/VACUUM to reduce storage/scan; delete temp tables; right-size clusters.\n• Use cluster policies to cap sizes/costs; monitor DBU usage."},{"q":"What are cluster policies?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster policies are admin-defined rules that constrain what cluster configurations users can create (max/min workers, VM types, allowed options, auto-terminate, tags).\nBenefits: cost control, governance, standardization, and self-service within limits. Enforced via Unity Catalog/workspace admin settings."},{"q":"How do you monitor production pipelines and handle failures?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Workflows — run history, task-level logs, notifications/alerts on success/failure/duration; repair-and-rerun for failed tasks.\n• ADF — Monitor tab, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics + KQL, alerts via action groups.\n• Audit tables — track load history/watermarks for traceability.\n• DQ checks — fail fast on critical violations, alert the team.\n• Foundation: idempotent MERGE + checkpoints so retries/recovery are safe."},{"q":"How do you parameterize pipelines for reuse across environments?","type":"concept","a":"• ADF: pipeline parameters, variables, global parameters, parameterized datasets/linked services, ARM template overrides per environment.\n• Databricks: widgets (dbutils.widgets.get), job/task parameters, DAB targets, and config files (paths, catalog/schema) per env.\n• Keep environment-specific values (paths, secrets, hosts) OUT of code; inject at deploy/runtime."},{"q":"Explain your CI/CD experience.","type":"concept","a":"• Typical Answer: We use Azure DevOps\n• Flow: Developer ↓ Git Branch ↓ Pull Request ↓ Build Pipeline ↓ Release Pipeline ↓ Dev ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on We deploy:  Notebooks  ADF Pipelines  ARM Templates  Libraries  Config files Tools:  Azure Repos  Azure DevOps  Git  YAML Pipelines","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How to deploy pipelines","type":"concept","a":"➢ Create a pipeline and select the ASP.NET Core template. ➢ Save the pipeline and queue a build to see it in action. ➢ Create a release pipeline and select the Azure App Service Deployment template for your stage. ➢ Link the build pipeline as an artifact for this release pipeline.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of deployment modes available?","type":"concept","a":"• Pubic 2\n• Private 3\n• Hybrid","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"V1 doesn't contain azure devops git repository integration.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"V1 doesn't contains CICD methodology of deployment","type":"concept","a":"======================SQL QUESTIONS===================","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"System Functions:","type":"concept","a":"--SYSTEM FUNCTIONS SELECT * FROM Products SELECT SUM(UnitPrice) AS TotalPrice FROM Products SELECT COUNT(UnitPrice) AS Total FROM Products SELECT MAX(UnitPrice) AS Maximum FROM Products SELECT MIN(UnitPrice) AS Minimum FROM Products SELECT AVG(UnitPrice) AS Average FROM Products -- STRING FUNCTIONS SELECT * FROM Customers SELECT UPPER(ContactName) as ContactName FROM Customers--uppercase letter SELECT LOWER(ContactName) as ContactName FROM Customers--lower case letter SELECT LEFT(ContactName,5) as ContactName FROM Customers-- it will give first five letter SELECT RIGHT(ContactName,4) as ContactName FROM Customers--it will give last four letter SELECT SUBSTRING('WELCOME',4,4)-- it will start from 4 letter SELECT REPLACE('Ahmedabad','Ahmed','Allah') as Result-- it will replace the letters SELECT LEN('ELEPHANT') AS Result-- it will count the letters SELECT LTRIM(' WELCOME')--it will erase the space from the left SELECT RTRIM('WELCOME ')--it will erase the space from the right SELECT TRIM(' WELCOME ')--it will erase the space SELECT REPLICATE('INDIA ',5)-- it will repate the words SELECT REVERSE('WELCOME')--it will reverse the words -- NUMERIC FUNCTIONS SELECT ABS(-20)-- it will convert the -ve to +ve SELECT ABS(20) SELECT SQRT(25)-- square root of the value SELECT FLOOR(10.8756)-- it will round figure in a lower value SELECT CEILING(10.8756)--it will round figure in a upper value Page | 43 SELECT ROUND(10.8756,2)--it will round the value given operation -- DATE FUNCTIONS SELECT GETDATE() SELECT YEAR(GETDATE()) SELECT MONTH(GETDATE()) SELECT DAY(GETDATE()) SELECT DATENAME(MONTH,GETDATE()) SELECT DATENAME(DW,GETDATE())--date name SELECT DATEPART(HOUR,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(MINUTE,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(SECOND,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(DAY,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(MONTH,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(YEAR,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEADD(DAY,1,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEADD(MONTH,2,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEADD(YEAR,5,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEDIFF(DAY,GETDATE(),'2021-12-24') SELECT DATEDIFF(DAY,'2021-12-24',GETDATE()) SELECT GETUTCDATE()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"DevOps & Version Control in Azure Data Projects","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"What is DevOps in a Data Engineering context?","type":"concept","a":"• DevOps brings together development (writing pipelines/code) and operations (deploying, monitoring, maintaining) to deliver data solutions faster and more reliably\n• Version Control with Git (Azure DevOps / GitHub) Every ADF pipeline, Databricks notebook, and configuration file is stored in a Git repository\n• This means: • Full history of every change made • Ability to roll back to a previous working version • Multiple team members can work in parallel using branches Branching Strategy — Simple Example A typical project uses 3 branches: Branch Purpose Example main Production-ready code only Live pipelines running in prod develop Integration / testing All devs merge their work here first feature/xyz Individual feature work feature/add-sales-pipeline CI/CD Pipeline Example When a developer pushes code to Git, the CI/CD pipeline automatically:","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Deploys to DEV environment automatically","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"After approval, deploys to UAT and then PROD","type":"concept","a":"• Example Azure DevOps YAML snippet: # azure-pipelines.yml trigger: branches: include: [develop, main] stages: - stage: Deploy_Dev jobs: - job: DeployADF steps: - task: AzureResourceManagerTemplateDeployment@3 inputs: resourceGroupName: 'rg-dev' templateLocation: 'Linked artifact' csmFile: 'adf_publish/ARMTemplateForFactory.json' 💡 Tip: In interviews, say: 'We used Azure DevOps Git integration with ADF\n• Every pipeline change was committed, reviewed via Pull Request, and deployed automatically through our CI/CD pipeline.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Implement prevention: add retry policies, better alerts, data validation","type":"concept","a":"• 💡 Tip: Interview answer example: 'At 2 AM I first checked ADF Monitor to find the failed activity and read the error\n• It turned out the source SQL database had a schema change — a column was renamed\n• I updated the column mapping in ADF, redeployed, ran the pipeline manually, verified the row counts matched, and sent an update to the team\n• We then added a schema validation check to catch this earlier in future.' Quick Reference Cheat Sheet Key phrases to use in interviews: • DevOps: 'We used Git integration with ADF, feature branches, Pull Requests, and Azure DevOps CI/CD pipelines for automated deployment.' • Data Flow: 'I built a mapping data flow with Source → Join → Aggregate → Sink, using Debug Mode to test transformations before deploying.' • Late Arriving Data: 'We used a grace period watermark combined with MERGE/Upsert to handle late records without duplicates.' • Alerts: 'We set up Azure Monitor alert rules for pipeline failures and Databricks job notifications, both routing to email and Teams.' • Child Pipelines: 'We parameterized child pipelines and passed load_date and environment from the parent using Execute Pipeline activity.' • Production Incident: 'I follow a structured approach: detect, diagnose, fix, verify, communicate, and prevent recurrence.' Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 10","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Deploy through CI/CD.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Monitor a(cid:332)er deployment.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you know fix works?","type":"concept","a":" Unit tes(cid:415)ng  Regression tes(cid:415)ng  Compare outputs  Validate with business users  Monitor produc(cid:415)on a(cid:332)er deployment","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are Dev and Produc(cid:415)on synchronized?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes, through Azure DevOps CI/CD pipelines\n• Code is version-controlled in Git, and deployments promote tested changes from Dev to QA/UAT and finally Produc(cid:415)on\n• DevOps Tool  Azure DevOps  Git  YAML Pipelines  ARM/Bicep/Terraform (if used)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"QA/UAT environment","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Typical environments: Dev ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on 45\n• Deploy code Typical CI/CD flow: Developer ↓ Git Commit ↓ Pull Request ↓ Build Pipeline ↓ Release Pipeline ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Which CI/CD you will be using. What is DAB?","type":"concept","a":"CI/CD + DAB CI/CD tools:  Azure DevOps / GitHub Ac(cid:415)ons DAB:  Databricks Asset Bundles  Used for deploying notebooks, jobs, pipelines","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If it fails, how do you debug and resolve it?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• A typical Databricks CI/CD pipeline (Azure DevOps or GitHub Actions): Pipeline stages: • 1\n• Source Control (Git) — Databricks Repos linked to feature branches. • 2\n• CI — Run unit tests (pytest / nutter), lint, notebook validation on Pull Request. • 3\n• CD to Dev — On merge to dev branch, deploy notebooks/jobs via Databricks CLI or REST API. • 4\n• CD to Staging/Prod — Triggered after approval gate; use environment-specific variable groups / Key Vault references for secrets\n• Debugging a failed pipeline: • Check the pipeline run logs in Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions for the exact failing step. • For Databricks job failures: open the job run URL in the logs, examine cluster logs, driver stderr/stdout, and notebook error output. • Common causes: wrong cluster config, missing library, permission issues (IAM/RBAC), bad secret reference, schema mismatch. • Use dbutils.notebook.run() with a timeout and catch exceptions to surface notebook errors in the pipeline. • Re-run the failed stage after fix; use pipeline variables to control which stage to re-execute without rerunning everything. — End of Document —","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Produc(cid:415)on issue you fixed","type":"concept","a":"• Example: \"A pipeline failed because the source schema changed and a new column was added\n• I analyzed the logs in ADF and Databricks, updated the schema handling in PySpark, validated the output in Dev, deployed through Azure DevOps, and monitored the next produc(cid:415)on run.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the CI/CD process in your company.","type":"concept","a":"• \"We use Azure DevOps for CI/CD\n• Developers commit code to Git repositories using feature branches\n• Pull requests are reviewed before merging into the main branch\n• The CI pipeline validates the code and packages the deployment ar(cid:415)facts\n• The CD pipeline deploys Azure Data Factory pipelines, Databricks notebooks, and related resources to Development, QA, and Produc(cid:415)on environments using release pipelines or YAML-based pipelines\n• Parameters and environment-specific configura(cid:415)ons are managed separately to ensure smooth deployments across environments.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"CI/CD Deployment in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• CI/CD Pipeline (e.g., Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions): • 1\n• Source Control: Notebooks/code in Git (Azure Repos / GitHub) • 2\n• CI (Continuous Integration): ◦ Run unit tests (pytest, nutter) ◦ Lint checks, code quality ◦ Build artifacts (wheel files, JARs) • 3\n• CD (Continuous Deployment): ◦ Deploy notebooks via Databricks CLI or REST API ◦ Deploy jobs using databricks bundle deploy ◦ Environment promotion: DEV → QA → PROD Databricks Asset Bundles (DAB) — Modern Approach: # databricks.yml bundle: name: my_pipeline targets: dev: { workspace: { host: https://dev.azuredatabricks.net } } prod: { workspace: { host: https://prod.azuredatabricks.net } } resources: jobs: my_job: { name: 'ETL Job', tasks: [...] } # Deploy commands databricks bundle deploy --target dev databricks bundle run --target dev my_job","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"fabric","name":"Microsoft Fabric","icon":"🧵","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Microsoft Fabric?","type":"concept","a":"Microsoft Fabric is an end-to-end, SaaS-based analytics platform that unifies data engineering, data science, real-time analytics, data warehousing, and BI (Power BI) in one experience.\n• Built on OneLake (single, unified lake) using Delta Parquet.\n• Integrates Power BI, Azure Synapse, and Data Factory.\n• Capacity-based SaaS: no infrastructure to manage."},{"q":"How does Microsoft Fabric differ from Azure Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"• Architecture: Fabric is SaaS (unified, one platform); Synapse is PaaS (manual provisioning of pools/workspaces).\n• Storage: Fabric uses OneLake (unified lakehouse); Synapse uses dedicated SQL pools + data lakes (separate).\n• Compute/pricing: Fabric is capacity-based (F SKUs); Synapse is pay-per-query/provisioned.\n• Integration: Fabric natively integrates Power BI/Azure AI; Synapse needs connectors.\nFabric is the newer consolidated experience."},{"q":"What are the core components/workloads of Fabric?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Factory — data integration/pipelines.\n• Synapse Data Engineering — Spark-based lakehouse, notebooks.\n• Synapse Data Science — ML/AI workloads.\n• Synapse Data Warehousing — managed T-SQL warehouse.\n• Real-Time Analytics — Kusto-based streaming/event processing.\n• Power BI — visualization/reporting.\n• OneLake — unified storage underpinning everything."},{"q":"What is OneLake?","type":"concept","a":"OneLake is the single, unified, logically-centralized storage layer for all Fabric workloads (like OneDrive for data).\n• Stores data in open Delta Parquet format.\n• One copy of data, accessed by all engines (Spark, SQL, KQL, Power BI) — no duplication/silos.\n• Supports shortcuts to external storage (ADLS/S3) without copying."},{"q":"What is Direct Lake mode in Power BI/Fabric?","type":"concept","a":"Direct Lake lets Power BI read Delta tables in OneLake directly without importing/caching data, giving near import-level performance with real-time freshness.\n• No data duplication; no scheduled refresh needed for base data.\n• Compare: Import mode (copies/caches data, fast but stale), DirectQuery (queries source live, slower, no cache).\nDirect Lake blends the speed of Import with the freshness of DirectQuery."},{"q":"How does Fabric handle security and governance?","type":"concept","a":"• Authentication: Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD).\n• Authorization: RBAC at workspace/item level; Row-Level Security (RLS) and Object-Level Security (OLS) in the semantic model/warehouse.\n• Governance: Microsoft Purview for discovery, cataloging, lineage, classification, and policy enforcement across Fabric.\n• Sensitivity labels/endorsements for items."},{"q":"Describe the Fabric capacity/licensing model and SKUs.","type":"concept","a":"Fabric uses capacity-based licensing: you buy Fabric Capacity Units (CUs) as an F-SKU (F2, F4, F8, ... higher = more compute/storage).\n• Capacity provides fixed compute + storage quotas shared across workloads.\n• Workloads consume the capacity; you can pause/resume to save cost.\n• Premium/Embedded P/EM SKUs can also power Fabric features.\nContrast with per-service/per-query pricing."},{"q":"How does Fabric support real-time analytics?","type":"concept","a":"Fabric Real-Time Analytics (based on the Kusto engine) ingests and processes high-throughput streaming data (Eventstreams/Event Hubs/Kafka) and provides low-latency KQL queries and real-time dashboards.\n• Use Eventstream for no-code ingestion/transformation.\n• KQL databases for fast time-series/event queries.\n• Integrates with Power BI for live dashboards."},{"q":"How does Fabric support ETL/ELT?","type":"concept","a":"• Dataflows Gen2 (Power Query) for low-code ELT.\n• Data Factory pipelines (copy activities + orchestration) for ETL/ELT.\n• Spark notebooks (Synapse Data Engineering) for code-based transforms.\n• Lakehouse/warehouse shortcuts and ingestion for ELT into OneLake.\nYou can mix low-code and code."},{"q":"SCENARIO: How would you migrate an existing data warehouse to Fabric?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Export/convert existing data to Delta Lake format.\n2. Load into OneLake (Lakehouse or Warehouse).\n3. Rework queries for the Fabric Warehouse / Direct Lake mode for performance.\n4. Rebuild BI reports in Power BI using Direct Lake.\n5. Apply governance (Purview), RLS/OLS, and workspace RBAC; validate and cut over.\nConsider shortcuts for existing ADLS data to avoid copying."}]},{"id":"scenario","name":"Scenario-Based & Troubleshooting","icon":"🧩","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"SCENARIO: A pipeline that usually completes in 10 hours is still running into the next day. How do you troubleshoot?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Check the ADF/Databricks Monitor + Spark UI — identify which stage/activity is slow.\n2. Spark UI: look for data skew (one task far longer), large shuffle read/write, spill to disk, uneven partitions.\n3. Compare current vs normal run: input volume growth, small-file explosion, lost broadcast (join strategy change), schema changes.\n4. Check cluster health: resource contention (shared cluster), executor loss/GC, autoscaling limits, pool starvation.\n5. Check upstream source latency/backlog and IR throughput.\nRemedies: AQE/skew handling, salt skewed keys, broadcast small tables, repartition, OPTIMIZE small files, scale cluster, fix bad join, restore filters/column pruning."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A notebook that completes in 15 minutes took 50 minutes today. How do you find the root cause?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the run in Spark UI; find the slow stage and compare task durations → look for skew/spill.\n• Check executor memory/GC and shuffle metrics; look for OOM-ish behavior or spilling.\n• Check cluster: shared usage/contention, autoscaling didn't scale, fewer/larger tasks.\n• Check data: more files/small files, larger volume, changed partitioning, source lag.\n• Check code changes: new join, UDF, missing filter, cartesian, broadcast removed.\n• Fix: repartition/broadcast, enable AQE/Photon, OPTIMIZE, cache reused DataFrames, scale cluster."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Databricks pipeline suddenly fails. What steps do you take to identify and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Open the job run → failed task → read the error/log (driver + executor logs).\n2. Classify: data-related (schema drift, new nulls/bad records), infra (cluster/OOM/network), dependency (upstream table missing), or code (bug).\n3. Reproduce on a small sample; check recent code or source changes (git diff).\n4. Verify upstream data availability, credentials/Key Vault, and table/permission changes.\n5. Apply fix, use repair-and-rerun (only failed tasks) for idempotent pipelines.\n6. Add guardrails: DQ checks, retries, alerts, schema drift handling."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Source data changed structurally (new column added / datatype changed). How do you detect and handle it without breaking pipelines?","type":"scenario","a":"Detect: schema validation step in the pipeline (expected vs actual), or Auto Loader schema evolution / rescued data column.\nHandle:\n• ADF: allow schema drift; parameterize mapping.\n• Ingest raw into Bronze as schema-flexible; add new columns automatically (mergeSchema / autoMerge).\n• For datatype change: cast/map in Silver, keep raw value in Bronze, quarantine incompatible rows.\n• Alert on drift and keep downstream consumers informed; evolve Delta schema explicitly (ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN).\nDesign principle: raw layer tolerant, curated layers strict."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You notice data skew in a large join. How do you confirm and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"Confirm: Spark UI shows a few tasks with much larger shuffle read and longer duration; one executor overloaded.\nFix:\n• Enable AQE skew join (auto-splits skewed partitions).\n• Salt the skewed key (add random suffix), join, then aggregate.\n• Broadcast the smaller table if it fits.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key or by range.\n• Filter reduce before the join; isolate the hot key and process separately."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Delta table has thousands of small files and reads are slow. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Run OPTIMIZE to compact into larger (~1 GB) files; schedule it after loads.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) to prevent small files going forward.\n• Use availableNow trigger / larger micro-batches in streaming or foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Adopt Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy; coalesce before writes.\n• Set a target file size and monitor file counts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Duplicate records are appearing in your Silver table. How do you fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Deduplicate on business key + recency: ROW_NUMBER() over key ordered by load_ts desc, keep rn=1.\n• Use dropDuplicates on the natural key.\n• For incremental loads, MERGE on the key (whenMatchedUpdate) so re-runs don't insert duplicates.\n• Add a DQ check that fails/alerts if duplicates exceed a threshold.\n• Identify the source (idempotency, retries, CDC reprocessing) and fix upstream."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to process millions/billions of records. What's your approach?","type":"scenario","a":"• Store in columnar Delta/Parquet with partitioning/Clustering; avoid CSV/JSON for large scale.\n• Define explicit schemas; filter/aggregate early; select only needed columns.\n• Use broadcast joins for small dims; AQE + skew handling; right-size shuffle partitions.\n• Use Photon; cache reused DataFrames; avoid UDFs.\n• Scale the cluster (workers/VM type) and use autoscaling; use Job clusters.\n• Incremental processing (watermark/MERGE) to avoid full reprocessing."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A memory/OOM error occurs during a large join/aggregation. How do you resolve it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Reduce data early (filter, select needed columns, aggregate before join).\n• Broadcast the small side instead of shuffling the huge side.\n• Increase shuffle partitions to make tasks smaller; use AQE.\n• Fix skew (salting/AQE) so one task doesn't blow up.\n• Use bigger memory VMs / more nodes; avoid collect()/large driver operations.\n• Use persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK) instead of MEMORY_ONLY if needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Compare these two queries and explain when to use each. Q1: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table WHERE salary>5000 GROUP BY emp_id; Q2: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table GROUP BY emp_id HAVING SUM(salary)>5000;","type":"scenario","a":"• Q1 (WHERE) filters individual ROWS before aggregation — only salaries > 5000 are summed per employee.\n• Q2 (HAVING) aggregates ALL rows, then keeps employees whose TOTAL salary > 5000.\nThey give different results. Use WHERE to filter rows cheaply before grouping; use HAVING to filter on aggregate results. You can combine both."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Design a POD-level ADF pipeline that feeds a Databricks job. How do you configure Linked Services, Datasets, and pipeline properties?","type":"scenario","a":"• Linked Services: ADLS (Azure IR), Databricks workspace (with access token from Key Vault), source DB (SHIR if on-prem).\n• Datasets: parameterized source/sink datasets (schema/table/path) so one pipeline handles many tables.\n• Pipeline: Get Metadata/Lookup → ForEach (metadata-driven) → Databricks Notebook activity; pass parameters (run_date, table).\n• Properties: set ForEach degree of parallelism, Copy DIUs, batch count for data flows, retry + timeout, and Tumbling Window trigger.\n• Balance throughput vs cost: use Job clusters, avoid over-parallelizing the source, scale SHIR/DIUs as needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A production job reading secrets from Key Vault starts failing intermittently. How do you diagnose it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Check Key Vault access: is the secret still present/permitted; is the managed identity/SPN expired or the secret rotated?\n• Check the Integration Runtime: is the SHIR online, throttled, or hitting network/timeouts?\n• Check pipeline config: correct secret scope/key, correct vault URI, no hard-coded expiry.\n• Look at error messages (AAD auth vs 403 vs timeout) and correlate with time of day (throttling).\n• Fix: renew/rotate credentials, add retry with backoff, monitor Key Vault diagnostics, add alerts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks cluster fails to start due to resource limitations. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Free up resources: terminate idle clusters and release the quota/cores.\n• Request a quota increase (vCPU/cores) in the subscription.\n• Reduce min workers/VM size, or use instance pools / serverless.\n• Check for subnet/network (no available IPs) or policy restrictions.\n• Retry after cleanup; use cluster policies to prevent oversized requests."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You must do real-time analysis on a streaming dataset in Databricks. How?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read with Spark Structured Streaming from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse JSON (from_json), apply watermark + windowed aggregation, dedupe.\n• Write to a Delta table with checkpointing (exactly-once); use update/append mode.\n• Optionally use DLT streaming tables for managed reliability + expectations.\n• Serve to Power BI/Real-Time Analytics; monitor lag and checkpoint health."},{"q":"What are the common challenges you've faced in pipelines (and fixes)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Data skew → AQE/salting/broadcast.\n• Schema drift → mergeSchema/schema drift/Auto Loader rescued data.\n• Duplicates → dedupe + idempotent MERGE.\n• OOM/memory → filter early, partition, right-size cluster, persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK).\n• Small-file problem → OPTIMIZE / Auto Optimize.\n• Slow large-large joins → broadcast/bucketing.\n• Connection timeouts → retry/backoff, connection pooling, scale IR.\n• Datatype mismatch → explicit schema + validation.\n• Incremental load failures → reliable watermark in a control table.\n• Late-arriving data → overlap window / reprocess."},{"q":"Scenario: 10 CSV files arrive every day at 10 AM.","type":"scenario","a":"Scenario : I have 10 files which arrives at 10.00 AM , employee files – 5 , attendance files – 5 , all are in csv format , no change in schema (same schema) – what would be, I want you to","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If job is running slow, what will you do?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:44934) Steps to troubleshoot:","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks job is running slow. What will you do?","type":"concept","a":"Steps to troubleshoot:","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Explain RAG to a non-technical person.","type":"concept","a":"• \"Imagine you ask a librarian a ques(cid:415)on\n• Instead of answering from memory, the librarian first searches the library for the most relevant books, reads the necessary informa(cid:415)on, and then gives you an accurate answer\n• RAG works in the same way\n• Instead of relying only on what the AI already knows, it first retrieves relevant informa(cid:415)on from your company's documents or databases and then generates an answer using that informa(cid:415)on\n• This makes the responses more accurate and up to date.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"}]},{"id":"behavioral","name":"Behavioral, Project & HR","icon":"💬","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Tell me about yourself.","type":"concept","a":"Structure (present → past → future, 60–90 seconds):\n• Present — current role, core stack (ADF, Databricks, PySpark, Delta, Azure), and what you build (ETL/ELT pipelines, lakehouse, real-time ingestion).\n• Past — key experiences that built your skills (projects, scale, domains).\n• Future — why this role excites you and how you add value.\nKeep it relevant to data engineering; end with a hook for the interviewer."},{"q":"Walk me through a complex data engineering project you built.","type":"concept","a":"Use STAR: Situation → Task → Action → Result.\n• Situation: business problem + scale (data volume, sources, latency).\n• Task: your responsibility/objective.\n• Action: architecture (ADF → ADLS → Databricks Bronze/Silver/Gold), tools, transformations, governance, CI/CD, challenges you solved.\n• Result: quantify (runtime reduced X%, cost saved, records processed, SLAs met, downtime avoided).\nMention one hard problem and how you solved it to show depth."},{"q":"Describe a challenging problem you faced and how you resolved it.","type":"concept","a":"Pick ONE real technical challenge (e.g., data skew causing a 10h job, small-file explosion, schema drift breaking a pipeline).\n• Context and impact.\n• How you diagnosed it (Spark UI, logs, metrics).\n• The fix (AQE/salting, OPTIMIZE/Auto Optimize, mergeSchema/rescued data, idempotent MERGE).\n• Measurable outcome + what you learned / preventive measure.\nShow systematic troubleshooting, not just luck."},{"q":"How do you handle tight deadlines or changing requirements?","type":"concept","a":"• Clarify and prioritize with stakeholders (MVP first, iterate).\n• Break work into deliverables; communicate risks early.\n• Reuse existing frameworks/patterns (metadata-driven pipelines, templates) to move faster.\n• Automate tests/CI-CD to reduce rework.\n• Document assumptions and keep decisions transparent.\nExample: delivered core pipeline first, added optimizations/DQ in later iterations."},{"q":"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a stakeholder/teammate.","type":"concept","a":"Use a professional, data-driven approach.\n• Explain the situation and the disagreement (e.g., aggressive SLA vs cluster cost).\n• How you listened, gathered evidence (benchmarks/cost analysis), and proposed alternatives.\n• Outcome: found a middle ground (tiered data, incremental processing, right-sized clusters).\n• Emphasize respect, evidence, and shared goals — not 'I was right'."},{"q":"Why are you looking for a change / why this company?","type":"concept","a":"• Change: growth (architect-level scope, modern stack, larger scale), not negativity about the current employer.\n• This company: align their data challenges/tech (Azure, Databricks, lakehouse) with your skills; mention something specific (their domain, scale, modernization).\n• Emphasize what you bring and how the role fits your growth.\nKeep it positive and forward-looking."},{"q":"What are your strengths and weaknesses?","type":"concept","a":"Strengths (2–3, with proof): e.g., end-to-end pipeline design, performance optimization (Spark/Delta), ownership/automation. Back each with an example.\nWeakness (1, genuine + improving): e.g., used to over-engineering → now start simple and iterate; or delegation → actively mentoring/assigning. Show self-awareness and a concrete improvement action.\nAvoid clichés and never use a disguised strength."},{"q":"Where do you see yourself in 5 years?","type":"concept","a":"• Short term: master the current stack, deliver high-impact pipelines, deepen cloud/lakehouse expertise.\n• Long term: grow into a lead/architect role — designing scalable data platforms, mentoring engineers, driving standards.\n• Tie it to continuous learning (certifications, new tech like Fabric/Unity Catalog).\nShow ambition that aligns with the company's path."},{"q":"How do you keep your skills updated / how do you learn?","type":"concept","a":"• Hands-on labs and personal projects (build mini lakehouse, streaming demos).\n• Official docs (Databricks, Microsoft Learn), certifications (DP-203, Databricks Data Engineer/Architect).\n• Community: blogs, meetups, forums, release notes.\n• Apply learning at work — e.g., piloted Auto Loader/DLT, shared findings with the team.\nEmphasize applying, not just reading."},{"q":"How do you estimate effort and prioritize tasks in a data project?","type":"concept","a":"• Break work into tasks; estimate by complexity/uncertainty (similar past work, spikes for unknowns).\n• Prioritize by business value, dependencies, and risk (MoSCoW / value vs effort).\n• Reserve buffer for data issues/integration surprises.\n• Communicate timelines, track progress, and re-plan when blockers arise.\n• Automate/testing reduces hidden effort rework."},{"q":"What do you do when you are blocked on a task?","type":"concept","a":"• Investigate first — logs, docs, minimal reproduction (timeboxed).\n• Try known solutions/search; check if others faced it.\n• Communicate early with the team/lead; don't silently stall.\n• Escalate with context: what you tried, the error, what you need.\n• Document the fix for future reference.\nShows resourcefulness + teamwork."},{"q":"How do you handle production incidents / on-call?","type":"concept","a":"• Triage: assess impact (SLA/customers) and severity.\n• Mitigate first (restart/repair-and-rerun/failover) to restore service, then root-cause.\n• Communicate status to stakeholders regularly.\n• Post-incident: RCA, fix the root cause, add monitoring/alerts and DQ checks to prevent recurrence.\nEmphasize calm, communication, and prevention."},{"q":"Describe a time you automated a manual process.","type":"concept","a":"• Situation: repetitive manual task (e.g., manual file loads, hand-written SQL per table).\n• Action: built metadata-driven ADF pipelines / parameterized Databricks notebooks, scheduled with Workflows, added logging and CI/CD.\n• Result: saved X hours/week, reduced human error, freed the team for higher-value work.\nTie automation to reliability and cost savings."},{"q":"Set Action Group — who gets notified (email, SMS, Teams webhook)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"What challenges did you face while pulling data from SQL Server to ADLS?","type":"concept","a":"• \"While inges(cid:415)ng data from SQL Server to ADLS, I encountered several challenges:\" Challenge 1: Large data volumes  Full loads took too long.  Solu(cid:415)on: Implemented incremental loading using a watermark column such as ModifiedDate or LastUpdatedDate\n• Challenge 2: Network/connec(cid:415)vity issues  Occasionally the Self-hosted Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me lost connec(cid:415)vity to the on-premises SQL Server.  Solu(cid:415)on: Configured retries, monitored the Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me, and worked with the infrastructure team to resolve connec(cid:415)vity issues\n• Challenge 3: Schema changes  New columns were added to source tables.  Solu(cid:415)on: Updated mappings and validated downstream pipelines before deployment\n• Challenge 4: Data quality issues  Found null values, duplicate records, and invalid data.  Solu(cid:415)on: Applied valida(cid:415)on and cleansing rules during the transforma(cid:415)on stage","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Have you done any work and got apprecia(cid:415)on in your experience?","type":"concept","a":"• A good STAR \"Yes\n• In one project, our daily data inges(cid:415)on pipeline took nearly three hours because it performed full loads\n• I analyzed the process and implemented incremental loading using a watermark column and op(cid:415)mized some Spark transforma(cid:415)ons\n• This reduced the pipeline run(cid:415)me to around 45 minutes\n• The improvement allowed reports to be available much earlier, and I received apprecia(cid:415)on from my project lead and the business team for improving performance and reliability.\" If you don't have exact numbers, you can say \"significantly reduced the execu(cid:415)on (cid:415)me.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Do you have any ques(cid:415)ons for us?","type":"concept","a":"• Always ask one or two though(cid:414)ul ques(cid:415)ons\n• Examples:  \"What kinds of data engineering projects would I be working on in this role?\"  \"What technologies are part of your current data pla(cid:414)orm?\"  \"How is the data engineering team structured?\"  \"What are the biggest technical challenges the team is currently working to solve?\"  \"What does success look like for someone in this role during the first six months?\" Avoid asking about salary or leave in the first technical interview unless the interviewer brings it up","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Do You Have Any Questions for Us?","type":"concept","a":"• Always prepare 3-5 thoughtful questions\n• They signal genuine interest and help you assess the role fit\n• Questions about team and project","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What are the biggest data engineering challenges the team is working through right now?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"}]},{"id":"imported","name":"Imported from Documents","icon":"•","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Pass parameters","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Configure Cluster","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Call Notebook","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Capture output","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Previously processed files are skipped.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Cluster Configuration & Optimization","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Loading Static & Database Files in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Autoloader & Implementation","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Data Skewness","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Types of Clusters in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Chain Activities Connect multiple notebook activities with arrows. Use 'On Success' /","type":"concept","a":"'On Failure' connectors for conditional logic.","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Set Schedule For daily at 2 AM: Recurrence = Every 1 Day, Start Time = 2:00 AM","type":"concept","a":"UTC.","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to load multiple files into single table","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to load multiple files into different tables","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is a framework that is open-source It is an open-source framework used for","type":"concept","a":"• which is used for writing data into the faster data processing\n• Hadoop Distributed File System","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is difficult to program as you It is easy to program.","type":"concept","a":"required code for every process.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"For performing the task, It is unable to It can cache the memory data for processing","type":"concept","a":"cache in memory. its task.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to write Data into storage from Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"A. csvdf=userdf.write.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to call one notebook in another notebook","type":"concept","a":"• The %run command allows you to include another notebook within a notebook\n• You can use %run to modularize your code, for example by putting supporting functions in a separate notebook\n• You can also use it to concatenate notebooks that implement the steps in an analysis","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to pass parameters inside Notebook","type":"concept","a":"• If you are running a notebook from another notebook, then use dbutils. notebook. run(path = \" \", args={}, timeout='120'), you can pass variables in args = {}\n• And you will use dbutils","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It’s understanding is very simple. While it’s understanding is difficult.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It has less number of foreign keys. While it has more number of foreign keys.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different category of services available?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"IAAS---Infra structure as a service { CPU, RAM, Hard disk, Memory….}","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SAAS---Software as a service{office 365…….}","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Queues or messages","type":"concept","a":"4. File share","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different Access tiers available in storage Accounts?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Hot tier: It is suitable for frequently accessing data, Cost also high & Performance also","type":"concept","a":"more.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Cool tier: It is suitable for in frequently accessing data. Cost also less &","type":"concept","a":"Performance also less.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Archive tier: It is suitable Po backup data.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Connection strings","type":"concept","a":"2. Access keys","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SAS (Share Access Signature","type":"concept","a":"Page | 25","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the advantage of SAS (Share Access Signature)?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Providing access to only particular resources","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Providing specific role","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Providing only particular IP system","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to change Access Tiers from Hot to cool Tier if files are older than 30 Days?","type":"concept","a":"By using life cycle management under storage account we can add rule","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"DTU(Data Transaction Unit)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Vcore processors","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Replication: Entire data will be distributed each node. Very small tables likes lookup tables.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is polybase what are steps needs to follow to implement polybase and","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create master key","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create database scoped credentials","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create external data source","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Create external file format","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is OLTP?","type":"concept","a":"A.OLTP or Online Transaction Processing is a type of data processing that consists of executing a number of transactions occurring concurrently—online banking, shopping, order entry, or sending text messages","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is OLAP?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Online analytical processing (OLAP) is a technology that organizes large business databases and supports complex analysis\n• It can be used to perform complex analytical queries without negatively affecting transactional systems","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is ER modelling?","type":"concept","a":"• A.An entity–relationship model describes interrelated things of interest in a specific domain of knowledge\n• A basic ER model is composed of entity types and specifies relationships that can exist between entities","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is primary and foreign key?","type":"concept","a":"• A.A primary key is used to assure the value in the particular column is unique\n• The foreign key provides the link between the two tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"V1 doesn't contain many of activites.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are dml and ddl comands?","type":"concept","a":"➢ DML COMMANDS: INSERT,UPDATE,DELETE ➢ DDL COMMANDS: CREATE,DROP,ALTER,TRUNCATE","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between delete and truncate?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ DELETE: it is used to delete records from a database table\n• There is a possibility to rollback the data. ➢ TRUNCATE: it is used to remove records from a database table\n• Once truncate the data. it will delete permanently","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is difference between isnull, nullif, coalesce function?","type":"concept","a":"ISNULL(): You can only provide one alternate value but with COALESCE you can provide more than one e.g. if col1 IS NULL then take value from column2, if that is NULL then take the default value NULLIF(): Function returns NULL if two expressions are equal, otherwise it returns the first expression.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Not Null Constraint","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Check Constraint","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Default Constraint","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Unique Constraint","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Primary Constraint","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Foreign Constraint","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to display running Total or cumulative sum in employee","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 -- department wise cumulative sum select empno,ename,job,deptno,sal,sum(sal) over(partition by deptno order by deptno,ename) as highsal from employee ---Solution2 --- total employees wise cumulative sum select empno,ename,job,deptno,sal,sum(sal) over (order by deptno,ename) as Page | 35 highsal from employee","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"NULL AND COALESCE:","type":"concept","a":"--isnull : it will take a only first if both colunms is null is shown a null. create table table_1 ( col1 varchar(10), col2 varchar(10)) insert into table_1 values('siva','krish'),(null,'ram'),('radhe',null),(null,null) select* from table_1 select isnull(col1,col2) as name from table_1 --nullif: in this both parameters are same it will give null and it is not same returns name only create table table_3 ( col1 varchar(10), col2 varchar(10)) insert into table_3 values('avi','avi'),('siva','krish'),(null,'ram'),('radhe',null),(null,null) select* from table_3 select nullif(col1,col2) as name from table_3 --coalesce: it is a like a null but in this we can use number of create table table_2 ( col1 varchar(10), col2 varchar(10),col3 varchar(10)) insert into table_2 values('hari',null,'varam'),('siva','krish','prasad'),(null,'ram','krishna'), (null,'radhe',null),(null,null,'aray'),(null,null,null) select * from table_2 select Coalesce(col1,col2,col3) as name from table_2 use proce select * from res","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"CURSOR: Cursor is a memory location for storing database tables.","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ It is a temporary work area allotted to the client at server when a select statements is Executed. ➢ A cursor contains information on a select statement and rows of data accessed by it. ➢ The temporary work area is used to store the data retrived from the data base and manipulate the data\n• Types of Cursors: 1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Derived Column — Create a new column: TotalRevenue = Quantity * Price","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Handling Late Arriving Data in Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Click 'New Alert Rule'","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Set condition — e.g., Count > 0 for failures","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Set threshold — alert if result > 0","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Set notification destination — Email, Slack, PagerDuty","type":"concept","a":"Option 2 — Job Failure Notifications (for Databricks Jobs):","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Go to Workflows → Your Job → Edit","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Under 'Notifications', add email or webhook","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Click on the canvas background → go to Parameters tab","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Add parameters — e.g., load_date (String), environment (String), batch_size (Int)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"In the Settings tab → Expand 'Parameters'","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Stay calm — do not panic or make random changes","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Acknowledge the alert — if there is an on-call chat or ticketing system, post that you are","type":"concept","a":"investigating","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Verify the data landed correctly in the target (row counts, spot check)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Update the incident ticket / on-call channel: what failed, why, how it was fixed","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Notify stakeholders if data was delayed (e.g., reports will be ready by 4 AM instead of 2 AM)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"say your approach ?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you check if files arrive at 6.00 AM ?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"I have a Customer Table and Orders Table, I want to get those customers who has","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"not purchased anything for the last 30days ? Whats your logic ?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Find the latest order date for each customer.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Compare the latest order date with today's date.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"project explana(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning","type":"concept","a":"➢ Manually repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on data using .repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on() or .par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy() to balance load.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Create App Registra(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Generate Secret","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Pass parameters if required.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Configure Wait on Comple(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Select Workspace URL.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Authen(cid:415)ca(cid:415)on (Managed Iden(cid:415)ty or Access Token).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Select Exis(cid:415)ng Cluster or Job Cluster.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Pass notebook parameters.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Configure (cid:415)meout and retries.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Why move only certain tables?","type":"concept","a":"Reasons:  Reduce storage costs  Improve performance  Avoid duplica(cid:415)ng opera(cid:415)onal data  Move only repor(cid:415)ng and historical data","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Missing dates with LAG vs DATEDIFF","type":"concept","a":"• Example: Jan1 Jan5 LAG() compares with the previous available row regardless of date gaps\n• Use DATEDIFF() to detect missing days: DATEDIFF(day, LAG(Date) OVER(ORDER BY Date), Date) If the result is greater than 1, dates are missing","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Match business key.","type":"concept","a":"2. If changed: o Expire old row (EndDate, IsCurrent = 0) o Insert new row (StartDate, IsCurrent = 1)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produc(cid:415)on issue","type":"concept","a":"• Steps: 1\n• Review logs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Compare Produc(cid:415)on vs Dev data.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Reproduce using similar input.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Iden(cid:415)fy root cause.","type":"concept","a":"• Fix in Dev\n• Validate","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Dev and Produc(cid:415)on environments","type":"concept","a":"Typical flow: Development ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on Each environment has its own workspace, storage, and configura(cid:415)on.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize data","type":"concept","a":" Reduce file size (avoid small files)  Use par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Increase cluster size","type":"concept","a":" Add more workers","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Output returned","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid SELECT * — Select only needed columns","type":"concept","a":"df.select('id','name','amount') # not df.select('*')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to Use Notebook 1 Inside Notebook 2","type":"concept","a":"Method 1 — dbutils.notebook.run() (Most Common) # In Notebook 2, call Notebook 1 with parameters result = dbutils.notebook.run( '/Users/me/notebook1', # full path to Notebook 1 timeout_seconds = 3600, # max time to wait arguments = { 'run_date': '2024-01-01', 'env': 'prod' } ) print(f'Notebook 1 returned: {result}') # In Notebook 1 — read parameters run_date = dbutils.widgets.get('run_date') # Return a value from Notebook 1 dbutils.notebook.exit('SUCCESS') Method 2 — %run (Import functions/variables from another notebook) # In Notebook 2 — runs Notebook 1 inline (shares scope/variables) %run /Users/me/notebook1 # After %run, all functions and variables from Notebook 1 are available # If Notebook 1 defines: def transform(df): ... # You can call it in Notebook 2: result = transform(my_df) Method Scope Sharing Parameters Return Value Use Case %run Yes — shares No (uses widget No Share utility functions across variables and defaults) notebooks functions dbutils.notebook.run() No — isolated Yes — pass dict of Yes — exit() Orchestrate sequential notebooks; execution args string pass/return data","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Drop rows with null in specific columns","type":"concept","a":"df_clean = df.dropna(subset=['id','name']) # drop if id OR name is null df_clean = df.dropna(how='all') # drop only if ALL columns are null","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Fill nulls with a default value","type":"concept","a":"df_clean = df.fillna(0) # fill all numeric nulls with 0 df_clean = df.fillna('Unknown') # fill all string nulls df_clean = df.fillna({'salary': 0, 'dept': 'N/A'}) # per-column fill values","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Repartitioning:","type":"concept","a":"Repartition by a more evenly distributed column to balance data across partitions. df = df.repartition(200, 'evenly_distributed_col')","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"You are loading multiple CSV files, but schema of those files is not consistent","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Suppose transaction_date column is in String type. How can you convert it to","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"You have sales table and customers table with customer_id in common. Retrieve","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Assume you have external table. Table has been deleted in databricks. Can we restore the","type":"concept","a":"","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you use par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in Databricks  Used to physically divide data Example: df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"year\", \"month\").format(\"delta\").save()","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you import or use third party JAR file in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Third-party JAR usage  Cluster Libraries upload  Maven dependency  Init scripts","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are the types of cluster?","type":"concept","a":"Types of clusters  All-purpose cluster  Job cluster  High concurrency cluster  Single node cluster","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you fix if job is failed in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Handling job failure  Check logs in Databricks UI  Iden(cid:415)fy root cause  Fix data/code issue  Retry job  Enable alerts","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is data skipping?","type":"concept","a":"Data Skipping  Delta op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on feature  Skips irrelevant files using metadata","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are the quality checks you have implemented in your project?","type":"concept","a":"Data Quality Checks  Null checks  Duplicate checks  Schema valida(cid:415)on  Row count valida(cid:415)on  Business rule valida(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Have you worked on any AI project. Any poc you worked.?","type":"concept","a":"AI Project / POC “I have explored GenAI tools like ChatGPT and worked on small automa(cid:415)on POCs using prompt- based workflows and Databricks features.”","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Monthly Transac(cid:415)on Summary","type":"concept","a":"","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Read source data","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Read target table","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Compare business keys","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Expire old records","type":"concept","a":"CurrentFlag='N' EndDate=current_date()","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Describe how you ensure data quality and validation in your ETL process.","type":"concept","a":"Mention:  Null validation  Duplicate checks  Primary key validation  Record count validation  Schema validation  Business rule validation  Data reconciliation  Logging  Exception tables Example Source Count = 1,000,000 Destination Count = 1,000,000 Duplicate Count = 0 Null CustomerID = 0","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you handle last-minute requirement changes from stakeholders?","type":"concept","a":"• First, I understand the business priority and assess the impact on timelines, dependencies, and downstream systems\n• If the change is urgent, I discuss trade-offs with stakeholders and deliver a minimum viable solution first, followed by remaining enhancements in later iterations","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Self-hosted IR","type":"concept","a":"o On-premises data transfer o Installed on local machine/server","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Go to Monitor tab.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Check failed ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Read error message.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Check ac(cid:415)vity output.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Validate parameters.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Retry failed ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Use retry policy.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Configure alerts.","type":"concept","a":"Common failures:  Authen(cid:415)ca(cid:415)on failure  Timeout  File not found  Schema mismatch  Network issue  Storage permission","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Will Copy Ac(cid:415)vity fail if there is column mismatch?","type":"concept","a":"• Answer: Depends\n• If schema mapping is Auto Mapping, Copy Ac(cid:415)vity ignores extra columns and maps matching columns\n• If explicit mapping exists and required columns are missing, it fails\n• If sink datatype is incompa(cid:415)ble, it also fails","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Iden(cid:415)fy slow ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Iden(cid:415)fy skew.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Verify cluster size.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Compare source read vs transforma(cid:415)on vs write (cid:415)me.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is Offset?","type":"concept","a":"• Unique posi(cid:415)on of a message inside a par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Consumers resume reading using offsets\n• Delivery Guarantees  At-most-once  At-least-once  Exactly-once Most systems use at-least-once with idempotent consumers to avoid duplicates","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If we have files with folder Main and inside folders Product,orders,sales and you have files","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Iden(cid:415)fy groups of consecu(cid:415)ve ids using the trick:","type":"concept","a":"o id - ROW_NUMBER() is constant for consecu(cid:415)ve IDs.","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Count the number of rows in each group.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Use a Copy Data ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Configure table mapping.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Iden(cid:415)fy the root cause from the logs.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Fix the issue (for example, missing file, schema mismatch, permission issue).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Rerun only the failed table or ac(cid:415)vity instead of reprocessing all 100 tables.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Verify the table loaded successfully and update the execu(cid:415)on log.\"","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"• What does a successful first 90 days look like in this position?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Classify each transac(cid:415)on as \"HIGH\" or \"LOW\" based on the following rule:","type":"concept","a":"o If amount >= 50, classify as HIGH o Otherwise, classify as LOW","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Add the classifica(cid:415)on as a new column named classifica(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Fla(cid:425)en the nested column so that price and quan(cid:415)ty become separate columns.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"What op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on strategies have you implemented in Databricks? Also, how have you","type":"concept","a":"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Job cluster configura(cid:415)on in produc(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":" Created dynamically per job run  Defined in Databricks job: o Run(cid:415)me version (DBR) o Worker type & autoscaling o Min/max workers  Terminated automa(cid:415)cally a(cid:332)er job comple(cid:415)on  Used for cost op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on + isola(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Other ETL tools","type":"concept","a":" Informa(cid:415)ca  Talend  SSIS  AWS Glue  Apache NiFi","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Iden(cid:415)fy data skew.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Use par(cid:415)(cid:415)on pruning.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid unnecessary repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on().","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Increase cluster resources if needed.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"List vs Tuple vs Dic(cid:415)onary","type":"concept","a":"List Tuple Dic(cid:415)onary Mutable Immutable Key-Value pairs Ordered Ordered Keys are unique [] () {}","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"List Comprehension","type":"concept","a":"squares = [x*x for x in range(5)] Output: [0,1,4,9,16]","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Disadvantage of Coalesce compared to Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• \"The main disadvantage of coalesce is that it doesn't fully redistribute data across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, so it can create uneven workloads\n• It is mainly used to reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons\n• If we need balanced par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons or want to increase par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on is the be(cid:425)er choice.\"","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cannot balance data well","type":"concept","a":"If par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons are uneven: 100GB 2GB 1GB A(cid:332)er coalesce: 103GB S(cid:415)ll uneven.","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cannot efficiently increase par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• df.coalesce(100) Not recommended\n• Use df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(100)","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your end-to-end project.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If there are mul(cid:415)ple files, use Get Metadata to list the files and a ForEach ac(cid:415)vity to copy","type":"concept","a":"each file.","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Add valida(cid:415)on, logging, and alerts to handle failures.\"","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Configure authen(cid:415)ca(cid:415)on, such as OAuth or API creden(cid:415)als.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Configure authen(cid:415)ca(cid:415)on using a Personal Access Token or Managed Iden(cid:415)ty.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Select the notebook path and specify the cluster (exis(cid:415)ng or job cluster).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Different Modes of Ingestion","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"All-Purpose Cluster (Interactive Cluster)","type":"concept","a":"• Used for: Interactive development, ad-hoc analysis, notebooks • Persists until manually terminated or auto-terminated • Can be shared by multiple users/notebooks • More expensive due to idle time","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Introduce yourself","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your project","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]}]},{"id":"databricks-architect","name":"Databricks Architect","description":"Databricks Architect interview preparation","topics":[{"id":"architect","name":"Databricks Architect — Design & Consulting","icon":"🏛️","roles":["architect"],"questions":[{"q":"How would you design a data platform for 10 data teams that need to share data but have different access requirements?","type":"scenario","a":"This is an ORGANIZATION design problem, not just a tech one. Cover:\n• Catalog structure by domain — a central 'enterprise' catalog for shared/curated (Gold) data, plus team-specific catalogs for team-owned data. Teams produce to their own catalog and consume from enterprise + granted peers.\n• Unity Catalog for account-level governance, lineage, and grants to groups (from the IdP), not individuals.\n• Data ownership model — each team owns its domain data (data-as-a-product); clear producers vs consumers.\n• Access requirements — row filters/column masks for sensitive data; GRANTs per catalog/schema.\n• Governance process — a data council for cross-team standards, naming, contracts, and conflict resolution.\n• Self-service enablement — templates, CI/CD, cluster policies so teams move fast safely.\nInterviewers test whether you design for people and process, not just tables."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You inherit a Databricks platform spending $50k/month. How do you reduce it by 40% without breaking things?","type":"scenario","a":"Business-impact, leadership answer (not just tactics):\n1. Attribute cost first — use system.billing.usage + system tables to break spend by workspace/team/job (chargeback model). You can't optimize what you can't see.\n2. Eliminate waste — idle interactive clusters (auto-terminate), oversized clusters, always-on SQL warehouses (auto-suspend), runaway jobs.\n3. Right-size compute — Job clusters vs All-Purpose, autoscaling, Spot instances for batch, instance pools.\n4. Optimize workloads — Photon, AQE, predictive optimization, liquid clustering, eliminate full reprocessing with incremental loads.\n5. Governance — cluster policies to cap sizes, budgets/alerts, and shift accountability to teams.\n6. Track results and communicate savings; iterate. Emphasize 80/20 (a few workloads drive most cost)."},{"q":"How do you approach a large warehouse-to-lakehouse migration?","type":"scenario","a":"Phased, low-risk migration:\n• Assess — inventory workloads, query patterns, SLAs, data volumes, dependencies; identify quick wins and high-risk areas.\n• Choose pattern — lift-and-shift (fast, minimal refactor) vs refactor/re-architect (better long-term). Often hybrid.\n• Land data — ingest into Bronze; model in Medallion; convert to Delta.\n• Workload-by-workload cutover — migrate the least critical/simplest first; validate outputs vs source (reconciliation).\n• Optimize — liquid clustering/Z-ORDER, partitioning, Photon; rebuild BI on Databricks SQL / Direct Lake.\n• Govern — Unity Catalog, RLS/CLS, lineage.\n• Run parallel until confidence; then decommission. Track business outcomes and communicate with stakeholders."},{"q":"How do you architect a high-concurrency, low-latency data warehouse on Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"Focus on the levers that drive concurrency and latency:\n• Compute — serverless SQL Warehouses with autoscaling + Intelligent Workload Management; separate workloads (BI vs ETL vs ad hoc).\n• Data layout — liquid clustering (over Hive partitioning/Z-ORDER) for data skipping; right-size files via predictive optimization; collect stats.\n• Query engine — Photon (vectorized C++ engine), AQE, caching, range join optimization.\n• Modeling — star schema Gold tables, pre-aggregations/materialized views for dashboards.\n• Concurrency — scale warehouses, connection pooling from BI tools, result caching.\n• Governance/monitoring — query history, system tables, budgets and alerts.\nFrame trade-offs: latency vs concurrency vs cost; iterate with real workloads, don't over-provision up front."},{"q":"Describe the Databricks Well-Architected Framework pillars and how they guide design.","type":"concept","a":"The well-architected lakehouse framework has these pillars:\n• Operational Excellence — automation, CI/CD, observability, incident response.\n• Security, Privacy & Compliance — identity, Unity Catalog, network isolation, encryption, least privilege.\n• Reliability — resilience, fault tolerance, DR, idempotency, retries, checkpointing.\n• Performance Efficiency — right-sizing, Photon/AQE, liquid clustering, serverless, workload design.\n• Cost Optimization — job clusters, Spot, auto-terminate, system table cost attribution.\n• Data & AI Governance — catalogs, lineage, quality, sharing.\nUse them as a checklist in reviews and to balance competing concerns (e.g., performance vs cost)."},{"q":"Centralized data platform vs data mesh — how do you decide?","type":"scenario","a":"• Centralized — one team/platform owns pipelines and data; simpler governance, faster to standardize; can become a bottleneck as domains grow.\n• Data mesh — domain teams own and publish data products with self-serve platform + federated governance; scales org-wide but demands maturity, standards, and a strong platform team.\nDecision factors: org size, number of domains, data literacy, governance maturity, need for speed vs control.\nPractical answer: a hybrid — central platform/standards (Unity Catalog + templates) with domain ownership of data products. Databricks enables this via UC catalogs per domain + Delta Sharing between domains."},{"q":"How do you design for disaster recovery and business continuity on Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"• Data — Delta in ADLS with geo-redundant storage (GRS/RA-GRS); replicate to a secondary region; external tables + UC so metadata follows data.\n• Metastore — account-level Unity Catalog supports failover to a secondary workspace/metastore in another region.\n• Compute — reproducible via Asset Bundles/IaC so you can stand up compute in DR region quickly.\n• RPO/RTO — define targets; Delta time travel + copy for point-in-time recovery; streaming checkpoint restart from replicated storage.\n• Testing — run DR drills; document runbooks; automate failover where possible.\nDiscuss cost trade-off: active-active is expensive; often warm standby or backup/restore meets the requirement."},{"q":"When do you choose serverless compute vs classic clusters?","type":"concept","a":"• Serverless (SQL warehouses, jobs, notebooks) — instant start, no infra management, auto-scaling, ideal for BI, short/elastic workloads, and reducing ops overhead; pay only for use.\n• Classic clusters — needed for custom libraries/init scripts, specific runtime/VM types, GPU/ML, VNet-injected/private networking, or when you want instance pools for steady heavy workloads.\nDecision: default to serverless for simplicity and elasticity; use classic when you need customization, isolation, specialized hardware, or predictable cost at constant high utilization."},{"q":"How do you design the DEV / QA / PROD environment topology?","type":"concept","a":"• Workspaces — separate workspaces per environment (isolation) attached to one Unity Catalog metastore (per region).\n• Data isolation — separate catalogs/schemas per env (dev_catalog, qa_catalog, prod_catalog) with grants; never point dev at prod data.\n• Config — parameterize paths, catalog/schema, cluster sizes per env (DAB targets, ADF global params).\n• Delivery — Git + CI/CD (Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions) deploying bundles/jobs with service principals; promote artifacts, not manual changes.\n• Governance — limit prod permissions; enforce cluster policies; separate service principal per env.\nConsider more granularity (feature branches/preview) only if the team is mature."},{"q":"How do you perform capacity planning and cost attribution at platform scale?","type":"concept","a":"• Cost attribution — system.billing.usage joined with system.billing.list_prices and workspace/job metadata; tag clusters/jobs by team/project.\n• Chargeback/showback — dashboards per team so consumers own their spend.\n• Right-sizing — analyze run history, peak vs average utilization, autoscaling behavior; avoid over-provisioning.\n• Forecast — model growth (data volume, users, jobs) and reserve capacity for spikes.\n• Optimize — predictive optimization, Photon, incremental processing, Spot.\n• Govern — budgets + alerts, cluster policies with max sizes, review regularly with stakeholders."},{"q":"What is the Databricks Photon engine and when does it help?","type":"concept","a":"Photon is Databricks' native, vectorized query engine written in C++ that processes data in columnar batches rather than row-by-row (like the JVM-based engine).\n• Accelerates SQL and DataFrame (Spark) workloads — often 2–8x faster, especially scans, joins, aggregations, and wide transformations.\n• Enabled on compatible runtimes; transparent to your code.\n• Helps most when CPU-bound; less benefit for I/O-bound or tiny workloads. Consider cost vs speed trade-off."},{"q":"What is Predictive Optimization?","type":"concept","a":"Predictive Optimization is a Databricks feature for Unity Catalog managed tables that automatically runs maintenance — OPTIMIZE (compaction), VACUUM, and ANALYZE (statistics) — based on observed usage patterns.\n• Removes the need to manually schedule and tune table maintenance.\n• Reduces storage and improves query performance (data skipping) with fewer small files.\n• Operates on UC managed tables; a key 'cost & performance optimization' answer for architect-level design."},{"q":"What is the range join optimization and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"Range join optimization speeds up joins where the predicate is a point-in-interval or interval-overlap condition (e.g., fact.timestamp BETWEEN dim.start AND dim.end — classic SCD2 lookups).\n• Databricks can bin the interval into a range and use a bucket join, giving order-of-magnitude improvements.\n• Requires manual tuning (range level/bins) and is used for large fact ↔ dimension interval joins.\nArchitect relevance: choosing it for SCD2/validity joins instead of exploding joins or UDFs."},{"q":"How do you design an ML/AI platform on Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"Unified data + ML on the lakehouse:\n• Data — Bronze/Silver/Gold with a Feature Store (feature tables, online/offline parity).\n• Experiments — MLflow tracking for params/metrics/artifacts across teams.\n• Training — GPU clusters; AutoML; distributed training; manage dependencies.\n• Registry — MLflow Model Registry for versioning/staging/production with approvals.\n• Serving — Model Serving (serverless endpoints), batch scoring, or feature lookups; monitor drift.\n• Governance — Unity Catalog for models/functions, lineage, and access.\n• For GenAI — RAG with Vector Search, embeddings, Foundation Model APIs, and governed data.\nEmphasize reuse of governed data and closed-loop monitoring."},{"q":"How do you standardize governance across many teams without blocking them?","type":"concept","a":"Federated governance with guardrails:\n• Central standards, local ownership — Unity Catalog gives account-level policy; teams own their catalogs/schemas.\n• Least privilege via IdP groups, not individuals; grant at the highest sensible level.\n• Policies as code — cluster policies, tagging standards, budgets enforced centrally but consumed self-service.\n• Templates/golden paths — CI/CD bundles, pipeline templates, DQ frameworks teams adopt.\n• Sensitive data — column masks + row filters; classification via Purview.\n• Audit & visibility — audit logs, lineage, cost dashboards; a data council resolves conflicts.\nBalance control with developer velocity."},{"q":"A customer insists on an open-source, self-built Spark platform over Databricks. How do you respond?","type":"scenario","a":"Consultative, no hard sell — compare on total cost of ownership and outcomes:\n• Acknowledge strengths of OSS Spark (no license, flexibility, no lock-in with open formats like Delta/Parquet).\n• Surface hidden costs of self-managed — cluster management, upgrades, security, governance, on-call, GPU/DBA effort; slow time-to-value.\n• Databricks advantages — managed + serverless, Photon performance, Unity Catalog governance, Delta/Lakeflow/MLflow, built-in reliability and observability.\n• De-risk — start with a proof-of-concept on ONE workload, measure performance/TCO, then scale what wins.\n• Keep it objective and tied to the customer's business goals; avoid competitor bashing."},{"q":"How do you design security and network isolation for Databricks?","type":"scenario","a":"• Identity — Entra ID SSO; SCIM provisioning; groups for access.\n• Network — VNet injection (customer-managed VNet, subnets, NSGs); Private Link/private endpoints for control plane and storage; no-public-IP workspaces; firewall.\n• Data at rest — SSE (Microsoft-managed or customer-managed keys); Unity Catalog + external locations restrict path access.\n• Secrets — Azure Key Vault via Databricks secret scopes; managed identities/Access Connector; no keys in code.\n• Access control — UC GRANTs, row filters, column masks, least privilege.\n• Monitoring — audit logs to Log Analytics, alerting.\nMap each control to compliance requirements (HIPAA/PCI/etc.)."},{"q":"How do you handle data contracts and data quality at platform scale?","type":"concept","a":"• Data contracts — agreed schema/semantics + SLAs between producer and consumer; enforce via schema validation and versioned interfaces.\n• Quality framework — DLT expectations / declarative expectations, or Great Expectations; classify rules as fail / drop / warn.\n• Quarantine — route bad records to a bad-data store with reasons for triage.\n• Observability — DQ metrics dashboards, alerting on breaches, lineage to trace impact.\n• Governance — data owners per domain; contracts reviewed in the data council.\nPrinciple: shift quality left (validate on ingest) and make failures visible and recoverable."},{"q":"How do you migrate from Hive/legacy tables to Unity Catalog?","type":"scenario","a":"• Inventory — Hive Metastore tables, views, permissions, and dependencies.\n• Upgrade — use the UC upgrade wizard / SYNC command to register Hive tables into UC (managed vs external handling).\n• Access — recreate grants; map legacy ACLs to UC GRANTs; validate per group.\n• Paths — ensure external locations + storage credentials cover the data paths.\n• Validate — compare row counts/checksums; test representative queries; monitor for broken jobs.\n• Cut over gradually — per catalog/domain; keep Hive read-only during transition; then decommission.\nCommunicate, automate, and rehearse rollback."},{"q":"Walk through how you would size and choose clusters for a mix of workloads.","type":"scenario","a":"Match compute to workload:\n• BI/SQL — serverless SQL warehouse with autoscaling and IWM; size by concurrency, not volume.\n• ETL/batch — Job clusters (auto-terminate), memory-optimized VMs for joins/aggregations, autoscaling, Spot for cost; Photon for speed.\n• Streaming — always-on job clusters; size for throughput + state; consider serverless.\n• ML — GPU clusters, single-node for driver-heavy work.\n• Guidance — prefer fewer larger workers over many small (less shuffle overhead); iterate with monitoring; use instance pools for warm start; enforce limits via cluster policies.\nTrade-off: performance vs cost vs concurrency."},{"q":"How do you build observability for pipelines and the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Workload monitoring — Databricks Workflows run history, alerts, repair-and-rerun; query history; Spark UI for bottlenecks.\n• System tables — system.billing.usage (cost), system.access.audit (activity), system.lakeflow.* (jobs), lineage — queryable via SQL for dashboards.\n• Logging — diagnostic logs + cluster logs to ADLS/Log Analytics; KQL queries and alerts.\n• DQ + freshness — expectation metrics, SLA/freshness checks, alerting.\n• Lineage — Unity Catalog column-level lineage to trace downstream impact.\nGoal: detect, diagnose, and alert fast; make cost and reliability visible to owners."},{"q":"What is Delta Sharing and where would an architect use it?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Sharing is an open protocol to share live data (and AI assets) across organizations, clouds, or business units WITHOUT copying it.\n• Provider publishes a share (subset of tables/volumes); recipients read via the share — Databricks-to-Databricks or open recipients (Power BI, pandas).\n• Governance and audit remain with the provider.\nArchitect use cases: B2B data exchange, cross-region/cross-org sharing, marketplace, or when copying data is undesirable/expensive. Complements UC (grants) for internal access."},{"q":"How do you present and defend an architecture decision to stakeholders?","type":"concept","a":"Use an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) mindset:\n• Context — the business problem and constraints (budget, SLA, compliance, scale).\n• Options — 2–3 realistic alternatives with trade-offs.\n• Decision + rationale — what you chose and why, tied to business outcomes.\n• Consequences — cost, performance, risk, and what you'd revisit.\n• Communicate in business terms (cost, time-to-market, risk), not just tech; use diagrams.\n• Be open to challenge; show you weighed alternatives. Interviewers want structured trade-off thinking."},{"q":"Why do you want to be a Databricks/Azure Data Architect, and how do you keep growing?","type":"concept","a":"• Motivation — you enjoy end-to-end problem solving, designing scalable platforms, and multiplying impact across teams (not just writing code).\n• Evidence — examples where you designed, optimized, or governed a platform and delivered measurable outcomes.\n• Growth — certifications (Databricks Data Engineer/Architect, DP-203/DP-700), hands-on labs, keeping up with features (Lakeflow, Declarative Pipelines, Unity Catalog, serverless, Fabric), community, mentoring.\n• Value — you bridge engineering and business, reduce cost/risk, and raise the team's standards.\nKeep it concrete and forward-looking."},{"q":"Why par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":" Parallel processing  Higher throughput  Scalability","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"}]},{"id":"spark","name":"Apache Spark & Databricks Fundamentals","icon":"⚡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the Apache Spark architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Spark uses a master-worker (driver-executor) model:\n• Driver — runs the user code, builds the DAG (logical plan), creates SparkContext, schedules tasks, collects results.\n• Cluster Manager — allocates resources (Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos). In Databricks it's managed.\n• Executors — JVM processes on worker nodes that run tasks and cache partitions in memory/disk.\n• Task — smallest unit of work (one per partition).\n• Stage — set of tasks with no shuffle boundary. Job — triggered by an action.\nFlow: action → Driver → DAG Scheduler splits into stages at shuffles → Task Scheduler sends tasks to executors → results returned."},{"q":"Explain DAG, Jobs, Stages, and Tasks in Spark.","type":"concept","a":"• DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) — the logical execution plan of transformations; built lazily.\n• Job — created when an action is called; a DAG broken at shuffle boundaries.\n• Stage — a set of transformations that can run without a shuffle (narrow pipeline); shuffle starts a new stage.\n• Task — one task per partition per stage; executes on an executor core.\nSo: 1 action → 1+ Jobs → each Job has Stages → each Stage has Tasks (= number of partitions)."},{"q":"What is the difference between narrow and wide transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow — each input partition contributes to exactly one output partition; no shuffle. Examples: map, filter, select, withColumn, union, mapPartitions. Data stays local → fast.\n• Wide — data from many input partitions must be reorganized into output partitions via a shuffle. Examples: groupBy/aggregate, join (non-broadcast), distinct, repartition, orderBy, sort.\nRule: wide transformations cause shuffles → new stages → usually the performance bottleneck."},{"q":"What is lazy evaluation in Spark and why is it useful?","type":"concept","a":"Spark does not execute transformations immediately; it records them and builds a logical plan (DAG). Execution happens only when an action is called.\n• Transformations (lazy): filter, select, join, groupBy, withColumn.\n• Actions (trigger execution): count, collect, show, write, save, take.\nBenefits: the Catalyst optimizer can optimize the whole plan (predicate pushdown, column pruning, join reordering), fewer passes over data, and avoids unnecessary computation."},{"q":"On what basis does Spark create stages?","type":"concept","a":"Stages are split at shuffle boundaries.\n• A stage = a sequence of narrow transformations that can be pipelined together without moving data across the network.\n• A new stage begins whenever a wide transformation (shuffle) is needed — e.g., groupBy, sort, non-broadcast join, repartition.\n• DAG Scheduler creates stages; each stage is submitted as a set of tasks (one per partition) to the Task Scheduler.\nMinimizing shuffles (fewer stages) is a key optimization goal."},{"q":"What is the Catalyst optimizer and how does it optimize queries?","type":"concept","a":"Catalyst is Spark's rule-based query optimizer for SQL/DataFrames. Phases:\n1. Analysis — resolves columns/tables and validates schema.\n2. Logical optimization — rule-based: predicate pushdown, constant folding, column pruning, filter reordering, join reorder.\n3. Physical planning — chooses join strategy (broadcast/sort-merge) and operators based on cost/stats.\n4. Code generation — Whole-Stage CodeGen / Tungsten emits optimized JVM bytecode.\nInspect with df.explain(True) or explain('formatted'). Using built-in functions (not UDFs) lets Catalyst optimize them."},{"q":"What is Tungsten in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"Tungsten is Spark's execution engine focused on CPU/memory efficiency:\n• Off-heap binary memory management (reduces GC overhead).\n• Cache-aware computation and explicit memory layout.\n• Whole-Stage Code Generation — fuses operators into a single optimized function, avoiding virtual function calls.\nResult: much faster execution and lower memory usage than the original Spark engine."},{"q":"What is the difference between RDD, DataFrame, and Dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD — low-level, untyped distributed collection; full control, no schema; no Catalyst optimization; slowest but most flexible.\n• DataFrame — distributed table with named columns + schema; runs through Catalyst + Tungsten; best for structured data (Python/Scala/Java/R).\n• Dataset — typed JVM objects (Scala/Java only); compile-time type safety + Catalyst; Python has no Dataset (uses DataFrame).\nPrefer DataFrames/Datasets over RDDs for performance; use RDDs only for fine-grained control."},{"q":"What are the main Databricks cluster types?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose (interactive) — development, notebooks, ad-hoc; shared; billed while running.\n• Job (automated) — created for a job, terminates on completion; cheapest for production pipelines.\n• High Concurrency — many concurrent users/BI; user isolation; SQL/Python/R (not Scala).\n• Single Node — driver only, no workers; dev/testing/small data.\n• SQL Warehouse — optimized for Databricks SQL/BI (Power BI, Tableau); serverless/pro/classic.\n• Serverless — fully managed compute, no cluster config.\nBest practice: Job clusters for production, All-Purpose for dev, SQL Warehouse for BI."},{"q":"Difference between All-Purpose Cluster and Job Cluster?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose: manually started, stays up (auto-terminate 30–120 min), shared by multiple users/notebooks, warm, higher cost. For interactive development.\n• Job: created automatically from job config, fresh environment each run, isolated, terminated after completion (no idle cost). For scheduled/production jobs.\nUse Job clusters for all production pipelines to control cost and ensure clean, isolated runs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"scenario","a":"• No distributed processing — one machine is both driver and worker.\n• Driver bottleneck — it coordinates AND executes, so it's overloaded.\n• Memory pressure — large datasets/joins can exceed node memory (OOM/spill).\n• CPU saturation — all tasks compete for the same 16 cores; long runtimes.\n• No fault tolerance — if the node fails, the whole workload fails.\nSuitable only for dev/testing/small data — not for production ETL."},{"q":"What is the Databricks Runtime (DBR)?","type":"concept","a":"DBR is Databricks' optimized, curated software stack for clusters. It bundles:\n• Apache Spark (specific version), Delta Lake, and performance optimizations (Photon).\n• Language runtimes: Python, Scala, Java, R, SQL.\n• Optimized libraries and security patches.\nVariants: Standard, ML, GPU, Photon, and LTS (Long-Term Support). Prefer LTS in production for stability/longer support."},{"q":"Difference between repartition and coalesce?","type":"concept","a":"• repartition(n) — full shuffle; can increase OR decrease partitions; even distribution; expensive. Use to fix skew or before heavy joins/aggregations.\n• coalesce(n) — reduces partitions WITHOUT a full shuffle (merges local partitions); cheap; used after filtering to reduce small files. Cannot increase partitions.\n• repartition(n, col) — hash-partition by column to co-locate join keys.\nExample: df.repartition(200, 'customer_id'); df.coalesce(4)."},{"q":"What is the default partition size and default number of shuffle partitions?","type":"concept","a":"• Target partition/file size in Spark/Delta — ~128 MB (block/file size).\n• Default shuffle partitions — spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200.\n• Default parallelism — equals the number of executor cores.\nTune shuffle partitions to data size (too few → huge tasks/OOM; too many → tiny tasks/overhead). AQE can coalesce partitions automatically at runtime."},{"q":"Difference between cache() and persist()?","type":"concept","a":"• cache() — stores the DataFrame/RDD using MEMORY_AND_DISK storage level; lazy (materializes on first action).\n• persist(level) — same but you choose the storage level: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK_SER, etc.\nBoth are lazy transformations; trigger with an action (count/write). Use when a DataFrame is reused 2+ times (iterative ML, repeated joins).\nAlways unpersist() when done to free memory."},{"q":"What is a broadcast join and when is it used?","type":"concept","a":"A broadcast join replicates a SMALL table to every executor so the large table doesn't need to be shuffled.\n• Use when one side is small (< ~10 MB default autoBroadcastJoinThreshold, up to ~200 MB feasible).\n• Eliminates the shuffle of the big table → big performance gain and also mitigates skew.\nPySpark: large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id'); SQL: /*+ BROADCAST(small) */.\nTypical for joining a fact with a small lookup/dimension table."},{"q":"What is data skew and how do you detect and fix it?","type":"concept","a":"Skew = uneven data distribution across partitions so a few tasks process far more data (stragglers).\nDetect: Spark UI shows one/few tasks much longer, uneven shuffle read sizes, one executor overloaded while others idle.\nFix:\n• Salting — add a random prefix/suffix to the skewed key to spread it.\n• Enable AQE skew join (spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true) — splits skewed partitions automatically.\n• Broadcast join for the small side.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key; filter early.\n• Isolate and process the hot key separately.","code":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\nspark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true')\n# Salting\ndf = df.withColumn('salt', (F.rand()*10).cast('int'))\ndf = df.withColumn('salted_key', F.concat(col('key'), F.lit('_'), col('salt')))"},{"q":"What is bucketing and when would you use it?","type":"concept","a":"Bucketing pre-shuffles data into a fixed number of buckets by hash of a column, persisted with the table (e.g., bucketBy(50,'customer_id').saveAsTable(...)).\n• Benefit: repeated joins/aggregations on the bucketed key avoid shuffles (sort-merge join becomes shuffle-free).\n• Differs from partitioning: partitioning creates directories (low cardinality); bucketing creates fixed files (higher cardinality, even distribution).\nUse for large, frequently joined tables on the same key. In Databricks, Liquid Clustering is now often preferred over manual bucketing/partitioning."},{"q":"What is a shuffle and how do you reduce it?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffle = redistribution of data across executors (network + disk I/O) for wide transformations like join, groupBy, distinct, sort.\nReduce it by: broadcast joins for small tables, filtering/aggregating before joins, bucketing on join keys, using map-side partial aggregation (reduceByKey over groupByKey), avoiding unnecessary repartition/distinct, and enabling AQE.\nWatch shuffle read/write metrics in the Spark UI — it's usually the top cost."},{"q":"What is Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) and its benefits?","type":"concept","a":"AQE re-optimizes the physical plan at runtime using actual statistics gathered after each stage. Benefits:\n• Dynamically coalesces shuffle partitions (reduces tiny tasks).\n• Switches join strategies (e.g., sort-merge → broadcast) based on actual sizes.\n• Detects and splits skewed partitions (skew join handling).\nEnable: spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true (plus skewJoin + coalescePartitions). Default on in Spark 3.x/Databricks runtime."},{"q":"What is Photon?","type":"concept","a":"Photon is Databricks' vectorized C++ query engine. It replaces the JVM execution for many SQL/DataFrame operators with native, columnar, vectorized execution.\n• Delivers ~3–10x speedups for SQL/scan/aggregation/join-heavy workloads.\n• Drop-in: enable in cluster config; no code changes.\n• Best for SQL and Delta workloads; some UDF-heavy or non-supported ops fall back to the JVM."},{"q":"How do you use the Spark UI to troubleshoot performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Jobs/Stages tabs — find slow stages; check task durations and counts.\n• Tasks — spot skew (one task far longer), spill to disk, retries.\n• SQL/DataFrame tab — inspect the physical plan, exchanges (shuffles), and per-operator metrics.\n• Storage tab — what's cached and its size.\n• Executors tab — memory/GC, shuffle read/write, disk spill.\nUse these to decide: broadcast, repartition, AQE, more partitions, or bigger cluster."},{"q":"How do you decide the size of a Databricks cluster?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster sizing depends on data volume, number of files, transformation complexity, SLA, and concurrency — it can't be predicted, so iterate:\n1. Start with a medium cluster (2–8 nodes) matching the workload class (F/DSv2 for ETL, DSv2 for ML, L for analytics).\n2. Run end-to-end on representative data; measure CPU, memory, I/O, shuffle.\n3. Remove bottlenecks: CPU-bound → add nodes/cores; network-bound → fewer bigger SSD machines; disk/spill-bound → more memory.\n4. Repeat 3–4 times on increasing data sizes; prefer fewer big instances over many small (1 executor/machine) for batch ETL, enable autoscaling.\nFirst guess: scale linearly from a small test (e.g., 5 nodes for 100 TB ≈ 50 nodes for 1 PB), then refine."},{"q":"What are the key cluster configuration options in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster mode: Single Node / Standard / High Concurrency (or Serverless).\n• Runtime version (prefer LTS, e.g., 15.4 LTS).\n• Driver + worker VM types (workers can be sized independently).\n• Worker count with autoscaling (min/max) or fixed.\n• Photon on/off; instance pools for fast start.\n• Auto-termination (idle timeout).\n• Access mode / Unity Catalog mode (Single user, Shared, No isolation).\n• Cluster policies, tags, init scripts, and Spark configs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks job that normally finishes quickly is now taking far longer. How do you investigate?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the job run → Spark UI; find the slow stage and inspect task times.\n• Check for data skew (one task much longer), shuffle size, and spill-to-disk in the Tasks/Executors tabs.\n• Check input data volume/growth and partition count; look for small-file explosion.\n• Check cluster health — resource contention (shared cluster), GC, executor loss, autoscaling lag.\n• Compare the current physical plan vs a known-good run; check join strategy changes (broadcast vs sort-merge).\n• Look for recently changed code (new joins/UDFs), source schema changes, or upstream backlog.\n• Remedy: tune partitions, enable AQE/Photon, broadcast small tables, salt skewed keys, scale cluster, OPTIMIZE tables."},{"q":"What is the difference between Spark SQL, DataFrame API, and RDD API?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD API — lowest level, no schema, no Catalyst; maximum control but least optimization.\n• DataFrame API — structured, schema-aware, optimized by Catalyst/Tungsten; expressive transforms.\n• Spark SQL — declarative SQL strings over the same engine (also Catalyst-optimized); easy for analysts and interop.\nAll compile to the same optimized plan; use DataFrames/SQL for day-to-day, RDDs only for niche low-level needs."},{"q":"Why is PySpark used and how does it interface with the JVM?","type":"concept","a":"PySpark is Spark's Python API. Spark is written in Scala and runs on the JVM; PySpark uses Py4J to let Python code call JVM objects.\n• Benefits: Python ecosystem (pandas, ML libs), ease of use, distributed processing across a cluster.\n• Note: Python UDFs incur serialization overhead (row-by-row), so prefer Spark built-in functions / pandas UDFs (vectorized) for performance.\n• Needs Java installed alongside Python and Spark for local development."},{"q":"What is SCD Type 1? How will you implement it?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 1 overwrites exis(cid:415)ng records and does not preserve history\n• Example: Old 101 John Chennai New 101 John Bangalore Result 101 John Bangalore History is lost\n• PySpark MERGE Example Requirement: If Selling_Period is NULL or 0, do not insert or update. from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"customer\") ( target.alias(\"t\") .merge( source.alias(\"s\"), \"t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID\" ) .whenMatchedUpdate( condi(cid:415)on=\"s.Selling_Period IS NOT NULL AND s.Selling_Period <> 0\", set={ \"CustomerName\":\"s.CustomerName\", \"City\":\"s.City\", \"Selling_Period\":\"s.Selling_Period\" } ) .whenNotMatchedInsert( condi(cid:415)on=\"s.Selling_Period IS NOT NULL AND s.Selling_Period <> 0\", values={ \"CustomerID\":\"s.CustomerID\", \"CustomerName\":\"s.CustomerName\", \"City\":\"s.City\", \"Selling_Period\":\"s.Selling_Period\" } ) .execute() This sa(cid:415)sfies the condi(cid:415)on that records with Selling_Period equal to NULL or 0 are ignored for both updates and inserts","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Small Table? How does Auto Loader know which files are already processed?","type":"concept","a":"• Small Table A small table is a table that is small enough to fit in the memory of each executor\n• Spark uses Broadcast Join for small tables\n• Example:  Customer table = 20 MB  Sales table = 500 GB Spark broadcasts the customer table to all workers instead of shuffling the huge sales table. from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast sales.join(broadcast(customers), \"customer_id\") How Auto Loader tracks processed files Auto Loader maintains a checkpoint\n• The checkpoint stores metadata about:  Files already processed  Offsets  Schema informa(cid:415)on  Progress When new files arrive:","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning? How does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning organizes data into separate folders based on one or more columns\n• Example: Sales Country=USA Country=India Country=Canada df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"Country\").saveAsTable(\"sales\") Benefits Suppose the table has 1 TB of data\n• Query: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE Country='India';\n• Without par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning:  Spark scans the en(cid:415)re 1 TB\n• With par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning:  Spark scans only the Country=India par(cid:415)(cid:415)on, greatly reducing I/O and improving performance\n• Choose par(cid:415)(cid:415)on columns that:  Are frequently used in filters.  Have moderate cardinality (not too few or too many dis(cid:415)nct values)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Spark","type":"concept","a":"A. Spark it is Inmemory parallel space and it will use ram inmemory for read & write operations","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Map reduce and Spark","type":"concept","a":"• S.No\n• Map Reduce Spark","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is having a very slow speed as It is much faster than Map Reduce.","type":"concept","a":"compared to Apache Spark.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It actually needs other queries to It has Spark SQL as its very own query","type":"concept","a":"perform the task. language.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the architecture of spark","type":"concept","a":"➢ Driver Node ➢ Cluster Manager ➢ Worker Node: In This, there is a Executor and Task","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what are different languages supported in Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Python or Pyspark ➢ Scala ➢ Sql ➢ R-Language 9.What is DAG A\n• A directed acyclic graph (DAG) is a conceptual representation of a series of activities\n• The order of the activities is depicted by a graph, which is visually presented as a set of circles, each one representing an activity, some of which are connected by lines, which represent the flow from one activity to another","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Languages using in your project","type":"concept","a":"A. Pyspark and Scala","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is RDD, Data frame, Data Set","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ RDD: A Resilient Distributed Dataset (RDD), the basic abstraction in Spark\n• Represents an immutable, partitioned collection of elements that can be operated on in parallel. ➢ Data Frame: A Data Frame is equivalent to a relational table in Spark SQL, and can be created using various functions ➢ Dataset: A Dataset is a distributed collection of data\n• Dataset is a new interface added in Spark 1.6 that provides the benefits of RDDs","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to convert RDD into Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"• Convert Spark RDD to Data Frame and Dataset as these provide more advantages over RDD\n• For instance, Data Frame is a distributed collection of data organized into named columns similar to Database tables and provides optimization and performance improvement\n• Syntax: val dfFromRDD1 = rdd.toDF() dfFromRDD1.printSchema()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Transformation and Action","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Transformation: Transformation refers to the operation applied on a RDD to create new RDD\n• Filter, groupBy and map are the examples of transformations. ➢ Actions: Actions refer to an operation which also applies on RDD, that instructs Spark to perform computation and send the result back to driver\n• Page | 13","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to create Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"A. # Create DataFrame. df = pd.DataFrame(data) # Print the output. print(df)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to read Data from csv file to create data frame","type":"concept","a":"A. usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to remove duplicate values from Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. dropdf = df.dropDuplicates([\"department\",\"salary\"]) print(\"Distinct count of department & salary :\"+ str(dropdf.count()))","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to add new column into Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to write sql commands on Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. %sql select * from user","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to select only few columns from dataframe","type":"concept","a":"• In PySpark we can select columns using the select() function\n• The select() function allows us to select single or multiple columns in different formats\n• Syntax: df.select(\"firstname\",\"lastname\").show() df.select(df.firstname,df.lastname).show() df.select(df[\"firstname\"],df[\"lastname\"]).show() #By using col() function from pyspark.sql.functions import col df.select(col(\"firstname\"),col(\"lastname\")).show() #Select columns by regular expression df.select(df.colRegex(\"`^.*name*`\")).show()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to drop columns from dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. \"\" import col is required \"\"\" df.drop(col(\"firstname\")) \\ .printSchema() Page | 14","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is diff between repartition and coalesce","type":"concept","a":"A. The repartition() the number of partitions can be increased/decreased, but with coalesce() the number of partitions can only be decreased and The repartition algorithm does a full shuffle of the data and creates equal sized partitions of data. coalesce combines existing partitions to avoid a full shuffle.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is caching and persistence","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ A\n• Caching or persistence are optimization techniques for (iterative and interactive) Spark computations\n• They help saving interim partial results so they can be reused in subsequent stages\n• These interim results as RDDs are thus kept in memory (default) or more solid storage like disk and/or replicated. ➢ These functions can be used to adjust the storage level of a RDD\n• When freeing up memory, Spark will use the storage level identifier to decide which partitions should be kept\n• The parameter less variants persist() and cache() are just abbreviations","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what are broad cast variables and broadcast joins","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Broadcast variables allow the programmer to keep a read-only variable cached on each machine rather than shipping a copy of it with tasks. ➢ Broadcast join is an important part of Spark SQL's execution engine\n• When used, it performs a join on two relations by first broadcasting the smaller one to all Spark executors, then evaluating the join criteria with each executor's partitions of the other relation","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Task Dura(cid:415)on Imbalance:","type":"concept","a":"➢ Use the Spark UI (h(cid:425)p://<spark-driver>:4040) → Stages tab. ➢ Look for stages where a few tasks take significantly longer than others. ➢ If most tasks finish in seconds while a few take minutes, it strongly indicates data skew.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Skewed Shuffle Par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:","type":"concept","a":"➢ Check for stages involving wide transforma(cid:415)ons like groupByKey(), join(), or reduceByKey(). ➢ Under the \"Tasks\" tab, look at the input size and run (cid:415)me of each task. ➢ Skewed keys will show dispropor(cid:415)onate data sizes for specific par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Logs and Metrics:","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Enable detailed logging (especially for jobs involving joins or aggrega(cid:415)ons). ➢ Use .rdd.getNumPar(cid:415)(cid:415)ons() or .rdd.glom().map(len).collect() to inspect distribu(cid:415)on manually\n• How to Resolve Data Skew in PySpark","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid groupByKey()","type":"concept","a":"➢Prefer reduceByKey() or aggregateByKey() which are more efficient and minimizeshuffles.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Skew Join Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on (in Spark 3+)","type":"concept","a":"➢If enabled, Spark can automa(cid:415)cally handle skew joins using adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on (AQE).","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"read employees.csv (schema same as employees table) into a dataframe df_emp","type":"concept","a":"add a column grade in df_emp, if salary > 10000 then A else B from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import when, col # Read CSV into DataFrame df_emp = spark.read \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"header\", \"true\") \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", \"true\") \\ .csv(\"employees.csv\") # Add grade column df_emp = df_emp.withColumn( \"grade\", when(col(\"salary\") > 10000, \"A\").otherwise(\"B\") )","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are the read modes available in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• When reading data, Spark supports different modes for handling malformed records\n• Mode Descrip(cid:415)on PERMISSIVE (Default) Invalid records are set to null, malformed records are captured\n• DROPMALFORMED Drops malformed records\n• FAILFAST Stops execu(cid:415)on if a malformed record is found\n• Example: df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"mode\", \"PERMISSIVE\").csv(\"/input/data.csv\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How are Jobs, Stages, and Tasks created in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Execu(cid:415)on hierarchy: Applica(cid:415)on │ ▼ Job │ ▼ Stage │ ▼ Task  Applica(cid:415)on: Spark program.  Job: Created when an ac(cid:415)on (show(), count(), collect(), write()) is executed.  Stage: Created based on shuffle boundaries.  Task: Smallest execu(cid:415)on unit, one task per par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Example: df = spark.read.csv(\"sales.csv\") df.filter(\"amount>100\").groupBy(\"city\").sum().show()  show() → 1 Job  groupBy() causes a shuffle → mul(cid:415)ple stages  Each par(cid:415)(cid:415)on becomes a task","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Read CSV, create DataFrame, and write Delta to ADLS","type":"concept","a":"df = spark.read \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True) \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", True) \\ .csv(\"abfss://raw@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/customer.csv\") Write to Delta: df.write \\ .format(\"delta\") \\ .mode(\"overwrite\") \\ .save(\"abfss://silver@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/customer\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Spark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"Driver Program │ Cluster Manager │ Executors │ Tasks Driver  Creates SparkSession  Creates DAG  Schedules jobs  Coordinates executors Cluster Manager  Allocates resources Examples:  Standalone  YARN  Kubernetes Executors  Execute tasks  Store cached data  Return results Worker Nodes Machines where executors run.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Managed and External Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Managed Table External Table Spark manages data and metadata Spark manages only metadata Dropping table deletes data Dropping table keeps data Default storage loca(cid:415)on User specifies loca(cid:415)on Suitable for internal data Suitable for shared/external data Example: Managed: CREATE TABLE sales (...);\n• External: CREATE TABLE sales USING DELTA LOCATION '/mnt/sales';","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Spark Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques","type":"concept","a":"• Common op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques include:  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning – Improve parallel processing.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on vs\n• Coalesce – Increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons efficiently.  Cache/Persist – Reuse frequently accessed DataFrames.  Broadcast Join – Broadcast small tables to avoid shuffles.  Predicate Pushdown – Filter data at the source.  Column Pruning – Read only required columns.  Avoid collect() – Prevent moving large datasets to the driver.  Use Delta Lake – Leverage transac(cid:415)on support and performance op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons.  Op(cid:415)mize file sizes – Avoid too many small files.  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) – Dynamically op(cid:415)mize joins and shuffle par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons at run(cid:415)me.  Op(cid:415)mize Z-Ordering (Delta Lake) – Improve data skipping for frequently filtered columns.  Use appropriate file formats – Prefer Parquet or Delta over CSV for analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Check Spark UI","type":"concept","a":" Iden(cid:415)fy slow stages / skewed tasks","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Check shuffle","type":"concept","a":"•  Reduce wide transforma(cid:415)ons (groupBy, join) 4\n• Use caching df.cache()","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Use AQE op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":" Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain PySpark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"(cid:44934) Components:  Driver o Creates SparkContext o Converts code into DAG  Cluster Manager o Allocates resources (YARN / Databricks)  Executors o Run tasks on par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons (cid:44934) Flow:","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Code → Driver","type":"concept","a":"2. DAG created","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Stages formed","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Tasks sent to executors","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Transformation in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• In Apache Spark (used by Databricks), every transformation is either Narrow or Wide\n• This classification directly affects how fast your pipeline runs\n• Narrow Transformations Definition: Each partition of input produces exactly one partition of output\n• No data movement between nodes\n• Fast and efficient\n• Examples: • filter() / where() — Remove rows based on a condition • select() — Choose specific columns • map() — Apply a function to each row • withColumn() — Add or modify a column • union() — Combine two DataFrames with same schema # Narrow transformation example — no shuffle at all from pyspark.sql.functions import col, upper # filter: each partition processed independently, no movement df_filtered = df.filter(col('sales_amount') > 1000) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 1 # select: columns picked per partition, no shuffle df_small = df_filtered.select('customer_id','region','sales_amount') # withColumn: computed row-by-row within the same partition df_final = df_small.withColumn('region_upper', upper(col('region'))) Wide Transformations Definition: Data from multiple partitions is combined and redistributed across nodes\n• Causes a SHUFFLE — most expensive operation in Spark\n• Creates a new stage boundary in the execution plan\n• Examples: • groupBy() + agg() — Group data across all partitions • join() — Match rows from two separate DataFrames • distinct() — Remove duplicates (needs to see all rows globally) • orderBy() / sort() — Sort across all partitions globally • repartition() — Redistribute into new number of partitions # Wide transformation example — SHUFFLE happens here from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, count # groupBy: Spark must move all rows with same 'region' # to the same partition before aggregating df_summary = df.groupBy('region').agg( sum('sales_amount').alias('total_sales'), count('customer_id').alias('num_customers') ) # join: both DataFrames shuffled to align matching keys df_joined = df_sales.join(df_customers, on='customer_id', how='inner') Side-by-Side Comparison Feature Narrow Transformation Wide Transformation Data Movement No shuffle — stays in same Full shuffle — data moves across partition all nodes Performance Very fast Slower — network + disk I/O overhead Examples filter, select, map, withColumn, groupBy, join, distinct, orderBy, union repartition Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 2 Stage Boundary No — same Spark stage Yes — creates a new stage When to use Column ops, filtering, cleaning Aggregations, joins, deduplication Interview Tip: Say in interview: 'Narrow transformations stay within a single partition — no shuffle\n• Wide transformations require shuffling data across the network and always create a stage boundary\n• We minimize wide transformations and use broadcast joins for small lookup tables to avoid expensive shuffles.' Q3 Coalesce vs Repartition","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Why Do Partitions Matter?","type":"concept","a":"• Spark splits data into partitions and processes them in parallel\n• Too many small partitions = scheduler overhead\n• Too few large partitions = memory pressure and slow tasks\n• Both coalesce and repartition change the partition count, but very differently. coalesce(n) Key Facts: Reduces partitions by merging existing ones together\n• Does NOT trigger a full shuffle — very efficient\n• Can only REDUCE partition count, not increase it\n• May result in slightly uneven partition sizes. # Before: 200 partitions (created from large file read) print(df.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 200 # coalesce: merge down to 10 — no shuffle needed df_coalesced = df.coalesce(10) print(df_coalesced.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 10 # Best use case: reduce output file count before writing df_coalesced.write.mode('overwrite').parquet('/mnt/output/sales/') # Writes 10 parquet files instead of 200 tiny ones # WARNING: coalesce does not rebalance — some partitions # may be larger than others (slight skew is OK here) repartition(n) Key Facts: Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 3 Redistributes data EVENLY across n new partitions\n• Always triggers a full shuffle — more expensive\n• Can both INCREASE and DECREASE partition count\n• Can also partition by a specific column (hash partitioning). # repartition: even redistribution — triggers full shuffle df_rep = df.repartition(50) print(df_rep.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 50 # repartition by column — all rows with same key go to same partition # Great before a join to avoid double-shuffle df_by_region = df.repartition(20, 'region') # Pre-partition both sides of a join on the join key: df_sales_r = df_sales.repartition(50, 'customer_id') df_cust_r = df_customers.repartition(50, 'customer_id') df_joined = df_sales_r.join(df_cust_r, 'customer_id') # Result: join is much faster — data already co-located!","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"When to Use Which?","type":"concept","a":"Scenario Use Reduce partitions before writing output coalesce — no shuffle, fast Increase partitions for more parallelism repartition — only option that can increase Fix data skew (uneven partition sizes) repartition — even redistribution Pre-partition before a join on a key repartition(n, 'key') — co-locate data After heavy filter reduced data volume coalesce — fewer tasks, no shuffle Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'coalesce reduces partitions without a shuffle — I use it before writing output files. repartition does a full shuffle to evenly redistribute data — I use it when I need to increase parallelism, fix data skew, or co-locate data by a join key.' Q4 Schema Enforcement vs Schema Evolution","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Spark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Apache Spark uses a Master-Worker (Driver-Executor) distributed computing model with the following components: Component Role Key Detail Driver Runs user code; builds DAG;\n• Creates SparkContext; schedules coordinates execution tasks Cluster Manager Resource allocation across nodes Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos Executor Runs tasks; caches partitions in JVM processes on worker nodes memory or disk Task Smallest unit of work (one per Sent from Task Scheduler to Executors partition) Stage Set of tasks with no shuffle boundary Created by DAG Scheduler Job Triggered by an action (collect, write, Broken into Stages count) DAG Logical execution plan Built lazily; executed only on action Execution Flow • User calls an action (df.write(), df.count()) • Driver sends job to DAG Scheduler • DAG Scheduler splits into Stages at shuffle boundaries • Task Scheduler sends Tasks to Executors • Executors run tasks, return results to Driver Key concept: Lazy Evaluation — transformations (filter, select, join) build the DAG but do NOT execute\n• Only when an action is called does Spark execute the full optimized plan through the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten engine\n• Page 1 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Join","type":"concept","a":"• Broadcast small tables (under 200 MB) to avoid shuffle in joins result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"AQE — Adaptive Query Execution","type":"concept","a":"• Dynamically re-optimizes query plan at runtime based on actual data stats spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled', 'true')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Photon Engine","type":"concept","a":"• Enable Photon in Databricks cluster config — 3-10x faster SQL and Delta operations 6. Caching • Cache frequently reused DataFrames: df.cache() or df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Filter Pushdown — Apply filters early","type":"concept","a":"# Apply filter BEFORE join — reduces shuffle data df_filtered = df.filter(col('status') == 'ACTIVE') result = df_filtered.join(df_other, 'id')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tune Shuffle Partitions","type":"concept","a":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '200') # default 200; tune to data size","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• Technique How When to Use Broadcast Join broadcast(small_df) One table < 200 MB — eliminates shuffle Predicate Pushdown filter() before join/groupBy Reduce data early before expensive ops Column Pruning select() only needed columns Avoid reading unused columns from Parquet Caching df.cache() or df.persist() DataFrame reused 2+ times in same job Repartition df.repartition(n, col) Before heavy groupBy/join on skewed data Coalesce df.coalesce(n) Reduce partitions after filter (no shuffle) reduceByKey vs groupByKey reduceByKey does partial local Aggregation on RDDs — less shuffle aggregation AQE spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true Runtime query plan re-optimization Avoid UDFs Use built-in Spark functions UDFs bypass Catalyst optimizer Bucketing bucketBy(n, col).saveAsTable() Eliminate shuffle for repeated large joins from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast, col from pyspark.sql import functions as F Page 7 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide # 1\n• Broadcast join result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id') # 2\n• Filter early + column pruning df = df.filter(col('status')=='ACTIVE').select('id','name','amount') # 3\n• Cache for reuse df.cache() count1 = df.count() # triggers cache result = df.groupBy('dept').sum('salary') # 4\n• Coalesce (no shuffle) vs repartition (with shuffle) df_small = df.filter(col('year')==2024).coalesce(4) # after big filter df_even = df.repartition(200, col('customer_id')) # even distribution # 5\n• AQE + Photon config spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '200')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Remove / Handle Nulls Using PySpark","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Drop rows with any null","type":"concept","a":"Page 14 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide df_clean = df.dropna() # drop row if ANY column is null","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"External Tables vs Managed Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Aspect Managed Table External Table Data location Databricks/Spark manages the YOU specify the data location (ADLS/S3) storage location DROP TABLE behavior Deletes metadata AND the data Deletes only metadata — data files files remain Data ownership Owned by Databricks/Spark Owned by external storage (ADLS Gen2) Use case Temporary/intermediate tables;\n• Shared data accessed by multiple tools DW tables Schema evolution Managed by Databricks You manage schema;\n• Spark reads it Performance Slightly faster (local storage Depends on network/storage optimized) performance Typical format Delta (default in Databricks) Parquet, Delta, CSV, ORC, JSON Page 20 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Multi-tool access Databricks only (unless Unity Any tool that can read the storage path Catalog) Create Managed Table -- Managed Table (Databricks controls storage location) CREATE TABLE silver.customers ( customer_id INT, name STRING, email STRING, created_date DATE ); -- Or from DataFrame df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('silver.customers') -- Data stored at: /user/hive/warehouse/silver.db/customers/ Create External Table -- External Table (you specify the ADLS path) CREATE TABLE bronze.raw_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/'; -- Or from DataFrame df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/') -- Register as external table spark.sql(''' CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bronze.raw_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/' ''') -- DROP TABLE only removes catalog entry; files remain in ADLS DROP TABLE bronze.raw_orders; -- data still in ADLS! Critical difference: DROP TABLE on a Managed table permanently deletes your data files\n• Always use External Tables in production for data stored on ADLS — this protects your data from accidental deletion\n• Quick Reference — All 25 Topics # Topic Key Point 1 Spark Architecture Driver + Cluster Manager + Executors + DAG + Stages + Lazy Eval 2 CDC & CDF CDC=source change capture;\n• CDF=Delta row-level change feed (_change_type) 3 Query Optimization Broadcast, Z-ORDER, AQE, Photon, cache, filter early, partition 4 Parquet vs JSON Parquet=columnar+compressed+schema;\n• JSON=text+row+nested; use Parquet for analytics 5 Schedule Notebooks Databricks Jobs (Workflows) OR ADF Notebook Activity OR dbutils.notebook.run() Page 21 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide 6 SCD Types Type1=overwrite;\n• Type2=new row+expire;\n• Type3=add column;\n• Type2 most common 7 SCD SQL Code MERGE INTO for Type1; expire+insert pattern for Type2;\n• UPDATE prev column for Type3 8 PySpark Optimization Broadcast, filter early, cache, coalesce, repartition, avoid UDFs, AQE 9 Databricks Optimization OPTIMIZE+ZORDER, Liquid Cluster, Photon, Delta Cache, AQE, Materialized Views 10 ADLS Security Service Principal + Key Vault secrets + RBAC roles + ACLs + Private Endpoints 11 Load from External abfss:// direct read, Mount, Auto Loader, COPY INTO, External Location 12 Left Anti Join Returns LEFT rows with NO match in RIGHT; how='left_anti' 13 2nd Highest Salary dense_rank() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) WHERE rnk=2 14 Split Col3 into rows split(col, ',') + explode() in PySpark;\n• LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT()) in SQL 15 Notebook in Notebook dbutils.notebook.run() for isolated exec with params; %run to share scope 16 Validation Checks Schema, null, duplicate, type, range, referential integrity, count, business rules 17 Remove Nulls dropna(), fillna(), when().isNull(), forward fill with Window last() 18 Cluster Types All-Purpose(dev), Job(prod), Single Node, SQL Warehouse(BI), DLT(managed) 19 Store after Transform Delta tables in Bronze/Silver/Gold layers on ADLS partitioned by date 20 Load Data into ADB spark.read.jdbc(), spark.read.csv(), Auto Loader (cloudFiles), COPY INTO 21 ADLS Gen2 vs Blob Gen2=HNS+POSIX ACL+analytics optimized;","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Table Has 3 Columns But Client Sends Only 1","type":"concept","a":"When the source data has fewer columns than the target table, there are several approaches: Approach Description Insert with NULLs for Insert the available column; let missing columns default to NULL (if missing columns nullable) Provide DEFAULT values Define DEFAULT constraints on the missing columns in the table schema Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects mismatched schemas by default — schema Delta evolution must be enabled Transform before writing Add the missing columns with NULL or derived values in the DataFrame before writing Communicate with client Raise a data quality issue and request the complete dataset as per the agreed contract PySpark Example — Add Missing Columns from pyspark.sql.functions import lit # Client sent only 'emp_id'; table expects emp_id, emp_name, salary df = df.withColumn('emp_name', lit(None).cast('string')) \\ .withColumn('salary', lit(None).cast('integer'))","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark – Salary Classification","type":"concept","a":"Input Data 1, 'AAA', 500, 'HYD' 2, 'BBB', 100, 'CHI' 3, NULL, 1000, 'BANG' 4, 'DDD', NULL, 'KOL' 5, 'EEE', 2500, NULL PySpark Code from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, IntegerType, StringType spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SalaryClassification').getOrCreate() data = [ (1, 'AAA', 500, 'HYD'), (2, 'BBB', 100, 'CHI'), (3, None, 1000, 'BANG'), (4, 'DDD', None, 'KOL'), (5, 'EEE', 2500, None) ] schema = StructType([ StructField('emp_id', IntegerType(), True), StructField('emp_name', StringType(), True), StructField('salary', IntegerType(), True), StructField('location', StringType(), True) ]) df = spark.createDataFrame(data, schema) df = df.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary').isNull(), 'Unknown') .when(col('salary') < 500, 'Low') .when((col('salary') >= 1000) & Page 8 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A (col('salary') < 1500), 'Mid') .when(col('salary') >= 1500, 'High') .otherwise('No Grade') ) df.show() Expected Output emp_id emp_name salary location salary_grade 1 AAA 500 HYD No Grade (500 is not < 500 nor >= 1000) 2 BBB 100 CHI Low 3 NULL 1000 BANG Mid 4 DDD NULL KOL Unknown 5 EEE 2500 NULL High","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Why a Particular Job Takes Long – Causes & Solutions","type":"concept","a":"• Common Root Causes of Slow Jobs Issue Cause Impact Data Skew One partition has far more Most executors idle while one overloaded data than others executor runs for hours Shuffle Operations Wide transformations Network I/O and disk spill dominate job time (groupBy, join, distinct) cause expensive data shuffles Too Many Small Files Thousands of tiny Parquet Driver and executors spend excessive time on files instead of a few large file metadata lookups ones Insufficient Cluster Not enough cores/memory Tasks queue up;\n• GC pressure;\n• OOM errors Resources for the data volume Missing Partitioning Full table scan on every Reads entire dataset instead of pruning query irrelevant partitions Cartesian / Broadcast Large table joined without Generates massive intermediate datasets Join Issues broadcast hint or wrong join strategy UDF (Python) Usage Row-by-row Python UDFs Dramatically slower than native Spark SQL bypass Spark functions optimizations No Caching Recomputing the same Redundant reads and transformations repeated DataFrame multiple times for each action in a DAG Solutions & Optimizations Problem Solution Code / Config Data Skew Salting technique — df.withColumn('salt_key', (rand()*10).cast('int')) add random key prefix to distribute skewed key Data Skew Skew hint in Spark spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true') 3.x AQE Shuffle Enable Adaptive spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true') Query Execution (AQE) Shuffle Use broadcast join df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id') for small tables Small Files Run OPTIMIZE on OPTIMIZE tablename ZORDER BY (col) Delta tables Page 8 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Small Files Coalesce or df.coalesce(10).write.format('delta').save(path) repartition before write Missing Partition Partition table on filter df.write.partitionBy('year','month').save(path) Pruning column Python UDFs Replace with Spark Use F.col(), F.when(), Pandas UDF with @pandas_udf SQL / Pandas UDF No Caching Cache reused df.cache() or DataFrames df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Cluster Sizing Right-size cluster or Set min/max workers; use photon runtime for SQL-heavy enable autoscaling jobs Diagnostic Tools in Databricks • Spark UI -> Stages tab: identify slow stages and skew via task duration distribution • Ganglia Metrics: CPU, memory, network I/O per executor • Event Timeline: spot executor idle time • EXPLAIN plan: check query plan for missing partition pruning or cartesian joins Page 9 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"Partitioning and Caching – Concepts & Advantages","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partitioning is the process of dividing a large dataset into smaller, manageable chunks (partitions) based on the values of one or more columns\n• This allows Spark to skip reading irrelevant partitions during queries — a technique called partition pruning\n• Two Types of Partitioning Type Description When to Use Storage Partitioning Data physically written into folder When tables are large and frequently (Write) hierarchy by column value (e.g., filtered by specific columns (date, region, /year=2024/month=01/) dept) Spark Partitioning (In- Number of in-memory partitions Control parallelism; repartition before memory) that Spark distributes across wide transformations or writes executors Storage Partitioning – Code # Write partitioned Delta table df.write \\ .format('delta') \\ .partitionBy('year', 'month') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/sales/') # Query benefits from partition pruning df_filtered = spark.read.format('delta').load('/mnt/delta/sales/') \\ .filter('year = 2024 AND month = 6') # Spark reads ONLY year=2024/month=6 folder -- skips all others Spark In-Memory Partitioning – Code # Repartition -- full shuffle, use for even distribution df = df.repartition(200, 'department_id') # Coalesce -- no shuffle, only reduces partitions (for writing fewer files) df = df.coalesce(10) # Check current number of partitions print(df.rdd.getNumPartitions()) Advantages of Partitioning Advantage Explanation Partition Pruning Spark skips irrelevant partitions entirely — massive read reduction Page 14 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Parallel Processing Each partition processed by a separate task — full cluster parallelism Faster Queries WHERE/filter on partition column can reduce scan from TBs to MBs Efficient Writes Large data written in parallel across many executors Better Maintenance VACUUM, OPTIMIZE, and MERGE can target specific partitions Caching Caching (persisting) stores a DataFrame or RDD in memory (and optionally disk) so that it does not need to be recomputed from scratch each time it is used in subsequent actions within the same Spark session\n• Cache vs Persist Method Storage Level Description df.cache() MEMORY_AND_DISK Stores in memory; spills to disk if (default) memory runs out df.persist() Configurable Allows specifying exact storage level StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY RAM only Fastest; data lost if evicted (recomputed) StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK RAM + Disk Balanced — no recomputation on eviction StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY Disk only Useful for DataFrames too large for RAM StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY_SER Serialized RAM Less memory; slight CPU overhead to deserialize Caching – Code Example from pyspark import StorageLevel # Cache a frequently reused DataFrame df_clean = df.filter(df.salary > 0).dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) df_clean.cache() # or df_clean.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) # Trigger caching (lazy evaluation -- first action materialises the cache) df_clean.count() # Reuse -- reads from cache, not original source dept_summary = df_clean.groupBy('department').agg(F.avg('salary')) top_earners = df_clean.filter(df_clean.salary > 100000) # Always unpersist when done to free memory df_clean.unpersist() Page 15 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Advantages of Caching Advantage Explanation Eliminates Recomputation DataFrame computed once; reused many times without re-reading source Faster Iterative Algorithms ML training loops (10–100 iterations) benefit enormously from caching input data Reduces I/O Avoids repeated reads from slow storage (ADLS, S3) for intermediate results Speeds Up Interactive Notebook queries on the same DataFrame return results instantly after Analysis first cache Pipeline Optimization Cache after expensive joins/aggregations shared across multiple downstream steps Page 16 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is Compute in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Compute in Databricks refers to the cluster of virtual machines (VMs) used to run Spark jobs, notebooks, and SQL queries\n• It is the processing engine behind all workloads\n• Types of Compute in Databricks: • All-Purpose Clusters: Long-running, interactive clusters for notebooks and development\n• Can be shared among multiple users. • Job Clusters: Ephemeral clusters created for a specific job run and terminated automatically after completion — cost-efficient for production pipelines. • SQL Warehouses (formerly SQL Endpoints): Optimized for running SQL queries via Databricks SQL\n• Uses Photon engine for faster performance. • Instance Pools: Pre-warmed VMs to reduce cluster start time\n• Cluster Configuration Includes: • Driver node: Orchestrates the Spark job, maintains SparkContext. • Worker nodes: Execute the actual data processing tasks. • Autoscaling: Automatically adds/removes workers based on workload","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Cache and Persisting?","type":"concept","a":"Both cache() and persist() are used to store intermediate Spark DataFrames/RDDs in memory to avoid recomputation. cache(): • Shortcut for persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK). • Stores data in memory; if memory is insufficient, spills to disk. • Syntax: df.cache() persist(): • Provides fine-grained control over storage level. • Storage levels: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_ONLY_SER, OFF_HEAP, etc. • Syntax: df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) When to Use: • Use when a DataFrame is used multiple times in the same job — avoids re-reading from disk/source. • Always unpersist() after use to free memory: df.unpersist()","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Your Spark job is running slow. What will you check to identify the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• Step-by-step Diagnosis: • Check Spark UI: Review the Jobs, Stages, and Tasks tabs\n• Look for long-running stages, failed tasks, and skewed partitions. • Data Skew: Check if one partition has significantly more data than others (one task takes 10x longer)\n• Fix with salting or repartitioning. • Shuffles: Wide transformations like groupBy, join, and distinct trigger expensive shuffles\n• Check shuffle read/write sizes in Spark UI. • Number of Partitions: Too few = underutilized executors\n• Too many = overhead of task scheduling\n• Ideal: 2-4x the number of CPU cores. • Spill to Disk: Memory spill means executors don't have enough memory\n• Increase executor memory or reduce partition size. • Broadcast Joins: If joining a large table with a small table, ensure the small table is broadcast to avoid shuffle. • Data Format & Compression: Are you reading CSV instead of Parquet? CSV lacks predicate pushdown\n• Switch to Parquet/Delta. • Predicate Pushdown & Partition Pruning: Ensure filters are applied early and partition columns are used in WHERE clauses. • GC Overhead: High Garbage Collection time in executor logs indicates memory pressure. • Driver Bottleneck: If Driver is doing too much work (e.g., collect() on large data), it becomes a bottleneck","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Write PySpark code to get the above output using Window function?","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, max as spark_max from pyspark.sql.window import Window spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SalesMax').getOrCreate() # Sample Data data = [('cust1', '2023-12-01', 100), ('cust2', '2023-12-02', 150), ('cust1', '2023-12-03', 200), ('cust2', '2023-12-04', 250)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['customer_id', 'transaction_date', 'sales']) # Define Window partitioned by customer_id window_spec = Window.partitionBy('customer_id') # Apply max() over the window df_with_max = df.withColumn('sales_max', spark_max('sales').over(window_spec)) # Drop duplicates to get one row per customer result = df_with_max.select('customer_id', 'sales_max').distinct() result.show() # Alternative: Using groupBy (simpler if window not required) result_alt = df.groupBy('customer_id').agg(spark_max('sales').alias('sales_max')) result_alt.show()","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Date type using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_date, to_timestamp # Method 1: to_date() with format pattern df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_date(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd')) # Method 2: to_timestamp() if time component exists df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_timestamp(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss')) # Method 3: Using cast() df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', col('transaction_date').cast('date')) # Verify schema df.printSchema() df.show() Note: Always match the format string to the actual string format in your data\n• Mismatched format returns null values","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"customer_ids that are NOT in Sales table using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• This is a classic anti-join / left anti join scenario. from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('AntiJoin').getOrCreate() # Sample Data customers_data = [('cust1',), ('cust2',), ('cust3',), ('cust4',)] sales_data = [('cust1', '2023-12-01', 100), ('cust2', '2023-12-02', 150)] customers_df = spark.createDataFrame(customers_data, ['customer_id']) sales_df = spark.createDataFrame(sales_data, ['customer_id', 'date', 'sales']) # ---- Method 1: Left Anti Join (Most Efficient) ---- result = customers_df.join(sales_df, on='customer_id', how='left_anti') result.show() # Output: cust3, cust4 # ---- Method 2: Left Join + Filter Nulls ---- result2 = (customers_df .join(sales_df, on='customer_id', how='left') .filter(sales_df['customer_id'].isNull()) .select(customers_df['customer_id'])) result2.show() # ---- Method 3: Using SQL ---- customers_df.createOrReplaceTempView('customers') sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') result3 = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM sales) ''') result3.show() # ---- Method 4: subtract() on RDD level ---- result4 = customers_df.select('customer_id').subtract( sales_df.select('customer_id')) result4.show() Best Practice: Use Method 1 (left_anti join) — it is the most efficient and idiomatic PySpark approach\n• It avoids shuffling unnecessary data and clearly expresses the intent\n• End of Interview Q&A Document","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is shuffling. Why it is expensive?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffling & why expensive  Data movement between executors Expensive due to:  Network transfer  Disk I/O  Serializa(cid:415)on overhead","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Consider you are having 1TB and 10MB file. How will spark execute?","type":"concept","a":"1TB + 10MB file execu(cid:415)on  Spark splits 1TB into par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons  10MB file may cause small-file problem  Uneven task distribu(cid:415)on → performance issues","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Databricks Run(cid:415)me?","type":"concept","a":"Databricks Run(cid:415)me  Op(cid:415)mized Spark environment  Includes Spark + Delta Lake + ML libraries","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is UDF in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"UDF in Databricks  User Defined Func(cid:415)on for custom logic (cid:57523)(cid:57524)(cid:57525) slower than built-in Spark func(cid:415)ons","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"24. What is AQE?","type":"concept","a":"AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on)  Run(cid:415)me op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on in Spark  Improves joins, par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, skew handling","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Lazy Evalua(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Spark does not execute transforma(cid:415)ons immediately\n• Execu(cid:415)on starts only when an ac(cid:415)on (such as count() or show()) is called","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cache vs Persist","type":"concept","a":"• Cache df.cache() Persist df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Use persist() when you need a storage level other than the default\n• Spark Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques Interviewers o(cid:332)en ask you to explain at least 8–10 techniques\n• Cache df.cache() Persist df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Broadcast Join from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2),\"id\") Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(10) Coalesce df.coalesce(2) Filter Early df.filter(df.salary>50000) Select Required Columns df.select(\"id\",\"name\") Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Data df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"country\") Bucke(cid:415)ng df.write.bucketBy(10,\"id\") AQE spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.adap(cid:415)ve.enabled\",\"true\") Explain Plan df.explain(True) Join Strategies in Spark","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Hash Join","type":"concept","a":"Used when one table is small. large.join(broadcast(small),\"id\") No shuffle for the small table.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Sort Merge Join","type":"concept","a":"• Default join for large tables\n• Shuffle ↓ Sort ↓ Merge","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Shuffle Hash Join","type":"concept","a":" Shuffle both tables.  Build a hash table for the smaller par(cid:415)(cid:415)on.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Shuffle-and-Replicate Nested Loop Join","type":"concept","a":"Used for complex join condi(cid:415)ons when Spark cannot use a more efficient join strategy.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you handle data skew and optimize Spark transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Common techniques: Identify skewed keys  Analyze the data distribution using groupBy() and count().  Review the Spark UI to identify tasks that take significantly longer than others\n• Use Broadcast Joins  If one table is small (lookup or dimension table), broadcast it to all executors to avoid expensive shuffles. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast result = largeDF.join( broadcast(smallDF), \"CustomerID\", \"inner\") Repartition Data  Redistribute data evenly before joins or aggregations. df = df.repartition(200, \"CustomerID\") Apply Salting  For heavily skewed keys, add a random salt to spread records across multiple partitions, then remove the salt after processing\n• Enable Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)  AQE automatically detects skewed partitions and optimizes shuffle execution at runtime\n• Filter Early  Remove unnecessary rows before joins to reduce the amount of data shuffled\n• Partition Data Properly  Store large datasets partitioned by frequently filtered columns, such as date or region, to minimize data scanning\n• Example answer: In one project, we noticed a join between transaction data and customer data was taking about 45 minutes\n• Spark UI showed that one partition contained most of the records because a few customer IDs had a very high number of transactions\n• We solved the issue by broadcasting the small customer table, repartitioning the transaction data, and enabling Adaptive Query Execution\n• The job execution time reduced to around 12 minutes, and cluster resource utilization became much more balanced","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Insert new version","type":"concept","a":"CurrentFlag='Y' StartDate=current_date() EndDate=NULL Columns typically used CustomerID Address StartDate EndDate CurrentFlag Pyspark Code: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, \"/target\") deltaTable.alias(\"t\").merge( sourceDF.alias(\"s\"), \"t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID AND t.CurrentFlag = 'Y'\" ).whenMatchedUpdate(condition=\"t.City <> s.City\", set={ \"CurrentFlag\": \"'N'\", \"EndDate\": \"current_date()\" } ).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ \"CustomerID\": \"s.CustomerID\", \"Name\": \"s.Name\", \"City\": \"s.City\", \"StartDate\": \"current_date()\", \"EndDate\": \"NULL\", \"CurrentFlag\": \"'Y'\" }).execute()","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"What are the most common performance bottlenecks you’ve faced in Spark and how","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"did you fix them?","type":"concept","a":"• Mention multiple points\n• Small Files Solution coalesce() repartition() Data Skew Solution Broadcast joins AQE Salting Shuffle Reduce unnecessary joins Filter early Partition correctly Expensive UDFs Use built-in Spark functions instead Repeated Computation Use cache() persist() Managerial Round","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you communicate technical issues to non-technical managers?","type":"concept","a":"• Instead of saying \"Spark executor memory exceeded\" Say The data processing job ran out of resources due to a significant increase in data volume\n• We increased processing capacity and optimized the job to ensure future runs complete within the expected time","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you mentor junior team members or help upskill the team?","type":"concept","a":"Mention  Code reviews  Pair programming  Documentation  SQL optimization sessions  Spark best practices  Knowledge-sharing meetings","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you resolve the data skew issue in Databricks / Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Data skew occurs when certain partition keys have far more data than others, causing some tasks to take much longer. • Salting: Add a random prefix (0-N) to the skewed key; join on the salted key, then aggregate results back. • Broadcast Join: If one side is small (<= 10 MB or tuned threshold), use broadcast to avoid shuffle altogether. • AQE (Adaptive Query Execution): Enable spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true — Spark automatically coalesces skewed partitions at runtime. • Skew Join Optimization (AQE): spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true splits skewed partitions into smaller ones. • Repartition: Use repartition(n, col) to manually redistribute data more evenly. • Increase spark.sql.shuffle.partitions from default 200 to a higher value for large datasets\n• DevOps Pipeline – Code Promotion & Debugging","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the cluster configura(cid:415)on you have done in your recent project","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster Configura(cid:415)on (Databricks) You can say:  We used Databricks Job Clusters in produc(cid:415)on  Cluster type: autoscaling enabled  Run(cid:415)me: DBR (Databricks Run(cid:415)me latest stable)  Mode: Standard cluster Example answer: “We used job clusters with autoscaling enabled between 2–8 workers depending on workload\n• We selected standard run(cid:415)me with op(cid:415)mized Spark configura(cid:415)ons for performance.”","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Check stage execu(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Are you familiar with cluster configura(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Typical configura(cid:415)on includes: Cluster Mode  Single Node  Standard  High Concurrency (legacy;\n• Unity Catalog environments now commonly use Standard or Serverless depending on the workload) Worker Type Example: Standard_D8s_v5 Driver Type Can be same or larger than workers\n• Worker Count Min = 2 Max = 8 Autoscaling enabled\n• Run(cid:415)me Version Example: 15.4 LTS Photon Enabled for SQL-heavy workloads\n• Auto Termina(cid:415)on 30 Minutes Unity Catalog Access Mode  Single User  Shared Interview Answer \"When configuring clusters, I select the run(cid:415)me version, VM size, worker count or autoscaling, access mode, auto termina(cid:415)on, Photon, and instance availability\n• In produc(cid:415)on we typically use autoscaling Job Clusters with LTS run(cid:415)mes.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"concept","a":"• A single-node cluster has one machine ac(cid:415)ng as both driver and worker\n• Possible issues: No Distributed Compu(cid:415)ng Spark cannot distribute work across mul(cid:415)ple machines\n• Driver Bo(cid:425)leneck Driver performs both coordina(cid:415)on and execu(cid:415)on\n• Memory Pressure Large datasets may exceed the memory available on the single node\n• CPU Satura(cid:415)on All tasks compete for the same 16 cores\n• Long Execu(cid:415)on Time Large joins and aggrega(cid:415)ons take longer because no addi(cid:415)onal workers are available\n• Fault Tolerance If the single node fails, the en(cid:415)re workload fails\n• Suitable For  Development  Tes(cid:415)ng  Small datasets  Learning Not recommended for produc(cid:415)on ETL workloads","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"For Dev and Test workspaces, should we create one metastore or different metastores?","type":"concept","a":"• Typical recommenda(cid:415)on: One metastore per region, shared across mul(cid:415)ple workspaces (Dev, Test, Prod), while keeping environments isolated using catalogs, schemas, and permissions\n• Example: Metastore ↓ Dev Workspace ↓ dev_catalog ------------------- Test Workspace ↓ test_catalog ------------------- Prod Workspace ↓ prod_catalog Different metastores are generally used when there are strong isola(cid:415)on requirements, such as:  Different cloud regions  Different legal en(cid:415)(cid:415)es or business units  Strict compliance or regulatory boundaries  Separate organiza(cid:415)ons \"In most enterprise implementa(cid:415)ons, we use a single metastore per region and a(cid:425)ach mul(cid:415)ple workspaces to it\n• Environment isola(cid:415)on is achieved using separate catalogs, schemas, and access controls\n• Separate metastores are typically reserved for cases where stronger isola(cid:415)on is required, such as different regions or compliance boundaries.\" Quick Revision (Most Important Points) Ques(cid:415)on Key Answer Compute Types All-Purpose, Job, SQL Warehouse, Serverless, Model Serving Job = produc(cid:415)on & auto-terminate;\n• All-Purpose = interac(cid:415)ve Job vs All-Purpose development Databricks Run(cid:415)me Op(cid:415)mized Spark + Delta Lake + Photon + language run(cid:415)mes Cluster Config Run(cid:415)me, VM size, autoscaling, access mode, Photon, auto-termina(cid:415)on No distribu(cid:415)on, driver bo(cid:425)leneck, limited scalability, memory/CPU Single Node Issues constraints Data Lake vs Delta Delta adds ACID, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, transac(cid:415)on log Deleted Managed Cannot recover without backups or source data once files/logs are Table permanently removed Managed vs External Managed = Databricks-managed storage;\n• External = customer-managed Volume cloud storage Unity Catalog Metastore → Catalog → Schema → Tables/Views/Volumes Usually one metastore per region with separate catalogs and Dev/Test Metastore permissions for each environment","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Read a text file with custom delimiter","type":"concept","a":"Input file id~!name~!=age 1~!Viswa~!=25 Desired Output id name age 1 Viswa 25 Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import split df = spark.read.text(\"input.txt\") result = df.select( split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(0).alias(\"id\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(1).alias(\"name\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(2).alias(\"age\") ) result.show() If the file has a header row that should be skipped: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import monotonically_increasing_id df = spark.read.text(\"input.txt\") data = ( df.withColumn(\"rn\", monotonically_increasing_id()) .filter(\"rn > 0\") ) result = data.select( split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(0).alias(\"id\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(1).alias(\"name\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(2).alias(\"age\") ) result.show()","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Q3. What is DAG?","type":"concept","a":"• DAG stands for Directed Acyclic Graph\n• In Apache Spark, a DAG represents the logical execution plan — a sequence of stages and tasks derived from transformations on RDDs or DataFrames\n• Three Key Properties • Directed — data flows in one direction (parent to child node) • Acyclic — no circular dependencies; no node can be its own ancestor • Graph — nodes represent RDDs/DataFrames; edges represent transformations How Spark Uses DAG • An action (collect, count, write) triggers job submission to the DAG Scheduler • DAG Scheduler divides the DAG into Stages based on shuffle boundaries • Each Stage splits into Tasks — one task per partition • Task Scheduler assigns tasks to available executors Lazy Evaluation: Transformations are NOT executed immediately\n• They build the DAG plan\n• Execution only happens when an action is triggered, allowing Spark to optimize the full plan first\n• Interview Tip: Mention the DAG can be viewed in Spark UI under the SQL/Jobs tab — useful for debugging performance and identifying shuffle stages","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow vs Wide Transformations","type":"concept","a":"Aspect Narrow Transformation Wide Transformation Shuffle No shuffle Shuffle occurs Performance Fast Slower (network I/O) Stage Boundary Same stage New stage is created Examples map, filter, select, union, flatMap groupBy, join, distinct, repartition, orderBy Fault Tolerance Re-compute from parent partition Must re-shuffle on node failure Best Practices • Use filter() early to reduce data before any shuffle operation • Use reduceByKey() instead of groupByKey() — performs partial aggregation locally before shuffle • Cache DataFrames before wide transformations if the result is reused • Broadcast small tables in joins to avoid shuffle entirely Page 2 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Additional Techniques","type":"concept","a":"• Data Skipping — Delta auto-collects min/max stats for first 32 columns • Caching — df.cache() for DataFrames reused multiple times in the same job • Broadcast Joins — broadcast hint for small dimension tables • AQE — spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true for runtime optimizations","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Skew — What it is and How to Handle It","type":"concept","a":"• Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across partitions\n• Some tasks (stragglers) take much longer and the entire job waits for them\n• Example: Joining on customer_id where 1 customer has 80% of all records\n• One executor processes millions of rows while others sit idle\n• Detect Skew • Spark UI: Stage details, Task duration histogram — few tasks taking 10x longer df.groupBy('key').count().orderBy(desc('count')).show() Solution 1: Salting from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, col, lit, floor, rand, explode, array salted = skewed_df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('key'), lit('_'), (floor(rand()*10)).cast('string'))) other_exp = other_df.withColumn('salt', explode(array([lit(i) for i in range(10)]))) other_exp = other_exp.withColumn('salted_key', Page 4 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide concat(col('key'), lit('_'), col('salt').cast('string'))) Solution 2: AQE — Recommended for Spark 3+ spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') AQE detects skewed partitions at runtime and automatically splits them\n• Available in Spark 3.0+\n• Easiest modern solution","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Joins — Inner, Left, Right","type":"concept","a":"Table A — Employees EmpID Name 1 Alice 2 Bob 3 Carol 4 Dave Table B — Departments EmpID Dept 1 Engineering 2 Marketing 5 Finance Inner Join — Only Matching Rows SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a INNER JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing Left Join — All from A, Matching from B (NULLs for unmatched) SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a LEFT JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing 3 Carol NULL 4 Dave NULL Right Join — All from B, Matching from A (NULLs for unmatched) SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a RIGHT JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing 5 NULL Finance Page 7 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide PySpark: df_a.join(df_b, 'EmpID', 'inner') | 'left' | 'right' | 'outer' | 'left_semi' | 'left_anti' Quick Reference Summary Topic Key Takeaway DAG Logical plan built lazily; executed on action trigger Narrow Transform No shuffle — same stage (map, filter, select) Wide Transform Shuffle — new stage (groupBy, join, distinct) Broadcast Join Replicate small table to all executors; eliminates shuffle OPTIMIZE Compact small Delta files for faster scans Z-ORDER Cluster data by column for file skipping on filters Data Skew Uneven partitions — fix with AQE (Spark 3+) or salting Delta Lake Data lake + ACID + Time Travel + MERGE on Parquet Auto Loader cloudFiles format — incremental ingestion with checkpointing dense_rank() Best for Top-N salary — handles ties without gaps Left Join All from left + NULL for unmatched right rows Right Join All from right + NULL for unmatched left rows Page 8 of 8","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on vs Wide Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on  No data shuffle.  Child par(cid:415)(cid:415)on depends on one parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  map()  filter()  union()  flatMap() Very fast","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is MongoDB?","type":"concept","a":"• NoSQL document database\n• Stores data as JSON-like BSON documents\n• Example: { \"empId\":101, \"name\":\"John\", \"skills\":[\"Python\",\"Spark\"] } Round 2:","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Spark job gives an Out Of Memory (OOM) error, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would check the Spark UI to iden(cid:415)fy which stage failed\n• Then I would:  Increase executor memory.  Increase the number of executors.  Reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)on size using repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on().  Avoid collect() on large datasets.  Use broadcast joins for small lookup tables.  Cache only when necessary.  Filter data before joins.  Inves(cid:415)gate data skew if one par(cid:415)(cid:415)on is much larger than the others.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Join a Huge Table with a Small Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Use a Broadcast Join\n• The small table is replicated to every executor so the join happens locally on each partition of the large table — no shuffle. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Explicit broadcast hint result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), on='customer_id', how='inner') # Configure auto-broadcast threshold (default 10 MB) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', '100mb') Page 2 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Aspect Broadcast Join Sort-Merge Join Shuffle None — no data movement Both tables shuffled Use when One table < ~200 MB Both tables are large Memory impact Small table on every executor Standard memory usage Performance Very fast Slower but scalable to any size Never broadcast a large table — it causes Out of Memory (OOM) on executors\n• Disable auto- broadcast: spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', '-1')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Join Two Large Tables in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Spark uses Sort-Merge Join (SMJ) by default for large-large joins\n• Additional strategies: Option 1 — Sort-Merge Join (default, Spark 2.3+) • Both tables are shuffled by the join key, sorted, then merged • Spark automatically picks SMJ when both tables exceed the broadcast threshold # SMJ happens automatically; or hint explicitly: result = df_orders.hint('merge').join(df_customers.hint('merge'), on='customer_id') Option 2 — Pre-bucket Both Tables (eliminates shuffle permanently) df_orders.write.bucketBy(64,'customer_id').sortBy('customer_id').saveAsTable('orders_bkt') df_customers.write.bucketBy(64,'customer_id').sortBy('customer_id').saveAsTable('cust_bkt') # All subsequent joins skip shuffle entirely result = spark.table('orders_bkt').join(spark.table('cust_bkt'), 'customer_id') Option 3 — Enable AQE (Adaptive Query Execution) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled', 'true') AQE dynamically adjusts join strategy at runtime\n• If one side turns out smaller than expected, it can switch from SMJ to Broadcast Join automatically","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Broadcast Join?","type":"concept","a":"• A Broadcast Join (map-side join) sends a complete copy of the smaller table to every executor node\n• The large table is never moved — each executor joins its local partition against the in-memory copy of the small table\n• No shuffle at all. • Eliminates shuffle — the most expensive Spark operation (network I/O, disk spill) • Best for: small lookup/dimension tables (under 10 MB default, up to ~200 MB practical) • Ideal pattern: large fact table JOIN broadcast(small dimension table) result = fact_orders.join(broadcast(dim_product), 'product_id') Section 3 | Delta Lake Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Cluster Configuration Would You Use for TB-Level Data?","type":"concept","a":"Aspect Recommendation Notes Cluster type Job Cluster (auto-terminate) Never use All-Purpose in production Driver size 16 cores, 64 GB RAM Larger driver for collect/broadcast ops Page 12 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Worker size 16 cores, 64 GB RAM per node 8-16 workers for 1 TB workloads Auto-scaling Min 4, Max 16 workers Scales with data volume dynamically Runtime Databricks Runtime + Photon Photon gives 3-10x speedup on SQL ops Spot workers Workers on Spot, Driver on On- Up to 70% cost savings Demand Delta Cache Enabled (SSD caching) Critical for repeated Delta table reads # Example Databricks cluster config { 'autoscale': { 'min_workers': 4, 'max_workers': 16 }, 'node_type_id': 'Standard_DS5_v2', # 16 cores, 56 GB RAM 'driver_node_type_id': 'Standard_DS5_v2', 'spark_conf': { 'spark.sql.adaptive.enabled': 'true', 'spark.sql.shuffle.partitions': '400' }, 'runtime_engine': 'PHOTON' }","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Executors Would You Use for Processing 1 TB of Data?","type":"concept","a":"• Default partition size: 128 MB (spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes) • 1 TB / 128 MB = ~8192 partitions (maximum concurrent tasks) • Practical executor config: 4-8 cores per executor, 16-32 GB RAM each • With 64 executors x 8 cores = 512 cores total — can run 512 tasks in parallel • Set shuffle partitions to 200-400: spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '400') Page 13 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Config Recommended Value Reasoning Executors 32-64 Balances parallelism and coordination overhead Cores per executor 4-8 (5 is sweet spot) Above 5 increases GC pressure RAM per executor 16-32 GB Covers 128-256 MB partitions with 4x overhead Total cores 128-512 Sufficient parallelism for 1 TB Shuffle partitions 200-400 Avoid 200 (too few) or 8192 (too many small tasks) Rule of thumb: 5 cores and 16 GB RAM per executor is a well-balanced starting point. Too many small executors = high task overhead. Too few large executors = GC pressure. Use auto-scaling with min/max bounds for production. Section 10 | Behavioral / Closing","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What skills or experiences have made previous hires most successful in this role?","type":"concept","a":"• Never say 'I have no questions' — it signals disinterest\n• Avoid asking about salary, leave policy, or working hours in a technical round\n• Save those for the HR discussion\n• Quick Reference — All 30 Questions at a Glance Page 14 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) # Question (Short) Key Answer / Keyword 1 Spark Architecture Driver + Cluster Manager + Executors + Stages + DAG + Lazy Eval 2 Notebooks per year 10-40 typical; organized by Bronze/Silver/Gold layers 3 Daily data volume Tailor to project;\n• 500 GB-2 TB is typical mid-scale 4 File types CSV, JSON, Parquet, Delta, Avro, ORC, XML 5 Huge + small table join Broadcast Join — no shuffle, small table to all executors 6 Two large tables join Sort-Merge Join (default) OR pre-bucket both tables 7 Broadcast Join Copy small table to every executor; eliminates shuffle entirely 8 Z-ORDER for transactions transaction_date + customer_id (high cardinality, frequent filters) 9 Default Z-ORDER column None — must be explicit;\n• Delta auto-stats on first 32 cols 10 Z-Order indexes Z-curve clustering; co-locates related rows; enables file skipping 11 CSV to Parquet Columnar, compressed, schema-aware, predicate pushdown, 5-20x faster 12 JSON to structured table from_json() + schema + explode() for nested/array fields 13 Remove duplicates dropDuplicates() / distinct() / Window row_number() / MERGE 14 Duplicate handling methods 7 methods: dropDuplicates, distinct, window rank, MERGE, GROUP BY\n• 15 Gender count per city GROUP BY city + COUNT(CASE WHEN gender=X) or PIVOT or PySpark agg 16 Liquid Clustering Replaces partition+ZORDER; dynamic, incremental, change columns anytime 17 View types Temp, Global Temp, Standard (metastore), Materialized (pre- computed) 18 Materialized Views Pre-computed Delta table; auto-refreshed; fast for heavy aggregations 19 Thousands of CSV files Auto Loader (cloudFiles) + checkpoint — incremental, scalable 20 Most efficient file loading Auto Loader for incremental;\n• COPY INTO for SQL batch; spark.read for one-time 21 Auto Loader incremental Directory listing OR file notifications (Event Grid/SNS) + checkpoint 22 Schedule Auto Loader Databricks Jobs, DLT pipelines, ADF triggers, Databricks Workflows 23 Auto Loader triggers availableNow, processingTime, continuous, once (deprecated) 24 Auto Loader file detection Directory scan vs checkpoint OR cloud file notification queue 25 Corrupted CSV rows PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + quarantine bad rows to Delta table 26 Bronze layer from CSV files Auto Loader + _ingestion_time + _source_file + mergeSchema=true 27 TB-level cluster config 16+ workers, Photon, auto-scale, Spot workers, AQE enabled Page 15 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) 28 Cluster types in Databricks All-Purpose, Job, High Concurrency, Single Node, SQL Warehouse, DLT 29 Executors for 1 TB 32-64 executors, 4-8 cores each, 16-32 GB RAM, shuffle.partitions=400 30 Questions for interviewer Ask about architecture, challenges, CI/CD, streaming, first 90 days Page 16 of 16","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Spark DataFrame from the given data.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Display the final DataFrame.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Convert the nested structure into a Spark StructType (if needed).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produce a final DataFrame with the following schema:","type":"concept","a":"product | price | quan(cid:415)ty","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"How will you join two large DataFrames effec(cid:415)vely in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• \"When both DataFrames are large, I avoid broadcast joins because broadcas(cid:415)ng large datasets can cause memory issues\n• Instead, I:  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on both DataFrames on the join key.  Filter unnecessary records before joining.  Select only required columns.  Enable Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE).  Ensure both DataFrames have a balanced par(cid:415)(cid:415)on distribu(cid:415)on to minimize shuffle.\" Example df1 = df1.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(\"user_id\") df2 = df2.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(\"user_id\") result = df1.join(df2, \"user_id\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark coding: Large table is skewed, join with a small table.","type":"concept","a":"• Interviewer Large table is skewed\n• Small table is normal\n• Join Key: user_id \"Since the large DataFrame is skewed and the second DataFrame is small, I would use a Broadcast Join\n• Broadcas(cid:415)ng the small table eliminates shuffle for that table and significantly improves performance\n• If skew is severe, I may also use key sal(cid:415)ng.\" Broadcast Join from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"user_id\", \"le(cid:332)\" ) If skew is extremely high (Sal(cid:415)ng) from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import floor, rand large_df = large_df.withColumn( \"salt\", floor(rand()*5) ) small_df = small_df.crossJoin( spark.range(5).toDF(\"salt\") ) result = large_df.join( small_df, [\"user_id\",\"salt\"] )","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Read CSV → Filter inac(cid:415)ve → Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on by Region → Write Delta to S3","type":"concept","a":"PySpark Code from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on( \"header\", True ).csv(\"s3://input/customer.csv\") inac(cid:415)ve = df.filter( col(\"status\") == \"inac(cid:415)ve\" ) inac(cid:415)ve.write \\ .format(\"delta\") \\ .par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"region\") \\ .mode(\"overwrite\") \\ .save(\"s3://output/customer_delta/\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you install a third-party JAR in Python?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the JAR is required for Spark, I a(cid:425)ach it to the Databricks cluster:  Go to the cluster configura(cid:415)on.  Select Libraries.  Install the JAR from Workspace, DBFS, Maven, or cloud storage.  Restart the cluster if required\n• If I need a Python package instead of a JAR, I use %pip install in Databricks notebooks or install it as a cluster library.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you explain technical strategies to non-technical stakeholders?","type":"concept","a":"• \"When speaking with non-technical stakeholders, I avoid technical jargon and focus on business outcomes\n• For example, instead of saying, 'We op(cid:415)mized Spark jobs using broadcast joins and par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning,' I would say: 'We improved the data processing process so reports are generated faster and more reliably, reducing wai(cid:415)ng (cid:415)me for business users.' I also use simple diagrams, real-world analogies, and dashboards to explain the impact of the solu(cid:415)on\n• My goal is to help stakeholders understand how the changes improve performance, reduce costs, or support be(cid:425)er decision-making without requiring technical knowledge.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark code (min salary by dept, cast emp id, null salary check)","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col, min df_clean = df \\ .withColumn(\"emp_id\", col(\"emp_id\").cast(\"int\")) \\ .filter(col(\"salary\").isNotNull()) df_result = df_clean.groupBy(\"dept_id\") \\ .agg(min(\"salary\").alias(\"min_salary\")) df_result.show()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If column name changed in withColumn, do we rerun both cells?","type":"concept","a":" Yes, you must rerun dependent cells  Because Spark is lazy evalua(cid:415)on, but notebook cells depend on execu(cid:415)on order  So if upstream transforma(cid:415)on changes column name, downstream cells must be re- executed","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Spark architecture + stages + cluster used","type":"concept","a":"Architecture:  Driver  Executors  Cluster Manager Stages:  Spark divides job into stages based on shuffle boundaries  Each stage has mul(cid:415)ple tasks  Example: groupBy or join causes shuffle → new stage Cluster used:  Typically Databricks job cluster / all-purpose cluster  Auto-scaling enabled in produc(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Upskilling & cer(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":" Learning PySpark, Azure Data Engineering  Microso(cid:332) cer(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons (DP-203)  Prac(cid:415)cing Databricks, Delta Lake  Hands-on projects","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Check Spark UI for stages and tasks.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Check shuffle size.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Cache reused DataFrames.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Use AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Wide vs. Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Narrow Wide No shuffle Shuffle required Faster Slower One parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Mul(cid:415)ple parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Examples: Narrow df.filter() df.map() Wide df.groupBy() df.join() df.dis(cid:415)nct()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Coalesce vs. Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Coalesce Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on Reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons No full shuffle (usually) Full shuffle Faster for reducing More expensive","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"You have 5 million records. How will you op(cid:415)mize?","type":"concept","a":" Read only required columns.  Filter early.  Use Parquet or Delta.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on appropriately.  Broadcast small lookup tables.  Cache reused DataFrames.  Avoid unnecessary shuffles.  Enable AQE.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"How do you see PySpark op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":" Spark UI  Physical execu(cid:415)on plan: df.explain(True) Check for:  Broadcast joins  Shuffle opera(cid:415)ons  Predicate pushdown  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on pruning","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Improvement using GenAI in your project","type":"concept","a":"• Example answer: \"We used GenAI tools such as ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot to improve developer produc(cid:415)vity\n• They helped generate PySpark transforma(cid:415)ons, SQL queries, unit tests, documenta(cid:415)on, and code explana(cid:415)ons\n• This reduced development (cid:415)me, improved consistency, and accelerated troubleshoo(cid:415)ng\n• We always reviewed and validated AI-generated code before using it.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Transforma(cid:415)on and Ac(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Transforma(cid:415)on Ac(cid:415)on Creates a new DataFrame Triggers execu(cid:415)on Lazy Eager Builds the DAG Executes the DAG Examples: Transforma(cid:415)ons filter() select() join() groupBy() withColumn() Ac(cid:415)ons show() count() collect() write() first()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"A Spark job processing 3 joined tables increased from 1 million to 50 million records. The","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"job now takes much longer. How would you debug it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would first analyze the Spark UI to iden(cid:415)fy the bo(cid:425)leneck, then check for data skew, shuffle size, join strategy, par(cid:415)(cid:415)on count, execu(cid:415)on plan, and executor resource usage\n• Based on the findings, I would op(cid:415)mize using broadcast joins, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, filtering before joins, selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, caching reusable DataFrames, or handling skew.\" Step-by-step approach Step 1: Check the Spark UI Look at:","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":" Which stage is taking the most (cid:415)me?","type":"concept","a":"•  Number of tasks  Shuffle Read/Write size  Executor CPU u(cid:415)liza(cid:415)on  Spill to disk  Skewed tasks (one task taking much longer than others) Step 2: Check Data Skew One of the join keys may have become highly skewed\n• Example: Customer_ID 1 -> 20 million records 2 -> 10 records 3 -> 5 records One executor processes huge data while others stay idle\n• Check: df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count().orderBy(\"count\", ascending=False).show() Possible solu(cid:415)ons:  Sal(cid:415)ng  Broadcast join (if one table is small)  AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on) Step 3: Check Join Strategy Run df.explain(True) Look for:  BroadcastHashJoin  SortMergeJoin  ShuffleHashJoin If Spark is doing SortMergeJoin because the table became larger, shuffle cost increases\n• If one table is small: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\") Step 4: Check Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Count Too few par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:  Executors become idle\n• Too many par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:  Scheduling overhead increases\n• Check: df.rdd.getNumPar(cid:415)(cid:415)ons() Adjust if necessary: df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(400) or spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.shuffle.par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons\", 400) (Default is o(cid:332)en 200.) Step 5: Check Shuffle Size Large shuffle is a common bo(cid:425)leneck\n• Spark UI → Shuffle Read/Write If shuffle is very high:  Filter data before join  Select only required columns  Broadcast small table Step 6: Filter Early Bad: Join ↓ Filter Be(cid:425)er: Filter ↓ Join This reduces data movement\n• Step 7: Remove Unnecessary Columns Instead of select(\"*\") Use select(\"id\", \"name\", \"amount\") Less data is shuffled\n• Step 8: Check Caching If the same DataFrame is reused mul(cid:415)ple (cid:415)mes: df.cache() Otherwise Spark recomputes it\n• Step 9: Check Executor Configura(cid:415)on Review:  Executor Memory  Executor Cores  Driver Memory Insufficient memory can lead to disk spilling\n• Step 10: Compare Execu(cid:415)on Plans df.explain(True) Look for:  Extra exchanges  Mul(cid:415)ple shuffles  Cartesian joins  Broadcast not happening","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Coalesce and Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Coalesce Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on Reduces par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Increases or decreases par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Avoids full shuffle (mostly) Always performs a full shuffle Faster Slower Used a(cid:332)er filtering Used before joins or for balancing data Cannot efficiently increase par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Can increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Example: df.coalesce(5) df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(20)","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"May create skewed par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Some executors process much more data.","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques have you used in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"\"The op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques I have used include:  Broadcast joins for small lookup tables.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning to balance data distribu(cid:415)on.  Caching frequently used DataFrames.  Filtering data early to reduce processing.  Using column pruning instead of SELECT *.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning large datasets.  Enabling Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE).  Op(cid:415)mizing Delta tables using OPTIMIZE and ZORDER.  Avoiding unnecessary shuffle opera(cid:415)ons.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How will you join a small table with a large dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If one table is significantly smaller than the other, I use a Broadcast Join\n• Spark sends the small table to every executor, allowing the large table to be processed locally without a shuffle\n• This improves performance by reducing network overhead.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Write PySpark code to create a new column as the sum of two columns.","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col df = df.withColumn( \"Total\", col(\"Salary\") + col(\"Bonus\") ) df.show()","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in PySpark.","type":"concept","a":"Men(cid:415)on:  Broadcast Join  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Cache/Persist  Filter Early  Column Pruning  AQE  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Delta Op(cid:415)mize  ZORDER  Avoid Shuffle","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Volume?","type":"concept","a":"• Volume refers to the amount/size of data being stored or processed\n• It is one of the 3 Vs of Big Data (Volume, Velocity, Variety). • Small scale: GBs - can fit on a single machine • Medium scale: TBs - requires distributed systems • Large scale: PBs/EBs - requires cloud-native distributed architectures (e.g., HDFS, S3, ADLS) In Databricks/Spark, volume impacts partitioning strategy, cluster sizing, and shuffle operations","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What to do when the Dataset is Slow?","type":"concept","a":"Diagnosis & Optimization Strategies: • Check Spark UI: Identify slow stages, skewed partitions, spill to disk • Partitioning: Repartition/coalesce to right number of partitions • Caching: cache() or persist() frequently accessed DataFrames • Broadcast Join: Use for small tables (< 10MB by default) • Predicate Pushdown: Filter early to reduce data scanned • File format: Use Parquet/Delta instead of CSV/JSON • Z-ordering: Co-locate related data for faster reads • AQE: Enable Adaptive Query Execution • Columnar pruning: Select only needed columns • Data skew: Apply salting or AQE skew hints • Cluster sizing: Increase worker nodes or use larger instance type SECTION 2: Data Modeling","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What is Partitioning?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning divides data into smaller, manageable chunks to improve query performance and parallelism\n• Types: • Hive-style Partitioning: Folders on disk (e.g., /data/year=2024/month=01/) • Spark RDD/DataFrame Partitioning: In-memory distribution across executors • Hash Partitioning: Distribute by hash of a column • Range Partitioning: Distribute by value ranges Best Practices: • Choose low-cardinality columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning (too many small files) • Use OPTIMIZE to compact small files in Delta # Write partitioned Delta table df.write.format('delta') \\ .partitionBy('year', 'month') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save('/delta/sales') # Repartition in Spark df = df.repartition(200, col('customer_id')) df = df.coalesce(10) # Reduce partitions without full shuffle SECTION 3: Azure Data Factory (ADF)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Single Node Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Driver only, no workers — for small data / development • No distributed processing Cluster Modes: • Standard Mode: Full Spark — Python, Scala, R, SQL • Enhanced Autoscaling: Dynamic worker count based on workload • Photon Engine: Vectorized query engine for faster SQL SECTION 4: CI/CD, Delta Lake & Architecture","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Lazy Evaluation in Spark","type":"concept","a":"• Lazy Evaluation means Spark does NOT execute transformations immediately\n• It builds a logical plan (DAG) and only executes when an action is called\n• Transformations (Lazy — build the plan): • filter(), select(), join(), groupBy(), withColumn(), map() Actions (Eager — trigger execution): • count(), collect(), show(), write(), save(), take() # None of these execute yet: df = spark.read.csv('/data/sales.csv', header=True) df = df.filter(col('amount') > 1000) # Lazy df = df.groupBy('region').sum('amount') # Lazy # Execution happens HERE: df.show() # Action — triggers the full plan Benefits: • Query optimization via Catalyst Optimizer before execution • Pipelining of transformations to avoid redundant passes • Spark can reorder/skip operations for efficiency","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Catalyst Optimizer in Spark","type":"concept","a":"• Catalyst Optimizer is Spark's built-in query optimizer that automatically optimizes SQL/DataFrame operations using a tree-based rule system\n• Optimization Phases: • 1\n• Analysis: Resolves column references, validates schema • 2\n• Logical Optimization: Applies rule-based optimizations: ◦ Predicate Pushdown: Push filters as close to source as possible ◦ Constant Folding: Pre-compute constant expressions ◦ Column Pruning: Drop unused columns early • 3\n• Physical Planning: Converts logical plan to physical plan, chooses join strategy • 4\n• Code Generation: Generates JVM bytecode (Whole-Stage CodeGen) # Catalyst auto-applies pushdown — you benefit automatically # To inspect the plan: df.explain(True) # Shows analyzed, optimized, physical plans # Extended explain df.explain(mode='formatted')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark — Process 100M Row CSV","type":"concept","a":"• from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, sum as spark_sum from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType # Initialize Spark with optimized config for 100M rows spark = SparkSession.builder \\ .appName('LargeCSVProcessor') \\ .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') \\ .config('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '400') \\ .config('spark.executor.memory', '8g') \\ .getOrCreate() # Define schema explicitly (faster than inferSchema for large files) schema = StructType([ StructField('transaction_id', StringType(), True), StructField('customer_id', StringType(), True), StructField('transaction_date', StringType(), True), StructField('amount', DoubleType(), True), StructField('category', StringType(), True), ]) # Step 1: Read large CSV df = spark.read \\ .option('header', 'true') \\ .option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED') \\ .schema(schema) \\ .csv('/data/large_transactions.csv') print(f'Total rows: {df.count()}') # Step 2: Filter out rows with missing values df_clean = df.dropna(subset=['transaction_id', 'customer_id', 'transaction_date', 'amount']) df_clean = df_clean.filter( col('transaction_id').isNotNull() & col('customer_id').isNotNull() & col('amount').isNotNull() ) print(f'Rows after null filter: {df_clean.count()}') # Step 3: Convert transaction_date to datetime df_clean = df_clean.withColumn( 'transaction_date', to_timestamp(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss') ) # Step 4: Group by category and calculate sum of amount df_aggregated = df_clean \\ .groupBy('category') \\ .agg(spark_sum('amount').alias('total_amount')) \\ .orderBy(col('total_amount').desc()) df_aggregated.show() # Step 5: Write result to new CSV file df_aggregated.coalesce(1) \\ .write \\ .option('header', 'true') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .csv('/output/revenue_by_category') print('Processing complete\n• Results written to /output/revenue_by_category') spark.stop() SECTION 7: Challenges & Issues in Pipelines","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Task is taking 45 mins, as an experience data engineer what can be the issue and how","type":"concept","a":"• will you rec(cid:415)fy\n• If a pipeline task is taking 45 minutes, I would first analyze logs and execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy the bo(cid:425)leneck stage—whether it's extrac(cid:415)on, transforma(cid:415)on, or load\n• Common causes include full table scans, missing indexes, lack of incremental load, inefficient joins, data skew, or small file issues\n• I would op(cid:415)mize by implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loading using watermark or CDC, op(cid:415)mizing joins using broadcast or par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, improving cluster configura(cid:415)on, and par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data properly in storage\n• I would also reduce shuffles and unnecessary transforma(cid:415)ons and monitor performance a(cid:332)er each change","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"If there are 100 columns, how will you create schema?","type":"concept","a":"“I prefer schema defini(cid:415)on over schema inference for be(cid:425)er control and performance.” (cid:48132) Approach 1: Explicit schema (best prac(cid:415)ce) from pyspark.sql.types import * schema = StructType([ StructField(\"col1\", StringType(), True), StructField(\"col2\", IntegerType(), True), ... ]) (cid:48131) Approach 2: Read from source with schema inference (not preferred in prod) df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", True).csv(path) (cid:44931) Approach 3: Schema registry / metadata-driven (enterprise approach)  Store schema in: o JSON o Control table  Dynamically generate schema “For 100 columns, I prefer metadata-driven schema genera(cid:415)on or explicit schema defini(cid:415)on to avoid type mismatches and improve performance.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"(cid:44869)(cid:44870) What happens if broadcast is NOT used?","type":"concept","a":"• “Spark automa(cid:415)cally decides the join type based on size and configura(cid:415)on.” Default behavior:  Small + large table → Broadcast join (auto)  Large + large → Sort-Merge Join (cid:55982)(cid:55983)(cid:55984) Join selec(cid:415)on logic: Scenario Join Type Small table Broadcast Join Large tables Sort Merge Join Skewed data May cause shuffle skew “If broadcast is not explicitly specified, Spark’s op(cid:415)mizer (Catalyst + AQE) decides the join strategy\n• If one table is small enough, it automa(cid:415)cally performs a broadcast join; otherwise it uses sort-merge join for large datasets.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"delta","name":"Delta Lake","icon":"🗄️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Delta Lake / a Delta table and what problems does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that sits on top of Parquet in cloud storage and adds: ACID transactions, a transaction log (_delta_log), schema enforcement & evolution, time travel, UPSERT/MERGE, and unified batch + streaming reads/writes.\nIt brings reliability and warehouse-like guarantees to a data lake (the 'lakehouse'), fixing typical data-lake issues: no ACID, no schema control, slow updates, and small-file problems."},{"q":"Explain ACID properties in Delta Lake and the transaction log.","type":"concept","a":"• Atomicity — a commit fully succeeds or fully fails (no partial writes).\n• Consistency — schema enforcement keeps data valid.\n• Isolation — snapshot isolation for concurrent reads/writes.\n• Durability — committed data persists even after failures.\nThe _delta_log is an ordered set of JSON commits recording every change (add/remove files, metadata, protocol). Each read reconstructs the table state from the log; the log is the source of truth and enables time travel and atomic operations."},{"q":"What are the file types inside the _delta_log folder?","type":"concept","a":"• .json — incremental transaction files, one per commit (AddFile, RemoveFile, metadata, protocol). No actual data.\n• .checkpoint.parquet — a snapshot of table state every ~10 commits so readers don't replay all JSON files.\n• .compacted.json — combines multiple small JSON commits to speed up log reads.\n• .crc — checksum/integrity file for checkpoints; on corruption Spark falls back to the previous valid checkpoint.\nTogether they keep metadata reads fast and reliable."},{"q":"What is Delta table metadata log management and how does it become a bottleneck?","type":"concept","a":"Every commit adds a JSON file to _delta_log; over time this grows and slows query planning. Mitigation:\n• Checkpoints (every 10 commits) collapse state so readers load 1 checkpoint + recent JSONs.\n• delta.logRetentionDuration (default 30 days) — old JSON commits are pruned once a checkpoint exists.\n• Compacted JSON files reduce the number of individual commits.\n• OPTIMIZE compacts the underlying Parquet data files (not the log).\nNote: copying the log elsewhere doesn't fix performance — checkpointing + retention does."},{"q":"How does time travel work in Delta Lake?","type":"concept","a":"Delta keeps every version in the log, so you can query historical snapshots:\n• By version: SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 5 (or option('versionAsOf',5)).\n• By timestamp: SELECT * FROM t TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01'.\n• DESCRIBE HISTORY t shows the commit history (operations, versions, timestamps).\nUse cases: audit, reproduce past results, recover from bad writes (RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF n).\nLimitation: time travel is bounded by VACUUM retention — once old files are removed, those versions are gone."},{"q":"Difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema enforcement (validation) — by default Delta rejects writes whose schema doesn't match the table, protecting data integrity.\n• Schema evolution — you can intentionally allow new/changed columns:\n  – df.write.option('mergeSchema','true').mode('append')\n  – SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true\n  – ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ...\n  – COPY INTO ... COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')\n• overwriteSchema replaces the whole schema (use with caution)."},{"q":"How do you implement MERGE (UPSERT) in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"MERGE matches source to target and conditionally updates/inserts/deletes — the standard upsert pattern for incremental/CDC loads.\nExample:\ntarget.alias('t').merge(incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.id = s.id').whenMatchedUpdateAll().whenNotMatchedInsertAll().execute()\nSQL: MERGE INTO t USING s ON t.id=s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\nSupports whenMatchedDelete, conditional updates, and partial column updates. Requires the join condition to be on key columns for efficiency."},{"q":"What does OPTIMIZE do?","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE compacts many small Parquet files into fewer, larger (~1 GB) files to improve read performance and reduce metadata overhead (fixes the small-file problem).\n• OPTIMIZE table; or OPTIMIZE table WHERE date >= '2026-01-01' (target recent partitions).\n• OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY (col) adds clustering for data skipping.\nRun after bulk loads / daily ETL. Target file size is configurable via delta.optimize.maxFileSize. Auto Optimize can do this automatically."},{"q":"What is Z-ORDER and how does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"Z-ORDER co-locates related rows into the same files by reordering data across multiple columns (multi-dimensional clustering).\n• Delta's data-skipping uses file min/max stats to skip files that can't match a filter; good clustering means fewer files to scan.\n• Best on HIGH-cardinality columns used frequently in WHERE/JOIN (customer_id, transaction_id, date).\n• Syntax: OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date).\n• It's expensive — apply to the most queried columns only. Don't combine with auto compaction."},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compacts small files into bigger files; no sorting; always useful for small-file cleanup.\n• Z-ORDER — does the compaction AND sorts/clusters data by column values within files for data skipping; best for high-cardinality filter columns; requires re-running to stay fresh.\n• Liquid Clustering — dynamic, incremental clustering on chosen keys; Databricks re-clusters new data automatically, no partition maintenance, works with evolving workloads; replaces partition+z-order.\nModern guidance: prefer Liquid Clustering; use OPTIMIZE (with ZORDER) on legacy partitioned tables."},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"Liquid Clustering is a Databricks Delta feature that organizes data by clustering keys without traditional Hive partitioning.\n• Define keys once: ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date) (or CLUSTER BY AUTO).\n• Databricks incrementally re-clusters new data; keys can change without rewriting the whole table.\n• Removes partition-column cardinality pitfalls and small-file issues; improves data skipping.\n• No need to run Z-ORDER manually; useful for evolving/changing query patterns."},{"q":"What is VACUUM and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM permanently deletes data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta log and are past the retention period (default 7 days).\n• Reduces storage cost and scan overhead.\n• VACUUM t RETAIN 168 HOURS; add DRY RUN to see what would be deleted.\n• Warning: vacuuming deletes old versions, so you LOSE time travel beyond the retention. Lowering retention below 7 days requires disabling the safety check (risky — can corrupt concurrent readers)."},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed (CDF) and how does it differ from CDC?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF (Delta feature) — records row-level changes (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) in a Delta table so downstream can read changes incrementally.\n  Enable: ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed=true); read with readChangeFeed=true + startingVersion.\n  Extra columns: _change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp.\n• CDC — the broader process of capturing INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from a SOURCE system (via log-based tools like Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, triggers, or timestamp polling).\nIn short: CDC captures changes at the source; CDF propagates Delta table changes downstream. CDF is a Delta-native way to consume changes."},{"q":"What are Deletion Vectors?","type":"concept","a":"Deletion Vectors mark deleted rows in a bitmap rather than immediately rewriting the entire Parquet file.\n• Makes DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE much faster (avoids rewriting whole files).\n• Readers transparently respect the deletion vector; compacted later by OPTIMIZE.\n• Enable via table property delta.enableDeletionVectors=true. Trade-off: slight read overhead until optimized."},{"q":"Difference between DEEP CLONE and SHALLOW CLONE?","type":"concept","a":"• DEEP CLONE — copies the data files AND the metadata/history to a new location; fully independent; supports time travel on the clone; used for migration/backup/experimentation.\n• SHALLOW CLONE — copies only the metadata and references the original data files (no data copy); fast/cheap; if the source files are vacuumed or changed, the clone breaks (depends on source).\nUse deep clone for independent copies; shallow clone for quick test/dev without data duplication.\nSyntax: CREATE TABLE dest DEEP|SHALLOW CLONE source."},{"q":"Difference between managed (internal) and external tables in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — Databricks manages the storage location and lifecycle; DROP TABLE deletes BOTH metadata AND data files. Default format is Delta. Best for lakehouse/DW tables.\n• External table — you specify the storage location (ADLS/S3); DROP TABLE deletes only metadata, data files REMAIN. Shared across tools.\nProduction rule: use external tables (or Unity Catalog managed with careful governance) on ADLS so a drop can't accidentally destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move/copy a Delta table to another location. What options do you have?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read + write new location: spark.read.format('delta').load(src).write.format('delta').save(dest).\n• DEEP CLONE — preserves data + history/metadata to a new path (independent).\n• SHALLOW CLONE — metadata-only, references original files (fast/cheap).\n• Export to Parquet/CSV for external consumption.\nChoose deep clone for a full copy, shallow clone for a quick reference."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A managed table was dropped and, after the retention period, the files were permanently removed. Can you recreate it?","type":"scenario","a":"No — not from Delta alone. Time travel and RESTORE depend on the transaction log AND the underlying data files still existing. Once both are permanently deleted, recovery options are:\n• Restore from backup/DR copy.\n• Reload from the source system and reprocess.\n• Recover via cloud storage versioning/soft-delete if enabled.\nLesson: use external tables (or backups) in production so a DROP doesn't destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The CRC file is corrupted. What happens?","type":"scenario","a":"• The .crc file validates checkpoint integrity. If the CRC check fails, Spark gracefully falls back to the PREVIOUS valid checkpoint and uses the recent JSON commits to rebuild current state.\n• So reads typically still succeed — the corrupted checkpoint is simply skipped.\n• Fix root cause (e.g., partial/interrupted write). Delta is resilient because the log + checkpoints are redundant; you can also force a new checkpoint with OPTIMIZE or Spark actions."},{"q":"What are Delta table optimization techniques?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compact small files.\n• Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering — co-locate related data for data skipping.\n• Partitioning — on low-cardinality columns (date/region) where partitions are ~1 GB+.\n• Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) — automatic file sizing.\n• VACUUM — remove stale files.\n• Deletion Vectors — fast deletes/updates.\n• Predictive Optimization (UC) — automatic maintenance.\n• Right file size (~128 MB–1 GB), avoid over-partitioning, use Photon + AQE, and keep statistics for data skipping."},{"q":"What is the small-file problem and how do you fix it in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"Streaming/micro-batch or many small writes create thousands of tiny files, causing high metadata/listing overhead and slow reads.\nFix:\n• OPTIMIZE to compact into larger files; run on a schedule.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) for automatic compaction.\n• Use trigger(availableNow=True) or larger micro-batches in streaming; consider foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy.\n• Set a target file size and coalesce before writes."},{"q":"How do COPY INTO and Auto Loader differ for ingestion?","type":"concept","a":"• COPY INTO — SQL-based, idempotent batch ingestion of files from cloud storage into a Delta table; tracks already-loaded files; good for scheduled/batch loads.\n• Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — Structured Streaming source that incrementally and continuously discovers new files (file notification or directory listing); scalable, handles schema inference/evolution and rescued data; best for high-volume, near-real-time ingestion.\nUse COPY INTO for simple batch; Auto Loader for continuous/scalable incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is Time Travel? What is the reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• Time Travel allows querying previous versions of a Delta table\n• Example: SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5; or SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01';\n• Default reten(cid:415)on:  Transac(cid:415)on Log = 30 days  Deleted data (VACUUM) reten(cid:415)on = 7 days","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore a table beyond the default reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• If the data has already been removed by VACUUM, it cannot be restored using Delta Time Travel because the underlying files have been permanently deleted\n• Possible recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from an ADLS backup or snapshot.  Recreate the table from the original source.  Use an external backup or disaster recovery solu(cid:415)on\n• If the files are s(cid:415)ll available (for example, because VACUUM has not removed them), you can restore with: RESTORE TABLE sales TO VERSION AS OF 10; or RESTORE TABLE sales TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-05-01';","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you increase reten(cid:415)on period for one table?","type":"concept","a":"• Example: ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.deletedFileReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='30 days' ); or ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.logReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='90 days' );\n• This changes reten(cid:415)on only for that specific Delta table","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE Over (cid:415)me, many small files can be created due to frequent writes\n• OPTIMIZE compacts these small files into fewer, larger files\n• Example: Before: 1000 files × 5 MB A(cid:332)er: 10 files × 500 MB OPTIMIZE sales;\n• Benefits:  Fewer files to open.  Faster reads.  Be(cid:425)er query performance\n• Z-ORDER ZORDER physically reorganizes data within the op(cid:415)mized files so that rows with similar values are stored close together\n• OPTIMIZE sales ZORDER BY (customer_id);\n• If you frequently run: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE customer_id = 101;\n• Z-Ordering helps Spark read only the relevant por(cid:415)ons of the data, reducing the amount scanned\n• Difference between OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Compacts small files into larger ones Reorders data within files based on specified columns Reduces file management overhead Improves data skipping for filtered queries Improves general read performance Improves queries filtering on Z-Ordered columns Can be run alone Used as part of OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER BY (...)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delta Lake Architecture","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE, ZORDER & VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Time Travel in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Why Is This Useful?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Case Example Debugging A bug caused wrong data to be written yesterday — query v10 to see the clean state Rollback Accidentally deleted rows — restore from a previous version Auditing Regulatory requirement to show what data looked like on Jan 1st Reproducibility ML model trained on v5 of the data — reproduce by reading that version Time Travel Code # ── Query by VERSION NUMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────── # SQL spark.sql('SELECT * FROM silver.orders VERSION AS OF 5') # Python df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', '5') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── Query by TIMESTAMP ──────────────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM silver.orders TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-15 10:00:00'\") df_yesterday = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── See all versions (history) ──────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql('DESCRIBE HISTORY silver.orders').show(truncate=False) # Shows: version, timestamp, operation (WRITE/DELETE/MERGE), user, etc. # ── RESTORE a table to a previous version ──────────────────────────── spark.sql('RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 3') # Or by timestamp: spark.sql(\"RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-14'\") ⚠️ Time Travel Limitation Time travel only works as long as the old Parquet files exist\n• Running VACUUM deletes those files permanently\n• Default VACUUM retention = 7 days → time travel available for the last 7 days by default\n• Increase retention period if you need longer time travel: ALTER TABLE \n• SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days')","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• Azure Data bricks is a data analytics platform optimized for the Microsoft Azure cloud services platform\n• Azure Data bricks offers three environments for developing data intensive applications: Data bricks SQL, Data bricks Data Science & Engineering, and Data bricks Machine Learning","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of clusters in Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ Single Node: Doesn’t have any worker nodes and recommended for the single user cluster the small data volume. ➢ Standard: Recommend for a Single user cluster and can run SQL, Python, R, SCALA workload . ➢ High Concurrency: Optimized to run concurrency SQL, Python, R workloads rund and scala is not supported.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is delta Lake, How to create delta lake tables","type":"concept","a":"• Delta lake is an open-source data format that provides ACID transactions, data reliability, query performance, data caching and indexing, and many other benefits\n• Delta lake can be thought of as an extension of existing data lakes and can be configured per the data requirements","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Difference Between Data Lake and Delta lake","type":"concept","a":"Page | 15 Page | 16 =============AZURE DATA BRICKS TABLES AND SYNTAX==================== https://sparkbyexamples.com/pyspark/pyspark-withcolumn/ --- This link is reference of any commands CREATE A DATAFRAME BY USING A SPARK It means that Output will be visual in Table Syntax : DF=spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema=colunm) Joins: There is a Different joins like Inner, Left Join(left outer, left semi), Right Outer Join, Semi Table Syntax: emp = [(1,\"Smith\",-1,\"2018\",\"10\",\"M\",3000), \\ (2,\"Rose\",1,\"2010\",\"20\",\"M\",4000), \\ (3,\"Williams\",1,\"2010\",\"10\",\"M\",1000), \\ (4,\"Jones\",2,\"2005\",\"10\",\"F\",2000), \\ (5,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"40\",\"\",-1), \\ (6,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"50\",\"\",-1) \\ ] empColumns = [\"emp_id\",\"name\",\"superior_emp_id\",\"year_joined\", \\ \"emp_dept_id\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] empDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=emp, schema = empColumns) empDF.show() --- Creating a Data Frame dept = [(\"Finance\",10), \\ (\"Marketing\",20), \\ (\"Sales\",30), \\ (\"IT\",40) \\ ] deptColumns = [\"dept_name\",\"dept_id\"] deptDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=dept, schema = deptColumns) deptDF.show() ---- Creating a Data Frame and show Syntax: Page | 17 empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"Inner\" ).show() --- it is a Inner join empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"leftsemi\" ).show() -- it is a Left join SELFJOIN from pyspark.sql.functions import col --- (If col is not define on that we define this is statement) empDF.alias(\"emp1\").join(empDF.alias(\"emp2\"), \\ col(\"emp1.superior_emp_id\") == col(\"emp2.emp_id\"),\"inner\") \\ .select ((\"emp1.emp_id\"), (\"emp1.name\"), \\ col(\"emp2.emp_id\").alias(\"superid\"), \\ col(\"emp2.name\").alias(\"supername\")).show() WTIH COLUMN It menas to define a New colunm and add a any expression like add, multiple etc\\ Table: data = [('James','','Smith','1991-04-01','M',3000), ('Michael','Rose','','2000-05-19','M',4000), ('Robert','','Williams','1978-09-05','M',4000), ('Maria','Anne','Jones','1967-12-01','F',4000), ('Jen','Mary','Brown','1980-02-17','F',-1) ] columns = [\"firstname\",\"middlename\",\"lastname\",\"dob\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] from pyspark.sql import SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SparkByExamples.com').getOrCreate() df = spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema = columns) df.show() Syntax : df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show() HOW TO LOAD THE IN DATA BRICKS Go to Data and click on create table and Upload the data Syntax: loaddf=spark.read.parquet(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.parquet\") DROP THE COLUNM Syntax: userdf=loaddf.drop(\"ip_address,cc\") Page | 18 CONVERT THE PARQUET TO CSV FILE Syntax: csvdf=userdf.write.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) -- it will convert and store a New file usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) --- it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe usercsvdf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"user\") --- it will convert the filename into Table name %sql --- it is important because there is a default is python, so by using a SQl Command to use (if R language %R) select * from user MOUNT FROM DATA LAKE GEN2 TO DATABRICKS ➢ create a data lake gen2 and upload a parquet file ➢ create a Azure Active Dictory and create a New App Registration and also create a new cerfitications and secret ➢ Remember three things to copy id's is cilent.id(Application id), client.secret(value of cerfitications and secret) and client.endpoint(tenant id) ➢ Give a API permission and add the permission is Azure blob storage, Azure data lake ➢ Once go back to data lake gen2 and give Access Control(IAM) and ➢ add Role assignment in that Give role is Storage blob data reader(for to read the data in databaricks) , ➢ add memebers give the App Name (when you create a Azure Activity) ➢ Create New Notebook and remember select the language is Scala for understanding purpose ➢ syntax: ➢ This is all id of datalake gen2 and azure active dictory for to load the data val configs = Map( \"fs.azure.account.auth.type\" -> \"OAuth\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type\" -> \"org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id\" -> \"b5e7b223-6ccb-4aab-86b2-fd9773f11fe1\", --(Application id) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret\" -> \"_KI8Q~FStvRvFTPguBS~wwAVthPsxQ4zy4sxndnX\", - --(value of certificate and secret) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint\" -> \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/dee06880-2584- 4c56-bf98-b4023454c861/oauth2/token\"); --- (tenant id) dbutils.fs.mount( -------------This is For Mount Purpose source = \"abfss://employee@storimgdatalakr.dfs.core.windows.net/\", mountPoint = \"/mnt/storingthedata\", extraConfigs = configs) Page | 19 %python df=spark.read.parquet(\"/mnt/storingthedata\") ---it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe %python display(df) -- it will the display dbutils.fs.unmount(\"/mnt/eclasessbatch112\") ----- it will unmount the data Delta Lake Delta lake it is like a Data Lake but in this Any changes in existing file it will store the data . ➢ schema enforcements ➢ Time travel /versions ➢ update ➢ delete ➢ merge create a Dataframe Syntax: df=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000),(200,'mani',2000),(300,'siva',3000)],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sa l\"]) df.show() df.write.format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") --- coverting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]).show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") -- it is to overwrite the data ---But it will not happend in Delta---------- df.write.format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") --- converting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]) df1.show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") – (it will get error so change the syntax) Page | 20 df1.write.mode(\"append\").format('delta').option(\"mergeschema\",True).save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingd elta/filedelta\") --- it will the update the table spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",timestampAsof=\"2022-06-21","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"MERGERS: It is a Comparing the table and dimension table if dimension table is not existiong the vale it","type":"concept","a":"will update , insert delete. create table customer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), price int) insert into customer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600),(4,'oiu',500) select * from customer create table dimcustomer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), Page | 46 price int) insert into dimcustomer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600) update customer set price=800 where price=400 delete customer where price=900 merge dimcustomer as target using customer as source on source.cno=target.cno when not matched by target then --- it will be insert insert(cno,cname,price) values(source.cno,source.cname,source.cno) ii ti will be update when matched and target.cno=2 then update set target.cname=source.cname, target.price=source.price when not matched by source then ---- it will delete . delete;","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"05 AM — it missed the window. This is late arriving data.","type":"concept","a":"• Strategy 1: Watermark with a Grace/Tolerance Period Add a buffer period to your WHERE clause to catch late data\n• Example — Pipeline picks up last 2 hours as buffer: -- Standard incremental load SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= '2024-01-15 00:00:00' AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' -- With 2-hour grace period to catch late arrivals SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= DATEADD(HOUR, -2, '2024-01-15 00:00:00') AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' Strategy 2: Upsert (Merge) Instead of Insert Only Instead of just inserting new rows, use MERGE so late data updates existing records without creating duplicates. -- Using MERGE in Azure Synapse / SQL MERGE INTO target_sales AS T USING source_sales AS S ON T.order_id = S.order_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET T.status = S.status, T.amount = S.amount WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, status, amount, created_at) VALUES (S.order_id, S.status, S.amount, S.created_at);\n• Strategy 3: Delta Lake (Databricks) — Time Travel Delta Lake keeps history of all changes\n• If late data arrives, you can reprocess a specific date partition. # Read Delta table as of a specific time (Time Travel) df = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('/mnt/sales/delta') # Upsert late data into Delta table Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 4 from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/sales/delta') deltaTable.alias('T').merge( late_df.alias('S'), 'T.order_id = S.order_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute() Strategy 4: Partition Reprocessing If late data arrives for an old date, re-run the pipeline only for that partition rather than the whole table. • Use ADF pipeline parameters to pass a specific date/partition • Overwrite only that partition in the target (e.g., ADLS partition: year=2024/month=01/day=15) • This avoids full reload and is much faster 💡 Tip: In an interview say: 'We handled late arriving data using a combination of a grace period watermark and MERGE/Upsert in Synapse\n• For Delta Lake, we used merge operations and partition reprocessing when needed.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How to handle incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• I implement incremental load using watermark columns like ModifiedDate or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table, and in each run I extract only new or updated records\n• Then I load data into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• A(cid:332)er successful load, I update the watermark to ensure no data duplica(cid:415)on","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If unchanged:","type":"concept","a":"o Do nothing Use Delta MERGE with update and insert logic.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A schema defines the structure of your data: column names, data types, and nullability\n• When new data arrives, it may or may not match the existing table schema\n• Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 4 Schema Enforcement (Default Behavior in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake validates all incoming data against the current table schema\n• If new data has extra columns or mismatched types — the write FAILS with a clear error\n• This is the DEFAULT — protects data quality in production. # Existing Delta table schema: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE # New data has an extra column 'discount' new_df = spark.createDataFrame( [(1001, 501, 99.99, 0.10)], ['order_id', 'customer_id', 'amount', 'discount'] ) # Schema Enforcement in action — this FAILS new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # AnalysisException: A schema mismatch detected when writing. # Column 'discount' is not defined in the table\n• When to enforce schema: • Production financial or compliance tables • When schema changes must go through a change control review • When you want the pipeline to fail loudly rather than silently corrupt data Schema Evolution (Opt-in in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake auto-updates the table schema when mergeSchema=true\n• New columns are added; existing columns are untouched\n• Old rows will show NULL for newly added columns. # Enable Schema Evolution — write succeeds, new column is added new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # Table schema is now: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE, discount DOUBLE # # Old rows: discount = NULL # New rows: discount = 0.10 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 5 # Enable globally at Spark session level: spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled','true') Comparison Table Aspect Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default? YES — Delta default No — opt-in with mergeSchema=true Extra column Write FAILS with error Column added automatically to arrives table Wrong data type Write FAILS Write FAILS (type mismatch not auto-evolved) Old rows for new N/A NULL value column Best for Prod, financial, compliance data Rapidly changing source systems Interview Tip: Say: 'Delta enforces schema by default to protect data quality\n• For sources that evolve their schema (add new columns), we enable mergeSchema=true\n• Data type changes always need a manual ALTER TABLE — Delta never auto-changes types.' Q5 Function to Insert Newly Arriving Data — MERGE / Upsert The Answer: Use MERGE (Upsert) When new data arrives, you typically need to: INSERT rows that do not exist yet, and UPDATE rows that already exist\n• The function for this is MERGE — also called Upsert (Update + Insert)\n• Delta Lake MERGE — Python API from delta.tables import DeltaTable # Existing Delta table (destination) delta_table = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/customers') # Incoming new data (source) new_data = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/raw/customers_today/') # MERGE: update matching rows, insert new rows Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 6 delta_table.alias('target').merge( new_data.alias('source'), 'target.customer_id = source.customer_id' # match key ) \\ .whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ # update all columns if key found .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ # insert full row if key not found .execute() print('MERGE complete — no duplicates, no data loss.') MERGE with SQL Syntax (Databricks SQL) MERGE INTO customers AS target USING new_customers AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND source.updated_at > target.updated_at THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = source.updated_at WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, source.created_at, source.updated_at) -- Optional: remove records deleted at source WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE Real-World Example: Daily Customer Incremental Load # Step 1: Read only changed records (incremental extract) new_customers = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM source_db.customers WHERE updated_at >= current_date() - INTERVAL 1 DAY ''') # Step 2: Merge into Delta dimension table delta_cust = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/dim_customers') delta_cust.alias('t').merge( new_customers.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdate(set={ 'name': 's.name', Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 7 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' )).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ 'customer_id': 's.customer_id', 'name': 's.name', 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'created_at': 's.created_at', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' }).execute() print('Incremental load complete.') Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'To insert newly arriving data into an existing Delta table, we use MERGE (upsert)","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Simple Definition A Delta Table is a table format built on Parquet files with an added transaction log (_delta_log)\n• It gives you ACID transactions, time travel, schema enforcement, and MERGE support\n• It is the standard table type in Databricks (open-source Delta Lake project)\n• Core Features Feature What It Means Example ACID Transactions All-or-nothing writes — no Pipeline fails mid-write? Table stays partial data clean Time Travel Query older versions of data SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 MERGE / Upsert Insert + Update in one atomic Daily incremental customer load step Schema Rejects wrong schema by Extra column fails the write Enforcement default Schema Evolution Auto-adds new columns (opt- mergeSchema=true in) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 8 OPTIMIZE Compacts small files Speeds up future reads automatically Z-Ordering Collocates related data in ZORDER BY date for fast range same files queries Streaming Support Works with Structured Real-time events land into Delta Streaming table How Delta Table Storage Works # Delta table = Parquet files + _delta_log folder # Folder structure on ADLS / S3: '/mnt/delta/sales/' ' _delta_log/' ' 00000000000000000000.json -- table created' ' 00000000000000000001.json -- first batch loaded' ' 00000000000000000002.json -- merge operation ran' ' 00000000000000000003.json -- OPTIMIZE ran' ' part-00000-abc123.snappy.parquet' ' part-00001-def456.snappy.parquet' ' ...' # Every write is an entry in _delta_log # This is what enables Time Travel and ACID Time Travel Examples # Read table as of 7 days ago (useful for debugging) df_old = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-08') \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # Read a specific version df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', 5) \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # View full history from delta.tables import DeltaTable dt = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/sales') dt.history(10).show(truncate=False) # SQL versions # SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-08' # SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 9 Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'A Delta Table is Parquet + a transaction log\n• The transaction log is what gives us ACID guarantees, time travel to query historical versions, and the ability to do MERGE operations\n• It is the foundation of the Lakehouse architecture in Databricks — data warehouse reliability with data lake scalability.' Q7 Pipeline Running Slowly — Causes and Fixes Step 1: Where to Look First • Databricks Spark UI: Job Run page -> Stages tab -> look for the slowest stage • ADF Monitor: Activity Runs -> check which activity consumes the most time • Compare to previous runs — if it was faster before, something changed Cause 1: Data Skew One partition has far more data than others\n• One task takes 30 minutes while all others finish in 2 minutes. # Symptom: Spark UI shows one task dramatically slower than others # Diagnose: check partition sizes from pyspark.sql.functions import spark_partition_id df.groupBy(spark_partition_id()).count().orderBy('count(1)',ascending=False).show() # Fix 1: Enable Adaptive Query Execution (Databricks default ON) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') # Fix 2: Salt the skewed join key from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, lit, rand SALT = 10 df_salted = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('customer_id'), lit('_'), (rand()*SALT).cast('int'))) Cause 2: Small File Problem Thousands of tiny Parquet files = thousands of file-open operations\n• Reads become very slow. # Diagnose: list files in Delta table folder files = dbutils.fs.ls('/mnt/delta/sales/') print(f'File count: {len(files)}') # > 1000 = problem # Fix: Run OPTIMIZE to compact small files into larger ones spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales`') Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 10 # Fix with Z-Ordering: collocate data by the most-queried column spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales` ZORDER BY (sale_date)') # Fix: Write fewer files by coalescing before write df.coalesce(20).write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/mnt/delta/sales') Cause 3: Missing Broadcast Join for Small Tables Default joins shuffle BOTH sides\n• If one table is small, broadcast it to avoid shuffle entirely. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Slow: both DataFrames shuffled df_result = df_large_sales.join(df_dim_region, 'region_id') # Fast: small table sent to all nodes — zero shuffle df_result = df_large_sales.join(broadcast(df_dim_region), 'region_id') # Raise auto-broadcast threshold from 10MB to 50MB spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', str(50*1024*1024)) Cause 4: Full Table Scan (No Partition Pruning) Reading the entire table when only a small date range is needed wastes time. # Slow: reads ALL partitions then filters in memory df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/') df_filtered = df.filter(col('sale_date') == '2024-01-15') # Fast: only reads the matching partition folder df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/year=2024/month=01/day=15/') # For Delta tables — use predicate pushdown in SQL queries df = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM sales WHERE sale_date = '2024-01-15' -- Spark prunes to this partition AND region = 'North' ''') Cause 5: Shuffle Partitions Too High or Too Low # Default spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200 # For 1GB data, 200 partitions = 5MB each — too many small tasks # Enable AQE to auto-tune (Databricks default) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') # AQE merges small shuffle partitions automatically Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 11 # Manual rule of thumb: aim for ~128MB per partition # 10 GB data / 128 MB = 80 partitions spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '80') Summary Checklist Problem Symptom Fix Data Skew 1 task much slower than AQE skewJoin + salting others Small Files Slow reads, many tiny files OPTIMIZE + ZORDER on Delta Missing broadcast Large shuffle for small broadcast() hint or raise threshold join lookup table Full table scan Reading GB/TB for MB Partition pruning, predicate pushdown result Too many shuffle 200 tiny tasks for small data Enable AQE or lower manually partitions Cluster too small Spill to disk, OOM errors Scale up memory, add more workers Interview Tip: Structure your answer: 'I open the Spark UI first to find which stage is slow and look for signs of skew, shuffle, or spill\n• The most common causes are data skew, small file problem, missing broadcast joins, or full table scans\n• I fix each systematically using AQE, OPTIMIZE, broadcast hints, and partition filters.' Q8 Python: First Occurrence of Values from a List with Duplicates Problem Given a list with duplicates, print or return only the FIRST occurrence of each value in order\n• Input: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 1: Using a Set (Most Common Interview Answer) # Method 1 — Set to track already-seen values # Time: O(n) | Space: O(n) | Order preserved: YES Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 12 def first_occurrences(lst): seen = set() # remembers values we have already added result = [] for item in lst: if item not in seen: result.append(item) seen.add(item) return result # Test data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] print(first_occurrences(data)) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 2: dict.fromkeys() — Python One-Liner # Method 2 — dict.fromkeys removes duplicates and preserves order # Python 3.7+ dicts are ordered by insertion data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = list(dict.fromkeys(data)) print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # How it works: # dict.fromkeys(data) => {3:None, 1:None, 4:None, 5:None, 9:None, 2:None, 6:None} # Keys are inherently unique and insertion-ordered # list() converts back to a plain list Method 3: Print Directly data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] seen = set() for item in data: if item not in seen: print(item, end=' ') seen.add(item) # Output: 3 1 4 5 9 2 6 Method 4: For Strings names = ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Alice', 'Charlie', 'Bob', 'David'] Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 13 seen = set() for name in names: if name not in seen: print(name) seen.add(name) # Output: # Alice # Bob # Charlie # David Method 5: Using pandas / PySpark (Data Engineering) # pandas import pandas as pd data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = pd.Series(data).drop_duplicates(keep='first').tolist() print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # PySpark — for large distributed datasets # df.dropDuplicates(['column_name']) # drops duplicate rows by column # df.dropDuplicates() # drops fully duplicate rows Complexity Comparison Method Time Space Order? Best For Set tracking (Method 1) O(n) O(n) Yes Most readable, interview default dict.fromkeys (Method O(n) O(n) Yes Shortest code — Python 3.7+ 2) pandas O(n) O(n) Yes DataFrames, data engineering drop_duplicates PySpark O(n) Low No Big data on cluster dropDuplicates dist. (unless sorted) Interview Tip: In interview: Start with Method 1 — explain the set-based logic step by step","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"CDC (Change Data Capture) and CDF (Change Data Feed)","type":"concept","a":"• CDC — Change Data Capture CDC is the process of identifying and capturing changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) made to a source database and propagating those changes to a target system — without doing a full reload every time\n• CDC Methods • Log-based CDC — reads the database transaction log (WAL/binlog)\n• Tools: Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, Oracle GoldenGate • Trigger-based CDC — DB triggers write change records to a shadow table • Timestamp-based CDC — query source using last_modified > watermark • Diff-based CDC — compare full snapshots to identify changes (slow, expensive) -- Timestamp-based CDC example (SQL Server / ADB) SELECT * FROM source_table WHERE last_modified_date > '2024-01-01 00:00:00' AND last_modified_date <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP CDF — Change Data Feed (Delta Lake Feature) CDF is a Delta Lake feature that tracks row-level changes (insert, update, delete, update_preimage) in a Delta table\n• It creates a built-in change log that downstream consumers can read incrementally\n• Enable and Use CDF -- Enable CDF on a Delta table ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Or at creation time CREATE TABLE my_table (id INT, name STRING) TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Read changes from version 5 onwards df = spark.read.format('delta') .option('readChangeFeed', 'true') .option('startingVersion', 5) .table('my_table') -- Columns added by CDF: -- _change_type: insert | update_preimage | update_postimage | delete -- _commit_version: Delta log version -- _commit_timestamp: When the change was committed Feature CDC CDF (Delta) Scope Source database changes Delta table row-level changes Granularity Row-level (I/U/D) Row-level with pre/post image for updates Setup External tool (Debezium) or DB ALTER TABLE + TBLPROPERTIES config Streaming Yes — Kafka/Event Hub Yes — readStream with readChangeFeed Use case Sync source DB to lakehouse Propagate Delta changes downstream Page 2 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Optimize Queries in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"1. Partitioning • Partition tables on low-cardinality filter columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning — too many small partitions increases metadata overhead df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save(path)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• Z-ORDER on high-cardinality columns used in WHERE clauses (customer_id, transaction_id) • Use Liquid Clustering for dynamic, incremental clustering without partition rewrites OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) -- compact + cluster VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- remove stale files","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Parquet Format vs JSON Format","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Feature Parquet JSON Storage layout Columnar — stores each column Row-based — stores each record together together Compression Snappy/GZIP — typically 75-90% Minimal — largely uncompressed smaller text Schema Embedded in file footer (strongly typed) No schema — inferred at read time Query speed 5-20x faster — column pruning + Slow — must read all fields per row predicate pushdown Predicate pushdown Yes — skips row groups outside filter Not supported range Splittable Yes — parallel reads Only with JSON Lines format Null handling Efficient native encoding String 'null' or missing key Nested types Supported natively (struct, array, map) Natural — JSON is inherently nested Human readable No — binary format Yes — text based Best for Analytics, large-scale processing, Delta APIs, config files, event messages Write speed Slower (encoding overhead) Faster (plain text write) Rule of thumb: Always convert landing-zone JSON/CSV files to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Parquet gives columnar compression and predicate pushdown\n• Delta adds ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement on top","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE + ZORDER — compact small Delta files and cluster data for file skipping • Liquid Clustering — dynamic incremental clustering; replaces partition + ZORDER • Photon Engine — vectorized C++ query engine; enable in cluster config for 3-10x speedup • Delta Caching — SSD-level caching of remote Parquet data on worker nodes • Auto Optimize — enable optimizeWrite and autoCompact for automatic file sizing • AQE — Adaptive Query Execution for runtime plan re-optimization • VACUUM — remove stale/orphan files to reduce storage scan overhead • Partition pruning — query only relevant partitions using WHERE on partition columns • Data skipping — Delta min/max stats skip irrelevant files automatically • Materialized Views — pre-compute expensive aggregations for fast dashboard reads -- Databricks-specific optimizations OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date); VACUUM transactions RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Auto Optimize properties ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' ); -- Liquid Clustering ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date);","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evaluation and Enforcement","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Enforcement (Schema Validation) Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects any writes where the incoming data does not match the existing table schema\n• It protects data quality by preventing corrupt or mismatched data from entering the table. • Throws an AnalysisException if new columns are added or data types mismatch • Enabled by default in Delta Lake Schema Evolution (mergeSchema) Schema evolution allows the table schema to be updated automatically when new columns are added to incoming data. # Allow new columns to be added automatically df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/table/') Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Feature Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default behaviour ON (strict) OFF (must opt-in) New columns in source Write fails Columns added automatically Data type mismatch Write fails Still fails (type safety) Use case Protect prod tables from bad Accommodate evolving source data schemas","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Tables Available in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Table Type Description Managed Table Both metadata AND data are managed by Databricks/Unity Catalog\n• Dropping the table deletes all data\n• External Table Only metadata is managed\n• Data resides at a specified external path\n• Dropping the table does NOT delete data\n• Delta Table Table backed by Delta Lake format\n• Supports ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement\n• Hive Metastore Table Legacy table registered in the Hive Metastore (available before Unity Catalog)\n• View A virtual table based on a query\n• No data stored; computed on-the-fly\n• Temporary View Session-scoped view that disappears when the session ends\n• Global Temporary View Cluster-scoped temp view; accessible across sessions but dropped when cluster restarts","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table? Advantages of Using Delta Table","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Table is a table stored in Delta Lake format — an open-source storage layer built on top of Parquet files with a transaction log (_delta_log) that brings ACID guarantees and advanced features to data lake storage\n• How Delta Table Works Internally • Data is stored as Parquet files in cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) • A transaction log directory (_delta_log) records every change as a JSON/Parquet checkpoint • Each write creates a new versioned set of Parquet files — old files are retained for time travel • Compaction (OPTIMIZE) merges small files into larger ones for query performance Advantages of Delta Table Feature Description ACID Transactions Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — concurrent reads/writes are safe Time Travel Query any previous version: SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 or TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' Schema Enforcement Rejects writes that don't match the table schema — prevents corrupt data Schema Evolution New columns can be added via mergeSchema without rewriting the table Upserts (MERGE) Efficient insert/update/delete using MERGE INTO — enables SCD and CDC patterns Unified Batch & Streaming Same Delta table can be written by a batch job and read as a stream simultaneously Scalable Metadata Z-ordering and data skipping speed up queries on large datasets OPTIMIZE & VACUUM OPTIMIZE compacts small files;\n• VACUUM removes old files saving storage costs Open Format Based on Parquet — readable by Spark, Hive, Presto, Trino, and other engines Useful Delta Table Commands -- Time travel SELECT * FROM employees VERSION AS OF 3;\n• SELECT * FROM employees TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-06-01'; -- Restore to previous version RESTORE TABLE employees TO VERSION AS OF 5;\n• Page 6 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 -- Compact small files OPTIMIZE employees ZORDER BY (department_id); -- Remove files older than 7 days VACUUM employees RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- View history DESCRIBE HISTORY employees;\n• Page 7 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"How to Build a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"• A fault-tolerant ETL pipeline continues to operate correctly even when individual components fail, ensuring no data loss, no duplicate processing, and automatic recovery — without manual intervention\n• Key Principles of Fault Tolerance Principle Implementation Idempotency Re-running the pipeline produces the same result — no duplicates, no data loss Exactly-Once Processing Each record processed exactly once using checkpoints and unique keys Checkpointing Stream state saved to durable storage so recovery resumes from last checkpoint Retry Logic Automatic retries on transient failures (network, API timeout, cluster preemption) Dead Letter Queue Bad records quarantined separately for later inspection instead of failing the job Monitoring & Alerting Proactive alerts on failures, SLA breaches, and data quality violations Schema Validation Reject or quarantine records that violate schema before writing to target ACID Writes Use Delta Lake MERGE for upserts — safe for partial failures and re-runs Architecture of a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline • Layer 1 – Ingest: Autoloader with checkpoints ensures no file is missed or processed twice • Layer 2 – Bronze: Raw Delta table written with ACID guarantees; idempotent via COPY INTO • Layer 3 – Silver: Cleansing + MERGE INTO Delta for upserts; bad records to quarantine table • Layer 4 – Gold: Aggregation; only runs after Silver passes data quality Expectations • Layer 5 – Orchestration: Databricks Workflows with retry policies and failure alerts Full PySpark Fault-Tolerant Pipeline Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F from delta.tables import DeltaTable import logging # ── CONFIG ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── SOURCE_PATH = '/mnt/landing/employees/' SCHEMA_PATH = '/mnt/schema/employees/' CHECKPOINT_PATH = '/mnt/checkpoint/employees/' BRONZE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/bronze/employees/' SILVER_PATH = '/mnt/delta/silver/employees/' QUARANTINE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/quarantine/employees/' Page 17 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # ── STEP 1: INGEST with Autoloader (Fault-Tolerant Streaming) ─── df_raw = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', SCHEMA_PATH) .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true') .load(SOURCE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 2: WRITE TO BRONZE (Idempotent via checkpoint) ───────── (df_raw.writeStream .format('delta') .outputMode('append') .option('checkpointLocation', CHECKPOINT_PATH + 'bronze/') .trigger(availableNow=True) # process all pending files once .start(BRONZE_PATH) .awaitTermination() ) # ── STEP 3: CLEANSE + QUARANTINE BAD RECORDS ──────────────────── df_bronze = spark.read.format('delta').load(BRONZE_PATH) df_valid = df_bronze.filter( F.col('emp_id').isNotNull() & F.col('salary').cast('integer').isNotNull() & (F.col('salary').cast('integer') > 0) ) df_invalid = df_bronze.subtract(df_valid) # Write bad records to quarantine for investigation (df_invalid .withColumn('quarantine_reason', F.lit('Failed validation: null emp_id or invalid salary')) .withColumn('quarantine_ts', F.current_timestamp()) .write.format('delta').mode('append').save(QUARANTINE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 4: UPSERT TO SILVER (Idempotent MERGE) ───────────────── df_clean = (df_valid .dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) .withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) .withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) .withColumn('updated_at', F.current_timestamp()) ) if DeltaTable.isDeltaTable(spark, SILVER_PATH): silver = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, SILVER_PATH) (silver.alias('tgt') .merge(df_clean.alias('src'), 'tgt.emp_id = src.emp_id') .whenMatchedUpdateAll() .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() .execute() Page 18 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 ) else: df_clean.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save(SILVER_PATH) # ── STEP 5: GOLD AGGREGATION ──────────────────────────────────── df_silver = spark.read.format('delta').load(SILVER_PATH) df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('department').agg( F.count('emp_id').alias('headcount'), F.avg('salary').alias('avg_salary'), F.max('salary').alias('max_salary') ) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/mnt/delta/gold/dept_summary /') print('Pipeline completed successfully.') Retry & Alerting in Databricks Workflows Feature How to Configure Job Retries Set Max Retries = 3 and Retry Interval on each task in Databricks Workflows UI Depends On Task B runs only if Task A succeeds — dependency chain stops cascade failures On Failure Alerting Configure email/Slack/Teams alerts in Workflows notification settings Timeout Set per-task timeout to detect hung jobs and trigger retry/failure handling Run If Condition Conditional task execution based on upstream task outcome (Success/Failed/All) DLT Expectations @dlt.expect_or_quarantine() routes bad records without failing the pipeline Summary Checklist for Fault-Tolerant ETL • Use Autoloader with checkpointing for idempotent file ingestion • Write all layers as Delta tables for ACID guarantees • Use MERGE INTO for upserts — safe to re-run without duplicates • Separate valid vs invalid records — quarantine bad data instead of failing • Validate schema and data quality at Silver layer before Gold • Configure retry policies and timeout in Databricks Workflows • Set up monitoring alerts on job failure, SLA breach, and quarantine record spike • Log pipeline metrics (record counts, error counts, durations) to a monitoring table • Test failure scenarios: missing file, schema change, duplicate keys, corrupt data Page 19","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"While migrating the data, did you just copy the data or did you use incremental","type":"concept","a":"• load? We used a combination of both approaches: Full Load (Initial Load): • For the first migration, we did a full historical load — extracting all records from the source and loading them into the Bronze layer as-is. • This was a one-time bulk load to establish the baseline dataset\n• Incremental Load (Ongoing Sync): • After the initial load, we switched to incremental loads to capture only new or changed records. • We used watermark columns (e.g., updated_at, created_date) or Change Data Capture (CDC) to identify changed rows. • In Databricks, we used Auto Loader for file-based incremental ingestion and MERGE INTO (Delta Lake) for upsert operations\n• This approach minimized load time, reduced compute costs, and kept the target data fresh without full re- processing\n• Section 2: Architecture Concepts","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"metadata stored?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Format: • By default, tables created in Databricks are stored in Delta Lake format (.parquet files + _delta_log transaction log). • Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that brings ACID transactions to data lakes. • Physically, data is stored as columnar Parquet files on cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS)\n• Metadata Storage: • Delta Transaction Log (_delta_log): JSON files stored alongside data files in the table directory\n• Records every operation (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE) — used for time travel and ACID guarantees. • Hive Metastore / Unity Catalog: Table-level metadata (table name, schema, location, partitioning) is stored in the Databricks Metastore (by default, a managed Hive Metastore or Unity Catalog in newer workspaces)\n• So metadata lives in two places: the _delta_log for data-level history and the Metastore for table-level schema/location information","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is VACUUM and when do we use it?","type":"concept","a":"• VACUUM is a Delta Lake command that removes old data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta transaction log\n• Why Vacuum is Needed: • Delta Lake's ACID support and time travel retain old file versions, which accumulate over time and consume storage. • VACUUM cleans up these obsolete files to reclaim storage space\n• Syntax: VACUUM table_name RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Default: 7 days -- Or in PySpark: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forName(spark, 'table_name') deltaTable.vacuum(168) # hours Important Notes: • Default retention period is 7 days (168 hours)\n• Setting it lower than 7 days requires disabling a safety check. • After VACUUM, you cannot time travel to versions older than the retention period. • Use it periodically in production to control storage costs\n• Section 4: Performance Optimization","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Is MERGE SCD-1 or SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• MERGE is primarily SCD-1 by default behavior. • SCD-1 (Overwrite): MERGE with WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE overwrites existing records — no history is kept. • SCD-2 (History): MERGE can implement SCD-2 but requires a more complex query — on match, you UPDATE the old row (close it) and INSERT a new row with the changed values\n• In Delta Lake, you can implement SCD-2 using MERGE with both WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE and WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT clauses, combined with is_current and date columns\n• Section 7: PySpark Coding","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What will be SCD2 logic and columns you will be using for your project. How will you","type":"concept","a":"implement? SCD Type 2 Logic + Columns Columns used:  business_key (emp_id)  start_date  end_date  is_ac(cid:415)ve  hash_diff (for change detec(cid:415)on) Logic:  If record changes → expire old record (end_date + is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  Insert new record with updated values  Maintain history Implementa(cid:415)on:  Delta MERGE INTO OR DLT apply_changes","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed?","type":"concept","a":"• Change Data Feed (CDF)  Tracks row-level changes in Delta tables  Captures INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE Used for:  incremental pipelines  CDC processing 7\n• Auto Loader  Incremental file inges(cid:415)on in Databricks  Automa(cid:415)cally detects new files  Handles schema evolu(cid:415)on Best for streaming inges(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore the table if data is corrupted?","type":"concept","a":"Restore corrupted table Using Delta Time Travel: RESTORE TABLE table_name TO VERSION AS OF 5;","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the use of SQL Warehouse in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Warehouse (previously called SQL Analytics Endpoint) is a serverless/dedicated compute resource optimized for running SQL queries on data stored in the Lakehouse (Delta Lake). • Enables BI tools (Tableau, Power BI) to connect via JDBC/ODBC. • Supports auto-stop and auto-scaling to control costs. • Serverless option eliminates cluster management; compute spins up in seconds. • Photon engine (vectorized query processing) dramatically speeds up SQL workloads\n• Change Data Feed (CDF)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is CDF (Change Data Feed) in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF is a Delta Lake feature that records row-level changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to a Delta table, producing a changelog that can be consumed by downstream processes. • Enable with: ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true) • Read changes: spark.readStream.format('delta').option('readChangeFeed','true').table('my_table') • Use cases: CDC pipelines, incremental ETL, audit trails, real-time aggregations. • Change types exposed: insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete\n• Efficient Model for Daily Streaming Data","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"We are getting streaming data daily — how do you design an efficient model for it?","type":"concept","a":"• Recommended architecture: Medallion (Bronze → Silver → Gold) with Delta Lake + Structured Streaming. • Bronze layer: Ingest raw streaming data with Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — schema evolution, exactly-once, checkpointing. • Silver layer: Parse, clean, deduplicate (use MERGE INTO for upserts via CDF or watermarking). • Gold layer: Aggregated, business-ready tables updated incrementally. • Use trigger(availableNow=True) for micro-batch on schedule, or trigger(processingTime='X seconds') for near-real-time. • Partition Delta tables by date (year/month/day) to enable partition pruning on queries. • Enable optimize & ZORDER on high-cardinality filter columns. • Monitor with Spark UI, Delta transaction log, and Databricks cluster metrics\n• Resolving Data Skew","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write the code for SCD2","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 Code (PySpark / Databricks) (cid:44934) Using Delta Lake MERGE INTO (most asked in interviews) MERGE INTO target_table t USING source_table s ON t.emp_id = s.emp_id AND t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = true WHEN MATCHED AND ( t.salary <> s.salary OR t.dept_id <> s.dept_id ) THEN UPDATE SET t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = false, t.end_date = current_date() WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, start_date, end_date, is_ac(cid:415)ve ) VALUES ( s.emp_id, s.name, s.dept_id, s.salary, current_date(), null, true ); (cid:44934) Key idea:  Old record → expired (is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  New record → inserted with new version  History preserved","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on strategies in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"Common answers:  Cache frequently used DataFrames  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Broadcast Join  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE)  Delta Cache  OPTIMIZE  ZORDER  Liquid Clustering  Predicate Pushdown  Column Pruning  Avoid UDFs  Auto Op(cid:415)mize","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, ZORDER and Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE ZORDER Liquid Clustering Compacts small files Organizes data by column values Automa(cid:415)cally manages clustering Improves file size Improves data skipping No manual par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning Reduces file count Faster filtering Be(cid:425)er for evolving workloads Doesn't sort Sorts within files Re-clusters automa(cid:415)cally Example: OPTIMIZE 1000 small files ↓ 10 large files ZORDER OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY(customer_id) Improves filtering: WHERE customer_id=100 Liquid Clustering No par(cid:415)(cid:415)on maintenance\n• Databricks automa(cid:415)cally reclusters data","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Data Lake and Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• Data Lake Delta Lake Storage layer Storage layer + transac(cid:415)on layer No ACID ACID transac(cid:415)ons No versioning Time Travel No schema enforcement Schema enforcement & evolu(cid:415)on Slow updates MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE No transac(cid:415)on log _delta_log maintains metadata Interview Answer \"A Data Lake stores files such as Parquet, CSV, or JSON, whereas Delta Lake builds on top of a Data Lake by adding ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, and a transac(cid:415)on log\n• Delta Lake makes lake storage more reliable for produc(cid:415)on data engineering.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Managed table deleted a(cid:332)er reten(cid:415)on and all files removed. How do you recreate it?","type":"concept","a":"• If:  Managed table is dropped  Reten(cid:415)on period has passed  Underlying data files are permanently removed  All versions are gone You cannot recover it using Delta Lake features\n• Time Travel and RESTORE depend on the transac(cid:415)on log and data files s(cid:415)ll exis(cid:415)ng\n• Recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from backup  Reload from the source system  Recover using cloud storage versioning (if enabled)  Use disaster recovery copies Interview Answer \"If both the Delta log and data files have been permanently deleted a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period, the table cannot be recreated from Delta metadata alone\n• Recovery would require restoring from backup, cloud storage versioning, or reloading the data from the source.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — File Compaction Delta tables accumulate many small files over time\n• OPTIMIZE compacts them into larger Parquet files for faster reads\n• OPTIMIZE my_table OPTIMIZE my_table WHERE date = '2024-01-01' -- with partition filter Z-ORDER — Co-locate Related Data Z-ORDER clusters related data within the same files\n• Queries on Z-ordered columns can skip irrelevant files automatically (data skipping)\n• OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_date) Feature OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Purpose Compact small files Cluster data by column values Benefit Faster scans, less overhead Data skipping on filter queries Best For After many small writes/streaming High-cardinality filter columns Used Together Yes — in a single OPTIMIZE ZORDER BY command Page 3 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide Best Z-ORDER columns: those appearing frequently in WHERE clauses with high cardinality (customer_id, event_timestamp)\n• Avoid low-cardinality columns like boolean flags","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize Delta Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partition on low-cardinality columns used in filters\n• Avoid over-partitioning. df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year', 'month').save(path)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + ZORDER","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"VACUUM — Remove Stale Files","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- 7 days default","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Optimize (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' )","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Lake vs Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"Feature Data Lake (Plain) Delta Lake ACID Transactions No Yes — full ACID support Schema Enforcement No Yes — schema-on-write Time Travel No Yes — VERSION AS OF Upserts / Deletes Complex or not supported Yes — MERGE INTO Streaming + Batch Separate pipelines needed Unified — same table File Format Parquet / CSV / JSON Parquet + _delta_log Data Quality Manual checks required Built-in constraints Key Delta Lake Commands -- Time Travel SELECT * FROM my_table VERSION AS OF 5 SELECT * FROM my_table TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' -- MERGE (Upsert) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Live Table?","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Live Table (DLT) is a managed ETL framework in Databricks used to build reliable data pipelines\n• Instead of manually crea(cid:415)ng jobs, DLT:  Reads data  Applies transforma(cid:415)ons  Loads data  Handles dependencies  Monitors pipeline health  Enforces data quality rules Example: CREATE OR REFRESH LIVE TABLE sales_clean AS SELECT * FROM LIVE.sales_raw WHERE amount > 0;\n• Data Quality in Delta Live Tables One of the biggest advantages is built-in expecta(cid:415)ons\n• Example: CONSTRAINT valid_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW;\n• This automa(cid:415)cally removes invalid records from the pipeline\n• Interview Answer If the interviewer asks: \"What is the difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table?\" \"A Delta Table is a storage layer based on Delta Lake that provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and (cid:415)me travel\n• A Delta Live Table is a managed ETL framework that builds and orchestrates data pipelines using Delta Tables\n• DLT adds features like automa(cid:415)c dependency management, scheduling, monitoring, lineage, and built-in data quality expecta(cid:415)ons, whereas with Delta Tables these aspects are managed manually.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"MERGE with UPDATE gives a duplicate rows error. What is the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• \"This usually means that the MERGE condi(cid:415)on is matching mul(cid:415)ple source rows to the same target row\n• Common causes are:  Duplicate business keys in the source.  Duplicate records in the target.  An incorrect ON condi(cid:415)on that is not unique\n• The solu(cid:415)on is to remove duplicates using ROW_NUMBER() or ensure the join key is unique before execu(cid:415)ng the MERGE.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Databricks job fails a(cid:332)er 5 minutes, what happens in Parquet and Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Parquet \"Parquet files do not support transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails while wri(cid:415)ng, par(cid:415)ally wri(cid:425)en files may remain in the storage loca(cid:415)on, requiring manual cleanup.\" Delta \"Delta Lake provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails before the transac(cid:415)on commits, the Delta transac(cid:415)on log is not updated, and readers con(cid:415)nue to see the previous consistent version\n• This prevents par(cid:415)al or corrupted data from being visible.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If ADLS storage is full, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would verify whether the issue is storage capacity, account limits, or quotas\n• Then I would:  Remove temporary or intermediate files.  Run VACUUM on Delta tables to delete obsolete files a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period.  Archive historical data to a lower-cost storage (cid:415)er.  Delete unused datasets a(cid:332)er confirming reten(cid:415)on policies.  Review lifecycle management rules to automa(cid:415)cally move or delete old data.  If business growth requires it, increase storage capacity or distribute data across addi(cid:415)onal storage accounts.\" Round 3 : (Client Round)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Files Do You Usually Work With?","type":"concept","a":"• CSV — legacy systems, flat files, exports from operational systems • JSON — API responses, event streams, semi-structured data • Parquet — preferred columnar format for analytics; compressed, schema-embedded • Delta — Parquet + transaction log; used for all curated/processed layers • Avro — schema-embedded binary format; common in Kafka/streaming pipelines • ORC — columnar format used in Hive-based ecosystems • XML — legacy enterprise and EDI data sources Best Practice: Always convert raw CSV/JSON to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer. Parquet provides columnar compression and predicate pushdown. Delta adds ACID transactions and time travel. Section 2 | Spark Joins & Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"In a Transaction Table, Which Column Would You Use for Z-Ordering?","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Choose columns that are: (a) frequently used in WHERE clauses, and (b) have high cardinality. • transaction_date / event_timestamp — most queries filter by date range • customer_id — if queries frequently look up a specific customer • store_id / region_id — if queries filter by location Best Answer: \"I would Z-ORDER on transaction_date combined with customer_id\n• Most business queries filter by a date range AND a specific customer, so co-locating transactions for the same customer within the same date files maximizes file skipping.\" Avoid Z-ORDERing on low-cardinality columns like status (Active/Inactive) or boolean flags — data skipping will be minimal since files will contain both values anyway","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Default Column for Z-Order Indexing?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Lake has NO default Z-ORDER column\n• Z-ORDER must always be explicitly specified: OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date) OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date, customer_id) However, Delta Lake automatically collects min/max statistics for the first 32 columns\n• These enable automatic data skipping (file-level pruning) even without explicit Z-ORDER\n• You can control this: ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.dataSkippingNumIndexedCols' = '5' -- collect stats for first 5 columns only )","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Z-Order Indexes","type":"concept","a":"• Z-Ordering (Z-curve/space-filling curve) is a multi-dimensional data clustering technique that maps multi- column data to a single linear order while preserving proximity — values close in N-dimensional space end up in the same files\n• How it works step-by-step • OPTIMIZE interleaves the bit representations of selected column values using a Z-curve algorithm • Related rows (same customer, same date) are co-located into the same Parquet files • Delta records min/max statistics per file for each column • When you query with WHERE clause on Z-ORDERed columns, Spark compares query predicates against file stats • Files whose min/max range does not include the query value are skipped entirely (data skipping) Aspect Partitioning Z-ORDER Best Together Cardinality Low (date, region) High (customer_id) Both Structure Physical folders per Co-locates within files Partition + ZORDER value Over-partition risk Yes — too many small No Partition on date files Changes needed Hard to change after Re-run OPTIMIZE Combine both creation Command partitionBy(col) OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER Same table, both applied BY -- Best practice: partition by date + Z-order by high-cardinality column Page 4 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save('/delta/transactions') OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_type)","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Liquid Clustering (introduced in Databricks Runtime 13.3+) is a dynamic data layout feature that replaces the need for partitioning + Z-ORDER\n• It automatically reorganizes data based on clustering columns without requiring manual partition planning or full table rewrites\n• Key advantages • No upfront partition column planning — clustering columns can be changed anytime with ALTER TABLE • Incremental: only newly written data is clustered — no full table rewrites Page 7 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Avoids partition explosion from high-cardinality columns • Auto-clustering runs in the background (Databricks-managed) -- Create table with Liquid Clustering CREATE TABLE transactions (id BIGINT, customer_id STRING, amount DOUBLE, txn_date DATE) CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date); -- Change clustering columns anytime ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id); -- Manually trigger clustering OPTIMIZE transactions;\n• Feature Partitioning Z-ORDER Liquid Clustering Cardinality Low only High Any Column flexibility Hard to change Re-run OPTIMIZE Change anytime Incremental No No Yes File management Manual VACUUM Manual OPTIMIZE Automatic Partition explosion Yes No No","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What are Materialized Views?","type":"concept","a":"• A Materialized View (MV) is a view whose results are pre-computed and stored as a physical Delta table\n• Unlike standard views that re-run the query on every read, MVs read from the stored result — fast and low-cost at query time. • Results stored physically — reads are as fast as a regular Delta table scan • Auto-refreshed when source tables change (Unity Catalog manages this) • Supports incremental refresh — only processes changed/new data, not full recompute • Ideal for expensive aggregations, multi-table joins, and frequently-queried reports Page 8 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales AS SELECT txn_date, store_id, SUM(amount) AS total_sales, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY txn_date, store_id;\n• REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales;\n• Aspect Standard View Materialized View Data stored No Yes — physical Delta table Query speed Depends on source tables Fast — reads pre-computed result Freshness Always up-to-date Refreshed on schedule or source change Cost at query time Full query recomputed Just a Delta table scan Best use case Simple aliasing, simple selects Heavy aggregations, dashboards, reports Section 7 | File Ingestion & Auto Loader","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"CSV Files Contain Corrupted Rows with Extra Columns — How Do You Handle","type":"concept","a":"• Them? Spark provides three read modes for handling malformed records in CSV/JSON: Mode 1 — PERMISSIVE (default, recommended for production) Null-fills mismatched columns and captures the raw corrupt record in a special column. df = (spark.read .option('mode', 'PERMISSIVE') .option('columnNameOfCorruptRecord', '_corrupt_record') .schema(defined_schema) # schema required to use _corrupt_record .csv('/data/raw/*.csv') ) good_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNull()) bad_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNotNull()) # Write bad records to quarantine Delta table Page 11 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) bad_df.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/delta/quarantine/csv_errors') Mode 2 — DROPMALFORMED (silent drop) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED').csv('/data/*.csv') # Bad rows silently discarded — use only if data loss is acceptable Mode 3 — FAILFAST (dev/test only) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'FAILFAST').csv('/data/*.csv') # Throws exception on first bad row — useful during development Mode 4 — Delta Live Tables Expectations (modern approach) @dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_row_count', 'col_count = expected') @dlt.expect_or_quarantine('no_null_id', 'id IS NOT NULL') Best Practice: Use PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + write bad rows to a quarantine Delta table\n• This keeps all data visible (no silent losses), enables root-cause analysis, and supports reprocessing after the source issue is fixed\n• Section 9 | Architecture & Cluster","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you load incremental data if the source has no Last Modified (cid:415)mestamp?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the source does not have a Last Modified or watermark column, there are several approaches depending on the business scenario:  Use the primary key and compare source and target records.  Perform a Delta MERGE to insert new records and update changed records.  Use Change Data Capture (CDC) if supported by the source system.  Generate a hash of the business columns and compare hashes to detect changes.  As a last resort, perform a full load if no reliable change detec(cid:415)on mechanism exists\n• In my projects, using a business key with hash comparison and a Delta MERGE has been an effec(cid:415)ve approach.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Apply Changes in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Yes — using:  Delta Live Tables (DLT) APPLY CHANGES  Or manually using MERGE INTO Used for:  SCD handling (Type 1/2)  CDC inges(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Pipeline re-run: overwrite or mul(cid:415)ple files? Delta log?","type":"concept","a":"Behavior:  In ADLS: o Usually creates mul(cid:415)ple part files  In Delta Lake: o Uses transac(cid:415)on log (_delta_log) Delta Log:  Stores metadata of all transac(cid:415)ons  Enables: o Time travel o ACID compliance o Rollback Format:  Parquet + _delta_log (JSON logs)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks components used","type":"concept","a":" Notebooks  Jobs  Clusters  Delta Lake  DBFS  Repos  Workflows","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize Delta tables (OPTIMIZE, ZORDER).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"Common methods:  Watermark column (LastModifiedDate)  Timestamp  CDC (Change Data Capture)  Delta MERGE Example: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE LastModifiedDate > ( SELECT MAX(LastLoadedDate) FROM AuditTable );","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Data format in your project","type":"concept","a":"Typical formats:  CSV (landing)  JSON (APIs)  Parquet (op(cid:415)mized storage)  Delta (curated layers) Interview (cid:415)p: \"We primarily store curated data in Delta format because it provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and be(cid:425)er performance.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What are Delta Tables?","type":"concept","a":"Delta tables are built on top of Parquet and provide:  ACID transac(cid:415)ons  Time Travel  Schema Enforcement  Schema Evolu(cid:415)on  MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE support  Be(cid:425)er performance with OPTIMIZE and ZORDER","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Write a MERGE statement.","type":"concept","a":"MERGE INTO Target t USING Source s ON t.EmpID = s.EmpID WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.Name = s.Name, t.Salary = s.Salary WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( EmpID, Name, Salary ) VALUES ( s.EmpID, s.Name, s.Salary );","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental Loading loads only new or changed data since the last load, instead of reloading everything (full load)\n• Common Approaches: • Timestamp-based: Load records where updated_at > last_run_timestamp • Watermark-based: Track max ID or timestamp as high watermark • CDC (Change Data Capture): Capture inserts/updates/deletes from source • Delta Lake MERGE (UPSERT): Merge changes into target table Benefits: Faster, cheaper, reduces source load # PySpark incremental load using watermark last_load_time = '2025-01-01 00:00:00' incremental_df = spark.read.jdbc(...) \\ .filter(col('updated_at') > last_load_time) # Delta MERGE (UPSERT) from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/delta/customers') target.alias('t').merge( incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE vs Z-ORDER","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE: Compacts small Delta files into larger files to improve read performance and reduce file count\n• OPTIMIZE delta.`/delta/sales` -- or OPTIMIZE sales_table Z-ORDER: Co-locates related data in the same set of files by reordering data based on specified columns\n• Best for high-cardinality columns used in filters\n• OPTIMIZE sales_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, order_date); -- Difference: -- OPTIMIZE alone: compacts files (smaller → bigger) -- ZORDER: compacts AND reorders data within files for faster skipping • OPTIMIZE: Use always for compacting small files • ZORDER: Use on frequently filtered/joined columns • Together: Maximum benefit — compact + co-locate","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"High Concurrency Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Optimized for multiple concurrent users • Supports Python, SQL, R — NOT Scala • Enforces user isolation (each user's code is isolated) • Best for BI tools and SQL analytics","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evolution","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Evolution allows changes to a Delta table's schema (adding/modifying columns) without breaking existing pipelines\n• Options: • mergeSchema: Add new columns from incoming data • overwriteSchema: Replace the entire schema (use with caution) # Enable schema evolution on write df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/delta/customers') # In SQL - enable auto merge SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true; # Alter table to add column manually ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN (loyalty_tier STRING);","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Transaction Log & ACID Properties","type":"concept","a":"• Transaction Log (_delta_log): The Delta transaction log is a directory of JSON files that records every change made to a Delta table\n• Each commit produces a new JSON file\n• ACID Properties in Delta: • Atomicity: Each transaction either fully succeeds or fully fails — no partial writes • Consistency: Schema enforcement ensures valid data at all times • Isolation: Concurrent reads/writes are isolated — snapshot isolation by default • Durability: Once committed, data is permanently stored even after failure # View transaction log DESCRIBE HISTORY orders_table; # Each JSON in _delta_log contains: # - add: files added # - remove: files removed/deleted # - metaData: schema changes # - protocol: reader/writer versions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Move Data from Delta Table to Another Location","type":"concept","a":"# Option 1: Write to new Delta location df = spark.read.format('delta').load('/delta/source_table') df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/delta/destination_table') # Option 2: DEEP CLONE (preserves history & metadata) CREATE TABLE destination_table DEEP CLONE source_table LOCATION '/delta/destination_table'; # Option 3: SHALLOW CLONE (references original files) CREATE TABLE destination_table SHALLOW CLONE source_table; # Option 4: Export to CSV/Parquet df.write.format('parquet').save('/output/parquet_export') df.write.format('csv').option('header', 'true').save('/output/csv_export')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental load","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load is a technique where we load only new or updated data instead of full refresh\n• I implement it using watermark columns like last_updated or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table and extract only delta data in each run\n• Then I load it into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• Finally, I update the watermark a(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on to ensure no duplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"What are the few small differences you see in databricks now comparing with 3 years","type":"concept","a":"back (only small differences not like unity catalog) “Over the last few years, Databricks has improved mainly in performance, automa(cid:415)on, and governance rather than just core func(cid:415)onality.” (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Key small differences: (cid:48132) a) Performance improvements  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) is much be(cid:425)er now  Faster shuffle handling  Be(cid:425)er auto-op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on of joins (cid:48131) b) Photon engine maturity  Much faster SQL execu(cid:415)on compared to earlier versions  Be(cid:425)er vectorized processing (cid:44931) c) Auto op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on features  Auto compac(cid:415)on of small files  Improved OPTIMIZE + ZORDER efficiency (cid:48130) d) Improved job orchestra(cid:415)on  Be(cid:425)er Workflows UI  Retry policies, task dependencies improved (cid:48133) e) Be(cid:425)er Delta Lake stability  More stable MERGE opera(cid:415)ons  Be(cid:425)er schema evolu(cid:415)on handling","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"“Yes, I have used both OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on environments in Delta Lake tables.” (cid:48132) OPTIMIZE  Used to compact small files  Improves read performance OPTIMIZE table_name; (cid:48131) ZORDER (with OPTIMIZE)  Improves query filtering OPTIMIZE table_name ZORDER BY (customer_id); (cid:44931) VACUUM  Removes old/unnecessary files VACUUM table_name; “We ensure reten(cid:415)on period is respected before running VACUUM to avoid dele(cid:415)ng files needed for (cid:415)me travel.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"governance","name":"Unity Catalog & Security","icon":"🔐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Unity Catalog and what problem does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance layer for data and AI assets across all workspaces in an account.\n• Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns, rows).\n• 3-level namespace: catalog.schema.table.\n• Built-in data lineage (column-level), audit logs, and Delta Sharing.\n• External locations + storage credentials for governed cloud storage access.\n• Row-level security (row filters) and column masking.\nSolves per-workspace (Hive metastore) silos with account-level governance and fine-grained security."},{"q":"Explain the Unity Catalog object hierarchy.","type":"concept","a":"Metastore (top-level, one per region) → Catalog → Schema (database) → objects (tables, views, volumes, functions, models).\n• Metastore stores metadata + permissions for all workspaces attached to it.\n• Permissions flow downward (grant at a higher level, inherits lower).\n• Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders.\nRelated objects: storage credentials, external locations, shares."},{"q":"Managed vs external tables AND managed vs external volumes in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — UC manages data lifecycle/location; DROP deletes metadata AND data.\n• External table — references a location you own (external location); DROP removes only metadata, files remain.\n• Managed volume — UC-managed storage for non-tabular files (images, PDFs, CSV); deleted with the volume.\n• External volume — references existing cloud storage you manage (via external location); files remain after dropping the volume.\nVolumes are like managed/external tables but for files."},{"q":"What are storage credentials and external locations in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage credential — a UC securable that holds cloud auth (managed identity / service principal / access connector) to access storage; no secrets in code.\n• External location — combines a cloud storage URL (abfss://...) with a storage credential, granting governed access to that path.\n• Access Connector — an Azure resource providing managed-identity access from Databricks to ADLS.\nTogether they let UC govern who can read/write specific storage paths."},{"q":"How do you implement row-level and column-level security in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Column-level: dynamic column masks — a UDF masks sensitive values based on IS_ACCOUNT_GROUP_MEMBER / current_user(): ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_fn.\n• Row-level: row filter functions — ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER filter_fn ON (region); returns only rows the user is allowed to see.\n• Unity Catalog enforces them centrally; also table/column GRANT/REVOKE.\nLegacy: table ACLs / AAD credential passthrough (being replaced by UC)."},{"q":"Difference between Unity Catalog and Hive Metastore?","type":"concept","a":"• Scope: Hive = per-workspace; UC = account-level across all workspaces.\n• Namespace: Hive 2-level (db.table); UC 3-level (catalog.schema.table).\n• Governance: Hive basic ACLs, no column/row security; UC fine-grained (row filters, column masks), lineage, audit logs.\n• Sharing: Hive none; UC Delta Sharing built-in.\n• UC is the current standard; Hive is legacy/being replaced."},{"q":"What is Delta Sharing?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Sharing is an open protocol to securely share data (and AI assets) across organizations/workspaces/business units WITHOUT copying data.\n• A provider shares a subset of tables/volumes; recipients read via the share (Databricks-to-Databricks or open recipients like Power BI/pandas).\n• Governance and audit are maintained; recipients only see what's shared.\nScenario: 3 business units with separate workspaces share data through Delta Sharing instead of duplicating into each workspace."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Three business units have separate workspaces. How do you share data between them?","type":"scenario","a":"Option A — Unity Catalog + Delta Sharing: publish selected tables as a share from the provider workspace; grant recipients access; they read without copying. Centralized governance/audit.\nOption B — one UC metastore per region attached to all workspaces with catalogs per BU and GRANTs (cross-workspace access within the same account).\nChoose Delta Sharing for cross-org/cross-cloud or strict isolation; UC catalogs when all workspaces share an account/metastore."},{"q":"How do you secure access to ADLS from Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Authentication: Service Principal (app registration) + Storage Blob Data Contributor, or Managed Identity / Access Connector (no secrets), or account key (avoid).\n• Secrets: store SPN keys in Azure Key Vault + Databricks secret scope; read via dbutils.secrets.\n• RBAC: Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor/Owner.\n• POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 HNS): file/folder-level rwx with masks + defaults.\n• Unity Catalog: external locations + storage credentials + GRANTs (preferred).\n• Network: private endpoints/service endpoints/firewall.\n• Encryption: SSE at rest (Microsoft-managed or CMK), TLS in transit.\n• Avoid mounting with keys; prefer UC or OAuth."},{"q":"What are service principals, managed identities, and secrets in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Service principal — a non-human identity (app registration) used by pipelines/CI-CD for automated auth.\n• Managed identity — Azure-managed identity so resources authenticate without storing credentials (via Access Connector to ADLS).\n• Secrets — Databricks secret scopes (backed by Azure Key Vault) store passwords/tokens/keys; accessed with dbutils.secrets.get().\nBest practice: least privilege, no secrets in code, rotate regularly."},{"q":"How do you audit who accessed what data in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog audit logs capture user actions (logins, cluster/job runs, permission changes, data access) and column-level lineage.\n• Enable diagnostic logging to a storage account / Log Analytics; query with KQL.\n• Lineage shows how data flows between tables/columns/notebooks/jobs.\n• Combine with Azure Monitor for alerting on suspicious access."},{"q":"What is the difference between RBAC and ACL in the lakehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — permissions granted to roles/principals at the control/service level (e.g., Azure roles on storage, workspace admin roles).\n• ACLs (Access Control Lists) — fine-grained permissions on specific objects (tables, columns, files/folders via POSIX ACLs).\nBoth are used together: RBAC for broad access, UC ACLs/GRANTs for fine-grained data-level control."},{"q":"What is least-privilege design and how do you apply it?","type":"concept","a":"Grant users/apps only the minimum permissions needed.\n• Use groups (not individual users) and grant at the highest sensible level.\n• Use read-only roles where possible; scope service principals to specific storage/workspaces.\n• Apply column masks/row filters for sensitive data.\n• Separate prod/dev credentials; audit and rotate regularly."},{"q":"What are components of Azure data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ User --A unique individual who has access to the system. ➢ Group --- A collection of users ➢ Access control list (ACL) ➢ Notebook ➢ Dashboard ➢ Library ➢ Repo ➢ Experiment","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It supports more security projects Its security is not as good as Map Reduce","type":"concept","a":"and continuously working on its security issues.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of security of Blob or storage account?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is RBAC?","type":"concept","a":"Role back access control Click on Access control (IAM) then click on add role assignment","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Provide Security Over ADLS Storage?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"RBAC — Role-Based Access Control","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Blob Data Reader — read-only access • Storage Blob Data Contributor — read + write access • Storage Blob Data Owner — full control including ACLs","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 hierarchical namespace)","type":"concept","a":"• File/folder level ACLs — grant rwx permissions at directory level • Masks and default ACLs for inheritance","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"• Centralized governance — define External Locations and Storage Credentials • Fine-grained access: GRANT SELECT ON TABLE to specific users or groups • Row-level and column-level security through Delta Lake row filters and column masks","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog vs Hive Metastore","type":"concept","a":"• Feature Hive Metastore Unity Catalog Page 19 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Scope Workspace-level (per ADB Account-level (across all workspaces) workspace) Governance Basic — no column/row security Fine-grained: table, column, row-level Namespace 2-level: database.table 3-level: catalog.schema.table Data lineage Not built-in Built-in — column-level lineage tracking Access control Workspace-level ACLs Centralized — groups, GRANT/REVOKE Audit logs Limited Full — who accessed what and when External tables Supported Supported via External Locations Delta Sharing Not supported Built-in Delta Sharing support Migration path Legacy — being replaced by UC Current standard — preferred Multiple workspaces Each workspace has own One Unity Catalog for all workspaces metastore -- Hive Metastore (2-level namespace) USE DATABASE my_database;\n• SELECT * FROM my_table; -- Unity Catalog (3-level namespace) SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table; -- Grant in Unity Catalog GRANT SELECT ON TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table TO `data_analysts`;\n• GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG my_catalog TO `data_engineers`;\n• Unity Catalog is the recommended governance solution for all new Databricks deployments\n• It provides centralized data access control, lineage, and auditing across the entire organization rather than per- workspace","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you handle PII data in your project?","type":"concept","a":"Handling PII data  Masking / tokeniza(cid:415)on  Encryp(cid:415)on (at rest + transit)  Unity Catalog access control  Row-level security  Restricted columns in Silver/Gold layer","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Standard, Premium, and Enterprise in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Standard — Core Apache Spark, notebooks, jobs, basic collaboration. No RBAC or advanced security. • Premium — Adds Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), Databricks SQL (SQL Warehouses), audit logs, token management, SSO. Suitable for enterprise data teams. • Enterprise — Full Premium features plus compliance certifications (HIPAA, FedRAMP), enhanced security controls, network isolation (Private Link), and dedicated support SLAs. SQL Warehouse (SQL Data Warehouse in Databricks)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Managed Volume vs External Volume","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog introduces Volumes for storing non-tabular files\n• Managed Volume External Volume Databricks manages storage Customer manages storage Files stored in managed loca(cid:415)on Files stored in external cloud storage Deleted with the volume Files remain a(cid:332)er dropping the volume Simpler management Greater flexibility Think of it like managed vs external tables, but for files rather than tables\n• Examples: Managed Volume: Catalog ↓ Schema ↓ Managed Volume ↓ Images PDFs CSV External Volume: ADLS ↓ External Loca(cid:415)on ↓ Unity Catalog Volume","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Are you familiar with Unity Catalog architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Basic architecture: Metastore ↓ Catalog ↓ Schema ↓ Tables Views Volumes Func(cid:415)ons Models Permissions flow downward\n• Example: Metastore ↓ Sales Catalog ↓ Finance Schema ↓ Orders Table Main components:  Metastore  Catalog  Schema  Tables  Views  Volumes  External Loca(cid:415)ons  Storage Creden(cid:415)als Benefits:  Centralized governance  Fine-grained access control  Data lineage  Audi(cid:415)ng  Cross-workspace sharing (when a(cid:425)ached to the same metastore)","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is Predicate Pushdown in Parquet?","type":"concept","a":"• Predicate pushdown means the storage layer filters data before Spark reads it\n• Example: df = spark.read.parquet(\"sales\") df.filter(\"country='US'\") Spark reads only row groups that contain country='US'\n• Benefits:  Less disk I/O  Faster execu(cid:415)on  Lower memory usage Parquet stores:  Min value  Max value  Sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs Spark uses these sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs to skip unnecessary row groups\n• RBAC vs ACL RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) Permissions are assigned to roles\n• Example: Admin Read Write Delete User Read User gets permissions by role\n• Advantages:  Easy management  Enterprise friendly ACL (Access Control List) Permissions are assigned directly to each resource\n• Example: File A John → Read Alice → Write Bob → No Access Useful for object-level permissions\n• Difference RBAC ACL Role based Resource based Easier to manage More granular Scalable Complex with many users Enterprise systems File/object permissions","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance solu(cid:415)on for managing data and AI assets across mul(cid:415)ple workspaces\n• It provides centralized access control, data discovery, audi(cid:415)ng, and lineage for tables, views, files, machine learning models, and other data assets.\" Key Features  Centralized data governance  Fine-grained access control (catalog, schema, table, column, row)  Data lineage  Data discovery  Audit logs  Cross-workspace data sharing  Integra(cid:415)on with cloud storage like ADLS, S3, and GCS Unity Catalog hierarchy Catalog │ Schema │ Table/View Example: Catalog : Sales Schema : Bronze Table : Orders Access example: SELECT * FROM Sales.Bronze.Orders;","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why is Unity Catalog used?","type":"concept","a":"\"Instead of managing permissions separately in each workspace, Unity Catalog provides a single place to manage security, governance, and metadata across the organiza(cid:415)on.\" Difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table (DLT) Delta Table Delta Live Table Storage format based on Delta Lake Framework for building ETL pipelines Stores data Creates and manages data pipelines Manually created Automa(cid:415)cally managed You write SQL or Spark code You define transforma(cid:415)ons declara(cid:415)vely Manual scheduling Built-in orchestra(cid:415)on No built-in data quality Supports data quality expecta(cid:415)ons Manual monitoring Built-in monitoring and lineage","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Unity Catalog? Have you implemented it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance solu(cid:415)on for managing data, AI assets, and permissions across workspaces\n• It provides:  Centralized access control.  Fine-grained permissions at catalog, schema, table, and column levels.  Data lineage to track data movement.  Audit logs for compliance.  Secure sharing through Delta Sharing\n• In my project, I worked with Unity Catalog by storing managed Delta tables in catalogs, assigning role-based permissions to different teams, and ensuring secure access to data.\" (If you haven't implemented it, say: \"I understand its architecture and concepts, and I have worked in environments where it was configured by the pla(cid:414)orm team.\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"AI tools / Unity Catalog","type":"concept","a":" AI tools: ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot  Unity Catalog: o Centralized governance layer in Databricks o Manages access control for data, tables, and models o Enables data lineage + security","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance solution for data and AI assets across all workspaces\n• Three-level namespace: catalog.schema.table -- Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders_table Key Features: • Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns) • Data lineage: Tracks data flow from source to destination • Audit logs: Who accessed what data and when • External locations: Unified access to cloud storage (ADLS, S3) • Delta Sharing: Securely share data across organizations • Column-level & Row-level security -- Grant permissions in Unity Catalog GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders TO `data_analyst@company.com`;\n• GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA prod_catalog.sales_schema TO `data_team`; -- Create external table via Unity Catalog CREATE TABLE prod_catalog.sales_schema.ext_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/orders';\n• Q13\n• Cache() cache() stores a DataFrame in memory (or memory+disk) so it doesn't need to be recomputed for subsequent actions. # cache() = persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) df_cached = df.cache() # Lazy - cached on first action df_cached.count() # Triggers caching # persist() allows specifying storage level from pyspark import StorageLevel df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) # Fastest, needs RAM df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) # Spills to disk if needed df.persist(StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY) # Saves RAM, slower df.unpersist() # Free cached data when done When to use cache(): • DataFrame is used multiple times in the same job • Iterative algorithms (ML training loops) • After expensive transformations (joins, aggregations)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"architecture","name":"Architecture & Platform Design","icon":"🏗️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold)?","type":"concept","a":"Medallion architecture organizes a lakehouse into progressive quality layers:\n• Bronze (raw) — exact copy of source, minimal transformation, append-only, audit trail, Delta format.\n• Silver (cleansed/conformed) — cleaned, validated, deduplicated, standardized, joined; the 'single source of truth' for analytics/DS.\n• Gold (business) — aggregated, enriched, business-ready (KPIs, star schemas) for BI/reporting.\nTypical flow: ADLS → Databricks → Bronze → Silver → Gold → Power BI."},{"q":"What is stored in each Medallion layer and in what format?","type":"concept","a":"• Bronze: raw as-is + metadata columns (load_date, ingestion_timestamp, source_file). Delta (converted from CSV/JSON/Parquet). No business transforms. Path e.g. /bronze/orders.\n• Silver: validated, deduplicated, type-cast, joined, conformed data. Delta. /silver/orders_clean.\n• Gold: business aggregates, KPIs, star-schema fact/dim tables. Delta (or Parquet for serving). /gold/daily_sales.\nAlways store transformed data as Delta for ACID, time travel, schema evolution, and MERGE."},{"q":"Walk through an end-to-end Medallion implementation.","type":"concept","a":"1. Ingest — ADF / Auto Loader / COPY INTO lands raw files into ADLS Bronze as Delta (add metadata columns).\n2. Transform to Silver — PySpark/DLT: cleanse, dedupe, cast types, standardize, apply DQ checks, join reference data; MERGE incrementally.\n3. Build Gold — aggregate to KPI tables / star schema (facts + dims), apply SCD logic, partition by date.\n4. Serve — Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse; ML from Silver.\n5. Govern — Unity Catalog for access, lineage, RLS/CLS; monitor with alerts + audit.\n6. Orchestrate — Databricks Workflows / ADF with retries, parameterization, dev→qa→prod promotion."},{"q":"What data validations do you perform on the Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema/type validation — columns and types match expectations.\n• Null checks on critical columns.\n• Duplicate checks on business/primary keys.\n• Range/domain validation (e.g., age 0–150, valid enums).\n• Referential integrity (child keys exist in parents via left anti join).\n• Record-count reconciliation vs source/threshold.\n• Business rules (end_date >= start_date).\nImplement as DQ framework / DLT expectations; quarantine or fail on critical violations."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Data moves from Silver to Gold. At what stage do you aggregate and why? How do you apply RLS/CLS there?","type":"scenario","a":"• Aggregate in the GOLD layer — Silver keeps granular, conformed data (reusable, single source of truth); Gold is purpose-built for BI with pre-computed aggregates (faster dashboards, smaller scans). Aggregating too early loses reusability.\n• RLS/CLS: apply in Gold (and where sensitive columns exist) using Unity Catalog row filters and column masks so Power BI/Tableau consumers only see permitted rows/columns. Enforce centrally in UC, not in each report.\n• Also restrict access via GRANTs on Gold tables/views."},{"q":"When would you choose batch vs streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — scheduled, high-throughput, latency-tolerant: end-of-day reports, historical/backfill loads, month-end close, large transformations.\n• Streaming — continuous, low-latency: fraud detection, IoT/telemetry, clickstream, real-time alerting/dashboards, CDC propagation.\nMany platforms use a lambda-style hybrid: streaming for speed, batch for corrections/reprocessing."},{"q":"How do you design a pipeline for incremental data loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Track a watermark (max updated_ts/ID) per table in a control table.\n• Extract only records > watermark (predicate pushdown at source).\n• Apply transforms; MERGE into the target (upsert; handle deletes via CDC if available).\n• Update the watermark only after success.\n• Handle late-arriving data (overlap window / reprocess) and ensure idempotency.\n• Schedule/orchestrate via ADF or Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"What is data quality and what are its dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"Data Quality = how fit data is for its intended use. Dimensions:\n• Completeness (no missing required values)\n• Accuracy (reflects reality)\n• Consistency (same across systems)\n• Timeliness (available when needed)\n• Uniqueness (no duplicates)\n• Validity (conforms to formats/rules).\nEnforced via DLT expectations, Great Expectations, or custom PySpark checks; fail fast or quarantine bad records."},{"q":"How do you handle bad/corrupt records?","type":"concept","a":"Never let bad records corrupt trusted layers. Options:\n• Quarantine — write invalid rows to a separate bad-records table/path for triage.\n• Rescue — Auto Loader's _rescued_data column keeps unmatched/corrupt values.\n• Drop/log — expect_or_drop in DLT (log + drop) or filter with logging.\n• Fail fast — expect_or_fail for critical rules (stop the pipeline).\n• Schema handling — mode PERMISSIVE (keeps corrupt as null), DROPMALFORMED (drop), FAILFAST (error)."},{"q":"What is the 'typical architecture' for an Azure data engineering project?","type":"concept","a":"Sources (SQL Server, APIs, CSV, SAP) → ADF (orchestration/ingestion) → ADLS Gen2 → Databricks (PySpark transformations) with Bronze/Silver/Gold Delta layers → serve to Power BI / Synapse.\nCross-cutting: Unity Catalog governance, Key Vault secrets, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics, CI/CD, and orchestration via ADF/Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"How do you decide between partitioning, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning — low-cardinality columns (date, region, country) where each partition is ≥ ~1 GB; enables partition pruning; avoid high cardinality/over-partitioning.\n• Z-ORDER — high-cardinality filter/join columns (customer_id) on existing (often partitioned) tables; re-cluster on OPTIMIZE.\n• Liquid Clustering — modern default for evolving queries; incremental auto re-clustering, no partition maintenance; can replace partition+Z-ORDER.\nDecide by query filter patterns + table size + maintenance overhead."},{"q":"How do you handle schema drift/evolution across the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Detect: compare source vs expected schema (Get Metadata/Validate) or let reads report new columns.\n• ADF: Allow schema drift in data flows; benign schema changes flow through.\n• Auto Loader: schema inference + evolution (_rescued_data for mismatches).\n• Delta: mergeSchema on write / delta.autoMerge; ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN.\n• Design Bronze to be schema-flexible (raw), enforce structure at Silver/Gold.\n• Add alerts on drift so downstream contracts don't break silently."},{"q":"Explain your Project Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Our project follows Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• Flow: Source Systems | | (SAP, SQL Server, APIs, CSV, Oracle) | Azure Data Factory | | Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 | | Databricks Bronze Layer (Raw Data) | Silver Layer (Cleansed & Transformed) | Gold Layer (Business Ready Tables) | Power BI / Synapse / ML Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2  Azure Databricks  Delta Lake  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI  Azure Monitor","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"what is difference between data lake and data warehouse","type":"concept","a":"• Data lakes and data warehouses are both widely used for storing big data, but they are not interchangeable terms\n• A data lake is a vast pool of raw data, the purpose for which is not yet defined\n• A data warehouse is a repository for structured, filtered data that has already been processed for a specific purpose","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It’s design is very simple. While it’s design is complex.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Architecture of Synapse Data ware house? And what are different kind of","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"19.What is DW?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Data ware house is nothing but collect the data, store the data, analysis the data and consume the data\n• A data warehouse is specially designed for data analytics, which involves reading large amounts of data to understand relationships and trends across the data\n• A database is used to capture and store data, such as recording details of a transaction","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is data mart?","type":"concept","a":"A data mart is a simple form of data warehouse focused on a single subject or line of business.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Can you introduce yourself?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Hi, I'm an Azure Data Engineer with experience in building end-to-end data pipelines using Azure Data Factory (ADF), Azure Databricks, ADLS Gen2, Azure SQL Database, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on inges(cid:415)ng data from mul(cid:415)ple source systems into Azure Data Lake, transforming it using PySpark in Databricks, and loading curated data into the Gold layer following the Medallion Architecture\n• I have experience in developing ADF pipelines, crea(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng data valida(cid:415)on and cleansing, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, handling incremental loads, monitoring pipelines, and suppor(cid:415)ng produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Our project is an employee analy(cid:415)cs pla(cid:414)orm\n• Data comes from HR systems, A(cid:425)endance systems, and Payroll applica(cid:415)ons in CSV and SQL formats\n• The architecture is: Source Systems │ Azure Data Factory │ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 │ Bronze Layer │ Azure Databricks (PySpark) │ Silver Layer │ Business Transforma(cid:415)ons │ Gold Layer │ Power BI Repor(cid:415)ng ADF is responsible for orchestra(cid:415)ng the pipelines\n• Databricks performs data cleansing and transforma(cid:415)ons\n• Gold tables are used by business users through Power BI dashboards.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are your roles and responsibili(cid:415)es?","type":"concept","a":"\"My daily responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing Azure Data Factory pipelines  Crea(cid:415)ng Databricks notebooks using PySpark  Reading and wri(cid:415)ng data to ADLS Gen2  Performing data cleansing and valida(cid:415)on  Removing duplicates and handling null values  Implemen(cid:415)ng Delta Lake tables  Loading data into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Monitoring pipeline failures  Fixing produc(cid:415)on issues  Working with Git for version control  Coordina(cid:415)ng with QA and business teams\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ETL and ELT","type":"concept","a":"• ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) and ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) are two approaches for moving and preparing data\n• Aspect ETL ELT Order Extract → Transform → Load Extract → Load → Transform Where Target data Separate ETL tool/server transformations occur warehouse/lakehouse Aspect ETL ELT Data loaded into Already transformed Raw data first, then transformed target Leverages warehouse compute Performance Depends on ETL server capacity power Traditional data warehouses and Modern cloud platforms with Best for strict data quality requirements scalable compute Raw data remains available for Flexibility Less flexible once transformed reprocessing","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your end-to-end project architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Sample Answer \"Our project is an Azure-based data pla(cid:414)orm built using a Medallion Architecture.\" Web Applica(cid:415)ons / ERP / APIs │ ▼ On-Prem SQL Server │ Azure Data Factory │ ▼ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 Bronze Layer (Raw) │ ▼ Azure Databricks (PySpark) Silver Layer (Cleaned) │ ▼ Azure Databricks Gold Layer │ ▼ Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs │ ▼ Power BI Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Databricks  ADLS Gen2  Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Where is the actual data stored?","type":"concept","a":"Different stages:  SQL Server → Opera(cid:415)onal (OLTP)  ADLS Bronze → Raw  ADLS Silver → Cleaned  ADLS Gold → Curated  Synapse → Analy(cid:415)cs/Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are you storing data in SQL Server, ADLS, or Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Yes, but each serves a different purpose: Storage Purpose SQL Server Opera(cid:415)onal data ADLS Data lake (Bronze, Silver, Gold) Synapse Repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Architecture of project (End-to-End pipeline)","type":"concept","a":"We follow Medallion Architecture in Azure Databricks: (cid:44934) Flow: Sources  SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake ADF (Inges(cid:415)on Layer)  Copy Ac(cid:415)vity / Mapping Data Flow  Moves data to ADLS ADLS Gen2 Storage  Bronze (Raw data) Databricks Processing  Bronze → Silver → Gold (cid:44934) Layers:  Bronze → Raw ingested data  Silver → Cleaned + validated + deduplicated  Gold → Business aggregated data Consump(cid:415)on Layer  Power BI / Tableau / SQL Warehouse","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"After Transformation, Where Do You Store the Data?","type":"concept","a":"• After transformation, data is stored in the appropriate Medallion Architecture layer as a Delta table: Layer What is Stored Format Location Example Bronze (Raw) Raw data as-is from source;\n• Delta (from delta/bronze/raw_orders metadata columns added CSV/JSON/Parquet) Silver (Cleansed) Validated, deduplicated, type-cast, Delta delta/silver/orders_clean joined data Gold (Curated) Business-level aggregations, KPIs, Delta or Parquet delta/gold/daily_sales reporting tables # Write transformed Silver data to Delta table silver_df.write .format('delta') .mode('overwrite') # or 'append' / 'merge' for incremental .option('mergeSchema','true') .partitionBy('year','month') .saveAsTable('silver.orders_clean') # Or write to ADLS path silver_df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders/') Always store transformed data as Delta format — it gives ACID transactions, time travel, schema evolution, and efficient MERGE operations for incremental updates","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Architecture in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks follows the Lakehouse Architecture — combining the scalability of a Data Lake with the governance and performance of a Data Warehouse\n• Medallion Architecture (Most Common) Data flows through three progressive layers — each improving quality and structure: Layer Also Called Purpose Format Bronze Raw Layer Ingest raw data as-is from source Delta / Parquet / JSON / — no transformations CSV Silver Cleansed Layer Clean, deduplicate, validate, join Delta Table — trusted, queryable data Gold Aggregated / Business-level aggregations, Delta Table Business Layer KPIs, reporting-ready data Core Databricks Architecture Components Component Description Control Plane Managed by Databricks (Azure/AWS/GCP) — stores metadata, notebooks, job configs, UI Data Plane Customer's cloud account — where Spark clusters run and data is stored in ADLS/S3 Clusters Spark compute nodes — All-Purpose (interactive) or Job Clusters (automated pipelines) DBFS Databricks File System — abstraction over cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) Unity Catalog Unified governance layer — manages tables, schemas, data lineage, access control Delta Lake Storage layer for all tables — provides ACID, versioning, schema enforcement MLflow Built-in ML lifecycle management — experiment tracking, model registry, deployment Workflows / Jobs Orchestration layer — schedule notebooks, DLT pipelines, dbt, Python scripts Autoloader Incremental file ingestion from cloud storage into Delta tables Delta Live Tables Declarative pipeline framework with built-in data quality and monitoring Data Flow in the Architecture • Sources (SQL Server, APIs, flat files, IoT) land raw data in ADLS/S3 Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 • Bronze layer ingests via Autoloader or COPY INTO — raw Delta tables • Silver layer cleanses, deduplicates, and enriches with business rules • Gold layer aggregates for BI tools (Power BI, Tableau) or ML models • Unity Catalog governs access and lineage across all three layers Page 5 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You mentioned in your resume that you worked on a migration project. Explain","type":"concept","a":"• about it\n• In the migration project, we moved our existing on-premises data warehouse (e.g., SQL Server / Oracle) to a cloud-based Lakehouse architecture on Databricks / Azure Synapse\n• Key Phases of the Migration: • Assessment & Planning: Audited existing tables, stored procedures, ETL jobs, and data volumes\n• Identified dependencies and prioritized migration order. • Architecture Design: Designed a Medallion Architecture (Bronze → Silver → Gold) on Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS Gen2) with Delta Lake format. • Data Extraction: Extracted data from the source (on-prem DB) using JDBC connectors or flat file exports. • Transformation & Load: Rewrote SQL-based transformations in PySpark\n• Applied business logic, cleansing, deduplication, and type casting. • Validation: Compared row counts, aggregates, and key metrics between source and target to ensure data integrity. • Cutover: Ran parallel loads, froze the source, did final delta sync, and switched downstream consumers to the new platform","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Project Architecture (End-to-End)","type":"concept","a":"We used Azure + Databricks Medallion Architecture:  Sources: SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake  ADF: Data inges(cid:415)on + orchestra(cid:415)on  ADLS Gen2: Storage layer  Databricks: o Bronze → Raw inges(cid:415)on o Silver → Cleaned + transformed o Gold → Business-ready data  Consump(cid:415)on: Power BI / SQL Warehouse","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How many layers you will be having in your databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Layers in Databricks  Bronze → Raw data  Silver → Cleaned + validated data  Gold → Aggregated business data (Some systems also use Pla(cid:415)num for ML)","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your current data pipeline architecture tools, flow, and data volume.","type":"concept","a":"• Our source systems include SQL Database, REST APIs, and CSV files uploaded to Azure Blob Storage\n• Azure Data Factory orchestrates data ingestion into ADLS Gen2 Bronze storage\n• Azure Databricks performs cleansing, deduplication, joins, aggregations, and business transformations using PySpark\n• Processed data is stored in Delta Lake Silver and Gold layers\n• Power BI consumes the Gold layer for reporting\n• Azure Monitor and Log Analytics are used for monitoring, while Git-based CI/CD manages deployments\n• On average, we process around 80–150 GB of data daily, which translates to several million records depending on the source","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain about yourself.","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Answer (2-3 minutes) \"Hi, I'm a Data Engineer with around X years of experience in designing and developing ETL/ELT data pipelines using Azure Data Factory, Azure Databricks, PySpark, SQL, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on building scalable data inges(cid:415)on frameworks where data is collected from mul(cid:415)ple sources such as SQL databases, APIs, and cloud storage into Azure Data Lake\n• We follow the Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• ADF is mainly used for orchestra(cid:415)on, Databricks performs data transforma(cid:415)on, and Delta Lake stores processed data\n• My responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing ADF pipelines  Wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Data valida(cid:415)on  Incremental loading  Performance tuning  Pipeline monitoring and troubleshoo(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the data framework that you are working on.","type":"concept","a":"A typical architecture: Source Systems (SQL, APIs, CSV, SAP) ↓ Azure Data Factory (Orchestra(cid:415)on) ↓ Azure Data Lake Gen2 ↓ Bronze Layer (Raw Delta Tables) ↓ Databricks ↓ Silver Layer (Cleansed + Standardized) ↓ Gold Layer (Business Aggrega(cid:415)ons) ↓ Power BI / Synapse Explain each layer: Bronze  Raw data  No transforma(cid:415)ons  Append only  Delta format Silver  Data cleaning  Remove duplicates  Null handling  Data type conversion  Standardiza(cid:415)on Gold  Business-ready data  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  KPI tables  Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Delta Live Tables?","type":"concept","a":"• DLT is a Databricks framework used for building reliable ETL pipelines\n• Features:  Automa(cid:415)c dependency management  Incremental processing  Data quality expecta(cid:415)ons  Auto retries  Pipeline monitoring  Automa(cid:415)c lineage  Schema evolu(cid:415)on Example: Bronze ↓ Silver ↓ Gold No need to manually manage execu(cid:415)on order","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How is data stored in Bronze?","type":"concept","a":"Usually:  Delta format  Append only  Raw  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oned (op(cid:415)onal)  Metadata columns Example: customer.csv ↓ Bronze customer_id name city load_date inges(cid:415)on_(cid:415)mestamp source_file No business transforma(cid:415)ons.","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Project Explanation","type":"concept","a":"• Use the STAR framework: Situation, Task, Action, Result\n• Always quantify outcomes. • Situation: Organization needed to process 10TB+ daily transaction data from Kafka, S3, and REST APIs for fraud detection. • Task: Design and implement an end-to-end pipeline using Databricks and Delta Lake, replacing legacy batch jobs. • Action: Built Bronze/Silver/Gold medallion architecture\n• Used Auto Loader for incremental ingestion, Structured Streaming for near-real-time processing, Z-ordering for query optimization, and Delta constraints for data quality. • Result: Reduced data latency from 6 hours to 15 minutes\n• Query performance improved by 70%\n• Storage costs reduced by 40% via Delta compaction\n• Page 1 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How many Delta tables are there in Silver and Gold layers?","type":"concept","a":"• \"In my project, each curated dataset is stored as a separate Delta table\n• The exact number depends on the project\n• For example, we maintain around 20–30 Delta tables in the Silver layer and 10–15 aggregated/business tables in the Gold layer\n• The key point is that each business en(cid:415)ty or repor(cid:415)ng dataset is maintained as its own Delta table.\" (Replace the numbers with your project's actual values if known.)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Notebooks Does a Data Engineer Typically Deal With in a Year?","type":"concept","a":"• This is experience-based\n• Tailor your answer to your actual project, but a typical range: • Small project: 10-20 notebooks per year Page 1 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Mid-size project: 25-50 notebooks (Bronze/Silver/Gold layers, 3-10 notebooks per domain per layer) • Large enterprise: 50-100+ notebooks organized by domain (Finance, Orders, Customers, etc.) Sample Answer: \"In my current project I work with approximately 30-40 notebooks organized by layer\n• We have Bronze ingestion notebooks, Silver cleansing/transformation notebooks, Gold aggregation notebooks for reporting, plus shared utility notebooks for config and common functions.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Daily Volume of Data You Process?","type":"concept","a":"• Scale Volume/Day Typical Cluster Small Less than 10 GB 4-8 cores, 16-32 GB RAM Medium 10 GB - 1 TB 16-64 cores, 128-256 GB RAM Large 1 TB - 100 TB 100+ cores, 500 GB+ RAM, Auto-scaling Enterprise 100 TB+ Hundreds of nodes, streaming pipelines Sample Answer: \"We process approximately 500 GB to 2 TB of raw data daily from 15 upstream systems\n• After transformation the Silver/Gold layers are 200-500 GB\n• Peak loads during month-end hit 5 TB.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What does the current data architecture look like — Medallion/Lakehouse or more traditional ETL?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What do you do in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":" Develop PySpark notebooks  Build ETL pipelines (bronze → silver → gold)  Op(cid:415)mize performance (par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching)  Handle incremental loads (Delta Lake)  Create jobs/workflows  Work with Delta tables","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project from source to repor(cid:415)ng?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I am working on an Employee Analy(cid:415)cs project using Azure Data Engineering services\n• The source systems include SQL Server databases, REST APIs, and CSV files from SFTP\n• Azure Data Factory (ADF) orchestrates the data inges(cid:415)on process\n• The raw data is stored in Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 (Bronze layer)\n• Azure Databricks processes the data using PySpark, where we perform data valida(cid:415)on, duplicate removal, null handling, joins, aggrega(cid:415)ons, and business transforma(cid:415)ons\n• The transformed data is stored in Delta tables in the Silver and Gold layers\n• The Gold layer is consumed by Power BI for dashboards and business repor(cid:415)ng\n• I am responsible for developing ADF pipelines, wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loads, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, monitoring produc(cid:415)on pipelines, and resolving produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Medallion Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold) is a data design pattern that organizes data into progressive quality layers in a Data Lakehouse\n• Bronze Layer (Raw): • Exact copy of source data, minimal transformation • All data lands here (append-only), serves as audit trail • Format: Delta or Parquet Silver Layer (Cleaned/Conformed): • Data is cleaned, deduplicated, validated • Joins applied, schema enforced • Ready for data science and analytics use cases Gold Layer (Business-level Aggregates): • Aggregated, enriched, business-ready data • Star schema models, KPIs, dashboards • Optimized for reporting tools (Power BI, Tableau) # Bronze: Raw ingest df_raw.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/bronze/orders') # Silver: Clean and deduplicate df_silver = df_raw.dropDuplicates(['order_id']).filter(col('amount') > 0) df_silver.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/silver/orders') # Gold: Aggregate for reporting df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('region').agg(sum('amount').alias('total_revenue')) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/gold/revenue_by_region') SECTION 5: Spark Internals & Optimization","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Medallian Architecture ?","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture is a layered data design pa(cid:425)ern used in data lakes where data is organized into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers\n• Bronze stores raw ingested data, Silver contains cleaned and standardized data, and Gold contains aggregated business-ready data for repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs\n• This approach improves data quality, maintainability, and scalability of pipelines","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Why Medallion Architecture?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:33158)(cid:57393) Benefits:  Be(cid:425)er data quality  Easy debugging (trace back to raw data)  Reusable datasets  Scalable pipeline design  Supports incremental processing","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you move on prem data to fabric to delta lake house ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move on-prem data to Microso(cid:332) Fabric Lakehouse, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to connect the source systems securely\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines in Fabric to ingest the data into OneLake in Delta format\n• The data is stored in Bronze layer as raw data, then transformed into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark or CDC-based extrac(cid:415)on to ensure efficiency","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Oracle to fabric ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move data from Oracle to Microso(cid:332) Fabric, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to establish a secure connec(cid:415)on if Oracle is on-prem\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines to extract data from Oracle using SQL queries\n• The data is loaded into OneLake Lakehouse in Delta format, typically in the Bronze layer\n• Later, I transform it into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark columns or Oracle CDC to ensure efficient data movement","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Which layer data is ready to use for presenta(cid:415)on purpose","type":"concept","a":"Gold layer is the final curated layer in Medallion Architecture that is op(cid:415)mized and structured for business repor(cid:415)ng and visualiza(cid:415)on tools.","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"As a team lead you have 300 tables in Oracle database, what will be your approach,","type":"concept","a":"• explain your flow\n• If I have 300 tables in Oracle, I will avoid building individual pipelines\n• Instead, I would design a metadata-driven inges(cid:415)on framework where table details like load type, watermark column, and query are stored in a control table\n• I would create a single dynamic ADF or Fabric pipeline that reads metadata and processes all tables using a ForEach loop\n• Data would be ingested into the Bronze layer, then transformed into silver and gold layers using Spark or SQL\n• I would also implement incremental load using watermark or CDC, enable parallel processing for performance, and add monitoring for failure tracking and retries\n• Data skew Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons in distributed systems like Spark, causing some tasks to take significantly longer than others\n• It usually happens during joins, groupBy, or shuffle opera(cid:415)ons\n• To resolve it, I use techniques like broadcast join for small tables, sal(cid:415)ng for skewed keys, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data, enabling Spark adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on, and pre-aggrega(cid:415)ng data before joins","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"scenario","name":"Scenario-Based Questions","icon":"🧩","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"SCENARIO: A pipeline that usually completes in 10 hours is still running into the next day. How do you troubleshoot?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Check the ADF/Databricks Monitor + Spark UI — identify which stage/activity is slow.\n2. Spark UI: look for data skew (one task far longer), large shuffle read/write, spill to disk, uneven partitions.\n3. Compare current vs normal run: input volume growth, small-file explosion, lost broadcast (join strategy change), schema changes.\n4. Check cluster health: resource contention (shared cluster), executor loss/GC, autoscaling limits, pool starvation.\n5. Check upstream source latency/backlog and IR throughput.\nRemedies: AQE/skew handling, salt skewed keys, broadcast small tables, repartition, OPTIMIZE small files, scale cluster, fix bad join, restore filters/column pruning."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A notebook that completes in 15 minutes took 50 minutes today. How do you find the root cause?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the run in Spark UI; find the slow stage and compare task durations → look for skew/spill.\n• Check executor memory/GC and shuffle metrics; look for OOM-ish behavior or spilling.\n• Check cluster: shared usage/contention, autoscaling didn't scale, fewer/larger tasks.\n• Check data: more files/small files, larger volume, changed partitioning, source lag.\n• Check code changes: new join, UDF, missing filter, cartesian, broadcast removed.\n• Fix: repartition/broadcast, enable AQE/Photon, OPTIMIZE, cache reused DataFrames, scale cluster."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Databricks pipeline suddenly fails. What steps do you take to identify and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Open the job run → failed task → read the error/log (driver + executor logs).\n2. Classify: data-related (schema drift, new nulls/bad records), infra (cluster/OOM/network), dependency (upstream table missing), or code (bug).\n3. Reproduce on a small sample; check recent code or source changes (git diff).\n4. Verify upstream data availability, credentials/Key Vault, and table/permission changes.\n5. Apply fix, use repair-and-rerun (only failed tasks) for idempotent pipelines.\n6. Add guardrails: DQ checks, retries, alerts, schema drift handling."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Source data changed structurally (new column added / datatype changed). How do you detect and handle it without breaking pipelines?","type":"scenario","a":"Detect: schema validation step in the pipeline (expected vs actual), or Auto Loader schema evolution / rescued data column.\nHandle:\n• ADF: allow schema drift; parameterize mapping.\n• Ingest raw into Bronze as schema-flexible; add new columns automatically (mergeSchema / autoMerge).\n• For datatype change: cast/map in Silver, keep raw value in Bronze, quarantine incompatible rows.\n• Alert on drift and keep downstream consumers informed; evolve Delta schema explicitly (ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN).\nDesign principle: raw layer tolerant, curated layers strict."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You notice data skew in a large join. How do you confirm and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"Confirm: Spark UI shows a few tasks with much larger shuffle read and longer duration; one executor overloaded.\nFix:\n• Enable AQE skew join (auto-splits skewed partitions).\n• Salt the skewed key (add random suffix), join, then aggregate.\n• Broadcast the smaller table if it fits.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key or by range.\n• Filter reduce before the join; isolate the hot key and process separately."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Delta table has thousands of small files and reads are slow. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Run OPTIMIZE to compact into larger (~1 GB) files; schedule it after loads.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) to prevent small files going forward.\n• Use availableNow trigger / larger micro-batches in streaming or foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Adopt Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy; coalesce before writes.\n• Set a target file size and monitor file counts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Duplicate records are appearing in your Silver table. How do you fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Deduplicate on business key + recency: ROW_NUMBER() over key ordered by load_ts desc, keep rn=1.\n• Use dropDuplicates on the natural key.\n• For incremental loads, MERGE on the key (whenMatchedUpdate) so re-runs don't insert duplicates.\n• Add a DQ check that fails/alerts if duplicates exceed a threshold.\n• Identify the source (idempotency, retries, CDC reprocessing) and fix upstream."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to process millions/billions of records. What's your approach?","type":"scenario","a":"• Store in columnar Delta/Parquet with partitioning/Clustering; avoid CSV/JSON for large scale.\n• Define explicit schemas; filter/aggregate early; select only needed columns.\n• Use broadcast joins for small dims; AQE + skew handling; right-size shuffle partitions.\n• Use Photon; cache reused DataFrames; avoid UDFs.\n• Scale the cluster (workers/VM type) and use autoscaling; use Job clusters.\n• Incremental processing (watermark/MERGE) to avoid full reprocessing."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A memory/OOM error occurs during a large join/aggregation. How do you resolve it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Reduce data early (filter, select needed columns, aggregate before join).\n• Broadcast the small side instead of shuffling the huge side.\n• Increase shuffle partitions to make tasks smaller; use AQE.\n• Fix skew (salting/AQE) so one task doesn't blow up.\n• Use bigger memory VMs / more nodes; avoid collect()/large driver operations.\n• Use persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK) instead of MEMORY_ONLY if needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Compare these two queries and explain when to use each. Q1: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table WHERE salary>5000 GROUP BY emp_id; Q2: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table GROUP BY emp_id HAVING SUM(salary)>5000;","type":"scenario","a":"• Q1 (WHERE) filters individual ROWS before aggregation — only salaries > 5000 are summed per employee.\n• Q2 (HAVING) aggregates ALL rows, then keeps employees whose TOTAL salary > 5000.\nThey give different results. Use WHERE to filter rows cheaply before grouping; use HAVING to filter on aggregate results. You can combine both."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Design a POD-level ADF pipeline that feeds a Databricks job. How do you configure Linked Services, Datasets, and pipeline properties?","type":"scenario","a":"• Linked Services: ADLS (Azure IR), Databricks workspace (with access token from Key Vault), source DB (SHIR if on-prem).\n• Datasets: parameterized source/sink datasets (schema/table/path) so one pipeline handles many tables.\n• Pipeline: Get Metadata/Lookup → ForEach (metadata-driven) → Databricks Notebook activity; pass parameters (run_date, table).\n• Properties: set ForEach degree of parallelism, Copy DIUs, batch count for data flows, retry + timeout, and Tumbling Window trigger.\n• Balance throughput vs cost: use Job clusters, avoid over-parallelizing the source, scale SHIR/DIUs as needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A production job reading secrets from Key Vault starts failing intermittently. How do you diagnose it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Check Key Vault access: is the secret still present/permitted; is the managed identity/SPN expired or the secret rotated?\n• Check the Integration Runtime: is the SHIR online, throttled, or hitting network/timeouts?\n• Check pipeline config: correct secret scope/key, correct vault URI, no hard-coded expiry.\n• Look at error messages (AAD auth vs 403 vs timeout) and correlate with time of day (throttling).\n• Fix: renew/rotate credentials, add retry with backoff, monitor Key Vault diagnostics, add alerts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks cluster fails to start due to resource limitations. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Free up resources: terminate idle clusters and release the quota/cores.\n• Request a quota increase (vCPU/cores) in the subscription.\n• Reduce min workers/VM size, or use instance pools / serverless.\n• Check for subnet/network (no available IPs) or policy restrictions.\n• Retry after cleanup; use cluster policies to prevent oversized requests."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You must do real-time analysis on a streaming dataset in Databricks. How?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read with Spark Structured Streaming from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse JSON (from_json), apply watermark + windowed aggregation, dedupe.\n• Write to a Delta table with checkpointing (exactly-once); use update/append mode.\n• Optionally use DLT streaming tables for managed reliability + expectations.\n• Serve to Power BI/Real-Time Analytics; monitor lag and checkpoint health."},{"q":"What are the common challenges you've faced in pipelines (and fixes)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Data skew → AQE/salting/broadcast.\n• Schema drift → mergeSchema/schema drift/Auto Loader rescued data.\n• Duplicates → dedupe + idempotent MERGE.\n• OOM/memory → filter early, partition, right-size cluster, persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK).\n• Small-file problem → OPTIMIZE / Auto Optimize.\n• Slow large-large joins → broadcast/bucketing.\n• Connection timeouts → retry/backoff, connection pooling, scale IR.\n• Datatype mismatch → explicit schema + validation.\n• Incremental load failures → reliable watermark in a control table.\n• Late-arriving data → overlap window / reprocess."},{"q":"Scenario: 10 CSV files arrive every day at 10 AM.","type":"scenario","a":"Scenario : I have 10 files which arrives at 10.00 AM , employee files – 5 , attendance files – 5 , all are in csv format , no change in schema (same schema) – what would be, I want you to","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If job is running slow, what will you do?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:44934) Steps to troubleshoot:","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks job is running slow. What will you do?","type":"concept","a":"Steps to troubleshoot:","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Explain RAG to a non-technical person.","type":"concept","a":"• \"Imagine you ask a librarian a ques(cid:415)on\n• Instead of answering from memory, the librarian first searches the library for the most relevant books, reads the necessary informa(cid:415)on, and then gives you an accurate answer\n• RAG works in the same way\n• Instead of relying only on what the AI already knows, it first retrieves relevant informa(cid:415)on from your company's documents or databases and then generates an answer using that informa(cid:415)on\n• This makes the responses more accurate and up to date.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"}]},{"id":"modeling","name":"Data Modeling","icon":"📐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is a data model? Explain star vs snowflake vs galaxy schema.","type":"concept","a":"A data model defines how data is structured, related, and constrained.\n• Star schema — one central FACT table surrounded by denormalized DIMENSION tables. Simple queries, fast aggregations, BI-friendly. Slight redundancy. Most common in data warehouses.\n• Snowflake schema — dimensions normalized into sub-dimensions (less redundancy, more joins, slower).\n• Galaxy / fact constellation — multiple fact tables sharing conformed dimensions.\nExample: Fact_Sales(sale_id,date_key,product_key,customer_key,amount,qty) + Dim_Date, Dim_Product, Dim_Customer."},{"q":"What are the SCD types (0,1,2,3,4,6)?","type":"concept","a":"• Type 0 — never update (static).\n• Type 1 — overwrite the value; no history.\n• Type 2 — add a new row on change; keep full history with start/end dates + current flag (most common in interviews).\n• Type 3 — add a column to store current + previous value only (1 version back).\n• Type 4 — keep current in main table, history in a separate history table.\n• Type 6 — hybrid (1+2+3): full history rows PLUS a current flag and a current-value column."},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 in detail and how you implement it.","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 preserves history by inserting a NEW row whenever a tracked attribute changes; the natural key stays the same, a surrogate key identifies each version.\nColumns: start_date, end_date, is_current (active flag), surrogate key.\nSteps (Delta MERGE):\n1. Expire the current row if tracked columns changed: set end_date = yesterday, is_current = 0.\n2. Insert a new row for the change: start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1.\n3. Insert brand-new keys as-is.\nOnly one active row per business key at any time."},{"q":"CODING: Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark using a hash/flag approach.","type":"coding","a":"Read history + daily data, compute a hash of tracked columns, split into unchanged / updated / new, expire updates, insert new + updates, then union.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, sha2, concat_ws, lit, current_date, when\n\nTRACK = ['name','city']\n\ndef with_hash(df):\n    return df.withColumn('row_hash', sha2(concat_ws('||', *[col(c) for c in TRACK]), 256))\n\nhist = with_hash(history_df)         # has start/end/is_current\nincr = with_hash(daily_df)\n\ncur = hist.filter(col('is_current')==1)\n\n# rows that changed\nupdates = (incr.alias('s').join(cur.alias('t'), 'customer_id')\n           .filter(col('s.row_hash') != col('t.row_hash'))\n           .select('customer_id', *TRACK))\n\n# new keys\nnew = incr.join(cur, 'customer_id', 'left_anti').select('customer_id', *TRACK)\n\n# expire old current rows for changed keys\nexpired = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id').withColumn('end_date', current_date()).withColumn('is_current', lit(0))\nno_change = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\n\n# new versions of changed + brand new\ninserts = (updates.unionByName(new)\n           .withColumn('start_date', current_date())\n           .withColumn('end_date', lit(None).cast('date'))\n           .withColumn('is_current', lit(1)))\n\nfinal = no_change.unionByName(expired).unionByName(inserts)\nfinal.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('dim_customer')"},{"q":"What is incremental loading and what approaches exist?","type":"concept","a":"Incremental loading processes only new/changed data since the last load instead of a full reload.\n• Timestamp/watermark — load where updated_at > last_run_timestamp; track a high-watermark in a control table.\n• CDC — capture inserts/updates/deletes from the source (log-based, e.g., Debezium/Azure SQL CDC).\n• Delta MERGE (UPSERT) — merge changes into the target.\nBenefits: faster, cheaper, less source load. Watch for late-arriving data and reliable watermark updates."},{"q":"Difference between full load and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Full load — reload all data every run; simple; high source impact and long runtime; used for first load/small tables.\n• Incremental — load only new/changed records; needs watermark/CDC; low source impact, fast; for large tables. Risk: must handle late-arriving data and deletes."},{"q":"Difference between CDC and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load — a loading strategy: only bring in new/changed data (often timestamp-based).\n• CDC — a mechanism that captures row-level INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from the source (log-based/trigger-based), so it can also capture deletes and provide before/after images.\nCDC is a superset that feeds richer incremental loads; plain timestamp incremental usually can't detect deletes."},{"q":"Which table loads first, fact or dimension? How do you relate them?","type":"concept","a":"Dimensions load FIRST — facts reference dimension surrogate keys, so the dimensions (and their keys) must exist before the fact rows can resolve those keys.\nRelation: fact tables store foreign keys pointing to dimension surrogate keys; the join is fact.dim_key = dim.surrogate_key.\nOften a 'late-arriving dimension' pattern is used: insert a placeholder (unknown) dimension row if a fact arrives before its dimension."},{"q":"What is the difference between a surrogate key and a natural key?","type":"concept","a":"• Natural (business) key — a meaningful identifier from the source (e.g., customer_id, SSN).\n• Surrogate key — a system-generated integer uniquely identifying each DIMENSION VERSION (used in SCD2 to distinguish versions).\nFacts join to dimensions on surrogate keys; the natural key links versions of the same entity."},{"q":"What are late-arriving dimensions/facts and how do you handle them?","type":"concept","a":"• Late-arriving dimension — a fact arrives referencing a dimension that hasn't loaded yet.\n• Late-arriving fact — a fact arrives after its expected processing window.\nHandling: insert a placeholder/inferred dimension row (unknown key) and update it when the real record arrives; or defer fact loading until the dimension exists; use watermark + reprocessing and idempotent MERGE so late facts are picked up on the next run."},{"q":"What is a slowly changing dimension and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"SCD manages dimension attributes that change slowly over time (e.g., customer address, department). The type chosen controls how (and whether) history is preserved — so reports can reflect either the current value or the value as of a point in time."},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• In a data warehouse, data is organized into Fact Tables (measures/events) and Dimension Tables (descriptive context)\n• Star and Snowflake are two common ways to arrange these tables\n• Star Schema Characteristics: Fact table is in the center\n• Dimension tables surround it like a star\n• Dimension tables are DENORMALIZED — all attributes in one flat table\n• Simple, fast queries — fewer JOINs needed\n• Best for BI tools and reporting (Power BI, Tableau). -- Star Schema Example: Sales Data Warehouse -- Fact Table (center of the star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key, store_key, sale_amount, quantity, discount -- Dimension Tables (flat, denormalized) dim_customer: customer_key, name, city, state, country, email, age_group dim_product: product_key, product_name, category, brand, color, size dim_date: date_key, date, month, quarter, year, weekday, is_holiday dim_store: store_key, store_name, city, state, region, manager ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 9 -- Query: Total sales by product category (only 2 tables needed) SELECT p.category, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key GROUP BY p.category Snowflake Schema Characteristics: Dimension tables are NORMALIZED — broken into sub-dimension tables\n• Looks like a snowflake because dimensions branch into more tables\n• Saves storage (no repeated values)\n• More complex queries with more JOINs\n• Better for data integrity\n• Used when storage is a constraint. -- Snowflake Schema: Same sales warehouse, normalized -- Fact Table (same as star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key ... -- dim_product is now split into sub-tables dim_product: product_key, product_name, category_key, brand_key dim_category: category_key, category_name, department_key dim_department:department_key, department_name dim_brand: brand_key, brand_name, brand_country -- dim_customer split into sub-tables dim_customer: customer_key, name, city_key, age_group dim_city: city_key, city_name, state_key dim_state: state_key, state_name, country_key dim_country: country_key, country_name, region -- Query: Same total sales by category (now 4 JOINs needed) SELECT dc.category_name, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_category dc ON p.category_key = dc.category_key GROUP BY dc.category_name Comparison Table Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension structure Denormalized (flat) Normalized (broken into sub- tables) Number of JOINs Fewer — simple queries More — complex queries ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 10 Query performance Faster reads Slightly slower due to more JOINs Storage More (repeated values) Less (normalized, no repetition) Data integrity Lower — some redundancy Higher — data in one place Complexity Simple to understand More complex to maintain Best for BI/Reporting, Power BI Large DW with storage constraints Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'We used Star Schema for our reporting layer because it gives faster query performance and is simpler to work with in Power BI","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Questions answered How much? How many? What Who? What? Where? When?","type":"concept","a":"• total? Primary key Surrogate key (sale_id) Surrogate key (customer_key) Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD) — Bonus • SCD Type 1 — Overwrite: Update the record directly\n• No history kept. (e.g., fix a typo in name) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 12 • SCD Type 2 — New Row: Add a new row with effective dates\n• Full history preserved. (e.g., customer moves city) • SCD Type 3 — Add Column: Add a 'previous value' column\n• Limited history. (e.g., previous_city column) Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'Fact tables store the measurable events — like each sale transaction with amount, quantity, and foreign keys\n• Dimension tables provide the context — customer name, product category, date details\n• In our warehouse, fact_sales had 500M rows while dim_customer had only 2M rows.' Q9 PySpark: Top 10 Customers by Total Sales Problem Given: DataFrame: sales_df Schema: customer_id (string), product_id (string), sale_amount (double) Task: Total sales per customer, return top 10 by total sales amount Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, desc # Step 1: Group by customer_id and sum their sale_amounts total_sales_df = sales_df.groupBy('customer_id') \\ .agg(sum('sale_amount').alias('total_sales')) # Step 2: Sort by total_sales descending, take top 10 top_10_customers = total_sales_df \\ .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) \\ .limit(10) # Display result top_10_customers.show() # Output: # +-----------+------------+ # |customer_id|total_sales | # +-----------+------------+ # |CUST_0042 | 245780.50 | # |CUST_0017 | 198340.00 | # |CUST_0099 | 176220.75 | ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 13 # |... | ... | # +-----------+------------+ Alternative: Using SQL in Databricks # Register as temp view sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') # SQL approach top_10_sql = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id, SUM(sale_amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY total_sales DESC LIMIT 10 ''') top_10_sql.show() With Rounding (Production Ready) from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, round as spark_round top_10_customers = ( sales_df .groupBy('customer_id') .agg(spark_round(sum('sale_amount'), 2).alias('total_sales')) .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) .limit(10) ) top_10_customers.show(truncate=False) Interview Tip: Key functions: groupBy() + agg(sum()) for totals, orderBy(col().desc()) for sorting, limit(10) for top N\n• For very large data, consider using window functions with rank() if you need ties included\n• Q10 PySpark: Fill Null Salaries and Add Salary Grade Problem ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 14 Given: DataFrame: employees_df Schema: employee_id (int), department (string), salary (int) — may have NULLs Task 1: Fill null salaries with 50000 Task 2: Add salary_grade column: >=100000 -> Executive, 50000-99999 -> Manager, <50000 -> Associate Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, coalesce, lit # Step 1: Fill null salaries with default value 50000 # Method A: fillna (simple, specific column) df_filled = employees_df.fillna({'salary': 50000}) # Method B: coalesce (returns first non-null value) df_filled = employees_df.withColumn( 'salary', coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) ) # Step 2: Add salary_grade column using when/otherwise df_final = df_filled.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary') >= 100000, 'Executive') .when((col('salary') >= 50000) & (col('salary') < 100000), 'Manager') .otherwise('Associate') ) df_final.show() # Output: # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # |employee_id|department|salary|salary_grade| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # | 101| Sales |120000| Executive| # | 102| HR | null| Manager| <- null -> 50000 -> Manager # | 103| Tech | 75000| Manager| # | 104| Sales | 35000| Associate| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ Key Functions Explained Function Purpose Example fillna(value, subset) Replace nulls in specific fillna({'salary': 50000}) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 15 columns coalesce(col, lit(val)) Return first non-null of multiple coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) exprs when(condition, value) If-else logic for new column when(col('salary')>=100000,'Execut ive') .otherwise(value) Default value if no when .otherwise('Associate') matches withColumn(name, Add or replace a column withColumn('salary_grade', expr) when(...)) Interview Tip: The when().when().otherwise() chain is PySpark's equivalent of SQL CASE WHEN\n• Always put the most specific condition first (>=100000 before >=50000)\n• Use fillna before applying when so null-handling is clean\n• Q11 PySpark: Most Recent Transaction Per User (Window Function) Problem Given: DataFrame: transactions_df Schema: user_id (string), transaction_timestamp (timestamp), transaction_amount (double) Task: Find the single MOST RECENT transaction for each user — return all 3 columns Solution 1: Window Function with rank() — Best Approach from pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Define window: partition by user_id, order by timestamp descending window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) # Add rank column — rank 1 = most recent for each user df_ranked = transactions_df.withColumn('rank', rank().over(window_spec)) # Keep only rank 1 (most recent) and drop the rank column most_recent = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') == 1) \\ .drop('rank') ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 16 most_recent.show() # Output: # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |user_id|transaction_timestamp |transaction_amount| # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |U001 |2024-01-15 14:32:00 | 129.99 | # |U002 |2024-01-16 09:15:00 | 49.50 | # |U003 |2024-01-14 21:00:00 | 299.00 | # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ Solution 2: Using row_number() — Guarantees Single Row from pyspark.sql.functions import col, row_number from pyspark.sql.window import Window # row_number() assigns 1 to the latest tx per user (no ties) # rank() would give ties the same number — use row_number to be safe window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) most_recent = ( transactions_df .withColumn('row_num', row_number().over(window_spec)) .filter(col('row_num') == 1) .drop('row_num') ) Solution 3: groupBy + join (Without Window Functions) from pyspark.sql.functions import max # Step 1: Find max timestamp per user max_ts = transactions_df \\ .groupBy('user_id') \\ .agg(max('transaction_timestamp').alias('max_ts')) # Step 2: Join back to original to get full row most_recent = transactions_df.join( max_ts, (transactions_df.user_id == max_ts.user_id) & (transactions_df.transaction_timestamp == max_ts.max_ts), how='inner' ).select( transactions_df.user_id, transactions_df.transaction_timestamp, ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 17 transactions_df.transaction_amount ) rank() vs row_number() vs dense_rank() Function Tie Handling Returns single row Use When per user? row_number() No ties — arbitrary order for Always exactly 1 Need strictly 1 ties row per group rank() Ties get same rank, gap May return >1 if Aware of ties, after timestamp tie want them included dense_rank() Ties get same rank, no gap May return >1 if Want timestamp tie consecutive rank numbers Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'I use a Window function with row_number() partitioned by user_id and ordered by transaction_timestamp descending. row_number() guarantees exactly one row per user even if two transactions have the same timestamp\n• Then I filter where row_num = 1 and drop the helper column.' Quick Reference — One-Line Answers Topic One-Line Answer Schedule Trigger Runs pipeline on fixed time — daily, hourly, weekly Tumbling Window Trigger Fixed non-overlapping windows — retryable, perfect for incremental loads Storage Event Trigger Fires when a file lands in ADLS Gen2 / Blob — event-driven On-Prem Migration SHIR -> ADF -> ADLS Gen2 (staging) -> Transform -> Azure SQL + watermark control table File Routing by Suffix Get Metadata + ForEach + dynamic sink table name using @replace(last(split(name,'_')),'.csv','') CSV-Driven Routing Lookup (read CSV) + ForEach + dynamic Copy Activity using @item().Country_code Star Schema Fact + flat denormalized dims — fewer JOINs, faster queries, best for BI Snowflake Schema Fact + normalized sub-dims — more JOINs, less storage, more data integrity Fact Table Measurable events (sale_amount, quantity) + FK to dimensions — very large ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 18 Dimension Table Descriptive context (name, city, category) — smaller, used for grouping/filtering Top 10 Customers groupBy('customer_id').agg(sum).orderBy(desc).limit(10) Fill Null + Salary Grade fillna({'salary':50000}) then withColumn('grade', when().when().otherwise()) Most Recent Transaction Window(partitionBy, orderBy desc) + row_number() + filter(row_num==1) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 19","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to implement Slowly changing type1","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"00:00.0\").show() -- it will add the Time stamp","type":"concept","a":"• spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",versionAsof=1).show() – if version is 0, it will execute the first table(df) if version is 1, it will execute the updated table ----In sql------- from delta.tables import DeltaTable -- (it will convert the file to Table) spark.sql(\"CREATE TABLE dbtablenew USING DELTA LOCATION '/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta'\") -- (it will create a new table ) %sql select * from dbtablenew %sql -- it will update and insert when mergering the files MERGE INTO dbtablenew USING dbtable ON dbtablenew.eno = dbtable.eno WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET dbtablenew.data = dbtable.data WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (eno, ename, sal) VALUES (eno, ename, sal) Page | 21 =========DATA WAREHOUSE CONCEPTS============ ➢ Data Ware house it is used for the ETL because fro analysis purpose. ➢ We want to design a OLTP system , we should a have modeling is E-R Modeling ( Entity Relational Modeling) and have a Primary key and Foreign Key. ➢ Entity relational is nothing but a tables like customer tables, location tables, category, sub category and making a relational between two tables. ➢ It is an real time object and this tables we can call as Master tables. ➢ There is a Transaction table(sales tables, etc) in that we can see the information but we want use a joins. ➢ OLTP concept it is for any update or insert should be a normalized because there is tables in a Small and performance is less. ➢ In that OLTP want get a information is difficult because there is a different tables like customer table and Details customer table . ➢ We want to implement the business intelligence, so we have to apply to data because it is not a format data\n• Master Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Primary Keys Product ID Product name 1 Honda Bike 2 Tvs Bike Customer ID Customer Name 1 Rama 2 Siva Transaction Table: Product ID Customer ID Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ By using a ETL to loading the data OLTP system to another system, so we called a Data Ware Housing or OLAP. ➢ By Designing a OLAP we have modeling is Dimensional and tables we can called as Dimensional Tables( Dimproduct, dimcustomer, etc) in that we have a Bussiness key. ➢ It will give the information from table will get a combination of Dimcustomer and Dim detalis customer. ➢ In this dimensional it will check the is existing or not, if not existing it will update or insert. ➢ In this Dimensional table will have a Surrogate key, while inserting the data automatically it will give a Unique number and easily we can identify the data. ➢ There is Fact Table it is a connection of all dimensions tables. ➢ We can merge and also we can add a Values like Quantity, amount etc\n• Dimensional Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Business Keys and Pkey and Ckey are called a Surrogate key\n• Page | 22 PKey Product ID Product name 1 1 Honda Bike 2 2 Tvs Bike CKey Customer ID Customer Name 1 1 Rama 2 2 Siva Fact Table: Pkey Ckey Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ In this Dimensional there is two schema 1\n• Star Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snow Flake Schema","type":"concept","a":"S.NO Star Schema Snowflake Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, The fact tables and While in snowflake schema, The fact tables,","type":"concept","a":"the dimension tables are contained. dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema is a top-down model. While it is a bottom-up model.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema uses more space. While it uses less space.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It takes less time for the execution While it takes more time than star schema for the","type":"concept","a":"of queries. execution of queries.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, Normalization is While in this, Both normalization and","type":"concept","a":"not used. denormalization are used.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"The query complexity of star While the query complexity of snowflake schema is","type":"concept","a":"schema is low. higher than star schema.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It has high data redundancy. While it has low data redundancy.","type":"concept","a":"Page | 23 Star Schema Diagram: Snow Flake Diagram: Page | 24 ===================Azure Questions===============================","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Round Robin: It will dis tribute data randomly row by row on each node. Less volume","type":"concept","a":"of data tables , dimension tables(customer, product) Disadvantage is data need to sorted at the time of combining from all nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Hash: It will distributed based on column similar values like same kind of product","type":"concept","a":"• ID will share one node\n• High volume data tables , Fact tables(Sales table) Advantage is no need of sorting while combining of data from nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is dimension modelling?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Dimensional Data Modelling is one of the data modelling techniques used in data warehouse design\n• To improve the data retrieval\n• Dimensional modeling involves the use of fact and dimension tables to maintain a record of historical data in data warehouses","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is star schema and snowflake schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A.In star schema, The fact tables and the dimension tables are contained\n• In this schema fewer foreign- key join is used\n• This schema forms a star with fact table and dimension tables\n• In snowflake schema, The fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained\n• This schema forms a snowflake with fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub- dimension tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is surrogate key?","type":"concept","a":"A.Surrogate key also called a synthetic primary key, is generated when a new record is inserted into a table automatically by a database that can be declared as the primary key of that table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"A.Fact means measures and dimension means description related to facts.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Slowly changing dimension: Changing over a period of time.is nothing but","type":"concept","a":"address These are three types Type1: need to override address, Type2: store all address Type3: need to store only current and previous address only or current previous and before one","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed dimension: A dimension table which can be used multiple fact tables is","type":"concept","a":"nothing but conformed dimension.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Degenerate dimension: Fact table contain dimension table information that is","type":"concept","a":"called as degenerate dimension table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Junk Dimension: If a model has to many small dimension, we can take all this model","type":"concept","a":"in one dimension table is nothing but junk dimensions Ex: ‘True or False’, ‘Yes or No’, ‘Male and Female’, ’Single or married’","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Role-Plying dimension: Fact table is more than two times relationship with dimension","type":"concept","a":"table is nothing but Role-Play dimension","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Static dimension: Something which never change like gender is nothing but static dimension","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is different kind of facts?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Additive facts: All data in the fact can be aggregate, we can apply all aggregate","type":"concept","a":"functions on data example sales.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Semi additive facts: we cannot apply all aggregate function but you will able to apply","type":"concept","a":"some aggregate functions example current account balance.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Non additive facts: we cannot apply any aggregate function like percentage tables","type":"concept","a":"Page | 30","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of fact tables in DWH?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Fact less fact table: It does not contain any measure in it contain only foreign","type":"concept","a":"from dimension table","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snapshot fact table: Store the data in relation to date or time","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Centipede fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What data is moved to Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Only analy(cid:415)cs-ready datasets:  Fact tables  Dimension tables  Aggregated tables  Repor(cid:415)ng datasets Opera(cid:415)onal tables remain in SQL Server.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Implement SCD Type 2","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Fact and Dimension","type":"concept","a":"Dimension:  Customer  Product  Employee Fact:  Sales  Orders  Transac(cid:415)ons SCD Type 2 is generally applied to dimension tables.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"About SCD (Slowly Changing Dimensions)","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) is a data warehousing technique for managing changes to dimension data over time\n• Different types handle historical tracking differently: SCD Type Strategy Keeps History? Storage Impact Type 0 Never update — static dimension No Minimal Type 1 Overwrite — replace old value with No Minimal new Type 2 Add new row — expire old, insert Yes (full history) Grows over time new with dates Type 3 Add new column — current + Partial (1 version Moderate previous value only back) Type 4 History table — current in main, Yes Moderate history in separate table Type 6 Hybrid: Type 1+2+3 combined Yes + current flag Larger SCD Type 2 is most commonly asked in interviews: • A new row is added for each change to a dimension record • The old row is 'expired' by setting end_date and is_current = 0 • The new row gets start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1 • The natural key (e.g., customer_id) remains the same; a surrogate key identifies each version Page 5 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me about yourself and your skill-set?","type":"concept","a":"• I am a Data Engineer with experience in building and optimizing data pipelines, ETL/ELT workflows, and cloud- based data platforms\n• My core skill-set includes: • Big Data Technologies: Apache Spark (PySpark), Databricks, Delta Lake • Cloud Platforms: Azure, AWS, or GCP — cloud storage, compute, and managed services • Data Warehousing: Designing star/snowflake schemas, building fact and dimension tables • Programming: Python, SQL, Scala (basic) • Data Ingestion: Auto Loader, Kafka, Azure Data Factory, or AWS Glue • Orchestration: Apache Airflow, Databricks Workflows, Azure Data Factory pipelines • Version Control: Git, CI/CD pipelines for data • Data Governance: Implementing SCD-1, SCD-2, data quality checks, schema evolution I have worked on end-to-end data platform projects including migrations, real-time ingestion, and building curated analytical layers for BI consumption","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 2 (Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2) tracks the full history of changes to a dimension record by creating a new row for each change rather than overwriting the existing record\n• Extra Columns Required for SCD-2: • start_date / effective_from: Date when the record became active. • end_date / effective_to: Date when the record was superseded (NULL for current record). • is_current / is_active: Boolean flag — TRUE for the latest active record. • (Optional) surrogate_key: A system-generated unique key for each row version\n• How SCD-2 Works: • When a record changes, the existing row is 'closed' by setting end_date = today and is_current = False. • A new row is inserted with the updated values, start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = True. • Historical queries can use the date range to retrieve the state of a record at any point in time","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 implementation using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) Type 2 is used to maintain the complete history of changes in dimension data\n• Instead of updating an existing record, we expire the old record and insert a new one whenever a tracked attribute changes\n• This allows us to preserve historical data for reporting and auditing Steps","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain all the SCD types and difference between them","type":"concept","a":"SCD Types & Differences Type Behavior History Use Case SCD 0 No change allowed No Sta(cid:415)c data SCD 1 Overwrite old data No history Fixing errors SCD 2 New row for changes Full history Tracking changes SCD 3 Adds new column for previous value Limited history Few a(cid:425)ributes only (cid:44934) Example:  Salary change: o SCD1 → overwrite old salary o SCD2 → new row inserted o SCD3 → old salary stored in another column","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Star & Snowflake schema","type":"concept","a":"Star vs Snowflake Schema Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Structure Denormalized Normalized Joins Fewer More Performance Faster Slower Storage More space Op(cid:415)mized Complexity Simple Complex Example:  Star → Fact table + dimension tables  Snowflake → dimension tables further split","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Do you know data modeling? How do you rate yourself out of 5?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes, I have worked with data modeling while designing data warehouse solu(cid:415)ons\n• I'm comfortable with fact and dimension tables, Star Schema, Snowflake Schema, normaliza(cid:415)on, and denormaliza(cid:415)on\n• I would rate myself 4 out of 5 because I have prac(cid:415)cal experience, while I'm s(cid:415)ll expanding my knowledge in advanced modeling techniques like Data Vault.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"We have transac(cid:415)onal data and analy(cid:415)cal data. Which schema do you use?","type":"concept","a":"• \"For transac(cid:415)onal systems (OLTP), I use a normalized schema (3NF) because it minimizes redundancy and maintains data integrity for frequent inserts, updates, and deletes\n• For analy(cid:415)cal systems (OLAP), I generally use a Star Schema because it is op(cid:415)mized for repor(cid:415)ng, aggrega(cid:415)on, and fast query performance.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Star Schema and Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension tables are denormalized Dimension tables are normalized Fewer joins More joins Be(cid:425)er query performance Slightly slower due to addi(cid:415)onal joins Easier to understand More complex More storage required Less storage required Preferred for most data warehouses Used when dimensions have significant redundancy Example: Star Schema Product | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Store Snowflake Schema Product | Product Category | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Geography | Country","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why do you choose Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal data?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I prefer a Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal workloads because repor(cid:415)ng queries usually aggregate millions of records\n• Since the dimension tables are denormalized, fewer joins are required, which improves query performance\n• It's also easier for BI tools like Power BI and Tableau to consume.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"When would you use Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I use a Snowflake Schema when dimension tables contain a lot of redundant data or have natural hierarchical rela(cid:415)onships, such as Country → State → City or Category → Subcategory → Product\n• Normalizing these dimensions reduces redundancy and makes updates easier, although it requires more joins.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What are Dimension and Fact Tables? Explain in detail.","type":"concept","a":"• Fact Table Stores measurable business events\n• Examples:  Sales Amount  Quan(cid:415)ty Sold  Revenue  Profit Contains:  Numeric measures  Foreign keys to dimension tables Example: ProductKey CustomerKey DateKey SalesAmount 101 201 20240101 500 Dimension Table Stores descrip(cid:415)ve informa(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  Customer  Product  Employee  Date  Store Example: ProductKey ProductName Category 101 Laptop Electronics Rela(cid:415)onship Customer Dimension | Product Dimension -- Sales Fact -- Date Dimension | Store Dimension Difference Fact Table Dimension Table Stores business measures Stores descrip(cid:415)ve a(cid:425)ributes Very large Smaller Contains foreign keys Contains primary keys Used for calcula(cid:415)ons Used for filtering and grouping","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Large Fact and Dimension Tables","type":"concept","a":"If both tables are large:  Prefer Sort Merge Join.  Ensure par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning on the join key.  Avoid broadcas(cid:415)ng either table.  Enable AQE to op(cid:415)mize joins dynamically.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Model? Which One Did You Use? (Star Schema)","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Model defines the structure, relationships, and rules for organizing data\n• Common models: Star Schema (Most Common in DW): One central Fact table surrounded by Dimension tables\n• No normalization of dimensions. • Fact Table: Contains measurable, quantitative data (sales_amount, quantity) • Dimension Tables: Descriptive attributes (customer, product, date, location) • Pros: Simple queries, fast aggregations, easy for BI tools • Cons: Data redundancy in dimensions Snowflake Schema: • Extension of star schema where dimensions are normalized into sub-dimensions • Pros: Less redundancy\n• Cons: More joins, slower queries Galaxy/Fact Constellation Schema: • Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables Example Star Schema: Fact_Sales (sale_id, date_key, product_key, customer_key, amount, qty) Dim_Date (date_key, date, month, quarter, year) Dim_Product(product_key, name, category, brand) Dim_Customer(customer_key, name, city, country)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]}]},{"id":"data-architect","name":"Data Architect","description":"Data Architect interview preparation","topics":[{"id":"architecture","name":"Data Architecture & Design","icon":"🏗️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold)?","type":"concept","a":"Medallion architecture organizes a lakehouse into progressive quality layers:\n• Bronze (raw) — exact copy of source, minimal transformation, append-only, audit trail, Delta format.\n• Silver (cleansed/conformed) — cleaned, validated, deduplicated, standardized, joined; the 'single source of truth' for analytics/DS.\n• Gold (business) — aggregated, enriched, business-ready (KPIs, star schemas) for BI/reporting.\nTypical flow: ADLS → Databricks → Bronze → Silver → Gold → Power BI."},{"q":"What is stored in each Medallion layer and in what format?","type":"concept","a":"• Bronze: raw as-is + metadata columns (load_date, ingestion_timestamp, source_file). Delta (converted from CSV/JSON/Parquet). No business transforms. Path e.g. /bronze/orders.\n• Silver: validated, deduplicated, type-cast, joined, conformed data. Delta. /silver/orders_clean.\n• Gold: business aggregates, KPIs, star-schema fact/dim tables. Delta (or Parquet for serving). /gold/daily_sales.\nAlways store transformed data as Delta for ACID, time travel, schema evolution, and MERGE."},{"q":"Walk through an end-to-end Medallion implementation.","type":"concept","a":"1. Ingest — ADF / Auto Loader / COPY INTO lands raw files into ADLS Bronze as Delta (add metadata columns).\n2. Transform to Silver — PySpark/DLT: cleanse, dedupe, cast types, standardize, apply DQ checks, join reference data; MERGE incrementally.\n3. Build Gold — aggregate to KPI tables / star schema (facts + dims), apply SCD logic, partition by date.\n4. Serve — Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse; ML from Silver.\n5. Govern — Unity Catalog for access, lineage, RLS/CLS; monitor with alerts + audit.\n6. Orchestrate — Databricks Workflows / ADF with retries, parameterization, dev→qa→prod promotion."},{"q":"What data validations do you perform on the Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema/type validation — columns and types match expectations.\n• Null checks on critical columns.\n• Duplicate checks on business/primary keys.\n• Range/domain validation (e.g., age 0–150, valid enums).\n• Referential integrity (child keys exist in parents via left anti join).\n• Record-count reconciliation vs source/threshold.\n• Business rules (end_date >= start_date).\nImplement as DQ framework / DLT expectations; quarantine or fail on critical violations."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Data moves from Silver to Gold. At what stage do you aggregate and why? How do you apply RLS/CLS there?","type":"scenario","a":"• Aggregate in the GOLD layer — Silver keeps granular, conformed data (reusable, single source of truth); Gold is purpose-built for BI with pre-computed aggregates (faster dashboards, smaller scans). Aggregating too early loses reusability.\n• RLS/CLS: apply in Gold (and where sensitive columns exist) using Unity Catalog row filters and column masks so Power BI/Tableau consumers only see permitted rows/columns. Enforce centrally in UC, not in each report.\n• Also restrict access via GRANTs on Gold tables/views."},{"q":"When would you choose batch vs streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — scheduled, high-throughput, latency-tolerant: end-of-day reports, historical/backfill loads, month-end close, large transformations.\n• Streaming — continuous, low-latency: fraud detection, IoT/telemetry, clickstream, real-time alerting/dashboards, CDC propagation.\nMany platforms use a lambda-style hybrid: streaming for speed, batch for corrections/reprocessing."},{"q":"How do you design a pipeline for incremental data loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Track a watermark (max updated_ts/ID) per table in a control table.\n• Extract only records > watermark (predicate pushdown at source).\n• Apply transforms; MERGE into the target (upsert; handle deletes via CDC if available).\n• Update the watermark only after success.\n• Handle late-arriving data (overlap window / reprocess) and ensure idempotency.\n• Schedule/orchestrate via ADF or Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"What is data quality and what are its dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"Data Quality = how fit data is for its intended use. Dimensions:\n• Completeness (no missing required values)\n• Accuracy (reflects reality)\n• Consistency (same across systems)\n• Timeliness (available when needed)\n• Uniqueness (no duplicates)\n• Validity (conforms to formats/rules).\nEnforced via DLT expectations, Great Expectations, or custom PySpark checks; fail fast or quarantine bad records."},{"q":"How do you handle bad/corrupt records?","type":"concept","a":"Never let bad records corrupt trusted layers. Options:\n• Quarantine — write invalid rows to a separate bad-records table/path for triage.\n• Rescue — Auto Loader's _rescued_data column keeps unmatched/corrupt values.\n• Drop/log — expect_or_drop in DLT (log + drop) or filter with logging.\n• Fail fast — expect_or_fail for critical rules (stop the pipeline).\n• Schema handling — mode PERMISSIVE (keeps corrupt as null), DROPMALFORMED (drop), FAILFAST (error)."},{"q":"What is the 'typical architecture' for an Azure data engineering project?","type":"concept","a":"Sources (SQL Server, APIs, CSV, SAP) → ADF (orchestration/ingestion) → ADLS Gen2 → Databricks (PySpark transformations) with Bronze/Silver/Gold Delta layers → serve to Power BI / Synapse.\nCross-cutting: Unity Catalog governance, Key Vault secrets, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics, CI/CD, and orchestration via ADF/Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"How do you decide between partitioning, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning — low-cardinality columns (date, region, country) where each partition is ≥ ~1 GB; enables partition pruning; avoid high cardinality/over-partitioning.\n• Z-ORDER — high-cardinality filter/join columns (customer_id) on existing (often partitioned) tables; re-cluster on OPTIMIZE.\n• Liquid Clustering — modern default for evolving queries; incremental auto re-clustering, no partition maintenance; can replace partition+Z-ORDER.\nDecide by query filter patterns + table size + maintenance overhead."},{"q":"How do you handle schema drift/evolution across the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Detect: compare source vs expected schema (Get Metadata/Validate) or let reads report new columns.\n• ADF: Allow schema drift in data flows; benign schema changes flow through.\n• Auto Loader: schema inference + evolution (_rescued_data for mismatches).\n• Delta: mergeSchema on write / delta.autoMerge; ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN.\n• Design Bronze to be schema-flexible (raw), enforce structure at Silver/Gold.\n• Add alerts on drift so downstream contracts don't break silently."},{"q":"Explain your Project Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Our project follows Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• Flow: Source Systems | | (SAP, SQL Server, APIs, CSV, Oracle) | Azure Data Factory | | Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 | | Databricks Bronze Layer (Raw Data) | Silver Layer (Cleansed & Transformed) | Gold Layer (Business Ready Tables) | Power BI / Synapse / ML Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2  Azure Databricks  Delta Lake  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI  Azure Monitor","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"what is difference between data lake and data warehouse","type":"concept","a":"• Data lakes and data warehouses are both widely used for storing big data, but they are not interchangeable terms\n• A data lake is a vast pool of raw data, the purpose for which is not yet defined\n• A data warehouse is a repository for structured, filtered data that has already been processed for a specific purpose","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It’s design is very simple. While it’s design is complex.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Architecture of Synapse Data ware house? And what are different kind of","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"19.What is DW?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Data ware house is nothing but collect the data, store the data, analysis the data and consume the data\n• A data warehouse is specially designed for data analytics, which involves reading large amounts of data to understand relationships and trends across the data\n• A database is used to capture and store data, such as recording details of a transaction","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is data mart?","type":"concept","a":"A data mart is a simple form of data warehouse focused on a single subject or line of business.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Can you introduce yourself?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Hi, I'm an Azure Data Engineer with experience in building end-to-end data pipelines using Azure Data Factory (ADF), Azure Databricks, ADLS Gen2, Azure SQL Database, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on inges(cid:415)ng data from mul(cid:415)ple source systems into Azure Data Lake, transforming it using PySpark in Databricks, and loading curated data into the Gold layer following the Medallion Architecture\n• I have experience in developing ADF pipelines, crea(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng data valida(cid:415)on and cleansing, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, handling incremental loads, monitoring pipelines, and suppor(cid:415)ng produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Our project is an employee analy(cid:415)cs pla(cid:414)orm\n• Data comes from HR systems, A(cid:425)endance systems, and Payroll applica(cid:415)ons in CSV and SQL formats\n• The architecture is: Source Systems │ Azure Data Factory │ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 │ Bronze Layer │ Azure Databricks (PySpark) │ Silver Layer │ Business Transforma(cid:415)ons │ Gold Layer │ Power BI Repor(cid:415)ng ADF is responsible for orchestra(cid:415)ng the pipelines\n• Databricks performs data cleansing and transforma(cid:415)ons\n• Gold tables are used by business users through Power BI dashboards.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are your roles and responsibili(cid:415)es?","type":"concept","a":"\"My daily responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing Azure Data Factory pipelines  Crea(cid:415)ng Databricks notebooks using PySpark  Reading and wri(cid:415)ng data to ADLS Gen2  Performing data cleansing and valida(cid:415)on  Removing duplicates and handling null values  Implemen(cid:415)ng Delta Lake tables  Loading data into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Monitoring pipeline failures  Fixing produc(cid:415)on issues  Working with Git for version control  Coordina(cid:415)ng with QA and business teams\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ETL and ELT","type":"concept","a":"• ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) and ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) are two approaches for moving and preparing data\n• Aspect ETL ELT Order Extract → Transform → Load Extract → Load → Transform Where Target data Separate ETL tool/server transformations occur warehouse/lakehouse Aspect ETL ELT Data loaded into Already transformed Raw data first, then transformed target Leverages warehouse compute Performance Depends on ETL server capacity power Traditional data warehouses and Modern cloud platforms with Best for strict data quality requirements scalable compute Raw data remains available for Flexibility Less flexible once transformed reprocessing","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your end-to-end project architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Sample Answer \"Our project is an Azure-based data pla(cid:414)orm built using a Medallion Architecture.\" Web Applica(cid:415)ons / ERP / APIs │ ▼ On-Prem SQL Server │ Azure Data Factory │ ▼ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 Bronze Layer (Raw) │ ▼ Azure Databricks (PySpark) Silver Layer (Cleaned) │ ▼ Azure Databricks Gold Layer │ ▼ Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs │ ▼ Power BI Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Databricks  ADLS Gen2  Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Where is the actual data stored?","type":"concept","a":"Different stages:  SQL Server → Opera(cid:415)onal (OLTP)  ADLS Bronze → Raw  ADLS Silver → Cleaned  ADLS Gold → Curated  Synapse → Analy(cid:415)cs/Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are you storing data in SQL Server, ADLS, or Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Yes, but each serves a different purpose: Storage Purpose SQL Server Opera(cid:415)onal data ADLS Data lake (Bronze, Silver, Gold) Synapse Repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Architecture of project (End-to-End pipeline)","type":"concept","a":"We follow Medallion Architecture in Azure Databricks: (cid:44934) Flow: Sources  SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake ADF (Inges(cid:415)on Layer)  Copy Ac(cid:415)vity / Mapping Data Flow  Moves data to ADLS ADLS Gen2 Storage  Bronze (Raw data) Databricks Processing  Bronze → Silver → Gold (cid:44934) Layers:  Bronze → Raw ingested data  Silver → Cleaned + validated + deduplicated  Gold → Business aggregated data Consump(cid:415)on Layer  Power BI / Tableau / SQL Warehouse","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"After Transformation, Where Do You Store the Data?","type":"concept","a":"• After transformation, data is stored in the appropriate Medallion Architecture layer as a Delta table: Layer What is Stored Format Location Example Bronze (Raw) Raw data as-is from source;\n• Delta (from delta/bronze/raw_orders metadata columns added CSV/JSON/Parquet) Silver (Cleansed) Validated, deduplicated, type-cast, Delta delta/silver/orders_clean joined data Gold (Curated) Business-level aggregations, KPIs, Delta or Parquet delta/gold/daily_sales reporting tables # Write transformed Silver data to Delta table silver_df.write .format('delta') .mode('overwrite') # or 'append' / 'merge' for incremental .option('mergeSchema','true') .partitionBy('year','month') .saveAsTable('silver.orders_clean') # Or write to ADLS path silver_df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders/') Always store transformed data as Delta format — it gives ACID transactions, time travel, schema evolution, and efficient MERGE operations for incremental updates","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Architecture in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks follows the Lakehouse Architecture — combining the scalability of a Data Lake with the governance and performance of a Data Warehouse\n• Medallion Architecture (Most Common) Data flows through three progressive layers — each improving quality and structure: Layer Also Called Purpose Format Bronze Raw Layer Ingest raw data as-is from source Delta / Parquet / JSON / — no transformations CSV Silver Cleansed Layer Clean, deduplicate, validate, join Delta Table — trusted, queryable data Gold Aggregated / Business-level aggregations, Delta Table Business Layer KPIs, reporting-ready data Core Databricks Architecture Components Component Description Control Plane Managed by Databricks (Azure/AWS/GCP) — stores metadata, notebooks, job configs, UI Data Plane Customer's cloud account — where Spark clusters run and data is stored in ADLS/S3 Clusters Spark compute nodes — All-Purpose (interactive) or Job Clusters (automated pipelines) DBFS Databricks File System — abstraction over cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) Unity Catalog Unified governance layer — manages tables, schemas, data lineage, access control Delta Lake Storage layer for all tables — provides ACID, versioning, schema enforcement MLflow Built-in ML lifecycle management — experiment tracking, model registry, deployment Workflows / Jobs Orchestration layer — schedule notebooks, DLT pipelines, dbt, Python scripts Autoloader Incremental file ingestion from cloud storage into Delta tables Delta Live Tables Declarative pipeline framework with built-in data quality and monitoring Data Flow in the Architecture • Sources (SQL Server, APIs, flat files, IoT) land raw data in ADLS/S3 Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 • Bronze layer ingests via Autoloader or COPY INTO — raw Delta tables • Silver layer cleanses, deduplicates, and enriches with business rules • Gold layer aggregates for BI tools (Power BI, Tableau) or ML models • Unity Catalog governs access and lineage across all three layers Page 5 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You mentioned in your resume that you worked on a migration project. Explain","type":"concept","a":"• about it\n• In the migration project, we moved our existing on-premises data warehouse (e.g., SQL Server / Oracle) to a cloud-based Lakehouse architecture on Databricks / Azure Synapse\n• Key Phases of the Migration: • Assessment & Planning: Audited existing tables, stored procedures, ETL jobs, and data volumes\n• Identified dependencies and prioritized migration order. • Architecture Design: Designed a Medallion Architecture (Bronze → Silver → Gold) on Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS Gen2) with Delta Lake format. • Data Extraction: Extracted data from the source (on-prem DB) using JDBC connectors or flat file exports. • Transformation & Load: Rewrote SQL-based transformations in PySpark\n• Applied business logic, cleansing, deduplication, and type casting. • Validation: Compared row counts, aggregates, and key metrics between source and target to ensure data integrity. • Cutover: Ran parallel loads, froze the source, did final delta sync, and switched downstream consumers to the new platform","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Project Architecture (End-to-End)","type":"concept","a":"We used Azure + Databricks Medallion Architecture:  Sources: SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake  ADF: Data inges(cid:415)on + orchestra(cid:415)on  ADLS Gen2: Storage layer  Databricks: o Bronze → Raw inges(cid:415)on o Silver → Cleaned + transformed o Gold → Business-ready data  Consump(cid:415)on: Power BI / SQL Warehouse","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How many layers you will be having in your databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Layers in Databricks  Bronze → Raw data  Silver → Cleaned + validated data  Gold → Aggregated business data (Some systems also use Pla(cid:415)num for ML)","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your current data pipeline architecture tools, flow, and data volume.","type":"concept","a":"• Our source systems include SQL Database, REST APIs, and CSV files uploaded to Azure Blob Storage\n• Azure Data Factory orchestrates data ingestion into ADLS Gen2 Bronze storage\n• Azure Databricks performs cleansing, deduplication, joins, aggregations, and business transformations using PySpark\n• Processed data is stored in Delta Lake Silver and Gold layers\n• Power BI consumes the Gold layer for reporting\n• Azure Monitor and Log Analytics are used for monitoring, while Git-based CI/CD manages deployments\n• On average, we process around 80–150 GB of data daily, which translates to several million records depending on the source","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain about yourself.","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Answer (2-3 minutes) \"Hi, I'm a Data Engineer with around X years of experience in designing and developing ETL/ELT data pipelines using Azure Data Factory, Azure Databricks, PySpark, SQL, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on building scalable data inges(cid:415)on frameworks where data is collected from mul(cid:415)ple sources such as SQL databases, APIs, and cloud storage into Azure Data Lake\n• We follow the Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• ADF is mainly used for orchestra(cid:415)on, Databricks performs data transforma(cid:415)on, and Delta Lake stores processed data\n• My responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing ADF pipelines  Wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Data valida(cid:415)on  Incremental loading  Performance tuning  Pipeline monitoring and troubleshoo(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the data framework that you are working on.","type":"concept","a":"A typical architecture: Source Systems (SQL, APIs, CSV, SAP) ↓ Azure Data Factory (Orchestra(cid:415)on) ↓ Azure Data Lake Gen2 ↓ Bronze Layer (Raw Delta Tables) ↓ Databricks ↓ Silver Layer (Cleansed + Standardized) ↓ Gold Layer (Business Aggrega(cid:415)ons) ↓ Power BI / Synapse Explain each layer: Bronze  Raw data  No transforma(cid:415)ons  Append only  Delta format Silver  Data cleaning  Remove duplicates  Null handling  Data type conversion  Standardiza(cid:415)on Gold  Business-ready data  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  KPI tables  Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Delta Live Tables?","type":"concept","a":"• DLT is a Databricks framework used for building reliable ETL pipelines\n• Features:  Automa(cid:415)c dependency management  Incremental processing  Data quality expecta(cid:415)ons  Auto retries  Pipeline monitoring  Automa(cid:415)c lineage  Schema evolu(cid:415)on Example: Bronze ↓ Silver ↓ Gold No need to manually manage execu(cid:415)on order","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How is data stored in Bronze?","type":"concept","a":"Usually:  Delta format  Append only  Raw  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oned (op(cid:415)onal)  Metadata columns Example: customer.csv ↓ Bronze customer_id name city load_date inges(cid:415)on_(cid:415)mestamp source_file No business transforma(cid:415)ons.","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Project Explanation","type":"concept","a":"• Use the STAR framework: Situation, Task, Action, Result\n• Always quantify outcomes. • Situation: Organization needed to process 10TB+ daily transaction data from Kafka, S3, and REST APIs for fraud detection. • Task: Design and implement an end-to-end pipeline using Databricks and Delta Lake, replacing legacy batch jobs. • Action: Built Bronze/Silver/Gold medallion architecture\n• Used Auto Loader for incremental ingestion, Structured Streaming for near-real-time processing, Z-ordering for query optimization, and Delta constraints for data quality. • Result: Reduced data latency from 6 hours to 15 minutes\n• Query performance improved by 70%\n• Storage costs reduced by 40% via Delta compaction\n• Page 1 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How many Delta tables are there in Silver and Gold layers?","type":"concept","a":"• \"In my project, each curated dataset is stored as a separate Delta table\n• The exact number depends on the project\n• For example, we maintain around 20–30 Delta tables in the Silver layer and 10–15 aggregated/business tables in the Gold layer\n• The key point is that each business en(cid:415)ty or repor(cid:415)ng dataset is maintained as its own Delta table.\" (Replace the numbers with your project's actual values if known.)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Notebooks Does a Data Engineer Typically Deal With in a Year?","type":"concept","a":"• This is experience-based\n• Tailor your answer to your actual project, but a typical range: • Small project: 10-20 notebooks per year Page 1 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Mid-size project: 25-50 notebooks (Bronze/Silver/Gold layers, 3-10 notebooks per domain per layer) • Large enterprise: 50-100+ notebooks organized by domain (Finance, Orders, Customers, etc.) Sample Answer: \"In my current project I work with approximately 30-40 notebooks organized by layer\n• We have Bronze ingestion notebooks, Silver cleansing/transformation notebooks, Gold aggregation notebooks for reporting, plus shared utility notebooks for config and common functions.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Daily Volume of Data You Process?","type":"concept","a":"• Scale Volume/Day Typical Cluster Small Less than 10 GB 4-8 cores, 16-32 GB RAM Medium 10 GB - 1 TB 16-64 cores, 128-256 GB RAM Large 1 TB - 100 TB 100+ cores, 500 GB+ RAM, Auto-scaling Enterprise 100 TB+ Hundreds of nodes, streaming pipelines Sample Answer: \"We process approximately 500 GB to 2 TB of raw data daily from 15 upstream systems\n• After transformation the Silver/Gold layers are 200-500 GB\n• Peak loads during month-end hit 5 TB.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What does the current data architecture look like — Medallion/Lakehouse or more traditional ETL?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What do you do in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":" Develop PySpark notebooks  Build ETL pipelines (bronze → silver → gold)  Op(cid:415)mize performance (par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching)  Handle incremental loads (Delta Lake)  Create jobs/workflows  Work with Delta tables","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project from source to repor(cid:415)ng?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I am working on an Employee Analy(cid:415)cs project using Azure Data Engineering services\n• The source systems include SQL Server databases, REST APIs, and CSV files from SFTP\n• Azure Data Factory (ADF) orchestrates the data inges(cid:415)on process\n• The raw data is stored in Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 (Bronze layer)\n• Azure Databricks processes the data using PySpark, where we perform data valida(cid:415)on, duplicate removal, null handling, joins, aggrega(cid:415)ons, and business transforma(cid:415)ons\n• The transformed data is stored in Delta tables in the Silver and Gold layers\n• The Gold layer is consumed by Power BI for dashboards and business repor(cid:415)ng\n• I am responsible for developing ADF pipelines, wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loads, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, monitoring produc(cid:415)on pipelines, and resolving produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Medallion Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold) is a data design pattern that organizes data into progressive quality layers in a Data Lakehouse\n• Bronze Layer (Raw): • Exact copy of source data, minimal transformation • All data lands here (append-only), serves as audit trail • Format: Delta or Parquet Silver Layer (Cleaned/Conformed): • Data is cleaned, deduplicated, validated • Joins applied, schema enforced • Ready for data science and analytics use cases Gold Layer (Business-level Aggregates): • Aggregated, enriched, business-ready data • Star schema models, KPIs, dashboards • Optimized for reporting tools (Power BI, Tableau) # Bronze: Raw ingest df_raw.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/bronze/orders') # Silver: Clean and deduplicate df_silver = df_raw.dropDuplicates(['order_id']).filter(col('amount') > 0) df_silver.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/silver/orders') # Gold: Aggregate for reporting df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('region').agg(sum('amount').alias('total_revenue')) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/gold/revenue_by_region') SECTION 5: Spark Internals & Optimization","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Medallian Architecture ?","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture is a layered data design pa(cid:425)ern used in data lakes where data is organized into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers\n• Bronze stores raw ingested data, Silver contains cleaned and standardized data, and Gold contains aggregated business-ready data for repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs\n• This approach improves data quality, maintainability, and scalability of pipelines","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Why Medallion Architecture?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:33158)(cid:57393) Benefits:  Be(cid:425)er data quality  Easy debugging (trace back to raw data)  Reusable datasets  Scalable pipeline design  Supports incremental processing","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you move on prem data to fabric to delta lake house ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move on-prem data to Microso(cid:332) Fabric Lakehouse, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to connect the source systems securely\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines in Fabric to ingest the data into OneLake in Delta format\n• The data is stored in Bronze layer as raw data, then transformed into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark or CDC-based extrac(cid:415)on to ensure efficiency","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Oracle to fabric ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move data from Oracle to Microso(cid:332) Fabric, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to establish a secure connec(cid:415)on if Oracle is on-prem\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines to extract data from Oracle using SQL queries\n• The data is loaded into OneLake Lakehouse in Delta format, typically in the Bronze layer\n• Later, I transform it into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark columns or Oracle CDC to ensure efficient data movement","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Which layer data is ready to use for presenta(cid:415)on purpose","type":"concept","a":"Gold layer is the final curated layer in Medallion Architecture that is op(cid:415)mized and structured for business repor(cid:415)ng and visualiza(cid:415)on tools.","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"As a team lead you have 300 tables in Oracle database, what will be your approach,","type":"concept","a":"• explain your flow\n• If I have 300 tables in Oracle, I will avoid building individual pipelines\n• Instead, I would design a metadata-driven inges(cid:415)on framework where table details like load type, watermark column, and query are stored in a control table\n• I would create a single dynamic ADF or Fabric pipeline that reads metadata and processes all tables using a ForEach loop\n• Data would be ingested into the Bronze layer, then transformed into silver and gold layers using Spark or SQL\n• I would also implement incremental load using watermark or CDC, enable parallel processing for performance, and add monitoring for failure tracking and retries\n• Data skew Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons in distributed systems like Spark, causing some tasks to take significantly longer than others\n• It usually happens during joins, groupBy, or shuffle opera(cid:415)ons\n• To resolve it, I use techniques like broadcast join for small tables, sal(cid:415)ng for skewed keys, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data, enabling Spark adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on, and pre-aggrega(cid:415)ng data before joins","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"modeling","name":"Data Modeling & SCD","icon":"📐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is a data model? Explain star vs snowflake vs galaxy schema.","type":"concept","a":"A data model defines how data is structured, related, and constrained.\n• Star schema — one central FACT table surrounded by denormalized DIMENSION tables. Simple queries, fast aggregations, BI-friendly. Slight redundancy. Most common in data warehouses.\n• Snowflake schema — dimensions normalized into sub-dimensions (less redundancy, more joins, slower).\n• Galaxy / fact constellation — multiple fact tables sharing conformed dimensions.\nExample: Fact_Sales(sale_id,date_key,product_key,customer_key,amount,qty) + Dim_Date, Dim_Product, Dim_Customer."},{"q":"What are the SCD types (0,1,2,3,4,6)?","type":"concept","a":"• Type 0 — never update (static).\n• Type 1 — overwrite the value; no history.\n• Type 2 — add a new row on change; keep full history with start/end dates + current flag (most common in interviews).\n• Type 3 — add a column to store current + previous value only (1 version back).\n• Type 4 — keep current in main table, history in a separate history table.\n• Type 6 — hybrid (1+2+3): full history rows PLUS a current flag and a current-value column."},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 in detail and how you implement it.","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 preserves history by inserting a NEW row whenever a tracked attribute changes; the natural key stays the same, a surrogate key identifies each version.\nColumns: start_date, end_date, is_current (active flag), surrogate key.\nSteps (Delta MERGE):\n1. Expire the current row if tracked columns changed: set end_date = yesterday, is_current = 0.\n2. Insert a new row for the change: start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1.\n3. Insert brand-new keys as-is.\nOnly one active row per business key at any time."},{"q":"CODING: Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark using a hash/flag approach.","type":"coding","a":"Read history + daily data, compute a hash of tracked columns, split into unchanged / updated / new, expire updates, insert new + updates, then union.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, sha2, concat_ws, lit, current_date, when\n\nTRACK = ['name','city']\n\ndef with_hash(df):\n    return df.withColumn('row_hash', sha2(concat_ws('||', *[col(c) for c in TRACK]), 256))\n\nhist = with_hash(history_df)         # has start/end/is_current\nincr = with_hash(daily_df)\n\ncur = hist.filter(col('is_current')==1)\n\n# rows that changed\nupdates = (incr.alias('s').join(cur.alias('t'), 'customer_id')\n           .filter(col('s.row_hash') != col('t.row_hash'))\n           .select('customer_id', *TRACK))\n\n# new keys\nnew = incr.join(cur, 'customer_id', 'left_anti').select('customer_id', *TRACK)\n\n# expire old current rows for changed keys\nexpired = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id').withColumn('end_date', current_date()).withColumn('is_current', lit(0))\nno_change = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\n\n# new versions of changed + brand new\ninserts = (updates.unionByName(new)\n           .withColumn('start_date', current_date())\n           .withColumn('end_date', lit(None).cast('date'))\n           .withColumn('is_current', lit(1)))\n\nfinal = no_change.unionByName(expired).unionByName(inserts)\nfinal.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('dim_customer')"},{"q":"What is incremental loading and what approaches exist?","type":"concept","a":"Incremental loading processes only new/changed data since the last load instead of a full reload.\n• Timestamp/watermark — load where updated_at > last_run_timestamp; track a high-watermark in a control table.\n• CDC — capture inserts/updates/deletes from the source (log-based, e.g., Debezium/Azure SQL CDC).\n• Delta MERGE (UPSERT) — merge changes into the target.\nBenefits: faster, cheaper, less source load. Watch for late-arriving data and reliable watermark updates."},{"q":"Difference between full load and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Full load — reload all data every run; simple; high source impact and long runtime; used for first load/small tables.\n• Incremental — load only new/changed records; needs watermark/CDC; low source impact, fast; for large tables. Risk: must handle late-arriving data and deletes."},{"q":"Difference between CDC and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load — a loading strategy: only bring in new/changed data (often timestamp-based).\n• CDC — a mechanism that captures row-level INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from the source (log-based/trigger-based), so it can also capture deletes and provide before/after images.\nCDC is a superset that feeds richer incremental loads; plain timestamp incremental usually can't detect deletes."},{"q":"Which table loads first, fact or dimension? How do you relate them?","type":"concept","a":"Dimensions load FIRST — facts reference dimension surrogate keys, so the dimensions (and their keys) must exist before the fact rows can resolve those keys.\nRelation: fact tables store foreign keys pointing to dimension surrogate keys; the join is fact.dim_key = dim.surrogate_key.\nOften a 'late-arriving dimension' pattern is used: insert a placeholder (unknown) dimension row if a fact arrives before its dimension."},{"q":"What is the difference between a surrogate key and a natural key?","type":"concept","a":"• Natural (business) key — a meaningful identifier from the source (e.g., customer_id, SSN).\n• Surrogate key — a system-generated integer uniquely identifying each DIMENSION VERSION (used in SCD2 to distinguish versions).\nFacts join to dimensions on surrogate keys; the natural key links versions of the same entity."},{"q":"What are late-arriving dimensions/facts and how do you handle them?","type":"concept","a":"• Late-arriving dimension — a fact arrives referencing a dimension that hasn't loaded yet.\n• Late-arriving fact — a fact arrives after its expected processing window.\nHandling: insert a placeholder/inferred dimension row (unknown key) and update it when the real record arrives; or defer fact loading until the dimension exists; use watermark + reprocessing and idempotent MERGE so late facts are picked up on the next run."},{"q":"What is a slowly changing dimension and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"SCD manages dimension attributes that change slowly over time (e.g., customer address, department). The type chosen controls how (and whether) history is preserved — so reports can reflect either the current value or the value as of a point in time."},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• In a data warehouse, data is organized into Fact Tables (measures/events) and Dimension Tables (descriptive context)\n• Star and Snowflake are two common ways to arrange these tables\n• Star Schema Characteristics: Fact table is in the center\n• Dimension tables surround it like a star\n• Dimension tables are DENORMALIZED — all attributes in one flat table\n• Simple, fast queries — fewer JOINs needed\n• Best for BI tools and reporting (Power BI, Tableau). -- Star Schema Example: Sales Data Warehouse -- Fact Table (center of the star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key, store_key, sale_amount, quantity, discount -- Dimension Tables (flat, denormalized) dim_customer: customer_key, name, city, state, country, email, age_group dim_product: product_key, product_name, category, brand, color, size dim_date: date_key, date, month, quarter, year, weekday, is_holiday dim_store: store_key, store_name, city, state, region, manager ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 9 -- Query: Total sales by product category (only 2 tables needed) SELECT p.category, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key GROUP BY p.category Snowflake Schema Characteristics: Dimension tables are NORMALIZED — broken into sub-dimension tables\n• Looks like a snowflake because dimensions branch into more tables\n• Saves storage (no repeated values)\n• More complex queries with more JOINs\n• Better for data integrity\n• Used when storage is a constraint. -- Snowflake Schema: Same sales warehouse, normalized -- Fact Table (same as star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key ... -- dim_product is now split into sub-tables dim_product: product_key, product_name, category_key, brand_key dim_category: category_key, category_name, department_key dim_department:department_key, department_name dim_brand: brand_key, brand_name, brand_country -- dim_customer split into sub-tables dim_customer: customer_key, name, city_key, age_group dim_city: city_key, city_name, state_key dim_state: state_key, state_name, country_key dim_country: country_key, country_name, region -- Query: Same total sales by category (now 4 JOINs needed) SELECT dc.category_name, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_category dc ON p.category_key = dc.category_key GROUP BY dc.category_name Comparison Table Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension structure Denormalized (flat) Normalized (broken into sub- tables) Number of JOINs Fewer — simple queries More — complex queries ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 10 Query performance Faster reads Slightly slower due to more JOINs Storage More (repeated values) Less (normalized, no repetition) Data integrity Lower — some redundancy Higher — data in one place Complexity Simple to understand More complex to maintain Best for BI/Reporting, Power BI Large DW with storage constraints Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'We used Star Schema for our reporting layer because it gives faster query performance and is simpler to work with in Power BI","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Questions answered How much? How many? What Who? What? Where? When?","type":"concept","a":"• total? Primary key Surrogate key (sale_id) Surrogate key (customer_key) Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD) — Bonus • SCD Type 1 — Overwrite: Update the record directly\n• No history kept. (e.g., fix a typo in name) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 12 • SCD Type 2 — New Row: Add a new row with effective dates\n• Full history preserved. (e.g., customer moves city) • SCD Type 3 — Add Column: Add a 'previous value' column\n• Limited history. (e.g., previous_city column) Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'Fact tables store the measurable events — like each sale transaction with amount, quantity, and foreign keys\n• Dimension tables provide the context — customer name, product category, date details\n• In our warehouse, fact_sales had 500M rows while dim_customer had only 2M rows.' Q9 PySpark: Top 10 Customers by Total Sales Problem Given: DataFrame: sales_df Schema: customer_id (string), product_id (string), sale_amount (double) Task: Total sales per customer, return top 10 by total sales amount Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, desc # Step 1: Group by customer_id and sum their sale_amounts total_sales_df = sales_df.groupBy('customer_id') \\ .agg(sum('sale_amount').alias('total_sales')) # Step 2: Sort by total_sales descending, take top 10 top_10_customers = total_sales_df \\ .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) \\ .limit(10) # Display result top_10_customers.show() # Output: # +-----------+------------+ # |customer_id|total_sales | # +-----------+------------+ # |CUST_0042 | 245780.50 | # |CUST_0017 | 198340.00 | # |CUST_0099 | 176220.75 | ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 13 # |... | ... | # +-----------+------------+ Alternative: Using SQL in Databricks # Register as temp view sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') # SQL approach top_10_sql = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id, SUM(sale_amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY total_sales DESC LIMIT 10 ''') top_10_sql.show() With Rounding (Production Ready) from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, round as spark_round top_10_customers = ( sales_df .groupBy('customer_id') .agg(spark_round(sum('sale_amount'), 2).alias('total_sales')) .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) .limit(10) ) top_10_customers.show(truncate=False) Interview Tip: Key functions: groupBy() + agg(sum()) for totals, orderBy(col().desc()) for sorting, limit(10) for top N\n• For very large data, consider using window functions with rank() if you need ties included\n• Q10 PySpark: Fill Null Salaries and Add Salary Grade Problem ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 14 Given: DataFrame: employees_df Schema: employee_id (int), department (string), salary (int) — may have NULLs Task 1: Fill null salaries with 50000 Task 2: Add salary_grade column: >=100000 -> Executive, 50000-99999 -> Manager, <50000 -> Associate Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, coalesce, lit # Step 1: Fill null salaries with default value 50000 # Method A: fillna (simple, specific column) df_filled = employees_df.fillna({'salary': 50000}) # Method B: coalesce (returns first non-null value) df_filled = employees_df.withColumn( 'salary', coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) ) # Step 2: Add salary_grade column using when/otherwise df_final = df_filled.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary') >= 100000, 'Executive') .when((col('salary') >= 50000) & (col('salary') < 100000), 'Manager') .otherwise('Associate') ) df_final.show() # Output: # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # |employee_id|department|salary|salary_grade| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # | 101| Sales |120000| Executive| # | 102| HR | null| Manager| <- null -> 50000 -> Manager # | 103| Tech | 75000| Manager| # | 104| Sales | 35000| Associate| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ Key Functions Explained Function Purpose Example fillna(value, subset) Replace nulls in specific fillna({'salary': 50000}) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 15 columns coalesce(col, lit(val)) Return first non-null of multiple coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) exprs when(condition, value) If-else logic for new column when(col('salary')>=100000,'Execut ive') .otherwise(value) Default value if no when .otherwise('Associate') matches withColumn(name, Add or replace a column withColumn('salary_grade', expr) when(...)) Interview Tip: The when().when().otherwise() chain is PySpark's equivalent of SQL CASE WHEN\n• Always put the most specific condition first (>=100000 before >=50000)\n• Use fillna before applying when so null-handling is clean\n• Q11 PySpark: Most Recent Transaction Per User (Window Function) Problem Given: DataFrame: transactions_df Schema: user_id (string), transaction_timestamp (timestamp), transaction_amount (double) Task: Find the single MOST RECENT transaction for each user — return all 3 columns Solution 1: Window Function with rank() — Best Approach from pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Define window: partition by user_id, order by timestamp descending window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) # Add rank column — rank 1 = most recent for each user df_ranked = transactions_df.withColumn('rank', rank().over(window_spec)) # Keep only rank 1 (most recent) and drop the rank column most_recent = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') == 1) \\ .drop('rank') ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 16 most_recent.show() # Output: # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |user_id|transaction_timestamp |transaction_amount| # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |U001 |2024-01-15 14:32:00 | 129.99 | # |U002 |2024-01-16 09:15:00 | 49.50 | # |U003 |2024-01-14 21:00:00 | 299.00 | # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ Solution 2: Using row_number() — Guarantees Single Row from pyspark.sql.functions import col, row_number from pyspark.sql.window import Window # row_number() assigns 1 to the latest tx per user (no ties) # rank() would give ties the same number — use row_number to be safe window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) most_recent = ( transactions_df .withColumn('row_num', row_number().over(window_spec)) .filter(col('row_num') == 1) .drop('row_num') ) Solution 3: groupBy + join (Without Window Functions) from pyspark.sql.functions import max # Step 1: Find max timestamp per user max_ts = transactions_df \\ .groupBy('user_id') \\ .agg(max('transaction_timestamp').alias('max_ts')) # Step 2: Join back to original to get full row most_recent = transactions_df.join( max_ts, (transactions_df.user_id == max_ts.user_id) & (transactions_df.transaction_timestamp == max_ts.max_ts), how='inner' ).select( transactions_df.user_id, transactions_df.transaction_timestamp, ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 17 transactions_df.transaction_amount ) rank() vs row_number() vs dense_rank() Function Tie Handling Returns single row Use When per user? row_number() No ties — arbitrary order for Always exactly 1 Need strictly 1 ties row per group rank() Ties get same rank, gap May return >1 if Aware of ties, after timestamp tie want them included dense_rank() Ties get same rank, no gap May return >1 if Want timestamp tie consecutive rank numbers Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'I use a Window function with row_number() partitioned by user_id and ordered by transaction_timestamp descending. row_number() guarantees exactly one row per user even if two transactions have the same timestamp\n• Then I filter where row_num = 1 and drop the helper column.' Quick Reference — One-Line Answers Topic One-Line Answer Schedule Trigger Runs pipeline on fixed time — daily, hourly, weekly Tumbling Window Trigger Fixed non-overlapping windows — retryable, perfect for incremental loads Storage Event Trigger Fires when a file lands in ADLS Gen2 / Blob — event-driven On-Prem Migration SHIR -> ADF -> ADLS Gen2 (staging) -> Transform -> Azure SQL + watermark control table File Routing by Suffix Get Metadata + ForEach + dynamic sink table name using @replace(last(split(name,'_')),'.csv','') CSV-Driven Routing Lookup (read CSV) + ForEach + dynamic Copy Activity using @item().Country_code Star Schema Fact + flat denormalized dims — fewer JOINs, faster queries, best for BI Snowflake Schema Fact + normalized sub-dims — more JOINs, less storage, more data integrity Fact Table Measurable events (sale_amount, quantity) + FK to dimensions — very large ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 18 Dimension Table Descriptive context (name, city, category) — smaller, used for grouping/filtering Top 10 Customers groupBy('customer_id').agg(sum).orderBy(desc).limit(10) Fill Null + Salary Grade fillna({'salary':50000}) then withColumn('grade', when().when().otherwise()) Most Recent Transaction Window(partitionBy, orderBy desc) + row_number() + filter(row_num==1) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 19","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to implement Slowly changing type1","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"00:00.0\").show() -- it will add the Time stamp","type":"concept","a":"• spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",versionAsof=1).show() – if version is 0, it will execute the first table(df) if version is 1, it will execute the updated table ----In sql------- from delta.tables import DeltaTable -- (it will convert the file to Table) spark.sql(\"CREATE TABLE dbtablenew USING DELTA LOCATION '/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta'\") -- (it will create a new table ) %sql select * from dbtablenew %sql -- it will update and insert when mergering the files MERGE INTO dbtablenew USING dbtable ON dbtablenew.eno = dbtable.eno WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET dbtablenew.data = dbtable.data WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (eno, ename, sal) VALUES (eno, ename, sal) Page | 21 =========DATA WAREHOUSE CONCEPTS============ ➢ Data Ware house it is used for the ETL because fro analysis purpose. ➢ We want to design a OLTP system , we should a have modeling is E-R Modeling ( Entity Relational Modeling) and have a Primary key and Foreign Key. ➢ Entity relational is nothing but a tables like customer tables, location tables, category, sub category and making a relational between two tables. ➢ It is an real time object and this tables we can call as Master tables. ➢ There is a Transaction table(sales tables, etc) in that we can see the information but we want use a joins. ➢ OLTP concept it is for any update or insert should be a normalized because there is tables in a Small and performance is less. ➢ In that OLTP want get a information is difficult because there is a different tables like customer table and Details customer table . ➢ We want to implement the business intelligence, so we have to apply to data because it is not a format data\n• Master Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Primary Keys Product ID Product name 1 Honda Bike 2 Tvs Bike Customer ID Customer Name 1 Rama 2 Siva Transaction Table: Product ID Customer ID Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ By using a ETL to loading the data OLTP system to another system, so we called a Data Ware Housing or OLAP. ➢ By Designing a OLAP we have modeling is Dimensional and tables we can called as Dimensional Tables( Dimproduct, dimcustomer, etc) in that we have a Bussiness key. ➢ It will give the information from table will get a combination of Dimcustomer and Dim detalis customer. ➢ In this dimensional it will check the is existing or not, if not existing it will update or insert. ➢ In this Dimensional table will have a Surrogate key, while inserting the data automatically it will give a Unique number and easily we can identify the data. ➢ There is Fact Table it is a connection of all dimensions tables. ➢ We can merge and also we can add a Values like Quantity, amount etc\n• Dimensional Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Business Keys and Pkey and Ckey are called a Surrogate key\n• Page | 22 PKey Product ID Product name 1 1 Honda Bike 2 2 Tvs Bike CKey Customer ID Customer Name 1 1 Rama 2 2 Siva Fact Table: Pkey Ckey Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ In this Dimensional there is two schema 1\n• Star Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snow Flake Schema","type":"concept","a":"S.NO Star Schema Snowflake Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, The fact tables and While in snowflake schema, The fact tables,","type":"concept","a":"the dimension tables are contained. dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema is a top-down model. While it is a bottom-up model.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema uses more space. While it uses less space.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It takes less time for the execution While it takes more time than star schema for the","type":"concept","a":"of queries. execution of queries.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, Normalization is While in this, Both normalization and","type":"concept","a":"not used. denormalization are used.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"The query complexity of star While the query complexity of snowflake schema is","type":"concept","a":"schema is low. higher than star schema.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It has high data redundancy. While it has low data redundancy.","type":"concept","a":"Page | 23 Star Schema Diagram: Snow Flake Diagram: Page | 24 ===================Azure Questions===============================","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Round Robin: It will dis tribute data randomly row by row on each node. Less volume","type":"concept","a":"of data tables , dimension tables(customer, product) Disadvantage is data need to sorted at the time of combining from all nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Hash: It will distributed based on column similar values like same kind of product","type":"concept","a":"• ID will share one node\n• High volume data tables , Fact tables(Sales table) Advantage is no need of sorting while combining of data from nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is dimension modelling?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Dimensional Data Modelling is one of the data modelling techniques used in data warehouse design\n• To improve the data retrieval\n• Dimensional modeling involves the use of fact and dimension tables to maintain a record of historical data in data warehouses","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is star schema and snowflake schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A.In star schema, The fact tables and the dimension tables are contained\n• In this schema fewer foreign- key join is used\n• This schema forms a star with fact table and dimension tables\n• In snowflake schema, The fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained\n• This schema forms a snowflake with fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub- dimension tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is surrogate key?","type":"concept","a":"A.Surrogate key also called a synthetic primary key, is generated when a new record is inserted into a table automatically by a database that can be declared as the primary key of that table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"A.Fact means measures and dimension means description related to facts.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Slowly changing dimension: Changing over a period of time.is nothing but","type":"concept","a":"address These are three types Type1: need to override address, Type2: store all address Type3: need to store only current and previous address only or current previous and before one","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed dimension: A dimension table which can be used multiple fact tables is","type":"concept","a":"nothing but conformed dimension.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Degenerate dimension: Fact table contain dimension table information that is","type":"concept","a":"called as degenerate dimension table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Junk Dimension: If a model has to many small dimension, we can take all this model","type":"concept","a":"in one dimension table is nothing but junk dimensions Ex: ‘True or False’, ‘Yes or No’, ‘Male and Female’, ’Single or married’","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Role-Plying dimension: Fact table is more than two times relationship with dimension","type":"concept","a":"table is nothing but Role-Play dimension","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Static dimension: Something which never change like gender is nothing but static dimension","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is different kind of facts?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Additive facts: All data in the fact can be aggregate, we can apply all aggregate","type":"concept","a":"functions on data example sales.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Semi additive facts: we cannot apply all aggregate function but you will able to apply","type":"concept","a":"some aggregate functions example current account balance.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Non additive facts: we cannot apply any aggregate function like percentage tables","type":"concept","a":"Page | 30","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of fact tables in DWH?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Fact less fact table: It does not contain any measure in it contain only foreign","type":"concept","a":"from dimension table","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snapshot fact table: Store the data in relation to date or time","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Centipede fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What data is moved to Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Only analy(cid:415)cs-ready datasets:  Fact tables  Dimension tables  Aggregated tables  Repor(cid:415)ng datasets Opera(cid:415)onal tables remain in SQL Server.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Implement SCD Type 2","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Fact and Dimension","type":"concept","a":"Dimension:  Customer  Product  Employee Fact:  Sales  Orders  Transac(cid:415)ons SCD Type 2 is generally applied to dimension tables.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"About SCD (Slowly Changing Dimensions)","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) is a data warehousing technique for managing changes to dimension data over time\n• Different types handle historical tracking differently: SCD Type Strategy Keeps History? Storage Impact Type 0 Never update — static dimension No Minimal Type 1 Overwrite — replace old value with No Minimal new Type 2 Add new row — expire old, insert Yes (full history) Grows over time new with dates Type 3 Add new column — current + Partial (1 version Moderate previous value only back) Type 4 History table — current in main, Yes Moderate history in separate table Type 6 Hybrid: Type 1+2+3 combined Yes + current flag Larger SCD Type 2 is most commonly asked in interviews: • A new row is added for each change to a dimension record • The old row is 'expired' by setting end_date and is_current = 0 • The new row gets start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1 • The natural key (e.g., customer_id) remains the same; a surrogate key identifies each version Page 5 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me about yourself and your skill-set?","type":"concept","a":"• I am a Data Engineer with experience in building and optimizing data pipelines, ETL/ELT workflows, and cloud- based data platforms\n• My core skill-set includes: • Big Data Technologies: Apache Spark (PySpark), Databricks, Delta Lake • Cloud Platforms: Azure, AWS, or GCP — cloud storage, compute, and managed services • Data Warehousing: Designing star/snowflake schemas, building fact and dimension tables • Programming: Python, SQL, Scala (basic) • Data Ingestion: Auto Loader, Kafka, Azure Data Factory, or AWS Glue • Orchestration: Apache Airflow, Databricks Workflows, Azure Data Factory pipelines • Version Control: Git, CI/CD pipelines for data • Data Governance: Implementing SCD-1, SCD-2, data quality checks, schema evolution I have worked on end-to-end data platform projects including migrations, real-time ingestion, and building curated analytical layers for BI consumption","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 2 (Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2) tracks the full history of changes to a dimension record by creating a new row for each change rather than overwriting the existing record\n• Extra Columns Required for SCD-2: • start_date / effective_from: Date when the record became active. • end_date / effective_to: Date when the record was superseded (NULL for current record). • is_current / is_active: Boolean flag — TRUE for the latest active record. • (Optional) surrogate_key: A system-generated unique key for each row version\n• How SCD-2 Works: • When a record changes, the existing row is 'closed' by setting end_date = today and is_current = False. • A new row is inserted with the updated values, start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = True. • Historical queries can use the date range to retrieve the state of a record at any point in time","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 implementation using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) Type 2 is used to maintain the complete history of changes in dimension data\n• Instead of updating an existing record, we expire the old record and insert a new one whenever a tracked attribute changes\n• This allows us to preserve historical data for reporting and auditing Steps","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain all the SCD types and difference between them","type":"concept","a":"SCD Types & Differences Type Behavior History Use Case SCD 0 No change allowed No Sta(cid:415)c data SCD 1 Overwrite old data No history Fixing errors SCD 2 New row for changes Full history Tracking changes SCD 3 Adds new column for previous value Limited history Few a(cid:425)ributes only (cid:44934) Example:  Salary change: o SCD1 → overwrite old salary o SCD2 → new row inserted o SCD3 → old salary stored in another column","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Star & Snowflake schema","type":"concept","a":"Star vs Snowflake Schema Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Structure Denormalized Normalized Joins Fewer More Performance Faster Slower Storage More space Op(cid:415)mized Complexity Simple Complex Example:  Star → Fact table + dimension tables  Snowflake → dimension tables further split","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Do you know data modeling? How do you rate yourself out of 5?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes, I have worked with data modeling while designing data warehouse solu(cid:415)ons\n• I'm comfortable with fact and dimension tables, Star Schema, Snowflake Schema, normaliza(cid:415)on, and denormaliza(cid:415)on\n• I would rate myself 4 out of 5 because I have prac(cid:415)cal experience, while I'm s(cid:415)ll expanding my knowledge in advanced modeling techniques like Data Vault.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"We have transac(cid:415)onal data and analy(cid:415)cal data. Which schema do you use?","type":"concept","a":"• \"For transac(cid:415)onal systems (OLTP), I use a normalized schema (3NF) because it minimizes redundancy and maintains data integrity for frequent inserts, updates, and deletes\n• For analy(cid:415)cal systems (OLAP), I generally use a Star Schema because it is op(cid:415)mized for repor(cid:415)ng, aggrega(cid:415)on, and fast query performance.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Star Schema and Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension tables are denormalized Dimension tables are normalized Fewer joins More joins Be(cid:425)er query performance Slightly slower due to addi(cid:415)onal joins Easier to understand More complex More storage required Less storage required Preferred for most data warehouses Used when dimensions have significant redundancy Example: Star Schema Product | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Store Snowflake Schema Product | Product Category | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Geography | Country","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why do you choose Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal data?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I prefer a Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal workloads because repor(cid:415)ng queries usually aggregate millions of records\n• Since the dimension tables are denormalized, fewer joins are required, which improves query performance\n• It's also easier for BI tools like Power BI and Tableau to consume.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"When would you use Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I use a Snowflake Schema when dimension tables contain a lot of redundant data or have natural hierarchical rela(cid:415)onships, such as Country → State → City or Category → Subcategory → Product\n• Normalizing these dimensions reduces redundancy and makes updates easier, although it requires more joins.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What are Dimension and Fact Tables? Explain in detail.","type":"concept","a":"• Fact Table Stores measurable business events\n• Examples:  Sales Amount  Quan(cid:415)ty Sold  Revenue  Profit Contains:  Numeric measures  Foreign keys to dimension tables Example: ProductKey CustomerKey DateKey SalesAmount 101 201 20240101 500 Dimension Table Stores descrip(cid:415)ve informa(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  Customer  Product  Employee  Date  Store Example: ProductKey ProductName Category 101 Laptop Electronics Rela(cid:415)onship Customer Dimension | Product Dimension -- Sales Fact -- Date Dimension | Store Dimension Difference Fact Table Dimension Table Stores business measures Stores descrip(cid:415)ve a(cid:425)ributes Very large Smaller Contains foreign keys Contains primary keys Used for calcula(cid:415)ons Used for filtering and grouping","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Large Fact and Dimension Tables","type":"concept","a":"If both tables are large:  Prefer Sort Merge Join.  Ensure par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning on the join key.  Avoid broadcas(cid:415)ng either table.  Enable AQE to op(cid:415)mize joins dynamically.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Model? Which One Did You Use? (Star Schema)","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Model defines the structure, relationships, and rules for organizing data\n• Common models: Star Schema (Most Common in DW): One central Fact table surrounded by Dimension tables\n• No normalization of dimensions. • Fact Table: Contains measurable, quantitative data (sales_amount, quantity) • Dimension Tables: Descriptive attributes (customer, product, date, location) • Pros: Simple queries, fast aggregations, easy for BI tools • Cons: Data redundancy in dimensions Snowflake Schema: • Extension of star schema where dimensions are normalized into sub-dimensions • Pros: Less redundancy\n• Cons: More joins, slower queries Galaxy/Fact Constellation Schema: • Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables Example Star Schema: Fact_Sales (sale_id, date_key, product_key, customer_key, amount, qty) Dim_Date (date_key, date, month, quarter, year) Dim_Product(product_key, name, category, brand) Dim_Customer(customer_key, name, city, country)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"governance","name":"Data Governance & Security","icon":"🔐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Unity Catalog and what problem does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance layer for data and AI assets across all workspaces in an account.\n• Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns, rows).\n• 3-level namespace: catalog.schema.table.\n• Built-in data lineage (column-level), audit logs, and Delta Sharing.\n• External locations + storage credentials for governed cloud storage access.\n• Row-level security (row filters) and column masking.\nSolves per-workspace (Hive metastore) silos with account-level governance and fine-grained security."},{"q":"Explain the Unity Catalog object hierarchy.","type":"concept","a":"Metastore (top-level, one per region) → Catalog → Schema (database) → objects (tables, views, volumes, functions, models).\n• Metastore stores metadata + permissions for all workspaces attached to it.\n• Permissions flow downward (grant at a higher level, inherits lower).\n• Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders.\nRelated objects: storage credentials, external locations, shares."},{"q":"Managed vs external tables AND managed vs external volumes in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — UC manages data lifecycle/location; DROP deletes metadata AND data.\n• External table — references a location you own (external location); DROP removes only metadata, files remain.\n• Managed volume — UC-managed storage for non-tabular files (images, PDFs, CSV); deleted with the volume.\n• External volume — references existing cloud storage you manage (via external location); files remain after dropping the volume.\nVolumes are like managed/external tables but for files."},{"q":"What are storage credentials and external locations in Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage credential — a UC securable that holds cloud auth (managed identity / service principal / access connector) to access storage; no secrets in code.\n• External location — combines a cloud storage URL (abfss://...) with a storage credential, granting governed access to that path.\n• Access Connector — an Azure resource providing managed-identity access from Databricks to ADLS.\nTogether they let UC govern who can read/write specific storage paths."},{"q":"How do you implement row-level and column-level security in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Column-level: dynamic column masks — a UDF masks sensitive values based on IS_ACCOUNT_GROUP_MEMBER / current_user(): ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_fn.\n• Row-level: row filter functions — ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER filter_fn ON (region); returns only rows the user is allowed to see.\n• Unity Catalog enforces them centrally; also table/column GRANT/REVOKE.\nLegacy: table ACLs / AAD credential passthrough (being replaced by UC)."},{"q":"Difference between Unity Catalog and Hive Metastore?","type":"concept","a":"• Scope: Hive = per-workspace; UC = account-level across all workspaces.\n• Namespace: Hive 2-level (db.table); UC 3-level (catalog.schema.table).\n• Governance: Hive basic ACLs, no column/row security; UC fine-grained (row filters, column masks), lineage, audit logs.\n• Sharing: Hive none; UC Delta Sharing built-in.\n• UC is the current standard; Hive is legacy/being replaced."},{"q":"What is Delta Sharing?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Sharing is an open protocol to securely share data (and AI assets) across organizations/workspaces/business units WITHOUT copying data.\n• A provider shares a subset of tables/volumes; recipients read via the share (Databricks-to-Databricks or open recipients like Power BI/pandas).\n• Governance and audit are maintained; recipients only see what's shared.\nScenario: 3 business units with separate workspaces share data through Delta Sharing instead of duplicating into each workspace."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Three business units have separate workspaces. How do you share data between them?","type":"scenario","a":"Option A — Unity Catalog + Delta Sharing: publish selected tables as a share from the provider workspace; grant recipients access; they read without copying. Centralized governance/audit.\nOption B — one UC metastore per region attached to all workspaces with catalogs per BU and GRANTs (cross-workspace access within the same account).\nChoose Delta Sharing for cross-org/cross-cloud or strict isolation; UC catalogs when all workspaces share an account/metastore."},{"q":"How do you secure access to ADLS from Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Authentication: Service Principal (app registration) + Storage Blob Data Contributor, or Managed Identity / Access Connector (no secrets), or account key (avoid).\n• Secrets: store SPN keys in Azure Key Vault + Databricks secret scope; read via dbutils.secrets.\n• RBAC: Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor/Owner.\n• POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 HNS): file/folder-level rwx with masks + defaults.\n• Unity Catalog: external locations + storage credentials + GRANTs (preferred).\n• Network: private endpoints/service endpoints/firewall.\n• Encryption: SSE at rest (Microsoft-managed or CMK), TLS in transit.\n• Avoid mounting with keys; prefer UC or OAuth."},{"q":"What are service principals, managed identities, and secrets in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Service principal — a non-human identity (app registration) used by pipelines/CI-CD for automated auth.\n• Managed identity — Azure-managed identity so resources authenticate without storing credentials (via Access Connector to ADLS).\n• Secrets — Databricks secret scopes (backed by Azure Key Vault) store passwords/tokens/keys; accessed with dbutils.secrets.get().\nBest practice: least privilege, no secrets in code, rotate regularly."},{"q":"How do you audit who accessed what data in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog audit logs capture user actions (logins, cluster/job runs, permission changes, data access) and column-level lineage.\n• Enable diagnostic logging to a storage account / Log Analytics; query with KQL.\n• Lineage shows how data flows between tables/columns/notebooks/jobs.\n• Combine with Azure Monitor for alerting on suspicious access."},{"q":"What is the difference between RBAC and ACL in the lakehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — permissions granted to roles/principals at the control/service level (e.g., Azure roles on storage, workspace admin roles).\n• ACLs (Access Control Lists) — fine-grained permissions on specific objects (tables, columns, files/folders via POSIX ACLs).\nBoth are used together: RBAC for broad access, UC ACLs/GRANTs for fine-grained data-level control."},{"q":"What is least-privilege design and how do you apply it?","type":"concept","a":"Grant users/apps only the minimum permissions needed.\n• Use groups (not individual users) and grant at the highest sensible level.\n• Use read-only roles where possible; scope service principals to specific storage/workspaces.\n• Apply column masks/row filters for sensitive data.\n• Separate prod/dev credentials; audit and rotate regularly."},{"q":"What are components of Azure data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ User --A unique individual who has access to the system. ➢ Group --- A collection of users ➢ Access control list (ACL) ➢ Notebook ➢ Dashboard ➢ Library ➢ Repo ➢ Experiment","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It supports more security projects Its security is not as good as Map Reduce","type":"concept","a":"and continuously working on its security issues.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of security of Blob or storage account?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is RBAC?","type":"concept","a":"Role back access control Click on Access control (IAM) then click on add role assignment","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Provide Security Over ADLS Storage?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"RBAC — Role-Based Access Control","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Blob Data Reader — read-only access • Storage Blob Data Contributor — read + write access • Storage Blob Data Owner — full control including ACLs","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"POSIX ACLs (ADLS Gen2 hierarchical namespace)","type":"concept","a":"• File/folder level ACLs — grant rwx permissions at directory level • Masks and default ACLs for inheritance","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"• Centralized governance — define External Locations and Storage Credentials • Fine-grained access: GRANT SELECT ON TABLE to specific users or groups • Row-level and column-level security through Delta Lake row filters and column masks","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog vs Hive Metastore","type":"concept","a":"• Feature Hive Metastore Unity Catalog Page 19 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Scope Workspace-level (per ADB Account-level (across all workspaces) workspace) Governance Basic — no column/row security Fine-grained: table, column, row-level Namespace 2-level: database.table 3-level: catalog.schema.table Data lineage Not built-in Built-in — column-level lineage tracking Access control Workspace-level ACLs Centralized — groups, GRANT/REVOKE Audit logs Limited Full — who accessed what and when External tables Supported Supported via External Locations Delta Sharing Not supported Built-in Delta Sharing support Migration path Legacy — being replaced by UC Current standard — preferred Multiple workspaces Each workspace has own One Unity Catalog for all workspaces metastore -- Hive Metastore (2-level namespace) USE DATABASE my_database;\n• SELECT * FROM my_table; -- Unity Catalog (3-level namespace) SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.my_table; -- Grant in Unity Catalog GRANT SELECT ON TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table TO `data_analysts`;\n• GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG my_catalog TO `data_engineers`;\n• Unity Catalog is the recommended governance solution for all new Databricks deployments\n• It provides centralized data access control, lineage, and auditing across the entire organization rather than per- workspace","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you handle PII data in your project?","type":"concept","a":"Handling PII data  Masking / tokeniza(cid:415)on  Encryp(cid:415)on (at rest + transit)  Unity Catalog access control  Row-level security  Restricted columns in Silver/Gold layer","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Standard, Premium, and Enterprise in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Standard — Core Apache Spark, notebooks, jobs, basic collaboration. No RBAC or advanced security. • Premium — Adds Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), Databricks SQL (SQL Warehouses), audit logs, token management, SSO. Suitable for enterprise data teams. • Enterprise — Full Premium features plus compliance certifications (HIPAA, FedRAMP), enhanced security controls, network isolation (Private Link), and dedicated support SLAs. SQL Warehouse (SQL Data Warehouse in Databricks)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Managed Volume vs External Volume","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog introduces Volumes for storing non-tabular files\n• Managed Volume External Volume Databricks manages storage Customer manages storage Files stored in managed loca(cid:415)on Files stored in external cloud storage Deleted with the volume Files remain a(cid:332)er dropping the volume Simpler management Greater flexibility Think of it like managed vs external tables, but for files rather than tables\n• Examples: Managed Volume: Catalog ↓ Schema ↓ Managed Volume ↓ Images PDFs CSV External Volume: ADLS ↓ External Loca(cid:415)on ↓ Unity Catalog Volume","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Are you familiar with Unity Catalog architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Basic architecture: Metastore ↓ Catalog ↓ Schema ↓ Tables Views Volumes Func(cid:415)ons Models Permissions flow downward\n• Example: Metastore ↓ Sales Catalog ↓ Finance Schema ↓ Orders Table Main components:  Metastore  Catalog  Schema  Tables  Views  Volumes  External Loca(cid:415)ons  Storage Creden(cid:415)als Benefits:  Centralized governance  Fine-grained access control  Data lineage  Audi(cid:415)ng  Cross-workspace sharing (when a(cid:425)ached to the same metastore)","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is Predicate Pushdown in Parquet?","type":"concept","a":"• Predicate pushdown means the storage layer filters data before Spark reads it\n• Example: df = spark.read.parquet(\"sales\") df.filter(\"country='US'\") Spark reads only row groups that contain country='US'\n• Benefits:  Less disk I/O  Faster execu(cid:415)on  Lower memory usage Parquet stores:  Min value  Max value  Sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs Spark uses these sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs to skip unnecessary row groups\n• RBAC vs ACL RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) Permissions are assigned to roles\n• Example: Admin Read Write Delete User Read User gets permissions by role\n• Advantages:  Easy management  Enterprise friendly ACL (Access Control List) Permissions are assigned directly to each resource\n• Example: File A John → Read Alice → Write Bob → No Access Useful for object-level permissions\n• Difference RBAC ACL Role based Resource based Easier to manage More granular Scalable Complex with many users Enterprise systems File/object permissions","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Unity Catalog?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance solu(cid:415)on for managing data and AI assets across mul(cid:415)ple workspaces\n• It provides centralized access control, data discovery, audi(cid:415)ng, and lineage for tables, views, files, machine learning models, and other data assets.\" Key Features  Centralized data governance  Fine-grained access control (catalog, schema, table, column, row)  Data lineage  Data discovery  Audit logs  Cross-workspace data sharing  Integra(cid:415)on with cloud storage like ADLS, S3, and GCS Unity Catalog hierarchy Catalog │ Schema │ Table/View Example: Catalog : Sales Schema : Bronze Table : Orders Access example: SELECT * FROM Sales.Bronze.Orders;","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why is Unity Catalog used?","type":"concept","a":"\"Instead of managing permissions separately in each workspace, Unity Catalog provides a single place to manage security, governance, and metadata across the organiza(cid:415)on.\" Difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table (DLT) Delta Table Delta Live Table Storage format based on Delta Lake Framework for building ETL pipelines Stores data Creates and manages data pipelines Manually created Automa(cid:415)cally managed You write SQL or Spark code You define transforma(cid:415)ons declara(cid:415)vely Manual scheduling Built-in orchestra(cid:415)on No built-in data quality Supports data quality expecta(cid:415)ons Manual monitoring Built-in monitoring and lineage","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Unity Catalog? Have you implemented it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Unity Catalog is Databricks' centralized governance solu(cid:415)on for managing data, AI assets, and permissions across workspaces\n• It provides:  Centralized access control.  Fine-grained permissions at catalog, schema, table, and column levels.  Data lineage to track data movement.  Audit logs for compliance.  Secure sharing through Delta Sharing\n• In my project, I worked with Unity Catalog by storing managed Delta tables in catalogs, assigning role-based permissions to different teams, and ensuring secure access to data.\" (If you haven't implemented it, say: \"I understand its architecture and concepts, and I have worked in environments where it was configured by the pla(cid:414)orm team.\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"AI tools / Unity Catalog","type":"concept","a":" AI tools: ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot  Unity Catalog: o Centralized governance layer in Databricks o Manages access control for data, tables, and models o Enables data lineage + security","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Unity Catalog","type":"concept","a":"• Unity Catalog is Databricks' unified governance solution for data and AI assets across all workspaces\n• Three-level namespace: catalog.schema.table -- Example: SELECT * FROM prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders_table Key Features: • Centralized access control (ACLs on catalogs, schemas, tables, columns) • Data lineage: Tracks data flow from source to destination • Audit logs: Who accessed what data and when • External locations: Unified access to cloud storage (ADLS, S3) • Delta Sharing: Securely share data across organizations • Column-level & Row-level security -- Grant permissions in Unity Catalog GRANT SELECT ON TABLE prod_catalog.sales_schema.orders TO `data_analyst@company.com`;\n• GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA prod_catalog.sales_schema TO `data_team`; -- Create external table via Unity Catalog CREATE TABLE prod_catalog.sales_schema.ext_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/orders';\n• Q13\n• Cache() cache() stores a DataFrame in memory (or memory+disk) so it doesn't need to be recomputed for subsequent actions. # cache() = persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) df_cached = df.cache() # Lazy - cached on first action df_cached.count() # Triggers caching # persist() allows specifying storage level from pyspark import StorageLevel df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) # Fastest, needs RAM df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) # Spills to disk if needed df.persist(StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY) # Saves RAM, slower df.unpersist() # Free cached data when done When to use cache(): • DataFrame is used multiple times in the same job • Iterative algorithms (ML training loops) • After expensive transformations (joins, aggregations)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"sql","name":"SQL & Database Design","icon":"🗃️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows BEFORE grouping/aggregation; cannot use aggregate functions.\n• HAVING filters groups AFTER GROUP BY; can use aggregates.\n• A query can have both: WHERE to reduce rows, then GROUP BY, then HAVING to filter groups.\nExample: SELECT emp, SUM(sal) FROM t WHERE dept='IT' GROUP BY emp HAVING SUM(sal) > 5000."},{"q":"Explain INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, CROSS, LEFT SEMI, and LEFT ANTI joins.","type":"concept","a":"• INNER — only matching rows from both tables.\n• LEFT — all left rows + matches; unmatched right = NULL.\n• RIGHT — all right rows + matches; unmatched left = NULL.\n• FULL OUTER — all rows from both; NULLs where no match.\n• CROSS — cartesian product (every combination).\n• LEFT SEMI — left rows with a match in right (no right columns).\n• LEFT ANTI — left rows with NO match in right.\nLEFT JOIN A,B = RIGHT JOIN B,A."},{"q":"CODING: Total revenue per month.","type":"coding","a":"Group by the formatted month and sum amounts.","code":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM') AS year_month,\n       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM')\nORDER BY year_month;"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in both January AND February 2025.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and count distinct months; keep those with 2. INTERSECT is an alternative.","code":"SELECT customer_id\nFROM orders\nWHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28'\n  AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1,2)\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2;\n\n-- Alternative\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01'\nINTERSECT\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in 3 consecutive months.","type":"coding","a":"Use LAG to look back two months and compare the dates.","code":"WITH m AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) AS mth\n  FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)\n),\nl AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, mth,\n         LAG(mth,1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev1,\n         LAG(mth,2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev2\n  FROM m\n)\nSELECT DISTINCT customer_id\nFROM l\nWHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2,1) = prev1 AND ADD_MONTHS(prev1,1) = mth;"},{"q":"CODING: Total order amount per customer (join + aggregation).","type":"coding","a":"Inner join customers to orders and sum.","code":"SELECT c.CustomerID, c.Name, SUM(o.Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Customer c\nINNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID\nGROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.Name;"},{"q":"CODING: Delete duplicate records keeping one row.","type":"coding","a":"Assign row numbers per duplicate key and delete rn>1 (or rebuild the table).","code":"WITH cte AS (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer\n)\nDELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n\n-- Delta-friendly rebuild\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n     PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer) WHERE rn = 1;"},{"q":"How would you optimize a slow SQL query?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read the execution plan (EXPLAIN) to find the bottleneck (scans, shuffles, spills).\n• Filter as early as possible; avoid SELECT * (select needed columns only).\n• Ensure joins/filters use indexed or partition columns.\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE (e.g., YEAR(date)=2025) — use range predicates for partition pruning.\n• Use proper join types and broadcast hints for small tables.\n• Replace correlated subqueries with joins; use CTEs to avoid repeated work.\n• Update statistics; partition large tables; avoid unnecessary DISTINCT.\n• Compare plans/times before and after."},{"q":"How does partition pruning work and how do you enable it?","type":"concept","a":"Partition pruning means the engine reads only the partitions matching the filter, skipping the rest.\n• Filter directly on the partition column with range/equality predicates (WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31').\n• Avoid wrapping the partition column in functions (WHERE YEAR(order_date)=2025 disables pruning).\n• In Spark/Delta, this avoids reading irrelevant files/folders → big speedup."},{"q":"What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?","type":"concept","a":"• UNION — removes duplicate rows (implies a distinct/sort → more expensive).\n• UNION ALL — keeps all rows including duplicates (faster).\nUse UNION ALL by default and de-duplicate only when needed."},{"q":"What is a CTE and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"A Common Table Expression (WITH clause) is a named temporary result set that makes complex queries readable and avoids repeating subqueries. It's not materialized by default in many engines (each reference may re-execute), but improves clarity and can be chained. Use for multi-step logic: dedupe → join → aggregate."},{"q":"CODING: Find the manager of each employee using a self join (SQL).","type":"coding","a":"Self join employee to itself; LEFT join so top-level employees (null manager) are kept.","code":"SELECT e.empid AS employee_id, e.empname AS employee_name,\n       e.mgrid AS manager_id, m.empname AS manager_name\nFROM employee e\nLEFT JOIN employee m ON e.mgrid = m.empid;"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions and how do they differ from window functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Aggregates (SUM/COUNT/AVG/MIN/MAX) collapse multiple rows into one row per group — you lose individual rows.\n• Window functions compute across a set of rows but KEEP every row (add a computed column).\nAggregate example: SUM(sal) per dept. Window example: SUM(sal) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) alongside each row."},{"q":"How do you implement SCD Type 1 and Type 2 in SQL?","type":"coding","a":"Type 1 overwrites; Type 2 expires the old row and inserts a new version with effective dates + current flag.","code":"-- SCD Type 1 (overwrite)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.name=s.name, t.email=s.email, t.updated_at=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id,name,email,updated_at) VALUES (s.customer_id,s.name,s.email,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n\n-- SCD Type 2 (expire + insert)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s\nON t.customer_id = s.customer_id AND t.is_current = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (t.name<>s.name OR t.city<>s.city) THEN\n  UPDATE SET t.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, t.is_current = 0;\n\nINSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id,name,city,start_date,end_date,is_current)\nSELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1\nFROM staging s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id=s.customer_id AND d.is_current=1);"},{"q":"How would you find the Nth highest salary?","type":"concept","a":"Use DENSE_RANK (handles ties) or a correlated subquery.\n• Window: SELECT * FROM (SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) rk FROM emp) WHERE rk = N.\n• Subquery: SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp) [repeat for Nth].\nDENSE_RANK is cleaner and scales to any N."},{"q":"Window Func(cid:415)ons (ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK)","type":"concept","a":"• Window func(cid:415)ons perform calcula(cid:415)ons across related rows without collapsing the data\n• Example data: Employee Dept Salary A IT 10000 B IT 9000 C IT 9000 D IT 8000 ROW_NUMBER() Assigns a unique number to each row\n• Salary Row_Number 10000 1 9000 2 9000 3 8000 4 from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import row_number window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"Dept\").orderBy(\"Salary\") df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(window)) RANK() Duplicate values receive the same rank, and the next rank is skipped\n• Salary Rank 10000 1 9000 2 9000 2 Salary Rank 8000 4 DENSE_RANK() Duplicate values receive the same rank, but there are no gaps\n• Salary Dense Rank 10000 1 9000 2 9000 2 8000 3","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Inner, Left & Outer Join — Worked Example","type":"concept","a":"Sample Tables Table A: Orders order_id customer_id amount 101 C1 500 102 C2 300 103 C3 800 104 C5 200 Table B: Customers customer_id customer_name C1 Alice C2 Bob C4 Charlie INNER JOIN Result INNER JOIN: A.customer_id = B.customer_id Returns ONLY rows where customer_id exists in BOTH tables. order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob Excluded: Order 103 (C3 not in Customers), Order 104 (C5 not in Customers), C4 Charlie (no orders) LEFT JOIN Result LEFT JOIN: All rows from Orders, match from Customers (NULL if none) order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob 103 | C3 | 800 | NULL ← C3 not in Customers 104 | C5 | 200 | NULL ← C5 not in Customers FULL OUTER JOIN Result FULL OUTER JOIN: All rows from BOTH tables order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob 103 | C3 | 800 | NULL ← C3 has no matching customer 104 | C5 | 200 | NULL ← C5 has no matching customer NULL | C4 | NULL | Charlie ← C4 has no orders","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"❌ What Breaks?","type":"concept","a":"• Source sends: { order_id, customer_id, amount, discount_code } ← new column added Your Delta table has: order_id, customer_id, amount Spark throws: AnalysisException: Column 'discount_code' not found in schema Solution 1: mergeSchema (Easiest) # In your writeStream, add mergeSchema option df_stream.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/orders/') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ # ← This is the fix! .outputMode('append') \\ .toTable('silver.orders') # mergeSchema automatically adds the new column 'discount_code' to # the Delta table\n• Old rows will have NULL for this new column\n• Solution 2: autoMerge (Spark Config) # Enable schema auto-merge globally spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled', 'true') # Now any write (streaming or batch) will automatically merge new columns Solution 3: Explicit Schema Evolution with Autoloader # With Autoloader, use schemaEvolutionMode df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/schema/') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') \\ # Options: 'addNewColumns' | 'rescue' | 'failOnNewColumns' | 'none' .load('/mnt/raw/orders/') # 'rescue': Puts unexpected columns into a _rescued_data JSON column # 'failOnNewColumns': Deliberately fail and alert when schema changes # 'addNewColumns': Automatically expand schema to include new columns 💡 Best Practice for Production Use 'failOnNewColumns' in strict environments to detect unexpected schema changes early\n• Use 'addNewColumns' or mergeSchema='true' in flexible pipelines where schema drift is expected\n• Always log schema changes and alert the data team when new columns appear\n• Schema Drift Schema drift refers to the phenomenon where the structure of your incoming data changes over time — new columns are added, columns are removed, data types change\n• This is one of the most common challenges in real data engineering projects\n• Types of Schema Drift Type Example & Impact New column added Source adds 'loyalty_points' column\n• Pipeline fails if not handled — breaks schema validation\n• Column removed Source stops sending 'middle_name'\n• Downstream queries on that column will return errors\n• Data type change 'order_amount' changes from INT to DECIMAL","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are Transformations used in Data Flows.","type":"concept","a":"A. Derived Column--Deriving new columns by writing expressions Lookup---Comparing two inputs finding matching non matching based on common column(left outer join) Alter row---categorizing the rows to perform upsert operations, which row needs to insert and update and delete Join---Joining of two inputs like inner, left, fullouter, customer Conditional Split---base don condition data will be spitted Ex: Male and female two outputs Filter: filtering of data by putting filter condition Assertions--Performing some data validations by specifying data rules Windows--to find duplicate or [performing rank, rownumber, running total Aggregate: performing aggregate and group by Union: appending of data into one output","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the Transformations used in Data Flows","type":"concept","a":"• DERIVED COLUMN: Deriving New Columns or adding new columns in Dataflow by writing of Expressions Ex: Firstname,LastName column,we can derive new column Full Name by Concat both firstname,lastname JOIN: If you want to perform all kind of join Inner join, Left outer Join, Right Outer Join..customer join in between two tables Ex:-Customer, Customer Address join both based on Location ID SELECT: Limiting Number of columns in Dataflow Ex: 10 columns from source, But want to load only 5 columns, Then use select transformation Limit only required columns FILTER: Applying filter on data coming from source Ex: Load only city= hyderabad customers into target Page | 4 CONDITIONAL SPLIT: I have one input customer data Male and female data and transgender data I want to load above customer data into 3 tables Male customer Female Customer Trans gender UNION: Appending data from multiple inputs into one output ex: Emphyd EmpBang combine all data from both data sources WINDOWS: If you want to apply Row_Number, Rank , Dense_Rank functionalities use this windows Ex: Finding duplicates using rownumber partition and order by and filtering using Filter Transformation AGGREGATE: Performing Aggregate operations includes Min , Max, Sum, Avg\n• Ex: Each gender wise yearly income, avg income\n• SORT: Just sorting order of data in acesending or descending based on column Ex :Sort data based on productid ASSERTION: Applying data validation rules on incoming data to identify unique, duplicates, condition met or not Ex: Phone number contains 10 digits or not LOOKUP: comparing two inputs based on common column and finding matched and non matched records(Left Outer Join) Ex: Comparing customer source data Destination Dimcustomer based on customerid ALTER ROW: categorizing records to perform upsert operations it includes insert, update, delete BRANCHING: It will take one input and giving multiple outputs of data customer data source table contains 10 rows, i want to load same data into multiple tables customer---dimcustomer customer---dimcustomerbackup customer---dimcustomercopy EXIST: Comparing two source inputs and verifying a record from first source input to second source input a custid in customer table exist or not exist in other table dimcustomer Page | 5","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Mapping Dataflows and Warngling Datflows","type":"concept","a":"• Mapping Data Flows: Use Mapping Data Flows to visually transform data without having to write any code\n• You can focus on the transformations and logic, while Azure Data Factory does the heavy lifting behind the scenes\n• It translates your transformations and logic to code that runs on scaled-out Azure Databricks clusters for maximum performance\n• A Mapping Data Flow can look something like this: The focus in this interface is on the flow\n• You can quickly identify your sources, transformations, branches, joins, and sinks\n• To see the actual data, you need to enable Data Flow Debug and preview the data per transformation\n• Wrangling Data Flows: Use Wrangling Data Flows to visually explore and prepare datasets using the Power Query Online mashup editor\n• You can focus on the modeling and logic, while Azure Data Factory does the heavy lifting behind the scenes\n• It translates the underlying M code to code that runs on a managed Spark environment for maximum performance\n• A Wrangling Data Flow can look something like this: The focus in this interface is on the data\n• You can quickly see what the final dataset will look like\n• To see the actual sources, transformations, and joins, you need to go through the list of Applied Steps","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Its scalability is good as you can add It is having low scalability as compared to","type":"concept","a":"up to n different nodes Map Reduce","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data Bricks Runtime Integration","type":"concept","a":"• Data bricks Runtime ML is a variant of Data bricks Runtime that adds multiple popular machine learning libraries, including Tensor Flow, Keras, PyTorch, and XGBoost\n• Photon\n• Photon is the Azure Data bricks native vectorized query engine that runs SQL workloads faster and reduces your total cost per workload\n• Page | 12","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"PAAS--- Platform as a service {Operating system, SQL server….}","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Elastic pool, what are different compute Tiers?","type":"concept","a":"Group of azure SQL data bases to share resources among all","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Distributions?","type":"concept","a":"• Application connect issue T-SQL commands a control node\n• The control node hosts the MPP Engine, which optimise queries for parallel processing and then passes operations to compute node to do their work in parallel\n• The compute nodes store all users data in Azure storage and run the parallel Queries Control Node: It will act lick human brain it will take request from application and passing to MPP MPP (Massive Parallel Processing): Splitting of Query into multiple smaller queries and execute queries parallel\n• Compute node: It is place where each query will execute\n• 0-60 compute nodes Page | 26 Distribution Method: Based on Distribution method created tables, data will be distributed across nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"One Database to Another Database","type":"concept","a":"create master key encryption by password='Eclasess@456' --Creating a New password create database scoped credential ploybase --Creating a New Database with identity='eclasessgrp', ---This is the Username and password from Source side secret='Eclasess@123' create external data source ploy -----Creating a New external Datasource with ( type=rdbms, location='eclasess1.database.windows.net', -----This is Server of the Source side database_name='source1', credential=ploybase) create external table empext -----Creating a new External tables ( id int, person varchar(100), salary float) with( data_source=ploy, schema_name='dbo', object_name='emp' ------The table from the source side ) select * from empext Ploybase from Blob storage to SQL Database create master key encryption by password='eclasess@123' --- Creating a New Password create database scoped credential ploybase ---Creating a New Database with identity='avinash', -----You can give a any name secret='XabXvCkBrzcio24wEMxhyHkj1ZX7pK19SfUe3/Ux1xj65tr8LKA+HFSCOurjaDgT0EE1mP KcyYlM+AStQwpk+Q==' --------it is of secrete of blob storage available in the ACCESS KEY create external data source ploy_source ---creating a external data source with ( type=hadoop, location='wasbs://eclasess456@eclasess123.blob.core.windows.net', -----(in this eclasess456 is a Container name and eclasess123 is a Blob storage name) credential=ploybase ) Page | 28 create external file format file1 -- creating a external format with( format_type=delimitedtext, format_options(field_terminator=',',first_row=2)) create external table product1 ( eno int, enmae varchar(10), epro varchar(10)) with( data_source=ploy_source, file_format=file1, location='/product/') select * from product1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is master and transaction tables?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Transaction table: This tables gets impacted with insert or update delete we perform business transactions Master table: This will be read and used during transactions, but this table will not get impacted for inset and update or delete\n• A transaction is an activity performed by entities (master tables) within the system\n• These activities are captured in transaction tables and usually, these transaction entries have foreign keys to master records\n• Transaction tables are designed to store events in the system\n• Page | 29","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between where and having clause?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ WHERE: Where clause is used to filter records. where clause cannot be used in aggregate function. where clause used to filter the records from table or used while joining more than one table only those records will be extracted who are satisfying the specified condition in WHERE clause\n• It can be used with select, update, delete statement. ➢ HAVING: Having clause is used to filter the records from the groups based on the given condition in the having clause those groups who will satisfy the given condition will appear in the final result having clause can only be used with select statement","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are distinct and top key word in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Distinct statement: It is used to return only distinct or different values inside a table, a column contains many duplicate values and sometime you only want to list the different values ➢ Top keyword: Advertisements\n• The SQL TOP clause is used to fetch a TOP N number or X percent records from a table\n• Note − All the databases do not support the TOP clause\n• For example MySQL supports the LIMIT clause to fetch limited number of records while Oracle uses the ROWNUM command to fetch a limited number of records","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is sql query order of writing","type":"concept","a":"• Six Operations to order of Writing: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, and ORDER BY\n• By using examples, we will explain the execution order of the six most common operations or pieces in an SQL query\n• Page | 31","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is order of execution of SQL query?","type":"concept","a":"• Six Operations to Order: to order of executing: FROM,WHERE,GROUPBY,HAVING,SELECT,ORDERBY By using examples, we will explain the execution order of the six most common operations or pieces in an SQL query\n• Because the database executes query components in a specific order, it's helpful for the developer to know this order","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"• COUNT Function: COUNT function is used to Count the number of rows in a database table. \n• SUM Function: Sum function is used to calculate the sum of all selected columns. \n• AVG Function: The AVG function is used to calculate the average value of the numeric type. ","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are constrains available in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server contains the following 6 types of constraints:","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is difference between the primary key and unique key?","type":"concept","a":"• Both Primary key and Unique Key are used to uniquely define of a row in a table\n• Primary Key creates a clustered index of the column whereas a Unique creates an unclustered index of the column \n• Primary Key doesn't allow NULL value, however a Unique Key does allow one NULL value","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is view and materilized view?","type":"concept","a":"• A view uses a query to pull data from the underlying tables\n• A materialized view is a table on disk that contains the result set of a query\n• Materialized views are primarily used to increase application performance when it isn't feasible or desirable to use a standard view with indexes applied to it","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are difference between table variable and temp tables, tables and cte?","type":"concept","a":"• A. . \n• This biggest difference is that a CTE can only be used in the current query scope whereas a temporary table or table variable can exist for the entire duration of the session allowing you to perform many different DML operations against them","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is the difference between storaged procedure and and functions?","type":"concept","a":"• A: The function must return a value but in Stored Procedure it is optional\n• Even a procedure can return zero or n values\n• Functions can have only input parameters for it whereas Procedures can have input or output parameters\n• Functions can be called from Procedure whereas Procedures cannot be called from a Function Page | 32","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of index available and difference between those?","type":"concept","a":"• Expression-based indexes efficiently evaluate queries with the indexed Expression. ➢ Unique and Non-Unique indexes ➢ Clustered and Non- Clustered indexes ➢ Partitioned and Non Partitioned indexes ➢ Expression-Based indexes Difference between those: • In addition to enforcing the uniqueness of data values, • A unique index can also be used to improve data retrieval performance during query processing\n• Non-unique indexes are not used to enforce constraints on the tables with which they are associated. • In Non-Clustered index leaf nodes are not the actual data itself rather they only contains included columns • In Clustered index leaf nodes are actual data itself • A non partitioned index is a single index object that refers to all rows in a partitioned table\n• Non partitioned indexes are always created as independent index objects in a single table space, even if the table data partitions span multiple table spaces. • When you create an index for a partitioned table, the index is a partitioned index by default unless you create one of the following types of indexes: • A unique index where the index key does not include all of the table- partitioning columns A spatial index","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of join available and syntax difference between each one?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Join statement is used to combine data or rows from two or more tables based on a common field between them\n• Different types of Joins are INNER JOIN: The INNER JOIN keyword selects all rows from both the tables as long as the condition satisfies.This keyword will create the result-set by combining all rows from both the tables where the condition satisfies i.e value of the common field will be same. syn: SELECT table1.column1,table1.column2,table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 INNER JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column LEFT JOIN: This join returns all the rows of the table on the left side of the join and matching rows for the table on the right side of join\n• The rows for which there is no matching row on right side, the result-set will contain null\n• LEFT JOIN is also known as LEFT OUTER JOIN Syn: SELECT table1.column1, table1.column2, table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 LEFT JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column;\n• RIGHT JOIN: RIGHT JOIN is similar to LEFT JOIN\n• This join returns all the rows of the table on the right side of the join and matching rows for the table on the left side of join\n• The rows for which there is no matching row on left side, the result-set will contain null\n• Page | 33 RIGHT JOIN is also known as RIGHT OUTER JOIN syn: SELECT table1.column1,table1.column2,table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 RIGHT JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column FULL JOIN: FULL JOIN creates the result-set by combining result of both LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN\n• The result-set will contain all the rows from both the tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to find Duplicate records in Employee Table","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 SELECT empno,ename,sal, count(*)FROM employee GROUP BY empno,ename,sal having count(*)>1 ---Solution2 select *, row_number() over (partition by empno order by empno) as rownumber from employee","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to remove duplicate Records in Employee Table using CTE","type":"concept","a":"with ABC as ( SELECT*, row_number() over (PARTITION BY empno ORDER BY empno) as RowNumber FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM ABC WHERE RowNumber>1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Find 2nd highest Salary employees in emp Table using CTE","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT SAL, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SAL DESC) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT TOP 2 SAL FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2 ---solution2 WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT *, Page | 34 DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SAL desc) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT ename,sal FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to find 2nd Highest salary Employees in emp Table with out using rank and cte","type":"concept","a":"use subquery ---Solution1 SELECT ename,sal FROM employee e1 WHERE 1=(SELECT COUNT (distinct sal) FROM employee e2 WHERE e2.sal > e1.sal) ---Solution2 SELECT TOP 1 sal FROM ( SELECT DISTINCT TOP 2 SAL FROM Employee ORDER BY SAL DESC ) result ORDER BY SAL ---Solution3 SELECT MAX(Sal) From Employee WHERE Sal < ( SELECT Max(Sal) FROM Employee) ---Solution4 find two top salaries select distinct top 2 sal from EMPLOYEE order by Sal desc","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Find 2nd highest Salary employees by each department using CTE","type":"concept","a":"WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (partition by deptno ORDER BY SAL desc) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT * FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Find Employees from employees tables, doen’t belongs to any department in dept","type":"concept","a":"table ---Solutio1 select * from employee e right outer join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where e.deptno is null ---Solution2 select e.ename,dname,loc from employee e right join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where e.deptno is null ---Solution3 select e.deptno,ename,dname,loc from employee e left join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where d.deptno is null","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to make combination of cricket match between below countries","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 select a.team+' Vs '+b.team from abc a,abc b where a.team<b.team order by a.team ---Solution2 SELECT * FROM abc A JOIN abc B on A.team<>B.team","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to find all employee having sal greater then the average salary of each","type":"concept","a":"dept select e.ename,e.sal,e.deptno,s.salavg from employee e, (select deptno,avg(sal) salavg from employee group by deptno)s where e.deptno=s.deptno and e.sal>s.salavg","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to find employee and manager name in below table","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 select e.eno,e.ename,m.ename managername from employee_test e,employee_test Page | 36 m where e.mgrid=m.eno ---Solution2 select e.ename,m.ename as manager from employee_test e left join employee_test m on e.mgrid=m.eno https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/samples/adventureworks-install-configure?view=sql- server-ver16&tabs=ssms ----Adventure scripts link.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Connecting the multiples files.","type":"concept","a":"• ======Adventure scripts =============== select * from dimproductsubcategory select * from dimproductcategory select * from FactInternetSales select * from dimproduct select * from DimProductSubcategory ---1.Display each category wise totalsalesamount select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName ------2.Display 2nd highest sales product with cte as ( select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount, DENSE_RANK() over (order by sum(salesamount) desc) as rank from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName ) select * from cte where rank =2 ----3.display product names dont have any sales select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey left join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName having sum(salesamount) is null ------4.display list of products belongs to categoryname is \"BIkes\" select p.EnglishProductName, c.EnglishProductCategoryName from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey Page | 37 where EnglishProductCategoryName='Bikes' To Know common column of the two tables select name from sys.columns where object_id=object_id('dbo.tablename1') intersect select name from sys.columns where object_id = object_id('dbo.tablename2') 44a4bxedxPwGQtSnEzGzdejnCdtrDJqkJ6uqWwGbXmdx5zrjGdHivmr9pyd7x8rVQMgumw GPCfuwseieCg8tuN5jNNeMJPU – This is the link of Emp and Dept scripts\n• SELECT VIEW: select * from sys.databases --display all database select * from sys.tables --display all tables select * from cons.sys.tables --display only given database name select * from sys.views -- display all the views select * from sys.columns --display all the coloumsin all database select * from sys.columns where name like '%emp%' --display only given table name","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"STORED PROCEDURE: A group of statements that to be existing.","type":"concept","a":"-- INNER PARAMETERS create procedure int_par1(@ename varchar(100),@var int)-- create a proce name as begin select empno,ename from emp where @ename=ename And @var=empno--on what table values you want end exec int_par1 'smith',7369 OUTPUT PARAMETER create procedure out_Par6(@ename varchar(100),@var int output)-- give name output as begin select @var=count(Empno) from emp where @ename=job end declare @totalcount2 varchar(100)-- give the specfic name of the output execute out_par6 'clerk', @totalcount2 out print @totalcount2 RETURN PARAMETERS create procedure out_Par7 as begin return (select count(Empno) from emp) end declare @totalcount3 int execute @totalcount3 = out_Par7 print @totalcount3 Page | 38 select * from emp create procedure sum2(@var1 int,@var2 int,@var3 int) as begin declare @sum int set @sum=(@var1+@var2+@var3) select @sum as colunm end exec sum2 1000,2000,3000","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SET OPREATORS: Set operators are used to combine the result of 2 or more tables as a single","type":"concept","a":"• set of values. create table emp_hyd( empid int, personname varchar(100), salary float ) insert into emp_hyd values(100,'name1',1000),(102,'name2',1000),(103,'name3',3000),(104,'name4',4000) select * from emp_hyd create table emp_pune( empid int, personname varchar(100), salary float ) insert into emp_pune values(100,'name1',1000),(107,'name7',7000),(108,'name8',8000),(109,'name9',9000) select * from emp_pune --operators --union: it will return the values but duplicate will not execute select * from emp_hyd union select * from emp_pune -- union all: it will return values and it will execute the duplicates also select * from emp_hyd union all select * from emp_pune -- intersect: it will execute only the common value select * from emp_hyd intersect select * from emp_pune -- EXPECT :it will return values from left hand table which are not right hand table select * from emp_hyd except select * from emp_pune select * from emp_pune except select * from emp_hyd Page | 39 16\n• TRIGGERS: --trigger; it is automatically perform --dml tigger:in response to upadte, insert, delete select * from res create trigger res_trig on res for insert as print 'hi' insert into res values('ai05',54,'ytre') --two magic create trigger res_trig2 on res for insert,update,delete as begin select * from inserted-- it will consider both update,insert select * from deleted-- it will consider delete end insert into res values ('aio6',45,'poiu') insert into res values ('aio8',45,'poiu') delete from res where aircrafe_code='aio6' create table res1( aircrafe_code varchar(100), seat_no int, clasess varchar(100)) -- if seat>25 fro other new table alter trigger res_trig2 on res for insert, update,delete as begin declare @seat_no int select @seat_no=seat_no from inserted if @seat_no>25 begin insert into res1 select * from inserted print 'it is suceed' end else print ' it is not' end insert into res values('aio10',21,'poiuy') insert into res values('aio10',29,'poiuy') select * from res1 --delete triggers create trigger trig_del1 on res1 instead of delete Page | 40 as print 'it have been deleted' set nocount on delete from res1 --ddl trigger-- in respones yo create,alter,drop --database scoped ddl trigger create trigger table_trig--it is a creating a database on database for create_table,alter_table,drop_procedure as print 'table is created' create table t1 ( col1 int, col2 int) create trigger table_drop on database for drop_table,alter_table as print 'table is deleted' rollback drop table t1 --server scope ddl trigger -- it is a servers create trigger server_trig on all server for create_database,drop_database as print 'server has been created' create database data_base drop trigger res_trig drop trigger table_trig on database drop trigger data_base on all server","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SUBQUERY: A sub query is a form of a SQL Statement that appears inside another sql","type":"concept","a":"• statement\n• It is also treate as nested query\n• The statement containing a Subquery is called Parent statements \n• The parent statement uses the rows returned by the subquery. create table sales_orders( order_no varchar(10), client_no varchar (10)) insert into sales_orders values('poo1','coo1'), ('poo2','coo2'),('poo3','coo1'),('poo4','coo3'),('poo5','coo3'), ('poo6','coo3'),('poo7','coo4') create table cilents( client_no varchar(20), person_name varchar(10), quality numeric (10)) insert into cilents values('coo1','name1',1),('coo2','name2',1), Page | 41 ('coo3','name3',1),('coo4','name4',1), ('coo5','name1',1),('coo6','name6',1) select * from sales_orders select * from cilents select * from cilents where person_name='name3' -- it is form of a sql statement appears inside the another sql statement select * from sales_orders where client_no=(select client_no from cilents where person_name='name3') select * from sales_orders where client_no not in (select client_no from cilents where person_name='name3')","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"GROUPING AND HAVING :","type":"concept","a":"• Group By: It is used to group rows based on distinct values that exist for specified columns ie it creates a data set containing several set of records grouped together based on a condition\n• Having : It is always used in conjuction with group by, It is imposes a condition on the group by clause which further filters the group created by the group by clause. --groups by create table sales_pro( product_name varchar(100), product_num varchar(10), quality_num int, quality_dis int ) insert into sales_pro values('pd1','pooo1',10,10), ('pd1','pooo1',9,9),('pd1','pooo1',8,8), ('pd2','pooo2',7,7), ('pd2','pooo2',8,8), ('pd3','pooo3',10,10), ('pd4','pooo4',5,5), ('pd4','pooo4',6,6), ('pd5','pooo5',9,9), ('pd5','pooo5',8,8), ('pd1','pooo1',10,10) select *from sales_pro select product_num,sum(quality_num) as \"total quality\" from sales_pro group by product_num -- group by it add rows having a same values in a colunm -- having condition select product_num,sum(quality_num) as \"total quality\" from sales_pro group by product_num having product_num in('pooo1','pooo5')-- this HAVING gives the filiters 19.FUNCTIONS: --functions: it is a return values --scaler function:it is a single value create function fun_sum(@var1 int,@var2 int,@var3 int) returns int as begin declare @sum int set @sum=@var1+@var2+@var3 Page | 42 return @sum end select dbo. fun_sum (100,200,300) as total alter function nrt_fun(@customerid varchar(100)) returns varchar(100) as begin declare @nrt_fun1 varchar(100) select @nrt_fun1=country from Customers where customerid=@customerid return @nrt_fun1 end select dbo.nrt_fun('usa') --table function retuen values select * from res create function tab_fun(@seat_no int) returns table as return (select * from res where @seat_no=seat_no) select * from tab_fun(87)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"TRANSACTIONS: A SET OF T-SQL STATEMENTS TO BE EXECUTED AS A SINGLE","type":"concept","a":"UNIT create table emp_tran( empid int, person_name varchar(100), salary int) select * from emp_tran --explicit transaction: it will commit and rollback begin tran-- only in this explicit insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) declare @row_count int select @row_count=count(*) from emp_tran if @row_count <=10 commit tran else rollback tran select count(*) from emp_tran truncate table emp_tran --implict tran: it is like explicit set implicit_transactions on--: imp command insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) declare @row_count int select @row_count=count(*) from emp_tran if @row_count <=10 commit tran else rollback tran Page | 44 select count(*) from emp_tran truncate table emp_tran --autocomtt tran & open transbegin tran insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) --isolation level --isolation level read and uncommitted set transaction ISOLATION level READ UNCOMMITTED insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) set transaction ISOLATION level READ COMMITTED insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) set transaction ISOLATION level SERIALIZABLE insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) --snapshot use master alter database [proce] set allow_snapshot_isolation on use [proce] set transaction isolation level snapshot begin tran insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) select * from emp_tran","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"QUEREY OPEATORS","type":"concept","a":"• -- TO HELP T-QUERIES PERTAINING TO SEARCHING AND SORTING ACTIVITIES, -- WE GENERALLY CREATE INDEXES CREATE TABLE EMP_DETAILS ( EMP_ID INT, EMP_NAME VARCHAR(20), EMP_SAL INT ) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1001,'SUNNY',1000) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1002,'PRIYANKA',1200) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1003,'RAVI',1060) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1004,'JEFF',2000) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1005,'JONATHAN',1220) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1006,'PETER',1044) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1007,'AMITABH',1456) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1008,'RAJENDRA',2100) Page | 45 INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1009,'ANUSHKA',1220) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(2001,'KAREENA',1880) SELECT * FROM EMP_DETAILS Index: It is used by queries to find data form table quickly\n• Indexs are created on tables & view , is very similar to an index that we find in a book. create index emp_details_index on emp_details(emp_name) SELECT EMP_NAME FROM EMP_DETAILS--index scan create nonclustered index emp_detalis_index_v2 on emp_details(emp_name) include(EMP_ID,EMP_SAL) select * from EMP_DETAILS where EMP_NAME='sunny'--index seek select * from EMP_DETAILS where EMP_NAME='xyz'--index seek","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Implicit Cursors: These cursor created by SQL server by default when select statement is","type":"concept","a":"executed.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Source 2 — Read Customer table from Azure SQL DB","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Filter — Remove rows where TotalRevenue < 0","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Aggregate — Group by Region, sum TotalRevenue","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Sink — Write results into Synapse Analytics table","type":"concept","a":"• 💡 Tip: When asked about Data Flow: always walk through Source → Transform → Sink\n• Mention that you used Debug mode to test, and that Data Flows run on auto-scaling Spark clusters\n• Common Data Flow Transformations to Know • Derived Column — Add or modify columns using expressions • Aggregate — GROUP BY with SUM, COUNT, AVG • Join — INNER, LEFT OUTER, RIGHT OUTER between two streams • Lookup — Similar to join but optimized for reference/dimension tables • Filter — Remove rows based on a condition • Select — Choose which columns to keep or rename • Conditional Split — Like a WHERE clause that routes rows to different paths • Flatten — Expand arrays/nested JSON into rows • Window — Rank, Row Number, Lead/Lag functions","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"What is Late Arriving Data?","type":"concept","a":"• Late arriving data means records that arrive after the expected processing window\n• For example: a transaction that happened on Monday but arrives in your system on Wednesday\n• Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 3 Example: You run a daily pipeline at midnight\n• A payment event from 11:58 PM arrives at","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Go to Databricks SQL → Alerts","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Create a query (e.g., SELECT COUNT(*) as failed_records FROM errors WHERE date =","type":"concept","a":"current_date())","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Employee and A(cid:425)endance Tables","type":"concept","a":"• \"I have Employee and A(cid:425)endance tables\n• How will you cleanse the data?\" Employee EmpID EmpName Role Manager A(cid:425)endance EmpID EmpName Date Loca(cid:415)on LoginTime LogoutTime Sample Answer: \"For the Employee table:  Remove duplicate Employee IDs using dropDuplicates()  Replace null employee names with 'Unknown' or another business-approved default  Validate mandatory fields For the A(cid:425)endance table:  Remove records where both LoginTime and LogoutTime are null  Instead of dele(cid:415)ng them permanently, store them in a Reject or Quaran(cid:415)ne table for future inves(cid:415)ga(cid:415)on\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Customers with no orders or whose latest order is older than 30 days should be","type":"concept","a":"returned.\" SELECT c.CustomerID, c.CustomerName FROM Customer c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID GROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.CustomerName HAVING MAX(o.OrderDate) IS NULL OR MAX(o.OrderDate) < DATEADD(DAY, -30, GETDATE());","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Joins in sql","type":"concept","a":"Returns Join Type Matching rows only INNER JOIN All le(cid:332) rows + matching right rows LEFT JOIN All right rows + matching le(cid:332) rows RIGHT JOIN All rows from both tables FULL OUTER JOIN Cartesian product CROSS JOIN Table joined with itself SELF JOIN","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize sql queries","type":"concept","a":"• To op(cid:415)mize SQL queries, I reduce data scanning by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, use indexing on join and filter columns, avoid func(cid:415)ons on indexed fields, push filters early, prefer EXISTS over IN for large datasets, and analyze execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks\n• I also use par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and avoid unnecessary opera(cid:415)ons like DISTINCT or correlated subqueries when possible a)\n• Select only required columns (avoid SELECT *) (cid:57872) Bad: SELECT * FROM Employee; ✔ Good: SELECT emp_id, name FROM Employee; (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Reduces I/O and memory usage b)\n• Use proper indexing  Create indexes on: o JOIN keys o WHERE clause columns o ORDER BY columns Example: CREATE INDEX idx_emp_dept ON Employee(dept_id); c).Avoid func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns (cid:57872) Bad: WHERE YEAR(join_date) = 2024; ✔ Good: WHERE join_date >= '2024-01-01' AND join_date < '2025-01-01'; (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Allows index usage d)\n• Filter data early (push filters) (cid:57872) Bad: SELECT * FROM A JOIN B ON A.id = B.id; ✔ Good: SELECT * FROM ( SELECT * FROM A WHERE status = 'ac(cid:415)ve' ) A JOIN B ON A.id = B.id; e)\n• Use EXISTS instead of IN (for large datasets) (cid:57872) IN: SELECT * FROM Employee WHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM Dept); ✔ EXISTS: SELECT * FROM Employee e WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM Dept d WHERE d.dept_id = e.dept_id ); f)\n• Op(cid:415)mize JOINs  Ensure join columns are indexed  Prefer INNER JOIN over OUTER JOIN when possible g)\n• Avoid unnecessary DISTINCT / ORDER BY  DISTINCT adds sor(cid:415)ng overhead  ORDER BY should be used only if needed h)\n• Use LIMIT / TOP for large datasets SELECT * FROM Orders LIMIT 100; i)\n• Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on large tables  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on by date / region / key column  Reduces scan (cid:415)me dras(cid:415)cally Example: PARTITION BY (order_date) j)\n• Use execu(cid:415)on plan analysis  Use: EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM Employee;\n• Check:  Full table scan  Index usage  Join strategy k)","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"write a sql query to get emp id, first name, last name, dept name for those employees","type":"concept","a":"who get the 2nd highest salary Assume table: Employee  emp_id  first_name  last_name  dept_id  salary Department  dept_id  dept_name (cid:33158)(cid:57393) SQL Query (2nd highest salary employees) WITH ranked_emp AS ( SELECT e.emp_id, e.first_name, e.last_name, d.dept_name, e.salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id ) SELECT emp_id, first_name, last_name, dept_name FROM ranked_emp WHERE rnk = 2;","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Why use HAVING instead of WHERE?","type":"concept","a":"•  WHERE filters rows before aggrega(cid:415)on.  HAVING filters groups a(cid:332)er aggrega(cid:415)on\n• For example, this is incorrect: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE SUM(amount) > 500; -- (cid:57872) Invalid This is correct: SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total_spending FROM Orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(amount) > 500;\n• This dis(cid:415)nc(cid:415)on is a frequent SQL interview topic\n• What are the read modes available in spark? How stages, tasks,jobs created in spark? How to call one pipeline from another pipeline in ADF? How to pass variables to pipeline in ADF? What are all the configura(cid:415)on se(cid:427)ngs have to provided to call the databricks workflow via ADF? How to read csv files and create and create a dataframe and write to delta format in ADLS? Explain me the spark architecture? what are all the indexes present in sql and types of it? create a dataframe using [(1,),(3,),(5,)] and find out the missing values in it? A=[1,2,3] and B=[1,2,3] what is the output of A==B,B==A,A is B What is the difference between par(cid:415)(cid:415)on and bucke(cid:415)ng in spark? Difference between managed and external tables in databricks? op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in spark? Below are interview-ready answers for each ques(cid:415)on, suitable for Azure Data Engineer (Azure Databricks + ADF + PySpark + SQL) interviews","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Indexes and Types","type":"concept","a":"• Clustered Index  Physically sorts table data.  Only one clustered index per table\n• Non-Clustered Index  Separate structure storing key + pointer.  Mul(cid:415)ple allowed\n• Unique Index  Prevents duplicate values\n• Composite Index  Built on mul(cid:415)ple columns\n• Filtered Index  Indexes only rows matching a condi(cid:415)on\n• Full-Text Index  Op(cid:415)mized for text searching","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Create DataFrame and find missing values","type":"concept","a":"• Input: data = [(1,), (3,), (5,)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, [\"num\"]) Expected sequence: 1 2 3 4 5 Solu(cid:415)on: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col expected = spark.range(1, 6).toDF(\"num\") missing = expected.join(df, \"num\", \"le(cid:332)_an(cid:415)\") missing.show() Output: 2 4 10\n• Output of A = [1,2,3] B = [1,2,3] print(A == B) print(B == A) print(A is B) Output: True True False Explana(cid:415)on:  == compares values.  is compares object iden(cid:415)ty (memory address)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What are the source systems?","type":"concept","a":"Examples:  Web Applica(cid:415)ons  SQL Server  Oracle  SAP  REST APIs  CSV files  Excel files","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How is data flowing from web applica(cid:415)ons to SQL Server?","type":"concept","a":"Users ↓ Web Applica(cid:415)on ↓ Applica(cid:415)on Server ↓ SQL Server The applica(cid:415)on writes transac(cid:415)onal data into SQL Server using applica(cid:415)on APIs or ORM frameworks.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Who loads web applica(cid:415)on data into SQL Server?","type":"concept","a":"• The applica(cid:415)on layer is responsible for inser(cid:415)ng and upda(cid:415)ng data\n• Data engineers consume that data for analy(cid:415)cs; they do not typically load it into SQL Server","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What transforma(cid:415)ons happen where?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server  Stored procedures  Basic filtering  Simple joins  Incremental extrac(cid:415)on Databricks  Data cleansing  Null handling  Deduplica(cid:415)on  Business rules  SCD implementa(cid:415)on  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  Delta tables Synapse  Repor(cid:415)ng views  Star schema  Aggregated datasets","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find customers who never placed an order","type":"concept","a":"SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID=o.CustomerID WHERE o.CustomerID IS NULL;","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Today's temperature higher than yesterday","type":"concept","a":"SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, LAG(Temperature) OVER(ORDER BY Date) AS PrevTemp FROM Weather )t WHERE Temperature>PrevTemp;","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid recomputa(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Use:  Cache  Persist  Delta intermediate tables  Materialized views (where applicable)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produc(cid:415)on issue when Dev differs","type":"concept","a":" Recreate the scenario using representa(cid:415)ve data.  Align configura(cid:415)on where possible.  Test in QA/UAT before Produc(cid:415)on deployment.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you connect on-prem SQL Server to Azure?","type":"concept","a":"A common approach is:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"SCD Type 1, 2, and 3 — SQL Code","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 1 — Overwrite (Simple UPDATE) -- SCD Type 1: Just overwrite the existing record MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND (target.name <> source.name OR target.email <> source.email) THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n• SCD Type 2 — Full History with Effective Dates -- Step 1: Expire existing active records that have changed MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id AND target.is_current = 1 WHEN MATCHED AND (target.name <> source.name OR target.city <> source.city) THEN UPDATE SET target.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, target.is_current = 0; -- Step 2: Insert new active rows for changed records INSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current) SELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE AS start_date, NULL AS end_date, 1 AS is_current FROM staging_customer s INNER JOIN dim_customer d ON s.customer_id = d.customer_id AND d.is_current = 0 AND d.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY UNION ALL -- Insert brand new customers SELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1 FROM staging_customer s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id = s.customer_id);\n• SCD Type 2 using Delta Lake MERGE (Recommended) -- Cleaner approach using Delta Lake MERGE with CDF MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING ( -- Changed records: expire old row SELECT d.surrogate_key, d.customer_id, d.name, d.city, d.start_date, CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY AS end_date, 0 AS is_current FROM dim_customer d INNER JOIN staging_customer s ON d.customer_id = s.customer_id WHERE d.is_current = 1 Page 6 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide AND (d.name <> s.name OR d.city <> s.city) UNION ALL -- New rows for changed + new customers SELECT NULL, s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1 FROM staging_customer s ) AS source ON target.surrogate_key = source.surrogate_key WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET target.end_date = source.end_date, target.is_current = 0 WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.city, source.start_date, source.end_date, source.is_current);\n• SCD Type 3 — Previous Value Column -- SCD Type 3: Keep current and one previous value MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND target.city <> source.city THEN UPDATE SET target.prev_city = target.city, target.city = source.city, target.updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unmatched Records from Left Table Only — Left Anti Join","type":"concept","a":"• A LEFT ANTI JOIN returns rows from the LEFT table that have NO matching row in the RIGHT table\n• It is the opposite of an INNER JOIN\n• SQL -- Get all orders that have NO matching customer in the customer table SELECT o.* FROM orders o LEFT ANTI JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id;\n• Page 10 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide -- Alternative using NOT EXISTS SELECT * FROM orders o WHERE NOT EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM customers c WHERE c.customer_id = o.customer_id ); -- Alternative using NOT IN (caution: behaves differently with NULLs) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id IS NOT NULL);\n• PySpark # Left Anti Join in PySpark unmatched = orders_df.join(customers_df, on='customer_id', how='left_anti') unmatched.show() # Equivalent using filter + left join unmatched = orders_df.join(customers_df, on='customer_id', how='left') .filter(col('customers_df.customer_id').isNull()) Join Type Returns INNER JOIN Only matching rows from both tables LEFT JOIN All rows from left + NULLs for unmatched right RIGHT JOIN All rows from right + NULLs for unmatched left LEFT ANTI JOIN Only left rows with NO match in right LEFT SEMI JOIN Only left rows that DO have a match in right (no right columns) FULL OUTER JOIN All rows from both — NULLs where no match","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Second Highest Salary by Department","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — dense_rank() Window Function (Recommended) SELECT dept, name, salary FROM ( SELECT dept, name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees ) ranked WHERE rnk = 2;\n• Method 2 — PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) df_ranked = df.withColumn('rnk', dense_rank().over(w)) df_ranked.filter(col('rnk') == 2).select('dept','name','salary').show() Page 11 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Method 3 — Subquery (without window function) SELECT dept, MAX(salary) AS second_highest_salary FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e2.dept = employees.dept) GROUP BY dept;\n• Function Ties Behavior Best for dense_rank() Same rank for ties; no gaps Top-N salary — most common interview answer rank() Same rank for ties; gaps after ties Ranking where gaps matter row_number() Unique number always; no ties When you need exactly one row per rank","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Validation Checks Before Moving Data to the Next Layer","type":"concept","a":"• Page 13 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Validation What to Check PySpark / SQL Example Schema Validation Column names, data types df.schema == expected_schema match expected schema Null Checks Critical columns must not be df.filter(col('id').isNull()).count() == 0 null Duplicate Checks No duplicate records on df.count() == primary key df.dropDuplicates(['id']).count() Data Type Validation Values match expected type Try cast; check for null after cast (int, date, etc.) Range / Domain Validation Numeric ranges, valid enum df.filter((col('age')<0)|(col('age')>150)) values Referential Integrity Foreign key exists in parent Left anti join between child and parent table Record Count Validation Row count matches source or abs(source_count - target_count) <= expected threshold threshold Business Rule Validation Custom business logic (e.g., df.filter(col('end_date') < col('start_date')) end_date > start_date) # Example: Validation framework in PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import col def validate_layer(df, layer_name): errors = [] # 1\n• Null check on key columns null_count = df.filter(col('id').isNull()).count() if null_count > 0: errors.append(f'NULL id count: {null_count}') # 2\n• Duplicate check total = df.count() dedup = df.dropDuplicates(['id']).count() if total != dedup: errors.append(f'Duplicates found: {total - dedup}') # 3\n• Range check invalid_age = df.filter((col('age') < 0) | (col('age') > 150)).count() if invalid_age > 0: errors.append(f'Invalid age values: {invalid_age}') if errors: raise Exception(f'{layer_name} validation FAILED: {errors}') else: print(f'{layer_name} validation PASSED ({total} rows)') validate_layer(silver_df, 'Silver')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Fill nulls with derived values","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, mean, lit # Fill with column mean avg_salary = df.select(mean('salary')).first()[0] df_clean = df.fillna({'salary': avg_salary}) # Conditional fill using when() df_clean = df.withColumn('salary', when(col('salary').isNull(), 0).otherwise(col('salary'))) # Fill with previous value (forward fill using Window) from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import last w = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy('date').rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, 0) df_clean = df.withColumn('salary', last('salary', ignorenulls=True).over(w))","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Filter out nulls","type":"concept","a":"df_clean = df.filter(col('id').isNotNull()) df_clean = df.where(col('name').isNotNull() & col('email').isNotNull())","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Full Load vs Incremental Load from SQL Server","type":"concept","a":"Page 18 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Aspect Full Load Incremental Load What Load ALL data every run Load only NEW or CHANGED data since last run When to use First-time historical load; small Large tables; daily ETL; tables with tables; no CDC support last_modified column Source impact High — full table scan every run Low — only reads delta records Processing time Long Short Complexity Simple Needs watermark tracking Risk Overwrites may cause data loss if Must handle late-arriving data not careful Full Load Code # Full load from SQL Server df_full = spark.read.jdbc( url='jdbc:sqlserver://server.db.windows.net:1433;database=db', table='dbo.Customers', properties={'user': 'user', 'password': 'pwd', 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'} ) df_full.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.customers') Incremental Load Code (Watermark-based) # Read last watermark from control table last_wm = spark.sql(\"SELECT MAX(last_load_ts) FROM ctrl.watermark WHERE table_name='Customers'\").first()[0] # Load only changed records df_incr = spark.read.jdbc( url='jdbc:sqlserver://server.db.windows.net:1433;database=db', table='dbo.Customers', predicates=[f\"ModifiedDate > '{last_wm}'\"], properties={'user': 'user', 'password': 'pwd', 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'} ) # Merge into target (upsert) df_incr.createOrReplaceTempView('incr') spark.sql(''' MERGE INTO silver.customers t USING incr s ON t.customer_id = s.customer_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT * ''') # Update watermark spark.sql(f\"UPDATE ctrl.watermark SET last_load_ts=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE table_name='Customers'\")","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL – Same Emp Name, Different Department (No JOIN)","type":"concept","a":"• Schema EMP (Emp_id, E_name, E_mid, E_mname, E_loc) DEPT (D_id, D_name, D_mid, D_loc) Approach — Using EXISTS / Subquery (No JOIN Keyword) -- Find employees who share the same name but belong to different departments SELECT e1.Emp_id, e1.E_name, e1.E_mid AS dept1, e2.E_mid AS dept2 FROM EMP e1, EMP e2 WHERE e1.E_name = e2.E_name AND e1.E_mid <> e2.E_mid AND e1.Emp_id < e2.Emp_id; -- avoid duplicate pairs Alternative — Using EXISTS SELECT DISTINCT e1.Emp_id, e1.E_name, e1.E_mid AS department FROM EMP e1 WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM EMP e2 WHERE e2.E_name = e1.E_name AND e2.E_mid <> e1.E_mid Page 9 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A );\n• Note: Both solutions use comma-separated FROM (implicit cross join notation) or subquery — NO explicit JOIN keyword is used","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Join Counts – Inner, Left, Right","type":"concept","a":"• Given Data Table A – ID Table B – ID 1 1 1 3 2 1 NULL 4 NULL 5 3 Join Results Join Type Logic Count Explanation INNER JOIN Only matching rows 3 IDs 1,1,1 (A has two 1s;\n• B has one 1) → 2 (A.ID = B.ID) rows for ID=1, plus ID=3 → total 3 rows\n• NULLs never match\n• LEFT JOIN All rows from A + 6 All 6 rows from A are kept\n• Matching B rows matches from B filled in;\n• NULLs from A and unmatched rows get NULL on B side\n• RIGHT JOIN All rows from B + 7 All 5 rows from B are kept\n• ID=1 in B matches from A matches 2 rows in A → 2 rows\n• IDs 3,4,5 from B each get 1 row; total 7\n• Detailed Breakdown Table A: [1, 1, 2, NULL, NULL, 3] → 6 rows Table B: [1, 3, 1, 4, 5] → 5 rows • INNER JOIN: Match A.ID=1 (×2) with B.ID=1 (×1) = 2 rows;\n• A.ID=3 with B.ID=3 = 1 row → Total = 3 • LEFT JOIN: All 6 A rows kept;\n• B columns NULL where no match (A.ID=2, NULL, NULL have no B match) → Total = 6 Page 10 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A • RIGHT JOIN: All 5 B rows kept;","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Warehouse (DWH) is a centralized, structured repository that stores large volumes of historical, integrated data from multiple source systems\n• It is optimized for analytical querying and reporting rather than transactional processing\n• Key Characteristics Property Description Subject-Oriented Organized around key business subjects: Sales, Finance, HR — not by application Integrated Data consolidated from multiple heterogeneous sources (CRM, ERP, flat files) Non-Volatile Once loaded, data is not updated or deleted — historical record is preserved Time-Variant Maintains historical snapshots; every record has a time dimension Read-Optimized Schema and indexes designed for fast analytical reads (not transactional writes) DWH vs OLTP vs Data Lake Aspect OLTP (Operational Data Warehouse Data Lake DB) Purpose Day-to-day transactions Analytics & reporting Raw data storage for all types Data type Current, real-time Historical, structured Raw — structured + unstructured Schema Schema-on-write (strict) Schema-on-write Schema-on-read (flexible) Query speed Fast for single rows Fast for aggregations Slower without optimization Example SQL Server, MySQL Snowflake, Synapse ADLS, S3, Databricks Lakehouse Common Schemas in a Data Warehouse • Star Schema: One central Fact table linked to multiple Dimension tables — simple, fast queries • Snowflake Schema: Dimensions are further normalized into sub-dimensions — saves space, more joins • Galaxy Schema: Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables — for complex enterprises Page 3 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is Data Cleansing and How is it Done?","type":"concept","a":"• Data cleansing (also called data cleaning or data scrubbing) is the process of detecting and correcting inaccurate, incomplete, duplicate, or irrelevant records in a dataset to improve data quality before analysis or loading into a warehouse\n• Common Data Quality Issues Issue Example Fix Missing / NULL Salary = NULL, Email = Fill with default, mean/median, or flag as values NULL unknown Duplicate records Same customer appears Deduplicate using dropDuplicates() or twice with same ID ROW_NUMBER() Incorrect data types Salary stored as '2500' Cast to correct type; handle parse errors (string) instead of integer Inconsistent formats Date as '01-Jan-2024' in one Standardize using to_date() with format file, '2024-01-01' in another pattern Outliers / Invalid Age = -5, Salary = 99999999 Apply business-rule filters; flag or exclude values outliers Whitespace / Case ' John ' vs 'john' vs 'JOHN' trim() + lower() / upper() for normalization issues Referential integrity Order has Customer_ID that Left join + filter nulls to find orphan records doesn't exist in Customer table Special characters Name = 'O\\u2019Brien' vs regexp_replace() to clean special chars 'OBrien' PySpark Data Cleansing Code Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F # 1\n• Remove duplicates df = df.dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) # 2\n• Handle NULL values df = df.fillna({'salary': 0, 'emp_name': 'Unknown', 'location': 'N/A'}) # 3\n• Fix data types df = df.withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) df = df.withColumn('join_date', F.to_date(F.col('join_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd')) # 4\n• Trim whitespace and standardize case df = df.withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) # 5\n• Remove outliers (salary must be between 1000 and 500000) df = df.filter(F.col('salary').between(1000, 500000)) Page 10 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # 6\n• Remove special characters from name df = df.withColumn('emp_name', F.regexp_replace(F.col('emp_name'), '[^a-zA-Z ]', '')) # 7\n• Flag records with remaining NULLs df = df.withColumn('has_nulls', F.when(F.col('emp_id').isNull() | F.col('emp_name').isNull(), True).otherwise(False)) df.show() Page 11 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What are Window Functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Window functions perform calculations across a set of rows that are related to the current row — similar to aggregate functions but WITHOUT collapsing rows into a single result\n• Each row retains its identity while gaining access to aggregated or ranked values from a defined window (partition)\n• Syntax Structure function_name() OVER ( PARTITION BY column1, column2 -- defines the group/window ORDER BY column3 [ASC|DESC] -- defines row ordering within window ROWS/RANGE BETWEEN \n• AND ... -- optional frame specification ) Types of Window Functions Category Function Description Ranking ROW_NUMBER() Unique sequential number per row within partition — no ties Ranking RANK() Same rank for ties; gaps in sequence after ties (1,1,3) Ranking DENSE_RANK() Same rank for ties;\n• NO gaps (1,1,2) Ranking NTILE(n) Divides rows into n equal buckets Ranking PERCENT_RANK() Relative rank as a percentage (0 to 1) Aggregate SUM() OVER(...) Running or partitioned sum without collapsing rows Aggregate AVG() OVER(...) Running or partitioned average Aggregate COUNT() OVER(...) Count per window — useful for % of total Aggregate MIN() / MAX() Min/max within the window OVER(...) Navigation LAG(col, n) Access value from n rows BEFORE current row Navigation LEAD(col, n) Access value from n rows AFTER current row Navigation FIRST_VALUE(col) First value in the window frame Navigation LAST_VALUE(col) Last value in the window frame PySpark Window Function Examples from pyspark.sql import Window from pyspark.sql import functions as F # Define window w = Window.partitionBy('department').orderBy(F.desc('salary')) # ROW_NUMBER -- unique rank per department df = df.withColumn('row_num', F.row_number().over(w)) Page 12 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # RANK -- same rank for ties, gaps after ties df = df.withColumn('rank', F.rank().over(w)) # DENSE_RANK -- same rank for ties, no gaps df = df.withColumn('dense_rank', F.dense_rank().over(w)) # Running total salary within department w2 = Window.partitionBy('department').orderBy('join_date') \\ .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow) df = df.withColumn('running_salary', F.sum('salary').over(w2)) # LAG -- previous row's salary df = df.withColumn('prev_salary', F.lag('salary', 1).over(w)) # Get top 1 earner per department top_earners = df.filter(F.col('row_num') == 1) Page 13 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You stored a table in Databricks — what format is it likely to be and where is the","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"max sales per customer?","type":"concept","a":"Input Data: customer_id | transaction_date | sales cust1 | 2023-12-01 | 100 cust2 | 2023-12-02 | 150 cust1 | 2023-12-03 | 200 cust2 | 2023-12-04 | 250 Expected Output: cust_id | sales_max cust1 | 200 cust2 | 250","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used databricks sql warehouse?","type":"concept","a":"Databricks SQL Warehouse  Serverless compute for BI queries  Used for Power BI / Tableau dashboards","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Diff between rank and dense rank","type":"concept","a":"Rank vs Dense Rank Func(cid:415)on Output RANK 1,1,3 DENSE_RANK 1,1,2","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you op(cid:415)mize sql query?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on  Indexing  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Avoid SELECT *  Filter early  Proper joins","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you do the par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in sql?","type":"concept","a":"Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in SQL  Splits data physically for performance  Usually date-based par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Transac(cid:415)ons table:","type":"concept","a":"+ — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — | id | dept | state | amount | date | + — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — | 1 | IT | approved | 1000 | 2018–12–18 | | 2 | IT | declined | 2000 | 2018–12–19 | | 3 | IT | approved | 2000 | 2019–01–01 | | 4 | Fin | approved | 2000 | 2019–01–07 | + — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — Output: + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — — --------------+ | month | dept | trans_count | approved_trans_cnt | total_amount | approved_total_amount | + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — -------------— -+ | 2018–12 | IT | 2 | 1 | 3000 | 1000 | | 2019–01 | IT | 1 | 1 | 2000 | 2000 | | 2019–01 | Fin | 1 | 1 | 2000 | 2000 | + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — — --------------+ Write a SQL query to find:  Month  Department  Total transac(cid:415)ons  Approved transac(cid:415)on count  Total amount  Approved amount Concepts Tested  GROUP BY  CASE WHEN  Aggregate func(cid:415)ons  DATE_FORMAT SELECT DATE_FORMAT(date,'%Y-%m') AS month, dept, COUNT(*) AS trans_count, SUM(CASE WHEN state='approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) approved_trans_cnt, SUM(amount) total_amount, SUM(CASE WHEN state='approved' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) approved_total_amount FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(date,'%Y-%m'), dept;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Reverse Route Distance","type":"concept","a":"Id | Place1 |Place2 |Distance | ------------------------------------| 1 |Hyd | Mumbai | 1200 | 2 |Banglore | Pune | 400 | 3 |Mumbai | Hyd | 1400 | 4 |Pune | Bangalore | 400 | 5 |Chennai |Hyd | 1200 | ------------------------------------| Output: -------------------------------- Place1 |Place2 |Distance | -------------------------------| Hyd | Mumbai | 2600 | Banglore | Pune | 800 | Chennai | Hyd | 1200 | -------------------------------- Input Hyd → Mumbai Mumbai → Hyd Output Hyd Mumbai 2600 SELECT LEAST(place1,place2) AS place1, GREATEST(place1,place2) AS place2, SUM(distance) total_distance FROM Routes GROUP BY LEAST(place1,place2), GREATEST(place1,place2);","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find Second Highest Salary","type":"concept","a":"SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee WHERE salary < ( SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee );","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Highest Salary in Each Department","type":"concept","a":"SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) rnk FROM Employee )t WHERE rnk=1;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Remove Duplicate Records","type":"concept","a":"• SELECT DISTINCT * FROM Employee;\n• Using ROW_NUMBER WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY id ORDER BY id) rn FROM Employee ) SELECT * FROM cte WHERE rn=1;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between UNION and UNION ALL","type":"concept","a":"UNION UNION ALL Removes duplicates Keeps duplicates Slower Faster","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between WHERE and HAVING","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows before aggrega(cid:415)on\n• HAVING filters groups a(cid:332)er aggrega(cid:415)on\n• PySpark Ques(cid:415)ons","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow vs Wide Transforma(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow No shuffle\n• Examples  map  filter  union Wide Shuffle happens\n• Examples  join  groupBy  reduceByKey  dis(cid:415)nct","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Top 2 items per category based on sales","type":"concept","a":"• sales itemId,categoryId,amount apple,fruit,100 apple,fruit,100 apple,fruit,100 mango,fruit,200 pear,fruit,150 shirt,fashion,5000 jeans,fashion,3000 jeans,fashion,3000 (cid:415)e,fashion,1000 apple,mobile,100000 Answer: SELECT itemId, categoryId, SUM(amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY itemId, categoryId ), ranked_sales AS ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY categoryId ORDER BY total_sales DESC ) AS rnk FROM item_sales ) SELECT itemId, categoryId, total_sales FROM ranked_sales WHERE rnk <= 2;\n• Pyspark: from pyspark.sql import func(cid:415)ons as F from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Step 1: aggregate df_agg = df.groupBy(\"itemId\", \"categoryId\") \\ .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total_sales\")) # Step 2: window ranking window_spec = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"categoryId\").orderBy(F.desc(\"total_sales\")) df_ranked = df_agg.withColumn(\"rnk\", F.dense_rank().over(window_spec)) # Step 3: filter top 2 result = df_ranked.filter(F.col(\"rnk\") <= 2) result.show()","source":"Fractal Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to find customers who spent more than ₹1000 in the last month","type":"concept","a":"using Orders and Order_Items tables Assume: Orders |OrderID|CustomerID|OrderDate| Order_Items |OrderID|Quantity|UnitPrice| SELECT o.CustomerID, SUM(oi.Quantity * oi.UnitPrice) AS TotalSpent FROM Orders o JOIN Order_Items oi ON o.OrderID = oi.OrderID WHERE o.OrderDate >= DATEADD(month, -1, GETDATE()) GROUP BY o.CustomerID HAVING SUM(oi.Quantity * oi.UnitPrice) > 1000;","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Follow-up: Why use HAVING instead of WHERE?","type":"concept","a":"Answer: HAVING filters aggregated results after GROUP BY, whereas WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation.","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Find the top 3 customers by total purchase amount per month using window","type":"concept","a":"functions? WITH MonthlySales AS ( SELECT CustomerID, YEAR(OrderDate) AS Year, MONTH(OrderDate) AS Month, SUM(TotalAmount) AS TotalPurchase FROM Orders GROUP BY CustomerID, YEAR(OrderDate), MONTH(OrderDate) ) SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY Year,Month ORDER BY TotalPurchase DESC ) RankNo FROM MonthlySales )t WHERE RankNo<=3;","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a query to calculate net revenue per day considering returns data?","type":"concept","a":"• Tables Orders |OrderID|OrderDate|Amount| Returns |OrderID|ReturnAmount| SELECT o.OrderDate, SUM(o.Amount)-COALESCE(SUM(r.ReturnAmount),0) AS NetRevenue FROM Orders o LEFT JOIN Returns r ON o.OrderID=r.OrderID GROUP BY o.OrderDate;\n• If no returns exist, COALESCE() avoids NULL","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark: Join two DataFrames using row numbers","type":"concept","a":"Suppose IDs don't match. from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number windowSpec = Window.orderBy(\"Name\") df1 = df1.withColumn( \"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec) ) df2 = df2.withColumn( \"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec) ) result = df1.join(df2,\"rn\").drop(\"rn\")","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a PySpark job to remove duplicate records from a large dataset efficiently.","type":"concept","a":"• Simple approach df = df.dropDuplicates() For selected columns df = df.dropDuplicates([\"CustomerID\"]) For large datasets from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number,col windowSpec = Window.partitionBy(\"CustomerID\") \\ .orderBy(col(\"UpdatedDate\").desc()) df = df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec)) df = df.filter(\"rn=1\").drop(\"rn\") This retains the latest record per customer\n• Round 2 – Technical Coding","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Describe a situation where you had to balance delivery speed with data quality","type":"concept","a":"\"I never compromise data accuracy for speed.\" Instead  Deliver critical fields first  Run validations  Automate testing  Release incrementally","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a query to get employees whose salary increased compared to the previous","type":"concept","a":"• year\n• Use the LAG() window function to fetch each employee's salary from the previous year, then filter where the current salary is greater\n• WITH cte AS ( SELECT emp_id, salary, year, LAG(salary, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY emp_id ORDER BY year) AS prev_sal FROM employee ) SELECT * FROM cte WHERE salary > prev_sal;\n• How it works: • PARTITION BY emp_id — computes LAG independently for each employee. • ORDER BY year — ensures LAG looks at the immediately preceding year. • The outer WHERE salary > prev_sal filters only those whose pay went up\n• TRUNCATE vs DELETE vs DROP","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between TRUNCATE, DELETE, and DROP?","type":"concept","a":"Feature TRUNCATE DELETE DROP Removes Data All rows Selected/all rows Entire table Structure Kept Kept Removed Rollback (TXN) No (DDL) Yes (DML) No (DDL) WHERE clause Not allowed Allowed N/A Triggers fired No Yes No Speed Fastest Slower Instant WHERE Clause vs HAVING Clause","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between GROUP BY and PARTITION BY?","type":"concept","a":"• GROUP BY collapses rows into one row per group — it reduces the result set. • PARTITION BY is used inside window functions (OVER clause) — it divides rows into partitions but DOES NOT reduce the result set; all original rows are returned. Example: -- GROUP BY: returns 1 row per dept SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept; -- PARTITION BY: returns all rows, adds avg per dept as a column SELECT emp_id, dept, salary, AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) AS dept_avg FROM emp;","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"How do you optimize a Mapping Data Flow in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Partitioning wisely — choose Round Robin for uniform distribution; use Hash partition on join keys to reduce data shuffle. • Enable staging — use PolyBase for Synapse sinks to speed up loading. • Push down transformations — filter and select only required columns early in the flow to reduce data volume. • Use Broadcast joins — for small lookup datasets, broadcast the smaller side to avoid shuffle joins. • Increase cluster size (Compute Optimized or Memory Optimized) for heavy transformations. • Avoid unnecessary sorts — sorts are expensive; only sort when required (e.g., for rank or window functions). • Cache sink — use in-memory lookup cache for repeated lookups on the same small dataset. • Monitor with Debug mode first, then use activity run metrics (rows processed, time per stage) to identify bottlenecks. 3. Databricks Standard vs Premium vs Enterprise Tiers","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to get employees that earn more than their managers","type":"concept","a":"• SQL: Employees earning more than their managers SELECT e.emp_id, e.emp_name, e.salary FROM employee e JOIN employee m ON e.manager_id = m.emp_id WHERE e.salary > m.salary;\n• Logic:  Self join on employee table  Compare employee salary with manager salary","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delete duplicate records","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose duplicates are based on: customer_id name email_id order_date SQL WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id ) rn FROM customer ) DELETE FROM customer WHERE (customer_id,name,email_id,order_date) IN ( SELECT customer_id,name,email_id,order_date FROM cte WHERE rn>1 );\n• If using Delta Lake, a more typical approach is: CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id ) rn FROM customer ) WHERE rn = 1;\n• PySpark from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import row_number window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy( \"customer_id\", \"name\", \"email_id\", \"order_date\" ).orderBy(\"customer_id\") df = df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(window)) df = df.filter(\"rn=1\").drop(\"rn\")","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Find the most frequently occurring item for each date.","type":"concept","a":"• -- schema CREATE TABLE Items ( Date date NOT NULL, Item INT NOT NULL ); -- data INSERT INTO Items (Date, Item) VALUES ('2022-09-01', 1), ('2022-09-01', 3), ('2022-09-01', 2), ('2022-09-01', 6), ('2022-09-01', 1), ('2022-09-02', 2), ('2022-09-02', 2), ('2022-09-02', 4) ;\n• Use this query: WITH Item_CTE AS ( SELECT Date, Item, COUNT(*) AS Cnt FROM Items GROUP BY Date, Item ), Rank_Item AS ( SELECT Date, Item, Cnt, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY Date ORDER BY Cnt DESC ) AS Rank_I FROM Item_CTE ) SELECT Date, Item, Cnt FROM Rank_Item WHERE Rank_I = 1;\n• Output Date Item Cnt 2022-09-01 1 2 2022-09-02 2 2","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Write a func(cid:415)on that takes in an array of at least three integers and, without sor(cid:415)ng the","type":"concept","a":"• input array, returns a sorted array of the three largest integers in the input array\n• The func(cid:415)on should return duplicate integers if necessary; for example, it should return [10, 10, 12] for an input array of [10, 5, 9, 10, 12]. def find_three_largest_numbers(array): first = second = third = float('-inf') for num in array: if num > first: third = second second = first first = num elif num > second: third = second second = num elif num > third: third = num return [third, second, first] (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Example arr = [10, 5, 9, 10, 12] print(find_three_largest_numbers(arr)) Output: [10, 10, 12] How it works For each number:  If it's bigger than the largest → shi(cid:332) all down  Else if bigger than second → shi(cid:332) second and third  Else if bigger than third → update third Round 2: SQL Ques(cid:415)ons","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Return Top 3 User_id","type":"concept","a":"• Transac(cid:415)on Table: transac(cid:415)on_id,user_id , date;\n• Answer: SELECT user_id, cnt FROM ( SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS cnt, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id ) t WHERE rnk <= 3;\n• OR If interviewer wants strictly top 3 users (no (cid:415)es): SELECT user_id, cnt FROM ( SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS cnt, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id ) t WHERE rnk <= 3;","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Compare transac(cid:415)on counts per user between consecu(cid:415)ve dates and find users whose","type":"concept","a":"ac(cid:415)vity increased” Step 1: Aggregate WITH cte AS ( SELECT user_id, date, COUNT(transac(cid:415)on_id) AS cnt FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id, date ) Step 2: Compare with previous day SELECT c2.user_id FROM cte c1 JOIN cte c2 ON c1.user_id = c2.user_id AND c2.date = DATE_ADD(c1.date, 1) WHERE c2.cnt > c1.cnt; (cid:55982)(cid:55983)(cid:55984) Explana(cid:415)on  Compare same user across 2 consecu(cid:415)ve days  Check if today's transac(cid:415)ons > yesterday Pyspark : from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"user_id\").orderBy(\"date\") df_agg = df.groupBy(\"user_id\", \"date\") \\ .agg(F.count(\"transac(cid:415)on_id\").alias(\"cnt\")) df_final = df_agg.withColumn(\"prev_cnt\", F.lag(\"cnt\").over(w)) result = df_final.filter(F.col(\"cnt\") > F.col(\"prev_cnt\")) \\ .select(\"user_id\", \"date\", \"cnt\", \"prev_cnt\") result.show()","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Split comma-separated sports into rows and group by Sport","type":"concept","a":"Input Name Sport Alice Badminton, Tennis Greg Cricket, Baseball Julie Swimming, Basket ball Alan Tennis, Swimming Xian Badminton, Baseball Expected Output Name Sport Alice Badminton Xian Badminton Alice Tennis Alan Tennis Greg Baseball Xian Baseball Julie Swimming Alan Swimming Greg Cricket Julie Basket ball PySpark Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql import func(cid:415)ons as F # Split the sports into an array df1 = df.withColumn(\"Sport\", F.split(F.col(\"Sport\"), \",\\\\s*\")) # Convert array into mul(cid:415)ple rows df2 = df1.withColumn(\"Sport\", F.explode(\"Sport\")) # Sort by Sport (op(cid:415)onal, to match expected output) result = df2.orderBy(\"Sport\") result.show(truncate=False) Func(cid:415)ons used:  split()  explode()  orderBy()","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Group values into an array","type":"concept","a":"Input Col1 Col2 Col3 a aa 1 a aa 2 b bb 5 b bb 3 b bb 4 Expected Output Col1 Col2 Col3 a aa [1,2] b bb [5,3,4] Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_list result = df.groupBy(\"Col1\",\"Col2\") \\ .agg(collect_list(\"Col3\").alias(\"Col3\")) result.show(truncate=False) If duplicate values should be removed: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_set result = df.groupBy(\"Col1\",\"Col2\") \\ .agg(collect_set(\"Col3\").alias(\"Col3\"))","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark: 1st & 2nd Highest Salary Per Department","type":"concept","a":"• Window Function with dense_rank() — Recommended from pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window data = [('Alice', 'Engineering', 90000), ('Bob', 'Engineering', 85000), ('Carol', 'Engineering', 85000), ('Dave', 'Marketing', 70000), ('Eve', 'Marketing', 65000)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['name', 'dept', 'salary']) window = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) df_ranked = df.withColumn('rank', dense_rank().over(window)) result = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') <= 2) result.show() Function Behavior on Ties Output Example rank() Skips ranks after ties 1, 1, 3, 4 dense_rank() No gaps after ties 1, 1, 2, 3 row_number() Always unique — no ties 1, 2, 3, 4 Use dense_rank() for Top-N salary — handles tied salaries without skipping ranks\n• SQL Approach df.createOrReplaceTempView('employees') spark.sql(''' SELECT name, dept, salary, dense_rank() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees ''').filter('rnk <= 2').show() Page 6 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Ques(cid:415)on Based on Window Func(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• Find latest record for every employee SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EmpID ORDER BY UpdatedDate DESC) rn FROM Employee )t WHERE rn=1;\n• Find second highest salary SELECT * FROM ( SELECT Salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rnk FROM Employee )t WHERE rnk=2;\n• Running Total SELECT OrderDate, Amount, SUM(Amount) OVER(ORDER BY OrderDate) AS RunningTotal FROM Orders;","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ROW_NUMBER(), RANK(), DENSE_RANK()","type":"concept","a":"Func(cid:415)on Duplicate Rank Gap ROW_NUMBER No No RANK Yes Yes DENSE_RANK Yes No","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Wide Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"•  Requires shuffle across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons.  More expensive\n• Examples:  groupBy()  join()  dis(cid:415)nct()  reduceByKey()  orderBy() Narrow transforma(cid:415)ons don't require data movement across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, whereas wide transforma(cid:415)ons involve shuffle, making them more expensive","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"For incremental loads, use:","type":"concept","a":"• o Watermark column (LastModifiedDate) o SQL query with dynamic parameters o Store the last processed watermark in a control table\n• Example SQL: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE ModifiedDate > @LastWatermark A(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on, update the watermark","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Ques(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Table: Stadium +---------------+---------+ | Column Name | Type | +---------------+---------+ | id | int | | visit_date | date | | people | int | +---------------+---------+ visit_date is the column with unique values for this table\n• Each row of this table contains the visit date and visit id to the stadium with the number of people during the visit\n• As the id increases, the date increases as well\n• Write a solu(cid:415)on to display the records with three or more rows with consecu(cid:415)ve id's, and the number of people is greater than or equal to 100 for each\n• Return the result table ordered by visit_date in ascending order\n• The result format is in the following example\n• Input: Stadium table: +------+------------+-----------+ | id | visit_date | people | +------+------------+-----------+ | 1 | 2017-01-01 | 10 | | 2 | 2017-01-02 | 109 | | 3 | 2017-01-03 | 150 | | 4 | 2017-01-04 | 99 | | 5 | 2017-01-05 | 145 | | 6 | 2017-01-06 | 1455 | | 7 | 2017-01-07 | 199 | | 8 | 2017-01-09 | 188 | Output: +------+------------+-----------+ | id | visit_date | people | +------+------------+-----------+ | 5 | 2017-01-05 | 145 | | 6 | 2017-01-06 | 1455 | | 7 | 2017-01-07 | 199 | | 8 | 2017-01-09 | 188 | Explana(cid:415)on: The four rows with ids 5, 6, 7, and 8 have consecu(cid:415)ve ids and each of them has >= 100 people a(cid:425)ended\n• Note that row 8 was included even though the visit_date was not the next day a(cid:332)er row 7\n• The rows with ids 2 and 3 are not included because we need at least three consecu(cid:415)ve ids\n• Answer: Approach","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Filter rows where people >= 100.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Return only groups having 3 or more consecu(cid:415)ve rows.","type":"concept","a":"• SQL Solu(cid:415)on WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id) AS grp FROM Stadium WHERE people >= 100 ), grp_count AS ( SELECT grp, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM cte GROUP BY grp HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3 ) SELECT c.id, c.visit_date, c.people FROM cte c JOIN grp_count g ON c.grp = g.grp ORDER BY c.visit_date;\n• Explana(cid:415)on Given the filtered data (people >= 100): id people ROW_NUMBER id - ROW_NUMBER 2 109 1 1 3 150 2 1 5 145 3 2 6 1455 4 2 7 199 5 2 8 188 6 2 No(cid:415)ce:  IDs 2, 3 have the same group (1) but only 2 rows, so they are excluded.  IDs 5, 6, 7, 8 have the same group (2) and 4 rows, so they are included\n• Output id visit_date people 5 2017-01-05 145 6 2017-01-06 1455 7 2017-01-07 199 8 2017-01-09 188 Client Round 2 :","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If Copy Ac(cid:415)vity is very slow, how will you op(cid:415)mize it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would first iden(cid:415)fy whether the bo(cid:425)leneck is the source, network, or sink\n• Then I would:  Increase the Degree of Copy Parallelism.  Enable parallel copy where supported.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on the source data.  Use staging for large data transfers.  Op(cid:415)mize the Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me size.  Filter unnecessary records.  Copy mul(cid:415)ple files in parallel.  Ensure source and sink indexes are appropriate.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What challenges did you face while doing transforma(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":"• \"One common challenge was handling duplicate records and inconsistent source data.\" Examples:  Duplicate records.  Null or invalid values.  Schema changes.  Large joins causing performance issues.  Data type mismatches\n• Solu(cid:415)ons:  Used dropDuplicates() or SQL window func(cid:415)ons.  Applied null handling and valida(cid:415)on rules.  Used par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and op(cid:415)mized joins.  Used Delta Lake features like MERGE for incremental updates","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Are you connec(cid:415)ng only with the SQL Server team or also with businesspeople?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I worked with both technical and business teams\n• I collaborated with the SQL Server team to understand the source schema, data availability, and any schema changes\n• I also interacted with business analysts and repor(cid:415)ng teams to clarify business rules, validate transformed data, and ensure the reports met their requirements.\" This demonstrates cross-func(cid:415)onal communica(cid:415)on skills","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why Do We Convert CSV Files to Parquet?","type":"concept","a":"• Feature CSV Parquet Storage format Row-based Columnar (each column stored separately) Compression None or minimal Snappy/GZIP — 75-90% smaller typically Schema No schema (inferred at read) Schema embedded in file footer Query speed Reads ALL columns even if Column pruning — reads only needed unused columns Predicate pushdown Not supported Row groups skipped if outside filter range Splittable Only with separate index Yes — parallel reads across tasks Null handling String representation Efficient native null encoding Nested types Flat only Supports nested/complex types natively Rule of thumb: A 10 GB CSV file becomes 1-3 GB Parquet (Snappy)\n• Queries run 5-20x faster on Parquet because only queried columns are read from disk\n• Always convert raw CSV to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Section 4 | Data Processing","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Remove Duplicates from a Table?","type":"concept","a":"Method 1 — dropDuplicates() df_clean = df.dropDuplicates() # all columns df_clean = df.dropDuplicates(['order_id','cust_id']) # key columns only Method 2 — distinct() df_clean = df.distinct() # removes fully identical rows Method 3 — Window row_number() — keep latest record from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.partitionBy('order_id').orderBy(col('updated_at').desc()) df_dedup = df.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter(col('rn')==1).drop('rn') Method 4 — Delta MERGE INTO (upsert on load) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Ways Can You Handle Duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• Method When to Use Key Consideration dropDuplicates() In-memory DataFrame dedup Specify key columns or all columns distinct() Exact row-level duplicates All columns must match Window row_number() Keep latest/earliest record per Partition by key, order by timestamp key Window dense_rank() Rank-based dedup with ties Use when ties should be preserved allowed Delta MERGE INTO Incremental load — avoid dupes Best for production pipelines at ingest SQL ROW_NUMBER() SQL-based in Spark SQL PARTITION BY key ORDER BY ts DESC GROUP BY + aggregation Collapse to summary rows Loses individual row detail Best Practice for production: Use MERGE INTO for Delta tables\n• It handles both new inserts and updates in one atomic operation, preventing duplicates at the table level without reprocessing old data\n• Page 6 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Section 5 | SQL / Transformation","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Calculate Male Count, Female Count, and Total Count per City","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — Conditional Aggregation (SQL) SELECT city, COUNT(CASE WHEN gender = 'Male' THEN 1 END) AS male_count, COUNT(CASE WHEN gender = 'Female' THEN 1 END) AS female_count, COUNT(*) AS total_count FROM people GROUP BY city ORDER BY city;\n• Method 2 — PIVOT (Spark SQL) SELECT city, Male AS male_count, Female AS female_count FROM (SELECT city, gender FROM people) PIVOT (COUNT(gender) FOR gender IN ('Male', 'Female')) ORDER BY city;\n• Method 3 — PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import count, when, col result = df.groupBy('city').agg( count(when(col('gender')=='Male', 1)).alias('male_count'), count(when(col('gender')=='Female', 1)).alias('female_count'), count('*').alias('total_count') ).orderBy('city') City male_count female_count total_count Bangalore 120 95 215 Chennai 80 110 190 Mumbai 200 180 380 Section 6 | Databricks Concepts","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Views Exist in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"View Type Persistence Data Description Stored? Temporary View Session only (lost on No createOrReplaceTempView(); only in restart) current SparkSession Global Temp View App lifetime (all No createOrReplaceGlobalTempView(); sessions) access via global_temp.view_name Standard View Metastore (permanent) No CREATE VIEW; saved in Hive/Unity metastore; no data copy Materialized View Metastore (permanent) Yes Pre-computed Delta table; refreshed on schedule or trigger df.createOrReplaceTempView('temp_v') df.createOrReplaceGlobalTempView('global_v') spark.sql('SELECT * FROM global_temp.global_v') CREATE VIEW gold.customer_summary AS SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY 1;","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Display the fla(cid:425)ened DataFrame.","type":"concept","a":"• (cid:57859) Expected Output +--------+-----+--------+ |product |price|quan(cid:415)ty| +--------+-----+--------+ |product1| 100| 2| |product2| 200| 3| +--------+-----+--------+ SQL:- (cid:57874)(cid:57875) SQL Interview Ques(cid:415)on: Running Total of Sales per Store You are given a table named sales with the following structure: sales ------ store_id VARCHAR sale_date DATE sales_amount INT Sample Data store_id sale_date sales_amount S1 2025-01-01 100 S1 2025-01-02 150 S1 2025-01-03 200 S2 2025-01-01 80 S2 2025-01-02 120 S2 2025-01-03 160 (cid:44472)(cid:44473)(cid:44474)(cid:44475) Problem Statement Write an SQL query to calculate the running total of sales for each store, ordered by sale_date\n• Requirements  The running total should reset for each store  The results should be ordered by store_id and sale_date  Use window func(cid:415)ons (cid:33158)(cid:57393) Expected Output store_id sale_date sales_amount running_total S1 2025-01-01 100 100 S1 2025-01-02 150 250 S1 2025-01-03 200 450 S2 2025-01-01 80 80 S2 2025-01-02 120 200 S2 2025-01-03 160 360 Q4 You are given a table named orders with the following structure: orders ------ customer_id INT order_id INT order_date DATE order_amount INT Sample Data customer_id order_id order_date order_amount 1 101 2025-01-01 100 1 102 2025-01-05 150 1 103 2025-01-10 200 2 201 2025-01-02 120 2 202 2025-01-06 110 3 301 2025-01-03 50 3 302 2025-01-07 80 (cid:44472)(cid:44473)(cid:44474)(cid:44475) Problem Statement Write an SQL query to find customers whose every order value is strictly higher than their previous order, ordered by order_date\n• Requirements  Orders must be compared chronologically  Customers with only one order should be considered valid  Use window func(cid:415)ons  Return dis(cid:415)nct customer IDs (cid:33158)(cid:57393) Expected Output customer_id 1 3","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques","type":"concept","a":" Create appropriate indexes.  Avoid SELECT *.  Filter early with WHERE.  Op(cid:415)mize joins.  Use execu(cid:415)on plans.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on large tables.  Use EXISTS instead of IN when appropriate.  Keep sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs updated.  Avoid unnecessary func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Two large tables. How will you join?","type":"concept","a":"• Typically use a Sort Merge Join because it scales well for large datasets\n• Also consider:  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on both tables on the join key.  Handle data skew if present.  Enable AQE for run(cid:415)me op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons\n• SQL Indexing Types:  Clustered  Non-Clustered  Composite  Unique  Filtered  Full-Text 16\n• SQL Problem Example: Customers with no orders SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL;","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Window Func(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Examples: ROW_NUMBER() RANK() DENSE_RANK() LAG() LEAD() NTILE() FIRST_VALUE() LAST_VALUE() Example: SELECT employee, salary, RANK() OVER(ORDER BY salary DESC) FROM Employee;","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Experience on NoSQL DB","type":"concept","a":"If you have experience: \"Yes, I have worked with MongoDB/Cosmos DB for storing semi-structured JSON data and reading it into Databricks for downstream processing.\" If not: \"I haven't worked extensively with NoSQL databases in produc(cid:415)on, but I understand they are used for schema-flexible, high-volume data such as JSON documents and key-value workloads.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Why use sets?","type":"concept","a":"•  Remove duplicates automa(cid:415)cally.  Fast membership checks (O(1) average (cid:415)me).  Perform set opera(cid:415)ons like union, intersec(cid:415)on, and difference\n• Example: nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4] unique_nums = set(nums) print(unique_nums) Output: {1, 2, 3, 4}","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is LAST_VALUE() Window Func(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"• It returns the last value within a window\n• Example Table Emp Salary A 2000 B 4000 C 6000 Query LAST_VALUE(salary) OVER( ORDER BY salary ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING ) Output Emp Last Salary A 6000 B 6000 C 6000 Without UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING Spark considers rows only up to the current row, so the result can be different\n• PySpark from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import last window = Window.orderBy(\"salary\") \\ .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.unboundedFollowing) df.withColumn(\"last_salary\", last(\"salary\").over(window))","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"For each customer, find the second and third highest order amount in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Data Customer Order A 500 A 700 A 900 A 300 Customer Order B 100 B 600 B 400 Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import dense_rank window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"customer\") \\ .orderBy(col(\"order_amount\").desc()) result = df.withColumn( \"rank\", dense_rank().over(window) ).filter(\"rank IN (2,3)\") result.show() Output Customer Order Rank A 700 2 A 500 3 B 400 2 B 100 3 If duplicate order amounts should receive unique rankings, use row_number() instead of dense_rank()\n• If you want gaps in ranking a(cid:332)er (cid:415)es, use rank()","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find matched and unmatched records between two tables in SQL","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose: Table A ID 1 2 3 Table B ID 2 3 4 Matched Records SELECT a.* FROM tableA a INNER JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id;\n• Output 2 3 Unmatched from Table A SELECT a.* FROM tableA a LEFT JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id WHERE b.id IS NULL;\n• Output 1 Unmatched from Table B SELECT b.* FROM tableB b LEFT JOIN tableA a ON b.id = a.id WHERE a.id IS NULL;\n• Output 4 All Matched and Unmatched (Full Outer Join) SELECT a.id AS tableA_id, b.id AS tableB_id FROM tableA a FULL OUTER JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id;\n• This returns matched rows along with records present only in either table","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Remove duplicate emails.","type":"concept","a":"Example ID Email 1 abc@gmail.com 2 abc@gmail.com 3 xyz@gmail.com SQL WITH CTE AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY Email ORDER BY ID ) rn FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM CTE WHERE rn > 1;","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Complex SQL Join","type":"concept","a":"• Typical interview ques(cid:415)on: Find department-wise total salary\n• SELECT d.DepartmentName, SUM(e.Salary) AS TotalSalary FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.DepartmentID=d.DepartmentID GROUP BY d.DepartmentName;\n• Client Round 3","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Find maximum salary per department name","type":"concept","a":"• Tables:  Employee(emp_id, emp_name, dept_id, salary)  Department(dept_id, dept_name) Answer SELECT d.dept_name, MAX(e.salary) AS max_salary FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id GROUP BY d.dept_name;\n• What if we want employee details also (name, id)?” Then use window func(cid:415)on: WITH ranked AS ( SELECT e.emp_id, e.emp_name, d.dept_name, e.salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC ) AS rnk FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id ) SELECT emp_id, emp_name, dept_name, salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk = 1;\n• Difference (important interview point) Method Output GROUP BY + MAX only salary per department DENSE_RANK full employee details with max salary","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How do you remove duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EMP_ID ORDER BY EMP_ID) AS rn FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n• Round 2:","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Find customers who have transac(cid:415)ons on more than 1 different date.","type":"concept","a":"Table: id, transac(cid:415)on_id, transaca(cid:415)on_date(cid:415)me , customer_id, product_id, net_qty, amount, transaca(cid:415)on_date SELECT customer_id FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT transac(cid:415)on_date) > 1;","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to calculate the total net quan(cid:415)ty for each product and rank the","type":"concept","a":"products based on total sales quan(cid:415)ty.” product_id net_qty 1 5 Answer: WITH CTE_PRODUCT AS ( SELECT product_id, SUM(net_qty) AS total_qty FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY product_id ) SELECT product_id, total_qty, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total_qty DESC) AS rnk_prod FROM CTE_PRODUCT;","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is DQ (Data Quality)?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Quality (DQ) refers to the degree to which data is fit for its intended use\n• Key dimensions: • Completeness: No missing/null values where required • Accuracy: Data correctly represents real-world values • Consistency: Same data looks the same across all systems • Timeliness: Data is available when needed • Uniqueness: No duplicate records • Validity: Data conforms to expected formats and rules DQ Checks in Databricks: # Using Delta Live Tables expectations @dlt.expect('valid_age', 'age >= 0 AND age <= 120') @dlt.expect_or_drop('not_null_id', 'customer_id IS NOT NULL') # Using Great Expectations or custom PySpark checks df.filter(col('customer_id').isNull()).count() # Should be 0","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize SQL Queries","type":"concept","a":"• Key SQL Optimization Techniques: • 1\n• Use WHERE early: Filter data as early as possible • 2\n• Avoid SELECT *: Only select needed columns • 3\n• Use proper JOIN type: INNER > LEFT OUTER > CROSS • 4\n• Index usage: Ensure joins/filters use indexed columns • 5\n• Partition pruning: Filter on partition columns • 6\n• Use CTEs / temp tables: Avoid repeating complex subqueries • 7\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE: f(col) = val kills indexes • 8\n• EXPLAIN PLAN: Analyze execution plan before running • 9\n• Use BROADCAST hint for small table joins • 10\n• Avoid DISTINCT when not needed — use GROUP BY -- Bad: Function on column disables partition pruning WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025 -- Good: Range filter enables partition pruning WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31' -- Broadcast hint for small table SELECT /*+ BROADCAST(d) */ f.*, d.product_name FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product d ON f.product_key = d.product_key SECTION 6: Coding Questions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark — Highest Salary per Designation","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, max as spark_max from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import rank spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('HighestSalary').getOrCreate() # Sample data data = [('Alice', 'Manager', 90000), ('Bob', 'Manager', 85000), ('Charlie', 'Analyst', 60000), ('Dave', 'Analyst', 62000), ('Eve', 'Director', 120000)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['name', 'designation', 'salary']) # Method 1: Window Function (RANK) window_spec = Window.partitionBy('designation').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) result = df.withColumn('rnk', rank().over(window_spec)) \\ .filter(col('rnk') == 1) \\ .select('name', 'designation', 'salary') result.show() # Method 2: Self-join with max salary max_sal = df.groupBy('designation').agg(spark_max('salary').alias('max_salary')) result2 = df.join(max_sal, (df.designation == max_sal.designation) & (df.salary == max_sal.max_salary)) \\ .select(df.name, df.designation, df.salary) result2.show()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"SQL — Revenue, Common Customers, Consecutive Months","type":"concept","a":"Setup: orders(order_id, customer_id, order_date, amount)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Total Revenue per Month","type":"concept","a":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM') AS year_month, SUM(amount) AS total_revenue FROM orders GROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM') ORDER BY year_month;","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Customers who Ordered in Both January AND February 2025","type":"concept","a":"SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28' AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1, 2) GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2; -- Alternative using INTERSECT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01' INTERSECT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Customers who Ordered in Three Consecutive Months","type":"concept","a":"WITH monthly_orders AS ( SELECT customer_id, DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM-01') AS order_month FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM-01') ), with_lag AS ( SELECT customer_id, order_month, LAG(order_month, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_month) AS prev_month, LAG(order_month, 2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_month) AS prev2_month FROM monthly_orders ) SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM with_lag WHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2_month, 1) = prev_month AND ADD_MONTHS(prev_month, 1) = order_month;","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Sql ques(cid:415)ons for 2nd highest salary and also find 4th highest salary","type":"concept","a":"WITH ranked AS ( SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employee ) SELECT salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk in( 2 , 4)","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you perform transforma(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"• I perform transforma(cid:415)ons based on business requirements using SQL or PySpark\n• In Silver layer, I clean and standardize data by removing duplicates, handling nulls, and applying joins\n• In Gold layer, I apply business logic and aggrega(cid:415)ons like SUM, COUNT, and CASE statements to prepare data for repor(cid:415)ng\n• I also use window func(cid:415)ons for advanced transforma(cid:415)ons like ranking and deduplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques.","type":"concept","a":"• Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques include reducing data scanned by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, using proper indexing, avoiding func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns, and pushing filters early\n• In distributed systems like Spark, I use broadcast joins, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching, and skew handling techniques\n• At the pipeline level, I implement incremental loads, par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, and use efficient file formats like Parquet or Delta\n• I also analyze execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks and improve performance","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"delta","name":"Delta Lake","icon":"🗄️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Delta Lake / a Delta table and what problems does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that sits on top of Parquet in cloud storage and adds: ACID transactions, a transaction log (_delta_log), schema enforcement & evolution, time travel, UPSERT/MERGE, and unified batch + streaming reads/writes.\nIt brings reliability and warehouse-like guarantees to a data lake (the 'lakehouse'), fixing typical data-lake issues: no ACID, no schema control, slow updates, and small-file problems."},{"q":"Explain ACID properties in Delta Lake and the transaction log.","type":"concept","a":"• Atomicity — a commit fully succeeds or fully fails (no partial writes).\n• Consistency — schema enforcement keeps data valid.\n• Isolation — snapshot isolation for concurrent reads/writes.\n• Durability — committed data persists even after failures.\nThe _delta_log is an ordered set of JSON commits recording every change (add/remove files, metadata, protocol). Each read reconstructs the table state from the log; the log is the source of truth and enables time travel and atomic operations."},{"q":"What are the file types inside the _delta_log folder?","type":"concept","a":"• .json — incremental transaction files, one per commit (AddFile, RemoveFile, metadata, protocol). No actual data.\n• .checkpoint.parquet — a snapshot of table state every ~10 commits so readers don't replay all JSON files.\n• .compacted.json — combines multiple small JSON commits to speed up log reads.\n• .crc — checksum/integrity file for checkpoints; on corruption Spark falls back to the previous valid checkpoint.\nTogether they keep metadata reads fast and reliable."},{"q":"What is Delta table metadata log management and how does it become a bottleneck?","type":"concept","a":"Every commit adds a JSON file to _delta_log; over time this grows and slows query planning. Mitigation:\n• Checkpoints (every 10 commits) collapse state so readers load 1 checkpoint + recent JSONs.\n• delta.logRetentionDuration (default 30 days) — old JSON commits are pruned once a checkpoint exists.\n• Compacted JSON files reduce the number of individual commits.\n• OPTIMIZE compacts the underlying Parquet data files (not the log).\nNote: copying the log elsewhere doesn't fix performance — checkpointing + retention does."},{"q":"How does time travel work in Delta Lake?","type":"concept","a":"Delta keeps every version in the log, so you can query historical snapshots:\n• By version: SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 5 (or option('versionAsOf',5)).\n• By timestamp: SELECT * FROM t TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01'.\n• DESCRIBE HISTORY t shows the commit history (operations, versions, timestamps).\nUse cases: audit, reproduce past results, recover from bad writes (RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF n).\nLimitation: time travel is bounded by VACUUM retention — once old files are removed, those versions are gone."},{"q":"Difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema enforcement (validation) — by default Delta rejects writes whose schema doesn't match the table, protecting data integrity.\n• Schema evolution — you can intentionally allow new/changed columns:\n  – df.write.option('mergeSchema','true').mode('append')\n  – SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true\n  – ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ...\n  – COPY INTO ... COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')\n• overwriteSchema replaces the whole schema (use with caution)."},{"q":"How do you implement MERGE (UPSERT) in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"MERGE matches source to target and conditionally updates/inserts/deletes — the standard upsert pattern for incremental/CDC loads.\nExample:\ntarget.alias('t').merge(incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.id = s.id').whenMatchedUpdateAll().whenNotMatchedInsertAll().execute()\nSQL: MERGE INTO t USING s ON t.id=s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\nSupports whenMatchedDelete, conditional updates, and partial column updates. Requires the join condition to be on key columns for efficiency."},{"q":"What does OPTIMIZE do?","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE compacts many small Parquet files into fewer, larger (~1 GB) files to improve read performance and reduce metadata overhead (fixes the small-file problem).\n• OPTIMIZE table; or OPTIMIZE table WHERE date >= '2026-01-01' (target recent partitions).\n• OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY (col) adds clustering for data skipping.\nRun after bulk loads / daily ETL. Target file size is configurable via delta.optimize.maxFileSize. Auto Optimize can do this automatically."},{"q":"What is Z-ORDER and how does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"Z-ORDER co-locates related rows into the same files by reordering data across multiple columns (multi-dimensional clustering).\n• Delta's data-skipping uses file min/max stats to skip files that can't match a filter; good clustering means fewer files to scan.\n• Best on HIGH-cardinality columns used frequently in WHERE/JOIN (customer_id, transaction_id, date).\n• Syntax: OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date).\n• It's expensive — apply to the most queried columns only. Don't combine with auto compaction."},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compacts small files into bigger files; no sorting; always useful for small-file cleanup.\n• Z-ORDER — does the compaction AND sorts/clusters data by column values within files for data skipping; best for high-cardinality filter columns; requires re-running to stay fresh.\n• Liquid Clustering — dynamic, incremental clustering on chosen keys; Databricks re-clusters new data automatically, no partition maintenance, works with evolving workloads; replaces partition+z-order.\nModern guidance: prefer Liquid Clustering; use OPTIMIZE (with ZORDER) on legacy partitioned tables."},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"Liquid Clustering is a Databricks Delta feature that organizes data by clustering keys without traditional Hive partitioning.\n• Define keys once: ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date) (or CLUSTER BY AUTO).\n• Databricks incrementally re-clusters new data; keys can change without rewriting the whole table.\n• Removes partition-column cardinality pitfalls and small-file issues; improves data skipping.\n• No need to run Z-ORDER manually; useful for evolving/changing query patterns."},{"q":"What is VACUUM and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM permanently deletes data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta log and are past the retention period (default 7 days).\n• Reduces storage cost and scan overhead.\n• VACUUM t RETAIN 168 HOURS; add DRY RUN to see what would be deleted.\n• Warning: vacuuming deletes old versions, so you LOSE time travel beyond the retention. Lowering retention below 7 days requires disabling the safety check (risky — can corrupt concurrent readers)."},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed (CDF) and how does it differ from CDC?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF (Delta feature) — records row-level changes (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) in a Delta table so downstream can read changes incrementally.\n  Enable: ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed=true); read with readChangeFeed=true + startingVersion.\n  Extra columns: _change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp.\n• CDC — the broader process of capturing INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from a SOURCE system (via log-based tools like Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, triggers, or timestamp polling).\nIn short: CDC captures changes at the source; CDF propagates Delta table changes downstream. CDF is a Delta-native way to consume changes."},{"q":"What are Deletion Vectors?","type":"concept","a":"Deletion Vectors mark deleted rows in a bitmap rather than immediately rewriting the entire Parquet file.\n• Makes DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE much faster (avoids rewriting whole files).\n• Readers transparently respect the deletion vector; compacted later by OPTIMIZE.\n• Enable via table property delta.enableDeletionVectors=true. Trade-off: slight read overhead until optimized."},{"q":"Difference between DEEP CLONE and SHALLOW CLONE?","type":"concept","a":"• DEEP CLONE — copies the data files AND the metadata/history to a new location; fully independent; supports time travel on the clone; used for migration/backup/experimentation.\n• SHALLOW CLONE — copies only the metadata and references the original data files (no data copy); fast/cheap; if the source files are vacuumed or changed, the clone breaks (depends on source).\nUse deep clone for independent copies; shallow clone for quick test/dev without data duplication.\nSyntax: CREATE TABLE dest DEEP|SHALLOW CLONE source."},{"q":"Difference between managed (internal) and external tables in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — Databricks manages the storage location and lifecycle; DROP TABLE deletes BOTH metadata AND data files. Default format is Delta. Best for lakehouse/DW tables.\n• External table — you specify the storage location (ADLS/S3); DROP TABLE deletes only metadata, data files REMAIN. Shared across tools.\nProduction rule: use external tables (or Unity Catalog managed with careful governance) on ADLS so a drop can't accidentally destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move/copy a Delta table to another location. What options do you have?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read + write new location: spark.read.format('delta').load(src).write.format('delta').save(dest).\n• DEEP CLONE — preserves data + history/metadata to a new path (independent).\n• SHALLOW CLONE — metadata-only, references original files (fast/cheap).\n• Export to Parquet/CSV for external consumption.\nChoose deep clone for a full copy, shallow clone for a quick reference."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A managed table was dropped and, after the retention period, the files were permanently removed. Can you recreate it?","type":"scenario","a":"No — not from Delta alone. Time travel and RESTORE depend on the transaction log AND the underlying data files still existing. Once both are permanently deleted, recovery options are:\n• Restore from backup/DR copy.\n• Reload from the source system and reprocess.\n• Recover via cloud storage versioning/soft-delete if enabled.\nLesson: use external tables (or backups) in production so a DROP doesn't destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The CRC file is corrupted. What happens?","type":"scenario","a":"• The .crc file validates checkpoint integrity. If the CRC check fails, Spark gracefully falls back to the PREVIOUS valid checkpoint and uses the recent JSON commits to rebuild current state.\n• So reads typically still succeed — the corrupted checkpoint is simply skipped.\n• Fix root cause (e.g., partial/interrupted write). Delta is resilient because the log + checkpoints are redundant; you can also force a new checkpoint with OPTIMIZE or Spark actions."},{"q":"What are Delta table optimization techniques?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compact small files.\n• Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering — co-locate related data for data skipping.\n• Partitioning — on low-cardinality columns (date/region) where partitions are ~1 GB+.\n• Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) — automatic file sizing.\n• VACUUM — remove stale files.\n• Deletion Vectors — fast deletes/updates.\n• Predictive Optimization (UC) — automatic maintenance.\n• Right file size (~128 MB–1 GB), avoid over-partitioning, use Photon + AQE, and keep statistics for data skipping."},{"q":"What is the small-file problem and how do you fix it in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"Streaming/micro-batch or many small writes create thousands of tiny files, causing high metadata/listing overhead and slow reads.\nFix:\n• OPTIMIZE to compact into larger files; run on a schedule.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) for automatic compaction.\n• Use trigger(availableNow=True) or larger micro-batches in streaming; consider foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy.\n• Set a target file size and coalesce before writes."},{"q":"How do COPY INTO and Auto Loader differ for ingestion?","type":"concept","a":"• COPY INTO — SQL-based, idempotent batch ingestion of files from cloud storage into a Delta table; tracks already-loaded files; good for scheduled/batch loads.\n• Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — Structured Streaming source that incrementally and continuously discovers new files (file notification or directory listing); scalable, handles schema inference/evolution and rescued data; best for high-volume, near-real-time ingestion.\nUse COPY INTO for simple batch; Auto Loader for continuous/scalable incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is Time Travel? What is the reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• Time Travel allows querying previous versions of a Delta table\n• Example: SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5; or SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01';\n• Default reten(cid:415)on:  Transac(cid:415)on Log = 30 days  Deleted data (VACUUM) reten(cid:415)on = 7 days","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore a table beyond the default reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• If the data has already been removed by VACUUM, it cannot be restored using Delta Time Travel because the underlying files have been permanently deleted\n• Possible recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from an ADLS backup or snapshot.  Recreate the table from the original source.  Use an external backup or disaster recovery solu(cid:415)on\n• If the files are s(cid:415)ll available (for example, because VACUUM has not removed them), you can restore with: RESTORE TABLE sales TO VERSION AS OF 10; or RESTORE TABLE sales TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-05-01';","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you increase reten(cid:415)on period for one table?","type":"concept","a":"• Example: ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.deletedFileReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='30 days' ); or ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.logReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='90 days' );\n• This changes reten(cid:415)on only for that specific Delta table","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE Over (cid:415)me, many small files can be created due to frequent writes\n• OPTIMIZE compacts these small files into fewer, larger files\n• Example: Before: 1000 files × 5 MB A(cid:332)er: 10 files × 500 MB OPTIMIZE sales;\n• Benefits:  Fewer files to open.  Faster reads.  Be(cid:425)er query performance\n• Z-ORDER ZORDER physically reorganizes data within the op(cid:415)mized files so that rows with similar values are stored close together\n• OPTIMIZE sales ZORDER BY (customer_id);\n• If you frequently run: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE customer_id = 101;\n• Z-Ordering helps Spark read only the relevant por(cid:415)ons of the data, reducing the amount scanned\n• Difference between OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Compacts small files into larger ones Reorders data within files based on specified columns Reduces file management overhead Improves data skipping for filtered queries Improves general read performance Improves queries filtering on Z-Ordered columns Can be run alone Used as part of OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER BY (...)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delta Lake Architecture","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE, ZORDER & VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Time Travel in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Why Is This Useful?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Case Example Debugging A bug caused wrong data to be written yesterday — query v10 to see the clean state Rollback Accidentally deleted rows — restore from a previous version Auditing Regulatory requirement to show what data looked like on Jan 1st Reproducibility ML model trained on v5 of the data — reproduce by reading that version Time Travel Code # ── Query by VERSION NUMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────── # SQL spark.sql('SELECT * FROM silver.orders VERSION AS OF 5') # Python df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', '5') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── Query by TIMESTAMP ──────────────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM silver.orders TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-15 10:00:00'\") df_yesterday = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── See all versions (history) ──────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql('DESCRIBE HISTORY silver.orders').show(truncate=False) # Shows: version, timestamp, operation (WRITE/DELETE/MERGE), user, etc. # ── RESTORE a table to a previous version ──────────────────────────── spark.sql('RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 3') # Or by timestamp: spark.sql(\"RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-14'\") ⚠️ Time Travel Limitation Time travel only works as long as the old Parquet files exist\n• Running VACUUM deletes those files permanently\n• Default VACUUM retention = 7 days → time travel available for the last 7 days by default\n• Increase retention period if you need longer time travel: ALTER TABLE \n• SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days')","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• Azure Data bricks is a data analytics platform optimized for the Microsoft Azure cloud services platform\n• Azure Data bricks offers three environments for developing data intensive applications: Data bricks SQL, Data bricks Data Science & Engineering, and Data bricks Machine Learning","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of clusters in Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ Single Node: Doesn’t have any worker nodes and recommended for the single user cluster the small data volume. ➢ Standard: Recommend for a Single user cluster and can run SQL, Python, R, SCALA workload . ➢ High Concurrency: Optimized to run concurrency SQL, Python, R workloads rund and scala is not supported.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is delta Lake, How to create delta lake tables","type":"concept","a":"• Delta lake is an open-source data format that provides ACID transactions, data reliability, query performance, data caching and indexing, and many other benefits\n• Delta lake can be thought of as an extension of existing data lakes and can be configured per the data requirements","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Difference Between Data Lake and Delta lake","type":"concept","a":"Page | 15 Page | 16 =============AZURE DATA BRICKS TABLES AND SYNTAX==================== https://sparkbyexamples.com/pyspark/pyspark-withcolumn/ --- This link is reference of any commands CREATE A DATAFRAME BY USING A SPARK It means that Output will be visual in Table Syntax : DF=spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema=colunm) Joins: There is a Different joins like Inner, Left Join(left outer, left semi), Right Outer Join, Semi Table Syntax: emp = [(1,\"Smith\",-1,\"2018\",\"10\",\"M\",3000), \\ (2,\"Rose\",1,\"2010\",\"20\",\"M\",4000), \\ (3,\"Williams\",1,\"2010\",\"10\",\"M\",1000), \\ (4,\"Jones\",2,\"2005\",\"10\",\"F\",2000), \\ (5,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"40\",\"\",-1), \\ (6,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"50\",\"\",-1) \\ ] empColumns = [\"emp_id\",\"name\",\"superior_emp_id\",\"year_joined\", \\ \"emp_dept_id\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] empDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=emp, schema = empColumns) empDF.show() --- Creating a Data Frame dept = [(\"Finance\",10), \\ (\"Marketing\",20), \\ (\"Sales\",30), \\ (\"IT\",40) \\ ] deptColumns = [\"dept_name\",\"dept_id\"] deptDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=dept, schema = deptColumns) deptDF.show() ---- Creating a Data Frame and show Syntax: Page | 17 empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"Inner\" ).show() --- it is a Inner join empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"leftsemi\" ).show() -- it is a Left join SELFJOIN from pyspark.sql.functions import col --- (If col is not define on that we define this is statement) empDF.alias(\"emp1\").join(empDF.alias(\"emp2\"), \\ col(\"emp1.superior_emp_id\") == col(\"emp2.emp_id\"),\"inner\") \\ .select ((\"emp1.emp_id\"), (\"emp1.name\"), \\ col(\"emp2.emp_id\").alias(\"superid\"), \\ col(\"emp2.name\").alias(\"supername\")).show() WTIH COLUMN It menas to define a New colunm and add a any expression like add, multiple etc\\ Table: data = [('James','','Smith','1991-04-01','M',3000), ('Michael','Rose','','2000-05-19','M',4000), ('Robert','','Williams','1978-09-05','M',4000), ('Maria','Anne','Jones','1967-12-01','F',4000), ('Jen','Mary','Brown','1980-02-17','F',-1) ] columns = [\"firstname\",\"middlename\",\"lastname\",\"dob\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] from pyspark.sql import SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SparkByExamples.com').getOrCreate() df = spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema = columns) df.show() Syntax : df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show() HOW TO LOAD THE IN DATA BRICKS Go to Data and click on create table and Upload the data Syntax: loaddf=spark.read.parquet(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.parquet\") DROP THE COLUNM Syntax: userdf=loaddf.drop(\"ip_address,cc\") Page | 18 CONVERT THE PARQUET TO CSV FILE Syntax: csvdf=userdf.write.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) -- it will convert and store a New file usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) --- it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe usercsvdf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"user\") --- it will convert the filename into Table name %sql --- it is important because there is a default is python, so by using a SQl Command to use (if R language %R) select * from user MOUNT FROM DATA LAKE GEN2 TO DATABRICKS ➢ create a data lake gen2 and upload a parquet file ➢ create a Azure Active Dictory and create a New App Registration and also create a new cerfitications and secret ➢ Remember three things to copy id's is cilent.id(Application id), client.secret(value of cerfitications and secret) and client.endpoint(tenant id) ➢ Give a API permission and add the permission is Azure blob storage, Azure data lake ➢ Once go back to data lake gen2 and give Access Control(IAM) and ➢ add Role assignment in that Give role is Storage blob data reader(for to read the data in databaricks) , ➢ add memebers give the App Name (when you create a Azure Activity) ➢ Create New Notebook and remember select the language is Scala for understanding purpose ➢ syntax: ➢ This is all id of datalake gen2 and azure active dictory for to load the data val configs = Map( \"fs.azure.account.auth.type\" -> \"OAuth\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type\" -> \"org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id\" -> \"b5e7b223-6ccb-4aab-86b2-fd9773f11fe1\", --(Application id) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret\" -> \"_KI8Q~FStvRvFTPguBS~wwAVthPsxQ4zy4sxndnX\", - --(value of certificate and secret) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint\" -> \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/dee06880-2584- 4c56-bf98-b4023454c861/oauth2/token\"); --- (tenant id) dbutils.fs.mount( -------------This is For Mount Purpose source = \"abfss://employee@storimgdatalakr.dfs.core.windows.net/\", mountPoint = \"/mnt/storingthedata\", extraConfigs = configs) Page | 19 %python df=spark.read.parquet(\"/mnt/storingthedata\") ---it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe %python display(df) -- it will the display dbutils.fs.unmount(\"/mnt/eclasessbatch112\") ----- it will unmount the data Delta Lake Delta lake it is like a Data Lake but in this Any changes in existing file it will store the data . ➢ schema enforcements ➢ Time travel /versions ➢ update ➢ delete ➢ merge create a Dataframe Syntax: df=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000),(200,'mani',2000),(300,'siva',3000)],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sa l\"]) df.show() df.write.format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") --- coverting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]).show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") -- it is to overwrite the data ---But it will not happend in Delta---------- df.write.format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") --- converting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]) df1.show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") – (it will get error so change the syntax) Page | 20 df1.write.mode(\"append\").format('delta').option(\"mergeschema\",True).save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingd elta/filedelta\") --- it will the update the table spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",timestampAsof=\"2022-06-21","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"MERGERS: It is a Comparing the table and dimension table if dimension table is not existiong the vale it","type":"concept","a":"will update , insert delete. create table customer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), price int) insert into customer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600),(4,'oiu',500) select * from customer create table dimcustomer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), Page | 46 price int) insert into dimcustomer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600) update customer set price=800 where price=400 delete customer where price=900 merge dimcustomer as target using customer as source on source.cno=target.cno when not matched by target then --- it will be insert insert(cno,cname,price) values(source.cno,source.cname,source.cno) ii ti will be update when matched and target.cno=2 then update set target.cname=source.cname, target.price=source.price when not matched by source then ---- it will delete . delete;","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"05 AM — it missed the window. This is late arriving data.","type":"concept","a":"• Strategy 1: Watermark with a Grace/Tolerance Period Add a buffer period to your WHERE clause to catch late data\n• Example — Pipeline picks up last 2 hours as buffer: -- Standard incremental load SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= '2024-01-15 00:00:00' AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' -- With 2-hour grace period to catch late arrivals SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= DATEADD(HOUR, -2, '2024-01-15 00:00:00') AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' Strategy 2: Upsert (Merge) Instead of Insert Only Instead of just inserting new rows, use MERGE so late data updates existing records without creating duplicates. -- Using MERGE in Azure Synapse / SQL MERGE INTO target_sales AS T USING source_sales AS S ON T.order_id = S.order_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET T.status = S.status, T.amount = S.amount WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, status, amount, created_at) VALUES (S.order_id, S.status, S.amount, S.created_at);\n• Strategy 3: Delta Lake (Databricks) — Time Travel Delta Lake keeps history of all changes\n• If late data arrives, you can reprocess a specific date partition. # Read Delta table as of a specific time (Time Travel) df = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('/mnt/sales/delta') # Upsert late data into Delta table Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 4 from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/sales/delta') deltaTable.alias('T').merge( late_df.alias('S'), 'T.order_id = S.order_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute() Strategy 4: Partition Reprocessing If late data arrives for an old date, re-run the pipeline only for that partition rather than the whole table. • Use ADF pipeline parameters to pass a specific date/partition • Overwrite only that partition in the target (e.g., ADLS partition: year=2024/month=01/day=15) • This avoids full reload and is much faster 💡 Tip: In an interview say: 'We handled late arriving data using a combination of a grace period watermark and MERGE/Upsert in Synapse\n• For Delta Lake, we used merge operations and partition reprocessing when needed.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How to handle incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• I implement incremental load using watermark columns like ModifiedDate or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table, and in each run I extract only new or updated records\n• Then I load data into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• A(cid:332)er successful load, I update the watermark to ensure no data duplica(cid:415)on","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If unchanged:","type":"concept","a":"o Do nothing Use Delta MERGE with update and insert logic.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A schema defines the structure of your data: column names, data types, and nullability\n• When new data arrives, it may or may not match the existing table schema\n• Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 4 Schema Enforcement (Default Behavior in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake validates all incoming data against the current table schema\n• If new data has extra columns or mismatched types — the write FAILS with a clear error\n• This is the DEFAULT — protects data quality in production. # Existing Delta table schema: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE # New data has an extra column 'discount' new_df = spark.createDataFrame( [(1001, 501, 99.99, 0.10)], ['order_id', 'customer_id', 'amount', 'discount'] ) # Schema Enforcement in action — this FAILS new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # AnalysisException: A schema mismatch detected when writing. # Column 'discount' is not defined in the table\n• When to enforce schema: • Production financial or compliance tables • When schema changes must go through a change control review • When you want the pipeline to fail loudly rather than silently corrupt data Schema Evolution (Opt-in in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake auto-updates the table schema when mergeSchema=true\n• New columns are added; existing columns are untouched\n• Old rows will show NULL for newly added columns. # Enable Schema Evolution — write succeeds, new column is added new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # Table schema is now: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE, discount DOUBLE # # Old rows: discount = NULL # New rows: discount = 0.10 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 5 # Enable globally at Spark session level: spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled','true') Comparison Table Aspect Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default? YES — Delta default No — opt-in with mergeSchema=true Extra column Write FAILS with error Column added automatically to arrives table Wrong data type Write FAILS Write FAILS (type mismatch not auto-evolved) Old rows for new N/A NULL value column Best for Prod, financial, compliance data Rapidly changing source systems Interview Tip: Say: 'Delta enforces schema by default to protect data quality\n• For sources that evolve their schema (add new columns), we enable mergeSchema=true\n• Data type changes always need a manual ALTER TABLE — Delta never auto-changes types.' Q5 Function to Insert Newly Arriving Data — MERGE / Upsert The Answer: Use MERGE (Upsert) When new data arrives, you typically need to: INSERT rows that do not exist yet, and UPDATE rows that already exist\n• The function for this is MERGE — also called Upsert (Update + Insert)\n• Delta Lake MERGE — Python API from delta.tables import DeltaTable # Existing Delta table (destination) delta_table = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/customers') # Incoming new data (source) new_data = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/raw/customers_today/') # MERGE: update matching rows, insert new rows Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 6 delta_table.alias('target').merge( new_data.alias('source'), 'target.customer_id = source.customer_id' # match key ) \\ .whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ # update all columns if key found .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ # insert full row if key not found .execute() print('MERGE complete — no duplicates, no data loss.') MERGE with SQL Syntax (Databricks SQL) MERGE INTO customers AS target USING new_customers AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND source.updated_at > target.updated_at THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = source.updated_at WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, source.created_at, source.updated_at) -- Optional: remove records deleted at source WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE Real-World Example: Daily Customer Incremental Load # Step 1: Read only changed records (incremental extract) new_customers = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM source_db.customers WHERE updated_at >= current_date() - INTERVAL 1 DAY ''') # Step 2: Merge into Delta dimension table delta_cust = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/dim_customers') delta_cust.alias('t').merge( new_customers.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdate(set={ 'name': 's.name', Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 7 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' )).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ 'customer_id': 's.customer_id', 'name': 's.name', 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'created_at': 's.created_at', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' }).execute() print('Incremental load complete.') Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'To insert newly arriving data into an existing Delta table, we use MERGE (upsert)","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Simple Definition A Delta Table is a table format built on Parquet files with an added transaction log (_delta_log)\n• It gives you ACID transactions, time travel, schema enforcement, and MERGE support\n• It is the standard table type in Databricks (open-source Delta Lake project)\n• Core Features Feature What It Means Example ACID Transactions All-or-nothing writes — no Pipeline fails mid-write? Table stays partial data clean Time Travel Query older versions of data SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 MERGE / Upsert Insert + Update in one atomic Daily incremental customer load step Schema Rejects wrong schema by Extra column fails the write Enforcement default Schema Evolution Auto-adds new columns (opt- mergeSchema=true in) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 8 OPTIMIZE Compacts small files Speeds up future reads automatically Z-Ordering Collocates related data in ZORDER BY date for fast range same files queries Streaming Support Works with Structured Real-time events land into Delta Streaming table How Delta Table Storage Works # Delta table = Parquet files + _delta_log folder # Folder structure on ADLS / S3: '/mnt/delta/sales/' ' _delta_log/' ' 00000000000000000000.json -- table created' ' 00000000000000000001.json -- first batch loaded' ' 00000000000000000002.json -- merge operation ran' ' 00000000000000000003.json -- OPTIMIZE ran' ' part-00000-abc123.snappy.parquet' ' part-00001-def456.snappy.parquet' ' ...' # Every write is an entry in _delta_log # This is what enables Time Travel and ACID Time Travel Examples # Read table as of 7 days ago (useful for debugging) df_old = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-08') \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # Read a specific version df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', 5) \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # View full history from delta.tables import DeltaTable dt = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/sales') dt.history(10).show(truncate=False) # SQL versions # SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-08' # SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 9 Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'A Delta Table is Parquet + a transaction log\n• The transaction log is what gives us ACID guarantees, time travel to query historical versions, and the ability to do MERGE operations\n• It is the foundation of the Lakehouse architecture in Databricks — data warehouse reliability with data lake scalability.' Q7 Pipeline Running Slowly — Causes and Fixes Step 1: Where to Look First • Databricks Spark UI: Job Run page -> Stages tab -> look for the slowest stage • ADF Monitor: Activity Runs -> check which activity consumes the most time • Compare to previous runs — if it was faster before, something changed Cause 1: Data Skew One partition has far more data than others\n• One task takes 30 minutes while all others finish in 2 minutes. # Symptom: Spark UI shows one task dramatically slower than others # Diagnose: check partition sizes from pyspark.sql.functions import spark_partition_id df.groupBy(spark_partition_id()).count().orderBy('count(1)',ascending=False).show() # Fix 1: Enable Adaptive Query Execution (Databricks default ON) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') # Fix 2: Salt the skewed join key from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, lit, rand SALT = 10 df_salted = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('customer_id'), lit('_'), (rand()*SALT).cast('int'))) Cause 2: Small File Problem Thousands of tiny Parquet files = thousands of file-open operations\n• Reads become very slow. # Diagnose: list files in Delta table folder files = dbutils.fs.ls('/mnt/delta/sales/') print(f'File count: {len(files)}') # > 1000 = problem # Fix: Run OPTIMIZE to compact small files into larger ones spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales`') Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 10 # Fix with Z-Ordering: collocate data by the most-queried column spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales` ZORDER BY (sale_date)') # Fix: Write fewer files by coalescing before write df.coalesce(20).write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/mnt/delta/sales') Cause 3: Missing Broadcast Join for Small Tables Default joins shuffle BOTH sides\n• If one table is small, broadcast it to avoid shuffle entirely. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Slow: both DataFrames shuffled df_result = df_large_sales.join(df_dim_region, 'region_id') # Fast: small table sent to all nodes — zero shuffle df_result = df_large_sales.join(broadcast(df_dim_region), 'region_id') # Raise auto-broadcast threshold from 10MB to 50MB spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', str(50*1024*1024)) Cause 4: Full Table Scan (No Partition Pruning) Reading the entire table when only a small date range is needed wastes time. # Slow: reads ALL partitions then filters in memory df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/') df_filtered = df.filter(col('sale_date') == '2024-01-15') # Fast: only reads the matching partition folder df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/year=2024/month=01/day=15/') # For Delta tables — use predicate pushdown in SQL queries df = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM sales WHERE sale_date = '2024-01-15' -- Spark prunes to this partition AND region = 'North' ''') Cause 5: Shuffle Partitions Too High or Too Low # Default spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200 # For 1GB data, 200 partitions = 5MB each — too many small tasks # Enable AQE to auto-tune (Databricks default) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') # AQE merges small shuffle partitions automatically Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 11 # Manual rule of thumb: aim for ~128MB per partition # 10 GB data / 128 MB = 80 partitions spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '80') Summary Checklist Problem Symptom Fix Data Skew 1 task much slower than AQE skewJoin + salting others Small Files Slow reads, many tiny files OPTIMIZE + ZORDER on Delta Missing broadcast Large shuffle for small broadcast() hint or raise threshold join lookup table Full table scan Reading GB/TB for MB Partition pruning, predicate pushdown result Too many shuffle 200 tiny tasks for small data Enable AQE or lower manually partitions Cluster too small Spill to disk, OOM errors Scale up memory, add more workers Interview Tip: Structure your answer: 'I open the Spark UI first to find which stage is slow and look for signs of skew, shuffle, or spill\n• The most common causes are data skew, small file problem, missing broadcast joins, or full table scans\n• I fix each systematically using AQE, OPTIMIZE, broadcast hints, and partition filters.' Q8 Python: First Occurrence of Values from a List with Duplicates Problem Given a list with duplicates, print or return only the FIRST occurrence of each value in order\n• Input: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 1: Using a Set (Most Common Interview Answer) # Method 1 — Set to track already-seen values # Time: O(n) | Space: O(n) | Order preserved: YES Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 12 def first_occurrences(lst): seen = set() # remembers values we have already added result = [] for item in lst: if item not in seen: result.append(item) seen.add(item) return result # Test data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] print(first_occurrences(data)) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 2: dict.fromkeys() — Python One-Liner # Method 2 — dict.fromkeys removes duplicates and preserves order # Python 3.7+ dicts are ordered by insertion data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = list(dict.fromkeys(data)) print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # How it works: # dict.fromkeys(data) => {3:None, 1:None, 4:None, 5:None, 9:None, 2:None, 6:None} # Keys are inherently unique and insertion-ordered # list() converts back to a plain list Method 3: Print Directly data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] seen = set() for item in data: if item not in seen: print(item, end=' ') seen.add(item) # Output: 3 1 4 5 9 2 6 Method 4: For Strings names = ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Alice', 'Charlie', 'Bob', 'David'] Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 13 seen = set() for name in names: if name not in seen: print(name) seen.add(name) # Output: # Alice # Bob # Charlie # David Method 5: Using pandas / PySpark (Data Engineering) # pandas import pandas as pd data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = pd.Series(data).drop_duplicates(keep='first').tolist() print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # PySpark — for large distributed datasets # df.dropDuplicates(['column_name']) # drops duplicate rows by column # df.dropDuplicates() # drops fully duplicate rows Complexity Comparison Method Time Space Order? Best For Set tracking (Method 1) O(n) O(n) Yes Most readable, interview default dict.fromkeys (Method O(n) O(n) Yes Shortest code — Python 3.7+ 2) pandas O(n) O(n) Yes DataFrames, data engineering drop_duplicates PySpark O(n) Low No Big data on cluster dropDuplicates dist. (unless sorted) Interview Tip: In interview: Start with Method 1 — explain the set-based logic step by step","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"CDC (Change Data Capture) and CDF (Change Data Feed)","type":"concept","a":"• CDC — Change Data Capture CDC is the process of identifying and capturing changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) made to a source database and propagating those changes to a target system — without doing a full reload every time\n• CDC Methods • Log-based CDC — reads the database transaction log (WAL/binlog)\n• Tools: Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, Oracle GoldenGate • Trigger-based CDC — DB triggers write change records to a shadow table • Timestamp-based CDC — query source using last_modified > watermark • Diff-based CDC — compare full snapshots to identify changes (slow, expensive) -- Timestamp-based CDC example (SQL Server / ADB) SELECT * FROM source_table WHERE last_modified_date > '2024-01-01 00:00:00' AND last_modified_date <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP CDF — Change Data Feed (Delta Lake Feature) CDF is a Delta Lake feature that tracks row-level changes (insert, update, delete, update_preimage) in a Delta table\n• It creates a built-in change log that downstream consumers can read incrementally\n• Enable and Use CDF -- Enable CDF on a Delta table ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Or at creation time CREATE TABLE my_table (id INT, name STRING) TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Read changes from version 5 onwards df = spark.read.format('delta') .option('readChangeFeed', 'true') .option('startingVersion', 5) .table('my_table') -- Columns added by CDF: -- _change_type: insert | update_preimage | update_postimage | delete -- _commit_version: Delta log version -- _commit_timestamp: When the change was committed Feature CDC CDF (Delta) Scope Source database changes Delta table row-level changes Granularity Row-level (I/U/D) Row-level with pre/post image for updates Setup External tool (Debezium) or DB ALTER TABLE + TBLPROPERTIES config Streaming Yes — Kafka/Event Hub Yes — readStream with readChangeFeed Use case Sync source DB to lakehouse Propagate Delta changes downstream Page 2 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Optimize Queries in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"1. Partitioning • Partition tables on low-cardinality filter columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning — too many small partitions increases metadata overhead df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save(path)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• Z-ORDER on high-cardinality columns used in WHERE clauses (customer_id, transaction_id) • Use Liquid Clustering for dynamic, incremental clustering without partition rewrites OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) -- compact + cluster VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- remove stale files","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Parquet Format vs JSON Format","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Feature Parquet JSON Storage layout Columnar — stores each column Row-based — stores each record together together Compression Snappy/GZIP — typically 75-90% Minimal — largely uncompressed smaller text Schema Embedded in file footer (strongly typed) No schema — inferred at read time Query speed 5-20x faster — column pruning + Slow — must read all fields per row predicate pushdown Predicate pushdown Yes — skips row groups outside filter Not supported range Splittable Yes — parallel reads Only with JSON Lines format Null handling Efficient native encoding String 'null' or missing key Nested types Supported natively (struct, array, map) Natural — JSON is inherently nested Human readable No — binary format Yes — text based Best for Analytics, large-scale processing, Delta APIs, config files, event messages Write speed Slower (encoding overhead) Faster (plain text write) Rule of thumb: Always convert landing-zone JSON/CSV files to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Parquet gives columnar compression and predicate pushdown\n• Delta adds ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement on top","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE + ZORDER — compact small Delta files and cluster data for file skipping • Liquid Clustering — dynamic incremental clustering; replaces partition + ZORDER • Photon Engine — vectorized C++ query engine; enable in cluster config for 3-10x speedup • Delta Caching — SSD-level caching of remote Parquet data on worker nodes • Auto Optimize — enable optimizeWrite and autoCompact for automatic file sizing • AQE — Adaptive Query Execution for runtime plan re-optimization • VACUUM — remove stale/orphan files to reduce storage scan overhead • Partition pruning — query only relevant partitions using WHERE on partition columns • Data skipping — Delta min/max stats skip irrelevant files automatically • Materialized Views — pre-compute expensive aggregations for fast dashboard reads -- Databricks-specific optimizations OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date); VACUUM transactions RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Auto Optimize properties ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' ); -- Liquid Clustering ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date);","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evaluation and Enforcement","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Enforcement (Schema Validation) Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects any writes where the incoming data does not match the existing table schema\n• It protects data quality by preventing corrupt or mismatched data from entering the table. • Throws an AnalysisException if new columns are added or data types mismatch • Enabled by default in Delta Lake Schema Evolution (mergeSchema) Schema evolution allows the table schema to be updated automatically when new columns are added to incoming data. # Allow new columns to be added automatically df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/table/') Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Feature Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default behaviour ON (strict) OFF (must opt-in) New columns in source Write fails Columns added automatically Data type mismatch Write fails Still fails (type safety) Use case Protect prod tables from bad Accommodate evolving source data schemas","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Tables Available in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Table Type Description Managed Table Both metadata AND data are managed by Databricks/Unity Catalog\n• Dropping the table deletes all data\n• External Table Only metadata is managed\n• Data resides at a specified external path\n• Dropping the table does NOT delete data\n• Delta Table Table backed by Delta Lake format\n• Supports ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement\n• Hive Metastore Table Legacy table registered in the Hive Metastore (available before Unity Catalog)\n• View A virtual table based on a query\n• No data stored; computed on-the-fly\n• Temporary View Session-scoped view that disappears when the session ends\n• Global Temporary View Cluster-scoped temp view; accessible across sessions but dropped when cluster restarts","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table? Advantages of Using Delta Table","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Table is a table stored in Delta Lake format — an open-source storage layer built on top of Parquet files with a transaction log (_delta_log) that brings ACID guarantees and advanced features to data lake storage\n• How Delta Table Works Internally • Data is stored as Parquet files in cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) • A transaction log directory (_delta_log) records every change as a JSON/Parquet checkpoint • Each write creates a new versioned set of Parquet files — old files are retained for time travel • Compaction (OPTIMIZE) merges small files into larger ones for query performance Advantages of Delta Table Feature Description ACID Transactions Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — concurrent reads/writes are safe Time Travel Query any previous version: SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 or TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' Schema Enforcement Rejects writes that don't match the table schema — prevents corrupt data Schema Evolution New columns can be added via mergeSchema without rewriting the table Upserts (MERGE) Efficient insert/update/delete using MERGE INTO — enables SCD and CDC patterns Unified Batch & Streaming Same Delta table can be written by a batch job and read as a stream simultaneously Scalable Metadata Z-ordering and data skipping speed up queries on large datasets OPTIMIZE & VACUUM OPTIMIZE compacts small files;\n• VACUUM removes old files saving storage costs Open Format Based on Parquet — readable by Spark, Hive, Presto, Trino, and other engines Useful Delta Table Commands -- Time travel SELECT * FROM employees VERSION AS OF 3;\n• SELECT * FROM employees TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-06-01'; -- Restore to previous version RESTORE TABLE employees TO VERSION AS OF 5;\n• Page 6 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 -- Compact small files OPTIMIZE employees ZORDER BY (department_id); -- Remove files older than 7 days VACUUM employees RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- View history DESCRIBE HISTORY employees;\n• Page 7 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"How to Build a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"• A fault-tolerant ETL pipeline continues to operate correctly even when individual components fail, ensuring no data loss, no duplicate processing, and automatic recovery — without manual intervention\n• Key Principles of Fault Tolerance Principle Implementation Idempotency Re-running the pipeline produces the same result — no duplicates, no data loss Exactly-Once Processing Each record processed exactly once using checkpoints and unique keys Checkpointing Stream state saved to durable storage so recovery resumes from last checkpoint Retry Logic Automatic retries on transient failures (network, API timeout, cluster preemption) Dead Letter Queue Bad records quarantined separately for later inspection instead of failing the job Monitoring & Alerting Proactive alerts on failures, SLA breaches, and data quality violations Schema Validation Reject or quarantine records that violate schema before writing to target ACID Writes Use Delta Lake MERGE for upserts — safe for partial failures and re-runs Architecture of a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline • Layer 1 – Ingest: Autoloader with checkpoints ensures no file is missed or processed twice • Layer 2 – Bronze: Raw Delta table written with ACID guarantees; idempotent via COPY INTO • Layer 3 – Silver: Cleansing + MERGE INTO Delta for upserts; bad records to quarantine table • Layer 4 – Gold: Aggregation; only runs after Silver passes data quality Expectations • Layer 5 – Orchestration: Databricks Workflows with retry policies and failure alerts Full PySpark Fault-Tolerant Pipeline Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F from delta.tables import DeltaTable import logging # ── CONFIG ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── SOURCE_PATH = '/mnt/landing/employees/' SCHEMA_PATH = '/mnt/schema/employees/' CHECKPOINT_PATH = '/mnt/checkpoint/employees/' BRONZE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/bronze/employees/' SILVER_PATH = '/mnt/delta/silver/employees/' QUARANTINE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/quarantine/employees/' Page 17 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # ── STEP 1: INGEST with Autoloader (Fault-Tolerant Streaming) ─── df_raw = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', SCHEMA_PATH) .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true') .load(SOURCE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 2: WRITE TO BRONZE (Idempotent via checkpoint) ───────── (df_raw.writeStream .format('delta') .outputMode('append') .option('checkpointLocation', CHECKPOINT_PATH + 'bronze/') .trigger(availableNow=True) # process all pending files once .start(BRONZE_PATH) .awaitTermination() ) # ── STEP 3: CLEANSE + QUARANTINE BAD RECORDS ──────────────────── df_bronze = spark.read.format('delta').load(BRONZE_PATH) df_valid = df_bronze.filter( F.col('emp_id').isNotNull() & F.col('salary').cast('integer').isNotNull() & (F.col('salary').cast('integer') > 0) ) df_invalid = df_bronze.subtract(df_valid) # Write bad records to quarantine for investigation (df_invalid .withColumn('quarantine_reason', F.lit('Failed validation: null emp_id or invalid salary')) .withColumn('quarantine_ts', F.current_timestamp()) .write.format('delta').mode('append').save(QUARANTINE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 4: UPSERT TO SILVER (Idempotent MERGE) ───────────────── df_clean = (df_valid .dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) .withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) .withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) .withColumn('updated_at', F.current_timestamp()) ) if DeltaTable.isDeltaTable(spark, SILVER_PATH): silver = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, SILVER_PATH) (silver.alias('tgt') .merge(df_clean.alias('src'), 'tgt.emp_id = src.emp_id') .whenMatchedUpdateAll() .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() .execute() Page 18 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 ) else: df_clean.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save(SILVER_PATH) # ── STEP 5: GOLD AGGREGATION ──────────────────────────────────── df_silver = spark.read.format('delta').load(SILVER_PATH) df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('department').agg( F.count('emp_id').alias('headcount'), F.avg('salary').alias('avg_salary'), F.max('salary').alias('max_salary') ) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/mnt/delta/gold/dept_summary /') print('Pipeline completed successfully.') Retry & Alerting in Databricks Workflows Feature How to Configure Job Retries Set Max Retries = 3 and Retry Interval on each task in Databricks Workflows UI Depends On Task B runs only if Task A succeeds — dependency chain stops cascade failures On Failure Alerting Configure email/Slack/Teams alerts in Workflows notification settings Timeout Set per-task timeout to detect hung jobs and trigger retry/failure handling Run If Condition Conditional task execution based on upstream task outcome (Success/Failed/All) DLT Expectations @dlt.expect_or_quarantine() routes bad records without failing the pipeline Summary Checklist for Fault-Tolerant ETL • Use Autoloader with checkpointing for idempotent file ingestion • Write all layers as Delta tables for ACID guarantees • Use MERGE INTO for upserts — safe to re-run without duplicates • Separate valid vs invalid records — quarantine bad data instead of failing • Validate schema and data quality at Silver layer before Gold • Configure retry policies and timeout in Databricks Workflows • Set up monitoring alerts on job failure, SLA breach, and quarantine record spike • Log pipeline metrics (record counts, error counts, durations) to a monitoring table • Test failure scenarios: missing file, schema change, duplicate keys, corrupt data Page 19","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"While migrating the data, did you just copy the data or did you use incremental","type":"concept","a":"• load? We used a combination of both approaches: Full Load (Initial Load): • For the first migration, we did a full historical load — extracting all records from the source and loading them into the Bronze layer as-is. • This was a one-time bulk load to establish the baseline dataset\n• Incremental Load (Ongoing Sync): • After the initial load, we switched to incremental loads to capture only new or changed records. • We used watermark columns (e.g., updated_at, created_date) or Change Data Capture (CDC) to identify changed rows. • In Databricks, we used Auto Loader for file-based incremental ingestion and MERGE INTO (Delta Lake) for upsert operations\n• This approach minimized load time, reduced compute costs, and kept the target data fresh without full re- processing\n• Section 2: Architecture Concepts","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"metadata stored?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Format: • By default, tables created in Databricks are stored in Delta Lake format (.parquet files + _delta_log transaction log). • Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that brings ACID transactions to data lakes. • Physically, data is stored as columnar Parquet files on cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS)\n• Metadata Storage: • Delta Transaction Log (_delta_log): JSON files stored alongside data files in the table directory\n• Records every operation (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE) — used for time travel and ACID guarantees. • Hive Metastore / Unity Catalog: Table-level metadata (table name, schema, location, partitioning) is stored in the Databricks Metastore (by default, a managed Hive Metastore or Unity Catalog in newer workspaces)\n• So metadata lives in two places: the _delta_log for data-level history and the Metastore for table-level schema/location information","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is VACUUM and when do we use it?","type":"concept","a":"• VACUUM is a Delta Lake command that removes old data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta transaction log\n• Why Vacuum is Needed: • Delta Lake's ACID support and time travel retain old file versions, which accumulate over time and consume storage. • VACUUM cleans up these obsolete files to reclaim storage space\n• Syntax: VACUUM table_name RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Default: 7 days -- Or in PySpark: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forName(spark, 'table_name') deltaTable.vacuum(168) # hours Important Notes: • Default retention period is 7 days (168 hours)\n• Setting it lower than 7 days requires disabling a safety check. • After VACUUM, you cannot time travel to versions older than the retention period. • Use it periodically in production to control storage costs\n• Section 4: Performance Optimization","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Is MERGE SCD-1 or SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• MERGE is primarily SCD-1 by default behavior. • SCD-1 (Overwrite): MERGE with WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE overwrites existing records — no history is kept. • SCD-2 (History): MERGE can implement SCD-2 but requires a more complex query — on match, you UPDATE the old row (close it) and INSERT a new row with the changed values\n• In Delta Lake, you can implement SCD-2 using MERGE with both WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE and WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT clauses, combined with is_current and date columns\n• Section 7: PySpark Coding","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What will be SCD2 logic and columns you will be using for your project. How will you","type":"concept","a":"implement? SCD Type 2 Logic + Columns Columns used:  business_key (emp_id)  start_date  end_date  is_ac(cid:415)ve  hash_diff (for change detec(cid:415)on) Logic:  If record changes → expire old record (end_date + is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  Insert new record with updated values  Maintain history Implementa(cid:415)on:  Delta MERGE INTO OR DLT apply_changes","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed?","type":"concept","a":"• Change Data Feed (CDF)  Tracks row-level changes in Delta tables  Captures INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE Used for:  incremental pipelines  CDC processing 7\n• Auto Loader  Incremental file inges(cid:415)on in Databricks  Automa(cid:415)cally detects new files  Handles schema evolu(cid:415)on Best for streaming inges(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore the table if data is corrupted?","type":"concept","a":"Restore corrupted table Using Delta Time Travel: RESTORE TABLE table_name TO VERSION AS OF 5;","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the use of SQL Warehouse in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Warehouse (previously called SQL Analytics Endpoint) is a serverless/dedicated compute resource optimized for running SQL queries on data stored in the Lakehouse (Delta Lake). • Enables BI tools (Tableau, Power BI) to connect via JDBC/ODBC. • Supports auto-stop and auto-scaling to control costs. • Serverless option eliminates cluster management; compute spins up in seconds. • Photon engine (vectorized query processing) dramatically speeds up SQL workloads\n• Change Data Feed (CDF)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is CDF (Change Data Feed) in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF is a Delta Lake feature that records row-level changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to a Delta table, producing a changelog that can be consumed by downstream processes. • Enable with: ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true) • Read changes: spark.readStream.format('delta').option('readChangeFeed','true').table('my_table') • Use cases: CDC pipelines, incremental ETL, audit trails, real-time aggregations. • Change types exposed: insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete\n• Efficient Model for Daily Streaming Data","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"We are getting streaming data daily — how do you design an efficient model for it?","type":"concept","a":"• Recommended architecture: Medallion (Bronze → Silver → Gold) with Delta Lake + Structured Streaming. • Bronze layer: Ingest raw streaming data with Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — schema evolution, exactly-once, checkpointing. • Silver layer: Parse, clean, deduplicate (use MERGE INTO for upserts via CDF or watermarking). • Gold layer: Aggregated, business-ready tables updated incrementally. • Use trigger(availableNow=True) for micro-batch on schedule, or trigger(processingTime='X seconds') for near-real-time. • Partition Delta tables by date (year/month/day) to enable partition pruning on queries. • Enable optimize & ZORDER on high-cardinality filter columns. • Monitor with Spark UI, Delta transaction log, and Databricks cluster metrics\n• Resolving Data Skew","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write the code for SCD2","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 Code (PySpark / Databricks) (cid:44934) Using Delta Lake MERGE INTO (most asked in interviews) MERGE INTO target_table t USING source_table s ON t.emp_id = s.emp_id AND t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = true WHEN MATCHED AND ( t.salary <> s.salary OR t.dept_id <> s.dept_id ) THEN UPDATE SET t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = false, t.end_date = current_date() WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, start_date, end_date, is_ac(cid:415)ve ) VALUES ( s.emp_id, s.name, s.dept_id, s.salary, current_date(), null, true ); (cid:44934) Key idea:  Old record → expired (is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  New record → inserted with new version  History preserved","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on strategies in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"Common answers:  Cache frequently used DataFrames  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Broadcast Join  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE)  Delta Cache  OPTIMIZE  ZORDER  Liquid Clustering  Predicate Pushdown  Column Pruning  Avoid UDFs  Auto Op(cid:415)mize","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, ZORDER and Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE ZORDER Liquid Clustering Compacts small files Organizes data by column values Automa(cid:415)cally manages clustering Improves file size Improves data skipping No manual par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning Reduces file count Faster filtering Be(cid:425)er for evolving workloads Doesn't sort Sorts within files Re-clusters automa(cid:415)cally Example: OPTIMIZE 1000 small files ↓ 10 large files ZORDER OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY(customer_id) Improves filtering: WHERE customer_id=100 Liquid Clustering No par(cid:415)(cid:415)on maintenance\n• Databricks automa(cid:415)cally reclusters data","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Data Lake and Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• Data Lake Delta Lake Storage layer Storage layer + transac(cid:415)on layer No ACID ACID transac(cid:415)ons No versioning Time Travel No schema enforcement Schema enforcement & evolu(cid:415)on Slow updates MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE No transac(cid:415)on log _delta_log maintains metadata Interview Answer \"A Data Lake stores files such as Parquet, CSV, or JSON, whereas Delta Lake builds on top of a Data Lake by adding ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, and a transac(cid:415)on log\n• Delta Lake makes lake storage more reliable for produc(cid:415)on data engineering.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Managed table deleted a(cid:332)er reten(cid:415)on and all files removed. How do you recreate it?","type":"concept","a":"• If:  Managed table is dropped  Reten(cid:415)on period has passed  Underlying data files are permanently removed  All versions are gone You cannot recover it using Delta Lake features\n• Time Travel and RESTORE depend on the transac(cid:415)on log and data files s(cid:415)ll exis(cid:415)ng\n• Recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from backup  Reload from the source system  Recover using cloud storage versioning (if enabled)  Use disaster recovery copies Interview Answer \"If both the Delta log and data files have been permanently deleted a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period, the table cannot be recreated from Delta metadata alone\n• Recovery would require restoring from backup, cloud storage versioning, or reloading the data from the source.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — File Compaction Delta tables accumulate many small files over time\n• OPTIMIZE compacts them into larger Parquet files for faster reads\n• OPTIMIZE my_table OPTIMIZE my_table WHERE date = '2024-01-01' -- with partition filter Z-ORDER — Co-locate Related Data Z-ORDER clusters related data within the same files\n• Queries on Z-ordered columns can skip irrelevant files automatically (data skipping)\n• OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_date) Feature OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Purpose Compact small files Cluster data by column values Benefit Faster scans, less overhead Data skipping on filter queries Best For After many small writes/streaming High-cardinality filter columns Used Together Yes — in a single OPTIMIZE ZORDER BY command Page 3 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide Best Z-ORDER columns: those appearing frequently in WHERE clauses with high cardinality (customer_id, event_timestamp)\n• Avoid low-cardinality columns like boolean flags","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize Delta Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partition on low-cardinality columns used in filters\n• Avoid over-partitioning. df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year', 'month').save(path)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + ZORDER","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"VACUUM — Remove Stale Files","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- 7 days default","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Optimize (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' )","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Lake vs Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"Feature Data Lake (Plain) Delta Lake ACID Transactions No Yes — full ACID support Schema Enforcement No Yes — schema-on-write Time Travel No Yes — VERSION AS OF Upserts / Deletes Complex or not supported Yes — MERGE INTO Streaming + Batch Separate pipelines needed Unified — same table File Format Parquet / CSV / JSON Parquet + _delta_log Data Quality Manual checks required Built-in constraints Key Delta Lake Commands -- Time Travel SELECT * FROM my_table VERSION AS OF 5 SELECT * FROM my_table TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' -- MERGE (Upsert) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Live Table?","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Live Table (DLT) is a managed ETL framework in Databricks used to build reliable data pipelines\n• Instead of manually crea(cid:415)ng jobs, DLT:  Reads data  Applies transforma(cid:415)ons  Loads data  Handles dependencies  Monitors pipeline health  Enforces data quality rules Example: CREATE OR REFRESH LIVE TABLE sales_clean AS SELECT * FROM LIVE.sales_raw WHERE amount > 0;\n• Data Quality in Delta Live Tables One of the biggest advantages is built-in expecta(cid:415)ons\n• Example: CONSTRAINT valid_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW;\n• This automa(cid:415)cally removes invalid records from the pipeline\n• Interview Answer If the interviewer asks: \"What is the difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table?\" \"A Delta Table is a storage layer based on Delta Lake that provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and (cid:415)me travel\n• A Delta Live Table is a managed ETL framework that builds and orchestrates data pipelines using Delta Tables\n• DLT adds features like automa(cid:415)c dependency management, scheduling, monitoring, lineage, and built-in data quality expecta(cid:415)ons, whereas with Delta Tables these aspects are managed manually.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"MERGE with UPDATE gives a duplicate rows error. What is the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• \"This usually means that the MERGE condi(cid:415)on is matching mul(cid:415)ple source rows to the same target row\n• Common causes are:  Duplicate business keys in the source.  Duplicate records in the target.  An incorrect ON condi(cid:415)on that is not unique\n• The solu(cid:415)on is to remove duplicates using ROW_NUMBER() or ensure the join key is unique before execu(cid:415)ng the MERGE.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Databricks job fails a(cid:332)er 5 minutes, what happens in Parquet and Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Parquet \"Parquet files do not support transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails while wri(cid:415)ng, par(cid:415)ally wri(cid:425)en files may remain in the storage loca(cid:415)on, requiring manual cleanup.\" Delta \"Delta Lake provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails before the transac(cid:415)on commits, the Delta transac(cid:415)on log is not updated, and readers con(cid:415)nue to see the previous consistent version\n• This prevents par(cid:415)al or corrupted data from being visible.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If ADLS storage is full, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would verify whether the issue is storage capacity, account limits, or quotas\n• Then I would:  Remove temporary or intermediate files.  Run VACUUM on Delta tables to delete obsolete files a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period.  Archive historical data to a lower-cost storage (cid:415)er.  Delete unused datasets a(cid:332)er confirming reten(cid:415)on policies.  Review lifecycle management rules to automa(cid:415)cally move or delete old data.  If business growth requires it, increase storage capacity or distribute data across addi(cid:415)onal storage accounts.\" Round 3 : (Client Round)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Files Do You Usually Work With?","type":"concept","a":"• CSV — legacy systems, flat files, exports from operational systems • JSON — API responses, event streams, semi-structured data • Parquet — preferred columnar format for analytics; compressed, schema-embedded • Delta — Parquet + transaction log; used for all curated/processed layers • Avro — schema-embedded binary format; common in Kafka/streaming pipelines • ORC — columnar format used in Hive-based ecosystems • XML — legacy enterprise and EDI data sources Best Practice: Always convert raw CSV/JSON to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer. Parquet provides columnar compression and predicate pushdown. Delta adds ACID transactions and time travel. Section 2 | Spark Joins & Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"In a Transaction Table, Which Column Would You Use for Z-Ordering?","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Choose columns that are: (a) frequently used in WHERE clauses, and (b) have high cardinality. • transaction_date / event_timestamp — most queries filter by date range • customer_id — if queries frequently look up a specific customer • store_id / region_id — if queries filter by location Best Answer: \"I would Z-ORDER on transaction_date combined with customer_id\n• Most business queries filter by a date range AND a specific customer, so co-locating transactions for the same customer within the same date files maximizes file skipping.\" Avoid Z-ORDERing on low-cardinality columns like status (Active/Inactive) or boolean flags — data skipping will be minimal since files will contain both values anyway","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Default Column for Z-Order Indexing?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Lake has NO default Z-ORDER column\n• Z-ORDER must always be explicitly specified: OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date) OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date, customer_id) However, Delta Lake automatically collects min/max statistics for the first 32 columns\n• These enable automatic data skipping (file-level pruning) even without explicit Z-ORDER\n• You can control this: ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.dataSkippingNumIndexedCols' = '5' -- collect stats for first 5 columns only )","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Z-Order Indexes","type":"concept","a":"• Z-Ordering (Z-curve/space-filling curve) is a multi-dimensional data clustering technique that maps multi- column data to a single linear order while preserving proximity — values close in N-dimensional space end up in the same files\n• How it works step-by-step • OPTIMIZE interleaves the bit representations of selected column values using a Z-curve algorithm • Related rows (same customer, same date) are co-located into the same Parquet files • Delta records min/max statistics per file for each column • When you query with WHERE clause on Z-ORDERed columns, Spark compares query predicates against file stats • Files whose min/max range does not include the query value are skipped entirely (data skipping) Aspect Partitioning Z-ORDER Best Together Cardinality Low (date, region) High (customer_id) Both Structure Physical folders per Co-locates within files Partition + ZORDER value Over-partition risk Yes — too many small No Partition on date files Changes needed Hard to change after Re-run OPTIMIZE Combine both creation Command partitionBy(col) OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER Same table, both applied BY -- Best practice: partition by date + Z-order by high-cardinality column Page 4 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save('/delta/transactions') OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_type)","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Liquid Clustering (introduced in Databricks Runtime 13.3+) is a dynamic data layout feature that replaces the need for partitioning + Z-ORDER\n• It automatically reorganizes data based on clustering columns without requiring manual partition planning or full table rewrites\n• Key advantages • No upfront partition column planning — clustering columns can be changed anytime with ALTER TABLE • Incremental: only newly written data is clustered — no full table rewrites Page 7 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Avoids partition explosion from high-cardinality columns • Auto-clustering runs in the background (Databricks-managed) -- Create table with Liquid Clustering CREATE TABLE transactions (id BIGINT, customer_id STRING, amount DOUBLE, txn_date DATE) CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date); -- Change clustering columns anytime ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id); -- Manually trigger clustering OPTIMIZE transactions;\n• Feature Partitioning Z-ORDER Liquid Clustering Cardinality Low only High Any Column flexibility Hard to change Re-run OPTIMIZE Change anytime Incremental No No Yes File management Manual VACUUM Manual OPTIMIZE Automatic Partition explosion Yes No No","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What are Materialized Views?","type":"concept","a":"• A Materialized View (MV) is a view whose results are pre-computed and stored as a physical Delta table\n• Unlike standard views that re-run the query on every read, MVs read from the stored result — fast and low-cost at query time. • Results stored physically — reads are as fast as a regular Delta table scan • Auto-refreshed when source tables change (Unity Catalog manages this) • Supports incremental refresh — only processes changed/new data, not full recompute • Ideal for expensive aggregations, multi-table joins, and frequently-queried reports Page 8 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales AS SELECT txn_date, store_id, SUM(amount) AS total_sales, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY txn_date, store_id;\n• REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales;\n• Aspect Standard View Materialized View Data stored No Yes — physical Delta table Query speed Depends on source tables Fast — reads pre-computed result Freshness Always up-to-date Refreshed on schedule or source change Cost at query time Full query recomputed Just a Delta table scan Best use case Simple aliasing, simple selects Heavy aggregations, dashboards, reports Section 7 | File Ingestion & Auto Loader","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"CSV Files Contain Corrupted Rows with Extra Columns — How Do You Handle","type":"concept","a":"• Them? Spark provides three read modes for handling malformed records in CSV/JSON: Mode 1 — PERMISSIVE (default, recommended for production) Null-fills mismatched columns and captures the raw corrupt record in a special column. df = (spark.read .option('mode', 'PERMISSIVE') .option('columnNameOfCorruptRecord', '_corrupt_record') .schema(defined_schema) # schema required to use _corrupt_record .csv('/data/raw/*.csv') ) good_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNull()) bad_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNotNull()) # Write bad records to quarantine Delta table Page 11 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) bad_df.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/delta/quarantine/csv_errors') Mode 2 — DROPMALFORMED (silent drop) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED').csv('/data/*.csv') # Bad rows silently discarded — use only if data loss is acceptable Mode 3 — FAILFAST (dev/test only) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'FAILFAST').csv('/data/*.csv') # Throws exception on first bad row — useful during development Mode 4 — Delta Live Tables Expectations (modern approach) @dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_row_count', 'col_count = expected') @dlt.expect_or_quarantine('no_null_id', 'id IS NOT NULL') Best Practice: Use PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + write bad rows to a quarantine Delta table\n• This keeps all data visible (no silent losses), enables root-cause analysis, and supports reprocessing after the source issue is fixed\n• Section 9 | Architecture & Cluster","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you load incremental data if the source has no Last Modified (cid:415)mestamp?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the source does not have a Last Modified or watermark column, there are several approaches depending on the business scenario:  Use the primary key and compare source and target records.  Perform a Delta MERGE to insert new records and update changed records.  Use Change Data Capture (CDC) if supported by the source system.  Generate a hash of the business columns and compare hashes to detect changes.  As a last resort, perform a full load if no reliable change detec(cid:415)on mechanism exists\n• In my projects, using a business key with hash comparison and a Delta MERGE has been an effec(cid:415)ve approach.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Apply Changes in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Yes — using:  Delta Live Tables (DLT) APPLY CHANGES  Or manually using MERGE INTO Used for:  SCD handling (Type 1/2)  CDC inges(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Pipeline re-run: overwrite or mul(cid:415)ple files? Delta log?","type":"concept","a":"Behavior:  In ADLS: o Usually creates mul(cid:415)ple part files  In Delta Lake: o Uses transac(cid:415)on log (_delta_log) Delta Log:  Stores metadata of all transac(cid:415)ons  Enables: o Time travel o ACID compliance o Rollback Format:  Parquet + _delta_log (JSON logs)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks components used","type":"concept","a":" Notebooks  Jobs  Clusters  Delta Lake  DBFS  Repos  Workflows","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize Delta tables (OPTIMIZE, ZORDER).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"Common methods:  Watermark column (LastModifiedDate)  Timestamp  CDC (Change Data Capture)  Delta MERGE Example: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE LastModifiedDate > ( SELECT MAX(LastLoadedDate) FROM AuditTable );","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Data format in your project","type":"concept","a":"Typical formats:  CSV (landing)  JSON (APIs)  Parquet (op(cid:415)mized storage)  Delta (curated layers) Interview (cid:415)p: \"We primarily store curated data in Delta format because it provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and be(cid:425)er performance.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What are Delta Tables?","type":"concept","a":"Delta tables are built on top of Parquet and provide:  ACID transac(cid:415)ons  Time Travel  Schema Enforcement  Schema Evolu(cid:415)on  MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE support  Be(cid:425)er performance with OPTIMIZE and ZORDER","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Write a MERGE statement.","type":"concept","a":"MERGE INTO Target t USING Source s ON t.EmpID = s.EmpID WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.Name = s.Name, t.Salary = s.Salary WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( EmpID, Name, Salary ) VALUES ( s.EmpID, s.Name, s.Salary );","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental Loading loads only new or changed data since the last load, instead of reloading everything (full load)\n• Common Approaches: • Timestamp-based: Load records where updated_at > last_run_timestamp • Watermark-based: Track max ID or timestamp as high watermark • CDC (Change Data Capture): Capture inserts/updates/deletes from source • Delta Lake MERGE (UPSERT): Merge changes into target table Benefits: Faster, cheaper, reduces source load # PySpark incremental load using watermark last_load_time = '2025-01-01 00:00:00' incremental_df = spark.read.jdbc(...) \\ .filter(col('updated_at') > last_load_time) # Delta MERGE (UPSERT) from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/delta/customers') target.alias('t').merge( incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE vs Z-ORDER","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE: Compacts small Delta files into larger files to improve read performance and reduce file count\n• OPTIMIZE delta.`/delta/sales` -- or OPTIMIZE sales_table Z-ORDER: Co-locates related data in the same set of files by reordering data based on specified columns\n• Best for high-cardinality columns used in filters\n• OPTIMIZE sales_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, order_date); -- Difference: -- OPTIMIZE alone: compacts files (smaller → bigger) -- ZORDER: compacts AND reorders data within files for faster skipping • OPTIMIZE: Use always for compacting small files • ZORDER: Use on frequently filtered/joined columns • Together: Maximum benefit — compact + co-locate","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"High Concurrency Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Optimized for multiple concurrent users • Supports Python, SQL, R — NOT Scala • Enforces user isolation (each user's code is isolated) • Best for BI tools and SQL analytics","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evolution","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Evolution allows changes to a Delta table's schema (adding/modifying columns) without breaking existing pipelines\n• Options: • mergeSchema: Add new columns from incoming data • overwriteSchema: Replace the entire schema (use with caution) # Enable schema evolution on write df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/delta/customers') # In SQL - enable auto merge SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true; # Alter table to add column manually ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN (loyalty_tier STRING);","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Transaction Log & ACID Properties","type":"concept","a":"• Transaction Log (_delta_log): The Delta transaction log is a directory of JSON files that records every change made to a Delta table\n• Each commit produces a new JSON file\n• ACID Properties in Delta: • Atomicity: Each transaction either fully succeeds or fully fails — no partial writes • Consistency: Schema enforcement ensures valid data at all times • Isolation: Concurrent reads/writes are isolated — snapshot isolation by default • Durability: Once committed, data is permanently stored even after failure # View transaction log DESCRIBE HISTORY orders_table; # Each JSON in _delta_log contains: # - add: files added # - remove: files removed/deleted # - metaData: schema changes # - protocol: reader/writer versions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Move Data from Delta Table to Another Location","type":"concept","a":"# Option 1: Write to new Delta location df = spark.read.format('delta').load('/delta/source_table') df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/delta/destination_table') # Option 2: DEEP CLONE (preserves history & metadata) CREATE TABLE destination_table DEEP CLONE source_table LOCATION '/delta/destination_table'; # Option 3: SHALLOW CLONE (references original files) CREATE TABLE destination_table SHALLOW CLONE source_table; # Option 4: Export to CSV/Parquet df.write.format('parquet').save('/output/parquet_export') df.write.format('csv').option('header', 'true').save('/output/csv_export')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental load","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load is a technique where we load only new or updated data instead of full refresh\n• I implement it using watermark columns like last_updated or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table and extract only delta data in each run\n• Then I load it into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• Finally, I update the watermark a(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on to ensure no duplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"What are the few small differences you see in databricks now comparing with 3 years","type":"concept","a":"back (only small differences not like unity catalog) “Over the last few years, Databricks has improved mainly in performance, automa(cid:415)on, and governance rather than just core func(cid:415)onality.” (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Key small differences: (cid:48132) a) Performance improvements  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) is much be(cid:425)er now  Faster shuffle handling  Be(cid:425)er auto-op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on of joins (cid:48131) b) Photon engine maturity  Much faster SQL execu(cid:415)on compared to earlier versions  Be(cid:425)er vectorized processing (cid:44931) c) Auto op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on features  Auto compac(cid:415)on of small files  Improved OPTIMIZE + ZORDER efficiency (cid:48130) d) Improved job orchestra(cid:415)on  Be(cid:425)er Workflows UI  Retry policies, task dependencies improved (cid:48133) e) Be(cid:425)er Delta Lake stability  More stable MERGE opera(cid:415)ons  Be(cid:425)er schema evolu(cid:415)on handling","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"“Yes, I have used both OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on environments in Delta Lake tables.” (cid:48132) OPTIMIZE  Used to compact small files  Improves read performance OPTIMIZE table_name; (cid:48131) ZORDER (with OPTIMIZE)  Improves query filtering OPTIMIZE table_name ZORDER BY (customer_id); (cid:44931) VACUUM  Removes old/unnecessary files VACUUM table_name; “We ensure reten(cid:415)on period is respected before running VACUUM to avoid dele(cid:415)ng files needed for (cid:415)me travel.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"spark","name":"Spark Fundamentals","icon":"⚡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the Apache Spark architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Spark uses a master-worker (driver-executor) model:\n• Driver — runs the user code, builds the DAG (logical plan), creates SparkContext, schedules tasks, collects results.\n• Cluster Manager — allocates resources (Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos). In Databricks it's managed.\n• Executors — JVM processes on worker nodes that run tasks and cache partitions in memory/disk.\n• Task — smallest unit of work (one per partition).\n• Stage — set of tasks with no shuffle boundary. Job — triggered by an action.\nFlow: action → Driver → DAG Scheduler splits into stages at shuffles → Task Scheduler sends tasks to executors → results returned."},{"q":"Explain DAG, Jobs, Stages, and Tasks in Spark.","type":"concept","a":"• DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) — the logical execution plan of transformations; built lazily.\n• Job — created when an action is called; a DAG broken at shuffle boundaries.\n• Stage — a set of transformations that can run without a shuffle (narrow pipeline); shuffle starts a new stage.\n• Task — one task per partition per stage; executes on an executor core.\nSo: 1 action → 1+ Jobs → each Job has Stages → each Stage has Tasks (= number of partitions)."},{"q":"What is the difference between narrow and wide transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow — each input partition contributes to exactly one output partition; no shuffle. Examples: map, filter, select, withColumn, union, mapPartitions. Data stays local → fast.\n• Wide — data from many input partitions must be reorganized into output partitions via a shuffle. Examples: groupBy/aggregate, join (non-broadcast), distinct, repartition, orderBy, sort.\nRule: wide transformations cause shuffles → new stages → usually the performance bottleneck."},{"q":"What is lazy evaluation in Spark and why is it useful?","type":"concept","a":"Spark does not execute transformations immediately; it records them and builds a logical plan (DAG). Execution happens only when an action is called.\n• Transformations (lazy): filter, select, join, groupBy, withColumn.\n• Actions (trigger execution): count, collect, show, write, save, take.\nBenefits: the Catalyst optimizer can optimize the whole plan (predicate pushdown, column pruning, join reordering), fewer passes over data, and avoids unnecessary computation."},{"q":"On what basis does Spark create stages?","type":"concept","a":"Stages are split at shuffle boundaries.\n• A stage = a sequence of narrow transformations that can be pipelined together without moving data across the network.\n• A new stage begins whenever a wide transformation (shuffle) is needed — e.g., groupBy, sort, non-broadcast join, repartition.\n• DAG Scheduler creates stages; each stage is submitted as a set of tasks (one per partition) to the Task Scheduler.\nMinimizing shuffles (fewer stages) is a key optimization goal."},{"q":"What is the Catalyst optimizer and how does it optimize queries?","type":"concept","a":"Catalyst is Spark's rule-based query optimizer for SQL/DataFrames. Phases:\n1. Analysis — resolves columns/tables and validates schema.\n2. Logical optimization — rule-based: predicate pushdown, constant folding, column pruning, filter reordering, join reorder.\n3. Physical planning — chooses join strategy (broadcast/sort-merge) and operators based on cost/stats.\n4. Code generation — Whole-Stage CodeGen / Tungsten emits optimized JVM bytecode.\nInspect with df.explain(True) or explain('formatted'). Using built-in functions (not UDFs) lets Catalyst optimize them."},{"q":"What is Tungsten in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"Tungsten is Spark's execution engine focused on CPU/memory efficiency:\n• Off-heap binary memory management (reduces GC overhead).\n• Cache-aware computation and explicit memory layout.\n• Whole-Stage Code Generation — fuses operators into a single optimized function, avoiding virtual function calls.\nResult: much faster execution and lower memory usage than the original Spark engine."},{"q":"What is the difference between RDD, DataFrame, and Dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD — low-level, untyped distributed collection; full control, no schema; no Catalyst optimization; slowest but most flexible.\n• DataFrame — distributed table with named columns + schema; runs through Catalyst + Tungsten; best for structured data (Python/Scala/Java/R).\n• Dataset — typed JVM objects (Scala/Java only); compile-time type safety + Catalyst; Python has no Dataset (uses DataFrame).\nPrefer DataFrames/Datasets over RDDs for performance; use RDDs only for fine-grained control."},{"q":"What are the main Databricks cluster types?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose (interactive) — development, notebooks, ad-hoc; shared; billed while running.\n• Job (automated) — created for a job, terminates on completion; cheapest for production pipelines.\n• High Concurrency — many concurrent users/BI; user isolation; SQL/Python/R (not Scala).\n• Single Node — driver only, no workers; dev/testing/small data.\n• SQL Warehouse — optimized for Databricks SQL/BI (Power BI, Tableau); serverless/pro/classic.\n• Serverless — fully managed compute, no cluster config.\nBest practice: Job clusters for production, All-Purpose for dev, SQL Warehouse for BI."},{"q":"Difference between All-Purpose Cluster and Job Cluster?","type":"concept","a":"• All-Purpose: manually started, stays up (auto-terminate 30–120 min), shared by multiple users/notebooks, warm, higher cost. For interactive development.\n• Job: created automatically from job config, fresh environment each run, isolated, terminated after completion (no idle cost). For scheduled/production jobs.\nUse Job clusters for all production pipelines to control cost and ensure clean, isolated runs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"scenario","a":"• No distributed processing — one machine is both driver and worker.\n• Driver bottleneck — it coordinates AND executes, so it's overloaded.\n• Memory pressure — large datasets/joins can exceed node memory (OOM/spill).\n• CPU saturation — all tasks compete for the same 16 cores; long runtimes.\n• No fault tolerance — if the node fails, the whole workload fails.\nSuitable only for dev/testing/small data — not for production ETL."},{"q":"What is the Databricks Runtime (DBR)?","type":"concept","a":"DBR is Databricks' optimized, curated software stack for clusters. It bundles:\n• Apache Spark (specific version), Delta Lake, and performance optimizations (Photon).\n• Language runtimes: Python, Scala, Java, R, SQL.\n• Optimized libraries and security patches.\nVariants: Standard, ML, GPU, Photon, and LTS (Long-Term Support). Prefer LTS in production for stability/longer support."},{"q":"Difference between repartition and coalesce?","type":"concept","a":"• repartition(n) — full shuffle; can increase OR decrease partitions; even distribution; expensive. Use to fix skew or before heavy joins/aggregations.\n• coalesce(n) — reduces partitions WITHOUT a full shuffle (merges local partitions); cheap; used after filtering to reduce small files. Cannot increase partitions.\n• repartition(n, col) — hash-partition by column to co-locate join keys.\nExample: df.repartition(200, 'customer_id'); df.coalesce(4)."},{"q":"What is the default partition size and default number of shuffle partitions?","type":"concept","a":"• Target partition/file size in Spark/Delta — ~128 MB (block/file size).\n• Default shuffle partitions — spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200.\n• Default parallelism — equals the number of executor cores.\nTune shuffle partitions to data size (too few → huge tasks/OOM; too many → tiny tasks/overhead). AQE can coalesce partitions automatically at runtime."},{"q":"Difference between cache() and persist()?","type":"concept","a":"• cache() — stores the DataFrame/RDD using MEMORY_AND_DISK storage level; lazy (materializes on first action).\n• persist(level) — same but you choose the storage level: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK_SER, etc.\nBoth are lazy transformations; trigger with an action (count/write). Use when a DataFrame is reused 2+ times (iterative ML, repeated joins).\nAlways unpersist() when done to free memory."},{"q":"What is a broadcast join and when is it used?","type":"concept","a":"A broadcast join replicates a SMALL table to every executor so the large table doesn't need to be shuffled.\n• Use when one side is small (< ~10 MB default autoBroadcastJoinThreshold, up to ~200 MB feasible).\n• Eliminates the shuffle of the big table → big performance gain and also mitigates skew.\nPySpark: large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id'); SQL: /*+ BROADCAST(small) */.\nTypical for joining a fact with a small lookup/dimension table."},{"q":"What is data skew and how do you detect and fix it?","type":"concept","a":"Skew = uneven data distribution across partitions so a few tasks process far more data (stragglers).\nDetect: Spark UI shows one/few tasks much longer, uneven shuffle read sizes, one executor overloaded while others idle.\nFix:\n• Salting — add a random prefix/suffix to the skewed key to spread it.\n• Enable AQE skew join (spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true) — splits skewed partitions automatically.\n• Broadcast join for the small side.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key; filter early.\n• Isolate and process the hot key separately.","code":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\nspark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true')\n# Salting\ndf = df.withColumn('salt', (F.rand()*10).cast('int'))\ndf = df.withColumn('salted_key', F.concat(col('key'), F.lit('_'), col('salt')))"},{"q":"What is bucketing and when would you use it?","type":"concept","a":"Bucketing pre-shuffles data into a fixed number of buckets by hash of a column, persisted with the table (e.g., bucketBy(50,'customer_id').saveAsTable(...)).\n• Benefit: repeated joins/aggregations on the bucketed key avoid shuffles (sort-merge join becomes shuffle-free).\n• Differs from partitioning: partitioning creates directories (low cardinality); bucketing creates fixed files (higher cardinality, even distribution).\nUse for large, frequently joined tables on the same key. In Databricks, Liquid Clustering is now often preferred over manual bucketing/partitioning."},{"q":"What is a shuffle and how do you reduce it?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffle = redistribution of data across executors (network + disk I/O) for wide transformations like join, groupBy, distinct, sort.\nReduce it by: broadcast joins for small tables, filtering/aggregating before joins, bucketing on join keys, using map-side partial aggregation (reduceByKey over groupByKey), avoiding unnecessary repartition/distinct, and enabling AQE.\nWatch shuffle read/write metrics in the Spark UI — it's usually the top cost."},{"q":"What is Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) and its benefits?","type":"concept","a":"AQE re-optimizes the physical plan at runtime using actual statistics gathered after each stage. Benefits:\n• Dynamically coalesces shuffle partitions (reduces tiny tasks).\n• Switches join strategies (e.g., sort-merge → broadcast) based on actual sizes.\n• Detects and splits skewed partitions (skew join handling).\nEnable: spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true (plus skewJoin + coalescePartitions). Default on in Spark 3.x/Databricks runtime."},{"q":"What is Photon?","type":"concept","a":"Photon is Databricks' vectorized C++ query engine. It replaces the JVM execution for many SQL/DataFrame operators with native, columnar, vectorized execution.\n• Delivers ~3–10x speedups for SQL/scan/aggregation/join-heavy workloads.\n• Drop-in: enable in cluster config; no code changes.\n• Best for SQL and Delta workloads; some UDF-heavy or non-supported ops fall back to the JVM."},{"q":"How do you use the Spark UI to troubleshoot performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Jobs/Stages tabs — find slow stages; check task durations and counts.\n• Tasks — spot skew (one task far longer), spill to disk, retries.\n• SQL/DataFrame tab — inspect the physical plan, exchanges (shuffles), and per-operator metrics.\n• Storage tab — what's cached and its size.\n• Executors tab — memory/GC, shuffle read/write, disk spill.\nUse these to decide: broadcast, repartition, AQE, more partitions, or bigger cluster."},{"q":"How do you decide the size of a Databricks cluster?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster sizing depends on data volume, number of files, transformation complexity, SLA, and concurrency — it can't be predicted, so iterate:\n1. Start with a medium cluster (2–8 nodes) matching the workload class (F/DSv2 for ETL, DSv2 for ML, L for analytics).\n2. Run end-to-end on representative data; measure CPU, memory, I/O, shuffle.\n3. Remove bottlenecks: CPU-bound → add nodes/cores; network-bound → fewer bigger SSD machines; disk/spill-bound → more memory.\n4. Repeat 3–4 times on increasing data sizes; prefer fewer big instances over many small (1 executor/machine) for batch ETL, enable autoscaling.\nFirst guess: scale linearly from a small test (e.g., 5 nodes for 100 TB ≈ 50 nodes for 1 PB), then refine."},{"q":"What are the key cluster configuration options in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster mode: Single Node / Standard / High Concurrency (or Serverless).\n• Runtime version (prefer LTS, e.g., 15.4 LTS).\n• Driver + worker VM types (workers can be sized independently).\n• Worker count with autoscaling (min/max) or fixed.\n• Photon on/off; instance pools for fast start.\n• Auto-termination (idle timeout).\n• Access mode / Unity Catalog mode (Single user, Shared, No isolation).\n• Cluster policies, tags, init scripts, and Spark configs."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks job that normally finishes quickly is now taking far longer. How do you investigate?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the job run → Spark UI; find the slow stage and inspect task times.\n• Check for data skew (one task much longer), shuffle size, and spill-to-disk in the Tasks/Executors tabs.\n• Check input data volume/growth and partition count; look for small-file explosion.\n• Check cluster health — resource contention (shared cluster), GC, executor loss, autoscaling lag.\n• Compare the current physical plan vs a known-good run; check join strategy changes (broadcast vs sort-merge).\n• Look for recently changed code (new joins/UDFs), source schema changes, or upstream backlog.\n• Remedy: tune partitions, enable AQE/Photon, broadcast small tables, salt skewed keys, scale cluster, OPTIMIZE tables."},{"q":"What is the difference between Spark SQL, DataFrame API, and RDD API?","type":"concept","a":"• RDD API — lowest level, no schema, no Catalyst; maximum control but least optimization.\n• DataFrame API — structured, schema-aware, optimized by Catalyst/Tungsten; expressive transforms.\n• Spark SQL — declarative SQL strings over the same engine (also Catalyst-optimized); easy for analysts and interop.\nAll compile to the same optimized plan; use DataFrames/SQL for day-to-day, RDDs only for niche low-level needs."},{"q":"Why is PySpark used and how does it interface with the JVM?","type":"concept","a":"PySpark is Spark's Python API. Spark is written in Scala and runs on the JVM; PySpark uses Py4J to let Python code call JVM objects.\n• Benefits: Python ecosystem (pandas, ML libs), ease of use, distributed processing across a cluster.\n• Note: Python UDFs incur serialization overhead (row-by-row), so prefer Spark built-in functions / pandas UDFs (vectorized) for performance.\n• Needs Java installed alongside Python and Spark for local development."},{"q":"What is SCD Type 1? How will you implement it?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 1 overwrites exis(cid:415)ng records and does not preserve history\n• Example: Old 101 John Chennai New 101 John Bangalore Result 101 John Bangalore History is lost\n• PySpark MERGE Example Requirement: If Selling_Period is NULL or 0, do not insert or update. from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, \"customer\") ( target.alias(\"t\") .merge( source.alias(\"s\"), \"t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID\" ) .whenMatchedUpdate( condi(cid:415)on=\"s.Selling_Period IS NOT NULL AND s.Selling_Period <> 0\", set={ \"CustomerName\":\"s.CustomerName\", \"City\":\"s.City\", \"Selling_Period\":\"s.Selling_Period\" } ) .whenNotMatchedInsert( condi(cid:415)on=\"s.Selling_Period IS NOT NULL AND s.Selling_Period <> 0\", values={ \"CustomerID\":\"s.CustomerID\", \"CustomerName\":\"s.CustomerName\", \"City\":\"s.City\", \"Selling_Period\":\"s.Selling_Period\" } ) .execute() This sa(cid:415)sfies the condi(cid:415)on that records with Selling_Period equal to NULL or 0 are ignored for both updates and inserts","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Small Table? How does Auto Loader know which files are already processed?","type":"concept","a":"• Small Table A small table is a table that is small enough to fit in the memory of each executor\n• Spark uses Broadcast Join for small tables\n• Example:  Customer table = 20 MB  Sales table = 500 GB Spark broadcasts the customer table to all workers instead of shuffling the huge sales table. from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast sales.join(broadcast(customers), \"customer_id\") How Auto Loader tracks processed files Auto Loader maintains a checkpoint\n• The checkpoint stores metadata about:  Files already processed  Offsets  Schema informa(cid:415)on  Progress When new files arrive:","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning? How does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"• Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning organizes data into separate folders based on one or more columns\n• Example: Sales Country=USA Country=India Country=Canada df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"Country\").saveAsTable(\"sales\") Benefits Suppose the table has 1 TB of data\n• Query: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE Country='India';\n• Without par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning:  Spark scans the en(cid:415)re 1 TB\n• With par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning:  Spark scans only the Country=India par(cid:415)(cid:415)on, greatly reducing I/O and improving performance\n• Choose par(cid:415)(cid:415)on columns that:  Are frequently used in filters.  Have moderate cardinality (not too few or too many dis(cid:415)nct values)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Spark","type":"concept","a":"A. Spark it is Inmemory parallel space and it will use ram inmemory for read & write operations","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is diff between Map reduce and Spark","type":"concept","a":"• S.No\n• Map Reduce Spark","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is having a very slow speed as It is much faster than Map Reduce.","type":"concept","a":"compared to Apache Spark.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It actually needs other queries to It has Spark SQL as its very own query","type":"concept","a":"perform the task. language.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the architecture of spark","type":"concept","a":"➢ Driver Node ➢ Cluster Manager ➢ Worker Node: In This, there is a Executor and Task","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what are different languages supported in Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Python or Pyspark ➢ Scala ➢ Sql ➢ R-Language 9.What is DAG A\n• A directed acyclic graph (DAG) is a conceptual representation of a series of activities\n• The order of the activities is depicted by a graph, which is visually presented as a set of circles, each one representing an activity, some of which are connected by lines, which represent the flow from one activity to another","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Languages using in your project","type":"concept","a":"A. Pyspark and Scala","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is RDD, Data frame, Data Set","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ RDD: A Resilient Distributed Dataset (RDD), the basic abstraction in Spark\n• Represents an immutable, partitioned collection of elements that can be operated on in parallel. ➢ Data Frame: A Data Frame is equivalent to a relational table in Spark SQL, and can be created using various functions ➢ Dataset: A Dataset is a distributed collection of data\n• Dataset is a new interface added in Spark 1.6 that provides the benefits of RDDs","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to convert RDD into Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"• Convert Spark RDD to Data Frame and Dataset as these provide more advantages over RDD\n• For instance, Data Frame is a distributed collection of data organized into named columns similar to Database tables and provides optimization and performance improvement\n• Syntax: val dfFromRDD1 = rdd.toDF() dfFromRDD1.printSchema()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Transformation and Action","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Transformation: Transformation refers to the operation applied on a RDD to create new RDD\n• Filter, groupBy and map are the examples of transformations. ➢ Actions: Actions refer to an operation which also applies on RDD, that instructs Spark to perform computation and send the result back to driver\n• Page | 13","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to create Data Frame","type":"concept","a":"A. # Create DataFrame. df = pd.DataFrame(data) # Print the output. print(df)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to read Data from csv file to create data frame","type":"concept","a":"A. usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to remove duplicate values from Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. dropdf = df.dropDuplicates([\"department\",\"salary\"]) print(\"Distinct count of department & salary :\"+ str(dropdf.count()))","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to add new column into Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to write sql commands on Dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. %sql select * from user","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to select only few columns from dataframe","type":"concept","a":"• In PySpark we can select columns using the select() function\n• The select() function allows us to select single or multiple columns in different formats\n• Syntax: df.select(\"firstname\",\"lastname\").show() df.select(df.firstname,df.lastname).show() df.select(df[\"firstname\"],df[\"lastname\"]).show() #By using col() function from pyspark.sql.functions import col df.select(col(\"firstname\"),col(\"lastname\")).show() #Select columns by regular expression df.select(df.colRegex(\"`^.*name*`\")).show()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to drop columns from dataframe","type":"concept","a":"A. \"\" import col is required \"\"\" df.drop(col(\"firstname\")) \\ .printSchema() Page | 14","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is diff between repartition and coalesce","type":"concept","a":"A. The repartition() the number of partitions can be increased/decreased, but with coalesce() the number of partitions can only be decreased and The repartition algorithm does a full shuffle of the data and creates equal sized partitions of data. coalesce combines existing partitions to avoid a full shuffle.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is caching and persistence","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ A\n• Caching or persistence are optimization techniques for (iterative and interactive) Spark computations\n• They help saving interim partial results so they can be reused in subsequent stages\n• These interim results as RDDs are thus kept in memory (default) or more solid storage like disk and/or replicated. ➢ These functions can be used to adjust the storage level of a RDD\n• When freeing up memory, Spark will use the storage level identifier to decide which partitions should be kept\n• The parameter less variants persist() and cache() are just abbreviations","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what are broad cast variables and broadcast joins","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Broadcast variables allow the programmer to keep a read-only variable cached on each machine rather than shipping a copy of it with tasks. ➢ Broadcast join is an important part of Spark SQL's execution engine\n• When used, it performs a join on two relations by first broadcasting the smaller one to all Spark executors, then evaluating the join criteria with each executor's partitions of the other relation","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Task Dura(cid:415)on Imbalance:","type":"concept","a":"➢ Use the Spark UI (h(cid:425)p://<spark-driver>:4040) → Stages tab. ➢ Look for stages where a few tasks take significantly longer than others. ➢ If most tasks finish in seconds while a few take minutes, it strongly indicates data skew.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Skewed Shuffle Par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:","type":"concept","a":"➢ Check for stages involving wide transforma(cid:415)ons like groupByKey(), join(), or reduceByKey(). ➢ Under the \"Tasks\" tab, look at the input size and run (cid:415)me of each task. ➢ Skewed keys will show dispropor(cid:415)onate data sizes for specific par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Logs and Metrics:","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Enable detailed logging (especially for jobs involving joins or aggrega(cid:415)ons). ➢ Use .rdd.getNumPar(cid:415)(cid:415)ons() or .rdd.glom().map(len).collect() to inspect distribu(cid:415)on manually\n• How to Resolve Data Skew in PySpark","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid groupByKey()","type":"concept","a":"➢Prefer reduceByKey() or aggregateByKey() which are more efficient and minimizeshuffles.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Skew Join Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on (in Spark 3+)","type":"concept","a":"➢If enabled, Spark can automa(cid:415)cally handle skew joins using adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on (AQE).","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"read employees.csv (schema same as employees table) into a dataframe df_emp","type":"concept","a":"add a column grade in df_emp, if salary > 10000 then A else B from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import when, col # Read CSV into DataFrame df_emp = spark.read \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"header\", \"true\") \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", \"true\") \\ .csv(\"employees.csv\") # Add grade column df_emp = df_emp.withColumn( \"grade\", when(col(\"salary\") > 10000, \"A\").otherwise(\"B\") )","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are the read modes available in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• When reading data, Spark supports different modes for handling malformed records\n• Mode Descrip(cid:415)on PERMISSIVE (Default) Invalid records are set to null, malformed records are captured\n• DROPMALFORMED Drops malformed records\n• FAILFAST Stops execu(cid:415)on if a malformed record is found\n• Example: df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"mode\", \"PERMISSIVE\").csv(\"/input/data.csv\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How are Jobs, Stages, and Tasks created in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Execu(cid:415)on hierarchy: Applica(cid:415)on │ ▼ Job │ ▼ Stage │ ▼ Task  Applica(cid:415)on: Spark program.  Job: Created when an ac(cid:415)on (show(), count(), collect(), write()) is executed.  Stage: Created based on shuffle boundaries.  Task: Smallest execu(cid:415)on unit, one task per par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Example: df = spark.read.csv(\"sales.csv\") df.filter(\"amount>100\").groupBy(\"city\").sum().show()  show() → 1 Job  groupBy() causes a shuffle → mul(cid:415)ple stages  Each par(cid:415)(cid:415)on becomes a task","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Read CSV, create DataFrame, and write Delta to ADLS","type":"concept","a":"df = spark.read \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True) \\ .op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", True) \\ .csv(\"abfss://raw@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/customer.csv\") Write to Delta: df.write \\ .format(\"delta\") \\ .mode(\"overwrite\") \\ .save(\"abfss://silver@storageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/customer\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Spark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"Driver Program │ Cluster Manager │ Executors │ Tasks Driver  Creates SparkSession  Creates DAG  Schedules jobs  Coordinates executors Cluster Manager  Allocates resources Examples:  Standalone  YARN  Kubernetes Executors  Execute tasks  Store cached data  Return results Worker Nodes Machines where executors run.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Managed and External Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Managed Table External Table Spark manages data and metadata Spark manages only metadata Dropping table deletes data Dropping table keeps data Default storage loca(cid:415)on User specifies loca(cid:415)on Suitable for internal data Suitable for shared/external data Example: Managed: CREATE TABLE sales (...);\n• External: CREATE TABLE sales USING DELTA LOCATION '/mnt/sales';","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Spark Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques","type":"concept","a":"• Common op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques include:  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning – Improve parallel processing.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on vs\n• Coalesce – Increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons efficiently.  Cache/Persist – Reuse frequently accessed DataFrames.  Broadcast Join – Broadcast small tables to avoid shuffles.  Predicate Pushdown – Filter data at the source.  Column Pruning – Read only required columns.  Avoid collect() – Prevent moving large datasets to the driver.  Use Delta Lake – Leverage transac(cid:415)on support and performance op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons.  Op(cid:415)mize file sizes – Avoid too many small files.  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) – Dynamically op(cid:415)mize joins and shuffle par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons at run(cid:415)me.  Op(cid:415)mize Z-Ordering (Delta Lake) – Improve data skipping for frequently filtered columns.  Use appropriate file formats – Prefer Parquet or Delta over CSV for analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Check Spark UI","type":"concept","a":" Iden(cid:415)fy slow stages / skewed tasks","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Check shuffle","type":"concept","a":"•  Reduce wide transforma(cid:415)ons (groupBy, join) 4\n• Use caching df.cache()","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Use AQE op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":" Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain PySpark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"(cid:44934) Components:  Driver o Creates SparkContext o Converts code into DAG  Cluster Manager o Allocates resources (YARN / Databricks)  Executors o Run tasks on par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons (cid:44934) Flow:","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Code → Driver","type":"concept","a":"2. DAG created","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Stages formed","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Tasks sent to executors","type":"concept","a":"","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Transformation in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• In Apache Spark (used by Databricks), every transformation is either Narrow or Wide\n• This classification directly affects how fast your pipeline runs\n• Narrow Transformations Definition: Each partition of input produces exactly one partition of output\n• No data movement between nodes\n• Fast and efficient\n• Examples: • filter() / where() — Remove rows based on a condition • select() — Choose specific columns • map() — Apply a function to each row • withColumn() — Add or modify a column • union() — Combine two DataFrames with same schema # Narrow transformation example — no shuffle at all from pyspark.sql.functions import col, upper # filter: each partition processed independently, no movement df_filtered = df.filter(col('sales_amount') > 1000) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 1 # select: columns picked per partition, no shuffle df_small = df_filtered.select('customer_id','region','sales_amount') # withColumn: computed row-by-row within the same partition df_final = df_small.withColumn('region_upper', upper(col('region'))) Wide Transformations Definition: Data from multiple partitions is combined and redistributed across nodes\n• Causes a SHUFFLE — most expensive operation in Spark\n• Creates a new stage boundary in the execution plan\n• Examples: • groupBy() + agg() — Group data across all partitions • join() — Match rows from two separate DataFrames • distinct() — Remove duplicates (needs to see all rows globally) • orderBy() / sort() — Sort across all partitions globally • repartition() — Redistribute into new number of partitions # Wide transformation example — SHUFFLE happens here from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, count # groupBy: Spark must move all rows with same 'region' # to the same partition before aggregating df_summary = df.groupBy('region').agg( sum('sales_amount').alias('total_sales'), count('customer_id').alias('num_customers') ) # join: both DataFrames shuffled to align matching keys df_joined = df_sales.join(df_customers, on='customer_id', how='inner') Side-by-Side Comparison Feature Narrow Transformation Wide Transformation Data Movement No shuffle — stays in same Full shuffle — data moves across partition all nodes Performance Very fast Slower — network + disk I/O overhead Examples filter, select, map, withColumn, groupBy, join, distinct, orderBy, union repartition Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 2 Stage Boundary No — same Spark stage Yes — creates a new stage When to use Column ops, filtering, cleaning Aggregations, joins, deduplication Interview Tip: Say in interview: 'Narrow transformations stay within a single partition — no shuffle\n• Wide transformations require shuffling data across the network and always create a stage boundary\n• We minimize wide transformations and use broadcast joins for small lookup tables to avoid expensive shuffles.' Q3 Coalesce vs Repartition","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Why Do Partitions Matter?","type":"concept","a":"• Spark splits data into partitions and processes them in parallel\n• Too many small partitions = scheduler overhead\n• Too few large partitions = memory pressure and slow tasks\n• Both coalesce and repartition change the partition count, but very differently. coalesce(n) Key Facts: Reduces partitions by merging existing ones together\n• Does NOT trigger a full shuffle — very efficient\n• Can only REDUCE partition count, not increase it\n• May result in slightly uneven partition sizes. # Before: 200 partitions (created from large file read) print(df.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 200 # coalesce: merge down to 10 — no shuffle needed df_coalesced = df.coalesce(10) print(df_coalesced.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 10 # Best use case: reduce output file count before writing df_coalesced.write.mode('overwrite').parquet('/mnt/output/sales/') # Writes 10 parquet files instead of 200 tiny ones # WARNING: coalesce does not rebalance — some partitions # may be larger than others (slight skew is OK here) repartition(n) Key Facts: Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 3 Redistributes data EVENLY across n new partitions\n• Always triggers a full shuffle — more expensive\n• Can both INCREASE and DECREASE partition count\n• Can also partition by a specific column (hash partitioning). # repartition: even redistribution — triggers full shuffle df_rep = df.repartition(50) print(df_rep.rdd.getNumPartitions()) # 50 # repartition by column — all rows with same key go to same partition # Great before a join to avoid double-shuffle df_by_region = df.repartition(20, 'region') # Pre-partition both sides of a join on the join key: df_sales_r = df_sales.repartition(50, 'customer_id') df_cust_r = df_customers.repartition(50, 'customer_id') df_joined = df_sales_r.join(df_cust_r, 'customer_id') # Result: join is much faster — data already co-located!","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"When to Use Which?","type":"concept","a":"Scenario Use Reduce partitions before writing output coalesce — no shuffle, fast Increase partitions for more parallelism repartition — only option that can increase Fix data skew (uneven partition sizes) repartition — even redistribution Pre-partition before a join on a key repartition(n, 'key') — co-locate data After heavy filter reduced data volume coalesce — fewer tasks, no shuffle Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'coalesce reduces partitions without a shuffle — I use it before writing output files. repartition does a full shuffle to evenly redistribute data — I use it when I need to increase parallelism, fix data skew, or co-locate data by a join key.' Q4 Schema Enforcement vs Schema Evolution","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Spark Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Apache Spark uses a Master-Worker (Driver-Executor) distributed computing model with the following components: Component Role Key Detail Driver Runs user code; builds DAG;\n• Creates SparkContext; schedules coordinates execution tasks Cluster Manager Resource allocation across nodes Standalone / YARN / Kubernetes / Mesos Executor Runs tasks; caches partitions in JVM processes on worker nodes memory or disk Task Smallest unit of work (one per Sent from Task Scheduler to Executors partition) Stage Set of tasks with no shuffle boundary Created by DAG Scheduler Job Triggered by an action (collect, write, Broken into Stages count) DAG Logical execution plan Built lazily; executed only on action Execution Flow • User calls an action (df.write(), df.count()) • Driver sends job to DAG Scheduler • DAG Scheduler splits into Stages at shuffle boundaries • Task Scheduler sends Tasks to Executors • Executors run tasks, return results to Driver Key concept: Lazy Evaluation — transformations (filter, select, join) build the DAG but do NOT execute\n• Only when an action is called does Spark execute the full optimized plan through the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten engine\n• Page 1 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Join","type":"concept","a":"• Broadcast small tables (under 200 MB) to avoid shuffle in joins result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"AQE — Adaptive Query Execution","type":"concept","a":"• Dynamically re-optimizes query plan at runtime based on actual data stats spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled', 'true')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Photon Engine","type":"concept","a":"• Enable Photon in Databricks cluster config — 3-10x faster SQL and Delta operations 6. Caching • Cache frequently reused DataFrames: df.cache() or df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Filter Pushdown — Apply filters early","type":"concept","a":"# Apply filter BEFORE join — reduces shuffle data df_filtered = df.filter(col('status') == 'ACTIVE') result = df_filtered.join(df_other, 'id')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tune Shuffle Partitions","type":"concept","a":"spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '200') # default 200; tune to data size","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• Technique How When to Use Broadcast Join broadcast(small_df) One table < 200 MB — eliminates shuffle Predicate Pushdown filter() before join/groupBy Reduce data early before expensive ops Column Pruning select() only needed columns Avoid reading unused columns from Parquet Caching df.cache() or df.persist() DataFrame reused 2+ times in same job Repartition df.repartition(n, col) Before heavy groupBy/join on skewed data Coalesce df.coalesce(n) Reduce partitions after filter (no shuffle) reduceByKey vs groupByKey reduceByKey does partial local Aggregation on RDDs — less shuffle aggregation AQE spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true Runtime query plan re-optimization Avoid UDFs Use built-in Spark functions UDFs bypass Catalyst optimizer Bucketing bucketBy(n, col).saveAsTable() Eliminate shuffle for repeated large joins from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast, col from pyspark.sql import functions as F Page 7 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide # 1\n• Broadcast join result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id') # 2\n• Filter early + column pruning df = df.filter(col('status')=='ACTIVE').select('id','name','amount') # 3\n• Cache for reuse df.cache() count1 = df.count() # triggers cache result = df.groupBy('dept').sum('salary') # 4\n• Coalesce (no shuffle) vs repartition (with shuffle) df_small = df.filter(col('year')==2024).coalesce(4) # after big filter df_even = df.repartition(200, col('customer_id')) # even distribution # 5\n• AQE + Photon config spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '200')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Remove / Handle Nulls Using PySpark","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Drop rows with any null","type":"concept","a":"Page 14 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide df_clean = df.dropna() # drop row if ANY column is null","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"External Tables vs Managed Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Aspect Managed Table External Table Data location Databricks/Spark manages the YOU specify the data location (ADLS/S3) storage location DROP TABLE behavior Deletes metadata AND the data Deletes only metadata — data files files remain Data ownership Owned by Databricks/Spark Owned by external storage (ADLS Gen2) Use case Temporary/intermediate tables;\n• Shared data accessed by multiple tools DW tables Schema evolution Managed by Databricks You manage schema;\n• Spark reads it Performance Slightly faster (local storage Depends on network/storage optimized) performance Typical format Delta (default in Databricks) Parquet, Delta, CSV, ORC, JSON Page 20 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Multi-tool access Databricks only (unless Unity Any tool that can read the storage path Catalog) Create Managed Table -- Managed Table (Databricks controls storage location) CREATE TABLE silver.customers ( customer_id INT, name STRING, email STRING, created_date DATE ); -- Or from DataFrame df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('silver.customers') -- Data stored at: /user/hive/warehouse/silver.db/customers/ Create External Table -- External Table (you specify the ADLS path) CREATE TABLE bronze.raw_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/'; -- Or from DataFrame df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/') -- Register as external table spark.sql(''' CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bronze.raw_orders USING DELTA LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/delta/orders/' ''') -- DROP TABLE only removes catalog entry; files remain in ADLS DROP TABLE bronze.raw_orders; -- data still in ADLS! Critical difference: DROP TABLE on a Managed table permanently deletes your data files\n• Always use External Tables in production for data stored on ADLS — this protects your data from accidental deletion\n• Quick Reference — All 25 Topics # Topic Key Point 1 Spark Architecture Driver + Cluster Manager + Executors + DAG + Stages + Lazy Eval 2 CDC & CDF CDC=source change capture;\n• CDF=Delta row-level change feed (_change_type) 3 Query Optimization Broadcast, Z-ORDER, AQE, Photon, cache, filter early, partition 4 Parquet vs JSON Parquet=columnar+compressed+schema;\n• JSON=text+row+nested; use Parquet for analytics 5 Schedule Notebooks Databricks Jobs (Workflows) OR ADF Notebook Activity OR dbutils.notebook.run() Page 21 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide 6 SCD Types Type1=overwrite;\n• Type2=new row+expire;\n• Type3=add column;\n• Type2 most common 7 SCD SQL Code MERGE INTO for Type1; expire+insert pattern for Type2;\n• UPDATE prev column for Type3 8 PySpark Optimization Broadcast, filter early, cache, coalesce, repartition, avoid UDFs, AQE 9 Databricks Optimization OPTIMIZE+ZORDER, Liquid Cluster, Photon, Delta Cache, AQE, Materialized Views 10 ADLS Security Service Principal + Key Vault secrets + RBAC roles + ACLs + Private Endpoints 11 Load from External abfss:// direct read, Mount, Auto Loader, COPY INTO, External Location 12 Left Anti Join Returns LEFT rows with NO match in RIGHT; how='left_anti' 13 2nd Highest Salary dense_rank() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) WHERE rnk=2 14 Split Col3 into rows split(col, ',') + explode() in PySpark;\n• LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT()) in SQL 15 Notebook in Notebook dbutils.notebook.run() for isolated exec with params; %run to share scope 16 Validation Checks Schema, null, duplicate, type, range, referential integrity, count, business rules 17 Remove Nulls dropna(), fillna(), when().isNull(), forward fill with Window last() 18 Cluster Types All-Purpose(dev), Job(prod), Single Node, SQL Warehouse(BI), DLT(managed) 19 Store after Transform Delta tables in Bronze/Silver/Gold layers on ADLS partitioned by date 20 Load Data into ADB spark.read.jdbc(), spark.read.csv(), Auto Loader (cloudFiles), COPY INTO 21 ADLS Gen2 vs Blob Gen2=HNS+POSIX ACL+analytics optimized;","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Table Has 3 Columns But Client Sends Only 1","type":"concept","a":"When the source data has fewer columns than the target table, there are several approaches: Approach Description Insert with NULLs for Insert the available column; let missing columns default to NULL (if missing columns nullable) Provide DEFAULT values Define DEFAULT constraints on the missing columns in the table schema Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects mismatched schemas by default — schema Delta evolution must be enabled Transform before writing Add the missing columns with NULL or derived values in the DataFrame before writing Communicate with client Raise a data quality issue and request the complete dataset as per the agreed contract PySpark Example — Add Missing Columns from pyspark.sql.functions import lit # Client sent only 'emp_id'; table expects emp_id, emp_name, salary df = df.withColumn('emp_name', lit(None).cast('string')) \\ .withColumn('salary', lit(None).cast('integer'))","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark – Salary Classification","type":"concept","a":"Input Data 1, 'AAA', 500, 'HYD' 2, 'BBB', 100, 'CHI' 3, NULL, 1000, 'BANG' 4, 'DDD', NULL, 'KOL' 5, 'EEE', 2500, NULL PySpark Code from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, IntegerType, StringType spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SalaryClassification').getOrCreate() data = [ (1, 'AAA', 500, 'HYD'), (2, 'BBB', 100, 'CHI'), (3, None, 1000, 'BANG'), (4, 'DDD', None, 'KOL'), (5, 'EEE', 2500, None) ] schema = StructType([ StructField('emp_id', IntegerType(), True), StructField('emp_name', StringType(), True), StructField('salary', IntegerType(), True), StructField('location', StringType(), True) ]) df = spark.createDataFrame(data, schema) df = df.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary').isNull(), 'Unknown') .when(col('salary') < 500, 'Low') .when((col('salary') >= 1000) & Page 8 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A (col('salary') < 1500), 'Mid') .when(col('salary') >= 1500, 'High') .otherwise('No Grade') ) df.show() Expected Output emp_id emp_name salary location salary_grade 1 AAA 500 HYD No Grade (500 is not < 500 nor >= 1000) 2 BBB 100 CHI Low 3 NULL 1000 BANG Mid 4 DDD NULL KOL Unknown 5 EEE 2500 NULL High","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Why a Particular Job Takes Long – Causes & Solutions","type":"concept","a":"• Common Root Causes of Slow Jobs Issue Cause Impact Data Skew One partition has far more Most executors idle while one overloaded data than others executor runs for hours Shuffle Operations Wide transformations Network I/O and disk spill dominate job time (groupBy, join, distinct) cause expensive data shuffles Too Many Small Files Thousands of tiny Parquet Driver and executors spend excessive time on files instead of a few large file metadata lookups ones Insufficient Cluster Not enough cores/memory Tasks queue up;\n• GC pressure;\n• OOM errors Resources for the data volume Missing Partitioning Full table scan on every Reads entire dataset instead of pruning query irrelevant partitions Cartesian / Broadcast Large table joined without Generates massive intermediate datasets Join Issues broadcast hint or wrong join strategy UDF (Python) Usage Row-by-row Python UDFs Dramatically slower than native Spark SQL bypass Spark functions optimizations No Caching Recomputing the same Redundant reads and transformations repeated DataFrame multiple times for each action in a DAG Solutions & Optimizations Problem Solution Code / Config Data Skew Salting technique — df.withColumn('salt_key', (rand()*10).cast('int')) add random key prefix to distribute skewed key Data Skew Skew hint in Spark spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled','true') 3.x AQE Shuffle Enable Adaptive spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true') Query Execution (AQE) Shuffle Use broadcast join df.join(broadcast(small_df), 'id') for small tables Small Files Run OPTIMIZE on OPTIMIZE tablename ZORDER BY (col) Delta tables Page 8 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Small Files Coalesce or df.coalesce(10).write.format('delta').save(path) repartition before write Missing Partition Partition table on filter df.write.partitionBy('year','month').save(path) Pruning column Python UDFs Replace with Spark Use F.col(), F.when(), Pandas UDF with @pandas_udf SQL / Pandas UDF No Caching Cache reused df.cache() or DataFrames df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Cluster Sizing Right-size cluster or Set min/max workers; use photon runtime for SQL-heavy enable autoscaling jobs Diagnostic Tools in Databricks • Spark UI -> Stages tab: identify slow stages and skew via task duration distribution • Ganglia Metrics: CPU, memory, network I/O per executor • Event Timeline: spot executor idle time • EXPLAIN plan: check query plan for missing partition pruning or cartesian joins Page 9 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"Partitioning and Caching – Concepts & Advantages","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partitioning is the process of dividing a large dataset into smaller, manageable chunks (partitions) based on the values of one or more columns\n• This allows Spark to skip reading irrelevant partitions during queries — a technique called partition pruning\n• Two Types of Partitioning Type Description When to Use Storage Partitioning Data physically written into folder When tables are large and frequently (Write) hierarchy by column value (e.g., filtered by specific columns (date, region, /year=2024/month=01/) dept) Spark Partitioning (In- Number of in-memory partitions Control parallelism; repartition before memory) that Spark distributes across wide transformations or writes executors Storage Partitioning – Code # Write partitioned Delta table df.write \\ .format('delta') \\ .partitionBy('year', 'month') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/sales/') # Query benefits from partition pruning df_filtered = spark.read.format('delta').load('/mnt/delta/sales/') \\ .filter('year = 2024 AND month = 6') # Spark reads ONLY year=2024/month=6 folder -- skips all others Spark In-Memory Partitioning – Code # Repartition -- full shuffle, use for even distribution df = df.repartition(200, 'department_id') # Coalesce -- no shuffle, only reduces partitions (for writing fewer files) df = df.coalesce(10) # Check current number of partitions print(df.rdd.getNumPartitions()) Advantages of Partitioning Advantage Explanation Partition Pruning Spark skips irrelevant partitions entirely — massive read reduction Page 14 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Parallel Processing Each partition processed by a separate task — full cluster parallelism Faster Queries WHERE/filter on partition column can reduce scan from TBs to MBs Efficient Writes Large data written in parallel across many executors Better Maintenance VACUUM, OPTIMIZE, and MERGE can target specific partitions Caching Caching (persisting) stores a DataFrame or RDD in memory (and optionally disk) so that it does not need to be recomputed from scratch each time it is used in subsequent actions within the same Spark session\n• Cache vs Persist Method Storage Level Description df.cache() MEMORY_AND_DISK Stores in memory; spills to disk if (default) memory runs out df.persist() Configurable Allows specifying exact storage level StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY RAM only Fastest; data lost if evicted (recomputed) StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK RAM + Disk Balanced — no recomputation on eviction StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY Disk only Useful for DataFrames too large for RAM StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY_SER Serialized RAM Less memory; slight CPU overhead to deserialize Caching – Code Example from pyspark import StorageLevel # Cache a frequently reused DataFrame df_clean = df.filter(df.salary > 0).dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) df_clean.cache() # or df_clean.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) # Trigger caching (lazy evaluation -- first action materialises the cache) df_clean.count() # Reuse -- reads from cache, not original source dept_summary = df_clean.groupBy('department').agg(F.avg('salary')) top_earners = df_clean.filter(df_clean.salary > 100000) # Always unpersist when done to free memory df_clean.unpersist() Page 15 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 Advantages of Caching Advantage Explanation Eliminates Recomputation DataFrame computed once; reused many times without re-reading source Faster Iterative Algorithms ML training loops (10–100 iterations) benefit enormously from caching input data Reduces I/O Avoids repeated reads from slow storage (ADLS, S3) for intermediate results Speeds Up Interactive Notebook queries on the same DataFrame return results instantly after Analysis first cache Pipeline Optimization Cache after expensive joins/aggregations shared across multiple downstream steps Page 16 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is Compute in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Compute in Databricks refers to the cluster of virtual machines (VMs) used to run Spark jobs, notebooks, and SQL queries\n• It is the processing engine behind all workloads\n• Types of Compute in Databricks: • All-Purpose Clusters: Long-running, interactive clusters for notebooks and development\n• Can be shared among multiple users. • Job Clusters: Ephemeral clusters created for a specific job run and terminated automatically after completion — cost-efficient for production pipelines. • SQL Warehouses (formerly SQL Endpoints): Optimized for running SQL queries via Databricks SQL\n• Uses Photon engine for faster performance. • Instance Pools: Pre-warmed VMs to reduce cluster start time\n• Cluster Configuration Includes: • Driver node: Orchestrates the Spark job, maintains SparkContext. • Worker nodes: Execute the actual data processing tasks. • Autoscaling: Automatically adds/removes workers based on workload","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Cache and Persisting?","type":"concept","a":"Both cache() and persist() are used to store intermediate Spark DataFrames/RDDs in memory to avoid recomputation. cache(): • Shortcut for persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK). • Stores data in memory; if memory is insufficient, spills to disk. • Syntax: df.cache() persist(): • Provides fine-grained control over storage level. • Storage levels: MEMORY_ONLY, MEMORY_AND_DISK, DISK_ONLY, MEMORY_ONLY_SER, OFF_HEAP, etc. • Syntax: df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY) When to Use: • Use when a DataFrame is used multiple times in the same job — avoids re-reading from disk/source. • Always unpersist() after use to free memory: df.unpersist()","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Your Spark job is running slow. What will you check to identify the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• Step-by-step Diagnosis: • Check Spark UI: Review the Jobs, Stages, and Tasks tabs\n• Look for long-running stages, failed tasks, and skewed partitions. • Data Skew: Check if one partition has significantly more data than others (one task takes 10x longer)\n• Fix with salting or repartitioning. • Shuffles: Wide transformations like groupBy, join, and distinct trigger expensive shuffles\n• Check shuffle read/write sizes in Spark UI. • Number of Partitions: Too few = underutilized executors\n• Too many = overhead of task scheduling\n• Ideal: 2-4x the number of CPU cores. • Spill to Disk: Memory spill means executors don't have enough memory\n• Increase executor memory or reduce partition size. • Broadcast Joins: If joining a large table with a small table, ensure the small table is broadcast to avoid shuffle. • Data Format & Compression: Are you reading CSV instead of Parquet? CSV lacks predicate pushdown\n• Switch to Parquet/Delta. • Predicate Pushdown & Partition Pruning: Ensure filters are applied early and partition columns are used in WHERE clauses. • GC Overhead: High Garbage Collection time in executor logs indicates memory pressure. • Driver Bottleneck: If Driver is doing too much work (e.g., collect() on large data), it becomes a bottleneck","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Write PySpark code to get the above output using Window function?","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, max as spark_max from pyspark.sql.window import Window spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SalesMax').getOrCreate() # Sample Data data = [('cust1', '2023-12-01', 100), ('cust2', '2023-12-02', 150), ('cust1', '2023-12-03', 200), ('cust2', '2023-12-04', 250)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['customer_id', 'transaction_date', 'sales']) # Define Window partitioned by customer_id window_spec = Window.partitionBy('customer_id') # Apply max() over the window df_with_max = df.withColumn('sales_max', spark_max('sales').over(window_spec)) # Drop duplicates to get one row per customer result = df_with_max.select('customer_id', 'sales_max').distinct() result.show() # Alternative: Using groupBy (simpler if window not required) result_alt = df.groupBy('customer_id').agg(spark_max('sales').alias('sales_max')) result_alt.show()","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Date type using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_date, to_timestamp # Method 1: to_date() with format pattern df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_date(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd')) # Method 2: to_timestamp() if time component exists df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_timestamp(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss')) # Method 3: Using cast() df = df.withColumn('transaction_date', col('transaction_date').cast('date')) # Verify schema df.printSchema() df.show() Note: Always match the format string to the actual string format in your data\n• Mismatched format returns null values","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"customer_ids that are NOT in Sales table using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• This is a classic anti-join / left anti join scenario. from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('AntiJoin').getOrCreate() # Sample Data customers_data = [('cust1',), ('cust2',), ('cust3',), ('cust4',)] sales_data = [('cust1', '2023-12-01', 100), ('cust2', '2023-12-02', 150)] customers_df = spark.createDataFrame(customers_data, ['customer_id']) sales_df = spark.createDataFrame(sales_data, ['customer_id', 'date', 'sales']) # ---- Method 1: Left Anti Join (Most Efficient) ---- result = customers_df.join(sales_df, on='customer_id', how='left_anti') result.show() # Output: cust3, cust4 # ---- Method 2: Left Join + Filter Nulls ---- result2 = (customers_df .join(sales_df, on='customer_id', how='left') .filter(sales_df['customer_id'].isNull()) .select(customers_df['customer_id'])) result2.show() # ---- Method 3: Using SQL ---- customers_df.createOrReplaceTempView('customers') sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') result3 = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM sales) ''') result3.show() # ---- Method 4: subtract() on RDD level ---- result4 = customers_df.select('customer_id').subtract( sales_df.select('customer_id')) result4.show() Best Practice: Use Method 1 (left_anti join) — it is the most efficient and idiomatic PySpark approach\n• It avoids shuffling unnecessary data and clearly expresses the intent\n• End of Interview Q&A Document","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is shuffling. Why it is expensive?","type":"concept","a":"Shuffling & why expensive  Data movement between executors Expensive due to:  Network transfer  Disk I/O  Serializa(cid:415)on overhead","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Consider you are having 1TB and 10MB file. How will spark execute?","type":"concept","a":"1TB + 10MB file execu(cid:415)on  Spark splits 1TB into par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons  10MB file may cause small-file problem  Uneven task distribu(cid:415)on → performance issues","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Databricks Run(cid:415)me?","type":"concept","a":"Databricks Run(cid:415)me  Op(cid:415)mized Spark environment  Includes Spark + Delta Lake + ML libraries","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is UDF in databricks?","type":"concept","a":"UDF in Databricks  User Defined Func(cid:415)on for custom logic (cid:57523)(cid:57524)(cid:57525) slower than built-in Spark func(cid:415)ons","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"24. What is AQE?","type":"concept","a":"AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on)  Run(cid:415)me op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on in Spark  Improves joins, par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, skew handling","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Lazy Evalua(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Spark does not execute transforma(cid:415)ons immediately\n• Execu(cid:415)on starts only when an ac(cid:415)on (such as count() or show()) is called","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cache vs Persist","type":"concept","a":"• Cache df.cache() Persist df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Use persist() when you need a storage level other than the default\n• Spark Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques Interviewers o(cid:332)en ask you to explain at least 8–10 techniques\n• Cache df.cache() Persist df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK) Broadcast Join from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2),\"id\") Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(10) Coalesce df.coalesce(2) Filter Early df.filter(df.salary>50000) Select Required Columns df.select(\"id\",\"name\") Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Data df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"country\") Bucke(cid:415)ng df.write.bucketBy(10,\"id\") AQE spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.adap(cid:415)ve.enabled\",\"true\") Explain Plan df.explain(True) Join Strategies in Spark","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Hash Join","type":"concept","a":"Used when one table is small. large.join(broadcast(small),\"id\") No shuffle for the small table.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Sort Merge Join","type":"concept","a":"• Default join for large tables\n• Shuffle ↓ Sort ↓ Merge","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Shuffle Hash Join","type":"concept","a":" Shuffle both tables.  Build a hash table for the smaller par(cid:415)(cid:415)on.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Shuffle-and-Replicate Nested Loop Join","type":"concept","a":"Used for complex join condi(cid:415)ons when Spark cannot use a more efficient join strategy.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you handle data skew and optimize Spark transformations?","type":"concept","a":"• Common techniques: Identify skewed keys  Analyze the data distribution using groupBy() and count().  Review the Spark UI to identify tasks that take significantly longer than others\n• Use Broadcast Joins  If one table is small (lookup or dimension table), broadcast it to all executors to avoid expensive shuffles. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast result = largeDF.join( broadcast(smallDF), \"CustomerID\", \"inner\") Repartition Data  Redistribute data evenly before joins or aggregations. df = df.repartition(200, \"CustomerID\") Apply Salting  For heavily skewed keys, add a random salt to spread records across multiple partitions, then remove the salt after processing\n• Enable Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)  AQE automatically detects skewed partitions and optimizes shuffle execution at runtime\n• Filter Early  Remove unnecessary rows before joins to reduce the amount of data shuffled\n• Partition Data Properly  Store large datasets partitioned by frequently filtered columns, such as date or region, to minimize data scanning\n• Example answer: In one project, we noticed a join between transaction data and customer data was taking about 45 minutes\n• Spark UI showed that one partition contained most of the records because a few customer IDs had a very high number of transactions\n• We solved the issue by broadcasting the small customer table, repartitioning the transaction data, and enabling Adaptive Query Execution\n• The job execution time reduced to around 12 minutes, and cluster resource utilization became much more balanced","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Insert new version","type":"concept","a":"CurrentFlag='Y' StartDate=current_date() EndDate=NULL Columns typically used CustomerID Address StartDate EndDate CurrentFlag Pyspark Code: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, \"/target\") deltaTable.alias(\"t\").merge( sourceDF.alias(\"s\"), \"t.CustomerID = s.CustomerID AND t.CurrentFlag = 'Y'\" ).whenMatchedUpdate(condition=\"t.City <> s.City\", set={ \"CurrentFlag\": \"'N'\", \"EndDate\": \"current_date()\" } ).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ \"CustomerID\": \"s.CustomerID\", \"Name\": \"s.Name\", \"City\": \"s.City\", \"StartDate\": \"current_date()\", \"EndDate\": \"NULL\", \"CurrentFlag\": \"'Y'\" }).execute()","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"What are the most common performance bottlenecks you’ve faced in Spark and how","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"did you fix them?","type":"concept","a":"• Mention multiple points\n• Small Files Solution coalesce() repartition() Data Skew Solution Broadcast joins AQE Salting Shuffle Reduce unnecessary joins Filter early Partition correctly Expensive UDFs Use built-in Spark functions instead Repeated Computation Use cache() persist() Managerial Round","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you communicate technical issues to non-technical managers?","type":"concept","a":"• Instead of saying \"Spark executor memory exceeded\" Say The data processing job ran out of resources due to a significant increase in data volume\n• We increased processing capacity and optimized the job to ensure future runs complete within the expected time","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you mentor junior team members or help upskill the team?","type":"concept","a":"Mention  Code reviews  Pair programming  Documentation  SQL optimization sessions  Spark best practices  Knowledge-sharing meetings","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"How do you resolve the data skew issue in Databricks / Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Data skew occurs when certain partition keys have far more data than others, causing some tasks to take much longer. • Salting: Add a random prefix (0-N) to the skewed key; join on the salted key, then aggregate results back. • Broadcast Join: If one side is small (<= 10 MB or tuned threshold), use broadcast to avoid shuffle altogether. • AQE (Adaptive Query Execution): Enable spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true — Spark automatically coalesces skewed partitions at runtime. • Skew Join Optimization (AQE): spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled=true splits skewed partitions into smaller ones. • Repartition: Use repartition(n, col) to manually redistribute data more evenly. • Increase spark.sql.shuffle.partitions from default 200 to a higher value for large datasets\n• DevOps Pipeline – Code Promotion & Debugging","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the cluster configura(cid:415)on you have done in your recent project","type":"concept","a":"• Cluster Configura(cid:415)on (Databricks) You can say:  We used Databricks Job Clusters in produc(cid:415)on  Cluster type: autoscaling enabled  Run(cid:415)me: DBR (Databricks Run(cid:415)me latest stable)  Mode: Standard cluster Example answer: “We used job clusters with autoscaling enabled between 2–8 workers depending on workload\n• We selected standard run(cid:415)me with op(cid:415)mized Spark configura(cid:415)ons for performance.”","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Check stage execu(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Are you familiar with cluster configura(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Typical configura(cid:415)on includes: Cluster Mode  Single Node  Standard  High Concurrency (legacy;\n• Unity Catalog environments now commonly use Standard or Serverless depending on the workload) Worker Type Example: Standard_D8s_v5 Driver Type Can be same or larger than workers\n• Worker Count Min = 2 Max = 8 Autoscaling enabled\n• Run(cid:415)me Version Example: 15.4 LTS Photon Enabled for SQL-heavy workloads\n• Auto Termina(cid:415)on 30 Minutes Unity Catalog Access Mode  Single User  Shared Interview Answer \"When configuring clusters, I select the run(cid:415)me version, VM size, worker count or autoscaling, access mode, auto termina(cid:415)on, Photon, and instance availability\n• In produc(cid:415)on we typically use autoscaling Job Clusters with LTS run(cid:415)mes.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"You have a single-node cluster with 16 cores. What issues might occur?","type":"concept","a":"• A single-node cluster has one machine ac(cid:415)ng as both driver and worker\n• Possible issues: No Distributed Compu(cid:415)ng Spark cannot distribute work across mul(cid:415)ple machines\n• Driver Bo(cid:425)leneck Driver performs both coordina(cid:415)on and execu(cid:415)on\n• Memory Pressure Large datasets may exceed the memory available on the single node\n• CPU Satura(cid:415)on All tasks compete for the same 16 cores\n• Long Execu(cid:415)on Time Large joins and aggrega(cid:415)ons take longer because no addi(cid:415)onal workers are available\n• Fault Tolerance If the single node fails, the en(cid:415)re workload fails\n• Suitable For  Development  Tes(cid:415)ng  Small datasets  Learning Not recommended for produc(cid:415)on ETL workloads","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"For Dev and Test workspaces, should we create one metastore or different metastores?","type":"concept","a":"• Typical recommenda(cid:415)on: One metastore per region, shared across mul(cid:415)ple workspaces (Dev, Test, Prod), while keeping environments isolated using catalogs, schemas, and permissions\n• Example: Metastore ↓ Dev Workspace ↓ dev_catalog ------------------- Test Workspace ↓ test_catalog ------------------- Prod Workspace ↓ prod_catalog Different metastores are generally used when there are strong isola(cid:415)on requirements, such as:  Different cloud regions  Different legal en(cid:415)(cid:415)es or business units  Strict compliance or regulatory boundaries  Separate organiza(cid:415)ons \"In most enterprise implementa(cid:415)ons, we use a single metastore per region and a(cid:425)ach mul(cid:415)ple workspaces to it\n• Environment isola(cid:415)on is achieved using separate catalogs, schemas, and access controls\n• Separate metastores are typically reserved for cases where stronger isola(cid:415)on is required, such as different regions or compliance boundaries.\" Quick Revision (Most Important Points) Ques(cid:415)on Key Answer Compute Types All-Purpose, Job, SQL Warehouse, Serverless, Model Serving Job = produc(cid:415)on & auto-terminate;\n• All-Purpose = interac(cid:415)ve Job vs All-Purpose development Databricks Run(cid:415)me Op(cid:415)mized Spark + Delta Lake + Photon + language run(cid:415)mes Cluster Config Run(cid:415)me, VM size, autoscaling, access mode, Photon, auto-termina(cid:415)on No distribu(cid:415)on, driver bo(cid:425)leneck, limited scalability, memory/CPU Single Node Issues constraints Data Lake vs Delta Delta adds ACID, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, transac(cid:415)on log Deleted Managed Cannot recover without backups or source data once files/logs are Table permanently removed Managed vs External Managed = Databricks-managed storage;\n• External = customer-managed Volume cloud storage Unity Catalog Metastore → Catalog → Schema → Tables/Views/Volumes Usually one metastore per region with separate catalogs and Dev/Test Metastore permissions for each environment","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Read a text file with custom delimiter","type":"concept","a":"Input file id~!name~!=age 1~!Viswa~!=25 Desired Output id name age 1 Viswa 25 Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import split df = spark.read.text(\"input.txt\") result = df.select( split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(0).alias(\"id\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(1).alias(\"name\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(2).alias(\"age\") ) result.show() If the file has a header row that should be skipped: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import monotonically_increasing_id df = spark.read.text(\"input.txt\") data = ( df.withColumn(\"rn\", monotonically_increasing_id()) .filter(\"rn > 0\") ) result = data.select( split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(0).alias(\"id\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(1).alias(\"name\"), split(\"value\", \"~!=\").getItem(2).alias(\"age\") ) result.show()","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Q3. What is DAG?","type":"concept","a":"• DAG stands for Directed Acyclic Graph\n• In Apache Spark, a DAG represents the logical execution plan — a sequence of stages and tasks derived from transformations on RDDs or DataFrames\n• Three Key Properties • Directed — data flows in one direction (parent to child node) • Acyclic — no circular dependencies; no node can be its own ancestor • Graph — nodes represent RDDs/DataFrames; edges represent transformations How Spark Uses DAG • An action (collect, count, write) triggers job submission to the DAG Scheduler • DAG Scheduler divides the DAG into Stages based on shuffle boundaries • Each Stage splits into Tasks — one task per partition • Task Scheduler assigns tasks to available executors Lazy Evaluation: Transformations are NOT executed immediately\n• They build the DAG plan\n• Execution only happens when an action is triggered, allowing Spark to optimize the full plan first\n• Interview Tip: Mention the DAG can be viewed in Spark UI under the SQL/Jobs tab — useful for debugging performance and identifying shuffle stages","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow vs Wide Transformations","type":"concept","a":"Aspect Narrow Transformation Wide Transformation Shuffle No shuffle Shuffle occurs Performance Fast Slower (network I/O) Stage Boundary Same stage New stage is created Examples map, filter, select, union, flatMap groupBy, join, distinct, repartition, orderBy Fault Tolerance Re-compute from parent partition Must re-shuffle on node failure Best Practices • Use filter() early to reduce data before any shuffle operation • Use reduceByKey() instead of groupByKey() — performs partial aggregation locally before shuffle • Cache DataFrames before wide transformations if the result is reused • Broadcast small tables in joins to avoid shuffle entirely Page 2 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Additional Techniques","type":"concept","a":"• Data Skipping — Delta auto-collects min/max stats for first 32 columns • Caching — df.cache() for DataFrames reused multiple times in the same job • Broadcast Joins — broadcast hint for small dimension tables • AQE — spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true for runtime optimizations","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Skew — What it is and How to Handle It","type":"concept","a":"• Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across partitions\n• Some tasks (stragglers) take much longer and the entire job waits for them\n• Example: Joining on customer_id where 1 customer has 80% of all records\n• One executor processes millions of rows while others sit idle\n• Detect Skew • Spark UI: Stage details, Task duration histogram — few tasks taking 10x longer df.groupBy('key').count().orderBy(desc('count')).show() Solution 1: Salting from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, col, lit, floor, rand, explode, array salted = skewed_df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('key'), lit('_'), (floor(rand()*10)).cast('string'))) other_exp = other_df.withColumn('salt', explode(array([lit(i) for i in range(10)]))) other_exp = other_exp.withColumn('salted_key', Page 4 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide concat(col('key'), lit('_'), col('salt').cast('string'))) Solution 2: AQE — Recommended for Spark 3+ spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') AQE detects skewed partitions at runtime and automatically splits them\n• Available in Spark 3.0+\n• Easiest modern solution","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Joins — Inner, Left, Right","type":"concept","a":"Table A — Employees EmpID Name 1 Alice 2 Bob 3 Carol 4 Dave Table B — Departments EmpID Dept 1 Engineering 2 Marketing 5 Finance Inner Join — Only Matching Rows SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a INNER JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing Left Join — All from A, Matching from B (NULLs for unmatched) SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a LEFT JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing 3 Carol NULL 4 Dave NULL Right Join — All from B, Matching from A (NULLs for unmatched) SELECT a.EmpID, a.Name, b.Dept FROM A a RIGHT JOIN B b ON a.EmpID = b.EmpID EmpID Name Dept 1 Alice Engineering 2 Bob Marketing 5 NULL Finance Page 7 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide PySpark: df_a.join(df_b, 'EmpID', 'inner') | 'left' | 'right' | 'outer' | 'left_semi' | 'left_anti' Quick Reference Summary Topic Key Takeaway DAG Logical plan built lazily; executed on action trigger Narrow Transform No shuffle — same stage (map, filter, select) Wide Transform Shuffle — new stage (groupBy, join, distinct) Broadcast Join Replicate small table to all executors; eliminates shuffle OPTIMIZE Compact small Delta files for faster scans Z-ORDER Cluster data by column for file skipping on filters Data Skew Uneven partitions — fix with AQE (Spark 3+) or salting Delta Lake Data lake + ACID + Time Travel + MERGE on Parquet Auto Loader cloudFiles format — incremental ingestion with checkpointing dense_rank() Best for Top-N salary — handles ties without gaps Left Join All from left + NULL for unmatched right rows Right Join All from right + NULL for unmatched left rows Page 8 of 8","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on vs Wide Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on  No data shuffle.  Child par(cid:415)(cid:415)on depends on one parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  map()  filter()  union()  flatMap() Very fast","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is MongoDB?","type":"concept","a":"• NoSQL document database\n• Stores data as JSON-like BSON documents\n• Example: { \"empId\":101, \"name\":\"John\", \"skills\":[\"Python\",\"Spark\"] } Round 2:","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Spark job gives an Out Of Memory (OOM) error, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would check the Spark UI to iden(cid:415)fy which stage failed\n• Then I would:  Increase executor memory.  Increase the number of executors.  Reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)on size using repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on().  Avoid collect() on large datasets.  Use broadcast joins for small lookup tables.  Cache only when necessary.  Filter data before joins.  Inves(cid:415)gate data skew if one par(cid:415)(cid:415)on is much larger than the others.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Join a Huge Table with a Small Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Use a Broadcast Join\n• The small table is replicated to every executor so the join happens locally on each partition of the large table — no shuffle. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Explicit broadcast hint result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), on='customer_id', how='inner') # Configure auto-broadcast threshold (default 10 MB) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', '100mb') Page 2 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Aspect Broadcast Join Sort-Merge Join Shuffle None — no data movement Both tables shuffled Use when One table < ~200 MB Both tables are large Memory impact Small table on every executor Standard memory usage Performance Very fast Slower but scalable to any size Never broadcast a large table — it causes Out of Memory (OOM) on executors\n• Disable auto- broadcast: spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', '-1')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Join Two Large Tables in Spark?","type":"concept","a":"• Spark uses Sort-Merge Join (SMJ) by default for large-large joins\n• Additional strategies: Option 1 — Sort-Merge Join (default, Spark 2.3+) • Both tables are shuffled by the join key, sorted, then merged • Spark automatically picks SMJ when both tables exceed the broadcast threshold # SMJ happens automatically; or hint explicitly: result = df_orders.hint('merge').join(df_customers.hint('merge'), on='customer_id') Option 2 — Pre-bucket Both Tables (eliminates shuffle permanently) df_orders.write.bucketBy(64,'customer_id').sortBy('customer_id').saveAsTable('orders_bkt') df_customers.write.bucketBy(64,'customer_id').sortBy('customer_id').saveAsTable('cust_bkt') # All subsequent joins skip shuffle entirely result = spark.table('orders_bkt').join(spark.table('cust_bkt'), 'customer_id') Option 3 — Enable AQE (Adaptive Query Execution) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled', 'true') AQE dynamically adjusts join strategy at runtime\n• If one side turns out smaller than expected, it can switch from SMJ to Broadcast Join automatically","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Broadcast Join?","type":"concept","a":"• A Broadcast Join (map-side join) sends a complete copy of the smaller table to every executor node\n• The large table is never moved — each executor joins its local partition against the in-memory copy of the small table\n• No shuffle at all. • Eliminates shuffle — the most expensive Spark operation (network I/O, disk spill) • Best for: small lookup/dimension tables (under 10 MB default, up to ~200 MB practical) • Ideal pattern: large fact table JOIN broadcast(small dimension table) result = fact_orders.join(broadcast(dim_product), 'product_id') Section 3 | Delta Lake Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Cluster Configuration Would You Use for TB-Level Data?","type":"concept","a":"Aspect Recommendation Notes Cluster type Job Cluster (auto-terminate) Never use All-Purpose in production Driver size 16 cores, 64 GB RAM Larger driver for collect/broadcast ops Page 12 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Worker size 16 cores, 64 GB RAM per node 8-16 workers for 1 TB workloads Auto-scaling Min 4, Max 16 workers Scales with data volume dynamically Runtime Databricks Runtime + Photon Photon gives 3-10x speedup on SQL ops Spot workers Workers on Spot, Driver on On- Up to 70% cost savings Demand Delta Cache Enabled (SSD caching) Critical for repeated Delta table reads # Example Databricks cluster config { 'autoscale': { 'min_workers': 4, 'max_workers': 16 }, 'node_type_id': 'Standard_DS5_v2', # 16 cores, 56 GB RAM 'driver_node_type_id': 'Standard_DS5_v2', 'spark_conf': { 'spark.sql.adaptive.enabled': 'true', 'spark.sql.shuffle.partitions': '400' }, 'runtime_engine': 'PHOTON' }","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Executors Would You Use for Processing 1 TB of Data?","type":"concept","a":"• Default partition size: 128 MB (spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes) • 1 TB / 128 MB = ~8192 partitions (maximum concurrent tasks) • Practical executor config: 4-8 cores per executor, 16-32 GB RAM each • With 64 executors x 8 cores = 512 cores total — can run 512 tasks in parallel • Set shuffle partitions to 200-400: spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '400') Page 13 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Config Recommended Value Reasoning Executors 32-64 Balances parallelism and coordination overhead Cores per executor 4-8 (5 is sweet spot) Above 5 increases GC pressure RAM per executor 16-32 GB Covers 128-256 MB partitions with 4x overhead Total cores 128-512 Sufficient parallelism for 1 TB Shuffle partitions 200-400 Avoid 200 (too few) or 8192 (too many small tasks) Rule of thumb: 5 cores and 16 GB RAM per executor is a well-balanced starting point. Too many small executors = high task overhead. Too few large executors = GC pressure. Use auto-scaling with min/max bounds for production. Section 10 | Behavioral / Closing","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What skills or experiences have made previous hires most successful in this role?","type":"concept","a":"• Never say 'I have no questions' — it signals disinterest\n• Avoid asking about salary, leave policy, or working hours in a technical round\n• Save those for the HR discussion\n• Quick Reference — All 30 Questions at a Glance Page 14 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) # Question (Short) Key Answer / Keyword 1 Spark Architecture Driver + Cluster Manager + Executors + Stages + DAG + Lazy Eval 2 Notebooks per year 10-40 typical; organized by Bronze/Silver/Gold layers 3 Daily data volume Tailor to project;\n• 500 GB-2 TB is typical mid-scale 4 File types CSV, JSON, Parquet, Delta, Avro, ORC, XML 5 Huge + small table join Broadcast Join — no shuffle, small table to all executors 6 Two large tables join Sort-Merge Join (default) OR pre-bucket both tables 7 Broadcast Join Copy small table to every executor; eliminates shuffle entirely 8 Z-ORDER for transactions transaction_date + customer_id (high cardinality, frequent filters) 9 Default Z-ORDER column None — must be explicit;\n• Delta auto-stats on first 32 cols 10 Z-Order indexes Z-curve clustering; co-locates related rows; enables file skipping 11 CSV to Parquet Columnar, compressed, schema-aware, predicate pushdown, 5-20x faster 12 JSON to structured table from_json() + schema + explode() for nested/array fields 13 Remove duplicates dropDuplicates() / distinct() / Window row_number() / MERGE 14 Duplicate handling methods 7 methods: dropDuplicates, distinct, window rank, MERGE, GROUP BY\n• 15 Gender count per city GROUP BY city + COUNT(CASE WHEN gender=X) or PIVOT or PySpark agg 16 Liquid Clustering Replaces partition+ZORDER; dynamic, incremental, change columns anytime 17 View types Temp, Global Temp, Standard (metastore), Materialized (pre- computed) 18 Materialized Views Pre-computed Delta table; auto-refreshed; fast for heavy aggregations 19 Thousands of CSV files Auto Loader (cloudFiles) + checkpoint — incremental, scalable 20 Most efficient file loading Auto Loader for incremental;\n• COPY INTO for SQL batch; spark.read for one-time 21 Auto Loader incremental Directory listing OR file notifications (Event Grid/SNS) + checkpoint 22 Schedule Auto Loader Databricks Jobs, DLT pipelines, ADF triggers, Databricks Workflows 23 Auto Loader triggers availableNow, processingTime, continuous, once (deprecated) 24 Auto Loader file detection Directory scan vs checkpoint OR cloud file notification queue 25 Corrupted CSV rows PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + quarantine bad rows to Delta table 26 Bronze layer from CSV files Auto Loader + _ingestion_time + _source_file + mergeSchema=true 27 TB-level cluster config 16+ workers, Photon, auto-scale, Spot workers, AQE enabled Page 15 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) 28 Cluster types in Databricks All-Purpose, Job, High Concurrency, Single Node, SQL Warehouse, DLT 29 Executors for 1 TB 32-64 executors, 4-8 cores each, 16-32 GB RAM, shuffle.partitions=400 30 Questions for interviewer Ask about architecture, challenges, CI/CD, streaming, first 90 days Page 16 of 16","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Create a Spark DataFrame from the given data.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Display the final DataFrame.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Convert the nested structure into a Spark StructType (if needed).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produce a final DataFrame with the following schema:","type":"concept","a":"product | price | quan(cid:415)ty","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"How will you join two large DataFrames effec(cid:415)vely in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• \"When both DataFrames are large, I avoid broadcast joins because broadcas(cid:415)ng large datasets can cause memory issues\n• Instead, I:  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on both DataFrames on the join key.  Filter unnecessary records before joining.  Select only required columns.  Enable Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE).  Ensure both DataFrames have a balanced par(cid:415)(cid:415)on distribu(cid:415)on to minimize shuffle.\" Example df1 = df1.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(\"user_id\") df2 = df2.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(\"user_id\") result = df1.join(df2, \"user_id\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark coding: Large table is skewed, join with a small table.","type":"concept","a":"• Interviewer Large table is skewed\n• Small table is normal\n• Join Key: user_id \"Since the large DataFrame is skewed and the second DataFrame is small, I would use a Broadcast Join\n• Broadcas(cid:415)ng the small table eliminates shuffle for that table and significantly improves performance\n• If skew is severe, I may also use key sal(cid:415)ng.\" Broadcast Join from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"user_id\", \"le(cid:332)\" ) If skew is extremely high (Sal(cid:415)ng) from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import floor, rand large_df = large_df.withColumn( \"salt\", floor(rand()*5) ) small_df = small_df.crossJoin( spark.range(5).toDF(\"salt\") ) result = large_df.join( small_df, [\"user_id\",\"salt\"] )","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Read CSV → Filter inac(cid:415)ve → Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on by Region → Write Delta to S3","type":"concept","a":"PySpark Code from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on( \"header\", True ).csv(\"s3://input/customer.csv\") inac(cid:415)ve = df.filter( col(\"status\") == \"inac(cid:415)ve\" ) inac(cid:415)ve.write \\ .format(\"delta\") \\ .par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"region\") \\ .mode(\"overwrite\") \\ .save(\"s3://output/customer_delta/\")","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you install a third-party JAR in Python?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the JAR is required for Spark, I a(cid:425)ach it to the Databricks cluster:  Go to the cluster configura(cid:415)on.  Select Libraries.  Install the JAR from Workspace, DBFS, Maven, or cloud storage.  Restart the cluster if required\n• If I need a Python package instead of a JAR, I use %pip install in Databricks notebooks or install it as a cluster library.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you explain technical strategies to non-technical stakeholders?","type":"concept","a":"• \"When speaking with non-technical stakeholders, I avoid technical jargon and focus on business outcomes\n• For example, instead of saying, 'We op(cid:415)mized Spark jobs using broadcast joins and par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning,' I would say: 'We improved the data processing process so reports are generated faster and more reliably, reducing wai(cid:415)ng (cid:415)me for business users.' I also use simple diagrams, real-world analogies, and dashboards to explain the impact of the solu(cid:415)on\n• My goal is to help stakeholders understand how the changes improve performance, reduce costs, or support be(cid:425)er decision-making without requiring technical knowledge.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark code (min salary by dept, cast emp id, null salary check)","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col, min df_clean = df \\ .withColumn(\"emp_id\", col(\"emp_id\").cast(\"int\")) \\ .filter(col(\"salary\").isNotNull()) df_result = df_clean.groupBy(\"dept_id\") \\ .agg(min(\"salary\").alias(\"min_salary\")) df_result.show()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If column name changed in withColumn, do we rerun both cells?","type":"concept","a":" Yes, you must rerun dependent cells  Because Spark is lazy evalua(cid:415)on, but notebook cells depend on execu(cid:415)on order  So if upstream transforma(cid:415)on changes column name, downstream cells must be re- executed","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Spark architecture + stages + cluster used","type":"concept","a":"Architecture:  Driver  Executors  Cluster Manager Stages:  Spark divides job into stages based on shuffle boundaries  Each stage has mul(cid:415)ple tasks  Example: groupBy or join causes shuffle → new stage Cluster used:  Typically Databricks job cluster / all-purpose cluster  Auto-scaling enabled in produc(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Upskilling & cer(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":" Learning PySpark, Azure Data Engineering  Microso(cid:332) cer(cid:415)fica(cid:415)ons (DP-203)  Prac(cid:415)cing Databricks, Delta Lake  Hands-on projects","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Check Spark UI for stages and tasks.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Check shuffle size.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Cache reused DataFrames.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Use AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Wide vs. Narrow Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Narrow Wide No shuffle Shuffle required Faster Slower One parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Mul(cid:415)ple parent par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Examples: Narrow df.filter() df.map() Wide df.groupBy() df.join() df.dis(cid:415)nct()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Coalesce vs. Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Coalesce Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on Reduce par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons No full shuffle (usually) Full shuffle Faster for reducing More expensive","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"You have 5 million records. How will you op(cid:415)mize?","type":"concept","a":" Read only required columns.  Filter early.  Use Parquet or Delta.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on appropriately.  Broadcast small lookup tables.  Cache reused DataFrames.  Avoid unnecessary shuffles.  Enable AQE.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"How do you see PySpark op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":" Spark UI  Physical execu(cid:415)on plan: df.explain(True) Check for:  Broadcast joins  Shuffle opera(cid:415)ons  Predicate pushdown  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on pruning","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Improvement using GenAI in your project","type":"concept","a":"• Example answer: \"We used GenAI tools such as ChatGPT and GitHub Copilot to improve developer produc(cid:415)vity\n• They helped generate PySpark transforma(cid:415)ons, SQL queries, unit tests, documenta(cid:415)on, and code explana(cid:415)ons\n• This reduced development (cid:415)me, improved consistency, and accelerated troubleshoo(cid:415)ng\n• We always reviewed and validated AI-generated code before using it.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Transforma(cid:415)on and Ac(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Transforma(cid:415)on Ac(cid:415)on Creates a new DataFrame Triggers execu(cid:415)on Lazy Eager Builds the DAG Executes the DAG Examples: Transforma(cid:415)ons filter() select() join() groupBy() withColumn() Ac(cid:415)ons show() count() collect() write() first()","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"A Spark job processing 3 joined tables increased from 1 million to 50 million records. The","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"job now takes much longer. How would you debug it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would first analyze the Spark UI to iden(cid:415)fy the bo(cid:425)leneck, then check for data skew, shuffle size, join strategy, par(cid:415)(cid:415)on count, execu(cid:415)on plan, and executor resource usage\n• Based on the findings, I would op(cid:415)mize using broadcast joins, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, filtering before joins, selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, caching reusable DataFrames, or handling skew.\" Step-by-step approach Step 1: Check the Spark UI Look at:","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":" Which stage is taking the most (cid:415)me?","type":"concept","a":"•  Number of tasks  Shuffle Read/Write size  Executor CPU u(cid:415)liza(cid:415)on  Spill to disk  Skewed tasks (one task taking much longer than others) Step 2: Check Data Skew One of the join keys may have become highly skewed\n• Example: Customer_ID 1 -> 20 million records 2 -> 10 records 3 -> 5 records One executor processes huge data while others stay idle\n• Check: df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count().orderBy(\"count\", ascending=False).show() Possible solu(cid:415)ons:  Sal(cid:415)ng  Broadcast join (if one table is small)  AQE (Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on) Step 3: Check Join Strategy Run df.explain(True) Look for:  BroadcastHashJoin  SortMergeJoin  ShuffleHashJoin If Spark is doing SortMergeJoin because the table became larger, shuffle cost increases\n• If one table is small: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\") Step 4: Check Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Count Too few par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:  Executors become idle\n• Too many par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons:  Scheduling overhead increases\n• Check: df.rdd.getNumPar(cid:415)(cid:415)ons() Adjust if necessary: df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(400) or spark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.shuffle.par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons\", 400) (Default is o(cid:332)en 200.) Step 5: Check Shuffle Size Large shuffle is a common bo(cid:425)leneck\n• Spark UI → Shuffle Read/Write If shuffle is very high:  Filter data before join  Select only required columns  Broadcast small table Step 6: Filter Early Bad: Join ↓ Filter Be(cid:425)er: Filter ↓ Join This reduces data movement\n• Step 7: Remove Unnecessary Columns Instead of select(\"*\") Use select(\"id\", \"name\", \"amount\") Less data is shuffled\n• Step 8: Check Caching If the same DataFrame is reused mul(cid:415)ple (cid:415)mes: df.cache() Otherwise Spark recomputes it\n• Step 9: Check Executor Configura(cid:415)on Review:  Executor Memory  Executor Cores  Driver Memory Insufficient memory can lead to disk spilling\n• Step 10: Compare Execu(cid:415)on Plans df.explain(True) Look for:  Extra exchanges  Mul(cid:415)ple shuffles  Cartesian joins  Broadcast not happening","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Coalesce and Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Coalesce Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on Reduces par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Increases or decreases par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Avoids full shuffle (mostly) Always performs a full shuffle Faster Slower Used a(cid:332)er filtering Used before joins or for balancing data Cannot efficiently increase par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Can increase or decrease par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons Example: df.coalesce(5) df.repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on(20)","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"May create skewed par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Some executors process much more data.","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques have you used in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"\"The op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques I have used include:  Broadcast joins for small lookup tables.  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning to balance data distribu(cid:415)on.  Caching frequently used DataFrames.  Filtering data early to reduce processing.  Using column pruning instead of SELECT *.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning large datasets.  Enabling Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE).  Op(cid:415)mizing Delta tables using OPTIMIZE and ZORDER.  Avoiding unnecessary shuffle opera(cid:415)ons.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How will you join a small table with a large dataset?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If one table is significantly smaller than the other, I use a Broadcast Join\n• Spark sends the small table to every executor, allowing the large table to be processed locally without a shuffle\n• This improves performance by reducing network overhead.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Write PySpark code to create a new column as the sum of two columns.","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col df = df.withColumn( \"Total\", col(\"Salary\") + col(\"Bonus\") ) df.show()","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in PySpark.","type":"concept","a":"Men(cid:415)on:  Broadcast Join  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Cache/Persist  Filter Early  Column Pruning  AQE  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Delta Op(cid:415)mize  ZORDER  Avoid Shuffle","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is Volume?","type":"concept","a":"• Volume refers to the amount/size of data being stored or processed\n• It is one of the 3 Vs of Big Data (Volume, Velocity, Variety). • Small scale: GBs - can fit on a single machine • Medium scale: TBs - requires distributed systems • Large scale: PBs/EBs - requires cloud-native distributed architectures (e.g., HDFS, S3, ADLS) In Databricks/Spark, volume impacts partitioning strategy, cluster sizing, and shuffle operations","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What to do when the Dataset is Slow?","type":"concept","a":"Diagnosis & Optimization Strategies: • Check Spark UI: Identify slow stages, skewed partitions, spill to disk • Partitioning: Repartition/coalesce to right number of partitions • Caching: cache() or persist() frequently accessed DataFrames • Broadcast Join: Use for small tables (< 10MB by default) • Predicate Pushdown: Filter early to reduce data scanned • File format: Use Parquet/Delta instead of CSV/JSON • Z-ordering: Co-locate related data for faster reads • AQE: Enable Adaptive Query Execution • Columnar pruning: Select only needed columns • Data skew: Apply salting or AQE skew hints • Cluster sizing: Increase worker nodes or use larger instance type SECTION 2: Data Modeling","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"What is Partitioning?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning divides data into smaller, manageable chunks to improve query performance and parallelism\n• Types: • Hive-style Partitioning: Folders on disk (e.g., /data/year=2024/month=01/) • Spark RDD/DataFrame Partitioning: In-memory distribution across executors • Hash Partitioning: Distribute by hash of a column • Range Partitioning: Distribute by value ranges Best Practices: • Choose low-cardinality columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning (too many small files) • Use OPTIMIZE to compact small files in Delta # Write partitioned Delta table df.write.format('delta') \\ .partitionBy('year', 'month') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .save('/delta/sales') # Repartition in Spark df = df.repartition(200, col('customer_id')) df = df.coalesce(10) # Reduce partitions without full shuffle SECTION 3: Azure Data Factory (ADF)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Single Node Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Driver only, no workers — for small data / development • No distributed processing Cluster Modes: • Standard Mode: Full Spark — Python, Scala, R, SQL • Enhanced Autoscaling: Dynamic worker count based on workload • Photon Engine: Vectorized query engine for faster SQL SECTION 4: CI/CD, Delta Lake & Architecture","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Lazy Evaluation in Spark","type":"concept","a":"• Lazy Evaluation means Spark does NOT execute transformations immediately\n• It builds a logical plan (DAG) and only executes when an action is called\n• Transformations (Lazy — build the plan): • filter(), select(), join(), groupBy(), withColumn(), map() Actions (Eager — trigger execution): • count(), collect(), show(), write(), save(), take() # None of these execute yet: df = spark.read.csv('/data/sales.csv', header=True) df = df.filter(col('amount') > 1000) # Lazy df = df.groupBy('region').sum('amount') # Lazy # Execution happens HERE: df.show() # Action — triggers the full plan Benefits: • Query optimization via Catalyst Optimizer before execution • Pipelining of transformations to avoid redundant passes • Spark can reorder/skip operations for efficiency","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Catalyst Optimizer in Spark","type":"concept","a":"• Catalyst Optimizer is Spark's built-in query optimizer that automatically optimizes SQL/DataFrame operations using a tree-based rule system\n• Optimization Phases: • 1\n• Analysis: Resolves column references, validates schema • 2\n• Logical Optimization: Applies rule-based optimizations: ◦ Predicate Pushdown: Push filters as close to source as possible ◦ Constant Folding: Pre-compute constant expressions ◦ Column Pruning: Drop unused columns early • 3\n• Physical Planning: Converts logical plan to physical plan, chooses join strategy • 4\n• Code Generation: Generates JVM bytecode (Whole-Stage CodeGen) # Catalyst auto-applies pushdown — you benefit automatically # To inspect the plan: df.explain(True) # Shows analyzed, optimized, physical plans # Extended explain df.explain(mode='formatted')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark — Process 100M Row CSV","type":"concept","a":"• from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, sum as spark_sum from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType # Initialize Spark with optimized config for 100M rows spark = SparkSession.builder \\ .appName('LargeCSVProcessor') \\ .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') \\ .config('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '400') \\ .config('spark.executor.memory', '8g') \\ .getOrCreate() # Define schema explicitly (faster than inferSchema for large files) schema = StructType([ StructField('transaction_id', StringType(), True), StructField('customer_id', StringType(), True), StructField('transaction_date', StringType(), True), StructField('amount', DoubleType(), True), StructField('category', StringType(), True), ]) # Step 1: Read large CSV df = spark.read \\ .option('header', 'true') \\ .option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED') \\ .schema(schema) \\ .csv('/data/large_transactions.csv') print(f'Total rows: {df.count()}') # Step 2: Filter out rows with missing values df_clean = df.dropna(subset=['transaction_id', 'customer_id', 'transaction_date', 'amount']) df_clean = df_clean.filter( col('transaction_id').isNotNull() & col('customer_id').isNotNull() & col('amount').isNotNull() ) print(f'Rows after null filter: {df_clean.count()}') # Step 3: Convert transaction_date to datetime df_clean = df_clean.withColumn( 'transaction_date', to_timestamp(col('transaction_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss') ) # Step 4: Group by category and calculate sum of amount df_aggregated = df_clean \\ .groupBy('category') \\ .agg(spark_sum('amount').alias('total_amount')) \\ .orderBy(col('total_amount').desc()) df_aggregated.show() # Step 5: Write result to new CSV file df_aggregated.coalesce(1) \\ .write \\ .option('header', 'true') \\ .mode('overwrite') \\ .csv('/output/revenue_by_category') print('Processing complete\n• Results written to /output/revenue_by_category') spark.stop() SECTION 7: Challenges & Issues in Pipelines","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Task is taking 45 mins, as an experience data engineer what can be the issue and how","type":"concept","a":"• will you rec(cid:415)fy\n• If a pipeline task is taking 45 minutes, I would first analyze logs and execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy the bo(cid:425)leneck stage—whether it's extrac(cid:415)on, transforma(cid:415)on, or load\n• Common causes include full table scans, missing indexes, lack of incremental load, inefficient joins, data skew, or small file issues\n• I would op(cid:415)mize by implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loading using watermark or CDC, op(cid:415)mizing joins using broadcast or par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, improving cluster configura(cid:415)on, and par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data properly in storage\n• I would also reduce shuffles and unnecessary transforma(cid:415)ons and monitor performance a(cid:332)er each change","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"If there are 100 columns, how will you create schema?","type":"concept","a":"“I prefer schema defini(cid:415)on over schema inference for be(cid:425)er control and performance.” (cid:48132) Approach 1: Explicit schema (best prac(cid:415)ce) from pyspark.sql.types import * schema = StructType([ StructField(\"col1\", StringType(), True), StructField(\"col2\", IntegerType(), True), ... ]) (cid:48131) Approach 2: Read from source with schema inference (not preferred in prod) df = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"inferSchema\", True).csv(path) (cid:44931) Approach 3: Schema registry / metadata-driven (enterprise approach)  Store schema in: o JSON o Control table  Dynamically generate schema “For 100 columns, I prefer metadata-driven schema genera(cid:415)on or explicit schema defini(cid:415)on to avoid type mismatches and improve performance.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"(cid:44869)(cid:44870) What happens if broadcast is NOT used?","type":"concept","a":"• “Spark automa(cid:415)cally decides the join type based on size and configura(cid:415)on.” Default behavior:  Small + large table → Broadcast join (auto)  Large + large → Sort-Merge Join (cid:55982)(cid:55983)(cid:55984) Join selec(cid:415)on logic: Scenario Join Type Small table Broadcast Join Large tables Sort Merge Join Skewed data May cause shuffle skew “If broadcast is not explicitly specified, Spark’s op(cid:415)mizer (Catalyst + AQE) decides the join strategy\n• If one table is small enough, it automa(cid:415)cally performs a broadcast join; otherwise it uses sort-merge join for large datasets.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]}]},{"id":"ai-data-engineer","name":"AI Data Engineer","description":"AI Data Engineering interview preparation","topics":[{"id":"pyspark","name":"PySpark & Coding","icon":"🐍","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the difference between transformations and actions in PySpark.","type":"concept","a":"• Transformations — lazy; build a new DataFrame/plan. Narrow: select, filter, withColumn, union. Wide (shuffle): groupBy, join, distinct, repartition, orderBy.\n• Actions — eager; trigger execution and return results/driver data or write output: count, show, collect, take, write, save, first.\nOnly actions trigger computation; the physical plan is optimized by Catalyst before execution."},{"q":"What is the difference between Python UDFs and Spark built-in functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Built-in functions (pyspark.sql.functions) run natively in the JVM → Catalyst can optimize them, and they work with Tungsten/Photon.\n• Python UDFs run row-by-row, serialize data between JVM and Python → large overhead, can't be optimized by Catalyst, slower.\nPrefer built-ins; when Python logic is unavoidable use pandas UDFs (vectorized, Arrow-based) which are much faster.\nRegister: @udf(StringType()) or F.udf(f, StringType())."},{"q":"Explain window functions in PySpark/SQL with an example.","type":"concept","a":"Window functions compute values across a set of related rows without collapsing them.\n• Ranking: row_number(), rank(), dense_rank().\n• Analytic: lead()/lag() for previous/next rows, running totals with sum() over unbounded window.\nSpec = partitionBy + orderBy (+ rowsBetween).\nUse case: find the latest/max record per group, top-N per category, running totals, month-over-month change.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter('rn = 1').show()"},{"q":"Difference between ROW_NUMBER, RANK, and DENSE_RANK?","type":"concept","a":"• ROW_NUMBER — unique sequential number; ties broken arbitrarily; no gaps.\n• RANK — same rank for ties, then SKIPS numbers (1,2,2,4).\n• DENSE_RANK — same rank for ties, NO gaps (1,2,2,3).\nFor 'top N per group' or finding the Nth salary, DENSE_RANK is usually the correct choice; ROW_NUMBER when you need exactly one row."},{"q":"CODING: Find the highest salary per designation (PySpark).","type":"coding","a":"Use a window ranked by salary descending per designation and filter rank=1. Two common approaches below.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank, max as spark_max\n\n# Method 1: window\nw = Window.partitionBy('designation').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rnk', rank().over(w)).filter(col('rnk')==1).show()\n\n# Method 2: self-join with max\nmx = df.groupBy('designation').agg(spark_max('salary').alias('m'))\ndf.join(mx, (df.designation==mx.designation) & (df.salary==mx.m)).select(df['*']).show()"},{"q":"CODING: Find the second highest salary by department.","type":"coding","a":"dense_rank() = 2 per department. Subquery/self-join alternative also works.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc())\ndf.withColumn('rnk', dense_rank().over(w)).filter(col('rnk')==2).select('dept','name','salary').show()"},{"q":"CODING: Find patients undergoing more than 2 different treatment types.","type":"coding","a":"Group by patient id, count DISTINCT treatments, filter > 2.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import countDistinct, col\nresult = (df.groupBy('pid')\n            .agg(countDistinct('treatment').alias('treatment_count'))\n            .filter(col('treatment_count') > 2)\n            .select('pid'))\nresult.show()"},{"q":"CODING: Get the manager of each employee (self join, handle null manager).","type":"coding","a":"Alias the employee table twice; LEFT join employee to its manager so top-level employees (mgrid null) still appear.","code":"emp = df.alias('emp')\nmgr = df.alias('mgr')\nresult = (emp.join(mgr, col('emp.mgrid')==col('mgr.empid'), 'left')\n            .select(col('emp.empid').alias('employee_id'),\n                    col('emp.empname').alias('employee_name'),\n                    col('emp.mgrid').alias('manager_id'),\n                    col('mgr.empname').alias('manager_name')))\nresult.show()"},{"q":"CODING: Split a comma-separated column into multiple rows.","type":"coding","a":"split() the string into an array, then explode() it into rows.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import split, explode, col\n# Input Col3 = 'G,Y,U' -> rows G, Y, U\nresult = df.withColumn('Col3', explode(split(col('Col3'), ',')))\nresult.show()\n\n# SQL\n# SELECT Col1, Col2, exploded_val AS Col3\n# FROM t LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) tmp AS exploded_val"},{"q":"CODING: Remove duplicate records (keep the latest).","type":"coding","a":"Use ROW_NUMBER over the business key ordered by a recency column (or a dedupe key set) and keep rn=1.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('customer_id','name','email_id','order_date').orderBy(col('load_ts').desc())\ndf2 = df.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter('rn = 1').drop('rn')\ndf2.show()\n\n# Simplest form when rows are identical:\n# df.dropDuplicates(['customer_id','order_date'])"},{"q":"CODING: Calculate the total purchase amount for each customer.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and sum the purchase amount.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import sum as _sum, col\ndf.groupBy('customer_id').agg(_sum('purchase_amount').alias('total_amount')).orderBy(col('total_amount').desc()).show()"},{"q":"CODING: Process a 100M-row CSV efficiently.","type":"coding","a":"Key points: define schema explicitly (avoid inferSchema), use Parquet/Delta, filter early, tune shuffle partitions + AQE, coalesce output.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, to_timestamp, sum as _sum\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType\n\nspark = (SparkSession.builder.appName('LargeCSV')\n         .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true')\n         .config('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions','400').getOrCreate())\n\nschema = StructType([StructField('transaction_id',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('customer_id',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('transaction_date',StringType(),True),\n                     StructField('amount',DoubleType(),True),\n                     StructField('category',StringType(),True)])\n\ndf = (spark.read.option('header','true').option('mode','DROPMALFORMED')\n      .schema(schema).csv('/data/large_transactions.csv'))\ndf = df.dropna(subset=['transaction_id','customer_id','amount'])\ndf = df.withColumn('transaction_date', to_timestamp('transaction_date','yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss'))\nagg = df.groupBy('category').agg(_sum('amount').alias('total_amount')).orderBy(col('total_amount').desc())\nagg.coalesce(1).write.option('header','true').mode('overwrite').csv('/output/revenue_by_category')"},{"q":"CODING: Convert a Parquet file/folder into a Delta table.","type":"coding","a":"Read Parquet and write as Delta. Use CONVERT TO DELTA (SQL) to convert in place.","code":"# PySpark\ndf = spark.read.format('parquet').load('abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/')\ndf.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_table')\n\n# In-place conversion (SQL)\n# CONVERT TO DELTA parquet.`abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/`;"},{"q":"CODING: Read from Azure SQL via JDBC with an incremental predicate.","type":"coding","a":"Use spark.read.jdbc with a predicates list (pushed down to the source) and write to Delta with MERGE.","code":"jdbc = 'jdbc:sqlserver://srv.database.windows.net:1433;database=db'\nprops = {'user': dbutils.secrets.get('kv','sql-user'),\n         'password': dbutils.secrets.get('kv','sql-pwd'),\n         'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'}\nlast_wm = spark.sql(\"SELECT MAX(last_load_ts) FROM ctrl.watermark WHERE table_name='Customers'\").first()[0]\ndf = spark.read.jdbc(jdbc, 'dbo.Customers',\n        predicates=[f\"ModifiedDate > '{last_wm}'\"], properties=props)\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView('incr')\nspark.sql('''MERGE INTO silver.customers t USING incr s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\n             WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *''')"},{"q":"CODING: Compute a running total (cumulative sum) per group.","type":"coding","a":"Window with partitionBy group and orderBy date, cumulating from unbounded preceding to current row.","code":"from pyspark.sql import Window\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import sum as _sum, col\nw = Window.partitionBy('customer_id').orderBy('order_date').rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)\ndf.withColumn('running_total', _sum('amount').over(w)).show()"},{"q":"How do you handle NULLs in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• Drop: df.dropna(); df.dropna(subset=['id','name']); df.dropna(how='all').\n• Fill: df.fillna(0); df.fillna({'salary':0,'dept':'N/A'}); fill with mean/median.\n• Conditional: withColumn('x', when(col('x').isNull(), lit(0)).otherwise(col('x'))).\n• Forward fill: last('col', ignorenulls=True) over a window.\n• Filter: df.filter(col('id').isNotNull()).\n• coalesce(col1, col2) to pick the first non-null."},{"q":"How do you read and flatten nested JSON / structs and arrays?","type":"concept","a":"• Access struct fields: df.select('col.field') or col('col')['field'].\n• Explode arrays: explode(col('arr')) (one row per element) or explode_outer (keeps nulls).\n• Flatten structs: select col('address.*') to promote nested fields.\n• explode map: explode(map_col) → key/value rows.\n• Define schema for nested reads, or let Auto Loader infer; use from_json() for JSON strings."},{"q":"When do you use left anti join and left semi join?","type":"concept","a":"• LEFT ANTI — returns LEFT rows with NO match in the right table (like NOT EXISTS). Use for finding orphans/unmatched records. how='left_anti'.\n• LEFT SEMI — returns LEFT rows that DO have a match in the right (no right columns returned). how='left_semi'. Use as an efficient EXISTS.\nBoth avoid duplicate rows from the right table (unlike a normal join).","code":"unmatched = orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\nexisting  = orders.join(customers, 'customer_id', 'left_semi')"},{"q":"How do you pivot and unpivot data in PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• Pivot: df.groupBy('dept').pivot('year').sum('salary') — rows → columns.\n• Unpivot/stack: use stack() in SQL or create arrays/maps and explode, or use melt pattern:\n  df.selectExpr(\"id\", \"stack(3,'A',A,'B',B,'C',C) as (key,value)\").\nCommon for turning wide reports into long format for aggregation."},{"q":"What is the difference between union and unionByName?","type":"concept","a":"• union — combines DataFrames by POSITION; schemas must have the same column order/types or you get wrong/NULL results.\n• unionByName — combines by column NAME; align columns correctly regardless of order; use allowMissingColumns=True to fill missing columns with null.\nPrefer unionByName for safety when schemas may differ slightly."},{"q":"How do you create a Spark session and read a DataFrame?","type":"coding","a":"Standard SparkSession pattern; in Databricks 'spark' already exists.","code":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession\nspark = (SparkSession.builder.appName('App')\n         .config('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled','true').getOrCreate())\ndf = spark.read.format('parquet').option('header',True).load('/path')\ndf.printSchema(); df.show(5, truncate=False)"},{"q":"Which services have you used in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Men(cid:415)on:  Databricks Workspace  Clusters  Job Clusters  Interac(cid:415)ve Clusters  Notebooks (Python, SQL, PySpark)  Delta Lake  Unity Catalog (if used)  Repos  Workflows  Secrets  Auto Loader","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Joins in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Inner / Outer / Left Join — Sample Query","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of joins available in Pyspark","type":"concept","a":"➢ INNER JOIN. ➢ CROSS JOIN. ➢ LEFT OUTER JOIN. ➢ RIGHT OUTER JOIN. ➢ FULL OUTER JOIN. ➢ LEFT SEMI JOIN. ➢ LEFT ANTI JOIN.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to write sql or any other langue in python Notebook","type":"concept","a":"A. %Scala & % R","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me how many records will come for","type":"concept","a":"INNER JOIN ---solution1 select * from dbo.table1 as a inner join table2 b on a.id=b.id ---solution2 SELECT A.id FROM table1 A INNER JOIN Table2 B ON A.id=B.id LEFT JOIN SELECT A.id FROM table1 A left join Table2 B ON A.id=B.id RIGHT JOIN SELECT A.id FROM table1 A right join Table2 B ON A.id=B.id FULL JOIN SELECT * FROM table1 A full join Table2 B ON A.id=B.id CROSS JOIN select * from table1, table2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Join — Join on CustomerID (Inner Join)","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How will you calculate Total Hours Worked?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would calculate the difference between LogoutTime and LoginTime\n• Total Hours = LogoutTime - LoginTime The result is converted into hours\n• If an employee worked in two loca(cid:415)ons (L1 and L2), I would keep both records separately because each loca(cid:415)on represents a different a(cid:425)endance entry.\" Write the PySpark code. from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import * emp = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True).csv(\"/employee\") a(cid:425)endance = spark.read.op(cid:415)on(\"header\", True).csv(\"/a(cid:425)endance\") # Remove duplicate employees emp = emp.dropDuplicates([\"EmpID\"]) # Handle null employee names emp = emp.fillna({\"EmpName\": \"Unknown\"}) # Reject invalid a(cid:425)endance reject_df = a(cid:425)endance.filter( col(\"LoginTime\").isNull() & col(\"LogoutTime\").isNull() ) # Valid a(cid:425)endance a(cid:425)endance = a(cid:425)endance.filter( ~(col(\"LoginTime\").isNull() & col(\"LogoutTime\").isNull()) ) # Calculate total hours a(cid:425)endance = a(cid:425)endance.withColumn( \"TotalHours\", (unix_(cid:415)mestamp(\"LogoutTime\") - unix_(cid:415)mestamp(\"LoginTime\")) / 3600 ) gold = a(cid:425)endance.join(emp, \"EmpID\", \"le(cid:332)\") gold.select( \"EmpID\", emp.EmpName, \"Loca(cid:415)on\", \"Date\", \"TotalHours\" ).show()","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Sal(cid:415)ng the Skewed Key","type":"concept","a":"➢Add a random number (salt) to the skewed key before the join or aggrega(cid:415)on. ➢Helps distribute the records more evenly.","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Use Databricks Notebook, Python, JAR, or Run Job ac(cid:415)vity.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on and Bucke(cid:415)ng","type":"concept","a":"Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on Bucke(cid:415)ng Splits data into folders Splits data into fixed buckets Based on column values Based on hash func(cid:415)on Good for filtering Good for joins Number of par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons depends on data Number of buckets is fixed Creates directory structure Stores files in bucket files Example: df.write.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"country\") df.write.bucketBy(8, \"customer_id\")","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Join 50 GB table with 3 GB table","type":"concept","a":"Use a Broadcast Join if the smaller table fits within Spark's broadcast threshold (or if explicitly broadcas(cid:415)ng is appropriate). from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"customer_id\" ) This avoids shuffling the large table across the cluster.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Use broadcast join (if small table)","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\")","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"We have added the credit amount and subtract the debit amount for a customer in","type":"concept","a":"python(pyspark) code Credit - Debit logic in PySpark Requirement:  Add credit  Subtract debit per customer Code: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import sum, when, col df_result = df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").agg( sum(when(col(\"type\") == \"credit\", col(\"amount\")).otherwise(0)).alias(\"total_credit\"), sum(when(col(\"type\") == \"debit\", col(\"amount\")).otherwise(0)).alias(\"total_debit\") ).withColumn( \"net_balance\", col(\"total_credit\") - col(\"total_debit\") ) df_result.show()","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Col1 a a b b col2 has Aa aa bb bb col3 has 1 2 3 4","type":"concept","a":"how to get output col1 a col2 b col3 [1,2] [3,4] in python(pyspark) code Grouping + array output problem (PySpark) Input: col1 col2 col3 a Aa 1 a aa 2 b bb 3 b bb 4 Output:  col1 = a → col3 = [1,2]  col1 = b → col3 = [3,4] (cid:33158)(cid:57393) PySpark Solu(cid:415)on: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_list df_result = df.groupBy(\"col1\").agg( collect_list(\"col3\").alias(\"col3_list\") ) df_result.show() If you also want col2 grouping logic: df.groupBy(\"col1\", \"col2\").agg( collect_list(\"col3\").alias(\"col3_list\") )","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Split Comma-Separated Column into Rows","type":"concept","a":"• Input: Col1 Col2 Col3 A 1 G,Y,U B 2 U,X,C Expected Output: Col1 Col2 Col3 A 1 G A 1 Y A 1 U B 2 U B 2 X B 2 C SQL — using EXPLODE + SPLIT -- Spark SQL SELECT Col1, Col2, exploded_val AS Col3 FROM my_table LATERAL VIEW EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) tmp AS exploded_val; -- Alternative using inline EXPLODE SELECT Col1, Col2, Col3_item AS Col3 FROM my_table CROSS JOIN LATERAL (SELECT EXPLODE(SPLIT(Col3, ',')) AS Col3_item);\n• PySpark — split() + explode() from pyspark.sql.functions import split, explode, col data = [('A', 1, 'G,Y,U'), ('B', 2, 'U,X,C')] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['Col1','Col2','Col3']) Page 12 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide # Split the comma-separated string into an array, then explode result = df.withColumn('Col3', explode(split(col('Col3'), ','))) result.show() # Output: # +----+----+----+ # |Col1|Col2|Col3| # +----+----+----+ # | A| 1| G| # | A| 1| Y| # | A| 1| U| # | B| 2| U| # | B| 2| X| # | B| 2| C| # +----+----+----+","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Difference Between Left and Right Joins","type":"concept","a":"Aspect LEFT JOIN RIGHT JOIN Definition All rows from LEFT table + matching All rows from RIGHT table + rows from RIGHT matching rows from LEFT Unmatched rows Unmatched RIGHT rows become NULL Unmatched LEFT rows become NULL Use case Keep all records from left (driving) table Keep all records from right (lookup) table Common use Find all orders including those without Find all customers including those customers without orders NULL side NULLs appear in RIGHT table columns NULLs appear in LEFT table columns Interchangeable YES — LEFT JOIN A,B = RIGHT JOIN Same relationship, different table B,A order Sample Data Table A — Orders: (order_id: 1,2,3,4) | Table B — Customers: (customer_id: 1,2,5) -- LEFT JOIN: all orders, customer info if exists SELECT o.order_id, c.name FROM orders o LEFT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id; -- Result: rows 1,2 have customer name | rows 3,4 have NULL for name -- RIGHT JOIN: all customers, order info if exists SELECT o.order_id, c.name FROM orders o RIGHT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id; -- Result: rows 1,2 have order_id | row 5 has NULL for order_id -- PySpark left_result = orders_df.join(customers_df, 'customer_id', 'left') right_result = orders_df.join(customers_df, 'customer_id', 'right')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How do you optimize if data is unevenly distributed?","type":"concept","a":"• Uneven data distribution (data skew) is one of the most common Spark performance problems\n• Solutions: 1\n• Salting: Add a random salt key to the join/group key to break the skewed key into multiple smaller partitions. import random from pyspark.sql.functions import col, lit, concat, floor, rand # Add salt to skewed key num_salts = 10 df_salted = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('skewed_key'), lit('_'), (rand() * num_salts).cast('int').cast('string')))","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"(one has more columns, one has less). How do you handle this?","type":"concept","a":"• Approach 1: Schema Merge with mergeSchema option df = (spark.read .option('header', 'true') .option('mergeSchema', 'true') .csv('/mnt/landing/csv_files/')) # Columns missing in a file will be filled with null Approach 2: Auto Loader with Schema Evolution df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/schema/') .load('/mnt/csv/')) Approach 3: Define an Explicit Schema Define a superset schema (all possible columns) and use it during read\n• Missing columns will be null. from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, DoubleType schema = StructType([ StructField('col1', StringType(), True), StructField('col2', StringType(), True), StructField('col3', DoubleType(), True) # May be missing in some files ]) df = spark.read.schema(schema).option('header', 'true').csv('/mnt/csv/') Section 6: SCD & Merge Operations","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Given the transaction table, write PySpark code using Window functions to get","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Transforma(cid:415)ons vs Ac(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Transforma(cid:415)ons  filter  select  join  groupBy  map Ac(cid:415)ons  show  count  collect  first  take  write","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast Nested Loop Join","type":"concept","a":"Used for non-equality joins.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Cartesian Join","type":"concept","a":"df1.crossJoin(df2) Produces every possible combina(cid:415)on of rows.","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Write the output for le(cid:332) join, an(cid:415)-le(cid:332) join & inner join for a given table","type":"concept","a":"• Output of joins (LEFT, INNER, LEFT ANTI) Assume: Table A (Employees) id name 1 A 2 B 3 C Table B (Departments) id dept 1 IT 3 HR (cid:44934) INNER JOIN SELECT * FROM A INNER JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;\n• Output: id name dept 1 A IT 3 C HR (cid:44934) LEFT JOIN SELECT * FROM A LEFT JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;\n• Output: id name dept 1 A IT 2 B NULL 3 C HR (cid:44934) LEFT ANTI JOIN SELECT * FROM A LEFT ANTI JOIN B ON A.id = B.id;\n• Output: id name 2 B Meaning:  Rows in A NOT present in B","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Can we implement schema enforcement?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Methods:  Explicit Column Mapping  Data Flow schema valida(cid:415)on  Databricks schema comparison  ValidateMetadata ac(cid:415)vity  Custom Python/PySpark valida(cid:415)on Example: Source Columns = Target Columns AND Source Count = Target Count If mismatch: Fail Ac(cid:415)vity","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Check par(cid:415)(cid:415)on count.","type":"concept","a":"7. Check joins.","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Rate yourself in Python, PySpark, SQL","type":"concept","a":"• Example: Python: 7.5/10 PySpark: 8.5/10 SQL: 9/10 Explain: \"I use SQL daily for transforma(cid:415)ons and valida(cid:415)ons\n• PySpark is my primary transforma(cid:415)on language in Databricks, and I use Python mainly for scrip(cid:415)ng, API integra(cid:415)on, and u(cid:415)lity func(cid:415)ons.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Join ques(cid:415)on , How many rows will be returned for inner,le(cid:332),right?","type":"concept","a":"Table 1 a b 0 1 0 2 1 4 1 5 Rows = 4 Table 2 a d 0 bat 0 net 0 ball 1 cat 2 dog Rows = 5 Inner Join For a = 0:  2 rows in Table 1 × 3 rows in Table 2 = 6 For a = 1:  2 × 1 = 2 For a = 2:  No match Total = 8 rows Le(cid:332) Join All matching rows plus unmatched rows from the le(cid:332) table.  Matches for a = 0: 6  Matches for a = 1: 2  No unmatched rows in Table 1 Total = 8 rows Right Join All matching rows plus unmatched rows from the right table.  Matches: 8  Unmatched a = 2 row from Table 2: 1 Total = 9 rows Final Answer Join Type Rows Inner Join 8 Le(cid:332) Join 8 Right Join 9 Round 2 :","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• \"For SCD Type 2, I compare the source and target using the business key (Customer_ID)\n• If a(cid:425)ributes like Address have changed, I update the exis(cid:415)ng record by se(cid:427)ng End_Date and Is_Current = 'N', then insert a new row with the updated values, a new Start_Date, End_Date = NULL, and Is_Current = 'Y'\n• This preserves the full history.\" Exis(cid:415)ng Table | Customer_ID | Name | Address | Start_Date | End_Date | Is_Current | Incoming Data | Customer_ID | Name | Address | Goal If the customer's address changes:  Expire the old record  Insert a new record  Maintain history Step 1: Join source and target joined = source.alias(\"s\").join( target.alias(\"t\"), \"Customer_ID\", \"le(cid:332)\" ) Step 2: Find changed records changed = joined.filter( (joined.Address != joined[\"t.Address\"]) & (joined[\"t.Is_Current\"] == \"Y\") ) Step 3: Expire old records expired = changed.select( \"Customer_ID\", \"t.Name\", \"t.Address\", \"t.Start_Date\" ).withColumn( \"End_Date\", current_date() ).withColumn( \"Is_Current\", lit(\"N\") ) Step 4: Insert new records new_records = changed.select( \"Customer_ID\", \"s.Name\", \"s.Address\" ).withColumn( \"Start_Date\", current_date() ).withColumn( \"End_Date\", lit(None) ).withColumn( \"Is_Current\", lit(\"Y\") ) Step 5: Keep unchanged records unchanged = target.filter(target.Is_Current == \"Y\") Step 6: Final output final_df = unchanged.unionByName(expired).unionByName(new_records)","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Convert JSON Data into a Structured Table?","type":"concept","a":"Flat JSON df = spark.read.format('json').load('/data/events.json') df.printSchema() # Spark auto-infers schema df.show() Nested JSON with explicit schema from pyspark.sql.functions import col, explode, from_json from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, IntegerType, ArrayType schema = StructType([ StructField('order_id', StringType()), StructField('customer', StructType([ StructField('id', StringType()), StructField('name', StringType()) ])), StructField('items', ArrayType(StructType([ StructField('product_id', StringType()), StructField('qty', IntegerType()) ]))) ]) df = spark.read.schema(schema).json('/data/orders.json') Page 5 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) # Flatten nested struct df_flat = df.select('order_id', col('customer.id').alias('cust_id'), col('customer.name').alias('name')) # Explode array column df_items = df.select('order_id', explode(col('items')).alias('item')) df_items = df_items.select('order_id', col('item.product_id'), col('item.qty')) # JSON stored as a string column df = df.withColumn('parsed', from_json(col('json_col'), schema)).select('parsed.*')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What do you do in Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":" Data cleansing: o Remove duplicates o Handle nulls o Standardize schema  Apply business rules  Join reference/master data  Data is validated and structured","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize joins (Broadcast Join if applicable).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Python Problem","type":"concept","a":"Example: A = [1,2,3] B = A A = A + [4] print(A, B) Output: [1,2,3,4] [1,2,3] Reason: + creates a new list, so A references a new object while B s(cid:415)ll points to the original list.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"22 & 23. What is Pandas?","type":"concept","a":"Pandas is a Python library for:  Data analysis  Data cleaning  Small to medium-sized datasets  DataFrames  CSV/Excel processing Interview point: \"Pandas works on a single machine, whereas PySpark is distributed and designed for big data.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Sets in Python – Why Sets?","type":"concept","a":"A set is an unordered collec(cid:415)on of unique elements.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used Broadcast Join in your project?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes\n• In my project, we join a large employee transac(cid:415)on table with a small department lookup table\n• Since the department table is small, we use a broadcast join to avoid shuffling the large dataset and improve performance.\" PySpark Syntax from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = employee_df.join( broadcast(department_df), \"DeptID\", \"le(cid:332)\" )","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Write Broadcast Join Syntax.","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast result = large_df.join( broadcast(small_df), \"EmpID\" ) Client Round 1","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"External Logic in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• External logic refers to code/functions that are defined outside of Databricks but invoked within pipelines: Ways to use External Logic: • UDFs (User Defined Functions): Python/Scala functions registered in Spark • Python libraries: pip install custom packages via %pip or cluster init scripts • Azure Functions / REST APIs: Called from notebooks via HTTP requests • External JARs: Custom Java/Scala libraries added to cluster • MLflow models: External ML models called for inference in pipelines # Registering a Python UDF from pyspark.sql.functions import udf from pyspark.sql.types import StringType @udf(StringType()) def clean_phone(phone): return phone.replace('-', '').strip() if phone else None df = df.withColumn('clean_phone', clean_phone(col('phone_number'))) Q6\n• Data Skew Data Skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across partitions in a Spark cluster, causing some tasks to take much longer than others (stragglers)\n• Causes: • High cardinality or NULL keys in joins/group-bys • Hot keys (e.g., one customer ID with millions of records) Solutions: • Salting: Add a random prefix to keys to spread load • AQE (Adaptive Query Execution): Spark 3.x automatically handles skew • Broadcast Join: Broadcast smaller table to avoid shuffle • Skew hints: Use SKEW hint in SQL • Repartition: Manually repartition on a more uniform key # Enable AQE (Spark 3.x) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') # Salting example import random salt_udf = udf(lambda: random.randint(0, 9), IntegerType()) df = df.withColumn('salt', salt_udf()) df = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('key'), lit('_'), col('salt')))","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"In which case will you prefer Data flow Gen2 and Notebook for pyspark.","type":"concept","a":"• I prefer Dataflow Gen2 when the transforma(cid:415)ons are simple, low-code, and business- oriented like filtering, joins, and basic aggrega(cid:415)ons\n• It is faster to implement and ideal for standard ETL pipelines\n• I prefer PySpark notebooks when dealing with large-scale data, complex transforma(cid:415)ons, performance tuning, or advanced logic like window func(cid:415)ons, incremental processing, and Delta Lake opera(cid:415)ons\n• PySpark gives more flexibility and control over distributed processing","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Broadcast join + default join behavior","type":"concept","a":"","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"(cid:48132) How to write broadcast join?","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import broadcast df1.join(broadcast(df2), \"id\") OR SQL style hint: SELECT /*+ BROADCAST(t2) */ * FROM t1 JOIN t2 ON t1.id = t2.id;","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"delta","name":"Delta Lake","icon":"🗄️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is Delta Lake / a Delta table and what problems does it solve?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that sits on top of Parquet in cloud storage and adds: ACID transactions, a transaction log (_delta_log), schema enforcement & evolution, time travel, UPSERT/MERGE, and unified batch + streaming reads/writes.\nIt brings reliability and warehouse-like guarantees to a data lake (the 'lakehouse'), fixing typical data-lake issues: no ACID, no schema control, slow updates, and small-file problems."},{"q":"Explain ACID properties in Delta Lake and the transaction log.","type":"concept","a":"• Atomicity — a commit fully succeeds or fully fails (no partial writes).\n• Consistency — schema enforcement keeps data valid.\n• Isolation — snapshot isolation for concurrent reads/writes.\n• Durability — committed data persists even after failures.\nThe _delta_log is an ordered set of JSON commits recording every change (add/remove files, metadata, protocol). Each read reconstructs the table state from the log; the log is the source of truth and enables time travel and atomic operations."},{"q":"What are the file types inside the _delta_log folder?","type":"concept","a":"• .json — incremental transaction files, one per commit (AddFile, RemoveFile, metadata, protocol). No actual data.\n• .checkpoint.parquet — a snapshot of table state every ~10 commits so readers don't replay all JSON files.\n• .compacted.json — combines multiple small JSON commits to speed up log reads.\n• .crc — checksum/integrity file for checkpoints; on corruption Spark falls back to the previous valid checkpoint.\nTogether they keep metadata reads fast and reliable."},{"q":"What is Delta table metadata log management and how does it become a bottleneck?","type":"concept","a":"Every commit adds a JSON file to _delta_log; over time this grows and slows query planning. Mitigation:\n• Checkpoints (every 10 commits) collapse state so readers load 1 checkpoint + recent JSONs.\n• delta.logRetentionDuration (default 30 days) — old JSON commits are pruned once a checkpoint exists.\n• Compacted JSON files reduce the number of individual commits.\n• OPTIMIZE compacts the underlying Parquet data files (not the log).\nNote: copying the log elsewhere doesn't fix performance — checkpointing + retention does."},{"q":"How does time travel work in Delta Lake?","type":"concept","a":"Delta keeps every version in the log, so you can query historical snapshots:\n• By version: SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 5 (or option('versionAsOf',5)).\n• By timestamp: SELECT * FROM t TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01'.\n• DESCRIBE HISTORY t shows the commit history (operations, versions, timestamps).\nUse cases: audit, reproduce past results, recover from bad writes (RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF n).\nLimitation: time travel is bounded by VACUUM retention — once old files are removed, those versions are gone."},{"q":"Difference between schema enforcement and schema evolution in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema enforcement (validation) — by default Delta rejects writes whose schema doesn't match the table, protecting data integrity.\n• Schema evolution — you can intentionally allow new/changed columns:\n  – df.write.option('mergeSchema','true').mode('append')\n  – SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true\n  – ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ...\n  – COPY INTO ... COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema'='true')\n• overwriteSchema replaces the whole schema (use with caution)."},{"q":"How do you implement MERGE (UPSERT) in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"MERGE matches source to target and conditionally updates/inserts/deletes — the standard upsert pattern for incremental/CDC loads.\nExample:\ntarget.alias('t').merge(incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.id = s.id').whenMatchedUpdateAll().whenNotMatchedInsertAll().execute()\nSQL: MERGE INTO t USING s ON t.id=s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\nSupports whenMatchedDelete, conditional updates, and partial column updates. Requires the join condition to be on key columns for efficiency."},{"q":"What does OPTIMIZE do?","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE compacts many small Parquet files into fewer, larger (~1 GB) files to improve read performance and reduce metadata overhead (fixes the small-file problem).\n• OPTIMIZE table; or OPTIMIZE table WHERE date >= '2026-01-01' (target recent partitions).\n• OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY (col) adds clustering for data skipping.\nRun after bulk loads / daily ETL. Target file size is configurable via delta.optimize.maxFileSize. Auto Optimize can do this automatically."},{"q":"What is Z-ORDER and how does it improve performance?","type":"concept","a":"Z-ORDER co-locates related rows into the same files by reordering data across multiple columns (multi-dimensional clustering).\n• Delta's data-skipping uses file min/max stats to skip files that can't match a filter; good clustering means fewer files to scan.\n• Best on HIGH-cardinality columns used frequently in WHERE/JOIN (customer_id, transaction_id, date).\n• Syntax: OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date).\n• It's expensive — apply to the most queried columns only. Don't combine with auto compaction."},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compacts small files into bigger files; no sorting; always useful for small-file cleanup.\n• Z-ORDER — does the compaction AND sorts/clusters data by column values within files for data skipping; best for high-cardinality filter columns; requires re-running to stay fresh.\n• Liquid Clustering — dynamic, incremental clustering on chosen keys; Databricks re-clusters new data automatically, no partition maintenance, works with evolving workloads; replaces partition+z-order.\nModern guidance: prefer Liquid Clustering; use OPTIMIZE (with ZORDER) on legacy partitioned tables."},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"Liquid Clustering is a Databricks Delta feature that organizes data by clustering keys without traditional Hive partitioning.\n• Define keys once: ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date) (or CLUSTER BY AUTO).\n• Databricks incrementally re-clusters new data; keys can change without rewriting the whole table.\n• Removes partition-column cardinality pitfalls and small-file issues; improves data skipping.\n• No need to run Z-ORDER manually; useful for evolving/changing query patterns."},{"q":"What is VACUUM and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM permanently deletes data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta log and are past the retention period (default 7 days).\n• Reduces storage cost and scan overhead.\n• VACUUM t RETAIN 168 HOURS; add DRY RUN to see what would be deleted.\n• Warning: vacuuming deletes old versions, so you LOSE time travel beyond the retention. Lowering retention below 7 days requires disabling the safety check (risky — can corrupt concurrent readers)."},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed (CDF) and how does it differ from CDC?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF (Delta feature) — records row-level changes (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) in a Delta table so downstream can read changes incrementally.\n  Enable: ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed=true); read with readChangeFeed=true + startingVersion.\n  Extra columns: _change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp.\n• CDC — the broader process of capturing INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from a SOURCE system (via log-based tools like Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, triggers, or timestamp polling).\nIn short: CDC captures changes at the source; CDF propagates Delta table changes downstream. CDF is a Delta-native way to consume changes."},{"q":"What are Deletion Vectors?","type":"concept","a":"Deletion Vectors mark deleted rows in a bitmap rather than immediately rewriting the entire Parquet file.\n• Makes DELETE/UPDATE/MERGE much faster (avoids rewriting whole files).\n• Readers transparently respect the deletion vector; compacted later by OPTIMIZE.\n• Enable via table property delta.enableDeletionVectors=true. Trade-off: slight read overhead until optimized."},{"q":"Difference between DEEP CLONE and SHALLOW CLONE?","type":"concept","a":"• DEEP CLONE — copies the data files AND the metadata/history to a new location; fully independent; supports time travel on the clone; used for migration/backup/experimentation.\n• SHALLOW CLONE — copies only the metadata and references the original data files (no data copy); fast/cheap; if the source files are vacuumed or changed, the clone breaks (depends on source).\nUse deep clone for independent copies; shallow clone for quick test/dev without data duplication.\nSyntax: CREATE TABLE dest DEEP|SHALLOW CLONE source."},{"q":"Difference between managed (internal) and external tables in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Managed table — Databricks manages the storage location and lifecycle; DROP TABLE deletes BOTH metadata AND data files. Default format is Delta. Best for lakehouse/DW tables.\n• External table — you specify the storage location (ADLS/S3); DROP TABLE deletes only metadata, data files REMAIN. Shared across tools.\nProduction rule: use external tables (or Unity Catalog managed with careful governance) on ADLS so a drop can't accidentally destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to move/copy a Delta table to another location. What options do you have?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read + write new location: spark.read.format('delta').load(src).write.format('delta').save(dest).\n• DEEP CLONE — preserves data + history/metadata to a new path (independent).\n• SHALLOW CLONE — metadata-only, references original files (fast/cheap).\n• Export to Parquet/CSV for external consumption.\nChoose deep clone for a full copy, shallow clone for a quick reference."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A managed table was dropped and, after the retention period, the files were permanently removed. Can you recreate it?","type":"scenario","a":"No — not from Delta alone. Time travel and RESTORE depend on the transaction log AND the underlying data files still existing. Once both are permanently deleted, recovery options are:\n• Restore from backup/DR copy.\n• Reload from the source system and reprocess.\n• Recover via cloud storage versioning/soft-delete if enabled.\nLesson: use external tables (or backups) in production so a DROP doesn't destroy data."},{"q":"SCENARIO: The CRC file is corrupted. What happens?","type":"scenario","a":"• The .crc file validates checkpoint integrity. If the CRC check fails, Spark gracefully falls back to the PREVIOUS valid checkpoint and uses the recent JSON commits to rebuild current state.\n• So reads typically still succeed — the corrupted checkpoint is simply skipped.\n• Fix root cause (e.g., partial/interrupted write). Delta is resilient because the log + checkpoints are redundant; you can also force a new checkpoint with OPTIMIZE or Spark actions."},{"q":"What are Delta table optimization techniques?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — compact small files.\n• Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering — co-locate related data for data skipping.\n• Partitioning — on low-cardinality columns (date/region) where partitions are ~1 GB+.\n• Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) — automatic file sizing.\n• VACUUM — remove stale files.\n• Deletion Vectors — fast deletes/updates.\n• Predictive Optimization (UC) — automatic maintenance.\n• Right file size (~128 MB–1 GB), avoid over-partitioning, use Photon + AQE, and keep statistics for data skipping."},{"q":"What is the small-file problem and how do you fix it in Delta?","type":"concept","a":"Streaming/micro-batch or many small writes create thousands of tiny files, causing high metadata/listing overhead and slow reads.\nFix:\n• OPTIMIZE to compact into larger files; run on a schedule.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) for automatic compaction.\n• Use trigger(availableNow=True) or larger micro-batches in streaming; consider foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy.\n• Set a target file size and coalesce before writes."},{"q":"How do COPY INTO and Auto Loader differ for ingestion?","type":"concept","a":"• COPY INTO — SQL-based, idempotent batch ingestion of files from cloud storage into a Delta table; tracks already-loaded files; good for scheduled/batch loads.\n• Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — Structured Streaming source that incrementally and continuously discovers new files (file notification or directory listing); scalable, handles schema inference/evolution and rescued data; best for high-volume, near-real-time ingestion.\nUse COPY INTO for simple batch; Auto Loader for continuous/scalable incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is Time Travel? What is the reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• Time Travel allows querying previous versions of a Delta table\n• Example: SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5; or SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-01-01';\n• Default reten(cid:415)on:  Transac(cid:415)on Log = 30 days  Deleted data (VACUUM) reten(cid:415)on = 7 days","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore a table beyond the default reten(cid:415)on period?","type":"concept","a":"• If the data has already been removed by VACUUM, it cannot be restored using Delta Time Travel because the underlying files have been permanently deleted\n• Possible recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from an ADLS backup or snapshot.  Recreate the table from the original source.  Use an external backup or disaster recovery solu(cid:415)on\n• If the files are s(cid:415)ll available (for example, because VACUUM has not removed them), you can restore with: RESTORE TABLE sales TO VERSION AS OF 10; or RESTORE TABLE sales TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2025-05-01';","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you increase reten(cid:415)on period for one table?","type":"concept","a":"• Example: ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.deletedFileReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='30 days' ); or ALTER TABLE sales SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.logReten(cid:415)onDura(cid:415)on'='90 days' );\n• This changes reten(cid:415)on only for that specific Delta table","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER?","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE Over (cid:415)me, many small files can be created due to frequent writes\n• OPTIMIZE compacts these small files into fewer, larger files\n• Example: Before: 1000 files × 5 MB A(cid:332)er: 10 files × 500 MB OPTIMIZE sales;\n• Benefits:  Fewer files to open.  Faster reads.  Be(cid:425)er query performance\n• Z-ORDER ZORDER physically reorganizes data within the op(cid:415)mized files so that rows with similar values are stored close together\n• OPTIMIZE sales ZORDER BY (customer_id);\n• If you frequently run: SELECT * FROM sales WHERE customer_id = 101;\n• Z-Ordering helps Spark read only the relevant por(cid:415)ons of the data, reducing the amount scanned\n• Difference between OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Compacts small files into larger ones Reorders data within files based on specified columns Reduces file management overhead Improves data skipping for filtered queries Improves general read performance Improves queries filtering on Z-Ordered columns Can be run alone Used as part of OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER BY (...)","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delta Lake Architecture","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE, ZORDER & VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Time Travel in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Why Is This Useful?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Case Example Debugging A bug caused wrong data to be written yesterday — query v10 to see the clean state Rollback Accidentally deleted rows — restore from a previous version Auditing Regulatory requirement to show what data looked like on Jan 1st Reproducibility ML model trained on v5 of the data — reproduce by reading that version Time Travel Code # ── Query by VERSION NUMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────── # SQL spark.sql('SELECT * FROM silver.orders VERSION AS OF 5') # Python df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', '5') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── Query by TIMESTAMP ──────────────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM silver.orders TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-15 10:00:00'\") df_yesterday = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders') # ── See all versions (history) ──────────────────────────────────────── spark.sql('DESCRIBE HISTORY silver.orders').show(truncate=False) # Shows: version, timestamp, operation (WRITE/DELETE/MERGE), user, etc. # ── RESTORE a table to a previous version ──────────────────────────── spark.sql('RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO VERSION AS OF 3') # Or by timestamp: spark.sql(\"RESTORE TABLE silver.orders TO TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-14'\") ⚠️ Time Travel Limitation Time travel only works as long as the old Parquet files exist\n• Running VACUUM deletes those files permanently\n• Default VACUUM retention = 7 days → time travel available for the last 7 days by default\n• Increase retention period if you need longer time travel: ALTER TABLE \n• SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.logRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days')","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"• Azure Data bricks is a data analytics platform optimized for the Microsoft Azure cloud services platform\n• Azure Data bricks offers three environments for developing data intensive applications: Data bricks SQL, Data bricks Data Science & Engineering, and Data bricks Machine Learning","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of clusters in Azure Data bricks","type":"concept","a":"➢ Single Node: Doesn’t have any worker nodes and recommended for the single user cluster the small data volume. ➢ Standard: Recommend for a Single user cluster and can run SQL, Python, R, SCALA workload . ➢ High Concurrency: Optimized to run concurrency SQL, Python, R workloads rund and scala is not supported.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is delta Lake, How to create delta lake tables","type":"concept","a":"• Delta lake is an open-source data format that provides ACID transactions, data reliability, query performance, data caching and indexing, and many other benefits\n• Delta lake can be thought of as an extension of existing data lakes and can be configured per the data requirements","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Difference Between Data Lake and Delta lake","type":"concept","a":"Page | 15 Page | 16 =============AZURE DATA BRICKS TABLES AND SYNTAX==================== https://sparkbyexamples.com/pyspark/pyspark-withcolumn/ --- This link is reference of any commands CREATE A DATAFRAME BY USING A SPARK It means that Output will be visual in Table Syntax : DF=spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema=colunm) Joins: There is a Different joins like Inner, Left Join(left outer, left semi), Right Outer Join, Semi Table Syntax: emp = [(1,\"Smith\",-1,\"2018\",\"10\",\"M\",3000), \\ (2,\"Rose\",1,\"2010\",\"20\",\"M\",4000), \\ (3,\"Williams\",1,\"2010\",\"10\",\"M\",1000), \\ (4,\"Jones\",2,\"2005\",\"10\",\"F\",2000), \\ (5,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"40\",\"\",-1), \\ (6,\"Brown\",2,\"2010\",\"50\",\"\",-1) \\ ] empColumns = [\"emp_id\",\"name\",\"superior_emp_id\",\"year_joined\", \\ \"emp_dept_id\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] empDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=emp, schema = empColumns) empDF.show() --- Creating a Data Frame dept = [(\"Finance\",10), \\ (\"Marketing\",20), \\ (\"Sales\",30), \\ (\"IT\",40) \\ ] deptColumns = [\"dept_name\",\"dept_id\"] deptDF = spark.createDataFrame(data=dept, schema = deptColumns) deptDF.show() ---- Creating a Data Frame and show Syntax: Page | 17 empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"Inner\" ).show() --- it is a Inner join empDF.join(deptDF,empDF.emp_dept_id == deptDF.dept_id,\"leftsemi\" ).show() -- it is a Left join SELFJOIN from pyspark.sql.functions import col --- (If col is not define on that we define this is statement) empDF.alias(\"emp1\").join(empDF.alias(\"emp2\"), \\ col(\"emp1.superior_emp_id\") == col(\"emp2.emp_id\"),\"inner\") \\ .select ((\"emp1.emp_id\"), (\"emp1.name\"), \\ col(\"emp2.emp_id\").alias(\"superid\"), \\ col(\"emp2.name\").alias(\"supername\")).show() WTIH COLUMN It menas to define a New colunm and add a any expression like add, multiple etc\\ Table: data = [('James','','Smith','1991-04-01','M',3000), ('Michael','Rose','','2000-05-19','M',4000), ('Robert','','Williams','1978-09-05','M',4000), ('Maria','Anne','Jones','1967-12-01','F',4000), ('Jen','Mary','Brown','1980-02-17','F',-1) ] columns = [\"firstname\",\"middlename\",\"lastname\",\"dob\",\"gender\",\"salary\"] from pyspark.sql import SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('SparkByExamples.com').getOrCreate() df = spark.createDataFrame(data=data, schema = columns) df.show() Syntax : df. withcolunm(\"salary\",df.salary+500).show() HOW TO LOAD THE IN DATA BRICKS Go to Data and click on create table and Upload the data Syntax: loaddf=spark.read.parquet(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.parquet\") DROP THE COLUNM Syntax: userdf=loaddf.drop(\"ip_address,cc\") Page | 18 CONVERT THE PARQUET TO CSV FILE Syntax: csvdf=userdf.write.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) -- it will convert and store a New file usercsvdf=spark.read.csv(\"/FileStore/tables/userdata1.csv\",header=True) --- it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe usercsvdf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"user\") --- it will convert the filename into Table name %sql --- it is important because there is a default is python, so by using a SQl Command to use (if R language %R) select * from user MOUNT FROM DATA LAKE GEN2 TO DATABRICKS ➢ create a data lake gen2 and upload a parquet file ➢ create a Azure Active Dictory and create a New App Registration and also create a new cerfitications and secret ➢ Remember three things to copy id's is cilent.id(Application id), client.secret(value of cerfitications and secret) and client.endpoint(tenant id) ➢ Give a API permission and add the permission is Azure blob storage, Azure data lake ➢ Once go back to data lake gen2 and give Access Control(IAM) and ➢ add Role assignment in that Give role is Storage blob data reader(for to read the data in databaricks) , ➢ add memebers give the App Name (when you create a Azure Activity) ➢ Create New Notebook and remember select the language is Scala for understanding purpose ➢ syntax: ➢ This is all id of datalake gen2 and azure active dictory for to load the data val configs = Map( \"fs.azure.account.auth.type\" -> \"OAuth\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth.provider.type\" -> \"org.apache.hadoop.fs.azurebfs.oauth2.ClientCredsTokenProvider\", \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.id\" -> \"b5e7b223-6ccb-4aab-86b2-fd9773f11fe1\", --(Application id) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.secret\" -> \"_KI8Q~FStvRvFTPguBS~wwAVthPsxQ4zy4sxndnX\", - --(value of certificate and secret) \"fs.azure.account.oauth2.client.endpoint\" -> \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/dee06880-2584- 4c56-bf98-b4023454c861/oauth2/token\"); --- (tenant id) dbutils.fs.mount( -------------This is For Mount Purpose source = \"abfss://employee@storimgdatalakr.dfs.core.windows.net/\", mountPoint = \"/mnt/storingthedata\", extraConfigs = configs) Page | 19 %python df=spark.read.parquet(\"/mnt/storingthedata\") ---it will read the data and remember always give new dataframe %python display(df) -- it will the display dbutils.fs.unmount(\"/mnt/eclasessbatch112\") ----- it will unmount the data Delta Lake Delta lake it is like a Data Lake but in this Any changes in existing file it will store the data . ➢ schema enforcements ➢ Time travel /versions ➢ update ➢ delete ➢ merge create a Dataframe Syntax: df=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000),(200,'mani',2000),(300,'siva',3000)],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sa l\"]) df.show() df.write.format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") --- coverting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]).show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('parquet').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storing/newfile\") -- it is to overwrite the data ---But it will not happend in Delta---------- df.write.format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") --- converting the parquet file create another dataframe syntax: df1=spark.createDataFrame([(100,'krish',1000,'guntur'),(200,'mani',2000,'vijaya'),(300,'siva',3000,'ong ole')],[\"eno\",\"ename\",\"sal\",\"place\"]) df1.show() df1.write.mode('overwrite').format('delta').save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\") – (it will get error so change the syntax) Page | 20 df1.write.mode(\"append\").format('delta').option(\"mergeschema\",True).save(\"/Filestore/tables/storingd elta/filedelta\") --- it will the update the table spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",timestampAsof=\"2022-06-21","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"MERGERS: It is a Comparing the table and dimension table if dimension table is not existiong the vale it","type":"concept","a":"will update , insert delete. create table customer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), price int) insert into customer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600),(4,'oiu',500) select * from customer create table dimcustomer ( cno int, cname varchar(10), Page | 46 price int) insert into dimcustomer values (1,'abs',200),(2,'tre',400),(3,'cbe',600) update customer set price=800 where price=400 delete customer where price=900 merge dimcustomer as target using customer as source on source.cno=target.cno when not matched by target then --- it will be insert insert(cno,cname,price) values(source.cno,source.cname,source.cno) ii ti will be update when matched and target.cno=2 then update set target.cname=source.cname, target.price=source.price when not matched by source then ---- it will delete . delete;","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"05 AM — it missed the window. This is late arriving data.","type":"concept","a":"• Strategy 1: Watermark with a Grace/Tolerance Period Add a buffer period to your WHERE clause to catch late data\n• Example — Pipeline picks up last 2 hours as buffer: -- Standard incremental load SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= '2024-01-15 00:00:00' AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' -- With 2-hour grace period to catch late arrivals SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at >= DATEADD(HOUR, -2, '2024-01-15 00:00:00') AND updated_at < '2024-01-16 00:00:00' Strategy 2: Upsert (Merge) Instead of Insert Only Instead of just inserting new rows, use MERGE so late data updates existing records without creating duplicates. -- Using MERGE in Azure Synapse / SQL MERGE INTO target_sales AS T USING source_sales AS S ON T.order_id = S.order_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET T.status = S.status, T.amount = S.amount WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, status, amount, created_at) VALUES (S.order_id, S.status, S.amount, S.created_at);\n• Strategy 3: Delta Lake (Databricks) — Time Travel Delta Lake keeps history of all changes\n• If late data arrives, you can reprocess a specific date partition. # Read Delta table as of a specific time (Time Travel) df = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-15') \\ .load('/mnt/sales/delta') # Upsert late data into Delta table Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 4 from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/sales/delta') deltaTable.alias('T').merge( late_df.alias('S'), 'T.order_id = S.order_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute() Strategy 4: Partition Reprocessing If late data arrives for an old date, re-run the pipeline only for that partition rather than the whole table. • Use ADF pipeline parameters to pass a specific date/partition • Overwrite only that partition in the target (e.g., ADLS partition: year=2024/month=01/day=15) • This avoids full reload and is much faster 💡 Tip: In an interview say: 'We handled late arriving data using a combination of a grace period watermark and MERGE/Upsert in Synapse\n• For Delta Lake, we used merge operations and partition reprocessing when needed.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"How to handle incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• I implement incremental load using watermark columns like ModifiedDate or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table, and in each run I extract only new or updated records\n• Then I load data into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• A(cid:332)er successful load, I update the watermark to ensure no data duplica(cid:415)on","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If unchanged:","type":"concept","a":"o Do nothing Use Delta MERGE with update and insert logic.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A schema defines the structure of your data: column names, data types, and nullability\n• When new data arrives, it may or may not match the existing table schema\n• Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 4 Schema Enforcement (Default Behavior in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake validates all incoming data against the current table schema\n• If new data has extra columns or mismatched types — the write FAILS with a clear error\n• This is the DEFAULT — protects data quality in production. # Existing Delta table schema: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE # New data has an extra column 'discount' new_df = spark.createDataFrame( [(1001, 501, 99.99, 0.10)], ['order_id', 'customer_id', 'amount', 'discount'] ) # Schema Enforcement in action — this FAILS new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # AnalysisException: A schema mismatch detected when writing. # Column 'discount' is not defined in the table\n• When to enforce schema: • Production financial or compliance tables • When schema changes must go through a change control review • When you want the pipeline to fail loudly rather than silently corrupt data Schema Evolution (Opt-in in Delta Lake) What it does: Delta Lake auto-updates the table schema when mergeSchema=true\n• New columns are added; existing columns are untouched\n• Old rows will show NULL for newly added columns. # Enable Schema Evolution — write succeeds, new column is added new_df.write.format('delta') \\ .mode('append') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/orders') # Table schema is now: # order_id INT, customer_id INT, amount DOUBLE, discount DOUBLE # # Old rows: discount = NULL # New rows: discount = 0.10 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 5 # Enable globally at Spark session level: spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled','true') Comparison Table Aspect Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default? YES — Delta default No — opt-in with mergeSchema=true Extra column Write FAILS with error Column added automatically to arrives table Wrong data type Write FAILS Write FAILS (type mismatch not auto-evolved) Old rows for new N/A NULL value column Best for Prod, financial, compliance data Rapidly changing source systems Interview Tip: Say: 'Delta enforces schema by default to protect data quality\n• For sources that evolve their schema (add new columns), we enable mergeSchema=true\n• Data type changes always need a manual ALTER TABLE — Delta never auto-changes types.' Q5 Function to Insert Newly Arriving Data — MERGE / Upsert The Answer: Use MERGE (Upsert) When new data arrives, you typically need to: INSERT rows that do not exist yet, and UPDATE rows that already exist\n• The function for this is MERGE — also called Upsert (Update + Insert)\n• Delta Lake MERGE — Python API from delta.tables import DeltaTable # Existing Delta table (destination) delta_table = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/customers') # Incoming new data (source) new_data = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/raw/customers_today/') # MERGE: update matching rows, insert new rows Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 6 delta_table.alias('target').merge( new_data.alias('source'), 'target.customer_id = source.customer_id' # match key ) \\ .whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ # update all columns if key found .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ # insert full row if key not found .execute() print('MERGE complete — no duplicates, no data loss.') MERGE with SQL Syntax (Databricks SQL) MERGE INTO customers AS target USING new_customers AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND source.updated_at > target.updated_at THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = source.updated_at WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, source.created_at, source.updated_at) -- Optional: remove records deleted at source WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE Real-World Example: Daily Customer Incremental Load # Step 1: Read only changed records (incremental extract) new_customers = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM source_db.customers WHERE updated_at >= current_date() - INTERVAL 1 DAY ''') # Step 2: Merge into Delta dimension table delta_cust = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/dim_customers') delta_cust.alias('t').merge( new_customers.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdate(set={ 'name': 's.name', Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 7 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' )).whenNotMatchedInsert(values={ 'customer_id': 's.customer_id', 'name': 's.name', 'email': 's.email', 'city': 's.city', 'created_at': 's.created_at', 'updated_at': 's.updated_at' }).execute() print('Incremental load complete.') Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'To insert newly arriving data into an existing Delta table, we use MERGE (upsert)","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table?","type":"concept","a":"• Simple Definition A Delta Table is a table format built on Parquet files with an added transaction log (_delta_log)\n• It gives you ACID transactions, time travel, schema enforcement, and MERGE support\n• It is the standard table type in Databricks (open-source Delta Lake project)\n• Core Features Feature What It Means Example ACID Transactions All-or-nothing writes — no Pipeline fails mid-write? Table stays partial data clean Time Travel Query older versions of data SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 MERGE / Upsert Insert + Update in one atomic Daily incremental customer load step Schema Rejects wrong schema by Extra column fails the write Enforcement default Schema Evolution Auto-adds new columns (opt- mergeSchema=true in) Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 8 OPTIMIZE Compacts small files Speeds up future reads automatically Z-Ordering Collocates related data in ZORDER BY date for fast range same files queries Streaming Support Works with Structured Real-time events land into Delta Streaming table How Delta Table Storage Works # Delta table = Parquet files + _delta_log folder # Folder structure on ADLS / S3: '/mnt/delta/sales/' ' _delta_log/' ' 00000000000000000000.json -- table created' ' 00000000000000000001.json -- first batch loaded' ' 00000000000000000002.json -- merge operation ran' ' 00000000000000000003.json -- OPTIMIZE ran' ' part-00000-abc123.snappy.parquet' ' part-00001-def456.snappy.parquet' ' ...' # Every write is an entry in _delta_log # This is what enables Time Travel and ACID Time Travel Examples # Read table as of 7 days ago (useful for debugging) df_old = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('timestampAsOf', '2024-01-08') \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # Read a specific version df_v5 = spark.read.format('delta') \\ .option('versionAsOf', 5) \\ .load('/mnt/delta/sales') # View full history from delta.tables import DeltaTable dt = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/mnt/delta/sales') dt.history(10).show(truncate=False) # SQL versions # SELECT * FROM sales TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-08' # SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 5 Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 9 Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'A Delta Table is Parquet + a transaction log\n• The transaction log is what gives us ACID guarantees, time travel to query historical versions, and the ability to do MERGE operations\n• It is the foundation of the Lakehouse architecture in Databricks — data warehouse reliability with data lake scalability.' Q7 Pipeline Running Slowly — Causes and Fixes Step 1: Where to Look First • Databricks Spark UI: Job Run page -> Stages tab -> look for the slowest stage • ADF Monitor: Activity Runs -> check which activity consumes the most time • Compare to previous runs — if it was faster before, something changed Cause 1: Data Skew One partition has far more data than others\n• One task takes 30 minutes while all others finish in 2 minutes. # Symptom: Spark UI shows one task dramatically slower than others # Diagnose: check partition sizes from pyspark.sql.functions import spark_partition_id df.groupBy(spark_partition_id()).count().orderBy('count(1)',ascending=False).show() # Fix 1: Enable Adaptive Query Execution (Databricks default ON) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled', 'true') # Fix 2: Salt the skewed join key from pyspark.sql.functions import concat, lit, rand SALT = 10 df_salted = df.withColumn('salted_key', concat(col('customer_id'), lit('_'), (rand()*SALT).cast('int'))) Cause 2: Small File Problem Thousands of tiny Parquet files = thousands of file-open operations\n• Reads become very slow. # Diagnose: list files in Delta table folder files = dbutils.fs.ls('/mnt/delta/sales/') print(f'File count: {len(files)}') # > 1000 = problem # Fix: Run OPTIMIZE to compact small files into larger ones spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales`') Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 10 # Fix with Z-Ordering: collocate data by the most-queried column spark.sql('OPTIMIZE delta.`/mnt/delta/sales` ZORDER BY (sale_date)') # Fix: Write fewer files by coalescing before write df.coalesce(20).write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/mnt/delta/sales') Cause 3: Missing Broadcast Join for Small Tables Default joins shuffle BOTH sides\n• If one table is small, broadcast it to avoid shuffle entirely. from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast # Slow: both DataFrames shuffled df_result = df_large_sales.join(df_dim_region, 'region_id') # Fast: small table sent to all nodes — zero shuffle df_result = df_large_sales.join(broadcast(df_dim_region), 'region_id') # Raise auto-broadcast threshold from 10MB to 50MB spark.conf.set('spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold', str(50*1024*1024)) Cause 4: Full Table Scan (No Partition Pruning) Reading the entire table when only a small date range is needed wastes time. # Slow: reads ALL partitions then filters in memory df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/') df_filtered = df.filter(col('sale_date') == '2024-01-15') # Fast: only reads the matching partition folder df = spark.read.parquet('/mnt/data/sales/year=2024/month=01/day=15/') # For Delta tables — use predicate pushdown in SQL queries df = spark.sql(''' SELECT * FROM sales WHERE sale_date = '2024-01-15' -- Spark prunes to this partition AND region = 'North' ''') Cause 5: Shuffle Partitions Too High or Too Low # Default spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 200 # For 1GB data, 200 partitions = 5MB each — too many small tasks # Enable AQE to auto-tune (Databricks default) spark.conf.set('spark.sql.adaptive.enabled', 'true') # AQE merges small shuffle partitions automatically Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 11 # Manual rule of thumb: aim for ~128MB per partition # 10 GB data / 128 MB = 80 partitions spark.conf.set('spark.sql.shuffle.partitions', '80') Summary Checklist Problem Symptom Fix Data Skew 1 task much slower than AQE skewJoin + salting others Small Files Slow reads, many tiny files OPTIMIZE + ZORDER on Delta Missing broadcast Large shuffle for small broadcast() hint or raise threshold join lookup table Full table scan Reading GB/TB for MB Partition pruning, predicate pushdown result Too many shuffle 200 tiny tasks for small data Enable AQE or lower manually partitions Cluster too small Spill to disk, OOM errors Scale up memory, add more workers Interview Tip: Structure your answer: 'I open the Spark UI first to find which stage is slow and look for signs of skew, shuffle, or spill\n• The most common causes are data skew, small file problem, missing broadcast joins, or full table scans\n• I fix each systematically using AQE, OPTIMIZE, broadcast hints, and partition filters.' Q8 Python: First Occurrence of Values from a List with Duplicates Problem Given a list with duplicates, print or return only the FIRST occurrence of each value in order\n• Input: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 1: Using a Set (Most Common Interview Answer) # Method 1 — Set to track already-seen values # Time: O(n) | Space: O(n) | Order preserved: YES Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 12 def first_occurrences(lst): seen = set() # remembers values we have already added result = [] for item in lst: if item not in seen: result.append(item) seen.add(item) return result # Test data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] print(first_occurrences(data)) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] Method 2: dict.fromkeys() — Python One-Liner # Method 2 — dict.fromkeys removes duplicates and preserves order # Python 3.7+ dicts are ordered by insertion data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = list(dict.fromkeys(data)) print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # How it works: # dict.fromkeys(data) => {3:None, 1:None, 4:None, 5:None, 9:None, 2:None, 6:None} # Keys are inherently unique and insertion-ordered # list() converts back to a plain list Method 3: Print Directly data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] seen = set() for item in data: if item not in seen: print(item, end=' ') seen.add(item) # Output: 3 1 4 5 9 2 6 Method 4: For Strings names = ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Alice', 'Charlie', 'Bob', 'David'] Databricks & Spark Interview Guide | Page 13 seen = set() for name in names: if name not in seen: print(name) seen.add(name) # Output: # Alice # Bob # Charlie # David Method 5: Using pandas / PySpark (Data Engineering) # pandas import pandas as pd data = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5] result = pd.Series(data).drop_duplicates(keep='first').tolist() print(result) # Output: [3, 1, 4, 5, 9, 2, 6] # PySpark — for large distributed datasets # df.dropDuplicates(['column_name']) # drops duplicate rows by column # df.dropDuplicates() # drops fully duplicate rows Complexity Comparison Method Time Space Order? Best For Set tracking (Method 1) O(n) O(n) Yes Most readable, interview default dict.fromkeys (Method O(n) O(n) Yes Shortest code — Python 3.7+ 2) pandas O(n) O(n) Yes DataFrames, data engineering drop_duplicates PySpark O(n) Low No Big data on cluster dropDuplicates dist. (unless sorted) Interview Tip: In interview: Start with Method 1 — explain the set-based logic step by step","source":"Databricks & Spark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"CDC (Change Data Capture) and CDF (Change Data Feed)","type":"concept","a":"• CDC — Change Data Capture CDC is the process of identifying and capturing changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) made to a source database and propagating those changes to a target system — without doing a full reload every time\n• CDC Methods • Log-based CDC — reads the database transaction log (WAL/binlog)\n• Tools: Debezium, Azure SQL CDC, Oracle GoldenGate • Trigger-based CDC — DB triggers write change records to a shadow table • Timestamp-based CDC — query source using last_modified > watermark • Diff-based CDC — compare full snapshots to identify changes (slow, expensive) -- Timestamp-based CDC example (SQL Server / ADB) SELECT * FROM source_table WHERE last_modified_date > '2024-01-01 00:00:00' AND last_modified_date <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP CDF — Change Data Feed (Delta Lake Feature) CDF is a Delta Lake feature that tracks row-level changes (insert, update, delete, update_preimage) in a Delta table\n• It creates a built-in change log that downstream consumers can read incrementally\n• Enable and Use CDF -- Enable CDF on a Delta table ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Or at creation time CREATE TABLE my_table (id INT, name STRING) TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true); -- Read changes from version 5 onwards df = spark.read.format('delta') .option('readChangeFeed', 'true') .option('startingVersion', 5) .table('my_table') -- Columns added by CDF: -- _change_type: insert | update_preimage | update_postimage | delete -- _commit_version: Delta log version -- _commit_timestamp: When the change was committed Feature CDC CDF (Delta) Scope Source database changes Delta table row-level changes Granularity Row-level (I/U/D) Row-level with pre/post image for updates Setup External tool (Debezium) or DB ALTER TABLE + TBLPROPERTIES config Streaming Yes — Kafka/Event Hub Yes — readStream with readChangeFeed Use case Sync source DB to lakehouse Propagate Delta changes downstream Page 2 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Optimize Queries in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"1. Partitioning • Partition tables on low-cardinality filter columns (date, region, status) • Avoid over-partitioning — too many small partitions increases metadata overhead df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save(path)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Z-ORDER / Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• Z-ORDER on high-cardinality columns used in WHERE clauses (customer_id, transaction_id) • Use Liquid Clustering for dynamic, incremental clustering without partition rewrites OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date)","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + VACUUM","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) -- compact + cluster VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- remove stale files","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Parquet Format vs JSON Format","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Feature Parquet JSON Storage layout Columnar — stores each column Row-based — stores each record together together Compression Snappy/GZIP — typically 75-90% Minimal — largely uncompressed smaller text Schema Embedded in file footer (strongly typed) No schema — inferred at read time Query speed 5-20x faster — column pruning + Slow — must read all fields per row predicate pushdown Predicate pushdown Yes — skips row groups outside filter Not supported range Splittable Yes — parallel reads Only with JSON Lines format Null handling Efficient native encoding String 'null' or missing key Nested types Supported natively (struct, array, map) Natural — JSON is inherently nested Human readable No — binary format Yes — text based Best for Analytics, large-scale processing, Delta APIs, config files, event messages Write speed Slower (encoding overhead) Faster (plain text write) Rule of thumb: Always convert landing-zone JSON/CSV files to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Parquet gives columnar compression and predicate pushdown\n• Delta adds ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement on top","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Optimization Techniques in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE + ZORDER — compact small Delta files and cluster data for file skipping • Liquid Clustering — dynamic incremental clustering; replaces partition + ZORDER • Photon Engine — vectorized C++ query engine; enable in cluster config for 3-10x speedup • Delta Caching — SSD-level caching of remote Parquet data on worker nodes • Auto Optimize — enable optimizeWrite and autoCompact for automatic file sizing • AQE — Adaptive Query Execution for runtime plan re-optimization • VACUUM — remove stale/orphan files to reduce storage scan overhead • Partition pruning — query only relevant partitions using WHERE on partition columns • Data skipping — Delta min/max stats skip irrelevant files automatically • Materialized Views — pre-compute expensive aggregations for fast dashboard reads -- Databricks-specific optimizations OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, txn_date); VACUUM transactions RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Auto Optimize properties ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' ); -- Liquid Clustering ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date);","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evaluation and Enforcement","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Enforcement (Schema Validation) Schema enforcement in Delta Lake rejects any writes where the incoming data does not match the existing table schema\n• It protects data quality by preventing corrupt or mismatched data from entering the table. • Throws an AnalysisException if new columns are added or data types mismatch • Enabled by default in Delta Lake Schema Evolution (mergeSchema) Schema evolution allows the table schema to be updated automatically when new columns are added to incoming data. # Allow new columns to be added automatically df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/mnt/delta/table/') Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Feature Schema Enforcement Schema Evolution Default behaviour ON (strict) OFF (must opt-in) New columns in source Write fails Columns added automatically Data type mismatch Write fails Still fails (type safety) Use case Protect prod tables from bad Accommodate evolving source data schemas","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Tables Available in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Table Type Description Managed Table Both metadata AND data are managed by Databricks/Unity Catalog\n• Dropping the table deletes all data\n• External Table Only metadata is managed\n• Data resides at a specified external path\n• Dropping the table does NOT delete data\n• Delta Table Table backed by Delta Lake format\n• Supports ACID, time travel, and schema enforcement\n• Hive Metastore Table Legacy table registered in the Hive Metastore (available before Unity Catalog)\n• View A virtual table based on a query\n• No data stored; computed on-the-fly\n• Temporary View Session-scoped view that disappears when the session ends\n• Global Temporary View Cluster-scoped temp view; accessible across sessions but dropped when cluster restarts","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Table? Advantages of Using Delta Table","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Table is a table stored in Delta Lake format — an open-source storage layer built on top of Parquet files with a transaction log (_delta_log) that brings ACID guarantees and advanced features to data lake storage\n• How Delta Table Works Internally • Data is stored as Parquet files in cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) • A transaction log directory (_delta_log) records every change as a JSON/Parquet checkpoint • Each write creates a new versioned set of Parquet files — old files are retained for time travel • Compaction (OPTIMIZE) merges small files into larger ones for query performance Advantages of Delta Table Feature Description ACID Transactions Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability — concurrent reads/writes are safe Time Travel Query any previous version: SELECT * FROM table VERSION AS OF 5 or TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' Schema Enforcement Rejects writes that don't match the table schema — prevents corrupt data Schema Evolution New columns can be added via mergeSchema without rewriting the table Upserts (MERGE) Efficient insert/update/delete using MERGE INTO — enables SCD and CDC patterns Unified Batch & Streaming Same Delta table can be written by a batch job and read as a stream simultaneously Scalable Metadata Z-ordering and data skipping speed up queries on large datasets OPTIMIZE & VACUUM OPTIMIZE compacts small files;\n• VACUUM removes old files saving storage costs Open Format Based on Parquet — readable by Spark, Hive, Presto, Trino, and other engines Useful Delta Table Commands -- Time travel SELECT * FROM employees VERSION AS OF 3;\n• SELECT * FROM employees TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-06-01'; -- Restore to previous version RESTORE TABLE employees TO VERSION AS OF 5;\n• Page 6 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 -- Compact small files OPTIMIZE employees ZORDER BY (department_id); -- Remove files older than 7 days VACUUM employees RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- View history DESCRIBE HISTORY employees;\n• Page 7 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"How to Build a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline","type":"concept","a":"• A fault-tolerant ETL pipeline continues to operate correctly even when individual components fail, ensuring no data loss, no duplicate processing, and automatic recovery — without manual intervention\n• Key Principles of Fault Tolerance Principle Implementation Idempotency Re-running the pipeline produces the same result — no duplicates, no data loss Exactly-Once Processing Each record processed exactly once using checkpoints and unique keys Checkpointing Stream state saved to durable storage so recovery resumes from last checkpoint Retry Logic Automatic retries on transient failures (network, API timeout, cluster preemption) Dead Letter Queue Bad records quarantined separately for later inspection instead of failing the job Monitoring & Alerting Proactive alerts on failures, SLA breaches, and data quality violations Schema Validation Reject or quarantine records that violate schema before writing to target ACID Writes Use Delta Lake MERGE for upserts — safe for partial failures and re-runs Architecture of a Fault-Tolerant ETL Pipeline • Layer 1 – Ingest: Autoloader with checkpoints ensures no file is missed or processed twice • Layer 2 – Bronze: Raw Delta table written with ACID guarantees; idempotent via COPY INTO • Layer 3 – Silver: Cleansing + MERGE INTO Delta for upserts; bad records to quarantine table • Layer 4 – Gold: Aggregation; only runs after Silver passes data quality Expectations • Layer 5 – Orchestration: Databricks Workflows with retry policies and failure alerts Full PySpark Fault-Tolerant Pipeline Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F from delta.tables import DeltaTable import logging # ── CONFIG ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── SOURCE_PATH = '/mnt/landing/employees/' SCHEMA_PATH = '/mnt/schema/employees/' CHECKPOINT_PATH = '/mnt/checkpoint/employees/' BRONZE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/bronze/employees/' SILVER_PATH = '/mnt/delta/silver/employees/' QUARANTINE_PATH = '/mnt/delta/quarantine/employees/' Page 17 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # ── STEP 1: INGEST with Autoloader (Fault-Tolerant Streaming) ─── df_raw = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', SCHEMA_PATH) .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true') .load(SOURCE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 2: WRITE TO BRONZE (Idempotent via checkpoint) ───────── (df_raw.writeStream .format('delta') .outputMode('append') .option('checkpointLocation', CHECKPOINT_PATH + 'bronze/') .trigger(availableNow=True) # process all pending files once .start(BRONZE_PATH) .awaitTermination() ) # ── STEP 3: CLEANSE + QUARANTINE BAD RECORDS ──────────────────── df_bronze = spark.read.format('delta').load(BRONZE_PATH) df_valid = df_bronze.filter( F.col('emp_id').isNotNull() & F.col('salary').cast('integer').isNotNull() & (F.col('salary').cast('integer') > 0) ) df_invalid = df_bronze.subtract(df_valid) # Write bad records to quarantine for investigation (df_invalid .withColumn('quarantine_reason', F.lit('Failed validation: null emp_id or invalid salary')) .withColumn('quarantine_ts', F.current_timestamp()) .write.format('delta').mode('append').save(QUARANTINE_PATH) ) # ── STEP 4: UPSERT TO SILVER (Idempotent MERGE) ───────────────── df_clean = (df_valid .dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) .withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) .withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) .withColumn('updated_at', F.current_timestamp()) ) if DeltaTable.isDeltaTable(spark, SILVER_PATH): silver = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, SILVER_PATH) (silver.alias('tgt') .merge(df_clean.alias('src'), 'tgt.emp_id = src.emp_id') .whenMatchedUpdateAll() .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() .execute() Page 18 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 ) else: df_clean.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save(SILVER_PATH) # ── STEP 5: GOLD AGGREGATION ──────────────────────────────────── df_silver = spark.read.format('delta').load(SILVER_PATH) df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('department').agg( F.count('emp_id').alias('headcount'), F.avg('salary').alias('avg_salary'), F.max('salary').alias('max_salary') ) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/mnt/delta/gold/dept_summary /') print('Pipeline completed successfully.') Retry & Alerting in Databricks Workflows Feature How to Configure Job Retries Set Max Retries = 3 and Retry Interval on each task in Databricks Workflows UI Depends On Task B runs only if Task A succeeds — dependency chain stops cascade failures On Failure Alerting Configure email/Slack/Teams alerts in Workflows notification settings Timeout Set per-task timeout to detect hung jobs and trigger retry/failure handling Run If Condition Conditional task execution based on upstream task outcome (Success/Failed/All) DLT Expectations @dlt.expect_or_quarantine() routes bad records without failing the pipeline Summary Checklist for Fault-Tolerant ETL • Use Autoloader with checkpointing for idempotent file ingestion • Write all layers as Delta tables for ACID guarantees • Use MERGE INTO for upserts — safe to re-run without duplicates • Separate valid vs invalid records — quarantine bad data instead of failing • Validate schema and data quality at Silver layer before Gold • Configure retry policies and timeout in Databricks Workflows • Set up monitoring alerts on job failure, SLA breach, and quarantine record spike • Log pipeline metrics (record counts, error counts, durations) to a monitoring table • Test failure scenarios: missing file, schema change, duplicate keys, corrupt data Page 19","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"While migrating the data, did you just copy the data or did you use incremental","type":"concept","a":"• load? We used a combination of both approaches: Full Load (Initial Load): • For the first migration, we did a full historical load — extracting all records from the source and loading them into the Bronze layer as-is. • This was a one-time bulk load to establish the baseline dataset\n• Incremental Load (Ongoing Sync): • After the initial load, we switched to incremental loads to capture only new or changed records. • We used watermark columns (e.g., updated_at, created_date) or Change Data Capture (CDC) to identify changed rows. • In Databricks, we used Auto Loader for file-based incremental ingestion and MERGE INTO (Delta Lake) for upsert operations\n• This approach minimized load time, reduced compute costs, and kept the target data fresh without full re- processing\n• Section 2: Architecture Concepts","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"metadata stored?","type":"concept","a":"• Storage Format: • By default, tables created in Databricks are stored in Delta Lake format (.parquet files + _delta_log transaction log). • Delta Lake is an open-source storage layer that brings ACID transactions to data lakes. • Physically, data is stored as columnar Parquet files on cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS)\n• Metadata Storage: • Delta Transaction Log (_delta_log): JSON files stored alongside data files in the table directory\n• Records every operation (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE) — used for time travel and ACID guarantees. • Hive Metastore / Unity Catalog: Table-level metadata (table name, schema, location, partitioning) is stored in the Databricks Metastore (by default, a managed Hive Metastore or Unity Catalog in newer workspaces)\n• So metadata lives in two places: the _delta_log for data-level history and the Metastore for table-level schema/location information","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What is VACUUM and when do we use it?","type":"concept","a":"• VACUUM is a Delta Lake command that removes old data files that are no longer referenced by the Delta transaction log\n• Why Vacuum is Needed: • Delta Lake's ACID support and time travel retain old file versions, which accumulate over time and consume storage. • VACUUM cleans up these obsolete files to reclaim storage space\n• Syntax: VACUUM table_name RETAIN 168 HOURS; -- Default: 7 days -- Or in PySpark: from delta.tables import DeltaTable deltaTable = DeltaTable.forName(spark, 'table_name') deltaTable.vacuum(168) # hours Important Notes: • Default retention period is 7 days (168 hours)\n• Setting it lower than 7 days requires disabling a safety check. • After VACUUM, you cannot time travel to versions older than the retention period. • Use it periodically in production to control storage costs\n• Section 4: Performance Optimization","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Is MERGE SCD-1 or SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• MERGE is primarily SCD-1 by default behavior. • SCD-1 (Overwrite): MERGE with WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE overwrites existing records — no history is kept. • SCD-2 (History): MERGE can implement SCD-2 but requires a more complex query — on match, you UPDATE the old row (close it) and INSERT a new row with the changed values\n• In Delta Lake, you can implement SCD-2 using MERGE with both WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE and WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT clauses, combined with is_current and date columns\n• Section 7: PySpark Coding","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"What will be SCD2 logic and columns you will be using for your project. How will you","type":"concept","a":"implement? SCD Type 2 Logic + Columns Columns used:  business_key (emp_id)  start_date  end_date  is_ac(cid:415)ve  hash_diff (for change detec(cid:415)on) Logic:  If record changes → expire old record (end_date + is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  Insert new record with updated values  Maintain history Implementa(cid:415)on:  Delta MERGE INTO OR DLT apply_changes","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is Change Data Feed?","type":"concept","a":"• Change Data Feed (CDF)  Tracks row-level changes in Delta tables  Captures INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE Used for:  incremental pipelines  CDC processing 7\n• Auto Loader  Incremental file inges(cid:415)on in Databricks  Automa(cid:415)cally detects new files  Handles schema evolu(cid:415)on Best for streaming inges(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you restore the table if data is corrupted?","type":"concept","a":"Restore corrupted table Using Delta Time Travel: RESTORE TABLE table_name TO VERSION AS OF 5;","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is the use of SQL Warehouse in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Warehouse (previously called SQL Analytics Endpoint) is a serverless/dedicated compute resource optimized for running SQL queries on data stored in the Lakehouse (Delta Lake). • Enables BI tools (Tableau, Power BI) to connect via JDBC/ODBC. • Supports auto-stop and auto-scaling to control costs. • Serverless option eliminates cluster management; compute spins up in seconds. • Photon engine (vectorized query processing) dramatically speeds up SQL workloads\n• Change Data Feed (CDF)","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is CDF (Change Data Feed) in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• CDF is a Delta Lake feature that records row-level changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to a Delta table, producing a changelog that can be consumed by downstream processes. • Enable with: ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true) • Read changes: spark.readStream.format('delta').option('readChangeFeed','true').table('my_table') • Use cases: CDC pipelines, incremental ETL, audit trails, real-time aggregations. • Change types exposed: insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete\n• Efficient Model for Daily Streaming Data","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"We are getting streaming data daily — how do you design an efficient model for it?","type":"concept","a":"• Recommended architecture: Medallion (Bronze → Silver → Gold) with Delta Lake + Structured Streaming. • Bronze layer: Ingest raw streaming data with Auto Loader (cloudFiles) — schema evolution, exactly-once, checkpointing. • Silver layer: Parse, clean, deduplicate (use MERGE INTO for upserts via CDF or watermarking). • Gold layer: Aggregated, business-ready tables updated incrementally. • Use trigger(availableNow=True) for micro-batch on schedule, or trigger(processingTime='X seconds') for near-real-time. • Partition Delta tables by date (year/month/day) to enable partition pruning on queries. • Enable optimize & ZORDER on high-cardinality filter columns. • Monitor with Spark UI, Delta transaction log, and Databricks cluster metrics\n• Resolving Data Skew","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write the code for SCD2","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 Code (PySpark / Databricks) (cid:44934) Using Delta Lake MERGE INTO (most asked in interviews) MERGE INTO target_table t USING source_table s ON t.emp_id = s.emp_id AND t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = true WHEN MATCHED AND ( t.salary <> s.salary OR t.dept_id <> s.dept_id ) THEN UPDATE SET t.is_ac(cid:415)ve = false, t.end_date = current_date() WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, start_date, end_date, is_ac(cid:415)ve ) VALUES ( s.emp_id, s.name, s.dept_id, s.salary, current_date(), null, true ); (cid:44934) Key idea:  Old record → expired (is_ac(cid:415)ve = false)  New record → inserted with new version  History preserved","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on strategies in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"Common answers:  Cache frequently used DataFrames  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Repar(cid:415)(cid:415)on  Broadcast Join  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE)  Delta Cache  OPTIMIZE  ZORDER  Liquid Clustering  Predicate Pushdown  Column Pruning  Avoid UDFs  Auto Op(cid:415)mize","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between OPTIMIZE, ZORDER and Liquid Clustering","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE ZORDER Liquid Clustering Compacts small files Organizes data by column values Automa(cid:415)cally manages clustering Improves file size Improves data skipping No manual par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning Reduces file count Faster filtering Be(cid:425)er for evolving workloads Doesn't sort Sorts within files Re-clusters automa(cid:415)cally Example: OPTIMIZE 1000 small files ↓ 10 large files ZORDER OPTIMIZE table ZORDER BY(customer_id) Improves filtering: WHERE customer_id=100 Liquid Clustering No par(cid:415)(cid:415)on maintenance\n• Databricks automa(cid:415)cally reclusters data","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Data Lake and Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• Data Lake Delta Lake Storage layer Storage layer + transac(cid:415)on layer No ACID ACID transac(cid:415)ons No versioning Time Travel No schema enforcement Schema enforcement & evolu(cid:415)on Slow updates MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE No transac(cid:415)on log _delta_log maintains metadata Interview Answer \"A Data Lake stores files such as Parquet, CSV, or JSON, whereas Delta Lake builds on top of a Data Lake by adding ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, (cid:415)me travel, and a transac(cid:415)on log\n• Delta Lake makes lake storage more reliable for produc(cid:415)on data engineering.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Managed table deleted a(cid:332)er reten(cid:415)on and all files removed. How do you recreate it?","type":"concept","a":"• If:  Managed table is dropped  Reten(cid:415)on period has passed  Underlying data files are permanently removed  All versions are gone You cannot recover it using Delta Lake features\n• Time Travel and RESTORE depend on the transac(cid:415)on log and data files s(cid:415)ll exis(cid:415)ng\n• Recovery op(cid:415)ons are:  Restore from backup  Reload from the source system  Recover using cloud storage versioning (if enabled)  Use disaster recovery copies Interview Answer \"If both the Delta log and data files have been permanently deleted a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period, the table cannot be recreated from Delta metadata alone\n• Recovery would require restoring from backup, cloud storage versioning, or reloading the data from the source.\"","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE and Z-ORDER in Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE — File Compaction Delta tables accumulate many small files over time\n• OPTIMIZE compacts them into larger Parquet files for faster reads\n• OPTIMIZE my_table OPTIMIZE my_table WHERE date = '2024-01-01' -- with partition filter Z-ORDER — Co-locate Related Data Z-ORDER clusters related data within the same files\n• Queries on Z-ordered columns can skip irrelevant files automatically (data skipping)\n• OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id) OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_date) Feature OPTIMIZE Z-ORDER Purpose Compact small files Cluster data by column values Benefit Faster scans, less overhead Data skipping on filter queries Best For After many small writes/streaming High-cardinality filter columns Used Together Yes — in a single OPTIMIZE ZORDER BY command Page 3 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide Best Z-ORDER columns: those appearing frequently in WHERE clauses with high cardinality (customer_id, event_timestamp)\n• Avoid low-cardinality columns like boolean flags","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize Delta Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning Partition on low-cardinality columns used in filters\n• Avoid over-partitioning. df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year', 'month').save(path)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE + ZORDER","type":"concept","a":"OPTIMIZE my_table ZORDER BY (customer_id)","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"VACUUM — Remove Stale Files","type":"concept","a":"VACUUM my_table RETAIN 168 HOURS -- 7 days default","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Optimize (Databricks)","type":"concept","a":"ALTER TABLE my_table SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite' = 'true', 'delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact' = 'true' )","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Data Lake vs Delta Lake","type":"concept","a":"Feature Data Lake (Plain) Delta Lake ACID Transactions No Yes — full ACID support Schema Enforcement No Yes — schema-on-write Time Travel No Yes — VERSION AS OF Upserts / Deletes Complex or not supported Yes — MERGE INTO Streaming + Batch Separate pipelines needed Unified — same table File Format Parquet / CSV / JSON Parquet + _delta_log Data Quality Manual checks required Built-in constraints Key Delta Lake Commands -- Time Travel SELECT * FROM my_table VERSION AS OF 5 SELECT * FROM my_table TIMESTAMP AS OF '2024-01-01' -- MERGE (Upsert) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Live Table?","type":"concept","a":"• A Delta Live Table (DLT) is a managed ETL framework in Databricks used to build reliable data pipelines\n• Instead of manually crea(cid:415)ng jobs, DLT:  Reads data  Applies transforma(cid:415)ons  Loads data  Handles dependencies  Monitors pipeline health  Enforces data quality rules Example: CREATE OR REFRESH LIVE TABLE sales_clean AS SELECT * FROM LIVE.sales_raw WHERE amount > 0;\n• Data Quality in Delta Live Tables One of the biggest advantages is built-in expecta(cid:415)ons\n• Example: CONSTRAINT valid_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW;\n• This automa(cid:415)cally removes invalid records from the pipeline\n• Interview Answer If the interviewer asks: \"What is the difference between Delta Table and Delta Live Table?\" \"A Delta Table is a storage layer based on Delta Lake that provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and (cid:415)me travel\n• A Delta Live Table is a managed ETL framework that builds and orchestrates data pipelines using Delta Tables\n• DLT adds features like automa(cid:415)c dependency management, scheduling, monitoring, lineage, and built-in data quality expecta(cid:415)ons, whereas with Delta Tables these aspects are managed manually.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"MERGE with UPDATE gives a duplicate rows error. What is the issue?","type":"concept","a":"• \"This usually means that the MERGE condi(cid:415)on is matching mul(cid:415)ple source rows to the same target row\n• Common causes are:  Duplicate business keys in the source.  Duplicate records in the target.  An incorrect ON condi(cid:415)on that is not unique\n• The solu(cid:415)on is to remove duplicates using ROW_NUMBER() or ensure the join key is unique before execu(cid:415)ng the MERGE.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If a Databricks job fails a(cid:332)er 5 minutes, what happens in Parquet and Delta?","type":"concept","a":"• Parquet \"Parquet files do not support transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails while wri(cid:415)ng, par(cid:415)ally wri(cid:425)en files may remain in the storage loca(cid:415)on, requiring manual cleanup.\" Delta \"Delta Lake provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons\n• If the job fails before the transac(cid:415)on commits, the Delta transac(cid:415)on log is not updated, and readers con(cid:415)nue to see the previous consistent version\n• This prevents par(cid:415)al or corrupted data from being visible.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If ADLS storage is full, how will you handle it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"First, I would verify whether the issue is storage capacity, account limits, or quotas\n• Then I would:  Remove temporary or intermediate files.  Run VACUUM on Delta tables to delete obsolete files a(cid:332)er the reten(cid:415)on period.  Archive historical data to a lower-cost storage (cid:415)er.  Delete unused datasets a(cid:332)er confirming reten(cid:415)on policies.  Review lifecycle management rules to automa(cid:415)cally move or delete old data.  If business growth requires it, increase storage capacity or distribute data across addi(cid:415)onal storage accounts.\" Round 3 : (Client Round)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Files Do You Usually Work With?","type":"concept","a":"• CSV — legacy systems, flat files, exports from operational systems • JSON — API responses, event streams, semi-structured data • Parquet — preferred columnar format for analytics; compressed, schema-embedded • Delta — Parquet + transaction log; used for all curated/processed layers • Avro — schema-embedded binary format; common in Kafka/streaming pipelines • ORC — columnar format used in Hive-based ecosystems • XML — legacy enterprise and EDI data sources Best Practice: Always convert raw CSV/JSON to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer. Parquet provides columnar compression and predicate pushdown. Delta adds ACID transactions and time travel. Section 2 | Spark Joins & Optimization","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"In a Transaction Table, Which Column Would You Use for Z-Ordering?","type":"concept","a":"• Page 3 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Choose columns that are: (a) frequently used in WHERE clauses, and (b) have high cardinality. • transaction_date / event_timestamp — most queries filter by date range • customer_id — if queries frequently look up a specific customer • store_id / region_id — if queries filter by location Best Answer: \"I would Z-ORDER on transaction_date combined with customer_id\n• Most business queries filter by a date range AND a specific customer, so co-locating transactions for the same customer within the same date files maximizes file skipping.\" Avoid Z-ORDERing on low-cardinality columns like status (Active/Inactive) or boolean flags — data skipping will be minimal since files will contain both values anyway","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Default Column for Z-Order Indexing?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Lake has NO default Z-ORDER column\n• Z-ORDER must always be explicitly specified: OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date) OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (transaction_date, customer_id) However, Delta Lake automatically collects min/max statistics for the first 32 columns\n• These enable automatic data skipping (file-level pruning) even without explicit Z-ORDER\n• You can control this: ALTER TABLE transactions SET TBLPROPERTIES ( 'delta.dataSkippingNumIndexedCols' = '5' -- collect stats for first 5 columns only )","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Z-Order Indexes","type":"concept","a":"• Z-Ordering (Z-curve/space-filling curve) is a multi-dimensional data clustering technique that maps multi- column data to a single linear order while preserving proximity — values close in N-dimensional space end up in the same files\n• How it works step-by-step • OPTIMIZE interleaves the bit representations of selected column values using a Z-curve algorithm • Related rows (same customer, same date) are co-located into the same Parquet files • Delta records min/max statistics per file for each column • When you query with WHERE clause on Z-ORDERed columns, Spark compares query predicates against file stats • Files whose min/max range does not include the query value are skipped entirely (data skipping) Aspect Partitioning Z-ORDER Best Together Cardinality Low (date, region) High (customer_id) Both Structure Physical folders per Co-locates within files Partition + ZORDER value Over-partition risk Yes — too many small No Partition on date files Changes needed Hard to change after Re-run OPTIMIZE Combine both creation Command partitionBy(col) OPTIMIZE \n• ZORDER Same table, both applied BY -- Best practice: partition by date + Z-order by high-cardinality column Page 4 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) df.write.format('delta').partitionBy('year','month').save('/delta/transactions') OPTIMIZE transactions ZORDER BY (customer_id, transaction_type)","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is Liquid Clustering in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Liquid Clustering (introduced in Databricks Runtime 13.3+) is a dynamic data layout feature that replaces the need for partitioning + Z-ORDER\n• It automatically reorganizes data based on clustering columns without requiring manual partition planning or full table rewrites\n• Key advantages • No upfront partition column planning — clustering columns can be changed anytime with ALTER TABLE • Incremental: only newly written data is clustered — no full table rewrites Page 7 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Avoids partition explosion from high-cardinality columns • Auto-clustering runs in the background (Databricks-managed) -- Create table with Liquid Clustering CREATE TABLE transactions (id BIGINT, customer_id STRING, amount DOUBLE, txn_date DATE) CLUSTER BY (customer_id, txn_date); -- Change clustering columns anytime ALTER TABLE transactions CLUSTER BY (customer_id); -- Manually trigger clustering OPTIMIZE transactions;\n• Feature Partitioning Z-ORDER Liquid Clustering Cardinality Low only High Any Column flexibility Hard to change Re-run OPTIMIZE Change anytime Incremental No No Yes File management Manual VACUUM Manual OPTIMIZE Automatic Partition explosion Yes No No","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What are Materialized Views?","type":"concept","a":"• A Materialized View (MV) is a view whose results are pre-computed and stored as a physical Delta table\n• Unlike standard views that re-run the query on every read, MVs read from the stored result — fast and low-cost at query time. • Results stored physically — reads are as fast as a regular Delta table scan • Auto-refreshed when source tables change (Unity Catalog manages this) • Supports incremental refresh — only processes changed/new data, not full recompute • Ideal for expensive aggregations, multi-table joins, and frequently-queried reports Page 8 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales AS SELECT txn_date, store_id, SUM(amount) AS total_sales, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY txn_date, store_id;\n• REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_sales;\n• Aspect Standard View Materialized View Data stored No Yes — physical Delta table Query speed Depends on source tables Fast — reads pre-computed result Freshness Always up-to-date Refreshed on schedule or source change Cost at query time Full query recomputed Just a Delta table scan Best use case Simple aliasing, simple selects Heavy aggregations, dashboards, reports Section 7 | File Ingestion & Auto Loader","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"CSV Files Contain Corrupted Rows with Extra Columns — How Do You Handle","type":"concept","a":"• Them? Spark provides three read modes for handling malformed records in CSV/JSON: Mode 1 — PERMISSIVE (default, recommended for production) Null-fills mismatched columns and captures the raw corrupt record in a special column. df = (spark.read .option('mode', 'PERMISSIVE') .option('columnNameOfCorruptRecord', '_corrupt_record') .schema(defined_schema) # schema required to use _corrupt_record .csv('/data/raw/*.csv') ) good_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNull()) bad_df = df.filter(col('_corrupt_record').isNotNull()) # Write bad records to quarantine Delta table Page 11 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) bad_df.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/delta/quarantine/csv_errors') Mode 2 — DROPMALFORMED (silent drop) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'DROPMALFORMED').csv('/data/*.csv') # Bad rows silently discarded — use only if data loss is acceptable Mode 3 — FAILFAST (dev/test only) df = spark.read.option('mode', 'FAILFAST').csv('/data/*.csv') # Throws exception on first bad row — useful during development Mode 4 — Delta Live Tables Expectations (modern approach) @dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_row_count', 'col_count = expected') @dlt.expect_or_quarantine('no_null_id', 'id IS NOT NULL') Best Practice: Use PERMISSIVE + _corrupt_record + write bad rows to a quarantine Delta table\n• This keeps all data visible (no silent losses), enables root-cause analysis, and supports reprocessing after the source issue is fixed\n• Section 9 | Architecture & Cluster","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How will you load incremental data if the source has no Last Modified (cid:415)mestamp?","type":"concept","a":"• \"If the source does not have a Last Modified or watermark column, there are several approaches depending on the business scenario:  Use the primary key and compare source and target records.  Perform a Delta MERGE to insert new records and update changed records.  Use Change Data Capture (CDC) if supported by the source system.  Generate a hash of the business columns and compare hashes to detect changes.  As a last resort, perform a full load if no reliable change detec(cid:415)on mechanism exists\n• In my projects, using a business key with hash comparison and a Delta MERGE has been an effec(cid:415)ve approach.\"","source":"T Systems Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Apply Changes in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Yes — using:  Delta Live Tables (DLT) APPLY CHANGES  Or manually using MERGE INTO Used for:  SCD handling (Type 1/2)  CDC inges(cid:415)on","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Pipeline re-run: overwrite or mul(cid:415)ple files? Delta log?","type":"concept","a":"Behavior:  In ADLS: o Usually creates mul(cid:415)ple part files  In Delta Lake: o Uses transac(cid:415)on log (_delta_log) Delta Log:  Stores metadata of all transac(cid:415)ons  Enables: o Time travel o ACID compliance o Rollback Format:  Parquet + _delta_log (JSON logs)","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks components used","type":"concept","a":" Notebooks  Jobs  Clusters  Delta Lake  DBFS  Repos  Workflows","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize Delta tables (OPTIMIZE, ZORDER).","type":"concept","a":"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"Common methods:  Watermark column (LastModifiedDate)  Timestamp  CDC (Change Data Capture)  Delta MERGE Example: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE LastModifiedDate > ( SELECT MAX(LastLoadedDate) FROM AuditTable );","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Data format in your project","type":"concept","a":"Typical formats:  CSV (landing)  JSON (APIs)  Parquet (op(cid:415)mized storage)  Delta (curated layers) Interview (cid:415)p: \"We primarily store curated data in Delta format because it provides ACID transac(cid:415)ons, schema enforcement, and be(cid:425)er performance.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What are Delta Tables?","type":"concept","a":"Delta tables are built on top of Parquet and provide:  ACID transac(cid:415)ons  Time Travel  Schema Enforcement  Schema Evolu(cid:415)on  MERGE, UPDATE, DELETE support  Be(cid:425)er performance with OPTIMIZE and ZORDER","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Write a MERGE statement.","type":"concept","a":"MERGE INTO Target t USING Source s ON t.EmpID = s.EmpID WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.Name = s.Name, t.Salary = s.Salary WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ( EmpID, Name, Salary ) VALUES ( s.EmpID, s.Name, s.Salary );","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Incremental Loading","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental Loading loads only new or changed data since the last load, instead of reloading everything (full load)\n• Common Approaches: • Timestamp-based: Load records where updated_at > last_run_timestamp • Watermark-based: Track max ID or timestamp as high watermark • CDC (Change Data Capture): Capture inserts/updates/deletes from source • Delta Lake MERGE (UPSERT): Merge changes into target table Benefits: Faster, cheaper, reduces source load # PySpark incremental load using watermark last_load_time = '2025-01-01 00:00:00' incremental_df = spark.read.jdbc(...) \\ .filter(col('updated_at') > last_load_time) # Delta MERGE (UPSERT) from delta.tables import DeltaTable target = DeltaTable.forPath(spark, '/delta/customers') target.alias('t').merge( incremental_df.alias('s'), 't.customer_id = s.customer_id' ).whenMatchedUpdateAll() \\ .whenNotMatchedInsertAll() \\ .execute()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"OPTIMIZE vs Z-ORDER","type":"concept","a":"• OPTIMIZE: Compacts small Delta files into larger files to improve read performance and reduce file count\n• OPTIMIZE delta.`/delta/sales` -- or OPTIMIZE sales_table Z-ORDER: Co-locates related data in the same set of files by reordering data based on specified columns\n• Best for high-cardinality columns used in filters\n• OPTIMIZE sales_table ZORDER BY (customer_id, order_date); -- Difference: -- OPTIMIZE alone: compacts files (smaller → bigger) -- ZORDER: compacts AND reorders data within files for faster skipping • OPTIMIZE: Use always for compacting small files • ZORDER: Use on frequently filtered/joined columns • Together: Maximum benefit — compact + co-locate","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"High Concurrency Cluster","type":"concept","a":"• Optimized for multiple concurrent users • Supports Python, SQL, R — NOT Scala • Enforces user isolation (each user's code is isolated) • Best for BI tools and SQL analytics","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Schema Evolution","type":"concept","a":"• Schema Evolution allows changes to a Delta table's schema (adding/modifying columns) without breaking existing pipelines\n• Options: • mergeSchema: Add new columns from incoming data • overwriteSchema: Replace the entire schema (use with caution) # Enable schema evolution on write df.write.format('delta') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .mode('append') \\ .save('/delta/customers') # In SQL - enable auto merge SET spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled = true; # Alter table to add column manually ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN (loyalty_tier STRING);","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Transaction Log & ACID Properties","type":"concept","a":"• Transaction Log (_delta_log): The Delta transaction log is a directory of JSON files that records every change made to a Delta table\n• Each commit produces a new JSON file\n• ACID Properties in Delta: • Atomicity: Each transaction either fully succeeds or fully fails — no partial writes • Consistency: Schema enforcement ensures valid data at all times • Isolation: Concurrent reads/writes are isolated — snapshot isolation by default • Durability: Once committed, data is permanently stored even after failure # View transaction log DESCRIBE HISTORY orders_table; # Each JSON in _delta_log contains: # - add: files added # - remove: files removed/deleted # - metaData: schema changes # - protocol: reader/writer versions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Move Data from Delta Table to Another Location","type":"concept","a":"# Option 1: Write to new Delta location df = spark.read.format('delta').load('/delta/source_table') df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/delta/destination_table') # Option 2: DEEP CLONE (preserves history & metadata) CREATE TABLE destination_table DEEP CLONE source_table LOCATION '/delta/destination_table'; # Option 3: SHALLOW CLONE (references original files) CREATE TABLE destination_table SHALLOW CLONE source_table; # Option 4: Export to CSV/Parquet df.write.format('parquet').save('/output/parquet_export') df.write.format('csv').option('header', 'true').save('/output/csv_export')","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Incremental load","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load is a technique where we load only new or updated data instead of full refresh\n• I implement it using watermark columns like last_updated or CDC depending on source capability\n• I store the last processed value in a control table and extract only delta data in each run\n• Then I load it into staging and use MERGE to upsert into the target\n• Finally, I update the watermark a(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on to ensure no duplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"What are the few small differences you see in databricks now comparing with 3 years","type":"concept","a":"back (only small differences not like unity catalog) “Over the last few years, Databricks has improved mainly in performance, automa(cid:415)on, and governance rather than just core func(cid:415)onality.” (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Key small differences: (cid:48132) a) Performance improvements  Adap(cid:415)ve Query Execu(cid:415)on (AQE) is much be(cid:425)er now  Faster shuffle handling  Be(cid:425)er auto-op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on of joins (cid:48131) b) Photon engine maturity  Much faster SQL execu(cid:415)on compared to earlier versions  Be(cid:425)er vectorized processing (cid:44931) c) Auto op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on features  Auto compac(cid:415)on of small files  Improved OPTIMIZE + ZORDER efficiency (cid:48130) d) Improved job orchestra(cid:415)on  Be(cid:425)er Workflows UI  Retry policies, task dependencies improved (cid:48133) e) Be(cid:425)er Delta Lake stability  More stable MERGE opera(cid:415)ons  Be(cid:425)er schema evolu(cid:415)on handling","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"“Yes, I have used both OPTIMIZE and VACUUM in produc(cid:415)on environments in Delta Lake tables.” (cid:48132) OPTIMIZE  Used to compact small files  Improves read performance OPTIMIZE table_name; (cid:48131) ZORDER (with OPTIMIZE)  Improves query filtering OPTIMIZE table_name ZORDER BY (customer_id); (cid:44931) VACUUM  Removes old/unnecessary files VACUUM table_name; “We ensure reten(cid:415)on period is respected before running VACUUM to avoid dele(cid:415)ng files needed for (cid:415)me travel.”","source":"UST GLobal - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"}]},{"id":"streaming","name":"Streaming & Ingestion","icon":"📡","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"Difference between batch and streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — bounded dataset processed on a schedule (hourly/daily); high throughput, higher latency; tools: Spark batch, ADF, SSIS.\n• Streaming — unbounded, continuously arriving data processed incrementally with low latency; tools: Spark Structured Streaming, Kafka, Event Hubs.\nChoose batch for EOD reports/historical loads; streaming for fraud detection, IoT, clickstream, real-time dashboards."},{"q":"What is Spark Structured Streaming?","type":"concept","a":"Structured Streaming is Spark's scalable, fault-tolerant stream engine built on the DataFrame/SQL API.\n• You write the same DataFrame transformations; Spark runs them incrementally as new data arrives.\n• Sources: Kafka, Event Hubs, Auto Loader (cloudFiles), Delta, files, sockets.\n• Sinks: Delta tables, files, foreachBatch, Kafka.\n• Guarantees: exactly-once via checkpointing + idempotent sinks.\nModel: output modes append / update / complete; triggers processingTime or availableNow."},{"q":"What is checkpointing and why is it important in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"Checkpointing persists stream progress (which offsets/data were processed) and state to durable storage (e.g., ADLS) so the stream can recover after failure and continue without reprocessing or losing data.\n• Enables fault tolerance and exactly-once processing.\n• option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/...').\n• Each stream needs its own checkpoint location; changing the query requires a new checkpoint (or careful migration)."},{"q":"What is watermarking in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"A watermark defines how long to wait for late-arriving events (bounded lateness). Spark retains state for aggregations/deduplication until the watermark passes, then drops old state to bound memory.\n• withWatermark('eventTime','10 minutes').\n• Events older than the watermark are treated as late and dropped from stateful ops.\n• Balances correctness (waiting for late data) vs resource usage."},{"q":"Difference between event time and processing time?","type":"concept","a":"• Event time — when the event actually occurred (timestamp in the data).\n• Processing time — when Spark processes/receives it.\nStreaming windows/watermarks should use EVENT time for correct results when data arrives out of order; processing time is simpler but inaccurate for late data."},{"q":"Explain window operations in Structured Streaming.","type":"concept","a":"• Tumbling window — fixed-size, non-overlapping (e.g., every 1 minute): window('ts','1 minute').\n• Sliding window — fixed-size, overlapping (e.g., 10-min window every 5 min): window('ts','10 minutes','5 minutes').\n• Session window — gaps-based (Spark 3.2+).\nUsed with groupBy + watermark for time-based aggregation over event time."},{"q":"What is Auto Loader (cloudFiles)?","type":"concept","a":"Auto Loader is a Structured Streaming source that incrementally and efficiently discovers and ingests NEW files from cloud storage into Delta.\n• Scales to billions of files; processes each file once (tracked via checkpoint + rocksdb state).\n• Schema inference + evolution; rescued data column captures malformed records.\n• Two discovery modes: directory listing (default) or file notification (Event Grid/Queue) for lower cost at scale.\nTypical: ADLS → Auto Loader → Bronze Delta → Silver → Gold.","code":"(spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles')\n  .option('cloudFiles.format','json')\n  .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation','/ckpt/schema')\n  .load('abfss://container@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/')\n  .writeStream.format('delta')\n  .option('checkpointLocation','/ckpt/bronze')\n  .trigger(availableNow=True)\n  .toTable('bronze.events'))"},{"q":"What is the rescued data column in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"When incoming data doesn't match the expected schema (type mismatch, corrupt record), Auto Loader doesn't lose it — it stores the raw value in a special rescued data column (_rescued_data) as JSON.\n• Enables later triage/quarantine instead of dropping bad records.\n• Set via schemaHints/schemaLocation; default column name _rescued_data (configurable)."},{"q":"File notification vs directory listing in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Directory listing (default) — periodically lists the directory to find new files; simple, no extra Azure setup; cost grows with directory size.\n• File notification — subscribes to cloud events (Azure Event Grid + Storage Queue) to be notified of new files; more scalable and cheaper for high-volume/very large directories.\nChoose notification for scale; listing for simplicity/small volumes."},{"q":"How do you integrate Kafka or Event Hubs with Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Both expose a Kafka-compatible endpoint; use Spark's Kafka source.\n• readStream.format('kafka').option('kafka.bootstrap.servers',...).option('subscribe','topic').\n• For Event Hubs: use the Kafka API with Event Hubs connection string/SASL (or the native Event Hubs connector).\n• Parse the value (JSON) with from_json, then write to Delta with a checkpoint.\n• Deliver exactly-once by using Delta as the sink + checkpointing."},{"q":"How do you deduplicate streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"Use dropDuplicates on the streaming DataFrame with an event-time key, combined with a watermark to bound state:\ndf.withWatermark('event_ts','10 minutes').dropDuplicates(['id']).\nFor unbounded/all-time dedupe, use MERGE into a Delta table (whenNotMatchedInsert) or foreachBatch with a merge on the unique key."},{"q":"What trigger modes does Structured Streaming support?","type":"concept","a":"• Default/processingTime — micro-batches at intervals (e.g., '1 minute', '5 seconds').\n• availableNow — processes all available data as fast as possible then stops (efficient incremental batch; great for Auto Loader/COPY-style loads).\n• once (deprecated) — similar to availableNow.\n• continuous — experimental low-latency (~ms) mode with limited operators.\navailableNow is commonly used for cost-efficient incremental ingestion."},{"q":"What is foreachBatch and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"foreachBatch lets you run arbitrary logic on each micro-batch as a batch DataFrame.\n• Use it when the sink isn't natively supported or you need MERGE/upsert, multiple sinks, or custom dedupe.\n• It handles idempotency per batch (use batchId + MERGE).\nSignature: df.writeStream.foreachBatch(lambda df, batch_id: ...)."},{"q":"How do you achieve exactly-once processing in streaming?","type":"concept","a":"• Use checkpointing to track offsets/state.\n• Use an idempotent/transactional sink — Delta Lake writes are atomic, so replay is safe.\n• With foreachBatch, key the MERGE on a stable business key so re-processing the same batch doesn't duplicate.\n• Combine replayable source (Kafka offsets) + atomic sink = exactly-once end-to-end."},{"q":"What is DLT / Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines and what are its key features?","type":"concept","a":"Delta Live Tables (now Databricks Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines) is a declarative framework for building reliable pipelines with dependency management.\n• Define @dlt.table functions; the engine builds the dependency graph automatically (no manual orchestration).\n• Streaming tables (incremental) + materialized views (refreshed).\n• Data quality EXPECTATIONS: expect, expect_or_drop, expect_or_fail.\n• Auto retries, automatic lineage, schema evolution, and managed compute.\nDLT evolved into Declarative Pipelines — same concepts, broader scope.","code":"import dlt\n@dlt.table\n@dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_amount', 'amount > 0')\ndef silver_orders():\n    return dlt.read_stream('bronze_orders').filter('status != \"CANCELLED\"')"},{"q":"Difference between Delta Live Tables and Databricks Declarative Pipelines?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables is the older name; Declarative Pipelines is the newer, broader framework from Databricks.\n• Same core concepts (declarative @dlt tables, expectations, auto dependency, streaming tables + materialized views) with additional capabilities and general data-pipeline scope beyond ETL.\nSimple answer: DLT has evolved into Databricks Declarative Pipelines."},{"q":"How do you design a real-time analytics pipeline (Event Hubs → Databricks → Delta → BI)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Ingest: Spark Structured Streaming reads from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse (from_json), apply watermarks + dedupe, transform aggregations.\n• Write to a Bronze streaming Delta table (or DLT streaming table) with checkpointing for exactly-once.\n• Silver cleans/enriches; Gold aggregates for serving.\n• Serve to Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse, or to Real-Time Analytics for millisecond dashboards.\n• Monitor lag, throughput, and checkpoint health; tune triggers/partitions."},{"q":"What are the modes for writing streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"• Append — only new rows (default; Delta table grows).\n• Update — emit updated aggregation results (no full recompute).\n• Complete — rewrite the entire result table each batch (for aggregations without watermark).\nMost Delta streaming sinks use append; aggregations use update/complete."},{"q":"What is DLT (Delta Live Tables)?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables (DLT) is a Databricks framework for building reliable, maintainable, and automated ETL/ELT pipelines\n• Features:  Automa(cid:415)cally manages pipeline execu(cid:415)on.  Supports batch and streaming data.  Built-in data quality checks using expecta(cid:415)ons.  Automa(cid:415)cally handles dependencies between tables.  Provides monitoring and lineage.  Simplifies produc(cid:415)on pipeline development\n• Example: import dlt @dlt.table def customers(): return spark.read.table(\"bronze.customers\")","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Loader checks the checkpoint.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Only new files are read.","type":"concept","a":"Example: df = (spark.readStream .format(\"cloudFiles\") .op(cid:415)on(\"cloudFiles.format\", \"csv\") .load(\"/input\")) (df.writeStream .op(cid:415)on(\"checkpointLoca(cid:415)on\", \"/checkpoint\") .start(\"/output\"))","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"New Column in Streaming Data","type":"concept","a":"13. Schema Drift","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Autoloader — Auto-Ingest Files as They Arrive","type":"concept","a":"• Autoloader is Databricks' built-in streaming ingestion tool\n• It monitors a cloud storage path and automatically processes new files as they land — without you writing custom file-tracking logic\n• Why Autoloader? (vs. regular spark.read) Feature Regular spark.read vs Autoloader New files You must re-run the job manually each time new files arrive Autoloader Automatically detects and processes ONLY new files (no reprocessing old files) Scale Regular read loads ALL files every time — slow for millions of files Autoloader scale Uses cloud file notifications or incremental listing — handles millions of files efficiently Schema You must define schema manually or re-infer every time Autoloader schema Built-in schema inference + schema evolution support Autoloader Implementation # Basic Autoloader — reads new CSV files as they land df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/schema/') \\ # saves inferred schema .option('header', 'true') \\ .load('abfss://container@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/orders/') # Write the stream to a Delta table query = df_stream.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/orders/') \\ # tracks processed files .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ # handles new columns automatically .outputMode('append') \\ .trigger(availableNow=True) \\ # run once, process all pending files, then stop .toTable('silver.orders') query.awaitTermination() Autoloader with Schema Evolution (Production Pattern) # Production-ready Autoloader with transformations from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, input_file_name df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/events/schema/') \\ .option('cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes', 'true') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') \\ # handles new columns .load('abfss://raw@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/events/') # Add audit columns df_enriched = df_stream \\ .withColumn('ingestion_time', current_timestamp()) \\ .withColumn('source_file', input_file_name()) df_enriched.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/events/') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ .outputMode('append') \\ .toTable('bronze.events') 🔑 Key Autoloader Concepts checkpointLocation: Remembers which files have been processed\n• NEVER change this path. schemaLocation: Stores the inferred/evolved schema over time. trigger(availableNow=True): Batch mode — process pending files and stop (like a scheduled batch job). trigger(processingTime='5 minutes'): Micro-batch — runs every 5 minutes continuously","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"New Column Added in Streaming — How to Handle","type":"concept","a":"• This is a very common real-world problem\n• Your source system adds a new column to the data (e.g., a new field in Kafka, a new column in the CSV files), and your streaming job fails or ignores it\n• The Problem","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It is unable to handle real-time It can deal with real-time processing.","type":"concept","a":"processing.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Load Data into Databricks from an External Location?","type":"concept","a":"• Page 9 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Method 1 — Direct Read via abfss:// path # Read directly using the ABFSS path (no mount needed) df = spark.read.format('csv') .option('header','true') .option('inferSchema','true') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/data.csv') df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_data') Method 2 — Mount ADLS and Read from Mount # After mounting (see Q10), read from mount point df = spark.read.format('parquet').load('/mnt/datalake/raw/') Method 3 — Auto Loader for Incremental File Ingestion df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/schema') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/') ) df.writeStream.format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/bronze') .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.incremental_data') Method 4 — COPY INTO (SQL-based batch load) COPY INTO bronze.raw_orders FROM 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/orders/' FILEFORMAT = CSV FORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true', 'inferSchema' = 'true') COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true');\n• Method 5 — Unity Catalog External Location -- Create external location (Unity Catalog) CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION my_adls_location URL 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/' WITH (STORAGE CREDENTIAL my_storage_credential); -- Create external table pointing to it CREATE TABLE bronze.raw_sales LOCATION 'abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/' AS SELECT * FROM read_files('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/sales/');","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Code to Load Data into Azure Databricks","type":"concept","a":"From ADLS Gen2 (CSV) df = (spark.read .format('csv') .option('header', 'true') .option('inferSchema', 'true') .option('mode', 'PERMISSIVE') .option('columnNameOfCorruptRecord', '_corrupt') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/orders.csv') ) df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.raw_orders') Page 16 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide From Azure SQL Database jdbc_url = 'jdbc:sqlserver://server.database.windows.net:1433;database=mydb' sql_props = { 'user': dbutils.secrets.get('kv-scope','sql-user'), 'password': dbutils.secrets.get('kv-scope','sql-password'), 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver' } # Full load df = spark.read.jdbc(url=jdbc_url, table='dbo.Orders', properties=sql_props) # Incremental load with pushdown predicate df = spark.read.jdbc( url=jdbc_url, table='dbo.Orders', predicates=['ModifiedDate > CAST(GETDATE()-1 AS DATE)'], properties=sql_props ) df.write.format('delta').mode('append').saveAsTable('bronze.sql_orders') Auto Loader (Incremental from ADLS) from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, input_file_name (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/ckpt/schema') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/landing/') .withColumn('_loaded_at', current_timestamp()) .withColumn('_file', input_file_name()) .writeStream .format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/bronze') .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.events') )","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Why Databricks Instead of ADF?","type":"concept","a":"Page 1 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A ADF is excellent for orchestration and simple copy/move tasks; however, Databricks is preferred for heavy-duty data engineering for the following reasons: Feature Databricks ADF Processing Power Distributed Spark clusters – Limited compute; not designed for handles TBs easily heavy transforms Complex Transformations Full Python / SQL / Scala / R Only basic via Mapping Data Flow support (GUI-based) ML & Analytics Built-in MLflow, notebooks, Not supported AutoML Delta Lake Support Native first-class support Limited / no native support Real-time Streaming Structured Streaming & Limited streaming capability Autoloader Debugging Interactive notebooks, easy cell- Pipeline debugging is complex by-cell debug Cost at Scale More efficient for heavy workloads Cheaper for simple ETL / copy pipelines Version Control Git integration in Repos ARM templates only Summary: Use ADF for orchestration and scheduling; use Databricks for transformation, streaming, ML, and Delta Lake operations.","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Autoloader and Its Uses?","type":"concept","a":"• Autoloader (cloudFiles) is a Databricks feature that incrementally and efficiently ingests new files arriving in cloud storage (ADLS Gen2, S3, GCS) into Delta tables — without reprocessing old files\n• Key Features • Automatically detects new files — no manual tracking required • Processes files exactly once using checkpointing • Schema inference and evolution support • Scales to billions of files using file notification mode (Event Grid / SNS) • Supports JSON, CSV, Parquet, Avro, ORC, text, binary formats Two Discovery Modes Mode How it works Best for Directory Listing Periodically lists all files and identifies new Small to medium file volumes ones File Notification Uses cloud events (Event Grid/SNS) to High-volume, millions of files receive notifications Basic Autoloader Code df = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/schema/') \\ .load('/mnt/landing/input/') df.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoint/') \\ .start('/mnt/delta/output/') Page 3 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Delta Live Table (DLT)?","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables (DLT) is a declarative framework in Databricks for building reliable, scalable, and maintainable data pipelines\n• Instead of writing imperative ETL code, you declare what data transformations you want, and DLT manages the execution, orchestration, error handling, and monitoring automatically\n• Key Benefits • Automatic pipeline orchestration — no need to manage job dependencies manually • Built-in data quality with Expectations (constraints on data) • Automatic schema management and evolution • End-to-end lineage and observability in the DLT UI • Supports both batch and streaming workloads in the same pipeline Basic DLT Example import dlt from pyspark.sql.functions import col @dlt.table(comment='Raw ingested data') Page 6 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A def raw_data(): return spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .load('/mnt/landing/') @dlt.table @dlt.expect('valid_salary', 'salary > 0') def cleaned_data(): return dlt.read_stream('raw_data').filter(col('salary').isNotNull())","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Types of Delta Live Tables","type":"concept","a":"• Type Description Use Case Streaming Table Append-only; processes each row Real-time ingestion from exactly once from a streaming source\n• Autoloader, Kafka, Event Hubs Defined with @dlt.table + readStream\n• Materialized View Pre-computed result of a query, Aggregations, joins, reporting- updated automatically by the pipeline. ready data Can perform aggregations\n• Live Table (batch) Standard DLT table refreshed in full or Batch ETL, slowly changing incrementally on each pipeline run. dimensions","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is an Autoloader? (Detailed)","type":"concept","a":"(See also Q6 above for overview) Additional Details • Autoloader maintains a checkpoint so it never reprocesses the same file twice, even across cluster restarts • Schema inference: on first run, Autoloader samples files and infers the schema automatically • Schema hints: you can manually specify data types for specific columns to override inference • Supports schema evolution — new columns in files can be automatically merged into the target schema • Works seamlessly with Delta Live Tables for production-grade ingestion pipelines File Notification vs Directory Listing — When to Use Scenario Recommended Mode < 10,000 files and simple setup needed Directory Listing Page 7 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A Millions of files / high-throughput ingestion File Notification (Event Grid on Azure, SNS/SQS on AWS) Near real-time latency required File Notification Cross-cloud or custom storage Directory Listing","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Auto Loader concept?","type":"concept","a":"• Auto Loader is a Databricks feature (built on Spark Structured Streaming) that efficiently ingests new files arriving in cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) into Delta Lake incrementally\n• Key Features: • Incremental Ingestion: Processes only new files since the last checkpoint — no need to list all files on every run. • Scalable File Detection: Uses cloud notification services (Azure Event Grid / AWS SNS+SQS) instead of listing directory contents, making it efficient at scale. • Schema Inference & Evolution: Can automatically infer schema from incoming files and handle schema changes. • Exactly-once Processing: Uses checkpointing to ensure files are not reprocessed\n• Basic Syntax: df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/schema_checkpoint/') .load('/mnt/landing/raw_data/')) df.writeStream .format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoint/') .outputMode('append') .start('/mnt/delta/bronze_table/')","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Difference b/w Copy into vs Auto loader?","type":"concept","a":"Copy Into vs Auto Loader Feature Copy Into Auto Loader Type Batch Streaming File tracking Manual Automa(cid:415)c Use case One-(cid:415)me load Con(cid:415)nuous inges(cid:415)on","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What is expecta(cid:415)on in DLT?","type":"concept","a":"Expecta(cid:415)ons in DLT  Define data quality rules: CONSTRAINT valid_salary EXPECT (salary > 0)  Can drop or quaran(cid:415)ne bad records","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What’s your plan for career growth as a Data Engineer in the next few years?","type":"concept","a":"• In the next few years, I want to deepen my expertise in Azure Data Engineering by working on large-scale, cloud-native data platforms\n• I aim to become proficient in advanced Spark optimization, real-time streaming, and data architecture\n• As I gain more experience, I'd like to take on technical leadership responsibilities, mentor junior engineers, and contribute to designing scalable, reliable, and cost-efficient data solutions that support business goals","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Auto Loader in ADF — Latest File Detection","type":"concept","a":"• Auto Loader is a Databricks feature that automatically detects and processes new files arriving in cloud storage using Structured Streaming's cloudFiles format source\n• Auto Loader Code (Databricks) df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/checkpoint/schema') .load('/mnt/landing-zone/') ) df.writeStream .option('checkpointLocation', '/checkpoint/data') Page 5 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.raw_events') How to Know the Latest File Placed • Method 1 — Auto Loader Checkpoint: tracks processed files automatically; new files picked up on next run # Method 2 — Check via dbutils files = dbutils.fs.ls('/mnt/landing-zone/') latest = max(files, key=lambda f: f.modificationTime) print(latest.name, latest.modificationTime) • Method 3 — ADF Get Metadata Activity: use 'Last Modified' field and compare with previous run timestamp -- Method 4 — Delta audit log DESCRIBE HISTORY my_table","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What value do you use from Lookup while giving dynamic parameters?","type":"concept","a":"If First row only = True: Use: @ac(cid:415)vity('Lookup1').output.firstRow.TableName Example: @ac(cid:415)vity('Lookup1').output.firstRow.Watermark If First row only = False: Use: @ac(cid:415)vity('Lookup1').output.value This returns an array, which is commonly passed to a ForEach ac(cid:415)vity.","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If Blob Storage Has Thousands of CSV Files, How Would You Load Them","type":"concept","a":"Efficiently? Use Auto Loader (cloudFiles format) — the Databricks-native solution for large-scale incremental file ingestion: df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('header', 'true') .option('inferSchema', 'true') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/schema') .load('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/raw/csv/') ) df.writeStream .format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/ckpt/bronze') .option('mergeSchema', 'true') .trigger(availableNow=True) # batch mode: process all new files, then stop .toTable('bronze.raw_csv_data')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Why Auto Loader over plain spark.read?","type":"concept","a":"• spark.read.csv() lists ALL files on every run — slow and redundant for thousands of files • Auto Loader tracks processed files in a checkpoint — only new files are processed each run • File notification mode (Azure Event Grid / AWS SNS) detects new files instantly without directory listing • Schema evolution handled automatically via schemaLocation — new columns merged gracefully","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Most Efficient Way to Load Files in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Method Best For Incremental? Notes Page 9 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Auto Loader (cloudFiles) Thousands of files, cloud Yes Best overall — uses file storage notifications + checkpoint COPY INTO SQL-based batch loads, Yes (tracks Simpler than Auto Loader; idempotent loaded files) SQL syntax spark.readStream (manual) Custom streaming with Yes (manual More complex; need to full control checkpoint) manage state spark.read.format() One-time full/historical No Simplest — not for loads incremental use Auto Loader is the recommended production approach for incremental ingestion\n• COPY INTO is good for simpler SQL-centric workflows. spark.read is fine for one-time historical data loads","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Does Auto Loader Perform Incremental Loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Mode 1 — Directory Listing (default) • Lists the source directory and compares against checkpoint of already-processed files • Simple to set up; suitable for directories with fewer than a few hundred thousand files Mode 2 — File Notification (recommended for scale) • Cloud storage sends event notifications (Azure Event Grid, AWS S3 Events, GCS Pub/Sub) when new files arrive • Auto Loader subscribes to the notification queue and processes only new files • Much faster than directory listing at scale; no full directory scan .option('cloudFiles.useNotifications', 'true') .option('cloudFiles.subscriptionId', '<azure-subscription-id>') .option('cloudFiles.resourceGroup', '<resource-group>') The checkpoint folder on cloud storage records every processed file path and offset\n• On the next run, Auto Loader resumes from the checkpoint and skips already-processed files","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Schedule Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Jobs — create a Job that runs the Auto Loader notebook on a cron schedule (e.g., every 30 minutes or hourly) • Delta Live Tables (DLT) — define Auto Loader as a DLT source; Databricks fully manages scheduling, retries, and monitoring • Azure Data Factory — ADF triggers a Databricks notebook/job on a schedule or via tumbling window triggers • Databricks Workflows — multi-task workflows with dependencies across Bronze/Silver/Gold layers .trigger(availableNow=True) # Scheduled batch: process all pending files then stop .trigger(processingTime='1 hour') # Continuous micro-batch every hour .trigger(continuous='1 minute') # Near real-time, low-latency streaming","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Triggers Are Available in Auto Loader?","type":"concept","a":"• Trigger Syntax Use Case Available Now trigger(availableNow=True) Process all pending files then stop; best for scheduled batch jobs Page 10 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Processing Time trigger(processingTime='30 Micro-batch every N seconds/minutes; seconds') near real-time Once (deprecated) trigger(once=True) Old version of availableNow; still works but deprecated in Spark 3.3+ Continuous trigger(continuous='1 minute') Ultra-low latency streaming; millisecond processing No trigger (default) Omit trigger option Continuous streaming; runs until manually stopped For scheduled batch jobs: use trigger(availableNow=True)\n• For near real-time: trigger(processingTime='30 seconds'). availableNow is the most common choice in production scheduled pipelines","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Does Auto Loader Detect New Files?","type":"concept","a":"• Directory Listing: scans the source path, compares full file list against checkpoint; files not in checkpoint are new • File Notification: cloud storage fires an event (S3 SNS -> SQS, ADLS -> Event Grid, GCS -> Pub/Sub) on each new file; Auto Loader polls the queue • Checkpoint: stored on cloud storage (similar to Delta log) — records every processed file and stream offset • Schema location: Auto Loader samples new file batches and updates schema if new columns are detected spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.useNotifications', 'false') # directory listing mode .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/ckpt/schema') # schema evolution tracking .option('cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger', '1000') # limit files per micro-batch .load('/mnt/landing/') Section 8 | Data Quality","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How is Data Loaded into the Raw/Bronze Layer from Thousands of CSV Files?","type":"concept","a":"• Auto Loader reads new CSV files from ADLS/S3 incrementally using the cloudFiles format • Minimal transformation in Bronze — raw data written as-is to a Delta table • Add metadata columns: _ingestion_time and _source_file for lineage/audit • Enable mergeSchema to handle new columns arriving in new file batches from pyspark.sql.functions import current_timestamp, input_file_name df = (spark.readStream .format('cloudFiles') .option('cloudFiles.format', 'csv') .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/ckpt/bronze_schema') .option('header', 'true') .load('/mnt/landing/csv/') .withColumn('_ingestion_time', current_timestamp()) .withColumn('_source_file', input_file_name()) ) df.writeStream.format('delta') .option('checkpointLocation', '/ckpt/bronze') .option('mergeSchema', 'true') .trigger(availableNow=True) .toTable('bronze.raw_transactions')","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Expectations, DLT expectations?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• Are there opportunities to work on real-time streaming use cases, or is the focus primarily batch?","type":"concept","a":"Questions about the role","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Have you worked on streaming data?","type":"concept","a":"• If your project is batch: \"My current project is primarily batch-based\n• We use Azure Data Factory to schedule daily incremental loads into ADLS, transform them using Databricks, and publish curated data for repor(cid:415)ng\n• Although I haven't implemented streaming in produc(cid:415)on, I'm familiar with Spark Structured Streaming concepts such as micro-batches, checkpoints, and event processing.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Micro-batch / Near Real-time","type":"concept","a":"• Data is processed in small batches at short intervals (seconds to minutes) • Tools: Spark Structured Streaming with trigger intervals","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Streaming / Real-time Ingestion","type":"concept","a":"• Data is ingested and processed as it arrives (continuous) • Tools: Kafka, Azure Event Hubs, Spark Streaming, Kinesis • Use case: Fraud detection, IoT sensor data, clickstream","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Delta Live Tables (DLT)","type":"concept","a":"• Delta Live Tables (DLT) is a declarative ETL framework in Databricks for building reliable, maintainable data pipelines with built-in data quality checks\n• Key Concepts: • LIVE TABLE: Materialized view updated by DLT engine • STREAMING LIVE TABLE: For streaming/incremental data • EXPECTATIONS: Data quality constraints import dlt from pyspark.sql.functions import col # Bronze layer - raw ingestion @dlt.table(comment='Raw orders from source') def bronze_orders(): return spark.readStream.format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .load('/landing/orders/') # Silver layer - cleaned with DQ checks @dlt.table(comment='Cleaned orders') @dlt.expect_or_drop('valid_amount', 'amount > 0') @dlt.expect('not_null_id', 'order_id IS NOT NULL') def silver_orders(): return dlt.read_stream('bronze_orders') \\ .filter(col('status') != 'CANCELLED') # Gold layer - aggregated @dlt.table(comment='Daily revenue') def gold_daily_revenue(): return dlt.read('silver_orders') \\ .groupBy('order_date') \\ .agg(sum('amount').alias('total_revenue'))","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"architecture","name":"Data Architecture for AI","icon":"🏗️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold)?","type":"concept","a":"Medallion architecture organizes a lakehouse into progressive quality layers:\n• Bronze (raw) — exact copy of source, minimal transformation, append-only, audit trail, Delta format.\n• Silver (cleansed/conformed) — cleaned, validated, deduplicated, standardized, joined; the 'single source of truth' for analytics/DS.\n• Gold (business) — aggregated, enriched, business-ready (KPIs, star schemas) for BI/reporting.\nTypical flow: ADLS → Databricks → Bronze → Silver → Gold → Power BI."},{"q":"What is stored in each Medallion layer and in what format?","type":"concept","a":"• Bronze: raw as-is + metadata columns (load_date, ingestion_timestamp, source_file). Delta (converted from CSV/JSON/Parquet). No business transforms. Path e.g. /bronze/orders.\n• Silver: validated, deduplicated, type-cast, joined, conformed data. Delta. /silver/orders_clean.\n• Gold: business aggregates, KPIs, star-schema fact/dim tables. Delta (or Parquet for serving). /gold/daily_sales.\nAlways store transformed data as Delta for ACID, time travel, schema evolution, and MERGE."},{"q":"Walk through an end-to-end Medallion implementation.","type":"concept","a":"1. Ingest — ADF / Auto Loader / COPY INTO lands raw files into ADLS Bronze as Delta (add metadata columns).\n2. Transform to Silver — PySpark/DLT: cleanse, dedupe, cast types, standardize, apply DQ checks, join reference data; MERGE incrementally.\n3. Build Gold — aggregate to KPI tables / star schema (facts + dims), apply SCD logic, partition by date.\n4. Serve — Power BI/Tableau via SQL Warehouse; ML from Silver.\n5. Govern — Unity Catalog for access, lineage, RLS/CLS; monitor with alerts + audit.\n6. Orchestrate — Databricks Workflows / ADF with retries, parameterization, dev→qa→prod promotion."},{"q":"What data validations do you perform on the Silver layer?","type":"concept","a":"• Schema/type validation — columns and types match expectations.\n• Null checks on critical columns.\n• Duplicate checks on business/primary keys.\n• Range/domain validation (e.g., age 0–150, valid enums).\n• Referential integrity (child keys exist in parents via left anti join).\n• Record-count reconciliation vs source/threshold.\n• Business rules (end_date >= start_date).\nImplement as DQ framework / DLT expectations; quarantine or fail on critical violations."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Data moves from Silver to Gold. At what stage do you aggregate and why? How do you apply RLS/CLS there?","type":"scenario","a":"• Aggregate in the GOLD layer — Silver keeps granular, conformed data (reusable, single source of truth); Gold is purpose-built for BI with pre-computed aggregates (faster dashboards, smaller scans). Aggregating too early loses reusability.\n• RLS/CLS: apply in Gold (and where sensitive columns exist) using Unity Catalog row filters and column masks so Power BI/Tableau consumers only see permitted rows/columns. Enforce centrally in UC, not in each report.\n• Also restrict access via GRANTs on Gold tables/views."},{"q":"When would you choose batch vs streaming processing?","type":"concept","a":"• Batch — scheduled, high-throughput, latency-tolerant: end-of-day reports, historical/backfill loads, month-end close, large transformations.\n• Streaming — continuous, low-latency: fraud detection, IoT/telemetry, clickstream, real-time alerting/dashboards, CDC propagation.\nMany platforms use a lambda-style hybrid: streaming for speed, batch for corrections/reprocessing."},{"q":"How do you design a pipeline for incremental data loads?","type":"concept","a":"• Track a watermark (max updated_ts/ID) per table in a control table.\n• Extract only records > watermark (predicate pushdown at source).\n• Apply transforms; MERGE into the target (upsert; handle deletes via CDC if available).\n• Update the watermark only after success.\n• Handle late-arriving data (overlap window / reprocess) and ensure idempotency.\n• Schedule/orchestrate via ADF or Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"What is data quality and what are its dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"Data Quality = how fit data is for its intended use. Dimensions:\n• Completeness (no missing required values)\n• Accuracy (reflects reality)\n• Consistency (same across systems)\n• Timeliness (available when needed)\n• Uniqueness (no duplicates)\n• Validity (conforms to formats/rules).\nEnforced via DLT expectations, Great Expectations, or custom PySpark checks; fail fast or quarantine bad records."},{"q":"How do you handle bad/corrupt records?","type":"concept","a":"Never let bad records corrupt trusted layers. Options:\n• Quarantine — write invalid rows to a separate bad-records table/path for triage.\n• Rescue — Auto Loader's _rescued_data column keeps unmatched/corrupt values.\n• Drop/log — expect_or_drop in DLT (log + drop) or filter with logging.\n• Fail fast — expect_or_fail for critical rules (stop the pipeline).\n• Schema handling — mode PERMISSIVE (keeps corrupt as null), DROPMALFORMED (drop), FAILFAST (error)."},{"q":"What is the 'typical architecture' for an Azure data engineering project?","type":"concept","a":"Sources (SQL Server, APIs, CSV, SAP) → ADF (orchestration/ingestion) → ADLS Gen2 → Databricks (PySpark transformations) with Bronze/Silver/Gold Delta layers → serve to Power BI / Synapse.\nCross-cutting: Unity Catalog governance, Key Vault secrets, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics, CI/CD, and orchestration via ADF/Databricks Workflows."},{"q":"How do you decide between partitioning, Z-ORDER, and Liquid Clustering?","type":"concept","a":"• Partitioning — low-cardinality columns (date, region, country) where each partition is ≥ ~1 GB; enables partition pruning; avoid high cardinality/over-partitioning.\n• Z-ORDER — high-cardinality filter/join columns (customer_id) on existing (often partitioned) tables; re-cluster on OPTIMIZE.\n• Liquid Clustering — modern default for evolving queries; incremental auto re-clustering, no partition maintenance; can replace partition+Z-ORDER.\nDecide by query filter patterns + table size + maintenance overhead."},{"q":"How do you handle schema drift/evolution across the platform?","type":"concept","a":"• Detect: compare source vs expected schema (Get Metadata/Validate) or let reads report new columns.\n• ADF: Allow schema drift in data flows; benign schema changes flow through.\n• Auto Loader: schema inference + evolution (_rescued_data for mismatches).\n• Delta: mergeSchema on write / delta.autoMerge; ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN.\n• Design Bronze to be schema-flexible (raw), enforce structure at Silver/Gold.\n• Add alerts on drift so downstream contracts don't break silently."},{"q":"Explain your Project Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Our project follows Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• Flow: Source Systems | | (SAP, SQL Server, APIs, CSV, Oracle) | Azure Data Factory | | Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 | | Databricks Bronze Layer (Raw Data) | Silver Layer (Cleansed & Transformed) | Gold Layer (Business Ready Tables) | Power BI / Synapse / ML Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2  Azure Databricks  Delta Lake  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI  Azure Monitor","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"what is difference between data lake and data warehouse","type":"concept","a":"• Data lakes and data warehouses are both widely used for storing big data, but they are not interchangeable terms\n• A data lake is a vast pool of raw data, the purpose for which is not yet defined\n• A data warehouse is a repository for structured, filtered data that has already been processed for a specific purpose","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It’s design is very simple. While it’s design is complex.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Architecture of Synapse Data ware house? And what are different kind of","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"19.What is DW?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Data ware house is nothing but collect the data, store the data, analysis the data and consume the data\n• A data warehouse is specially designed for data analytics, which involves reading large amounts of data to understand relationships and trends across the data\n• A database is used to capture and store data, such as recording details of a transaction","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is data mart?","type":"concept","a":"A data mart is a simple form of data warehouse focused on a single subject or line of business.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Can you introduce yourself?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Hi, I'm an Azure Data Engineer with experience in building end-to-end data pipelines using Azure Data Factory (ADF), Azure Databricks, ADLS Gen2, Azure SQL Database, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on inges(cid:415)ng data from mul(cid:415)ple source systems into Azure Data Lake, transforming it using PySpark in Databricks, and loading curated data into the Gold layer following the Medallion Architecture\n• I have experience in developing ADF pipelines, crea(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng data valida(cid:415)on and cleansing, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, handling incremental loads, monitoring pipelines, and suppor(cid:415)ng produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project architecture?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Our project is an employee analy(cid:415)cs pla(cid:414)orm\n• Data comes from HR systems, A(cid:425)endance systems, and Payroll applica(cid:415)ons in CSV and SQL formats\n• The architecture is: Source Systems │ Azure Data Factory │ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 │ Bronze Layer │ Azure Databricks (PySpark) │ Silver Layer │ Business Transforma(cid:415)ons │ Gold Layer │ Power BI Repor(cid:415)ng ADF is responsible for orchestra(cid:415)ng the pipelines\n• Databricks performs data cleansing and transforma(cid:415)ons\n• Gold tables are used by business users through Power BI dashboards.\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"What are your roles and responsibili(cid:415)es?","type":"concept","a":"\"My daily responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing Azure Data Factory pipelines  Crea(cid:415)ng Databricks notebooks using PySpark  Reading and wri(cid:415)ng data to ADLS Gen2  Performing data cleansing and valida(cid:415)on  Removing duplicates and handling null values  Implemen(cid:415)ng Delta Lake tables  Loading data into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Monitoring pipeline failures  Fixing produc(cid:415)on issues  Working with Git for version control  Coordina(cid:415)ng with QA and business teams\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ETL and ELT","type":"concept","a":"• ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) and ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) are two approaches for moving and preparing data\n• Aspect ETL ELT Order Extract → Transform → Load Extract → Load → Transform Where Target data Separate ETL tool/server transformations occur warehouse/lakehouse Aspect ETL ELT Data loaded into Already transformed Raw data first, then transformed target Leverages warehouse compute Performance Depends on ETL server capacity power Traditional data warehouses and Modern cloud platforms with Best for strict data quality requirements scalable compute Raw data remains available for Flexibility Less flexible once transformed reprocessing","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your end-to-end project architecture.","type":"concept","a":"Sample Answer \"Our project is an Azure-based data pla(cid:414)orm built using a Medallion Architecture.\" Web Applica(cid:415)ons / ERP / APIs │ ▼ On-Prem SQL Server │ Azure Data Factory │ ▼ Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 Bronze Layer (Raw) │ ▼ Azure Databricks (PySpark) Silver Layer (Cleaned) │ ▼ Azure Databricks Gold Layer │ ▼ Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs │ ▼ Power BI Services used:  Azure Data Factory  Azure Databricks  ADLS Gen2  Azure Synapse Analy(cid:415)cs  Azure Key Vault  Azure DevOps  Power BI","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Where is the actual data stored?","type":"concept","a":"Different stages:  SQL Server → Opera(cid:415)onal (OLTP)  ADLS Bronze → Raw  ADLS Silver → Cleaned  ADLS Gold → Curated  Synapse → Analy(cid:415)cs/Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are you storing data in SQL Server, ADLS, or Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Yes, but each serves a different purpose: Storage Purpose SQL Server Opera(cid:415)onal data ADLS Data lake (Bronze, Silver, Gold) Synapse Repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Architecture of project (End-to-End pipeline)","type":"concept","a":"We follow Medallion Architecture in Azure Databricks: (cid:44934) Flow: Sources  SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake ADF (Inges(cid:415)on Layer)  Copy Ac(cid:415)vity / Mapping Data Flow  Moves data to ADLS ADLS Gen2 Storage  Bronze (Raw data) Databricks Processing  Bronze → Silver → Gold (cid:44934) Layers:  Bronze → Raw ingested data  Silver → Cleaned + validated + deduplicated  Gold → Business aggregated data Consump(cid:415)on Layer  Power BI / Tableau / SQL Warehouse","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"After Transformation, Where Do You Store the Data?","type":"concept","a":"• After transformation, data is stored in the appropriate Medallion Architecture layer as a Delta table: Layer What is Stored Format Location Example Bronze (Raw) Raw data as-is from source;\n• Delta (from delta/bronze/raw_orders metadata columns added CSV/JSON/Parquet) Silver (Cleansed) Validated, deduplicated, type-cast, Delta delta/silver/orders_clean joined data Gold (Curated) Business-level aggregations, KPIs, Delta or Parquet delta/gold/daily_sales reporting tables # Write transformed Silver data to Delta table silver_df.write .format('delta') .mode('overwrite') # or 'append' / 'merge' for incremental .option('mergeSchema','true') .partitionBy('year','month') .saveAsTable('silver.orders_clean') # Or write to ADLS path silver_df.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite') .save('abfss://container@account.dfs.core.windows.net/silver/orders/') Always store transformed data as Delta format — it gives ACID transactions, time travel, schema evolution, and efficient MERGE operations for incremental updates","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Architecture in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks follows the Lakehouse Architecture — combining the scalability of a Data Lake with the governance and performance of a Data Warehouse\n• Medallion Architecture (Most Common) Data flows through three progressive layers — each improving quality and structure: Layer Also Called Purpose Format Bronze Raw Layer Ingest raw data as-is from source Delta / Parquet / JSON / — no transformations CSV Silver Cleansed Layer Clean, deduplicate, validate, join Delta Table — trusted, queryable data Gold Aggregated / Business-level aggregations, Delta Table Business Layer KPIs, reporting-ready data Core Databricks Architecture Components Component Description Control Plane Managed by Databricks (Azure/AWS/GCP) — stores metadata, notebooks, job configs, UI Data Plane Customer's cloud account — where Spark clusters run and data is stored in ADLS/S3 Clusters Spark compute nodes — All-Purpose (interactive) or Job Clusters (automated pipelines) DBFS Databricks File System — abstraction over cloud storage (ADLS, S3, GCS) Unity Catalog Unified governance layer — manages tables, schemas, data lineage, access control Delta Lake Storage layer for all tables — provides ACID, versioning, schema enforcement MLflow Built-in ML lifecycle management — experiment tracking, model registry, deployment Workflows / Jobs Orchestration layer — schedule notebooks, DLT pipelines, dbt, Python scripts Autoloader Incremental file ingestion from cloud storage into Delta tables Delta Live Tables Declarative pipeline framework with built-in data quality and monitoring Data Flow in the Architecture • Sources (SQL Server, APIs, flat files, IoT) land raw data in ADLS/S3 Page 4 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 • Bronze layer ingests via Autoloader or COPY INTO — raw Delta tables • Silver layer cleanses, deduplicates, and enriches with business rules • Gold layer aggregates for BI tools (Power BI, Tableau) or ML models • Unity Catalog governs access and lineage across all three layers Page 5 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You mentioned in your resume that you worked on a migration project. Explain","type":"concept","a":"• about it\n• In the migration project, we moved our existing on-premises data warehouse (e.g., SQL Server / Oracle) to a cloud-based Lakehouse architecture on Databricks / Azure Synapse\n• Key Phases of the Migration: • Assessment & Planning: Audited existing tables, stored procedures, ETL jobs, and data volumes\n• Identified dependencies and prioritized migration order. • Architecture Design: Designed a Medallion Architecture (Bronze → Silver → Gold) on Azure Data Lake Storage (ADLS Gen2) with Delta Lake format. • Data Extraction: Extracted data from the source (on-prem DB) using JDBC connectors or flat file exports. • Transformation & Load: Rewrote SQL-based transformations in PySpark\n• Applied business logic, cleansing, deduplication, and type casting. • Validation: Compared row counts, aggregates, and key metrics between source and target to ensure data integrity. • Cutover: Ran parallel loads, froze the source, did final delta sync, and switched downstream consumers to the new platform","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Project Architecture (End-to-End)","type":"concept","a":"We used Azure + Databricks Medallion Architecture:  Sources: SQL Server / APIs / Files / Snowflake  ADF: Data inges(cid:415)on + orchestra(cid:415)on  ADLS Gen2: Storage layer  Databricks: o Bronze → Raw inges(cid:415)on o Silver → Cleaned + transformed o Gold → Business-ready data  Consump(cid:415)on: Power BI / SQL Warehouse","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How many layers you will be having in your databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Layers in Databricks  Bronze → Raw data  Silver → Cleaned + validated data  Gold → Aggregated business data (Some systems also use Pla(cid:415)num for ML)","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain your current data pipeline architecture tools, flow, and data volume.","type":"concept","a":"• Our source systems include SQL Database, REST APIs, and CSV files uploaded to Azure Blob Storage\n• Azure Data Factory orchestrates data ingestion into ADLS Gen2 Bronze storage\n• Azure Databricks performs cleansing, deduplication, joins, aggregations, and business transformations using PySpark\n• Processed data is stored in Delta Lake Silver and Gold layers\n• Power BI consumes the Gold layer for reporting\n• Azure Monitor and Log Analytics are used for monitoring, while Git-based CI/CD manages deployments\n• On average, we process around 80–150 GB of data daily, which translates to several million records depending on the source","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain about yourself.","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Answer (2-3 minutes) \"Hi, I'm a Data Engineer with around X years of experience in designing and developing ETL/ELT data pipelines using Azure Data Factory, Azure Databricks, PySpark, SQL, and Delta Lake\n• In my current project, I work on building scalable data inges(cid:415)on frameworks where data is collected from mul(cid:415)ple sources such as SQL databases, APIs, and cloud storage into Azure Data Lake\n• We follow the Medallion Architecture (Bronze, Silver, Gold)\n• ADF is mainly used for orchestra(cid:415)on, Databricks performs data transforma(cid:415)on, and Delta Lake stores processed data\n• My responsibili(cid:415)es include:  Developing ADF pipelines  Wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks  Op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs  Data valida(cid:415)on  Incremental loading  Performance tuning  Pipeline monitoring and troubleshoo(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the data framework that you are working on.","type":"concept","a":"A typical architecture: Source Systems (SQL, APIs, CSV, SAP) ↓ Azure Data Factory (Orchestra(cid:415)on) ↓ Azure Data Lake Gen2 ↓ Bronze Layer (Raw Delta Tables) ↓ Databricks ↓ Silver Layer (Cleansed + Standardized) ↓ Gold Layer (Business Aggrega(cid:415)ons) ↓ Power BI / Synapse Explain each layer: Bronze  Raw data  No transforma(cid:415)ons  Append only  Delta format Silver  Data cleaning  Remove duplicates  Null handling  Data type conversion  Standardiza(cid:415)on Gold  Business-ready data  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  KPI tables  Repor(cid:415)ng","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What do you know about Delta Live Tables?","type":"concept","a":"• DLT is a Databricks framework used for building reliable ETL pipelines\n• Features:  Automa(cid:415)c dependency management  Incremental processing  Data quality expecta(cid:415)ons  Auto retries  Pipeline monitoring  Automa(cid:415)c lineage  Schema evolu(cid:415)on Example: Bronze ↓ Silver ↓ Gold No need to manually manage execu(cid:415)on order","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How is data stored in Bronze?","type":"concept","a":"Usually:  Delta format  Append only  Raw  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oned (op(cid:415)onal)  Metadata columns Example: customer.csv ↓ Bronze customer_id name city load_date inges(cid:415)on_(cid:415)mestamp source_file No business transforma(cid:415)ons.","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Project Explanation","type":"concept","a":"• Use the STAR framework: Situation, Task, Action, Result\n• Always quantify outcomes. • Situation: Organization needed to process 10TB+ daily transaction data from Kafka, S3, and REST APIs for fraud detection. • Task: Design and implement an end-to-end pipeline using Databricks and Delta Lake, replacing legacy batch jobs. • Action: Built Bronze/Silver/Gold medallion architecture\n• Used Auto Loader for incremental ingestion, Structured Streaming for near-real-time processing, Z-ordering for query optimization, and Delta constraints for data quality. • Result: Reduced data latency from 6 hours to 15 minutes\n• Query performance improved by 70%\n• Storage costs reduced by 40% via Delta compaction\n• Page 1 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How many Delta tables are there in Silver and Gold layers?","type":"concept","a":"• \"In my project, each curated dataset is stored as a separate Delta table\n• The exact number depends on the project\n• For example, we maintain around 20–30 Delta tables in the Silver layer and 10–15 aggregated/business tables in the Gold layer\n• The key point is that each business en(cid:415)ty or repor(cid:415)ng dataset is maintained as its own Delta table.\" (Replace the numbers with your project's actual values if known.)","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Notebooks Does a Data Engineer Typically Deal With in a Year?","type":"concept","a":"• This is experience-based\n• Tailor your answer to your actual project, but a typical range: • Small project: 10-20 notebooks per year Page 1 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) • Mid-size project: 25-50 notebooks (Bronze/Silver/Gold layers, 3-10 notebooks per domain per layer) • Large enterprise: 50-100+ notebooks organized by domain (Finance, Orders, Customers, etc.) Sample Answer: \"In my current project I work with approximately 30-40 notebooks organized by layer\n• We have Bronze ingestion notebooks, Silver cleansing/transformation notebooks, Gold aggregation notebooks for reporting, plus shared utility notebooks for config and common functions.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What is the Daily Volume of Data You Process?","type":"concept","a":"• Scale Volume/Day Typical Cluster Small Less than 10 GB 4-8 cores, 16-32 GB RAM Medium 10 GB - 1 TB 16-64 cores, 128-256 GB RAM Large 1 TB - 100 TB 100+ cores, 500 GB+ RAM, Auto-scaling Enterprise 100 TB+ Hundreds of nodes, streaming pipelines Sample Answer: \"We process approximately 500 GB to 2 TB of raw data daily from 15 upstream systems\n• After transformation the Silver/Gold layers are 200-500 GB\n• Peak loads during month-end hit 5 TB.\"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"• What does the current data architecture look like — Medallion/Lakehouse or more traditional ETL?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What do you do in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":" Develop PySpark notebooks  Build ETL pipelines (bronze → silver → gold)  Op(cid:415)mize performance (par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching)  Handle incremental loads (Delta Lake)  Create jobs/workflows  Work with Delta tables","source":"TCS - Interview Questions - Round 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Can you explain your project from source to repor(cid:415)ng?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I am working on an Employee Analy(cid:415)cs project using Azure Data Engineering services\n• The source systems include SQL Server databases, REST APIs, and CSV files from SFTP\n• Azure Data Factory (ADF) orchestrates the data inges(cid:415)on process\n• The raw data is stored in Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 (Bronze layer)\n• Azure Databricks processes the data using PySpark, where we perform data valida(cid:415)on, duplicate removal, null handling, joins, aggrega(cid:415)ons, and business transforma(cid:415)ons\n• The transformed data is stored in Delta tables in the Silver and Gold layers\n• The Gold layer is consumed by Power BI for dashboards and business repor(cid:415)ng\n• I am responsible for developing ADF pipelines, wri(cid:415)ng PySpark notebooks, implemen(cid:415)ng incremental loads, op(cid:415)mizing Spark jobs, monitoring produc(cid:415)on pipelines, and resolving produc(cid:415)on issues.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Medallion Architecture","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture (Bronze/Silver/Gold) is a data design pattern that organizes data into progressive quality layers in a Data Lakehouse\n• Bronze Layer (Raw): • Exact copy of source data, minimal transformation • All data lands here (append-only), serves as audit trail • Format: Delta or Parquet Silver Layer (Cleaned/Conformed): • Data is cleaned, deduplicated, validated • Joins applied, schema enforced • Ready for data science and analytics use cases Gold Layer (Business-level Aggregates): • Aggregated, enriched, business-ready data • Star schema models, KPIs, dashboards • Optimized for reporting tools (Power BI, Tableau) # Bronze: Raw ingest df_raw.write.format('delta').mode('append').save('/bronze/orders') # Silver: Clean and deduplicate df_silver = df_raw.dropDuplicates(['order_id']).filter(col('amount') > 0) df_silver.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/silver/orders') # Gold: Aggregate for reporting df_gold = df_silver.groupBy('region').agg(sum('amount').alias('total_revenue')) df_gold.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').save('/gold/revenue_by_region') SECTION 5: Spark Internals & Optimization","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Medallian Architecture ?","type":"concept","a":"• Medallion Architecture is a layered data design pa(cid:425)ern used in data lakes where data is organized into Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers\n• Bronze stores raw ingested data, Silver contains cleaned and standardized data, and Gold contains aggregated business-ready data for repor(cid:415)ng and analy(cid:415)cs\n• This approach improves data quality, maintainability, and scalability of pipelines","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Why Medallion Architecture?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:33158)(cid:57393) Benefits:  Be(cid:425)er data quality  Easy debugging (trace back to raw data)  Reusable datasets  Scalable pipeline design  Supports incremental processing","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you move on prem data to fabric to delta lake house ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move on-prem data to Microso(cid:332) Fabric Lakehouse, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to connect the source systems securely\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines in Fabric to ingest the data into OneLake in Delta format\n• The data is stored in Bronze layer as raw data, then transformed into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark or CDC-based extrac(cid:415)on to ensure efficiency","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Oracle to fabric ?","type":"concept","a":"• To move data from Oracle to Microso(cid:332) Fabric, I use On-Premises Data Gateway to establish a secure connec(cid:415)on if Oracle is on-prem\n• Then I use Dataflow Gen2 or Data Factory pipelines to extract data from Oracle using SQL queries\n• The data is loaded into OneLake Lakehouse in Delta format, typically in the Bronze layer\n• Later, I transform it into Silver and Gold layers using Spark notebooks or SQL endpoints\n• For incremental loads, I use watermark columns or Oracle CDC to ensure efficient data movement","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Which layer data is ready to use for presenta(cid:415)on purpose","type":"concept","a":"Gold layer is the final curated layer in Medallion Architecture that is op(cid:415)mized and structured for business repor(cid:415)ng and visualiza(cid:415)on tools.","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"As a team lead you have 300 tables in Oracle database, what will be your approach,","type":"concept","a":"• explain your flow\n• If I have 300 tables in Oracle, I will avoid building individual pipelines\n• Instead, I would design a metadata-driven inges(cid:415)on framework where table details like load type, watermark column, and query are stored in a control table\n• I would create a single dynamic ADF or Fabric pipeline that reads metadata and processes all tables using a ForEach loop\n• Data would be ingested into the Bronze layer, then transformed into silver and gold layers using Spark or SQL\n• I would also implement incremental load using watermark or CDC, enable parallel processing for performance, and add monitoring for failure tracking and retries\n• Data skew Data skew occurs when data is unevenly distributed across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons in distributed systems like Spark, causing some tasks to take significantly longer than others\n• It usually happens during joins, groupBy, or shuffle opera(cid:415)ons\n• To resolve it, I use techniques like broadcast join for small tables, sal(cid:415)ng for skewed keys, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning data, enabling Spark adap(cid:415)ve query execu(cid:415)on, and pre-aggrega(cid:415)ng data before joins","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"scenario","name":"Scenario-Based Questions","icon":"🧩","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"SCENARIO: A pipeline that usually completes in 10 hours is still running into the next day. How do you troubleshoot?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Check the ADF/Databricks Monitor + Spark UI — identify which stage/activity is slow.\n2. Spark UI: look for data skew (one task far longer), large shuffle read/write, spill to disk, uneven partitions.\n3. Compare current vs normal run: input volume growth, small-file explosion, lost broadcast (join strategy change), schema changes.\n4. Check cluster health: resource contention (shared cluster), executor loss/GC, autoscaling limits, pool starvation.\n5. Check upstream source latency/backlog and IR throughput.\nRemedies: AQE/skew handling, salt skewed keys, broadcast small tables, repartition, OPTIMIZE small files, scale cluster, fix bad join, restore filters/column pruning."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A notebook that completes in 15 minutes took 50 minutes today. How do you find the root cause?","type":"scenario","a":"• Open the run in Spark UI; find the slow stage and compare task durations → look for skew/spill.\n• Check executor memory/GC and shuffle metrics; look for OOM-ish behavior or spilling.\n• Check cluster: shared usage/contention, autoscaling didn't scale, fewer/larger tasks.\n• Check data: more files/small files, larger volume, changed partitioning, source lag.\n• Check code changes: new join, UDF, missing filter, cartesian, broadcast removed.\n• Fix: repartition/broadcast, enable AQE/Photon, OPTIMIZE, cache reused DataFrames, scale cluster."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Databricks pipeline suddenly fails. What steps do you take to identify and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"1. Open the job run → failed task → read the error/log (driver + executor logs).\n2. Classify: data-related (schema drift, new nulls/bad records), infra (cluster/OOM/network), dependency (upstream table missing), or code (bug).\n3. Reproduce on a small sample; check recent code or source changes (git diff).\n4. Verify upstream data availability, credentials/Key Vault, and table/permission changes.\n5. Apply fix, use repair-and-rerun (only failed tasks) for idempotent pipelines.\n6. Add guardrails: DQ checks, retries, alerts, schema drift handling."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Source data changed structurally (new column added / datatype changed). How do you detect and handle it without breaking pipelines?","type":"scenario","a":"Detect: schema validation step in the pipeline (expected vs actual), or Auto Loader schema evolution / rescued data column.\nHandle:\n• ADF: allow schema drift; parameterize mapping.\n• Ingest raw into Bronze as schema-flexible; add new columns automatically (mergeSchema / autoMerge).\n• For datatype change: cast/map in Silver, keep raw value in Bronze, quarantine incompatible rows.\n• Alert on drift and keep downstream consumers informed; evolve Delta schema explicitly (ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN).\nDesign principle: raw layer tolerant, curated layers strict."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You notice data skew in a large join. How do you confirm and fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"Confirm: Spark UI shows a few tasks with much larger shuffle read and longer duration; one executor overloaded.\nFix:\n• Enable AQE skew join (auto-splits skewed partitions).\n• Salt the skewed key (add random suffix), join, then aggregate.\n• Broadcast the smaller table if it fits.\n• Repartition on a better/more even key or by range.\n• Filter reduce before the join; isolate the hot key and process separately."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Your Delta table has thousands of small files and reads are slow. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Run OPTIMIZE to compact into larger (~1 GB) files; schedule it after loads.\n• Enable Auto Optimize (optimizeWrite + autoCompact) to prevent small files going forward.\n• Use availableNow trigger / larger micro-batches in streaming or foreachBatch with repartition.\n• Adopt Liquid Clustering / right partition strategy; coalesce before writes.\n• Set a target file size and monitor file counts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Duplicate records are appearing in your Silver table. How do you fix it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Deduplicate on business key + recency: ROW_NUMBER() over key ordered by load_ts desc, keep rn=1.\n• Use dropDuplicates on the natural key.\n• For incremental loads, MERGE on the key (whenMatchedUpdate) so re-runs don't insert duplicates.\n• Add a DQ check that fails/alerts if duplicates exceed a threshold.\n• Identify the source (idempotency, retries, CDC reprocessing) and fix upstream."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You need to process millions/billions of records. What's your approach?","type":"scenario","a":"• Store in columnar Delta/Parquet with partitioning/Clustering; avoid CSV/JSON for large scale.\n• Define explicit schemas; filter/aggregate early; select only needed columns.\n• Use broadcast joins for small dims; AQE + skew handling; right-size shuffle partitions.\n• Use Photon; cache reused DataFrames; avoid UDFs.\n• Scale the cluster (workers/VM type) and use autoscaling; use Job clusters.\n• Incremental processing (watermark/MERGE) to avoid full reprocessing."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A memory/OOM error occurs during a large join/aggregation. How do you resolve it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Reduce data early (filter, select needed columns, aggregate before join).\n• Broadcast the small side instead of shuffling the huge side.\n• Increase shuffle partitions to make tasks smaller; use AQE.\n• Fix skew (salting/AQE) so one task doesn't blow up.\n• Use bigger memory VMs / more nodes; avoid collect()/large driver operations.\n• Use persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK) instead of MEMORY_ONLY if needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Compare these two queries and explain when to use each. Q1: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table WHERE salary>5000 GROUP BY emp_id; Q2: SELECT emp_id, SUM(salary) FROM salary_table GROUP BY emp_id HAVING SUM(salary)>5000;","type":"scenario","a":"• Q1 (WHERE) filters individual ROWS before aggregation — only salaries > 5000 are summed per employee.\n• Q2 (HAVING) aggregates ALL rows, then keeps employees whose TOTAL salary > 5000.\nThey give different results. Use WHERE to filter rows cheaply before grouping; use HAVING to filter on aggregate results. You can combine both."},{"q":"SCENARIO: Design a POD-level ADF pipeline that feeds a Databricks job. How do you configure Linked Services, Datasets, and pipeline properties?","type":"scenario","a":"• Linked Services: ADLS (Azure IR), Databricks workspace (with access token from Key Vault), source DB (SHIR if on-prem).\n• Datasets: parameterized source/sink datasets (schema/table/path) so one pipeline handles many tables.\n• Pipeline: Get Metadata/Lookup → ForEach (metadata-driven) → Databricks Notebook activity; pass parameters (run_date, table).\n• Properties: set ForEach degree of parallelism, Copy DIUs, batch count for data flows, retry + timeout, and Tumbling Window trigger.\n• Balance throughput vs cost: use Job clusters, avoid over-parallelizing the source, scale SHIR/DIUs as needed."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A production job reading secrets from Key Vault starts failing intermittently. How do you diagnose it?","type":"scenario","a":"• Check Key Vault access: is the secret still present/permitted; is the managed identity/SPN expired or the secret rotated?\n• Check the Integration Runtime: is the SHIR online, throttled, or hitting network/timeouts?\n• Check pipeline config: correct secret scope/key, correct vault URI, no hard-coded expiry.\n• Look at error messages (AAD auth vs 403 vs timeout) and correlate with time of day (throttling).\n• Fix: renew/rotate credentials, add retry with backoff, monitor Key Vault diagnostics, add alerts."},{"q":"SCENARIO: A Databricks cluster fails to start due to resource limitations. What do you do?","type":"scenario","a":"• Free up resources: terminate idle clusters and release the quota/cores.\n• Request a quota increase (vCPU/cores) in the subscription.\n• Reduce min workers/VM size, or use instance pools / serverless.\n• Check for subnet/network (no available IPs) or policy restrictions.\n• Retry after cleanup; use cluster policies to prevent oversized requests."},{"q":"SCENARIO: You must do real-time analysis on a streaming dataset in Databricks. How?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read with Spark Structured Streaming from Event Hubs/Kafka (Kafka API).\n• Parse JSON (from_json), apply watermark + windowed aggregation, dedupe.\n• Write to a Delta table with checkpointing (exactly-once); use update/append mode.\n• Optionally use DLT streaming tables for managed reliability + expectations.\n• Serve to Power BI/Real-Time Analytics; monitor lag and checkpoint health."},{"q":"What are the common challenges you've faced in pipelines (and fixes)?","type":"scenario","a":"• Data skew → AQE/salting/broadcast.\n• Schema drift → mergeSchema/schema drift/Auto Loader rescued data.\n• Duplicates → dedupe + idempotent MERGE.\n• OOM/memory → filter early, partition, right-size cluster, persist(MEMORY_AND_DISK).\n• Small-file problem → OPTIMIZE / Auto Optimize.\n• Slow large-large joins → broadcast/bucketing.\n• Connection timeouts → retry/backoff, connection pooling, scale IR.\n• Datatype mismatch → explicit schema + validation.\n• Incremental load failures → reliable watermark in a control table.\n• Late-arriving data → overlap window / reprocess."},{"q":"Scenario: 10 CSV files arrive every day at 10 AM.","type":"scenario","a":"Scenario : I have 10 files which arrives at 10.00 AM , employee files – 5 , attendance files – 5 , all are in csv format , no change in schema (same schema) – what would be, I want you to","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If job is running slow, what will you do?","type":"concept","a":"(cid:44934) Steps to troubleshoot:","source":"CTS - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Databricks job is running slow. What will you do?","type":"concept","a":"Steps to troubleshoot:","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Explain RAG to a non-technical person.","type":"concept","a":"• \"Imagine you ask a librarian a ques(cid:415)on\n• Instead of answering from memory, the librarian first searches the library for the most relevant books, reads the necessary informa(cid:415)on, and then gives you an accurate answer\n• RAG works in the same way\n• Instead of relying only on what the AI already knows, it first retrieves relevant informa(cid:415)on from your company's documents or databases and then generates an answer using that informa(cid:415)on\n• This makes the responses more accurate and up to date.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"}]},{"id":"sql","name":"SQL & Query Optimization","icon":"🗃️","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING?","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows BEFORE grouping/aggregation; cannot use aggregate functions.\n• HAVING filters groups AFTER GROUP BY; can use aggregates.\n• A query can have both: WHERE to reduce rows, then GROUP BY, then HAVING to filter groups.\nExample: SELECT emp, SUM(sal) FROM t WHERE dept='IT' GROUP BY emp HAVING SUM(sal) > 5000."},{"q":"Explain INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, CROSS, LEFT SEMI, and LEFT ANTI joins.","type":"concept","a":"• INNER — only matching rows from both tables.\n• LEFT — all left rows + matches; unmatched right = NULL.\n• RIGHT — all right rows + matches; unmatched left = NULL.\n• FULL OUTER — all rows from both; NULLs where no match.\n• CROSS — cartesian product (every combination).\n• LEFT SEMI — left rows with a match in right (no right columns).\n• LEFT ANTI — left rows with NO match in right.\nLEFT JOIN A,B = RIGHT JOIN B,A."},{"q":"CODING: Total revenue per month.","type":"coding","a":"Group by the formatted month and sum amounts.","code":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM') AS year_month,\n       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date,'yyyy-MM')\nORDER BY year_month;"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in both January AND February 2025.","type":"coding","a":"Group by customer and count distinct months; keep those with 2. INTERSECT is an alternative.","code":"SELECT customer_id\nFROM orders\nWHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28'\n  AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1,2)\nGROUP BY customer_id\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2;\n\n-- Alternative\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01'\nINTERSECT\nSELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';"},{"q":"CODING: Customers who ordered in 3 consecutive months.","type":"coding","a":"Use LAG to look back two months and compare the dates.","code":"WITH m AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date) AS mth\n  FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)\n),\nl AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, mth,\n         LAG(mth,1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev1,\n         LAG(mth,2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY mth) AS prev2\n  FROM m\n)\nSELECT DISTINCT customer_id\nFROM l\nWHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2,1) = prev1 AND ADD_MONTHS(prev1,1) = mth;"},{"q":"CODING: Total order amount per customer (join + aggregation).","type":"coding","a":"Inner join customers to orders and sum.","code":"SELECT c.CustomerID, c.Name, SUM(o.Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Customer c\nINNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID\nGROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.Name;"},{"q":"CODING: Delete duplicate records keeping one row.","type":"coding","a":"Assign row numbers per duplicate key and delete rn>1 (or rebuild the table).","code":"WITH cte AS (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer\n)\nDELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n\n-- Delta-friendly rebuild\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n     PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id) rn\n  FROM customer) WHERE rn = 1;"},{"q":"How would you optimize a slow SQL query?","type":"scenario","a":"• Read the execution plan (EXPLAIN) to find the bottleneck (scans, shuffles, spills).\n• Filter as early as possible; avoid SELECT * (select needed columns only).\n• Ensure joins/filters use indexed or partition columns.\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE (e.g., YEAR(date)=2025) — use range predicates for partition pruning.\n• Use proper join types and broadcast hints for small tables.\n• Replace correlated subqueries with joins; use CTEs to avoid repeated work.\n• Update statistics; partition large tables; avoid unnecessary DISTINCT.\n• Compare plans/times before and after."},{"q":"How does partition pruning work and how do you enable it?","type":"concept","a":"Partition pruning means the engine reads only the partitions matching the filter, skipping the rest.\n• Filter directly on the partition column with range/equality predicates (WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31').\n• Avoid wrapping the partition column in functions (WHERE YEAR(order_date)=2025 disables pruning).\n• In Spark/Delta, this avoids reading irrelevant files/folders → big speedup."},{"q":"What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?","type":"concept","a":"• UNION — removes duplicate rows (implies a distinct/sort → more expensive).\n• UNION ALL — keeps all rows including duplicates (faster).\nUse UNION ALL by default and de-duplicate only when needed."},{"q":"What is a CTE and when do you use it?","type":"concept","a":"A Common Table Expression (WITH clause) is a named temporary result set that makes complex queries readable and avoids repeating subqueries. It's not materialized by default in many engines (each reference may re-execute), but improves clarity and can be chained. Use for multi-step logic: dedupe → join → aggregate."},{"q":"CODING: Find the manager of each employee using a self join (SQL).","type":"coding","a":"Self join employee to itself; LEFT join so top-level employees (null manager) are kept.","code":"SELECT e.empid AS employee_id, e.empname AS employee_name,\n       e.mgrid AS manager_id, m.empname AS manager_name\nFROM employee e\nLEFT JOIN employee m ON e.mgrid = m.empid;"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions and how do they differ from window functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Aggregates (SUM/COUNT/AVG/MIN/MAX) collapse multiple rows into one row per group — you lose individual rows.\n• Window functions compute across a set of rows but KEEP every row (add a computed column).\nAggregate example: SUM(sal) per dept. Window example: SUM(sal) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) alongside each row."},{"q":"How do you implement SCD Type 1 and Type 2 in SQL?","type":"coding","a":"Type 1 overwrites; Type 2 expires the old row and inserts a new version with effective dates + current flag.","code":"-- SCD Type 1 (overwrite)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s ON t.customer_id=s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.name=s.name, t.email=s.email, t.updated_at=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id,name,email,updated_at) VALUES (s.customer_id,s.name,s.email,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n\n-- SCD Type 2 (expire + insert)\nMERGE INTO dim_customer t USING staging s\nON t.customer_id = s.customer_id AND t.is_current = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (t.name<>s.name OR t.city<>s.city) THEN\n  UPDATE SET t.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, t.is_current = 0;\n\nINSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id,name,city,start_date,end_date,is_current)\nSELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1\nFROM staging s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id=s.customer_id AND d.is_current=1);"},{"q":"How would you find the Nth highest salary?","type":"concept","a":"Use DENSE_RANK (handles ties) or a correlated subquery.\n• Window: SELECT * FROM (SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) rk FROM emp) WHERE rk = N.\n• Subquery: SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp) [repeat for Nth].\nDENSE_RANK is cleaner and scales to any N."},{"q":"Window Func(cid:415)ons (ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK)","type":"concept","a":"• Window func(cid:415)ons perform calcula(cid:415)ons across related rows without collapsing the data\n• Example data: Employee Dept Salary A IT 10000 B IT 9000 C IT 9000 D IT 8000 ROW_NUMBER() Assigns a unique number to each row\n• Salary Row_Number 10000 1 9000 2 9000 3 8000 4 from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import row_number window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"Dept\").orderBy(\"Salary\") df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(window)) RANK() Duplicate values receive the same rank, and the next rank is skipped\n• Salary Rank 10000 1 9000 2 9000 2 Salary Rank 8000 4 DENSE_RANK() Duplicate values receive the same rank, but there are no gaps\n• Salary Dense Rank 10000 1 9000 2 9000 2 8000 3","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Inner, Left & Outer Join — Worked Example","type":"concept","a":"Sample Tables Table A: Orders order_id customer_id amount 101 C1 500 102 C2 300 103 C3 800 104 C5 200 Table B: Customers customer_id customer_name C1 Alice C2 Bob C4 Charlie INNER JOIN Result INNER JOIN: A.customer_id = B.customer_id Returns ONLY rows where customer_id exists in BOTH tables. order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob Excluded: Order 103 (C3 not in Customers), Order 104 (C5 not in Customers), C4 Charlie (no orders) LEFT JOIN Result LEFT JOIN: All rows from Orders, match from Customers (NULL if none) order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob 103 | C3 | 800 | NULL ← C3 not in Customers 104 | C5 | 200 | NULL ← C5 not in Customers FULL OUTER JOIN Result FULL OUTER JOIN: All rows from BOTH tables order_id | customer_id | amount | customer_name 101 | C1 | 500 | Alice 102 | C2 | 300 | Bob 103 | C3 | 800 | NULL ← C3 has no matching customer 104 | C5 | 200 | NULL ← C5 has no matching customer NULL | C4 | NULL | Charlie ← C4 has no orders","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"❌ What Breaks?","type":"concept","a":"• Source sends: { order_id, customer_id, amount, discount_code } ← new column added Your Delta table has: order_id, customer_id, amount Spark throws: AnalysisException: Column 'discount_code' not found in schema Solution 1: mergeSchema (Easiest) # In your writeStream, add mergeSchema option df_stream.writeStream \\ .format('delta') \\ .option('checkpointLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/orders/') \\ .option('mergeSchema', 'true') \\ # ← This is the fix! .outputMode('append') \\ .toTable('silver.orders') # mergeSchema automatically adds the new column 'discount_code' to # the Delta table\n• Old rows will have NULL for this new column\n• Solution 2: autoMerge (Spark Config) # Enable schema auto-merge globally spark.conf.set('spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled', 'true') # Now any write (streaming or batch) will automatically merge new columns Solution 3: Explicit Schema Evolution with Autoloader # With Autoloader, use schemaEvolutionMode df_stream = spark.readStream \\ .format('cloudFiles') \\ .option('cloudFiles.format', 'json') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaLocation', '/mnt/checkpoints/schema/') \\ .option('cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode', 'addNewColumns') \\ # Options: 'addNewColumns' | 'rescue' | 'failOnNewColumns' | 'none' .load('/mnt/raw/orders/') # 'rescue': Puts unexpected columns into a _rescued_data JSON column # 'failOnNewColumns': Deliberately fail and alert when schema changes # 'addNewColumns': Automatically expand schema to include new columns 💡 Best Practice for Production Use 'failOnNewColumns' in strict environments to detect unexpected schema changes early\n• Use 'addNewColumns' or mergeSchema='true' in flexible pipelines where schema drift is expected\n• Always log schema changes and alert the data team when new columns appear\n• Schema Drift Schema drift refers to the phenomenon where the structure of your incoming data changes over time — new columns are added, columns are removed, data types change\n• This is one of the most common challenges in real data engineering projects\n• Types of Schema Drift Type Example & Impact New column added Source adds 'loyalty_points' column\n• Pipeline fails if not handled — breaks schema validation\n• Column removed Source stops sending 'middle_name'\n• Downstream queries on that column will return errors\n• Data type change 'order_amount' changes from INT to DECIMAL","source":"ACCENTURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are Transformations used in Data Flows.","type":"concept","a":"A. Derived Column--Deriving new columns by writing expressions Lookup---Comparing two inputs finding matching non matching based on common column(left outer join) Alter row---categorizing the rows to perform upsert operations, which row needs to insert and update and delete Join---Joining of two inputs like inner, left, fullouter, customer Conditional Split---base don condition data will be spitted Ex: Male and female two outputs Filter: filtering of data by putting filter condition Assertions--Performing some data validations by specifying data rules Windows--to find duplicate or [performing rank, rownumber, running total Aggregate: performing aggregate and group by Union: appending of data into one output","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the Transformations used in Data Flows","type":"concept","a":"• DERIVED COLUMN: Deriving New Columns or adding new columns in Dataflow by writing of Expressions Ex: Firstname,LastName column,we can derive new column Full Name by Concat both firstname,lastname JOIN: If you want to perform all kind of join Inner join, Left outer Join, Right Outer Join..customer join in between two tables Ex:-Customer, Customer Address join both based on Location ID SELECT: Limiting Number of columns in Dataflow Ex: 10 columns from source, But want to load only 5 columns, Then use select transformation Limit only required columns FILTER: Applying filter on data coming from source Ex: Load only city= hyderabad customers into target Page | 4 CONDITIONAL SPLIT: I have one input customer data Male and female data and transgender data I want to load above customer data into 3 tables Male customer Female Customer Trans gender UNION: Appending data from multiple inputs into one output ex: Emphyd EmpBang combine all data from both data sources WINDOWS: If you want to apply Row_Number, Rank , Dense_Rank functionalities use this windows Ex: Finding duplicates using rownumber partition and order by and filtering using Filter Transformation AGGREGATE: Performing Aggregate operations includes Min , Max, Sum, Avg\n• Ex: Each gender wise yearly income, avg income\n• SORT: Just sorting order of data in acesending or descending based on column Ex :Sort data based on productid ASSERTION: Applying data validation rules on incoming data to identify unique, duplicates, condition met or not Ex: Phone number contains 10 digits or not LOOKUP: comparing two inputs based on common column and finding matched and non matched records(Left Outer Join) Ex: Comparing customer source data Destination Dimcustomer based on customerid ALTER ROW: categorizing records to perform upsert operations it includes insert, update, delete BRANCHING: It will take one input and giving multiple outputs of data customer data source table contains 10 rows, i want to load same data into multiple tables customer---dimcustomer customer---dimcustomerbackup customer---dimcustomercopy EXIST: Comparing two source inputs and verifying a record from first source input to second source input a custid in customer table exist or not exist in other table dimcustomer Page | 5","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Mapping Dataflows and Warngling Datflows","type":"concept","a":"• Mapping Data Flows: Use Mapping Data Flows to visually transform data without having to write any code\n• You can focus on the transformations and logic, while Azure Data Factory does the heavy lifting behind the scenes\n• It translates your transformations and logic to code that runs on scaled-out Azure Databricks clusters for maximum performance\n• A Mapping Data Flow can look something like this: The focus in this interface is on the flow\n• You can quickly identify your sources, transformations, branches, joins, and sinks\n• To see the actual data, you need to enable Data Flow Debug and preview the data per transformation\n• Wrangling Data Flows: Use Wrangling Data Flows to visually explore and prepare datasets using the Power Query Online mashup editor\n• You can focus on the modeling and logic, while Azure Data Factory does the heavy lifting behind the scenes\n• It translates the underlying M code to code that runs on a managed Spark environment for maximum performance\n• A Wrangling Data Flow can look something like this: The focus in this interface is on the data\n• You can quickly see what the final dataset will look like\n• To see the actual sources, transformations, and joins, you need to go through the list of Applied Steps","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Its scalability is good as you can add It is having low scalability as compared to","type":"concept","a":"up to n different nodes Map Reduce","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Azure Data Bricks Runtime Integration","type":"concept","a":"• Data bricks Runtime ML is a variant of Data bricks Runtime that adds multiple popular machine learning libraries, including Tensor Flow, Keras, PyTorch, and XGBoost\n• Photon\n• Photon is the Azure Data bricks native vectorized query engine that runs SQL workloads faster and reduces your total cost per workload\n• Page | 12","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"PAAS--- Platform as a service {Operating system, SQL server….}","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is Elastic pool, what are different compute Tiers?","type":"concept","a":"Group of azure SQL data bases to share resources among all","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Distributions?","type":"concept","a":"• Application connect issue T-SQL commands a control node\n• The control node hosts the MPP Engine, which optimise queries for parallel processing and then passes operations to compute node to do their work in parallel\n• The compute nodes store all users data in Azure storage and run the parallel Queries Control Node: It will act lick human brain it will take request from application and passing to MPP MPP (Massive Parallel Processing): Splitting of Query into multiple smaller queries and execute queries parallel\n• Compute node: It is place where each query will execute\n• 0-60 compute nodes Page | 26 Distribution Method: Based on Distribution method created tables, data will be distributed across nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"One Database to Another Database","type":"concept","a":"create master key encryption by password='Eclasess@456' --Creating a New password create database scoped credential ploybase --Creating a New Database with identity='eclasessgrp', ---This is the Username and password from Source side secret='Eclasess@123' create external data source ploy -----Creating a New external Datasource with ( type=rdbms, location='eclasess1.database.windows.net', -----This is Server of the Source side database_name='source1', credential=ploybase) create external table empext -----Creating a new External tables ( id int, person varchar(100), salary float) with( data_source=ploy, schema_name='dbo', object_name='emp' ------The table from the source side ) select * from empext Ploybase from Blob storage to SQL Database create master key encryption by password='eclasess@123' --- Creating a New Password create database scoped credential ploybase ---Creating a New Database with identity='avinash', -----You can give a any name secret='XabXvCkBrzcio24wEMxhyHkj1ZX7pK19SfUe3/Ux1xj65tr8LKA+HFSCOurjaDgT0EE1mP KcyYlM+AStQwpk+Q==' --------it is of secrete of blob storage available in the ACCESS KEY create external data source ploy_source ---creating a external data source with ( type=hadoop, location='wasbs://eclasess456@eclasess123.blob.core.windows.net', -----(in this eclasess456 is a Container name and eclasess123 is a Blob storage name) credential=ploybase ) Page | 28 create external file format file1 -- creating a external format with( format_type=delimitedtext, format_options(field_terminator=',',first_row=2)) create external table product1 ( eno int, enmae varchar(10), epro varchar(10)) with( data_source=ploy_source, file_format=file1, location='/product/') select * from product1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is master and transaction tables?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Transaction table: This tables gets impacted with insert or update delete we perform business transactions Master table: This will be read and used during transactions, but this table will not get impacted for inset and update or delete\n• A transaction is an activity performed by entities (master tables) within the system\n• These activities are captured in transaction tables and usually, these transaction entries have foreign keys to master records\n• Transaction tables are designed to store events in the system\n• Page | 29","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between where and having clause?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ WHERE: Where clause is used to filter records. where clause cannot be used in aggregate function. where clause used to filter the records from table or used while joining more than one table only those records will be extracted who are satisfying the specified condition in WHERE clause\n• It can be used with select, update, delete statement. ➢ HAVING: Having clause is used to filter the records from the groups based on the given condition in the having clause those groups who will satisfy the given condition will appear in the final result having clause can only be used with select statement","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are distinct and top key word in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"• ➢ Distinct statement: It is used to return only distinct or different values inside a table, a column contains many duplicate values and sometime you only want to list the different values ➢ Top keyword: Advertisements\n• The SQL TOP clause is used to fetch a TOP N number or X percent records from a table\n• Note − All the databases do not support the TOP clause\n• For example MySQL supports the LIMIT clause to fetch limited number of records while Oracle uses the ROWNUM command to fetch a limited number of records","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is sql query order of writing","type":"concept","a":"• Six Operations to order of Writing: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, and ORDER BY\n• By using examples, we will explain the execution order of the six most common operations or pieces in an SQL query\n• Page | 31","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is order of execution of SQL query?","type":"concept","a":"• Six Operations to Order: to order of executing: FROM,WHERE,GROUPBY,HAVING,SELECT,ORDERBY By using examples, we will explain the execution order of the six most common operations or pieces in an SQL query\n• Because the database executes query components in a specific order, it's helpful for the developer to know this order","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are aggregate functions in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"• COUNT Function: COUNT function is used to Count the number of rows in a database table. \n• SUM Function: Sum function is used to calculate the sum of all selected columns. \n• AVG Function: The AVG function is used to calculate the average value of the numeric type. ","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are constrains available in SQL?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server contains the following 6 types of constraints:","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is difference between the primary key and unique key?","type":"concept","a":"• Both Primary key and Unique Key are used to uniquely define of a row in a table\n• Primary Key creates a clustered index of the column whereas a Unique creates an unclustered index of the column \n• Primary Key doesn't allow NULL value, however a Unique Key does allow one NULL value","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is view and materilized view?","type":"concept","a":"• A view uses a query to pull data from the underlying tables\n• A materialized view is a table on disk that contains the result set of a query\n• Materialized views are primarily used to increase application performance when it isn't feasible or desirable to use a standard view with indexes applied to it","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are difference between table variable and temp tables, tables and cte?","type":"concept","a":"• A. . \n• This biggest difference is that a CTE can only be used in the current query scope whereas a temporary table or table variable can exist for the entire duration of the session allowing you to perform many different DML operations against them","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"what is the difference between storaged procedure and and functions?","type":"concept","a":"• A: The function must return a value but in Stored Procedure it is optional\n• Even a procedure can return zero or n values\n• Functions can have only input parameters for it whereas Procedures can have input or output parameters\n• Functions can be called from Procedure whereas Procedures cannot be called from a Function Page | 32","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of index available and difference between those?","type":"concept","a":"• Expression-based indexes efficiently evaluate queries with the indexed Expression. ➢ Unique and Non-Unique indexes ➢ Clustered and Non- Clustered indexes ➢ Partitioned and Non Partitioned indexes ➢ Expression-Based indexes Difference between those: • In addition to enforcing the uniqueness of data values, • A unique index can also be used to improve data retrieval performance during query processing\n• Non-unique indexes are not used to enforce constraints on the tables with which they are associated. • In Non-Clustered index leaf nodes are not the actual data itself rather they only contains included columns • In Clustered index leaf nodes are actual data itself • A non partitioned index is a single index object that refers to all rows in a partitioned table\n• Non partitioned indexes are always created as independent index objects in a single table space, even if the table data partitions span multiple table spaces. • When you create an index for a partitioned table, the index is a partitioned index by default unless you create one of the following types of indexes: • A unique index where the index key does not include all of the table- partitioning columns A spatial index","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of join available and syntax difference between each one?","type":"concept","a":"• A SQL Join statement is used to combine data or rows from two or more tables based on a common field between them\n• Different types of Joins are INNER JOIN: The INNER JOIN keyword selects all rows from both the tables as long as the condition satisfies.This keyword will create the result-set by combining all rows from both the tables where the condition satisfies i.e value of the common field will be same. syn: SELECT table1.column1,table1.column2,table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 INNER JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column LEFT JOIN: This join returns all the rows of the table on the left side of the join and matching rows for the table on the right side of join\n• The rows for which there is no matching row on right side, the result-set will contain null\n• LEFT JOIN is also known as LEFT OUTER JOIN Syn: SELECT table1.column1, table1.column2, table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 LEFT JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column;\n• RIGHT JOIN: RIGHT JOIN is similar to LEFT JOIN\n• This join returns all the rows of the table on the right side of the join and matching rows for the table on the left side of join\n• The rows for which there is no matching row on left side, the result-set will contain null\n• Page | 33 RIGHT JOIN is also known as RIGHT OUTER JOIN syn: SELECT table1.column1,table1.column2,table2.column1,\n• FROM table1 RIGHT JOIN table2 ON table1.matching_column = table2.matching_column FULL JOIN: FULL JOIN creates the result-set by combining result of both LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN\n• The result-set will contain all the rows from both the tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to find Duplicate records in Employee Table","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 SELECT empno,ename,sal, count(*)FROM employee GROUP BY empno,ename,sal having count(*)>1 ---Solution2 select *, row_number() over (partition by empno order by empno) as rownumber from employee","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to remove duplicate Records in Employee Table using CTE","type":"concept","a":"with ABC as ( SELECT*, row_number() over (PARTITION BY empno ORDER BY empno) as RowNumber FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM ABC WHERE RowNumber>1","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Find 2nd highest Salary employees in emp Table using CTE","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT SAL, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SAL DESC) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT TOP 2 SAL FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2 ---solution2 WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT *, Page | 34 DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SAL desc) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT ename,sal FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to find 2nd Highest salary Employees in emp Table with out using rank and cte","type":"concept","a":"use subquery ---Solution1 SELECT ename,sal FROM employee e1 WHERE 1=(SELECT COUNT (distinct sal) FROM employee e2 WHERE e2.sal > e1.sal) ---Solution2 SELECT TOP 1 sal FROM ( SELECT DISTINCT TOP 2 SAL FROM Employee ORDER BY SAL DESC ) result ORDER BY SAL ---Solution3 SELECT MAX(Sal) From Employee WHERE Sal < ( SELECT Max(Sal) FROM Employee) ---Solution4 find two top salaries select distinct top 2 sal from EMPLOYEE order by Sal desc","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"How to Find 2nd highest Salary employees by each department using CTE","type":"concept","a":"WITH RESULT AS ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (partition by deptno ORDER BY SAL desc) AS TOPRANK FROM EMPLOYEE ) SELECT * FROM RESULT WHERE TOPRANK = 2","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Find Employees from employees tables, doen’t belongs to any department in dept","type":"concept","a":"table ---Solutio1 select * from employee e right outer join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where e.deptno is null ---Solution2 select e.ename,dname,loc from employee e right join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where e.deptno is null ---Solution3 select e.deptno,ename,dname,loc from employee e left join dept d on e.deptno=d.deptno where d.deptno is null","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to make combination of cricket match between below countries","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 select a.team+' Vs '+b.team from abc a,abc b where a.team<b.team order by a.team ---Solution2 SELECT * FROM abc A JOIN abc B on A.team<>B.team","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to find all employee having sal greater then the average salary of each","type":"concept","a":"dept select e.ename,e.sal,e.deptno,s.salavg from employee e, (select deptno,avg(sal) salavg from employee group by deptno)s where e.deptno=s.deptno and e.sal>s.salavg","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Write query to find employee and manager name in below table","type":"concept","a":"---Solution1 select e.eno,e.ename,m.ename managername from employee_test e,employee_test Page | 36 m where e.mgrid=m.eno ---Solution2 select e.ename,m.ename as manager from employee_test e left join employee_test m on e.mgrid=m.eno https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/samples/adventureworks-install-configure?view=sql- server-ver16&tabs=ssms ----Adventure scripts link.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Connecting the multiples files.","type":"concept","a":"• ======Adventure scripts =============== select * from dimproductsubcategory select * from dimproductcategory select * from FactInternetSales select * from dimproduct select * from DimProductSubcategory ---1.Display each category wise totalsalesamount select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName ------2.Display 2nd highest sales product with cte as ( select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount, DENSE_RANK() over (order by sum(salesamount) desc) as rank from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName ) select * from cte where rank =2 ----3.display product names dont have any sales select EnglishProductCategoryName, sum(salesamount) as totalsalesamount from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey left join FactInternetSales as f on f.ProductKey=p.ProductKey group by c.EnglishProductCategoryName having sum(salesamount) is null ------4.display list of products belongs to categoryname is \"BIkes\" select p.EnglishProductName, c.EnglishProductCategoryName from DimProductCategory as c join DimProductSubcategory as s on c.ProductCategoryKey=s.ProductCategoryKey join DimProduct as p on p.ProductSubcategoryKey=s.ProductSubcategoryKey Page | 37 where EnglishProductCategoryName='Bikes' To Know common column of the two tables select name from sys.columns where object_id=object_id('dbo.tablename1') intersect select name from sys.columns where object_id = object_id('dbo.tablename2') 44a4bxedxPwGQtSnEzGzdejnCdtrDJqkJ6uqWwGbXmdx5zrjGdHivmr9pyd7x8rVQMgumw GPCfuwseieCg8tuN5jNNeMJPU – This is the link of Emp and Dept scripts\n• SELECT VIEW: select * from sys.databases --display all database select * from sys.tables --display all tables select * from cons.sys.tables --display only given database name select * from sys.views -- display all the views select * from sys.columns --display all the coloumsin all database select * from sys.columns where name like '%emp%' --display only given table name","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"STORED PROCEDURE: A group of statements that to be existing.","type":"concept","a":"-- INNER PARAMETERS create procedure int_par1(@ename varchar(100),@var int)-- create a proce name as begin select empno,ename from emp where @ename=ename And @var=empno--on what table values you want end exec int_par1 'smith',7369 OUTPUT PARAMETER create procedure out_Par6(@ename varchar(100),@var int output)-- give name output as begin select @var=count(Empno) from emp where @ename=job end declare @totalcount2 varchar(100)-- give the specfic name of the output execute out_par6 'clerk', @totalcount2 out print @totalcount2 RETURN PARAMETERS create procedure out_Par7 as begin return (select count(Empno) from emp) end declare @totalcount3 int execute @totalcount3 = out_Par7 print @totalcount3 Page | 38 select * from emp create procedure sum2(@var1 int,@var2 int,@var3 int) as begin declare @sum int set @sum=(@var1+@var2+@var3) select @sum as colunm end exec sum2 1000,2000,3000","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SET OPREATORS: Set operators are used to combine the result of 2 or more tables as a single","type":"concept","a":"• set of values. create table emp_hyd( empid int, personname varchar(100), salary float ) insert into emp_hyd values(100,'name1',1000),(102,'name2',1000),(103,'name3',3000),(104,'name4',4000) select * from emp_hyd create table emp_pune( empid int, personname varchar(100), salary float ) insert into emp_pune values(100,'name1',1000),(107,'name7',7000),(108,'name8',8000),(109,'name9',9000) select * from emp_pune --operators --union: it will return the values but duplicate will not execute select * from emp_hyd union select * from emp_pune -- union all: it will return values and it will execute the duplicates also select * from emp_hyd union all select * from emp_pune -- intersect: it will execute only the common value select * from emp_hyd intersect select * from emp_pune -- EXPECT :it will return values from left hand table which are not right hand table select * from emp_hyd except select * from emp_pune select * from emp_pune except select * from emp_hyd Page | 39 16\n• TRIGGERS: --trigger; it is automatically perform --dml tigger:in response to upadte, insert, delete select * from res create trigger res_trig on res for insert as print 'hi' insert into res values('ai05',54,'ytre') --two magic create trigger res_trig2 on res for insert,update,delete as begin select * from inserted-- it will consider both update,insert select * from deleted-- it will consider delete end insert into res values ('aio6',45,'poiu') insert into res values ('aio8',45,'poiu') delete from res where aircrafe_code='aio6' create table res1( aircrafe_code varchar(100), seat_no int, clasess varchar(100)) -- if seat>25 fro other new table alter trigger res_trig2 on res for insert, update,delete as begin declare @seat_no int select @seat_no=seat_no from inserted if @seat_no>25 begin insert into res1 select * from inserted print 'it is suceed' end else print ' it is not' end insert into res values('aio10',21,'poiuy') insert into res values('aio10',29,'poiuy') select * from res1 --delete triggers create trigger trig_del1 on res1 instead of delete Page | 40 as print 'it have been deleted' set nocount on delete from res1 --ddl trigger-- in respones yo create,alter,drop --database scoped ddl trigger create trigger table_trig--it is a creating a database on database for create_table,alter_table,drop_procedure as print 'table is created' create table t1 ( col1 int, col2 int) create trigger table_drop on database for drop_table,alter_table as print 'table is deleted' rollback drop table t1 --server scope ddl trigger -- it is a servers create trigger server_trig on all server for create_database,drop_database as print 'server has been created' create database data_base drop trigger res_trig drop trigger table_trig on database drop trigger data_base on all server","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"SUBQUERY: A sub query is a form of a SQL Statement that appears inside another sql","type":"concept","a":"• statement\n• It is also treate as nested query\n• The statement containing a Subquery is called Parent statements \n• The parent statement uses the rows returned by the subquery. create table sales_orders( order_no varchar(10), client_no varchar (10)) insert into sales_orders values('poo1','coo1'), ('poo2','coo2'),('poo3','coo1'),('poo4','coo3'),('poo5','coo3'), ('poo6','coo3'),('poo7','coo4') create table cilents( client_no varchar(20), person_name varchar(10), quality numeric (10)) insert into cilents values('coo1','name1',1),('coo2','name2',1), Page | 41 ('coo3','name3',1),('coo4','name4',1), ('coo5','name1',1),('coo6','name6',1) select * from sales_orders select * from cilents select * from cilents where person_name='name3' -- it is form of a sql statement appears inside the another sql statement select * from sales_orders where client_no=(select client_no from cilents where person_name='name3') select * from sales_orders where client_no not in (select client_no from cilents where person_name='name3')","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"GROUPING AND HAVING :","type":"concept","a":"• Group By: It is used to group rows based on distinct values that exist for specified columns ie it creates a data set containing several set of records grouped together based on a condition\n• Having : It is always used in conjuction with group by, It is imposes a condition on the group by clause which further filters the group created by the group by clause. --groups by create table sales_pro( product_name varchar(100), product_num varchar(10), quality_num int, quality_dis int ) insert into sales_pro values('pd1','pooo1',10,10), ('pd1','pooo1',9,9),('pd1','pooo1',8,8), ('pd2','pooo2',7,7), ('pd2','pooo2',8,8), ('pd3','pooo3',10,10), ('pd4','pooo4',5,5), ('pd4','pooo4',6,6), ('pd5','pooo5',9,9), ('pd5','pooo5',8,8), ('pd1','pooo1',10,10) select *from sales_pro select product_num,sum(quality_num) as \"total quality\" from sales_pro group by product_num -- group by it add rows having a same values in a colunm -- having condition select product_num,sum(quality_num) as \"total quality\" from sales_pro group by product_num having product_num in('pooo1','pooo5')-- this HAVING gives the filiters 19.FUNCTIONS: --functions: it is a return values --scaler function:it is a single value create function fun_sum(@var1 int,@var2 int,@var3 int) returns int as begin declare @sum int set @sum=@var1+@var2+@var3 Page | 42 return @sum end select dbo. fun_sum (100,200,300) as total alter function nrt_fun(@customerid varchar(100)) returns varchar(100) as begin declare @nrt_fun1 varchar(100) select @nrt_fun1=country from Customers where customerid=@customerid return @nrt_fun1 end select dbo.nrt_fun('usa') --table function retuen values select * from res create function tab_fun(@seat_no int) returns table as return (select * from res where @seat_no=seat_no) select * from tab_fun(87)","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"TRANSACTIONS: A SET OF T-SQL STATEMENTS TO BE EXECUTED AS A SINGLE","type":"concept","a":"UNIT create table emp_tran( empid int, person_name varchar(100), salary int) select * from emp_tran --explicit transaction: it will commit and rollback begin tran-- only in this explicit insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) declare @row_count int select @row_count=count(*) from emp_tran if @row_count <=10 commit tran else rollback tran select count(*) from emp_tran truncate table emp_tran --implict tran: it is like explicit set implicit_transactions on--: imp command insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) declare @row_count int select @row_count=count(*) from emp_tran if @row_count <=10 commit tran else rollback tran Page | 44 select count(*) from emp_tran truncate table emp_tran --autocomtt tran & open transbegin tran insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) --isolation level --isolation level read and uncommitted set transaction ISOLATION level READ UNCOMMITTED insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) set transaction ISOLATION level READ COMMITTED insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) set transaction ISOLATION level SERIALIZABLE insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) --snapshot use master alter database [proce] set allow_snapshot_isolation on use [proce] set transaction isolation level snapshot begin tran insert into emp_tran values (100,'name1',1000),(200,'name2',2000),(100,'name1',1000), (100,'name1',1000),(100,'name1',1000) select * from emp_tran","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"QUEREY OPEATORS","type":"concept","a":"• -- TO HELP T-QUERIES PERTAINING TO SEARCHING AND SORTING ACTIVITIES, -- WE GENERALLY CREATE INDEXES CREATE TABLE EMP_DETAILS ( EMP_ID INT, EMP_NAME VARCHAR(20), EMP_SAL INT ) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1001,'SUNNY',1000) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1002,'PRIYANKA',1200) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1003,'RAVI',1060) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1004,'JEFF',2000) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1005,'JONATHAN',1220) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1006,'PETER',1044) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1007,'AMITABH',1456) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1008,'RAJENDRA',2100) Page | 45 INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(1009,'ANUSHKA',1220) INSERT INTO EMP_DETAILS VALUES(2001,'KAREENA',1880) SELECT * FROM EMP_DETAILS Index: It is used by queries to find data form table quickly\n• Indexs are created on tables & view , is very similar to an index that we find in a book. create index emp_details_index on emp_details(emp_name) SELECT EMP_NAME FROM EMP_DETAILS--index scan create nonclustered index emp_detalis_index_v2 on emp_details(emp_name) include(EMP_ID,EMP_SAL) select * from EMP_DETAILS where EMP_NAME='sunny'--index seek select * from EMP_DETAILS where EMP_NAME='xyz'--index seek","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Implicit Cursors: These cursor created by SQL server by default when select statement is","type":"concept","a":"executed.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Source 2 — Read Customer table from Azure SQL DB","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Filter — Remove rows where TotalRevenue < 0","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Aggregate — Group by Region, sum TotalRevenue","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Sink — Write results into Synapse Analytics table","type":"concept","a":"• 💡 Tip: When asked about Data Flow: always walk through Source → Transform → Sink\n• Mention that you used Debug mode to test, and that Data Flows run on auto-scaling Spark clusters\n• Common Data Flow Transformations to Know • Derived Column — Add or modify columns using expressions • Aggregate — GROUP BY with SUM, COUNT, AVG • Join — INNER, LEFT OUTER, RIGHT OUTER between two streams • Lookup — Similar to join but optimized for reference/dimension tables • Filter — Remove rows based on a condition • Select — Choose which columns to keep or rename • Conditional Split — Like a WHERE clause that routes rows to different paths • Flatten — Expand arrays/nested JSON into rows • Window — Rank, Row Number, Lead/Lag functions","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"What is Late Arriving Data?","type":"concept","a":"• Late arriving data means records that arrive after the expected processing window\n• For example: a transaction that happened on Monday but arrives in your system on Wednesday\n• Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 3 Example: You run a daily pipeline at midnight\n• A payment event from 11:58 PM arrives at","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Go to Databricks SQL → Alerts","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Create a query (e.g., SELECT COUNT(*) as failed_records FROM errors WHERE date =","type":"concept","a":"current_date())","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Employee and A(cid:425)endance Tables","type":"concept","a":"• \"I have Employee and A(cid:425)endance tables\n• How will you cleanse the data?\" Employee EmpID EmpName Role Manager A(cid:425)endance EmpID EmpName Date Loca(cid:415)on LoginTime LogoutTime Sample Answer: \"For the Employee table:  Remove duplicate Employee IDs using dropDuplicates()  Replace null employee names with 'Unknown' or another business-approved default  Validate mandatory fields For the A(cid:425)endance table:  Remove records where both LoginTime and LogoutTime are null  Instead of dele(cid:415)ng them permanently, store them in a Reject or Quaran(cid:415)ne table for future inves(cid:415)ga(cid:415)on\"","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Customers with no orders or whose latest order is older than 30 days should be","type":"concept","a":"returned.\" SELECT c.CustomerID, c.CustomerName FROM Customer c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID GROUP BY c.CustomerID, c.CustomerName HAVING MAX(o.OrderDate) IS NULL OR MAX(o.OrderDate) < DATEADD(DAY, -30, GETDATE());","source":"Capgemini - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Explain Joins in sql","type":"concept","a":"Returns Join Type Matching rows only INNER JOIN All le(cid:332) rows + matching right rows LEFT JOIN All right rows + matching le(cid:332) rows RIGHT JOIN All rows from both tables FULL OUTER JOIN Cartesian product CROSS JOIN Table joined with itself SELF JOIN","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)mize sql queries","type":"concept","a":"• To op(cid:415)mize SQL queries, I reduce data scanning by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, use indexing on join and filter columns, avoid func(cid:415)ons on indexed fields, push filters early, prefer EXISTS over IN for large datasets, and analyze execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks\n• I also use par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and avoid unnecessary opera(cid:415)ons like DISTINCT or correlated subqueries when possible a)\n• Select only required columns (avoid SELECT *) (cid:57872) Bad: SELECT * FROM Employee; ✔ Good: SELECT emp_id, name FROM Employee; (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Reduces I/O and memory usage b)\n• Use proper indexing  Create indexes on: o JOIN keys o WHERE clause columns o ORDER BY columns Example: CREATE INDEX idx_emp_dept ON Employee(dept_id); c).Avoid func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns (cid:57872) Bad: WHERE YEAR(join_date) = 2024; ✔ Good: WHERE join_date >= '2024-01-01' AND join_date < '2025-01-01'; (cid:36400)(cid:36401)(cid:36402)(cid:36403) Allows index usage d)\n• Filter data early (push filters) (cid:57872) Bad: SELECT * FROM A JOIN B ON A.id = B.id; ✔ Good: SELECT * FROM ( SELECT * FROM A WHERE status = 'ac(cid:415)ve' ) A JOIN B ON A.id = B.id; e)\n• Use EXISTS instead of IN (for large datasets) (cid:57872) IN: SELECT * FROM Employee WHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM Dept); ✔ EXISTS: SELECT * FROM Employee e WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM Dept d WHERE d.dept_id = e.dept_id ); f)\n• Op(cid:415)mize JOINs  Ensure join columns are indexed  Prefer INNER JOIN over OUTER JOIN when possible g)\n• Avoid unnecessary DISTINCT / ORDER BY  DISTINCT adds sor(cid:415)ng overhead  ORDER BY should be used only if needed h)\n• Use LIMIT / TOP for large datasets SELECT * FROM Orders LIMIT 100; i)\n• Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on large tables  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on by date / region / key column  Reduces scan (cid:415)me dras(cid:415)cally Example: PARTITION BY (order_date) j)\n• Use execu(cid:415)on plan analysis  Use: EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM Employee;\n• Check:  Full table scan  Index usage  Join strategy k)","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"write a sql query to get emp id, first name, last name, dept name for those employees","type":"concept","a":"who get the 2nd highest salary Assume table: Employee  emp_id  first_name  last_name  dept_id  salary Department  dept_id  dept_name (cid:33158)(cid:57393) SQL Query (2nd highest salary employees) WITH ranked_emp AS ( SELECT e.emp_id, e.first_name, e.last_name, d.dept_name, e.salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id ) SELECT emp_id, first_name, last_name, dept_name FROM ranked_emp WHERE rnk = 2;","source":"CGI - Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Why use HAVING instead of WHERE?","type":"concept","a":"•  WHERE filters rows before aggrega(cid:415)on.  HAVING filters groups a(cid:332)er aggrega(cid:415)on\n• For example, this is incorrect: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE SUM(amount) > 500; -- (cid:57872) Invalid This is correct: SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total_spending FROM Orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(amount) > 500;\n• This dis(cid:415)nc(cid:415)on is a frequent SQL interview topic\n• What are the read modes available in spark? How stages, tasks,jobs created in spark? How to call one pipeline from another pipeline in ADF? How to pass variables to pipeline in ADF? What are all the configura(cid:415)on se(cid:427)ngs have to provided to call the databricks workflow via ADF? How to read csv files and create and create a dataframe and write to delta format in ADLS? Explain me the spark architecture? what are all the indexes present in sql and types of it? create a dataframe using [(1,),(3,),(5,)] and find out the missing values in it? A=[1,2,3] and B=[1,2,3] what is the output of A==B,B==A,A is B What is the difference between par(cid:415)(cid:415)on and bucke(cid:415)ng in spark? Difference between managed and external tables in databricks? op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques in spark? Below are interview-ready answers for each ques(cid:415)on, suitable for Azure Data Engineer (Azure Databricks + ADF + PySpark + SQL) interviews","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Indexes and Types","type":"concept","a":"• Clustered Index  Physically sorts table data.  Only one clustered index per table\n• Non-Clustered Index  Separate structure storing key + pointer.  Mul(cid:415)ple allowed\n• Unique Index  Prevents duplicate values\n• Composite Index  Built on mul(cid:415)ple columns\n• Filtered Index  Indexes only rows matching a condi(cid:415)on\n• Full-Text Index  Op(cid:415)mized for text searching","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Create DataFrame and find missing values","type":"concept","a":"• Input: data = [(1,), (3,), (5,)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, [\"num\"]) Expected sequence: 1 2 3 4 5 Solu(cid:415)on: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import col expected = spark.range(1, 6).toDF(\"num\") missing = expected.join(df, \"num\", \"le(cid:332)_an(cid:415)\") missing.show() Output: 2 4 10\n• Output of A = [1,2,3] B = [1,2,3] print(A == B) print(B == A) print(A is B) Output: True True False Explana(cid:415)on:  == compares values.  is compares object iden(cid:415)ty (memory address)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What are the source systems?","type":"concept","a":"Examples:  Web Applica(cid:415)ons  SQL Server  Oracle  SAP  REST APIs  CSV files  Excel files","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How is data flowing from web applica(cid:415)ons to SQL Server?","type":"concept","a":"Users ↓ Web Applica(cid:415)on ↓ Applica(cid:415)on Server ↓ SQL Server The applica(cid:415)on writes transac(cid:415)onal data into SQL Server using applica(cid:415)on APIs or ORM frameworks.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Who loads web applica(cid:415)on data into SQL Server?","type":"concept","a":"• The applica(cid:415)on layer is responsible for inser(cid:415)ng and upda(cid:415)ng data\n• Data engineers consume that data for analy(cid:415)cs; they do not typically load it into SQL Server","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"What transforma(cid:415)ons happen where?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Server  Stored procedures  Basic filtering  Simple joins  Incremental extrac(cid:415)on Databricks  Data cleansing  Null handling  Deduplica(cid:415)on  Business rules  SCD implementa(cid:415)on  Aggrega(cid:415)ons  Delta tables Synapse  Repor(cid:415)ng views  Star schema  Aggregated datasets","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find customers who never placed an order","type":"concept","a":"SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID=o.CustomerID WHERE o.CustomerID IS NULL;","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Today's temperature higher than yesterday","type":"concept","a":"SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, LAG(Temperature) OVER(ORDER BY Date) AS PrevTemp FROM Weather )t WHERE Temperature>PrevTemp;","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Avoid recomputa(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"Use:  Cache  Persist  Delta intermediate tables  Materialized views (where applicable)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Produc(cid:415)on issue when Dev differs","type":"concept","a":" Recreate the scenario using representa(cid:415)ve data.  Align configura(cid:415)on where possible.  Test in QA/UAT before Produc(cid:415)on deployment.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you connect on-prem SQL Server to Azure?","type":"concept","a":"A common approach is:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"SCD Type 1, 2, and 3 — SQL Code","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 1 — Overwrite (Simple UPDATE) -- SCD Type 1: Just overwrite the existing record MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND (target.name <> source.name OR target.email <> source.email) THEN UPDATE SET target.name = source.name, target.email = source.email, target.updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, email, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n• SCD Type 2 — Full History with Effective Dates -- Step 1: Expire existing active records that have changed MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id AND target.is_current = 1 WHEN MATCHED AND (target.name <> source.name OR target.city <> source.city) THEN UPDATE SET target.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY, target.is_current = 0; -- Step 2: Insert new active rows for changed records INSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current) SELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE AS start_date, NULL AS end_date, 1 AS is_current FROM staging_customer s INNER JOIN dim_customer d ON s.customer_id = d.customer_id AND d.is_current = 0 AND d.end_date = CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY UNION ALL -- Insert brand new customers SELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1 FROM staging_customer s WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM dim_customer d WHERE d.customer_id = s.customer_id);\n• SCD Type 2 using Delta Lake MERGE (Recommended) -- Cleaner approach using Delta Lake MERGE with CDF MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING ( -- Changed records: expire old row SELECT d.surrogate_key, d.customer_id, d.name, d.city, d.start_date, CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 1 DAY AS end_date, 0 AS is_current FROM dim_customer d INNER JOIN staging_customer s ON d.customer_id = s.customer_id WHERE d.is_current = 1 Page 6 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide AND (d.name <> s.name OR d.city <> s.city) UNION ALL -- New rows for changed + new customers SELECT NULL, s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, NULL, 1 FROM staging_customer s ) AS source ON target.surrogate_key = source.surrogate_key WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET target.end_date = source.end_date, target.is_current = 0 WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (customer_id, name, city, start_date, end_date, is_current) VALUES (source.customer_id, source.name, source.city, source.start_date, source.end_date, source.is_current);\n• SCD Type 3 — Previous Value Column -- SCD Type 3: Keep current and one previous value MERGE INTO dim_customer AS target USING staging_customer AS source ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id WHEN MATCHED AND target.city <> source.city THEN UPDATE SET target.prev_city = target.city, target.city = source.city, target.updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Unmatched Records from Left Table Only — Left Anti Join","type":"concept","a":"• A LEFT ANTI JOIN returns rows from the LEFT table that have NO matching row in the RIGHT table\n• It is the opposite of an INNER JOIN\n• SQL -- Get all orders that have NO matching customer in the customer table SELECT o.* FROM orders o LEFT ANTI JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id;\n• Page 10 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide -- Alternative using NOT EXISTS SELECT * FROM orders o WHERE NOT EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM customers c WHERE c.customer_id = o.customer_id ); -- Alternative using NOT IN (caution: behaves differently with NULLs) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE customer_id IS NOT NULL);\n• PySpark # Left Anti Join in PySpark unmatched = orders_df.join(customers_df, on='customer_id', how='left_anti') unmatched.show() # Equivalent using filter + left join unmatched = orders_df.join(customers_df, on='customer_id', how='left') .filter(col('customers_df.customer_id').isNull()) Join Type Returns INNER JOIN Only matching rows from both tables LEFT JOIN All rows from left + NULLs for unmatched right RIGHT JOIN All rows from right + NULLs for unmatched left LEFT ANTI JOIN Only left rows with NO match in right LEFT SEMI JOIN Only left rows that DO have a match in right (no right columns) FULL OUTER JOIN All rows from both — NULLs where no match","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Second Highest Salary by Department","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — dense_rank() Window Function (Recommended) SELECT dept, name, salary FROM ( SELECT dept, name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees ) ranked WHERE rnk = 2;\n• Method 2 — PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) df_ranked = df.withColumn('rnk', dense_rank().over(w)) df_ranked.filter(col('rnk') == 2).select('dept','name','salary').show() Page 11 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Method 3 — Subquery (without window function) SELECT dept, MAX(salary) AS second_highest_salary FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e2.dept = employees.dept) GROUP BY dept;\n• Function Ties Behavior Best for dense_rank() Same rank for ties; no gaps Top-N salary — most common interview answer rank() Same rank for ties; gaps after ties Ranking where gaps matter row_number() Unique number always; no ties When you need exactly one row per rank","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Validation Checks Before Moving Data to the Next Layer","type":"concept","a":"• Page 13 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Validation What to Check PySpark / SQL Example Schema Validation Column names, data types df.schema == expected_schema match expected schema Null Checks Critical columns must not be df.filter(col('id').isNull()).count() == 0 null Duplicate Checks No duplicate records on df.count() == primary key df.dropDuplicates(['id']).count() Data Type Validation Values match expected type Try cast; check for null after cast (int, date, etc.) Range / Domain Validation Numeric ranges, valid enum df.filter((col('age')<0)|(col('age')>150)) values Referential Integrity Foreign key exists in parent Left anti join between child and parent table Record Count Validation Row count matches source or abs(source_count - target_count) <= expected threshold threshold Business Rule Validation Custom business logic (e.g., df.filter(col('end_date') < col('start_date')) end_date > start_date) # Example: Validation framework in PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import col def validate_layer(df, layer_name): errors = [] # 1\n• Null check on key columns null_count = df.filter(col('id').isNull()).count() if null_count > 0: errors.append(f'NULL id count: {null_count}') # 2\n• Duplicate check total = df.count() dedup = df.dropDuplicates(['id']).count() if total != dedup: errors.append(f'Duplicates found: {total - dedup}') # 3\n• Range check invalid_age = df.filter((col('age') < 0) | (col('age') > 150)).count() if invalid_age > 0: errors.append(f'Invalid age values: {invalid_age}') if errors: raise Exception(f'{layer_name} validation FAILED: {errors}') else: print(f'{layer_name} validation PASSED ({total} rows)') validate_layer(silver_df, 'Silver')","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Fill nulls with derived values","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, mean, lit # Fill with column mean avg_salary = df.select(mean('salary')).first()[0] df_clean = df.fillna({'salary': avg_salary}) # Conditional fill using when() df_clean = df.withColumn('salary', when(col('salary').isNull(), 0).otherwise(col('salary'))) # Fill with previous value (forward fill using Window) from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import last w = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy('date').rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, 0) df_clean = df.withColumn('salary', last('salary', ignorenulls=True).over(w))","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Filter out nulls","type":"concept","a":"df_clean = df.filter(col('id').isNotNull()) df_clean = df.where(col('name').isNotNull() & col('email').isNotNull())","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Full Load vs Incremental Load from SQL Server","type":"concept","a":"Page 18 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide Aspect Full Load Incremental Load What Load ALL data every run Load only NEW or CHANGED data since last run When to use First-time historical load; small Large tables; daily ETL; tables with tables; no CDC support last_modified column Source impact High — full table scan every run Low — only reads delta records Processing time Long Short Complexity Simple Needs watermark tracking Risk Overwrites may cause data loss if Must handle late-arriving data not careful Full Load Code # Full load from SQL Server df_full = spark.read.jdbc( url='jdbc:sqlserver://server.db.windows.net:1433;database=db', table='dbo.Customers', properties={'user': 'user', 'password': 'pwd', 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'} ) df_full.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('bronze.customers') Incremental Load Code (Watermark-based) # Read last watermark from control table last_wm = spark.sql(\"SELECT MAX(last_load_ts) FROM ctrl.watermark WHERE table_name='Customers'\").first()[0] # Load only changed records df_incr = spark.read.jdbc( url='jdbc:sqlserver://server.db.windows.net:1433;database=db', table='dbo.Customers', predicates=[f\"ModifiedDate > '{last_wm}'\"], properties={'user': 'user', 'password': 'pwd', 'driver': 'com.microsoft.sqlserver.jdbc.SQLServerDriver'} ) # Merge into target (upsert) df_incr.createOrReplaceTempView('incr') spark.sql(''' MERGE INTO silver.customers t USING incr s ON t.customer_id = s.customer_id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT * ''') # Update watermark spark.sql(f\"UPDATE ctrl.watermark SET last_load_ts=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE table_name='Customers'\")","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL – Same Emp Name, Different Department (No JOIN)","type":"concept","a":"• Schema EMP (Emp_id, E_name, E_mid, E_mname, E_loc) DEPT (D_id, D_name, D_mid, D_loc) Approach — Using EXISTS / Subquery (No JOIN Keyword) -- Find employees who share the same name but belong to different departments SELECT e1.Emp_id, e1.E_name, e1.E_mid AS dept1, e2.E_mid AS dept2 FROM EMP e1, EMP e2 WHERE e1.E_name = e2.E_name AND e1.E_mid <> e2.E_mid AND e1.Emp_id < e2.Emp_id; -- avoid duplicate pairs Alternative — Using EXISTS SELECT DISTINCT e1.Emp_id, e1.E_name, e1.E_mid AS department FROM EMP e1 WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM EMP e2 WHERE e2.E_name = e1.E_name AND e2.E_mid <> e1.E_mid Page 9 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A );\n• Note: Both solutions use comma-separated FROM (implicit cross join notation) or subquery — NO explicit JOIN keyword is used","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"Join Counts – Inner, Left, Right","type":"concept","a":"• Given Data Table A – ID Table B – ID 1 1 1 3 2 1 NULL 4 NULL 5 3 Join Results Join Type Logic Count Explanation INNER JOIN Only matching rows 3 IDs 1,1,1 (A has two 1s;\n• B has one 1) → 2 (A.ID = B.ID) rows for ID=1, plus ID=3 → total 3 rows\n• NULLs never match\n• LEFT JOIN All rows from A + 6 All 6 rows from A are kept\n• Matching B rows matches from B filled in;\n• NULLs from A and unmatched rows get NULL on B side\n• RIGHT JOIN All rows from B + 7 All 5 rows from B are kept\n• ID=1 in B matches from A matches 2 rows in A → 2 rows\n• IDs 3,4,5 from B each get 1 row; total 7\n• Detailed Breakdown Table A: [1, 1, 2, NULL, NULL, 3] → 6 rows Table B: [1, 3, 1, 4, 5] → 5 rows • INNER JOIN: Match A.ID=1 (×2) with B.ID=1 (×1) = 2 rows;\n• A.ID=3 with B.ID=3 = 1 row → Total = 3 • LEFT JOIN: All 6 A rows kept;\n• B columns NULL where no match (A.ID=2, NULL, NULL have no B match) → Total = 6 Page 10 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A • RIGHT JOIN: All 5 B rows kept;","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_TCS.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse?","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Warehouse (DWH) is a centralized, structured repository that stores large volumes of historical, integrated data from multiple source systems\n• It is optimized for analytical querying and reporting rather than transactional processing\n• Key Characteristics Property Description Subject-Oriented Organized around key business subjects: Sales, Finance, HR — not by application Integrated Data consolidated from multiple heterogeneous sources (CRM, ERP, flat files) Non-Volatile Once loaded, data is not updated or deleted — historical record is preserved Time-Variant Maintains historical snapshots; every record has a time dimension Read-Optimized Schema and indexes designed for fast analytical reads (not transactional writes) DWH vs OLTP vs Data Lake Aspect OLTP (Operational Data Warehouse Data Lake DB) Purpose Day-to-day transactions Analytics & reporting Raw data storage for all types Data type Current, real-time Historical, structured Raw — structured + unstructured Schema Schema-on-write (strict) Schema-on-write Schema-on-read (flexible) Query speed Fast for single rows Fast for aggregations Slower without optimization Example SQL Server, MySQL Snowflake, Synapse ADLS, S3, Databricks Lakehouse Common Schemas in a Data Warehouse • Star Schema: One central Fact table linked to multiple Dimension tables — simple, fast queries • Snowflake Schema: Dimensions are further normalized into sub-dimensions — saves space, more joins • Galaxy Schema: Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables — for complex enterprises Page 3 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What is Data Cleansing and How is it Done?","type":"concept","a":"• Data cleansing (also called data cleaning or data scrubbing) is the process of detecting and correcting inaccurate, incomplete, duplicate, or irrelevant records in a dataset to improve data quality before analysis or loading into a warehouse\n• Common Data Quality Issues Issue Example Fix Missing / NULL Salary = NULL, Email = Fill with default, mean/median, or flag as values NULL unknown Duplicate records Same customer appears Deduplicate using dropDuplicates() or twice with same ID ROW_NUMBER() Incorrect data types Salary stored as '2500' Cast to correct type; handle parse errors (string) instead of integer Inconsistent formats Date as '01-Jan-2024' in one Standardize using to_date() with format file, '2024-01-01' in another pattern Outliers / Invalid Age = -5, Salary = 99999999 Apply business-rule filters; flag or exclude values outliers Whitespace / Case ' John ' vs 'john' vs 'JOHN' trim() + lower() / upper() for normalization issues Referential integrity Order has Customer_ID that Left join + filter nulls to find orphan records doesn't exist in Customer table Special characters Name = 'O\\u2019Brien' vs regexp_replace() to clean special chars 'OBrien' PySpark Data Cleansing Code Example from pyspark.sql import functions as F # 1\n• Remove duplicates df = df.dropDuplicates(['emp_id']) # 2\n• Handle NULL values df = df.fillna({'salary': 0, 'emp_name': 'Unknown', 'location': 'N/A'}) # 3\n• Fix data types df = df.withColumn('salary', F.col('salary').cast('integer')) df = df.withColumn('join_date', F.to_date(F.col('join_date'), 'yyyy-MM-dd')) # 4\n• Trim whitespace and standardize case df = df.withColumn('emp_name', F.trim(F.lower(F.col('emp_name')))) # 5\n• Remove outliers (salary must be between 1000 and 500000) df = df.filter(F.col('salary').between(1000, 500000)) Page 10 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # 6\n• Remove special characters from name df = df.withColumn('emp_name', F.regexp_replace(F.col('emp_name'), '[^a-zA-Z ]', '')) # 7\n• Flag records with remaining NULLs df = df.withColumn('has_nulls', F.when(F.col('emp_id').isNull() | F.col('emp_name').isNull(), True).otherwise(False)) df.show() Page 11 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"What are Window Functions?","type":"concept","a":"• Window functions perform calculations across a set of rows that are related to the current row — similar to aggregate functions but WITHOUT collapsing rows into a single result\n• Each row retains its identity while gaining access to aggregated or ranked values from a defined window (partition)\n• Syntax Structure function_name() OVER ( PARTITION BY column1, column2 -- defines the group/window ORDER BY column3 [ASC|DESC] -- defines row ordering within window ROWS/RANGE BETWEEN \n• AND ... -- optional frame specification ) Types of Window Functions Category Function Description Ranking ROW_NUMBER() Unique sequential number per row within partition — no ties Ranking RANK() Same rank for ties; gaps in sequence after ties (1,1,3) Ranking DENSE_RANK() Same rank for ties;\n• NO gaps (1,1,2) Ranking NTILE(n) Divides rows into n equal buckets Ranking PERCENT_RANK() Relative rank as a percentage (0 to 1) Aggregate SUM() OVER(...) Running or partitioned sum without collapsing rows Aggregate AVG() OVER(...) Running or partitioned average Aggregate COUNT() OVER(...) Count per window — useful for % of total Aggregate MIN() / MAX() Min/max within the window OVER(...) Navigation LAG(col, n) Access value from n rows BEFORE current row Navigation LEAD(col, n) Access value from n rows AFTER current row Navigation FIRST_VALUE(col) First value in the window frame Navigation LAST_VALUE(col) Last value in the window frame PySpark Window Function Examples from pyspark.sql import Window from pyspark.sql import functions as F # Define window w = Window.partitionBy('department').orderBy(F.desc('salary')) # ROW_NUMBER -- unique rank per department df = df.withColumn('row_num', F.row_number().over(w)) Page 12 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2 # RANK -- same rank for ties, gaps after ties df = df.withColumn('rank', F.rank().over(w)) # DENSE_RANK -- same rank for ties, no gaps df = df.withColumn('dense_rank', F.dense_rank().over(w)) # Running total salary within department w2 = Window.partitionBy('department').orderBy('join_date') \\ .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow) df = df.withColumn('running_salary', F.sum('salary').over(w2)) # LAG -- previous row's salary df = df.withColumn('prev_salary', F.lag('salary', 1).over(w)) # Get top 1 earner per department top_earners = df.filter(F.col('row_num') == 1) Page 13 Databricks & Data Engineering – Interview Q&A | Part 2","source":"Databricks_DataEngineering_UST.pdf"},{"q":"You stored a table in Databricks — what format is it likely to be and where is the","type":"concept","a":"","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"max sales per customer?","type":"concept","a":"Input Data: customer_id | transaction_date | sales cust1 | 2023-12-01 | 100 cust2 | 2023-12-02 | 150 cust1 | 2023-12-03 | 200 cust2 | 2023-12-04 | 250 Expected Output: cust_id | sales_max cust1 | 200 cust2 | 250","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Have you used databricks sql warehouse?","type":"concept","a":"Databricks SQL Warehouse  Serverless compute for BI queries  Used for Power BI / Tableau dashboards","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Diff between rank and dense rank","type":"concept","a":"Rank vs Dense Rank Func(cid:415)on Output RANK 1,1,3 DENSE_RANK 1,1,2","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you op(cid:415)mize sql query?","type":"concept","a":"SQL Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on  Indexing  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning  Avoid SELECT *  Filter early  Proper joins","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How will you do the par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in sql?","type":"concept","a":"Par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning in SQL  Splits data physically for performance  Usually date-based par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Transac(cid:415)ons table:","type":"concept","a":"+ — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — | id | dept | state | amount | date | + — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — | 1 | IT | approved | 1000 | 2018–12–18 | | 2 | IT | declined | 2000 | 2018–12–19 | | 3 | IT | approved | 2000 | 2019–01–01 | | 4 | Fin | approved | 2000 | 2019–01–07 | + — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — + — — — — + — — — Output: + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — — --------------+ | month | dept | trans_count | approved_trans_cnt | total_amount | approved_total_amount | + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — -------------— -+ | 2018–12 | IT | 2 | 1 | 3000 | 1000 | | 2019–01 | IT | 1 | 1 | 2000 | 2000 | | 2019–01 | Fin | 1 | 1 | 2000 | 2000 | + — — — — — + — — — — -+ — — — — — — -+ — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — + — — — — — — — — — — — --------------+ Write a SQL query to find:  Month  Department  Total transac(cid:415)ons  Approved transac(cid:415)on count  Total amount  Approved amount Concepts Tested  GROUP BY  CASE WHEN  Aggregate func(cid:415)ons  DATE_FORMAT SELECT DATE_FORMAT(date,'%Y-%m') AS month, dept, COUNT(*) AS trans_count, SUM(CASE WHEN state='approved' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) approved_trans_cnt, SUM(amount) total_amount, SUM(CASE WHEN state='approved' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) approved_total_amount FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(date,'%Y-%m'), dept;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Reverse Route Distance","type":"concept","a":"Id | Place1 |Place2 |Distance | ------------------------------------| 1 |Hyd | Mumbai | 1200 | 2 |Banglore | Pune | 400 | 3 |Mumbai | Hyd | 1400 | 4 |Pune | Bangalore | 400 | 5 |Chennai |Hyd | 1200 | ------------------------------------| Output: -------------------------------- Place1 |Place2 |Distance | -------------------------------| Hyd | Mumbai | 2600 | Banglore | Pune | 800 | Chennai | Hyd | 1200 | -------------------------------- Input Hyd → Mumbai Mumbai → Hyd Output Hyd Mumbai 2600 SELECT LEAST(place1,place2) AS place1, GREATEST(place1,place2) AS place2, SUM(distance) total_distance FROM Routes GROUP BY LEAST(place1,place2), GREATEST(place1,place2);","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find Second Highest Salary","type":"concept","a":"SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee WHERE salary < ( SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee );","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Highest Salary in Each Department","type":"concept","a":"SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER(PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) rnk FROM Employee )t WHERE rnk=1;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Remove Duplicate Records","type":"concept","a":"• SELECT DISTINCT * FROM Employee;\n• Using ROW_NUMBER WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY id ORDER BY id) rn FROM Employee ) SELECT * FROM cte WHERE rn=1;","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between UNION and UNION ALL","type":"concept","a":"UNION UNION ALL Removes duplicates Keeps duplicates Slower Faster","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between WHERE and HAVING","type":"concept","a":"• WHERE filters rows before aggrega(cid:415)on\n• HAVING filters groups a(cid:332)er aggrega(cid:415)on\n• PySpark Ques(cid:415)ons","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Narrow vs Wide Transforma(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• Narrow No shuffle\n• Examples  map  filter  union Wide Shuffle happens\n• Examples  join  groupBy  reduceByKey  dis(cid:415)nct","source":"EY Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Top 2 items per category based on sales","type":"concept","a":"• sales itemId,categoryId,amount apple,fruit,100 apple,fruit,100 apple,fruit,100 mango,fruit,200 pear,fruit,150 shirt,fashion,5000 jeans,fashion,3000 jeans,fashion,3000 (cid:415)e,fashion,1000 apple,mobile,100000 Answer: SELECT itemId, categoryId, SUM(amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY itemId, categoryId ), ranked_sales AS ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY categoryId ORDER BY total_sales DESC ) AS rnk FROM item_sales ) SELECT itemId, categoryId, total_sales FROM ranked_sales WHERE rnk <= 2;\n• Pyspark: from pyspark.sql import func(cid:415)ons as F from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Step 1: aggregate df_agg = df.groupBy(\"itemId\", \"categoryId\") \\ .agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total_sales\")) # Step 2: window ranking window_spec = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"categoryId\").orderBy(F.desc(\"total_sales\")) df_ranked = df_agg.withColumn(\"rnk\", F.dense_rank().over(window_spec)) # Step 3: filter top 2 result = df_ranked.filter(F.col(\"rnk\") <= 2) result.show()","source":"Fractal Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to find customers who spent more than ₹1000 in the last month","type":"concept","a":"using Orders and Order_Items tables Assume: Orders |OrderID|CustomerID|OrderDate| Order_Items |OrderID|Quantity|UnitPrice| SELECT o.CustomerID, SUM(oi.Quantity * oi.UnitPrice) AS TotalSpent FROM Orders o JOIN Order_Items oi ON o.OrderID = oi.OrderID WHERE o.OrderDate >= DATEADD(month, -1, GETDATE()) GROUP BY o.CustomerID HAVING SUM(oi.Quantity * oi.UnitPrice) > 1000;","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Follow-up: Why use HAVING instead of WHERE?","type":"concept","a":"Answer: HAVING filters aggregated results after GROUP BY, whereas WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation.","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Find the top 3 customers by total purchase amount per month using window","type":"concept","a":"functions? WITH MonthlySales AS ( SELECT CustomerID, YEAR(OrderDate) AS Year, MONTH(OrderDate) AS Month, SUM(TotalAmount) AS TotalPurchase FROM Orders GROUP BY CustomerID, YEAR(OrderDate), MONTH(OrderDate) ) SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY Year,Month ORDER BY TotalPurchase DESC ) RankNo FROM MonthlySales )t WHERE RankNo<=3;","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a query to calculate net revenue per day considering returns data?","type":"concept","a":"• Tables Orders |OrderID|OrderDate|Amount| Returns |OrderID|ReturnAmount| SELECT o.OrderDate, SUM(o.Amount)-COALESCE(SUM(r.ReturnAmount),0) AS NetRevenue FROM Orders o LEFT JOIN Returns r ON o.OrderID=r.OrderID GROUP BY o.OrderDate;\n• If no returns exist, COALESCE() avoids NULL","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark: Join two DataFrames using row numbers","type":"concept","a":"Suppose IDs don't match. from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number windowSpec = Window.orderBy(\"Name\") df1 = df1.withColumn( \"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec) ) df2 = df2.withColumn( \"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec) ) result = df1.join(df2,\"rn\").drop(\"rn\")","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a PySpark job to remove duplicate records from a large dataset efficiently.","type":"concept","a":"• Simple approach df = df.dropDuplicates() For selected columns df = df.dropDuplicates([\"CustomerID\"]) For large datasets from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number,col windowSpec = Window.partitionBy(\"CustomerID\") \\ .orderBy(col(\"UpdatedDate\").desc()) df = df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(windowSpec)) df = df.filter(\"rn=1\").drop(\"rn\") This retains the latest record per customer\n• Round 2 – Technical Coding","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Describe a situation where you had to balance delivery speed with data quality","type":"concept","a":"\"I never compromise data accuracy for speed.\" Instead  Deliver critical fields first  Run validations  Automate testing  Release incrementally","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Write a query to get employees whose salary increased compared to the previous","type":"concept","a":"• year\n• Use the LAG() window function to fetch each employee's salary from the previous year, then filter where the current salary is greater\n• WITH cte AS ( SELECT emp_id, salary, year, LAG(salary, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY emp_id ORDER BY year) AS prev_sal FROM employee ) SELECT * FROM cte WHERE salary > prev_sal;\n• How it works: • PARTITION BY emp_id — computes LAG independently for each employee. • ORDER BY year — ensures LAG looks at the immediately preceding year. • The outer WHERE salary > prev_sal filters only those whose pay went up\n• TRUNCATE vs DELETE vs DROP","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between TRUNCATE, DELETE, and DROP?","type":"concept","a":"Feature TRUNCATE DELETE DROP Removes Data All rows Selected/all rows Entire table Structure Kept Kept Removed Rollback (TXN) No (DDL) Yes (DML) No (DDL) WHERE clause Not allowed Allowed N/A Triggers fired No Yes No Speed Fastest Slower Instant WHERE Clause vs HAVING Clause","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between GROUP BY and PARTITION BY?","type":"concept","a":"• GROUP BY collapses rows into one row per group — it reduces the result set. • PARTITION BY is used inside window functions (OVER clause) — it divides rows into partitions but DOES NOT reduce the result set; all original rows are returned. Example: -- GROUP BY: returns 1 row per dept SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept; -- PARTITION BY: returns all rows, adds avg per dept as a column SELECT emp_id, dept, salary, AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept) AS dept_avg FROM emp;","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"How do you optimize a Mapping Data Flow in ADF?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Partitioning wisely — choose Round Robin for uniform distribution; use Hash partition on join keys to reduce data shuffle. • Enable staging — use PolyBase for Synapse sinks to speed up loading. • Push down transformations — filter and select only required columns early in the flow to reduce data volume. • Use Broadcast joins — for small lookup datasets, broadcast the smaller side to avoid shuffle joins. • Increase cluster size (Compute Optimized or Memory Optimized) for heavy transformations. • Avoid unnecessary sorts — sorts are expensive; only sort when required (e.g., for rank or window functions). • Cache sink — use in-memory lookup cache for repeated lookups on the same small dataset. • Monitor with Debug mode first, then use activity run metrics (rows processed, time per stage) to identify bottlenecks. 3. Databricks Standard vs Premium vs Enterprise Tiers","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to get employees that earn more than their managers","type":"concept","a":"• SQL: Employees earning more than their managers SELECT e.emp_id, e.emp_name, e.salary FROM employee e JOIN employee m ON e.manager_id = m.emp_id WHERE e.salary > m.salary;\n• Logic:  Self join on employee table  Compare employee salary with manager salary","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Delete duplicate records","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose duplicates are based on: customer_id name email_id order_date SQL WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id ) rn FROM customer ) DELETE FROM customer WHERE (customer_id,name,email_id,order_date) IN ( SELECT customer_id,name,email_id,order_date FROM cte WHERE rn>1 );\n• If using Delta Lake, a more typical approach is: CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customer AS SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id,name,email_id,order_date ORDER BY customer_id ) rn FROM customer ) WHERE rn = 1;\n• PySpark from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import row_number window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy( \"customer_id\", \"name\", \"email_id\", \"order_date\" ).orderBy(\"customer_id\") df = df.withColumn(\"rn\", row_number().over(window)) df = df.filter(\"rn=1\").drop(\"rn\")","source":"Netscribes - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Find the most frequently occurring item for each date.","type":"concept","a":"• -- schema CREATE TABLE Items ( Date date NOT NULL, Item INT NOT NULL ); -- data INSERT INTO Items (Date, Item) VALUES ('2022-09-01', 1), ('2022-09-01', 3), ('2022-09-01', 2), ('2022-09-01', 6), ('2022-09-01', 1), ('2022-09-02', 2), ('2022-09-02', 2), ('2022-09-02', 4) ;\n• Use this query: WITH Item_CTE AS ( SELECT Date, Item, COUNT(*) AS Cnt FROM Items GROUP BY Date, Item ), Rank_Item AS ( SELECT Date, Item, Cnt, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY Date ORDER BY Cnt DESC ) AS Rank_I FROM Item_CTE ) SELECT Date, Item, Cnt FROM Rank_Item WHERE Rank_I = 1;\n• Output Date Item Cnt 2022-09-01 1 2 2022-09-02 2 2","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Write a func(cid:415)on that takes in an array of at least three integers and, without sor(cid:415)ng the","type":"concept","a":"• input array, returns a sorted array of the three largest integers in the input array\n• The func(cid:415)on should return duplicate integers if necessary; for example, it should return [10, 10, 12] for an input array of [10, 5, 9, 10, 12]. def find_three_largest_numbers(array): first = second = third = float('-inf') for num in array: if num > first: third = second second = first first = num elif num > second: third = second second = num elif num > third: third = num return [third, second, first] (cid:44869)(cid:44870) Example arr = [10, 5, 9, 10, 12] print(find_three_largest_numbers(arr)) Output: [10, 10, 12] How it works For each number:  If it's bigger than the largest → shi(cid:332) all down  Else if bigger than second → shi(cid:332) second and third  Else if bigger than third → update third Round 2: SQL Ques(cid:415)ons","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Return Top 3 User_id","type":"concept","a":"• Transac(cid:415)on Table: transac(cid:415)on_id,user_id , date;\n• Answer: SELECT user_id, cnt FROM ( SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS cnt, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id ) t WHERE rnk <= 3;\n• OR If interviewer wants strictly top 3 users (no (cid:415)es): SELECT user_id, cnt FROM ( SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS cnt, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS rnk FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id ) t WHERE rnk <= 3;","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Compare transac(cid:415)on counts per user between consecu(cid:415)ve dates and find users whose","type":"concept","a":"ac(cid:415)vity increased” Step 1: Aggregate WITH cte AS ( SELECT user_id, date, COUNT(transac(cid:415)on_id) AS cnt FROM Transac(cid:415)on GROUP BY user_id, date ) Step 2: Compare with previous day SELECT c2.user_id FROM cte c1 JOIN cte c2 ON c1.user_id = c2.user_id AND c2.date = DATE_ADD(c1.date, 1) WHERE c2.cnt > c1.cnt; (cid:55982)(cid:55983)(cid:55984) Explana(cid:415)on  Compare same user across 2 consecu(cid:415)ve days  Check if today's transac(cid:415)ons > yesterday Pyspark : from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"user_id\").orderBy(\"date\") df_agg = df.groupBy(\"user_id\", \"date\") \\ .agg(F.count(\"transac(cid:415)on_id\").alias(\"cnt\")) df_final = df_agg.withColumn(\"prev_cnt\", F.lag(\"cnt\").over(w)) result = df_final.filter(F.col(\"cnt\") > F.col(\"prev_cnt\")) \\ .select(\"user_id\", \"date\", \"cnt\", \"prev_cnt\") result.show()","source":"NineLeaps - Interview Questions - Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Split comma-separated sports into rows and group by Sport","type":"concept","a":"Input Name Sport Alice Badminton, Tennis Greg Cricket, Baseball Julie Swimming, Basket ball Alan Tennis, Swimming Xian Badminton, Baseball Expected Output Name Sport Alice Badminton Xian Badminton Alice Tennis Alan Tennis Greg Baseball Xian Baseball Julie Swimming Alan Swimming Greg Cricket Julie Basket ball PySpark Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql import func(cid:415)ons as F # Split the sports into an array df1 = df.withColumn(\"Sport\", F.split(F.col(\"Sport\"), \",\\\\s*\")) # Convert array into mul(cid:415)ple rows df2 = df1.withColumn(\"Sport\", F.explode(\"Sport\")) # Sort by Sport (op(cid:415)onal, to match expected output) result = df2.orderBy(\"Sport\") result.show(truncate=False) Func(cid:415)ons used:  split()  explode()  orderBy()","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Group values into an array","type":"concept","a":"Input Col1 Col2 Col3 a aa 1 a aa 2 b bb 5 b bb 3 b bb 4 Expected Output Col1 Col2 Col3 a aa [1,2] b bb [5,3,4] Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_list result = df.groupBy(\"Col1\",\"Col2\") \\ .agg(collect_list(\"Col3\").alias(\"Col3\")) result.show(truncate=False) If duplicate values should be removed: from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import collect_set result = df.groupBy(\"Col1\",\"Col2\") \\ .agg(collect_set(\"Col3\").alias(\"Col3\"))","source":"Pyspark Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark: 1st & 2nd Highest Salary Per Department","type":"concept","a":"• Window Function with dense_rank() — Recommended from pyspark.sql.functions import dense_rank, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window data = [('Alice', 'Engineering', 90000), ('Bob', 'Engineering', 85000), ('Carol', 'Engineering', 85000), ('Dave', 'Marketing', 70000), ('Eve', 'Marketing', 65000)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['name', 'dept', 'salary']) window = Window.partitionBy('dept').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) df_ranked = df.withColumn('rank', dense_rank().over(window)) result = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') <= 2) result.show() Function Behavior on Ties Output Example rank() Skips ranks after ties 1, 1, 3, 4 dense_rank() No gaps after ties 1, 1, 2, 3 row_number() Always unique — no ties 1, 2, 3, 4 Use dense_rank() for Top-N salary — handles tied salaries without skipping ranks\n• SQL Approach df.createOrReplaceTempView('employees') spark.sql(''' SELECT name, dept, salary, dense_rank() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees ''').filter('rnk <= 2').show() Page 6 of 8 PySpark & Delta Lake — Interview Preparation Guide","source":"PySpark_DeltaLake_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Ques(cid:415)on Based on Window Func(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"• Find latest record for every employee SELECT * FROM ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EmpID ORDER BY UpdatedDate DESC) rn FROM Employee )t WHERE rn=1;\n• Find second highest salary SELECT * FROM ( SELECT Salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rnk FROM Employee )t WHERE rnk=2;\n• Running Total SELECT OrderDate, Amount, SUM(Amount) OVER(ORDER BY OrderDate) AS RunningTotal FROM Orders;","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between ROW_NUMBER(), RANK(), DENSE_RANK()","type":"concept","a":"Func(cid:415)on Duplicate Rank Gap ROW_NUMBER No No RANK Yes Yes DENSE_RANK Yes No","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Wide Transforma(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"•  Requires shuffle across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons.  More expensive\n• Examples:  groupBy()  join()  dis(cid:415)nct()  reduceByKey()  orderBy() Narrow transforma(cid:415)ons don't require data movement across par(cid:415)(cid:415)ons, whereas wide transforma(cid:415)ons involve shuffle, making them more expensive","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"For incremental loads, use:","type":"concept","a":"• o Watermark column (LastModifiedDate) o SQL query with dynamic parameters o Store the last processed watermark in a control table\n• Example SQL: SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE ModifiedDate > @LastWatermark A(cid:332)er successful execu(cid:415)on, update the watermark","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Ques(cid:415)on","type":"concept","a":"• Table: Stadium +---------------+---------+ | Column Name | Type | +---------------+---------+ | id | int | | visit_date | date | | people | int | +---------------+---------+ visit_date is the column with unique values for this table\n• Each row of this table contains the visit date and visit id to the stadium with the number of people during the visit\n• As the id increases, the date increases as well\n• Write a solu(cid:415)on to display the records with three or more rows with consecu(cid:415)ve id's, and the number of people is greater than or equal to 100 for each\n• Return the result table ordered by visit_date in ascending order\n• The result format is in the following example\n• Input: Stadium table: +------+------------+-----------+ | id | visit_date | people | +------+------------+-----------+ | 1 | 2017-01-01 | 10 | | 2 | 2017-01-02 | 109 | | 3 | 2017-01-03 | 150 | | 4 | 2017-01-04 | 99 | | 5 | 2017-01-05 | 145 | | 6 | 2017-01-06 | 1455 | | 7 | 2017-01-07 | 199 | | 8 | 2017-01-09 | 188 | Output: +------+------------+-----------+ | id | visit_date | people | +------+------------+-----------+ | 5 | 2017-01-05 | 145 | | 6 | 2017-01-06 | 1455 | | 7 | 2017-01-07 | 199 | | 8 | 2017-01-09 | 188 | Explana(cid:415)on: The four rows with ids 5, 6, 7, and 8 have consecu(cid:415)ve ids and each of them has >= 100 people a(cid:425)ended\n• Note that row 8 was included even though the visit_date was not the next day a(cid:332)er row 7\n• The rows with ids 2 and 3 are not included because we need at least three consecu(cid:415)ve ids\n• Answer: Approach","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Filter rows where people >= 100.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Return only groups having 3 or more consecu(cid:415)ve rows.","type":"concept","a":"• SQL Solu(cid:415)on WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id) AS grp FROM Stadium WHERE people >= 100 ), grp_count AS ( SELECT grp, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM cte GROUP BY grp HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3 ) SELECT c.id, c.visit_date, c.people FROM cte c JOIN grp_count g ON c.grp = g.grp ORDER BY c.visit_date;\n• Explana(cid:415)on Given the filtered data (people >= 100): id people ROW_NUMBER id - ROW_NUMBER 2 109 1 1 3 150 2 1 5 145 3 2 6 1455 4 2 7 199 5 2 8 188 6 2 No(cid:415)ce:  IDs 2, 3 have the same group (1) but only 2 rows, so they are excluded.  IDs 5, 6, 7, 8 have the same group (2) and 4 rows, so they are included\n• Output id visit_date people 5 2017-01-05 145 6 2017-01-06 1455 7 2017-01-07 199 8 2017-01-09 188 Client Round 2 :","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"If Copy Ac(cid:415)vity is very slow, how will you op(cid:415)mize it?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I would first iden(cid:415)fy whether the bo(cid:425)leneck is the source, network, or sink\n• Then I would:  Increase the Degree of Copy Parallelism.  Enable parallel copy where supported.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on the source data.  Use staging for large data transfers.  Op(cid:415)mize the Integra(cid:415)on Run(cid:415)me size.  Filter unnecessary records.  Copy mul(cid:415)ple files in parallel.  Ensure source and sink indexes are appropriate.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What challenges did you face while doing transforma(cid:415)ons?","type":"concept","a":"• \"One common challenge was handling duplicate records and inconsistent source data.\" Examples:  Duplicate records.  Null or invalid values.  Schema changes.  Large joins causing performance issues.  Data type mismatches\n• Solu(cid:415)ons:  Used dropDuplicates() or SQL window func(cid:415)ons.  Applied null handling and valida(cid:415)on rules.  Used par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning and op(cid:415)mized joins.  Used Delta Lake features like MERGE for incremental updates","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Are you connec(cid:415)ng only with the SQL Server team or also with businesspeople?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I worked with both technical and business teams\n• I collaborated with the SQL Server team to understand the source schema, data availability, and any schema changes\n• I also interacted with business analysts and repor(cid:415)ng teams to clarify business rules, validate transformed data, and ensure the reports met their requirements.\" This demonstrates cross-func(cid:415)onal communica(cid:415)on skills","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why Do We Convert CSV Files to Parquet?","type":"concept","a":"• Feature CSV Parquet Storage format Row-based Columnar (each column stored separately) Compression None or minimal Snappy/GZIP — 75-90% smaller typically Schema No schema (inferred at read) Schema embedded in file footer Query speed Reads ALL columns even if Column pruning — reads only needed unused columns Predicate pushdown Not supported Row groups skipped if outside filter range Splittable Only with separate index Yes — parallel reads across tasks Null handling String representation Efficient native null encoding Nested types Flat only Supports nested/complex types natively Rule of thumb: A 10 GB CSV file becomes 1-3 GB Parquet (Snappy)\n• Queries run 5-20x faster on Parquet because only queried columns are read from disk\n• Always convert raw CSV to Parquet or Delta in the Bronze layer\n• Section 4 | Data Processing","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Do You Remove Duplicates from a Table?","type":"concept","a":"Method 1 — dropDuplicates() df_clean = df.dropDuplicates() # all columns df_clean = df.dropDuplicates(['order_id','cust_id']) # key columns only Method 2 — distinct() df_clean = df.distinct() # removes fully identical rows Method 3 — Window row_number() — keep latest record from pyspark.sql.functions import row_number, col from pyspark.sql.window import Window w = Window.partitionBy('order_id').orderBy(col('updated_at').desc()) df_dedup = df.withColumn('rn', row_number().over(w)).filter(col('rn')==1).drop('rn') Method 4 — Delta MERGE INTO (upsert on load) MERGE INTO target t USING source s ON t.id = s.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How Many Ways Can You Handle Duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• Method When to Use Key Consideration dropDuplicates() In-memory DataFrame dedup Specify key columns or all columns distinct() Exact row-level duplicates All columns must match Window row_number() Keep latest/earliest record per Partition by key, order by timestamp key Window dense_rank() Rank-based dedup with ties Use when ties should be preserved allowed Delta MERGE INTO Incremental load — avoid dupes Best for production pipelines at ingest SQL ROW_NUMBER() SQL-based in Spark SQL PARTITION BY key ORDER BY ts DESC GROUP BY + aggregation Collapse to summary rows Loses individual row detail Best Practice for production: Use MERGE INTO for Delta tables\n• It handles both new inserts and updates in one atomic operation, preventing duplicates at the table level without reprocessing old data\n• Page 6 of 16 Spark, Databricks & Delta Lake — Full Interview Preparation Guide (30 Questions) Section 5 | SQL / Transformation","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Calculate Male Count, Female Count, and Total Count per City","type":"concept","a":"• Method 1 — Conditional Aggregation (SQL) SELECT city, COUNT(CASE WHEN gender = 'Male' THEN 1 END) AS male_count, COUNT(CASE WHEN gender = 'Female' THEN 1 END) AS female_count, COUNT(*) AS total_count FROM people GROUP BY city ORDER BY city;\n• Method 2 — PIVOT (Spark SQL) SELECT city, Male AS male_count, Female AS female_count FROM (SELECT city, gender FROM people) PIVOT (COUNT(gender) FOR gender IN ('Male', 'Female')) ORDER BY city;\n• Method 3 — PySpark from pyspark.sql.functions import count, when, col result = df.groupBy('city').agg( count(when(col('gender')=='Male', 1)).alias('male_count'), count(when(col('gender')=='Female', 1)).alias('female_count'), count('*').alias('total_count') ).orderBy('city') City male_count female_count total_count Bangalore 120 95 215 Chennai 80 110 190 Mumbai 200 180 380 Section 6 | Databricks Concepts","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"What Types of Views Exist in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"View Type Persistence Data Description Stored? Temporary View Session only (lost on No createOrReplaceTempView(); only in restart) current SparkSession Global Temp View App lifetime (all No createOrReplaceGlobalTempView(); sessions) access via global_temp.view_name Standard View Metastore (permanent) No CREATE VIEW; saved in Hive/Unity metastore; no data copy Materialized View Metastore (permanent) Yes Pre-computed Delta table; refreshed on schedule or trigger df.createOrReplaceTempView('temp_v') df.createOrReplaceGlobalTempView('global_v') spark.sql('SELECT * FROM global_temp.global_v') CREATE VIEW gold.customer_summary AS SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM silver.transactions GROUP BY 1;","source":"Spark_Databricks_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Display the fla(cid:425)ened DataFrame.","type":"concept","a":"• (cid:57859) Expected Output +--------+-----+--------+ |product |price|quan(cid:415)ty| +--------+-----+--------+ |product1| 100| 2| |product2| 200| 3| +--------+-----+--------+ SQL:- (cid:57874)(cid:57875) SQL Interview Ques(cid:415)on: Running Total of Sales per Store You are given a table named sales with the following structure: sales ------ store_id VARCHAR sale_date DATE sales_amount INT Sample Data store_id sale_date sales_amount S1 2025-01-01 100 S1 2025-01-02 150 S1 2025-01-03 200 S2 2025-01-01 80 S2 2025-01-02 120 S2 2025-01-03 160 (cid:44472)(cid:44473)(cid:44474)(cid:44475) Problem Statement Write an SQL query to calculate the running total of sales for each store, ordered by sale_date\n• Requirements  The running total should reset for each store  The results should be ordered by store_id and sale_date  Use window func(cid:415)ons (cid:33158)(cid:57393) Expected Output store_id sale_date sales_amount running_total S1 2025-01-01 100 100 S1 2025-01-02 150 250 S1 2025-01-03 200 450 S2 2025-01-01 80 80 S2 2025-01-02 120 200 S2 2025-01-03 160 360 Q4 You are given a table named orders with the following structure: orders ------ customer_id INT order_id INT order_date DATE order_amount INT Sample Data customer_id order_id order_date order_amount 1 101 2025-01-01 100 1 102 2025-01-05 150 1 103 2025-01-10 200 2 201 2025-01-02 120 2 202 2025-01-06 110 3 301 2025-01-03 50 3 302 2025-01-07 80 (cid:44472)(cid:44473)(cid:44474)(cid:44475) Problem Statement Write an SQL query to find customers whose every order value is strictly higher than their previous order, ordered by order_date\n• Requirements  Orders must be compared chronologically  Customers with only one order should be considered valid  Use window func(cid:415)ons  Return dis(cid:415)nct customer IDs (cid:33158)(cid:57393) Expected Output customer_id 1 3","source":"SQL & Pyspark coding questions.pdf"},{"q":"SQL Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on Techniques","type":"concept","a":" Create appropriate indexes.  Avoid SELECT *.  Filter early with WHERE.  Op(cid:415)mize joins.  Use execu(cid:415)on plans.  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on large tables.  Use EXISTS instead of IN when appropriate.  Keep sta(cid:415)s(cid:415)cs updated.  Avoid unnecessary func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Two large tables. How will you join?","type":"concept","a":"• Typically use a Sort Merge Join because it scales well for large datasets\n• Also consider:  Par(cid:415)(cid:415)on both tables on the join key.  Handle data skew if present.  Enable AQE for run(cid:415)me op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)ons\n• SQL Indexing Types:  Clustered  Non-Clustered  Composite  Unique  Filtered  Full-Text 16\n• SQL Problem Example: Customers with no orders SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL;","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Window Func(cid:415)ons","type":"concept","a":"Examples: ROW_NUMBER() RANK() DENSE_RANK() LAG() LEAD() NTILE() FIRST_VALUE() LAST_VALUE() Example: SELECT employee, salary, RANK() OVER(ORDER BY salary DESC) FROM Employee;","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Experience on NoSQL DB","type":"concept","a":"If you have experience: \"Yes, I have worked with MongoDB/Cosmos DB for storing semi-structured JSON data and reading it into Databricks for downstream processing.\" If not: \"I haven't worked extensively with NoSQL databases in produc(cid:415)on, but I understand they are used for schema-flexible, high-volume data such as JSON documents and key-value workloads.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Why use sets?","type":"concept","a":"•  Remove duplicates automa(cid:415)cally.  Fast membership checks (O(1) average (cid:415)me).  Perform set opera(cid:415)ons like union, intersec(cid:415)on, and difference\n• Example: nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4] unique_nums = set(nums) print(unique_nums) Output: {1, 2, 3, 4}","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is LAST_VALUE() Window Func(cid:415)on?","type":"concept","a":"• It returns the last value within a window\n• Example Table Emp Salary A 2000 B 4000 C 6000 Query LAST_VALUE(salary) OVER( ORDER BY salary ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING ) Output Emp Last Salary A 6000 B 6000 C 6000 Without UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING Spark considers rows only up to the current row, so the result can be different\n• PySpark from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import last window = Window.orderBy(\"salary\") \\ .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.unboundedFollowing) df.withColumn(\"last_salary\", last(\"salary\").over(window))","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"For each customer, find the second and third highest order amount in PySpark","type":"concept","a":"• Sample Data Customer Order A 500 A 700 A 900 A 300 Customer Order B 100 B 600 B 400 Solu(cid:415)on from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.func(cid:415)ons import dense_rank window = Window.par(cid:415)(cid:415)onBy(\"customer\") \\ .orderBy(col(\"order_amount\").desc()) result = df.withColumn( \"rank\", dense_rank().over(window) ).filter(\"rank IN (2,3)\") result.show() Output Customer Order Rank A 700 2 A 500 3 B 400 2 B 100 3 If duplicate order amounts should receive unique rankings, use row_number() instead of dense_rank()\n• If you want gaps in ranking a(cid:332)er (cid:415)es, use rank()","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Find matched and unmatched records between two tables in SQL","type":"concept","a":"• Suppose: Table A ID 1 2 3 Table B ID 2 3 4 Matched Records SELECT a.* FROM tableA a INNER JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id;\n• Output 2 3 Unmatched from Table A SELECT a.* FROM tableA a LEFT JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id WHERE b.id IS NULL;\n• Output 1 Unmatched from Table B SELECT b.* FROM tableB b LEFT JOIN tableA a ON b.id = a.id WHERE a.id IS NULL;\n• Output 4 All Matched and Unmatched (Full Outer Join) SELECT a.id AS tableA_id, b.id AS tableB_id FROM tableA a FULL OUTER JOIN tableB b ON a.id = b.id;\n• This returns matched rows along with records present only in either table","source":"Tech Mahindra Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Remove duplicate emails.","type":"concept","a":"Example ID Email 1 abc@gmail.com 2 abc@gmail.com 3 xyz@gmail.com SQL WITH CTE AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER ( PARTITION BY Email ORDER BY ID ) rn FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM CTE WHERE rn > 1;","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Complex SQL Join","type":"concept","a":"• Typical interview ques(cid:415)on: Find department-wise total salary\n• SELECT d.DepartmentName, SUM(e.Salary) AS TotalSalary FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.DepartmentID=d.DepartmentID GROUP BY d.DepartmentName;\n• Client Round 3","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Find maximum salary per department name","type":"concept","a":"• Tables:  Employee(emp_id, emp_name, dept_id, salary)  Department(dept_id, dept_name) Answer SELECT d.dept_name, MAX(e.salary) AS max_salary FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id GROUP BY d.dept_name;\n• What if we want employee details also (name, id)?” Then use window func(cid:415)on: WITH ranked AS ( SELECT e.emp_id, e.emp_name, d.dept_name, e.salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER ( PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC ) AS rnk FROM Employee e JOIN Department d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id ) SELECT emp_id, emp_name, dept_name, salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk = 1;\n• Difference (important interview point) Method Output GROUP BY + MAX only salary per department DENSE_RANK full employee details with max salary","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"How do you remove duplicates?","type":"concept","a":"• WITH cte AS ( SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY EMP_ID ORDER BY EMP_ID) AS rn FROM Employee ) DELETE FROM cte WHERE rn > 1;\n• Round 2:","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Find customers who have transac(cid:415)ons on more than 1 different date.","type":"concept","a":"Table: id, transac(cid:415)on_id, transaca(cid:415)on_date(cid:415)me , customer_id, product_id, net_qty, amount, transaca(cid:415)on_date SELECT customer_id FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT transac(cid:415)on_date) > 1;","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"Write a SQL query to calculate the total net quan(cid:415)ty for each product and rank the","type":"concept","a":"products based on total sales quan(cid:415)ty.” product_id net_qty 1 5 Answer: WITH CTE_PRODUCT AS ( SELECT product_id, SUM(net_qty) AS total_qty FROM Transac(cid:415)ons GROUP BY product_id ) SELECT product_id, total_qty, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total_qty DESC) AS rnk_prod FROM CTE_PRODUCT;","source":"Tiger Analytics - Interview Questions Round 1 & 2.pdf"},{"q":"What is DQ (Data Quality)?","type":"concept","a":"• Data Quality (DQ) refers to the degree to which data is fit for its intended use\n• Key dimensions: • Completeness: No missing/null values where required • Accuracy: Data correctly represents real-world values • Consistency: Same data looks the same across all systems • Timeliness: Data is available when needed • Uniqueness: No duplicate records • Validity: Data conforms to expected formats and rules DQ Checks in Databricks: # Using Delta Live Tables expectations @dlt.expect('valid_age', 'age >= 0 AND age <= 120') @dlt.expect_or_drop('not_null_id', 'customer_id IS NOT NULL') # Using Great Expectations or custom PySpark checks df.filter(col('customer_id').isNull()).count() # Should be 0","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"How to Optimize SQL Queries","type":"concept","a":"• Key SQL Optimization Techniques: • 1\n• Use WHERE early: Filter data as early as possible • 2\n• Avoid SELECT *: Only select needed columns • 3\n• Use proper JOIN type: INNER > LEFT OUTER > CROSS • 4\n• Index usage: Ensure joins/filters use indexed columns • 5\n• Partition pruning: Filter on partition columns • 6\n• Use CTEs / temp tables: Avoid repeating complex subqueries • 7\n• Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE: f(col) = val kills indexes • 8\n• EXPLAIN PLAN: Analyze execution plan before running • 9\n• Use BROADCAST hint for small table joins • 10\n• Avoid DISTINCT when not needed — use GROUP BY -- Bad: Function on column disables partition pruning WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2025 -- Good: Range filter enables partition pruning WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-12-31' -- Broadcast hint for small table SELECT /*+ BROADCAST(d) */ f.*, d.product_name FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product d ON f.product_key = d.product_key SECTION 6: Coding Questions","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"PySpark — Highest Salary per Designation","type":"concept","a":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession from pyspark.sql.functions import col, max as spark_max from pyspark.sql.window import Window from pyspark.sql.functions import rank spark = SparkSession.builder.appName('HighestSalary').getOrCreate() # Sample data data = [('Alice', 'Manager', 90000), ('Bob', 'Manager', 85000), ('Charlie', 'Analyst', 60000), ('Dave', 'Analyst', 62000), ('Eve', 'Director', 120000)] df = spark.createDataFrame(data, ['name', 'designation', 'salary']) # Method 1: Window Function (RANK) window_spec = Window.partitionBy('designation').orderBy(col('salary').desc()) result = df.withColumn('rnk', rank().over(window_spec)) \\ .filter(col('rnk') == 1) \\ .select('name', 'designation', 'salary') result.show() # Method 2: Self-join with max salary max_sal = df.groupBy('designation').agg(spark_max('salary').alias('max_salary')) result2 = df.join(max_sal, (df.designation == max_sal.designation) & (df.salary == max_sal.max_salary)) \\ .select(df.name, df.designation, df.salary) result2.show()","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"SQL — Revenue, Common Customers, Consecutive Months","type":"concept","a":"Setup: orders(order_id, customer_id, order_date, amount)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Total Revenue per Month","type":"concept","a":"SELECT DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM') AS year_month, SUM(amount) AS total_revenue FROM orders GROUP BY DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM') ORDER BY year_month;","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Customers who Ordered in Both January AND February 2025","type":"concept","a":"SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-02-28' AND MONTH(order_date) IN (1, 2) GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT MONTH(order_date)) = 2; -- Alternative using INTERSECT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01' AND order_date < '2025-02-01' INTERSECT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= '2025-02-01' AND order_date < '2025-03-01';","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Customers who Ordered in Three Consecutive Months","type":"concept","a":"WITH monthly_orders AS ( SELECT customer_id, DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM-01') AS order_month FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id, DATE_FORMAT(order_date, 'yyyy-MM-01') ), with_lag AS ( SELECT customer_id, order_month, LAG(order_month, 1) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_month) AS prev_month, LAG(order_month, 2) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_month) AS prev2_month FROM monthly_orders ) SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM with_lag WHERE ADD_MONTHS(prev2_month, 1) = prev_month AND ADD_MONTHS(prev_month, 1) = order_month;","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"},{"q":"Sql ques(cid:415)ons for 2nd highest salary and also find 4th highest salary","type":"concept","a":"WITH ranked AS ( SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM Employee ) SELECT salary FROM ranked WHERE rnk in( 2 , 4)","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"How will you perform transforma(cid:415)on.","type":"concept","a":"• I perform transforma(cid:415)ons based on business requirements using SQL or PySpark\n• In Silver layer, I clean and standardize data by removing duplicates, handling nulls, and applying joins\n• In Gold layer, I apply business logic and aggrega(cid:415)ons like SUM, COUNT, and CASE statements to prepare data for repor(cid:415)ng\n• I also use window func(cid:415)ons for advanced transforma(cid:415)ons like ranking and deduplica(cid:415)on","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"},{"q":"Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques.","type":"concept","a":"• Op(cid:415)miza(cid:415)on techniques include reducing data scanned by selec(cid:415)ng only required columns, using proper indexing, avoiding func(cid:415)ons on indexed columns, and pushing filters early\n• In distributed systems like Spark, I use broadcast joins, repar(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, caching, and skew handling techniques\n• At the pipeline level, I implement incremental loads, par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning, and use efficient file formats like Parquet or Delta\n• I also analyze execu(cid:415)on plans to iden(cid:415)fy bo(cid:425)lenecks and improve performance","source":"Tredence - Interview Questions for Fabric.pdf"}]},{"id":"cicd","name":"CI/CD, Orchestration & Production","icon":"🚀","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"How do you implement CI/CD for Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"Pipeline (Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions):\n• Source control — notebooks/code in Git (Azure Repos/GitHub) via Databricks Repos/Git folders.\n• CI — run unit tests (pytest/nutter), lint, build artifacts (wheel/JAR).\n• CD — deploy notebooks/jobs via Databricks CLI/REST API or databricks bundle deploy (Asset Bundles); promote DEV→QA→PROD.\n• Use service principals for deployment auth; store secrets in Key Vault."},{"q":"What are Databricks Asset Bundles (DAB)?","type":"concept","a":"DAB is an infrastructure-as-code way to define Databricks resources (jobs, pipelines, notebooks, clusters, permissions) in a databricks.yml bundle.\n• Supports multiple targets (dev/qa/prod) and parameterization.\n• Deploy with: databricks bundle deploy --target prod; run with databricks bundle run.\n• Enables version-controlled, repeatable deployments across environments."},{"q":"How do you orchestrate/schedule Databricks notebooks?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Workflows (Jobs) — native; multi-task jobs with dependencies, retries, alerting, repair-and-rerun; best for production.\n• ADF — pipeline with Databricks Notebook activities chained on success; best when orchestrating across Azure services (ADB+SQL+Functions).\n• dbutils.notebook.run() — simple chaining from a master notebook (pass args, get exit value).\nRecommendation: use Workflows for production scheduling; ADF for cross-service orchestration."},{"q":"Difference between %run and dbutils.notebook.run()?","type":"concept","a":"• %run — runs another notebook INLINE, sharing scope/variables/functions; no parameters (uses widget defaults); no return value. Use to share utility functions.\n• dbutils.notebook.run() — runs in an ISOLATED context; pass a dict of parameters; returns a string via dbutils.notebook.exit(); has a timeout. Use to orchestrate sequential notebooks and pass/return data."},{"q":"How do you promote code across Dev → QA → Prod in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Separate workspaces (or catalogs/schemas) per environment attached to the same UC metastore.\n• Parameterize storage paths, catalog/schema names, and cluster configs per environment.\n• Use Git + CI/CD (Azure DevOps/GitHub Actions) with service principals to deploy bundles/jobs.\n• Environment-specific config files (databricks.yml targets) or ADF global parameters/ARM params.\n• Run tests in QA before promoting to Prod; keep secrets in Key Vault per environment."},{"q":"For Dev/Test/Prod, should you use one metastore or multiple?","type":"concept","a":"Typical: ONE metastore per region, shared across Dev/Test/Prod workspaces; isolate environments using separate CATALOGS (dev_catalog, test_catalog, prod_catalog), schemas, and permissions.\nUse SEPARATE metastores only for strong isolation: different regions, legal entities, compliance boundaries, or separate organizations."},{"q":"How do you optimize cost in Databricks?","type":"concept","a":"• Use Job clusters (auto-terminate) for production; avoid idle All-Purpose clusters.\n• Enable autoscaling; set auto-termination aggressively.\n• Use instance pools and Spot instances where appropriate.\n• Serverless/SQL Warehouse with auto-suspend for BI.\n• Enable Photon + AQE + predictive optimization to reduce run time.\n• OPTIMIZE/VACUUM to reduce storage/scan; delete temp tables; right-size clusters.\n• Use cluster policies to cap sizes/costs; monitor DBU usage."},{"q":"What are cluster policies?","type":"concept","a":"Cluster policies are admin-defined rules that constrain what cluster configurations users can create (max/min workers, VM types, allowed options, auto-terminate, tags).\nBenefits: cost control, governance, standardization, and self-service within limits. Enforced via Unity Catalog/workspace admin settings."},{"q":"How do you monitor production pipelines and handle failures?","type":"concept","a":"• Databricks Workflows — run history, task-level logs, notifications/alerts on success/failure/duration; repair-and-rerun for failed tasks.\n• ADF — Monitor tab, Azure Monitor/Log Analytics + KQL, alerts via action groups.\n• Audit tables — track load history/watermarks for traceability.\n• DQ checks — fail fast on critical violations, alert the team.\n• Foundation: idempotent MERGE + checkpoints so retries/recovery are safe."},{"q":"How do you parameterize pipelines for reuse across environments?","type":"concept","a":"• ADF: pipeline parameters, variables, global parameters, parameterized datasets/linked services, ARM template overrides per environment.\n• Databricks: widgets (dbutils.widgets.get), job/task parameters, DAB targets, and config files (paths, catalog/schema) per env.\n• Keep environment-specific values (paths, secrets, hosts) OUT of code; inject at deploy/runtime."},{"q":"Explain your CI/CD experience.","type":"concept","a":"• Typical Answer: We use Azure DevOps\n• Flow: Developer ↓ Git Branch ↓ Pull Request ↓ Build Pipeline ↓ Release Pipeline ↓ Dev ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on We deploy:  Notebooks  ADF Pipelines  ARM Templates  Libraries  Config files Tools:  Azure Repos  Azure DevOps  Git  YAML Pipelines","source":"Accenture Interview Questions - Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"How to deploy pipelines","type":"concept","a":"➢ Create a pipeline and select the ASP.NET Core template. ➢ Save the pipeline and queue a build to see it in action. ➢ Create a release pipeline and select the Azure App Service Deployment template for your stage. ➢ Link the build pipeline as an artifact for this release pipeline.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of deployment modes available?","type":"concept","a":"• Pubic 2\n• Private 3\n• Hybrid","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"V1 doesn't contain azure devops git repository integration.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"V1 doesn't contains CICD methodology of deployment","type":"concept","a":"======================SQL QUESTIONS===================","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"System Functions:","type":"concept","a":"--SYSTEM FUNCTIONS SELECT * FROM Products SELECT SUM(UnitPrice) AS TotalPrice FROM Products SELECT COUNT(UnitPrice) AS Total FROM Products SELECT MAX(UnitPrice) AS Maximum FROM Products SELECT MIN(UnitPrice) AS Minimum FROM Products SELECT AVG(UnitPrice) AS Average FROM Products -- STRING FUNCTIONS SELECT * FROM Customers SELECT UPPER(ContactName) as ContactName FROM Customers--uppercase letter SELECT LOWER(ContactName) as ContactName FROM Customers--lower case letter SELECT LEFT(ContactName,5) as ContactName FROM Customers-- it will give first five letter SELECT RIGHT(ContactName,4) as ContactName FROM Customers--it will give last four letter SELECT SUBSTRING('WELCOME',4,4)-- it will start from 4 letter SELECT REPLACE('Ahmedabad','Ahmed','Allah') as Result-- it will replace the letters SELECT LEN('ELEPHANT') AS Result-- it will count the letters SELECT LTRIM(' WELCOME')--it will erase the space from the left SELECT RTRIM('WELCOME ')--it will erase the space from the right SELECT TRIM(' WELCOME ')--it will erase the space SELECT REPLICATE('INDIA ',5)-- it will repate the words SELECT REVERSE('WELCOME')--it will reverse the words -- NUMERIC FUNCTIONS SELECT ABS(-20)-- it will convert the -ve to +ve SELECT ABS(20) SELECT SQRT(25)-- square root of the value SELECT FLOOR(10.8756)-- it will round figure in a lower value SELECT CEILING(10.8756)--it will round figure in a upper value Page | 43 SELECT ROUND(10.8756,2)--it will round the value given operation -- DATE FUNCTIONS SELECT GETDATE() SELECT YEAR(GETDATE()) SELECT MONTH(GETDATE()) SELECT DAY(GETDATE()) SELECT DATENAME(MONTH,GETDATE()) SELECT DATENAME(DW,GETDATE())--date name SELECT DATEPART(HOUR,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(MINUTE,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(SECOND,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(DAY,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(MONTH,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEPART(YEAR,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEADD(DAY,1,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEADD(MONTH,2,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEADD(YEAR,5,GETDATE()) SELECT DATEDIFF(DAY,GETDATE(),'2021-12-24') SELECT DATEDIFF(DAY,'2021-12-24',GETDATE()) SELECT GETUTCDATE()","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"DevOps & Version Control in Azure Data Projects","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"What is DevOps in a Data Engineering context?","type":"concept","a":"• DevOps brings together development (writing pipelines/code) and operations (deploying, monitoring, maintaining) to deliver data solutions faster and more reliably\n• Version Control with Git (Azure DevOps / GitHub) Every ADF pipeline, Databricks notebook, and configuration file is stored in a Git repository\n• This means: • Full history of every change made • Ability to roll back to a previous working version • Multiple team members can work in parallel using branches Branching Strategy — Simple Example A typical project uses 3 branches: Branch Purpose Example main Production-ready code only Live pipelines running in prod develop Integration / testing All devs merge their work here first feature/xyz Individual feature work feature/add-sales-pipeline CI/CD Pipeline Example When a developer pushes code to Git, the CI/CD pipeline automatically:","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Deploys to DEV environment automatically","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"After approval, deploys to UAT and then PROD","type":"concept","a":"• Example Azure DevOps YAML snippet: # azure-pipelines.yml trigger: branches: include: [develop, main] stages: - stage: Deploy_Dev jobs: - job: DeployADF steps: - task: AzureResourceManagerTemplateDeployment@3 inputs: resourceGroupName: 'rg-dev' templateLocation: 'Linked artifact' csmFile: 'adf_publish/ARMTemplateForFactory.json' 💡 Tip: In interviews, say: 'We used Azure DevOps Git integration with ADF\n• Every pipeline change was committed, reviewed via Pull Request, and deployed automatically through our CI/CD pipeline.'","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Implement prevention: add retry policies, better alerts, data validation","type":"concept","a":"• 💡 Tip: Interview answer example: 'At 2 AM I first checked ADF Monitor to find the failed activity and read the error\n• It turned out the source SQL database had a schema change — a column was renamed\n• I updated the column mapping in ADF, redeployed, ran the pipeline manually, verified the row counts matched, and sent an update to the team\n• We then added a schema validation check to catch this earlier in future.' Quick Reference Cheat Sheet Key phrases to use in interviews: • DevOps: 'We used Git integration with ADF, feature branches, Pull Requests, and Azure DevOps CI/CD pipelines for automated deployment.' • Data Flow: 'I built a mapping data flow with Source → Join → Aggregate → Sink, using Debug Mode to test transformations before deploying.' • Late Arriving Data: 'We used a grace period watermark combined with MERGE/Upsert to handle late records without duplicates.' • Alerts: 'We set up Azure Monitor alert rules for pipeline failures and Databricks job notifications, both routing to email and Teams.' • Child Pipelines: 'We parameterized child pipelines and passed load_date and environment from the parent using Execute Pipeline activity.' • Production Incident: 'I follow a structured approach: detect, diagnose, fix, verify, communicate, and prevent recurrence.' Azure Data Engineer Interview Guide | Page 10","source":"Azure_Data_Engineer_Interview_Ans.pdf"},{"q":"Deploy through CI/CD.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Monitor a(cid:332)er deployment.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"How do you know fix works?","type":"concept","a":" Unit tes(cid:415)ng  Regression tes(cid:415)ng  Compare outputs  Validate with business users  Monitor produc(cid:415)on a(cid:332)er deployment","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Are Dev and Produc(cid:415)on synchronized?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes, through Azure DevOps CI/CD pipelines\n• Code is version-controlled in Git, and deployments promote tested changes from Dev to QA/UAT and finally Produc(cid:415)on\n• DevOps Tool  Azure DevOps  Git  YAML Pipelines  ARM/Bicep/Terraform (if used)","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"QA/UAT environment","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• Typical environments: Dev ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on 45\n• Deploy code Typical CI/CD flow: Developer ↓ Git Commit ↓ Pull Request ↓ Build Pipeline ↓ Release Pipeline ↓ QA ↓ UAT ↓ Produc(cid:415)on","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Which CI/CD you will be using. What is DAB?","type":"concept","a":"CI/CD + DAB CI/CD tools:  Azure DevOps / GitHub Ac(cid:415)ons DAB:  Databricks Asset Bundles  Used for deploying notebooks, jobs, pipelines","source":"EY - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"If it fails, how do you debug and resolve it?","type":"concept","a":"• Yes\n• A typical Databricks CI/CD pipeline (Azure DevOps or GitHub Actions): Pipeline stages: • 1\n• Source Control (Git) — Databricks Repos linked to feature branches. • 2\n• CI — Run unit tests (pytest / nutter), lint, notebook validation on Pull Request. • 3\n• CD to Dev — On merge to dev branch, deploy notebooks/jobs via Databricks CLI or REST API. • 4\n• CD to Staging/Prod — Triggered after approval gate; use environment-specific variable groups / Key Vault references for secrets\n• Debugging a failed pipeline: • Check the pipeline run logs in Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions for the exact failing step. • For Databricks job failures: open the job run URL in the logs, examine cluster logs, driver stderr/stdout, and notebook error output. • Common causes: wrong cluster config, missing library, permission issues (IAM/RBAC), bad secret reference, schema mismatch. • Use dbutils.notebook.run() with a timeout and catch exceptions to surface notebook errors in the pipeline. • Re-run the failed stage after fix; use pipeline variables to control which stage to re-execute without rerunning everything. — End of Document —","source":"interview_tcs_Q&A.pdf"},{"q":"Produc(cid:415)on issue you fixed","type":"concept","a":"• Example: \"A pipeline failed because the source schema changed and a new column was added\n• I analyzed the logs in ADF and Databricks, updated the schema handling in PySpark, validated the output in Dev, deployed through Azure DevOps, and monitored the next produc(cid:415)on run.\"","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"Explain the CI/CD process in your company.","type":"concept","a":"• \"We use Azure DevOps for CI/CD\n• Developers commit code to Git repositories using feature branches\n• Pull requests are reviewed before merging into the main branch\n• The CI pipeline validates the code and packages the deployment ar(cid:415)facts\n• The CD pipeline deploys Azure Data Factory pipelines, Databricks notebooks, and related resources to Development, QA, and Produc(cid:415)on environments using release pipelines or YAML-based pipelines\n• Parameters and environment-specific configura(cid:415)ons are managed separately to ensure smooth deployments across environments.\"","source":"Tek Systems - Interview Questions - Round 1, 2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"CI/CD Deployment in Databricks","type":"concept","a":"• CI/CD Pipeline (e.g., Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions): • 1\n• Source Control: Notebooks/code in Git (Azure Repos / GitHub) • 2\n• CI (Continuous Integration): ◦ Run unit tests (pytest, nutter) ◦ Lint checks, code quality ◦ Build artifacts (wheel files, JARs) • 3\n• CD (Continuous Deployment): ◦ Deploy notebooks via Databricks CLI or REST API ◦ Deploy jobs using databricks bundle deploy ◦ Environment promotion: DEV → QA → PROD Databricks Asset Bundles (DAB) — Modern Approach: # databricks.yml bundle: name: my_pipeline targets: dev: { workspace: { host: https://dev.azuredatabricks.net } } prod: { workspace: { host: https://prod.azuredatabricks.net } } resources: jobs: my_job: { name: 'ETL Job', tasks: [...] } # Deploy commands databricks bundle deploy --target dev databricks bundle run --target dev my_job","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]},{"id":"modeling","name":"Data Modeling","icon":"📐","roles":["ade","architect"],"questions":[{"q":"What is a data model? Explain star vs snowflake vs galaxy schema.","type":"concept","a":"A data model defines how data is structured, related, and constrained.\n• Star schema — one central FACT table surrounded by denormalized DIMENSION tables. Simple queries, fast aggregations, BI-friendly. Slight redundancy. Most common in data warehouses.\n• Snowflake schema — dimensions normalized into sub-dimensions (less redundancy, more joins, slower).\n• Galaxy / fact constellation — multiple fact tables sharing conformed dimensions.\nExample: Fact_Sales(sale_id,date_key,product_key,customer_key,amount,qty) + Dim_Date, Dim_Product, Dim_Customer."},{"q":"What are the SCD types (0,1,2,3,4,6)?","type":"concept","a":"• Type 0 — never update (static).\n• Type 1 — overwrite the value; no history.\n• Type 2 — add a new row on change; keep full history with start/end dates + current flag (most common in interviews).\n• Type 3 — add a column to store current + previous value only (1 version back).\n• Type 4 — keep current in main table, history in a separate history table.\n• Type 6 — hybrid (1+2+3): full history rows PLUS a current flag and a current-value column."},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 in detail and how you implement it.","type":"concept","a":"SCD Type 2 preserves history by inserting a NEW row whenever a tracked attribute changes; the natural key stays the same, a surrogate key identifies each version.\nColumns: start_date, end_date, is_current (active flag), surrogate key.\nSteps (Delta MERGE):\n1. Expire the current row if tracked columns changed: set end_date = yesterday, is_current = 0.\n2. Insert a new row for the change: start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1.\n3. Insert brand-new keys as-is.\nOnly one active row per business key at any time."},{"q":"CODING: Implement SCD Type 2 in PySpark using a hash/flag approach.","type":"coding","a":"Read history + daily data, compute a hash of tracked columns, split into unchanged / updated / new, expire updates, insert new + updates, then union.","code":"from pyspark.sql.functions import col, sha2, concat_ws, lit, current_date, when\n\nTRACK = ['name','city']\n\ndef with_hash(df):\n    return df.withColumn('row_hash', sha2(concat_ws('||', *[col(c) for c in TRACK]), 256))\n\nhist = with_hash(history_df)         # has start/end/is_current\nincr = with_hash(daily_df)\n\ncur = hist.filter(col('is_current')==1)\n\n# rows that changed\nupdates = (incr.alias('s').join(cur.alias('t'), 'customer_id')\n           .filter(col('s.row_hash') != col('t.row_hash'))\n           .select('customer_id', *TRACK))\n\n# new keys\nnew = incr.join(cur, 'customer_id', 'left_anti').select('customer_id', *TRACK)\n\n# expire old current rows for changed keys\nexpired = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id').withColumn('end_date', current_date()).withColumn('is_current', lit(0))\nno_change = cur.join(updates, 'customer_id', 'left_anti')\n\n# new versions of changed + brand new\ninserts = (updates.unionByName(new)\n           .withColumn('start_date', current_date())\n           .withColumn('end_date', lit(None).cast('date'))\n           .withColumn('is_current', lit(1)))\n\nfinal = no_change.unionByName(expired).unionByName(inserts)\nfinal.write.format('delta').mode('overwrite').saveAsTable('dim_customer')"},{"q":"What is incremental loading and what approaches exist?","type":"concept","a":"Incremental loading processes only new/changed data since the last load instead of a full reload.\n• Timestamp/watermark — load where updated_at > last_run_timestamp; track a high-watermark in a control table.\n• CDC — capture inserts/updates/deletes from the source (log-based, e.g., Debezium/Azure SQL CDC).\n• Delta MERGE (UPSERT) — merge changes into the target.\nBenefits: faster, cheaper, less source load. Watch for late-arriving data and reliable watermark updates."},{"q":"Difference between full load and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Full load — reload all data every run; simple; high source impact and long runtime; used for first load/small tables.\n• Incremental — load only new/changed records; needs watermark/CDC; low source impact, fast; for large tables. Risk: must handle late-arriving data and deletes."},{"q":"Difference between CDC and incremental load?","type":"concept","a":"• Incremental load — a loading strategy: only bring in new/changed data (often timestamp-based).\n• CDC — a mechanism that captures row-level INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from the source (log-based/trigger-based), so it can also capture deletes and provide before/after images.\nCDC is a superset that feeds richer incremental loads; plain timestamp incremental usually can't detect deletes."},{"q":"Which table loads first, fact or dimension? How do you relate them?","type":"concept","a":"Dimensions load FIRST — facts reference dimension surrogate keys, so the dimensions (and their keys) must exist before the fact rows can resolve those keys.\nRelation: fact tables store foreign keys pointing to dimension surrogate keys; the join is fact.dim_key = dim.surrogate_key.\nOften a 'late-arriving dimension' pattern is used: insert a placeholder (unknown) dimension row if a fact arrives before its dimension."},{"q":"What is the difference between a surrogate key and a natural key?","type":"concept","a":"• Natural (business) key — a meaningful identifier from the source (e.g., customer_id, SSN).\n• Surrogate key — a system-generated integer uniquely identifying each DIMENSION VERSION (used in SCD2 to distinguish versions).\nFacts join to dimensions on surrogate keys; the natural key links versions of the same entity."},{"q":"What are late-arriving dimensions/facts and how do you handle them?","type":"concept","a":"• Late-arriving dimension — a fact arrives referencing a dimension that hasn't loaded yet.\n• Late-arriving fact — a fact arrives after its expected processing window.\nHandling: insert a placeholder/inferred dimension row (unknown key) and update it when the real record arrives; or defer fact loading until the dimension exists; use watermark + reprocessing and idempotent MERGE so late facts are picked up on the next run."},{"q":"What is a slowly changing dimension and why is it needed?","type":"concept","a":"SCD manages dimension attributes that change slowly over time (e.g., customer address, department). The type chosen controls how (and whether) history is preserved — so reports can reflect either the current value or the value as of a point in time."},{"q":"What is a Data Warehouse Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• In a data warehouse, data is organized into Fact Tables (measures/events) and Dimension Tables (descriptive context)\n• Star and Snowflake are two common ways to arrange these tables\n• Star Schema Characteristics: Fact table is in the center\n• Dimension tables surround it like a star\n• Dimension tables are DENORMALIZED — all attributes in one flat table\n• Simple, fast queries — fewer JOINs needed\n• Best for BI tools and reporting (Power BI, Tableau). -- Star Schema Example: Sales Data Warehouse -- Fact Table (center of the star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key, store_key, sale_amount, quantity, discount -- Dimension Tables (flat, denormalized) dim_customer: customer_key, name, city, state, country, email, age_group dim_product: product_key, product_name, category, brand, color, size dim_date: date_key, date, month, quarter, year, weekday, is_holiday dim_store: store_key, store_name, city, state, region, manager ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 9 -- Query: Total sales by product category (only 2 tables needed) SELECT p.category, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key GROUP BY p.category Snowflake Schema Characteristics: Dimension tables are NORMALIZED — broken into sub-dimension tables\n• Looks like a snowflake because dimensions branch into more tables\n• Saves storage (no repeated values)\n• More complex queries with more JOINs\n• Better for data integrity\n• Used when storage is a constraint. -- Snowflake Schema: Same sales warehouse, normalized -- Fact Table (same as star) fact_sales: sale_id, date_key, customer_key, product_key ... -- dim_product is now split into sub-tables dim_product: product_key, product_name, category_key, brand_key dim_category: category_key, category_name, department_key dim_department:department_key, department_name dim_brand: brand_key, brand_name, brand_country -- dim_customer split into sub-tables dim_customer: customer_key, name, city_key, age_group dim_city: city_key, city_name, state_key dim_state: state_key, state_name, country_key dim_country: country_key, country_name, region -- Query: Same total sales by category (now 4 JOINs needed) SELECT dc.category_name, SUM(f.sale_amount) FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_category dc ON p.category_key = dc.category_key GROUP BY dc.category_name Comparison Table Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension structure Denormalized (flat) Normalized (broken into sub- tables) Number of JOINs Fewer — simple queries More — complex queries ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 10 Query performance Faster reads Slightly slower due to more JOINs Storage More (repeated values) Less (normalized, no repetition) Data integrity Lower — some redundancy Higher — data in one place Complexity Simple to understand More complex to maintain Best for BI/Reporting, Power BI Large DW with storage constraints Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'We used Star Schema for our reporting layer because it gives faster query performance and is simpler to work with in Power BI","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Questions answered How much? How many? What Who? What? Where? When?","type":"concept","a":"• total? Primary key Surrogate key (sale_id) Surrogate key (customer_key) Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD) — Bonus • SCD Type 1 — Overwrite: Update the record directly\n• No history kept. (e.g., fix a typo in name) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 12 • SCD Type 2 — New Row: Add a new row with effective dates\n• Full history preserved. (e.g., customer moves city) • SCD Type 3 — Add Column: Add a 'previous value' column\n• Limited history. (e.g., previous_city column) Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'Fact tables store the measurable events — like each sale transaction with amount, quantity, and foreign keys\n• Dimension tables provide the context — customer name, product category, date details\n• In our warehouse, fact_sales had 500M rows while dim_customer had only 2M rows.' Q9 PySpark: Top 10 Customers by Total Sales Problem Given: DataFrame: sales_df Schema: customer_id (string), product_id (string), sale_amount (double) Task: Total sales per customer, return top 10 by total sales amount Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, desc # Step 1: Group by customer_id and sum their sale_amounts total_sales_df = sales_df.groupBy('customer_id') \\ .agg(sum('sale_amount').alias('total_sales')) # Step 2: Sort by total_sales descending, take top 10 top_10_customers = total_sales_df \\ .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) \\ .limit(10) # Display result top_10_customers.show() # Output: # +-----------+------------+ # |customer_id|total_sales | # +-----------+------------+ # |CUST_0042 | 245780.50 | # |CUST_0017 | 198340.00 | # |CUST_0099 | 176220.75 | ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 13 # |... | ... | # +-----------+------------+ Alternative: Using SQL in Databricks # Register as temp view sales_df.createOrReplaceTempView('sales') # SQL approach top_10_sql = spark.sql(''' SELECT customer_id, SUM(sale_amount) AS total_sales FROM sales GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY total_sales DESC LIMIT 10 ''') top_10_sql.show() With Rounding (Production Ready) from pyspark.sql.functions import sum, col, round as spark_round top_10_customers = ( sales_df .groupBy('customer_id') .agg(spark_round(sum('sale_amount'), 2).alias('total_sales')) .orderBy(col('total_sales').desc()) .limit(10) ) top_10_customers.show(truncate=False) Interview Tip: Key functions: groupBy() + agg(sum()) for totals, orderBy(col().desc()) for sorting, limit(10) for top N\n• For very large data, consider using window functions with rank() if you need ties included\n• Q10 PySpark: Fill Null Salaries and Add Salary Grade Problem ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 14 Given: DataFrame: employees_df Schema: employee_id (int), department (string), salary (int) — may have NULLs Task 1: Fill null salaries with 50000 Task 2: Add salary_grade column: >=100000 -> Executive, 50000-99999 -> Manager, <50000 -> Associate Solution from pyspark.sql.functions import col, when, coalesce, lit # Step 1: Fill null salaries with default value 50000 # Method A: fillna (simple, specific column) df_filled = employees_df.fillna({'salary': 50000}) # Method B: coalesce (returns first non-null value) df_filled = employees_df.withColumn( 'salary', coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) ) # Step 2: Add salary_grade column using when/otherwise df_final = df_filled.withColumn( 'salary_grade', when(col('salary') >= 100000, 'Executive') .when((col('salary') >= 50000) & (col('salary') < 100000), 'Manager') .otherwise('Associate') ) df_final.show() # Output: # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # |employee_id|department|salary|salary_grade| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ # | 101| Sales |120000| Executive| # | 102| HR | null| Manager| <- null -> 50000 -> Manager # | 103| Tech | 75000| Manager| # | 104| Sales | 35000| Associate| # +-----------+----------+------+------------+ Key Functions Explained Function Purpose Example fillna(value, subset) Replace nulls in specific fillna({'salary': 50000}) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 15 columns coalesce(col, lit(val)) Return first non-null of multiple coalesce(col('salary'), lit(50000)) exprs when(condition, value) If-else logic for new column when(col('salary')>=100000,'Execut ive') .otherwise(value) Default value if no when .otherwise('Associate') matches withColumn(name, Add or replace a column withColumn('salary_grade', expr) when(...)) Interview Tip: The when().when().otherwise() chain is PySpark's equivalent of SQL CASE WHEN\n• Always put the most specific condition first (>=100000 before >=50000)\n• Use fillna before applying when so null-handling is clean\n• Q11 PySpark: Most Recent Transaction Per User (Window Function) Problem Given: DataFrame: transactions_df Schema: user_id (string), transaction_timestamp (timestamp), transaction_amount (double) Task: Find the single MOST RECENT transaction for each user — return all 3 columns Solution 1: Window Function with rank() — Best Approach from pyspark.sql.functions import col, rank from pyspark.sql.window import Window # Define window: partition by user_id, order by timestamp descending window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) # Add rank column — rank 1 = most recent for each user df_ranked = transactions_df.withColumn('rank', rank().over(window_spec)) # Keep only rank 1 (most recent) and drop the rank column most_recent = df_ranked.filter(col('rank') == 1) \\ .drop('rank') ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 16 most_recent.show() # Output: # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |user_id|transaction_timestamp |transaction_amount| # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ # |U001 |2024-01-15 14:32:00 | 129.99 | # |U002 |2024-01-16 09:15:00 | 49.50 | # |U003 |2024-01-14 21:00:00 | 299.00 | # +-------+-------------------------+------------------+ Solution 2: Using row_number() — Guarantees Single Row from pyspark.sql.functions import col, row_number from pyspark.sql.window import Window # row_number() assigns 1 to the latest tx per user (no ties) # rank() would give ties the same number — use row_number to be safe window_spec = Window.partitionBy('user_id') \\ .orderBy(col('transaction_timestamp').desc()) most_recent = ( transactions_df .withColumn('row_num', row_number().over(window_spec)) .filter(col('row_num') == 1) .drop('row_num') ) Solution 3: groupBy + join (Without Window Functions) from pyspark.sql.functions import max # Step 1: Find max timestamp per user max_ts = transactions_df \\ .groupBy('user_id') \\ .agg(max('transaction_timestamp').alias('max_ts')) # Step 2: Join back to original to get full row most_recent = transactions_df.join( max_ts, (transactions_df.user_id == max_ts.user_id) & (transactions_df.transaction_timestamp == max_ts.max_ts), how='inner' ).select( transactions_df.user_id, transactions_df.transaction_timestamp, ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 17 transactions_df.transaction_amount ) rank() vs row_number() vs dense_rank() Function Tie Handling Returns single row Use When per user? row_number() No ties — arbitrary order for Always exactly 1 Need strictly 1 ties row per group rank() Ties get same rank, gap May return >1 if Aware of ties, after timestamp tie want them included dense_rank() Ties get same rank, no gap May return >1 if Want timestamp tie consecutive rank numbers Interview Tip: Interview answer: 'I use a Window function with row_number() partitioned by user_id and ordered by transaction_timestamp descending. row_number() guarantees exactly one row per user even if two transactions have the same timestamp\n• Then I filter where row_num = 1 and drop the helper column.' Quick Reference — One-Line Answers Topic One-Line Answer Schedule Trigger Runs pipeline on fixed time — daily, hourly, weekly Tumbling Window Trigger Fixed non-overlapping windows — retryable, perfect for incremental loads Storage Event Trigger Fires when a file lands in ADLS Gen2 / Blob — event-driven On-Prem Migration SHIR -> ADF -> ADLS Gen2 (staging) -> Transform -> Azure SQL + watermark control table File Routing by Suffix Get Metadata + ForEach + dynamic sink table name using @replace(last(split(name,'_')),'.csv','') CSV-Driven Routing Lookup (read CSV) + ForEach + dynamic Copy Activity using @item().Country_code Star Schema Fact + flat denormalized dims — fewer JOINs, faster queries, best for BI Snowflake Schema Fact + normalized sub-dims — more JOINs, less storage, more data integrity Fact Table Measurable events (sale_amount, quantity) + FK to dimensions — very large ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 18 Dimension Table Descriptive context (name, city, category) — smaller, used for grouping/filtering Top 10 Customers groupBy('customer_id').agg(sum).orderBy(desc).limit(10) Fill Null + Salary Grade fillna({'salary':50000}) then withColumn('grade', when().when().otherwise()) Most Recent Transaction Window(partitionBy, orderBy desc) + row_number() + filter(row_num==1) ADF & Data Engineering Interview Guide | Page 19","source":"ADF_DataEng_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"How to implement Slowly changing type1","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"00:00.0\").show() -- it will add the Time stamp","type":"concept","a":"• spark.read.format('delta').load(\"/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta\",versionAsof=1).show() – if version is 0, it will execute the first table(df) if version is 1, it will execute the updated table ----In sql------- from delta.tables import DeltaTable -- (it will convert the file to Table) spark.sql(\"CREATE TABLE dbtablenew USING DELTA LOCATION '/Filestore/tables/storingdelta/filedelta'\") -- (it will create a new table ) %sql select * from dbtablenew %sql -- it will update and insert when mergering the files MERGE INTO dbtablenew USING dbtable ON dbtablenew.eno = dbtable.eno WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET dbtablenew.data = dbtable.data WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (eno, ename, sal) VALUES (eno, ename, sal) Page | 21 =========DATA WAREHOUSE CONCEPTS============ ➢ Data Ware house it is used for the ETL because fro analysis purpose. ➢ We want to design a OLTP system , we should a have modeling is E-R Modeling ( Entity Relational Modeling) and have a Primary key and Foreign Key. ➢ Entity relational is nothing but a tables like customer tables, location tables, category, sub category and making a relational between two tables. ➢ It is an real time object and this tables we can call as Master tables. ➢ There is a Transaction table(sales tables, etc) in that we can see the information but we want use a joins. ➢ OLTP concept it is for any update or insert should be a normalized because there is tables in a Small and performance is less. ➢ In that OLTP want get a information is difficult because there is a different tables like customer table and Details customer table . ➢ We want to implement the business intelligence, so we have to apply to data because it is not a format data\n• Master Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Primary Keys Product ID Product name 1 Honda Bike 2 Tvs Bike Customer ID Customer Name 1 Rama 2 Siva Transaction Table: Product ID Customer ID Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ By using a ETL to loading the data OLTP system to another system, so we called a Data Ware Housing or OLAP. ➢ By Designing a OLAP we have modeling is Dimensional and tables we can called as Dimensional Tables( Dimproduct, dimcustomer, etc) in that we have a Bussiness key. ➢ It will give the information from table will get a combination of Dimcustomer and Dim detalis customer. ➢ In this dimensional it will check the is existing or not, if not existing it will update or insert. ➢ In this Dimensional table will have a Surrogate key, while inserting the data automatically it will give a Unique number and easily we can identify the data. ➢ There is Fact Table it is a connection of all dimensions tables. ➢ We can merge and also we can add a Values like Quantity, amount etc\n• Dimensional Tables: Product ID and Customer ID are Business Keys and Pkey and Ckey are called a Surrogate key\n• Page | 22 PKey Product ID Product name 1 1 Honda Bike 2 2 Tvs Bike CKey Customer ID Customer Name 1 1 Rama 2 2 Siva Fact Table: Pkey Ckey Quantity Amount 1 1 6 123455 2 2 5 7654567 ➢ In this Dimensional there is two schema 1\n• Star Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snow Flake Schema","type":"concept","a":"S.NO Star Schema Snowflake Schema","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, The fact tables and While in snowflake schema, The fact tables,","type":"concept","a":"the dimension tables are contained. dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema is a top-down model. While it is a bottom-up model.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Star schema uses more space. While it uses less space.","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It takes less time for the execution While it takes more time than star schema for the","type":"concept","a":"of queries. execution of queries.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"In star schema, Normalization is While in this, Both normalization and","type":"concept","a":"not used. denormalization are used.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"The query complexity of star While the query complexity of snowflake schema is","type":"concept","a":"schema is low. higher than star schema.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"It has high data redundancy. While it has low data redundancy.","type":"concept","a":"Page | 23 Star Schema Diagram: Snow Flake Diagram: Page | 24 ===================Azure Questions===============================","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Round Robin: It will dis tribute data randomly row by row on each node. Less volume","type":"concept","a":"of data tables , dimension tables(customer, product) Disadvantage is data need to sorted at the time of combining from all nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Hash: It will distributed based on column similar values like same kind of product","type":"concept","a":"• ID will share one node\n• High volume data tables , Fact tables(Sales table) Advantage is no need of sorting while combining of data from nodes","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is dimension modelling?","type":"concept","a":"• A.Dimensional Data Modelling is one of the data modelling techniques used in data warehouse design\n• To improve the data retrieval\n• Dimensional modeling involves the use of fact and dimension tables to maintain a record of historical data in data warehouses","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is star schema and snowflake schema?","type":"concept","a":"• A.In star schema, The fact tables and the dimension tables are contained\n• In this schema fewer foreign- key join is used\n• This schema forms a star with fact table and dimension tables\n• In snowflake schema, The fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub dimension tables are contained\n• This schema forms a snowflake with fact tables, dimension tables as well as sub- dimension tables","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is surrogate key?","type":"concept","a":"A.Surrogate key also called a synthetic primary key, is generated when a new record is inserted into a table automatically by a database that can be declared as the primary key of that table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are different kind of dimensions?","type":"concept","a":"A.Fact means measures and dimension means description related to facts.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Slowly changing dimension: Changing over a period of time.is nothing but","type":"concept","a":"address These are three types Type1: need to override address, Type2: store all address Type3: need to store only current and previous address only or current previous and before one","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed dimension: A dimension table which can be used multiple fact tables is","type":"concept","a":"nothing but conformed dimension.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Degenerate dimension: Fact table contain dimension table information that is","type":"concept","a":"called as degenerate dimension table.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Junk Dimension: If a model has to many small dimension, we can take all this model","type":"concept","a":"in one dimension table is nothing but junk dimensions Ex: ‘True or False’, ‘Yes or No’, ‘Male and Female’, ’Single or married’","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Role-Plying dimension: Fact table is more than two times relationship with dimension","type":"concept","a":"table is nothing but Role-Play dimension","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Static dimension: Something which never change like gender is nothing but static dimension","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What is different kind of facts?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Additive facts: All data in the fact can be aggregate, we can apply all aggregate","type":"concept","a":"functions on data example sales.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Semi additive facts: we cannot apply all aggregate function but you will able to apply","type":"concept","a":"some aggregate functions example current account balance.","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Non additive facts: we cannot apply any aggregate function like percentage tables","type":"concept","a":"Page | 30","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What are the different kind of fact tables in DWH?","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Fact less fact table: It does not contain any measure in it contain only foreign","type":"concept","a":"from dimension table","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Snapshot fact table: Store the data in relation to date or time","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Centipede fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"Conformed fact table:","type":"concept","a":"","source":"AZURE INTERVIEW QUESTIONS.pdf"},{"q":"What data is moved to Synapse?","type":"concept","a":"Only analy(cid:415)cs-ready datasets:  Fact tables  Dimension tables  Aggregated tables  Repor(cid:415)ng datasets Opera(cid:415)onal tables remain in SQL Server.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Implement SCD Type 2","type":"concept","a":"Steps:","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"Fact and Dimension","type":"concept","a":"Dimension:  Customer  Product  Employee Fact:  Sales  Orders  Transac(cid:415)ons SCD Type 2 is generally applied to dimension tables.","source":"Common Interview Questions.pdf"},{"q":"About SCD (Slowly Changing Dimensions)","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) is a data warehousing technique for managing changes to dimension data over time\n• Different types handle historical tracking differently: SCD Type Strategy Keeps History? Storage Impact Type 0 Never update — static dimension No Minimal Type 1 Overwrite — replace old value with No Minimal new Type 2 Add new row — expire old, insert Yes (full history) Grows over time new with dates Type 3 Add new column — current + Partial (1 version Moderate previous value only back) Type 4 History table — current in main, Yes Moderate history in separate table Type 6 Hybrid: Type 1+2+3 combined Yes + current flag Larger SCD Type 2 is most commonly asked in interviews: • A new row is added for each change to a dimension record • The old row is 'expired' by setting end_date and is_current = 0 • The new row gets start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = 1 • The natural key (e.g., customer_id) remains the same; a surrogate key identifies each version Page 5 of 22 Databricks, Spark & Azure — Advanced Interview Preparation Guide","source":"Databricks_Advanced_Interview_Guide.pdf"},{"q":"Tell me about yourself and your skill-set?","type":"concept","a":"• I am a Data Engineer with experience in building and optimizing data pipelines, ETL/ELT workflows, and cloud- based data platforms\n• My core skill-set includes: • Big Data Technologies: Apache Spark (PySpark), Databricks, Delta Lake • Cloud Platforms: Azure, AWS, or GCP — cloud storage, compute, and managed services • Data Warehousing: Designing star/snowflake schemas, building fact and dimension tables • Programming: Python, SQL, Scala (basic) • Data Ingestion: Auto Loader, Kafka, Azure Data Factory, or AWS Glue • Orchestration: Apache Airflow, Databricks Workflows, Azure Data Factory pipelines • Version Control: Git, CI/CD pipelines for data • Data Governance: Implementing SCD-1, SCD-2, data quality checks, schema evolution I have worked on end-to-end data platform projects including migrations, real-time ingestion, and building curated analytical layers for BI consumption","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD-2?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD Type 2 (Slowly Changing Dimension Type 2) tracks the full history of changes to a dimension record by creating a new row for each change rather than overwriting the existing record\n• Extra Columns Required for SCD-2: • start_date / effective_from: Date when the record became active. • end_date / effective_to: Date when the record was superseded (NULL for current record). • is_current / is_active: Boolean flag — TRUE for the latest active record. • (Optional) surrogate_key: A system-generated unique key for each row version\n• How SCD-2 Works: • When a record changes, the existing row is 'closed' by setting end_date = today and is_current = False. • A new row is inserted with the updated values, start_date = today, end_date = NULL, is_current = True. • Historical queries can use the date range to retrieve the state of a record at any point in time","source":"DataEngineering_Interview_Accenture_QA.pdf"},{"q":"Explain SCD Type 2 implementation using PySpark?","type":"concept","a":"• SCD (Slowly Changing Dimension) Type 2 is used to maintain the complete history of changes in dimension data\n• Instead of updating an existing record, we expire the old record and insert a new one whenever a tracked attribute changes\n• This allows us to preserve historical data for reporting and auditing Steps","source":"Hexaware - Interview Questions - Round 1,2&3.pdf"},{"q":"Explain all the SCD types and difference between them","type":"concept","a":"SCD Types & Differences Type Behavior History Use Case SCD 0 No change allowed No Sta(cid:415)c data SCD 1 Overwrite old data No history Fixing errors SCD 2 New row for changes Full history Tracking changes SCD 3 Adds new column for previous value Limited history Few a(cid:425)ributes only (cid:44934) Example:  Salary change: o SCD1 → overwrite old salary o SCD2 → new row inserted o SCD3 → old salary stored in another column","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Difference between Star & Snowflake schema","type":"concept","a":"Star vs Snowflake Schema Feature Star Schema Snowflake Schema Structure Denormalized Normalized Joins Fewer More Performance Faster Slower Storage More space Op(cid:415)mized Complexity Simple Complex Example:  Star → Fact table + dimension tables  Snowflake → dimension tables further split","source":"LTM - Interview Questions Round 1.pdf"},{"q":"Do you know data modeling? How do you rate yourself out of 5?","type":"concept","a":"• \"Yes, I have worked with data modeling while designing data warehouse solu(cid:415)ons\n• I'm comfortable with fact and dimension tables, Star Schema, Snowflake Schema, normaliza(cid:415)on, and denormaliza(cid:415)on\n• I would rate myself 4 out of 5 because I have prac(cid:415)cal experience, while I'm s(cid:415)ll expanding my knowledge in advanced modeling techniques like Data Vault.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"We have transac(cid:415)onal data and analy(cid:415)cal data. Which schema do you use?","type":"concept","a":"• \"For transac(cid:415)onal systems (OLTP), I use a normalized schema (3NF) because it minimizes redundancy and maintains data integrity for frequent inserts, updates, and deletes\n• For analy(cid:415)cal systems (OLAP), I generally use a Star Schema because it is op(cid:415)mized for repor(cid:415)ng, aggrega(cid:415)on, and fast query performance.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What is the difference between Star Schema and Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"Star Schema Snowflake Schema Dimension tables are denormalized Dimension tables are normalized Fewer joins More joins Be(cid:425)er query performance Slightly slower due to addi(cid:415)onal joins Easier to understand More complex More storage required Less storage required Preferred for most data warehouses Used when dimensions have significant redundancy Example: Star Schema Product | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Store Snowflake Schema Product | Product Category | Customer -- Sales Fact -- Date | Geography | Country","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Why do you choose Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal data?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I prefer a Star Schema for analy(cid:415)cal workloads because repor(cid:415)ng queries usually aggregate millions of records\n• Since the dimension tables are denormalized, fewer joins are required, which improves query performance\n• It's also easier for BI tools like Power BI and Tableau to consume.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"When would you use Snowflake Schema?","type":"concept","a":"• \"I use a Snowflake Schema when dimension tables contain a lot of redundant data or have natural hierarchical rela(cid:415)onships, such as Country → State → City or Category → Subcategory → Product\n• Normalizing these dimensions reduces redundancy and makes updates easier, although it requires more joins.\"","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"What are Dimension and Fact Tables? Explain in detail.","type":"concept","a":"• Fact Table Stores measurable business events\n• Examples:  Sales Amount  Quan(cid:415)ty Sold  Revenue  Profit Contains:  Numeric measures  Foreign keys to dimension tables Example: ProductKey CustomerKey DateKey SalesAmount 101 201 20240101 500 Dimension Table Stores descrip(cid:415)ve informa(cid:415)on\n• Examples:  Customer  Product  Employee  Date  Store Example: ProductKey ProductName Category 101 Laptop Electronics Rela(cid:415)onship Customer Dimension | Product Dimension -- Sales Fact -- Date Dimension | Store Dimension Difference Fact Table Dimension Table Stores business measures Stores descrip(cid:415)ve a(cid:425)ributes Very large Smaller Contains foreign keys Contains primary keys Used for calcula(cid:415)ons Used for filtering and grouping","source":"Sapient Interview Questions 1,2 & 3.pdf"},{"q":"Large Fact and Dimension Tables","type":"concept","a":"If both tables are large:  Prefer Sort Merge Join.  Ensure par(cid:415)(cid:415)oning on the join key.  Avoid broadcas(cid:415)ng either table.  Enable AQE to op(cid:415)mize joins dynamically.","source":"TCS - Interview Questions Round 1 – Exp 4 to 5 yrs.pdf"},{"q":"What is a Data Model? Which One Did You Use? (Star Schema)","type":"concept","a":"• A Data Model defines the structure, relationships, and rules for organizing data\n• Common models: Star Schema (Most Common in DW): One central Fact table surrounded by Dimension tables\n• No normalization of dimensions. • Fact Table: Contains measurable, quantitative data (sales_amount, quantity) • Dimension Tables: Descriptive attributes (customer, product, date, location) • Pros: Simple queries, fast aggregations, easy for BI tools • Cons: Data redundancy in dimensions Snowflake Schema: • Extension of star schema where dimensions are normalized into sub-dimensions • Pros: Less redundancy\n• Cons: More joins, slower queries Galaxy/Fact Constellation Schema: • Multiple fact tables sharing dimension tables Example Star Schema: Fact_Sales (sale_id, date_key, product_key, customer_key, amount, qty) Dim_Date (date_key, date, month, quarter, year) Dim_Product(product_key, name, category, brand) Dim_Customer(customer_key, name, city, country)","source":"Tiger_Analytics DE Answers.pdf"}]}]}]};
